window.RV_LOADED['ib-physics-sl'] = {"name":"IB Physics SL","topics":[{"name":"Fields","subtopics":[{"name":"Electromagnetic Fields","questions":[{"ref":"PH0048","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0048\n\nFour fixed charged point particles are at the vertices of a rectangle. Which arrow represents the net electric field at the center of the rectangle? \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0048.png\" alt=\"PH0048\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nSumming the four electric field vectors from the individual point charges, the fields from the charge at the top left and the bottom right will cancel out. The fields from the bottom left and top right will add together to give a net field in the direction of the charge in the top right. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0048-1.png\" alt=\"PH0048-1\">"},{"ref":"PH0108","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0108\n\nTwo positive charges of magnitude $2\\,$C and $8\\,$C respectively are placed 9 cm apart. A third charge is introduced between the two charges as shown below;\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0108-1aa.png\" alt=\"PH0108-1aa\">\n\nAt what separation, X, from the 2 C charge is the electric field strength equal to zero?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $0.19\\,$cm\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $1.70\\,$cm\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $3.00\\,$cm\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $4.50\\,$cm","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0108MS.png\" alt=\"PH0108MS\">\n\nThe charge experiences a net zero force when $E_1=E_2$\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\n\nE&=\\frac{kq}{r^2}\\\\\n\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n\\frac{2}{x^2} &= \\frac{8}{(9-x)^2}\\\\\n\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n(9-x)^2&=4x^2\\\\\n\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n9-x&=2x\\\\\n\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n3x&=9\\\\\n\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\nx&=3.00\\,\\ce{cm}\\\\\n\n\\end{aligned}$"},{"ref":"PH0227","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0227\n\nA charge of $X$ and a charge of 20 nC are held at a fixed distance.  \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0227.png\" alt=\"PH0227\">\n\nA point where the electric field is zero can only be found in region 1. Which of the following statements is true about the charge on $X$? \n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Positive and bigger than 20 nC\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Positive and smaller than 20 nC\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Negative and bigger than 20 nC\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Negative and smaller than 20 nC","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe 20 nC charge produces a leftward electric field in region 1. Therefore, the electric field generated by the $X$ charge should be rightward  to cancel the electric field of the 20 nC charge. This is achieved by a negative charge at X. The magnitude of the electric field decreases by distance. All locations in region 1 are closer to point charge $X$ than they are to the 20 nC charge. Therefore $X$ should be smaller than the charge of 20 nC. The answer is D."},{"ref":"PH0355","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0355\n\nA plastic rod carrying a uniformly distributed positive charge is bent into a quarter-circle. What is the direction of the electric field at point $P$?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0355.png\" alt=\"PH0355\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe electric field strength direction is the same as the direction of the electric force.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0355-1.png\" alt=\"PH0355-1\">\n\nThe electric field will be directed out of the rod in all directions, as it contains uniformly distributed positive charges. \n\nGetting the vector sum for the electric field, due to symmetry the total electric field strength will be directed out of the rod with an angle away from the midpoint of the rod as shown\n\nAlso we can think of B as the only correct solution, because it’s a positively charged rod, so the direction must be out of the rod."},{"ref":"PH0372","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0372\n\nA hydrogen nucleus is held stationary in a uniform vertical electric field, represented by the arrows. Which of the paths shown would require the largest postive work to be done by an external force on the hydrogen nucleus?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/Screenshot%202026-02-02%20at%208.34.02%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-02-02 at 8.34.02 AM\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe hydrogen nucleus is a proton, so it has positive charge.\n\nThe electric field lines are pointed upwards, meaning that the electric force on the nucleus due to the field is upwards.\n\nThe question asks for the largest positive work done, therefore we are looking for the largest displacement opposite to the direction the field lines, against the electric force. This is shown in D."},{"ref":"PH0391","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0391\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the unit of electric potential difference?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$    N m $\\ce{C^{-1}}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    N  $\\ce{m^{-1}} \\, \\ce{C^{-1}}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    J C\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    J $\\ce{m^{-1}} \\, \\ce{C^{-1}}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe unit of electric potential is V.\n\nIt is equivalent to J $\\ce{C^{-1}}$ since it is defined as the work done per unit charge.\n\nWork = (force)(displacement), so $1\\,\\ce{J} = 1\\,\\ce{N} \\,\\ce{m}$\n\nTherefore $1\\, \\ce{V} = 1\\,\\ce{J}\\, \\ce{C^{-1}} = 1\\,\\ce{N} \\,\\ce{m} \\,\\ce{C^{-1}}$"},{"ref":"PH0409","marks":"11","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0409\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nTwo point charges of $-4.0\\, \\ce{\\mu}$C and $8.0\\,\\ce{\\mu}$C are separated by a distance $r = 2.0$ cm as shown in the diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0409.png\" alt=\"PH0409\">\n\n1. State what is meant by electric field strength. [2 marks]\n\n2. Calculate the magnitude of the electrostatic force exerted on each charge. [1 mark]\n\n3. Three points $A$, $B$ and $C$ are at the regions shown in the diagram.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0409-1.png\" alt=\"PH0409-1\">\n\n    1. Deduce which point(s) could have zero electric field. Explain your reasoning. [3 marks]\n\n    2. Calculate the distance(s) from the negative charge to the point(s) of zero electric field. [2 marks]\n\n   \n\n    3.  The two charges are inserted in a water tank separated by the same distance as in  The permittivity of water is 80 times that of free space.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0409-2.png\" alt=\"PH0409-2\">\n\n$\\hspace{3em}$Discuss what change, if any, will occur in the location of the point(s) of zero electric field in this case. Justify your answer. [2 marks]\n\n4. The charges are removed from the water tank, and the negative charge  is replaced by another charge of 8.0µC\n\n    Point P is at equidistant from both charges.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/Screenshot%202024-10-27%20at%206.30.36%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2024-10-27 at 6.30.36 PM\">\n\n    \n\n    Draw an arrow indicating the direction of the electric field at point $P$. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. The force exerted per unit charge $\\checkmark$\n\n    \n\n    on a small positive test charge $\\checkmark$\n\n    \n\n    Accept either “small” or “test” or both.\n\n2. ::indent\n\n    - $F=\\ll(8.99 \\times 10^9)\\dfrac{(4\\times 10^{-6})(8\\times 10^{-6})}{(0.02)^2}\\gg$ \n\n    - $F=$:answer[720 (719.2) $\\ll \\ce{N}\\gg$] /Allow bald correct answer $\\checkmark$\n\n   \n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    The electrostatic force $F$,\n\n    \n\n    - $F=k\\dfrac{q_1 q_2}{r^2} \\hspace{1em}$\n\n    \n\n    - $ F =(8.99 \\times 10^9)\\dfrac{(4.0\\times 10^{-6})(8.0\\times 10^{-6})}{(0.02)^2} = 719.2\\,\\ce{N}$ \n\n3. \n\n    1. $\\ll$Point$\\gg$ $A$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Electric field is a vector quantity / Electric field is coming out of the positive charge and going into a negative charge (Allow for electric field arrows drawn on the diagram) $\\checkmark$\n\n        Electric fields closer to the smaller charge may cancel each other/electric field away from the smaller charge is weak / OWTTE $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The directions of the electric field due to charge 1 and charge 2 are shown\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0409-4.png\" alt=\"PH0409-4\">\n\n        In the middle region, at point $B$, the electric fields are in the same direction so they can't cancel each other.\n\n        For point $C$, its distance from $q_1$ is bigger than that from $q_2$, also $q_1$ is less than $q_2$.\n\n        Since\n\n $E=k \\frac{q}{r^2}$,\n\n        The electric field is directly proportional to the quantity of charge and inversely proportional to the square of the distance from this charge.\n\n        Therefore, the electric field due to $q_1$ is less than that of $q_2$.\n\n        For point $A$, it's closer to $q_1$, so the electric fields due to both charges can cancel each other and become zero. \n\n    2. ::indent\n\n        - $E_1=E_2$\n\n        \n\n        **OR**\n\n        - $\\dfrac{4}{x^2} =\\dfrac{8}{(x+2)^2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - x=:answer[4.82]$\\ll \\ce{cm}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$ \n\n        \n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The electric field at point $A$ is zero, so\n\n        - $E_1=E_2 k\\dfrac{q_1}{r_1\\textrm{}^2} =k\\dfrac{q_2}{r_2\\textrm{}^2}$\n\n        \n\n        let the distance between $q_1$ and point $A$ is $x$,\n\n        \n\n        - $\\dfrac{4}{x^2} =\\dfrac{8}{(x+2)^2}$\n\n        Solving for $x$,\n\n        - $x=4.82\\,\\ce{cm}$\n\n    3. Same $\\ll$ as calculated before $\\gg$ / no change. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $k \\dfrac{q_1}{r_1\\textrm{}^2} = k\\dfrac{q_2}{r_2\\textrm{}^2} / k$ will cancel from both sides and not affect the value. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        the electric fields from both charges are equal,\n\n        - $k\\dfrac{q_1}{r_1\\textrm{}^2} =k\\dfrac{q_2}{r_2\\textrm{}^2}$\n\n        Where\n\n        \n\n        - $k=\\dfrac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon}$\n\n        $k$ will cancel from both sides, so it won't affect the value calculated in (c)(ii)\n\n4. Vertically upwards arrow $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    The two charges are identical, so they both are positive equal charges.\n\nThe electric field is directed out from a positive charge, so the electric field due to the individual charges will show symmetry about the vertical. Because P is equidistant from both charges, the magnitudes of the electric field vectors are equal.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/Screenshot%202024-10-27%20at%206.33.22%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2024-10-27 at 6.33.22 PM\">\n\n    Adding the two electric field vectors together will result in a vertical vector that represents the net electric field.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/Screenshot%202024-10-27%20at%206.36.28%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2024-10-27 at 6.36.28 PM\">"},{"ref":"PH0411","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0411\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nA non-conducting sphere with radius R carries a uniformly distributed positive charge with charge per unit volume $\\rho$.\n\nPoint $P$ is at a distance $r$ from the center of the sphere.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0411aaaa.png\" alt=\"PH0411aaaa\">\n\nThe electric field outside this sphere is given by\n\n- $E_{out}=\\dfrac{\\rho R^3}{3\\varepsilon_0 r^2}$\n\nWhile the electric field inside the sphere is given by\n\n- $E_{in}=\\dfrac{\\rho r}{3\\varepsilon_0}$\n\nWhere $\\varepsilon_0$ is the free space permittivity.\n\nThe radius of the sphere is 2.0 cm. \n\n1. Calculate $\\rho$ when the charge on the sphere is 0.1 $\\mu \\ce{C}$. [2 marks]\n\n2. Determine the electric field at point $P$, when $r = 3$ cm. [2 marks]\n\n3. In the space provided, sketch the variation of electric field $E$ with the distance from the center of the sphere $r$. [2 marks]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/Screenshot%202026-09-19%20at%205.51.35%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-09-19 at 5.51.35 AM\">\n\nA positive charge is released from the surface of the sphere.\n\n4. Describe the motion of this released charge. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. ::indent\n\n    - $\\rho=\\dfrac{0.1\\,\\times \\,10^{-6}}{(\\frac{4}{3})(\\pi)(0.02^3)}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    \n\n    - $\\rho =$ :answer[$2.98 \\times 10^{-3}\\,\\ll \\ce{C\\,m^{-3}}\\gg$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    $\\rho$ is the charge per unit volume,\n\n    - $\\rho=\\dfrac{Q}{V}$\n\n    The volume of a sphere is $\\frac{4}{3} \\pi R^3$\n\n    - $\\rho=\\dfrac{Q}{\\frac{4}{3} \\pi R^3}=\\dfrac{0.1\\,\\times\\, 10^{-6}}{(\\frac{4}{3})(\\pi)(0.02^3)}=2.98\\times 10^{-3}\\, \\ce{C\\,m^{-3}}$\n\n2. ::indent\n\n    - $E=\\dfrac{(2.98\\,\\times \\,10^{-3})(0.02^3)}{(3)(8.85\\,\\times \\,10^{-12})(0.03)^2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $E =$ :answer[$9.98\\times 10^5\\ll \\ce{N\\,C^{-1}}\\gg$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    \n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    When $r = 3$ cm, this means that point $P$ is outside the sphere, since $r$ > $R$.\n\n    Therefore,\n\n    - $E_{out}=\\dfrac{\\rho R^3}{3\\varepsilon_0 r^2}$\n\n    \n\n    - $E=\\dfrac{(2.98\\,\\times\\, 10^{-3})(0.02^3)}{(3)(8.85\\,\\times\\, 10^{-12})(0.03)^2}=9.98\\,\\times\\, 10^5\\, \\ce{N\\,C^{-1}}$\n\n3. Line with positive gradient from the origin till $r$ = $R$. $\\checkmark$\n\n    Decreasing curve for $r$ > $R$. $\\checkmark$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/Screenshot%202026-09-19%20at%205.53.38%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-09-19 at 5.53.38 AM\">\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    For $r \\leq R$,\n\n    - $E_{in}=\\dfrac{\\rho r}{3\\varepsilon_0}$\n\n    Electric field $E$ is directly proportional to $r$.\n\n    The graph should be a line from the origin with a positive gradient.\n\n    For $r > R$,\n\n    - $E_{out}=\\dfrac{\\rho R^3}{3\\varepsilon_0 r^2}$\n\n    This is a reciprocal squared function, so the graph will be a decreasing curve since the electric field $E$ is inversely proportional to the square of $r$.\n\n4. Accelerated motion away from the sphere $\\checkmark$\n\n    The acceleration decreases with time/distance away from the sphere $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    When a positive charge is released from the sphere, there will be an electrostatic force between the sphere and this charge, so the charge will accelerate away from the sphere.\n\n    Since the force is inversely proportional to the square of the separating distance,\n\n    - $F=k \\dfrac{(q_1 q_2)}{r^2}$\n\n    The force will decrease when the separating distance increases, accordingly the acceleration of the charge will decrease.\n\n    Recall that $F=ma$"},{"ref":"PH0417","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0417\n\nTwo positive charges $q_1$ and $q_2$ are separated by a distance $d$. A third positive charge $q$ is placed at a distance $\\dfrac{d}{4}$ from $q_1$ so that the charge $q$ is in equilibrium.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0417.png\" alt=\"PH0417\">\n\nWhat is the ratio $\\dfrac{q_1}{q_2}$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 16\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 4\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{1}{9}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 3","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nFor charge $q$ to be in equilibrium, the magnitude of forces from $q_1$ and $q_2$ must be equal\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0417-1.png\" alt=\"PH0417-1\">\n\n- $\\begin{aligned}\n\nF_1&=(F_2)\\\\\n\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n\\frac{kq_1 q}{(\\dfrac{d}{4})^2} &=\\frac{kqq_2}{(d-\\dfrac{d}{4})^2}\\\\\n\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n\\frac{q_1}{(\\dfrac{d}{4})^2} &= \\frac{q_2}{(\\dfrac{3d}{4})^2}\\\\\n\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n\\frac{q_1}{q_2} &=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{d^2}{16}}{\\dfrac{9d^2}{16}} =\\frac{1}{9}\\\\\n\n\\end{aligned}$"},{"ref":"PH0418","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0418\n\nTwo charges separated by a distance $r$ are placed in a medium with permittivity $\\varepsilon$. The charges are then moved to air so that they have the same force between them as that in the medium.\n\nWhat is the distance between the charges in air, knowing that $\\varepsilon=\\varepsilon_r\\varepsilon_0$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\varepsilon_rr$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{r}{\\varepsilon_r}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{r}{\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_r}}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $r\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_r}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe electric force in the medium\n\n- $F=\\dfrac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon} \\dfrac{q_1 q_2}{r^2}$ \n\nThe electric force in air\n\n- $F=\\dfrac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0} \\dfrac{q_1 q_2}{r_2\\textrm{}^2}$ \n\nSince the two forces are equal\n\n- $\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon} \\frac{q_1 q_2}{r^2} &= \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0} \\frac{q_1 q_2}{r_2\\textrm{}^2}\\\\\n\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\ \\frac{1}{\\varepsilon_r \\varepsilon_0 r^2} &= \\frac{1}{\\varepsilon_0 r_2\\textrm{}^2}\\\\\n\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\ \\frac{1}{\\varepsilon_r r^2} &= \\frac{1}{r_2\\textrm{}^2}\\\\ \\hspace{1em}\\\\r_2&=r\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_r}\n\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n\\end{aligned}$"},{"ref":"PH0420","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0420\n\nA point charge is moved between two parallel plates separated by a distance of 5 cm. The potential difference between the points is 50 V. \n\nWhat is the average electric field $E$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1000 $\\ce{N\\,C^{-1}}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 250 $\\ce{N\\,C^{-1}}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 10 $\\ce{N\\,C^{-1}}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2.5 $\\ce{N\\,C^{-1}}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe potential difference V,\n\n- $V=\\dfrac{W}{q}$\n\nThe work done $W$,\n\n- $W=Fs$\n\nAnd\n\n- $F=Eq$\n\nSubstituting in the first equation,\n\n- $F=Eq$\n\n- $V=\\dfrac{Eqs}{q}=Es \\hspace{1em}$\n\n- $E=\\dfrac{V}{s}=\\dfrac{50}{5 \\times 10^{-2}}=1000\\, \\ce{NC^{-1}}$"},{"ref":"PH0594","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0594\n\nA positive and a negative charge of equal magnitude are located on the same horizontal line.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0594_q.png\" alt=\"PH0594_q\">\n\nWhat is the direction of the net electric field at the point marked X, equidistant from the two charges?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ To the right\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    To the left\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    Up\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    Down","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe electric field at X due to -Q points toward -Q. The field at X due to +Q points away from +Q, with the same magnitude at X as the field from -Q. Adding the electric field vectors gives a vector pointing to the left.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0594_ms.png\" alt=\"PH0594_ms\">"},{"ref":"PH0596","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0596\n\nA beam of electrons enters a uniform electric field, as shown.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0596a.png\" alt=\"PH0596a\">\n\nWhat effect does this field have on the magnitudes of the horizontal and vertical components of the electrons\\' velocity?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Horizontal velocity | Vertical velocity |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| Increases | Stays the same |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Decreases | Stays the same |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Stays the same | Increase |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Stays the same | Decrease |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nBecause the electric field is only in the vertical direction, it does not affect the electrons\\' horizontal speed.\n\nAs described by $E=\\dfrac{F}{q}$ in the data booklet, the electrons do experience a vertical force which causes them to accelerate vertically.\n\nTherefore when they enter the electric field, the horizontal speed of the electrons remains the same while their vertical speed increases."},{"ref":"PH0597","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0597\n\nTwo charges, separated by a large distance, are shown below, along with the electric field lines around each charge.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0597a.png\" alt=\"PH0597a\">\n\nWhich of the following statements is correct?\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$ The charge $q$ is positive.\n- II.    $\\hspace{.7em}$ The charge $q$ is equal in magnitude to $+Q$.\n- III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ The electric field magnitude near the charge $q$ is greater than the magnitude of the field near the charge $+Q$.\n\n- A.    $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B.    $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III\n\n- C.    $\\hspace{1em}$ III only\n\n- D.    $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nBecause the electric field lines point toward the charge $q$, this charge must be negative, meaning that statement I is false.\n\nThe relatively greater number of field lines around charge $q$ means that this charge magnitude is greater than the charge +Q, indicating that statement II is also untrue.\n\nElectric field line density is proportional to electric field strength. Because the field line density near $q$ is greater than that near $+Q$, statement III is correct.\n\nTherefore the correct answer is option C."},{"ref":"PH0598","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0598\n\nIn the following diagram, the size of the charges is small compared to the distance *R*.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0598.png\" alt=\"PH0598\">\n\nWhat is the direction of the force on the charge in the center?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Up\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ To the right\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ To the left\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The charge does not move","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nKnowing that $F=k\\dfrac{q_1q_2}{r^2}$, we analyze the effect of pairs of surrounding charges on the center charge.\n\nIn the vertical direction, $\\dfrac{(Q)(Q)}{R^2}=\\dfrac{(4Q)(Q)}{(2R)^2}$, so there is no net force on the center charge.\n\nIn the horizontal direction, $\\dfrac{(3Q)(Q)}{(3R)^2}<\\dfrac{(2Q)(Q)}{(2R)^2}$, so there is a net force on the center charge, and it points to the left."},{"ref":"PH0640","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0640\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nAn atom is being modeled with an electron assumed to be orbiting the nucleus in the first energy level with a speed of $2.18 \\times 10^6$ m s$^{-1}$.\n\nThe orbital radius of the ground state, in m, is given by\n\n- $r=(0.53\\times{10}^{-10})n^2$\n\nWhere\n\n- $n$ = order of the energy level.\n\n1. Determine the charge of the nucleus. [3 marks]\n\n2.\n    1. The electron moves to the second energy level where it orbits with a kinetic energy of $5.44 \\times 10^{-19}$ J.\n\n        Explain whether the centripetal acceleration increases, decreases or stays the same. [3 marks]\n\n    2. Calculate the orbital period of the electron in the second energy level. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. The centripetal force is the electrostatic force between the electron and the nucleus:\n\n    $\\hspace{2em}$ $k\\dfrac{eq}{r^2}=\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\hspace{2em}$ $q = \\ \\ll\\dfrac{mv^2r}{ek}\\gg =\\dfrac{\\left(9.11\\times{10}^{-31}\\right)\\left(2.18\\times{10}^6\\right)^2\\left(0.53\\times{10}^{-10}\\right)}{\\left(1.6\\times{10}^{-19}\\right)\\left(8.99\\times{10}^9\\right)}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\hspace{2em}$ $q$  = :answer[$1.6\\times{10}^{-19}\\ \\ll\\ \\ce{C}\\gg$] $\\checkmark$\n\n2.\n    1. The kinetic energy in level 2 (5.44 × 10⁻¹⁹ J) is less than in level 1 $\\ll$so speed decreases$\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\\\n    The orbital radius increases $\\ll$ as $r \\propto n^2$ $\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\\\n    Centripetal acceleration $a = \\dfrac{v^2}{r}$ decreases $\\ll$since $v$ decreases and $r$ increases$\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        (some justification using equations must be shown to receive full marks)\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The kinetic energy in the first energy level is\n\n        - $E_K = \\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2 = \\dfrac{1}{2}(9.11 \\times 10^{-31})(2.18 \\times 10^6)^2 = 2.17 \\times 10^{-18}$ J\n\n        Since $5.44 \\times 10^{-19}$ J $< 2.17 \\times 10^{-18}$ J, the kinetic energy — and therefore the orbital speed — is smaller in the second energy level.\n\n        The orbital radius is given by $r = (0.53 \\times 10^{-10})n^2$, so the radius increases from level 1 to level 2.\n\n        The centripetal acceleration is\n\n        - $a = \\dfrac{v^2}{r}$\n\n        Since $v$ has decreased and $r$ has increased, the centripetal acceleration decreases.\n\n    2.  ::indent\n        - $r= \\ \\ll\\left(0.53\\times{10}^{-10}\\right)\\left(2\\right)^2\\gg \\ =2.12\\times{10}^{-10}\\ll$ m $\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $v=\\ll\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2E_K}{m}}\\gg \\ = \\ \\ll\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\left(2\\right)\\left(5.44\\times{10}^{-19}\\right)}{\\left(9.11\\times{10}^{-31}\\right)}}\\gg \\ = \\ 1.09\\times{10}^6\\ \\ll$ m s$^{-1}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $T=\\ll\\dfrac{2\\pi r}{v}\\gg \\ = \\ \\ll\\dfrac{\\left(2\\pi\\right)\\left(2.12\\times{10}^{-10}\\right)}{1.09\\times{10}^6}\\gg $ =:answer[$1.22\\times{10}^{-15}\\ \\ll$ s $\\gg$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        For the second energy level:\n\n        - $r=\\left(0.53\\times{10}^{-10}\\right)n^2$\n\n        - $r=\\left(0.53\\times{10}^{-10}\\right)\\left(2\\right)^2=2.12\\times{10}^{-10}$ m\n\n        And\n\n        - $\\begin{aligned}\n        E_K&=\\frac{1}{2}mv^2\\\\\n        v&=\\sqrt{\\frac{2E_K}{m}}\\\\\n        &=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\left(2\\right)\\left(5.44\\times{10}^{-19}\\right)}{\\left(9.11\\times{10}^{-31}\\right)}}\\\\\n        &=1.09\\times{10}^6\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}\n        \\end{aligned}$\n\n        To find the orbital period,\n\n        - $v=\\dfrac{2\\pi r}{T}$\n\n        - $T=\\dfrac{2\\pi r}{v}=\\dfrac{\\left(2\\pi\\right)\\left(2.12\\times{10}^{-10}\\right)}{1.09\\times{10}^6}=1.22\\times{10}^{-15}$ s"},{"ref":"PH0673","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0673\n\nTwo point charges are separated by a distance $r$ as shown. The negative charge has a mass $m_1$ and the mass of the positive charge is $m_2$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0673.png\" alt=\"PH0673\">\n\nThe negative charge's acceleration is 3 times that of the positive charge.\n\nWhat is the ratio $\\dfrac{{\\textrm{Gravitational\\ force}}}{{\\textrm{Electrostatic\\ force}}}$ between the two point charges?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{G(m_1)^2}{30ke^2}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{Gm_em_p}{ke^2}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{G{(m_2)}^2}{10ke^2}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{G{(m_1)}^2}{10ke^2}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\\\nBy Newton's 3rd Law, the charges have equal and opposite forces on each other.\n\nThen\\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $m_1a_1=m_2a_2$\n\nSince\\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $a_1=3a_2$\n\nThen\\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $m_2=3m_1$\n\nThe gravitational force between the two charges is given by\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $F_g=G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $F_g=G\\dfrac{m_1(3)m_1}{r^2}=G\\dfrac{{{3m}_1}^2}{r^2}$\n\nThe electrostatic force between the two charges is given by\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $F_e=k\\dfrac{q_1q_2}{r^2}$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $F_e=k\\dfrac{\\left(3\\right)e\\left(10\\right)e}{r^2}=k\\dfrac{30e^2}{r^2}$\n\nThe ratio\n$\\dfrac{Gravitational\\ force}{Electrostatic\\ force}=\\dfrac{F_g}{F_e}\n=\\dfrac{G\\dfrac{{{3m}_1}^2}{r^2}}{k\\dfrac{30e^2}{r^2}}$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{Gravitational\\ force}{Electrostatic\\ force}=\\dfrac{G{m_1}^2}{10ke^2}$"},{"ref":"PH0880","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0880\n\nThree charges $-Q$, $-Q$ and $+Q$ form an equilateral triangle. Point **X** lies at the center of the triangle. The distance between **X** and the center of each of the charges is $d$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0880.png\" alt=\"PH0880\">\n\nWhat is the magnitude of the electric field strength at **X**?\n\nNote that $\\cos (60\\degree) = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $\\sin (60\\degree) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{kQ}{d^2}$\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{2kQ}{d^2}$\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{9kQ}{4d^2}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\\\nThe magnitude of the electric field due to each charge is given by $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{kQ}{d^2}$\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0880_MS.png\" alt=\"PH0880_MS\">\n\nThe horizontal component of electric fields of two negative charges are equal and opposite so cancel out.\n\nThe vertical component of the electric field due to one of the negative charges is given by\n\n- $\\dfrac{kQ}{d^2}\\cos 60 = \\dfrac{kQ}{2d^2}$\n\n(Angles in equilateral triangle of\n$60\\degree$ $\\text{\\textendash}$ see diagram above for illustration)\n\nTotal electric field points vertically downwards on diagram and magnitude is\n\n- $2 \\left( \\dfrac{kQ}{2d^2} \\right) + \\dfrac{kQ}{d^2} = \\dfrac{2kQ}{d^2}$"},{"ref":"PH0908","marks":"10","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0908\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\n1. An initially neutral rod and cloth are rubbed together. Explain why the cloth becomes negatively charged. [2 marks]\n\n2. 1. The rod retains its charge and is now placed near an initially neutral metal sphere that is grounded (Earthed) by a wire as shown in the diagram below.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n        The sphere and the rod do not make contact. Explain why an electric current flows in the wire connecting the sphere and the ground for a short time. [2 marks]\n\n    2. State the direction of the conventional current in the wire. [1 mark]\n\n    3. It is given that the current has an average magnitude of $0.80\\,\\mu$ A and flows for a total time of $1.4\\,\\mu $s. Show that the total charge passing a point on the wire in this time is around $1.1$ pC. [1 mark]\n\n    4. The ground connection is then removed, and the sphere remains charged. State the name of the phenomenon by which the sphere gained its electric charge. [1 mark]\n\n3. The diameter of the sphere is 6.0 cm. Calculate the electric field strength at a point 40 cm away from the surface of the sphere.\n\n     [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Friction transfers electrons.   $\\checkmark$\n\n    From the rod to the cloth.  $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    The objects initially are neutral because they each have the same number of electrons and protons. The chemical properties of the molecules in the cloth are such that it attracts electrons more than the rod. As the objects are rubbed together some electrons are transferred from the rod to the cloth.\n\n    Note: A very small number of electrons is transferred compared to the total number of electrons in each of the objects. The electric force is very strong, so even a small imbalance is detectable.\n\n2. 1. The (positively) charged rod causes negative/opposite charge to build up on sphere near to the rod.\n\n        OR\n\n        Opposite charges attract. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\text{Electrons}}$ flow between the ground and the sphere / from the ground to the sphere. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        The rod is initially positively charged. (In part (a)(i) the cloth is negatively charged so rod must be positively charged due to conservation of charge).\n\n        A negative charge will build up on the sphere near to the rod because opposite charges attract. This negative charge is comprised of electrons that flow from the ground to the sphere.\n\n    2. From sphere to the ground/downwards $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        The rod is initially positively charged. (In part (a)(i) the cloth is negatively charged so rod must be positively charged due to conservation of charge.)\n\n        The sphere becomes negatively charged. Electrons must flow from the ground to the sphere. Conventional current is the rate of flow of positive charge so current flows from the sphere to the ground.\n\n    3. $\\hspace{0.5em}Q=It$\n\n        - $=(0.80\\times 10^{-6})(1.40\\times 10^{-6})$\n\n        - $=1.12\\times 10^{-12} \\approx 1.1$ pC $\\checkmark$\n\n        Must see formula, substitution  and extra significant figure for mark.\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n        - $Q=It=(0.80\\times 10^{-6})(1.40\\times 10^{-6})=1.12\\times 10^{-12} \\approx 1.1$ pC\n\n        $\\mu$ (micro) has a value of $10^{-6}$ and $p$ (pico) has a value of $10^{-12}$ - Remember these are given in your formula book.\n\n    4. (Charging by electrostatic) induction. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        Electrostatic induction is the process by which a neutral object is charged without touching another charged object.\n\n3. $E=\\dfrac{kq}{r^2}$\n\n    $E=\\dfrac{(8.99\\times 10^9)(1.1\\times 10^{-12})}{(0.43)^2}$\n\n    $E=0.053\\,\\ce{N\\,C^{-1}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    (Note: Allow use of $q=1.12$ pC leading to answer of $0.054 \\, \\ce{N\\,C^{-1}}$)\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n    - $F=k\\dfrac{q_1 q_2}{r^2}$ - $F$ is the force between two charges $q_1$ and $q_2$\n\n    - $E=\\dfrac{F}{q}$  - $E$ is the force per unit charge at a point\n\n    We can let $q_1$ be an arbitrary unit charge at a point near a fixed charge $q_2$:\n\n    - $E=\\dfrac{F}{q_1}$\n\n    Combining these equations gives:\n\n    - $Eq_1=k\\dfrac{q_1 q_2}{r^2}$\n\n    - $E=\\dfrac{kq_2}{r^2}$\n\n    Here our fixed charge is the charge of the sphere - this is equal to the charge passing a point in wire (see part (b)(iii)) $=1.1\\times 10^{-12}$ C.\n\n    $r$ is the distance between the point and the center of the sphere $=$ distance between point and surface of sphere $+$ radius of sphere $= 40\\,\\ce{cm}+\\dfrac{6}{2}\\,\\ce{cm}=43\\, \\ce{cm}=0.43$ m\n\n    Substituting these values into the equations (Coulomb constant $k$ is given in your formula book) gives:\n\n    - $E=\\dfrac{(8.99\\times 10^9)(1.1\\times 10^{-12})}{(0.43)^2} = 0.053\\, \\ce{N\\,C^{-1}}$"},{"ref":"PH0909","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0909\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\n1. 1. In an experiment a thin mist of oil of density ${\\rho}_{oil}$ is created and allowed to fall into a region between two charged parallel plates separated by a distance $d$. The oil drops are negatively charged when they enter the region between the plates.\n\n        The plates are connected to an external power supply so that the potential difference between them can be varied as shown in the diagram below.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n        In the experiment, it is observed that some of the oil drops move up and some move down between the plates. Explain these observations. [3 marks]\n\n    2. State the direction of the electric field between the plates. [1 mark]\n\n2. 1. One particular drop between the plates is identified, and the potential difference adjusted to a value $V$  such that this drop remains stationary between the plates.\n\n        By considering the two largest forces acting on the drop, show that the charge of the drop $q$ is given by\n\n        $q=\\dfrac{4\\pi r^3 gd{\\rho}_{oil}}{3V}$\n\n        Where $r$ is the radius of the drop and it is assumed to be spherical. [3 marks]\n\n    2. Outline why it is difficult to measure the radius of the drop directly. [1 mark]\n\n    The radius of the drop can be determined by measuring the terminal velocity of the drop in the absence of the electric field.\n\n    When the stationary oil drop is the only remaining drop suspended between the plates, the potential difference is set to zero. The drop falls down and quickly reaches terminal velocity.\n\n    3. By considering only the two largest forces acting on the drop, show that its radius is given by\n\n        $r=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{9\\eta v}{2g{\\rho}_{oil}}}$\n\n        where $\\eta$ is the viscosity of air and $v$ is the terminal velocity of the drop. [3 marks]\n\n    4. The following data are available for the oil drop:\n\n        - $v=1.3\\times 10^{-4}\\, \\ce{m\\,s^{-1}}$\n\n        - $\\eta = 1.60\\times 10^{-5}\\, \\ce{Pa\\,s}$\n\n        - ${\\rho}_{oil}=920\\, \\ce{kg\\,m^{-3}}$\n\n        - $d = 1.4\\times 10^{-2}\\, \\ce{m}$\n\n        - $V=700\\, V$\n\n        Determine the charge on the oil drop. [2 marks]\n\n3. The experiment is then repeated 7 times. The charge of each drop is calculated and recorded in the table below.\n\n    | | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Charge / $\\times 10^{-19}\\, \\ce{C}$ $\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n    | :-: | :-: |\n    | $\\hspace{1.5em}$1$\\hspace{1.5em}$| 6.4 |\n    | $\\hspace{1.5em}$2$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 9.6 |\n    | $\\hspace{1.5em}$3$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 4.8 |\n    | $\\hspace{1.5em}$4$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 8.0 |\n    | $\\hspace{1.5em}$5$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 12.8 |\n    | $\\hspace{1.5em}$6$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 6.4 |\n    | $\\hspace{1.5em}$7$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 4.8 |\n\n    Outline how the data in the table supports the hypothesis that charge is quantized. [1 mark]\n\n4. It is suggested that the value of $q$ can be determined with greater accuracy using the following equation:\n\n    $q=\\dfrac{4\\pi r^3 gd({\\rho}_{oil}-{\\rho}_{air})}{3V}$\n\n    This equation accounts for a force that the equation in part (b)(i) assumes to be negligible. State the name of this force. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. 1. Drops have different mass and charge / mass to charge ratio.  $\\checkmark$\n\n        Drops experience weight force downwards and electric force (+ buoyancy) upwards.  $\\checkmark$\n\n        For drops moving up electric force (+ buoyancy) > weight\n\n        OR\n\n        For drops moving down weight < electric force (+ buoyancy)  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        Weight acts downwards. From the fact that some drops are moving up, we can deduce that the electric force is directed upwards (buoyancy is much smaller than weight).\n\n        Drops will initially be moving downwards (as they have fallen from some height). So, their free body diagram is as follows (treating buoyancy as negligible)\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n        If the electric force = weight, then initially there is a net force upwards (drag) causing the object to decelerate. Drag $\\propto$ speed, so drop will decelerate until speed = zero (object is stationary and net force = zero)\n\n        If weight > electric force, then the drop will reach an equilibrium where drag + electric force = gravitational force. The drop will be moving downwards at a constant velocity.\n\n        If weight < electric force, then the drop will reach an equilibrium where electric force = drag + weight. The drop will be moving upwards at a constant velocity.\n\n    2. Downwards / towards bottom plate OWTTE.  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        Electric force acting on the oil drops is directed upwards (we know this because some of the oil drops moved upwards). Oil drops are negatively charged (stated in part (a)) and electric field is directed the way a positive charge would move. So field is directed downwards.\n\n2. 1. $Eq=mg$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $m=\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^3 {\\rho}_{oil}$ and $E=\\dfrac{V}{d}$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        Correct substitution and rearranging clearly leading to  $q=\\dfrac{4\\pi r^3 gd{\\rho}_{oil}}{3V}$    $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        For the stationary drop Electric force = gravitational force - drop must be stationary (not moving at a constant speed) as moving drops experience drag.\n\n        - $mg=Eq$\n\n        - $E=\\dfrac{V}{d}$ and $m={\\rho}_{oil}$(volume of oil drop) $= {\\rho}_{oil} \\Bigl(\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^3 \\Bigr)$\n\n        (Oil drops are spherical so volume $=\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^3$  )\n\n        - ${\\rho}_{oil} \\Bigl(\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^3 \\Bigr)=\\dfrac{Vq}{d}$\n\n        - $q=\\dfrac{4\\pi r^3 gd{\\rho}_{oil}}{3V}$\n\n    2. The drop is very small / drop must be looked at through a microscope.  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        Drop is too small for radius to be measured using a ruler. Micrometer cannot be used as drop will lose its shape upon contact (it may also be too small to be identified with micrometer).\n\n    3. Force identified as drag / $F_d=6\\pi \\eta rv$ seen  $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $mg=6\\pi \\eta rv$   $\\checkmark$\n\n        correct substitution (of $m=\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^3 {\\rho}_{oil})$ and rearranging clearly leading to $r=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{9ηv}{2g{\\rho}_{oil}}}$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        At terminal velocity, weight = drag (buoyancy is also present but we are only considering two largest forces).\n\n        - $mg=6\\pi \\eta rv$\n\n        Where $m$ is the mass of the oil drop, $g$ is the acceleration of freefall on Earth,  $\\eta$ is the viscosity of air, $r$ is the radius of the oil drop and $v$ is the terminal velocity of the oil drop.\n\n        - $m={\\rho}_{oil}\\times$ Volume of oil\n\n        Volume of oil drop $=\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3$ (volume of sphere and we are assuming the drop is spherical)\n\n        Combining the two above equations gives\n\n        - $m=\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi {\\rho}_{oil} r^3$\n\n        Substituting this $m$ into $mg=6\\pi \\eta rv$ gives\n\n        - $\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi {\\rho}_{oil} r^3 g=6\\pi \\eta rv$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{4}{3}{\\rho}_{oil} r^2 g=6\\eta v$\n\n        - $r^2=\\dfrac{\\frac{3}{4} 6\\eta v}{{\\rho}_{oil} g}=\\dfrac{9\\eta v}{2{\\rho}_{oil} g}$\n\n        - $r=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{9\\eta v}{2g{\\rho}_{oil}}}$\n\n    4. Calculation of $r=1.0(2)\\times 10^{-6}\\, \\ce{m}$\n\n        OR\n\n        Correct expression for $q$ in terms of variables given: $q=\\dfrac{4\\pi (\\frac{9\\eta v}{2g{\\rho}_{oil}})^{\\frac{3}{2}} gd{\\rho}_{oil}}{3V}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $q=8.0\\times 10^{-19}\\, \\ce{C}$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        Exact value of $r$ and use of $r$ rounded to 3sf gives an answer of $8.0\\times 10^{-19}\\, \\ce{C} $ 2sf. Award 1 mark for answer of $7.6\\times 10^{-19}\\,\\ce{C}$ (use of $r$ rounded to 2sf).\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n        - $r=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{9\\eta v}{2g{\\rho}_{oil}}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{(9)(1.6\\times 10^{-5})(1.3\\times 10^{-4})}{(2)(9.8)(920)}}=1.018…\\times 10^{-6}$\n\n        It is important that we use exact value of $r$ (or $r$ correct to at least 3sf) as $q \\propto r^3$ so the percentage error in $q$ will be around three times the percentage error in $r$.\n\n        - $q=\\dfrac{4\\pi r^3 gd{\\rho}_{oil}}{3V}=\\dfrac{4\\pi(1.018…\\times 10^{-6)})^3 (9.8)(1.4\\times 10^{-2})(920)}{(3)(700)}=8.0\\times 10^{-19}$\n\n        Note: $8.0\\times 10^{-19}=5e$\n\n3. All multiples of $1.6\\times 10^{-19}$ / All have a common factor (of $e$) OWTTE  $\\checkmark$\n\n    Allow values go up in increments/steps of e/$1.6\\times 10^{-19}$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    The term charge is quantized means that charge comes in discrete packets. This means that the charge of any object is an integer multiple of the elementary charge.\n\n    Millikan's experiment showed that charge of oil drops is always a multiple of $1.6\\times 10^{-19}$. From this it was deduced that the elementary charge (also the charge of the electron) $=1.6\\times 10^{-19}$.\n\n4. Buoyancy/Upthrust  $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    The oil drop is submerged in a fluid (air). A buoyancy force acts on the oil drop as there is a greater pressure at the bottom (as the weight of fluid above is greater) causing an upward force.\n\n    Buoyancy force $F_B=\\rho Vg$\n\n    Where $\\rho$ is the density of the fluid, $V$ is the volume of the object submerged in the fluid and $g$ is the acceleration of freefall on Earth.\n\n    - $F_B={\\rho}_{air} V_{drop} g=\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3 {\\rho}_{air} g$\n\n    For the stationary oil drop in equilibrium Electric force + Buoyancy = Weight\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n    - $Eq+\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3 {\\rho}_{air} g=mg$\n\n    - $\\dfrac{V}{d} q+\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3 {\\rho}_{air} g=\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3 {\\rho}_{oil} g$\n\n    - $\\dfrac{V}{d} q=\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3 {\\rho}_{oil} g-\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3 {\\rho}_{air} g$\n\n    - $\\dfrac{V}{d} q=\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3 g({\\rho}_{oil}-{\\rho}_{air})$\n\n    - $q=\\dfrac{4πr^3 gd({\\rho}_{oil}-{\\rho}_{air})}{3V}$"},{"ref":"PH0921","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0921\n\nThe diagram below shows a charged oil drop traveling upwards at a constant velocity between two parallel plates. There is a potential difference between the plates causing an electric force to act on the oil drop.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0921.png\" alt=\"PH0921\">\n\nWhich of the following free body diagrams correctly shows the forces acting on the drop? It is assumed that buoyant force is negligible.\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    | $\\hspace{2cm}$  |B.   ||\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0921a.png\" alt=\"PH0921a\"> |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0921b.png\" alt=\"PH0921b\">|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0921c.png\" alt=\"PH0921c\">   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0921d.png\" alt=\"PH0921d\">|","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nDrag always acts in the opposite direction to motion. Drag force must point down (as drop is moving up). So we can disregard A and C.\n\nThe object is traveling at a constant velocity so net force = 0 (Newton's first law) therefore the magnitude of the upward and downward forces are the same (magnitude of force proportional to size of arrow in free body diagram) therefore the answer is D."},{"ref":"PH0922","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0922\n\nWhich of the following was the subject of an experiment conducted by Robert Millikan?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ The wave-like behavior of particles.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ The magnitude of the elementary charge.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ The existence of the nucleus.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ The particle-like behavior of photons.","markScheme":"**答案：B**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nIn 1909, Robert Millikan and his PhD student Harvey Fletcher conducted the “oil drop” experiment to determine the elementary charge (they also determined Avogadro's constant in the same experiment)."},{"ref":"PH0923","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0923\n\nA negatively charged rod and neutral sphere are made from the same conducting material.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\nThe rod and the sphere come into contact and are separated. Which of the following correctly describes the charge on the rod and the sphere after this encounter?\n\nAssume that no charge is transferred to the surroundings at any point.\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Rod | Sphere |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| Neutral | Negatively charged |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Negatively charged$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Neutral |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Negatively charged | Negatively charged |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Positively charged | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Negatively charged$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nCharge is transferred from the rod to the sphere by contact. Negative charges (electrons) will be distributed between the rod and sphere until their potentials are equal.\n\nGiven that there was an excess of negative charge to begin with – there will be the same excess of negative charge distributed between the rod and sphere a short time after they come into contact (electrons will travel quickly inside conducting material).\n\nRod and sphere will retain this negative charge when separated so will both be negatively charged in the end."},{"ref":"PH0925","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0925\n\nA spherical oil drop of diameter $d$, density $\\rho$, and charge $q$ is suspended between two horizontal parallel plates separated by a distance $x$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\nThe drop is stationary. Which of the following is a correct expression for the potential difference between the plates?\n\nAssume buoyant force is negligible.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{3q}{4\\pi \\rho gxd^3}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{6q}{\\pi \\rho gxd^3}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6q}\\rho gxd^3$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3q}\\rho gxd^3$","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFor the drop to be stationary, it must be in equilibrium. The two forces acting on the drop are weight $=mg$ (downwards) and electric force $=Eq$ (upwards). The free body diagram is as follows:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\nEquating weight and electric force:\n\n- $mg=Eq$\n\n- (Volume)$\\rho g=\\dfrac{V}{x} q$\n\nAssuming drop is spherical:\n\n- $\\Bigl(\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi \\Bigl(\\dfrac{d}{2}\\Bigr)^3 \\Bigr) \\rho g=\\dfrac{V}{x} q $\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\rho gd^3=\\dfrac{V}{x} q$\n\n- $V=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6q} \\rho gxd^3$"},{"ref":"PH0926","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0926\n\nThe diagrams below shows two drops of the same type of oil.\n\nOil drop $A$ is held stationary between two parallel plates a distance $d$ apart with a potential difference $V$ between them.\n\nOil drop $B$ is held stationary between two parallel plates a distance $3d$ apart with a potential difference $2V$ between them.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\nThe charge per unit mass of oil drop A is $\\alpha$. What is the charge per unit mass of oil drop B?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{1}{6}\\alpha$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{2}{3}\\alpha$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{3}{2}\\alpha$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $6\\alpha$","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFor the drop to be stationary, it must be in equilibrium. The two forces acting on the drop are weight $=mg$ (downwards) and electric force $=Eq$ (upwards). The free body diagram is as follows:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/FBDoildrop.png\" alt=\"FBDoildrop\">\n\nEquating weight and electric force:\n\n- $mg=Eq$\n\nFor drop $A$:\n\n- $m_A g = \\dfrac{V}{d}q_A$\n\n- $\\dfrac{dg}{V}=\\dfrac{q_A}{m_A}=\\alpha\\hspace{1em}$ (as $\\alpha$ is charge per unit mass)\n\nFor drop $B$:\n\n- $m_B g=\\dfrac{2V}{3d}q_B$\n\n- $\\dfrac{3dg}{2V}=\\dfrac{q_B}{m_B} =\\dfrac{3}{2} \\alpha$"},{"ref":"PH0927","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0927\n\nIn an experiment, oil drops are passed through a thin tube of air. As they move, the oil droplets become negatively charged.\n\nWhich of the following rows correctly identifies the name of the process and the direction of electron transfer?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Name of process$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Direction of electron transfer$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| Charging by friction |Air molecules to oil drop |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Charging by friction | Oil drop to air molecules  |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Charging by electrostatic induction$\\hspace{1.5em}$  | Air molecules to oil drop |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Charging by electrostatic induction  | Oil drop to air molecules  |","markScheme":"**答案：A**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nElectrons are transferred to the oil drops (we know this as the question states that the oil drops become negatively charged) as they move past air molecules. This is an example of transfer of charge via friction."},{"ref":"PH0928","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0928\n\nIn an experiment negatively charged spherical oil drops are allowed to fall into a region between two charged parallel plates. The plates are connected to an external power supply as shown in the diagram below:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\nEach drop has a different charge and size. For a given potential difference it is observed that some of the drops are suspended stationary between the plates.\n\nWhich graph correctly shows the relationship between the charge $q$ and the radius $r$ of these stationary drops?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n|| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">  |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">|\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">|","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFor the stationary oil drop in equilibrium - Electric force $=$ Weight\n\n- $Eq=mg $\n\n$E$ and $g$ are constant but $m$ will depend on $r$.\n\nWe can write $m$ in terms of $r$ as follows:\n\n- $m=\\rho V=\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^3 \\rho$\n\nWhere $\\rho$ is the density of the oil drop (or more correctly (density of oil drop - density of air) due to buoyancy)\n\nSubstituting this $m$ into our initial equation gives:\n\n- $Eq=\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^3 \\rho g$\n\nSo $q \\propto r^3$ the correct answer is D.\n\nAlternatively:\n\nDrops that are stationary all have the same mass/ charge ratio.\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\text{Mass}}{\\text{charge}} =$ constant\n\nAnd mass $\\propto$ volume $\\propto r^3$\n\nSo $\\dfrac{q}{r^3} =$ constant\n\n- $q \\propto r^3$"},{"ref":"PH0965","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0965\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\n1. In a cathode ray tube (CRT) TV, an electron is accelerated from rest to a high speed by a potential difference of $3.4\\times 10^3$ V. The electron then enters a region of uniform electric field horizontally, where it is deflected. The horizontal displacement of the electron in the region of the field is $5.0\\times10^{-3}$ m, and the magnitude of the electric field $E$ is $7.5\\times10^4\\,\\ce{V\\,m^{-1}}$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0965aaaa.png\" alt=\"PH0965aaaa\">\n\n    1. Define net force in terms of Newton's Second Law of Motion. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Show that the speed of the electron just before reaching the region of the electric field is around $3.5\\times10^7  \\, \\ce{m\\,s^{-1}}$. [2 marks]\n\n    3.     The electron takes 12 ns to reach the region of the electric field. Determine the average force applied to the electron during this time.  [2 marks]\n\n2. The electron enters an electric field region and is deflected upward.\n\n    1. Show that the acceleration of the electron in the region of the electric field is around $1.3\\times10^{16} \\, \\ce{m\\,s^{-2}}$. [2 marks]\n\n    2.     Determine the angle of deflection of the electron as it leaves the region. [3 marks]\n\n    3. Instead of electrons, protons are accelerated to the same speed before entering the electric field region. Deduce how the deflection path of the protons differs from that of the electrons. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. The rate of change in momentum/the product of an object's acceleration and its mass $\\checkmark$\n\n        (If the equation is given, then the symbols must be defined)\n\n    2. Kinetic energy gained by electron $=(1.6\\times 10^{-19})(3.4\\times 10^3)=5.4\\times 10^{-16}$ J\n\n        $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2E}{m}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2(5.4\\times 10^{-16})}{9.11\\times 10^{-31}}}=$ :answer[$3.46\\times 10^7 \\, \\ce{m\\,s^{-1}}$]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        To kinetic energy $E$ gained by the electron can be expressed as\n\n        - $E=qV$\n\n        Where $q$ is the charge of the electron and $V$ is the potential difference in which the electron is accelerated.\n\n        - $E=(1.6\\times 10^{-19})(3.4\\times 10^3)=5.4\\times 10^{-16}\\, \\ce{J}$\n\n        By using the equation of kinetic energy\n\n        - $E=\\dfrac{mv^2}{2}$\n\n        - $v^2=\\dfrac{2E}{m}$\n\n        - $v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2E}{m}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2(5.4\\times 10^{-16})}{9.11\\times 10^{-31}}}=3.46\\times 10^7 \\,\\ce{m\\,s^{-1}}$\n\n    3. $\\dfrac{mv}{t}=\\dfrac{(9.11\\times 10^{-31})(3.5\\times 10^7)}{12\\times 10^{-9}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        :answer[$2.6\\times 10^{-15}\\, \\ce{N}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        The force $F$\n\n        - $F=\\dfrac{\\Delta p}{t}$\n\n        Where the change in momentum $\\Delta p$ is the final momentum of the electron\n\n        - $F=\\dfrac{m\\Delta v}{t}=\\dfrac{(9.11\\times 10^{-31})(3.5\\times 10^7)}{12\\times 10^{-9}}=2.6\\times 10^{-15}\\, \\ce{N}$\n\n2. 1. $\\dfrac{qE}{m}=\\dfrac{(1.6\\times 10^{-19})(7.5\\times 10^4)}{9.11\\times 10^{-31}}$\n\n        :answer[$1.32\\times 10^{16}\\, \\ce{m\\,s^{-2}}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        The net force $F$ acting on the electron in the region of the electric field:\n\n        - $F=qE=ma$\n\n        - $a=\\dfrac{qE}{m}=\\dfrac{(1.6\\times 10^{-19})(7.5\\times 10^4)}{9.11\\times 10^{-31}}=1.32\\times 10^{16}\\, \\ce{m\\,s^{-2}}$\n\n    2. time of motion between the plates $=\\dfrac{5.0\\times 10^{-3}}{3.5\\times 10^7}=1.4\\times 10^{-10}$ s $\\checkmark$\n\n        vertical velocity as it leaves $=(1.3\\times 10^{16})(1.4\\times 10^{-10})=1.9\\times 10^6\\, \\ce{m\\,s^{-1}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\dfrac{1.9\\times 10^6}{3.5\\times 10^7}=$ :answer[$3.0\\degree$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        The horizontal velocity $v_h$ of the electron is constant during the motion between the plates. The time $t$ electron spent between parallel plates:\n\n        - $t=\\dfrac{s}{v_h}$\n\n        where $s$ is the horizontal displacement of the electron\n\n        - $t=\\dfrac{s}{v_h}=\\dfrac{5.0\\times 10^{-3}}{3.5\\times 10^7}=1.4\\times 10^{-10}$ s\n\n        The vertical velocity $v_v$  of the electron at the instant of leaving the plate:\n\n        - $v_v=u+at=0+(1.3\\times 10^{16})(1.4\\times 10^{-10})=1.9\\times 10^6\\, \\ce{m\\,s^{-1}}$\n\n        The angle of deflection $\\theta$ can be found by\n\n        - $\\tan{\\theta}=\\dfrac{v_v}{v_h}=\\dfrac{1.9\\times 10^6}{3.5\\times 10^7}$\n\n        - $\\theta =3.0\\degree$\n\n    3. The force acting on the protons are opposite so they deflect in the opposite direction/downwards $\\checkmark$\n\n        They are heavier so they deflect less  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        Protons and electrons experience the same magnitude of the force from an electric field but in opposite directions. Therefore, protons are deflected in the opposite direction to electrons. Additionally, protons have greater mass than electrons, so they accelerate slower and have lower vertical velocity as they leave the electric field region. Consequently, the angle of deflection for protons is less than for electrons."},{"ref":"PH0978","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0978\n\nTwo charges are positioned as shown, each a distance $d$ from point $A$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\nWhat is the magnitude and direction of the electric field at point $A$ due to the two charges?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Magnitude$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Direction$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\dfrac{2kQ\\cos(60\\degree)}{d^2}$ |To the left  |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{2kQ\\cos(60\\degree)}{d^2}$ | To the right |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{2kQ\\sin(60\\degree)}{d^2}$ | To the left  |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{2kQ\\sin(60\\degree)}{d^2}$ | To the right |","markScheme":"**答案：B**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe electric field vector points in the direction of force that a positive charge would experience and the magnitude of electric field at a point due to a charge is given by\n\n- $E=\\dfrac{kQ}{r^2}$\n\nWhere $k$ the Coulomb constant, $Q$ is the charge and $r$ is the separation between the charge and the point.\n\nSo, at point $A$ the electric fields from each charge may be represented as follows\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\nThe combination of the two vectors results in zero vertical component - the resultant electric field points to the right.\n\nSo, we consider only the horizontal components of the field vectors for the magnitude. The magnitude of the field is\n\n- $2\\Bigl(\\dfrac{kQ}{d^2}\\cos(60\\degree)\\Bigr)=\\dfrac{2kQ\\cos(60\\degree)}{d^2}$\n\nThis is illustrated below\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\nNotice the triangle is equilateral.\n\n- $\\dfrac{2kQ\\cos(60\\degree)}{d^2}=\\dfrac{kQ}{d^2}$"},{"ref":"PH1461","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1461\n\nOn the graphs below the solid line shows how electric field strength $E$ varies with distance $d$ from the center to a point charge embedded inside a material of permittivity $\\varepsilon$.\n\nA dotted line is also plotted on each of the graphs showing how electric field strength varies with distance for the same charge in a different material. For which of the graphs does the dotted line represent a charge embedded in a material with a permittivity $2\\varepsilon$?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    | $\\hspace{2cm}$  |B.   ||\n| |  <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/Screenshoot%202024-11-28%20at%2012.52.02%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshoot 2024-11-28 at 12.52.02 PM\">|  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH1551q2.JPEG\" alt=\"PH1551q2\">|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH1551q3.JPEG\" alt=\"PH1551q3\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH1551q4.JPEG\" alt=\"PH1551q4\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nElectric field strength at a point is given by\n\n- $E=\\dfrac{Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon r^2}$\n\nWhere $Q$ is the charge, $\\varepsilon$ is permittivity, and $r$ is the distance between the point and the charge.\n\nFor constant $r$ we have that $E\\propto\\dfrac{1}{\\varepsilon}$. So if $\\varepsilon$ is doubled we expect $E$ to be at half the original value for every distance $d$.\n\nThe dotted line is at half the height of the solid line for every $d$ in the graph below:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH1551q1.JPEG\" alt=\"PH1551q1\">"},{"ref":"PH1871","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1871\n\nWhich of the following could not be the charge of a particle?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $3.2\\times10^{-19}$ C\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $4.0\\times10^{-19}$ C\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $3.2\\times10^{-18}$ C\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $4.0\\times10^{-18}$ C","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nCharge is quantised. The charge of a particle must be an integer multiple of the elementary charge:\n\n- $e=1.6\\times10^{-19}$ C\n\nThis means any valid particle charge must be:\n\n- $q=n\\times e$\n\nWhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nChecking each option we have:\n\n- A. $\\hspace {2em}3.2\\times10^{-19}$ C $=2\\times e$\n\n- B. $\\hspace {2em}4.0\\times10^{-19}$ C $=2.5\\times e$\n\n- C. $\\hspace {2em}3.2\\times10^{-18}$ C $=20\\times e$\n\n- D. $\\hspace {2em}4.0\\times10^{-18}$ C $=25\\times e$\n\nTherefore, option B is not a possible charge for a particle."}]},{"name":"Gravitational Fields","questions":[{"ref":"PH0053","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0053\n\nPlanet $X$ has radius $R$ and density $\\rho$. The gravitational field strength at the surface of Planet $X$ is $g$.\n\nWhat is the ratio of the gravitational field strength at the surface of planet Y, which has radius 0.25$R$ and density 3$\\rho$, to that at the surface of Planet X?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{3}{4}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 4\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 16\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 48","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n- $g=G\\dfrac{M}{r^2}$\n\nwhere\n\n- $M = \\rho V$ and $\\ V=\\ \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3$\n\n- $g=G\\bigg(\\dfrac{4\\rho \\pi r^3 }{3r^2}\\bigg)=\\dfrac{4}{3}G\\rho\\pi r$\n\nWhich gives\n\n- $g \\propto \\rho r$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1.5em} \\frac{g_y}{g_x} &=\\ \\frac{3\\rho(0.25r)}{\\rho r} \\\\\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\ng_y&= \\frac{3}{4}g_x\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\\end{aligned}$"},{"ref":"PH0089","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0089\n\nA satellite $X$ is in a circular orbit of radius $R$ with a period $T$ around Earth.\n\nWhat will be the orbital radius of satellite $Y$, travelling in a circular orbit with a period of $8T$ around Earth?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em} 16R$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}    8 R$\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}    4 R$\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}    2 R$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nTwo formulae are needed here.\n\nGravitational field strength: $g=\\dfrac{GM}{r^2}$\n\nCentripetal acceleration: $a=\\dfrac{v^2}{r}=\\dfrac{4\\pi^2r}{T^2}$\n\nRearrangement gives Kepler's third law, which relates orbital period to radius.\n\n- $\\begin{aligned}\ng &= a \\\\\n\\hspace{.1em} \\\\\nT^2 &=\\frac{4\\pi^2}{GM}\\ r^3 \\\\\n\\hspace{.1em} \\\\\n\\left(\\frac{T_Y}{T_X}\\right)^2 &= \\left(\\frac{R_Y}{R_X}\\right)^3 \\\\\n\\hspace{.1em} \\\\\n\\left(8\\right)^2 &=\\left(\\frac{R_Y}{R}\\right)^3 \\\\\n\\hspace{.1em} \\\\\nR_Y &=(\\sqrt[3]{64}) R \\\\\n\\hspace{.1em}\n\\end{aligned}$\n\n    $\\hspace{3.3em}$ = :answer[4 $R$]\n\nHence C is the correct answer option."},{"ref":"PH0232","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0232\n\nThe gravitational field strength on the surface of the Moon is  $\\dfrac{g_E}{6}$. Two objects of different masses $m$ and $2m$ are released with zero speed on the Moon from heights of $h$ and $2h$, respectively, where $h$ is small compared to the radius of the Moon.\n\nWhat are the magnitudes of the accelerations of these objects right after release?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$ **Mass m** $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$**Mass 2m** $\\hspace{1em}$|\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\dfrac{g_E}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{g_E}{12}$ |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\dfrac{g_E}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{g_E}{6}$ |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\dfrac{g_E}{12}$ | $\\dfrac{g_E}{6}$ |\n| $\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\dfrac{g_E}{12}$ | $\\dfrac{g_E}{12}$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe only force acting on the masses is their weight. According to Newton\\'s 2nd law ($F = ma$);\n\n$W = mg$  and $W = m \\left(\\dfrac{g_E}{6}\\right)$\n\n- $m \\left(\\dfrac{g_E}{6}\\right) = ma $\n\n- $a = \\dfrac{g_E}{6}$\n\nThe acceleration of the masses is not dependent on the magnitude of mass and height. It is equal to the acceleration of freefall on the moon.\n\nTherefore the answer is B."},{"ref":"PH0292","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0292\n\nSatellites $A$ and $B$ orbit the same planet of mass $M$ and radius $R$. Satellite $A$ has a mass of $3m$ and it is located at a height of $R$ from the surface of the planet. Satellite $B$ has a mass of $m$ and it is located at a height of 2$R$ from the surface of the planet.\n\nWhat is the ratio of the centripetal force acting on $A$ to the centripetal force acting on $B$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{27}{4}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{4}{27}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 6\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 12","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe centripetal force on a satellite is given by universal law of gravitation formula $ F = \\dfrac{GMm}{r^2}$\n\nThe orbital radius for each is the sum of the radius of the planet and height from the surface, thus $2R$ and $3R$. We can substitute these radii as well as the satellite masses to find the ratio:\n\n$\\begin{aligned} \\\\\n\\frac{Force\\ acting\\ on\\ A}{Force\\ acting\\ on\\ B}\n& =\\frac{\\frac{GM(3m)}{{(2R)}^2}}{\\frac{GMm}{{(3R)}^2}} \\\\\n\\hspace{1em} \\\\\n &=\\frac{\\frac{3}{4}}{\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\\n \\hspace{1em} \\\\\n  \\end{aligned}$\n\n$\\hspace{8em}$ = :answer[$\\dfrac{27}{4}$]"},{"ref":"PH0359","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0359\n\nThe centers of three masses lie on the same line. The centers of the masses are separated as shown.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/PH0359xxx.png\" alt=\"PH0359xxx\">\n\nThe net gravitational force on m$_2$ is zero when the value of $d$ is one third of $x$. What is the ratio $\\dfrac{m_1}{m_3}$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{1}{4}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{2}{3}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{1}{3}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nSince the net gravitational force is zero,\n\nThe gravitational force between $m_1$ and $m_2$ = The gravitational force between $m_3$ and $m_2$.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2cm}\\frac{Gm_1m_2}{d^2} &=\\frac{Gm_2m_3}{\\left(x-d\\right)^2}  \\\\\n\\hspace{1em} \\\\\n\\left(\\frac{m_1}{d^2}\\right)&=\\frac{m_3}{\\left(x-d\\right)^2} \\\\\n\\hspace{1em} \\\\\n\\left(\\frac{m_1}{m_3}\\right)&=\\frac{d^2}{\\left(x-d\\right)^2} \\\\\n\\hspace{1em} \\\\\n&=\\ \\frac{{(\\frac{x}{3})}^2}{{(x-\\frac{x}{3})}^2} \\\\\n\\hspace{1em} \\\\\n&=\\frac{{\\frac{x}{9}}^2}{{\\frac{4x}{9}}^2} \\\\\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}$"},{"ref":"PH0442","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0442\n\nPlanet $A$ has a gravitational field strength $g$ at its surface. Planet $B$ has twice the density and twice the radius of planet $A$.\n\nWhat is the gravitational field strength at the surface of $B$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em} g$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em} 2g$\n- C. $\\hspace{1em} 4g$\n- D. $\\hspace{1em} 8g$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nGravitational field strength is given by $g=\\dfrac{GM}{r^2}$, where $M$ is the mass of the planet and $r$ is the radius.\n\nThe mass of planet $B$ is given by $mass=density \\times  volume$.\n\nThe volume of a sphere is given by $\\dfrac{4\\pi r^3}{3}$, hence proportional to the cube of its radius, giving Planet B 8 times the volume of Planet A.\n\nThis gives $M_B= (2\\rho)(8V_A) = 16\\rho V_A$.\n\nIf the mass of planet A is given by $\\rho V_A$,\n\n- $M_B= 16M_A$\n\nSubstituting values into the formula,\n\n- $\\begin{aligned}\ng_B &= \\frac{GM_B}{r_B^2} \\\\\n\\hspace{1em} \\\\\n&=\\frac{G(16M_A)}{{(2r_A)}^2}\\\\\n\\hspace{1em} \\\\\n&=\\frac{G(16M_A)}{{4r_A}^2} \\\\\n\\hspace{1em} \\\\\n&=4\\frac{GM_A}{{r_A}^2} \\\\\n\\hspace{1em} \\\\\n&=4g\\\\\n\\end{aligned}$"},{"ref":"PH0443","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0443\n\nA satellite is in a circular orbit of radius $R$ around a planet of mass $M$. Which of the following graphs represents the relationship between the centripetal acceleration of the satellite and its orbital radius?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |   |   |B.|\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/PH0443_A.png\" alt=\"PH0443_A\">|   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/PH0443_B.png\" alt=\"PH0443_B\">|\n|C. |   |   |  |D. |\n|<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/PH0443_C.png\" alt=\"PH0443_C\"> |   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/PH0443_D.png\" alt=\"PH0443_D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nCentripetal acceleration of an orbiting satellite is the gravitational acceleration of the planet, which is given by\n\n $g=\\dfrac{GM}{r^2}$.\n\nSince the gravitational acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the orbital radius, the correct graphical representation is given in C."},{"ref":"PH0446","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0446\n\nTwo stars of equal mass $M$ are shown in the diagram, distance $d$ apart. A planet is equidistant from the two stars, with distance $\\dfrac{d}{2}$ across the midpoint of the line joining the stars as shown.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/PH0446.png\" alt=\"PH0446\">\n\nWhat is the resultant gravitational field strength at the position of the planet due to the two stars?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2\\dfrac{GM}{d^2}\\cos(45\\degree)$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $4\\dfrac{GM}{d^2}\\cos(45\\degree)$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2\\dfrac{GM}{d^2}\\tan(45\\degree)$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $4\\dfrac{GM}{d^2}\\tan(45\\degree)$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe distance from each star to the planet is $\\dfrac{d}{2}\\sqrt2$\n\nThe gravitational field strength of one star at the location of the planet is given by\n$\\dfrac{GM}{{(\\frac{d}{2}\\sqrt2)}^2}=2\\dfrac{GM}{d^2}$\n\nSince the stars are identical and distances are equal, the resultant gravitational field strength will be the sum of the components directed towards the midpoint of the two stars as shown in red.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/PH0446_MS.png\" alt=\"PH0446_MS\">\n\nSince the angle is 45$\\degree$, the component of each field vector is\n $2\\dfrac{GM}{d^2}\\cos(45\\degree)$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the two vectors is $4\\dfrac{GM}{d^2}\\cos(45\\degree)$"},{"ref":"PH0501","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0501\n\n1. Define gravitational field strength. [2 marks]\n\n2. The Earth\\'s average radius is given as $6.37 \\times 10^3$ km. Calculate the height above the Earth\\'s surface at which gravitational field strength becomes half of its surface value. [3 marks]\n\n3. Calculate the gravitational force acting on a satellite of mass $2.63 \\times 10^6$ kg, placed at the height in part (b). State your answer to the appropriate number of significant figures. [3 marks]\n\n4. Using the available information, calculate the mass of Earth. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Gravitational force $\\underline{\\textrm{per unit mass}} \\,\\checkmark$ \\\nOn a small/test mass placed in the field. $\\checkmark$\n\n    Do not accept “force acting on a mass.”\n\n2. $\\dfrac{g}{2}=\\dfrac{GM}{{(R+h)}^2}$ ($g$ at height $h$) $\\bf{and}$ $\\dfrac{g}{2}\\,=\\dfrac{GM}{{2R}^2}$ (half of $g$ at surface) $\\checkmark$\n\n    Equating the two expressions:\n\n    - $\\left(\\dfrac{GM}{2R^2}=\\dfrac{GM}{{(R+h)}^2}\\right)$\n\n    - Therefore ${2R}^2={(R+h)}^2$ $\\checkmark$\n\n       Solving gives $h = 2.64 \\times 10^3$ km $\\checkmark$\n\n       The first two marking points are awarded if the expression is given correctly as stated in marking point 2.\nError carried forward allowed for marking point 3.\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nThe gravitational field strength is calculated by the formula:\n\n- $g=\\dfrac{GM}{r^2}$\n\n    where $M$ is the mass of Earth, and $r$ is the distance from the center of Earth.\n\n    At its surface, $r$ is equal to the radius of Earth, $R = 6.37 \\times 10^3$ km.\n\n    At height $h$ above the surface, the total distance from the center of Earth becomes $R + h$.\n\n    Since the question asks for the height where the gravitational field strength becomes $\\dfrac{g}{2}$, the expression becomes $\\dfrac{g}{2}=\\dfrac{GM}{2R^2}$\n\n    Equating this to the expression for the new field strength in terms of $h$ gives the answer as in the markscheme above.\n\n    $\\left(\\dfrac{GM}{2R^2}=\\dfrac{GM}{{(R+h)}^2}\\right)$\n\n    Substituting all values and solving for h\n\n    gives $h = 2.64 \\times 10^3$ km\n\n3. Gravitational field strength is $\\dfrac{g}{2} = \\dfrac{9.81}{2} = 4.905$ N $\\ce{kg^{-1}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $F = mg = (2.63 \\times 10^6)(4.905) = 12.9 \\times 10^6$ N $\\checkmark$\n\n    Answer to 3 significant figures $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The two values used for this calculation, $9.81\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}$ and $2.63 \\times 10^6\\, \\ce{kg}$ are both to three significant figures, so there should be three significant figures on the answer.\n\n4. :br\n$\\begin{aligned}\nF&=\\frac{GMm}{r^2}\\\\\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n12.9\\ \\times {10}^6&=\\frac{(6.67 \\times {10}^{-11})(2.63 \\times {10}^6)(M_{Earth})}{{\\left((6.37+2.64\\right) \\times{10}^6)}^2} \\checkmark \\\\\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\\textrm{Giving}  \\\\\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\nM_{Earth} &= 5.97 \\times 10^{24}\\, \\ce{kg}  \\checkmark\\\\\n\\end{aligned}$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The distances in km must be converted into m for this calculation\n\n    The force of gravity on the object at the known height can be substituted into Newton's Universal Law of Gravitation. The equation can then be solved to find the mass of the Earth."},{"ref":"PH0502","marks":"16","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0502\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\n1. A space probe lands on the surface of a planet which has the same radius as the Earth, and a gravitational field strength of 6.76 N kg$^{-1}$ at its surface.\n\n    1. The mass of Earth is given as $5.98 \\times 10^{24}\\ $ kg. Show that the planet’s mass is approximately $4 \\times10^{24} \\ $kg. [2 marks]\n\n    2. The average radius of the planet is $6.37 \\times 10^3 \\ $km. Calculate the planet\\'s average density in SI base units. [2 marks]\n\n    3.  The planet is at a distance of $1.43 \\times 10^{11} \\, $m from the center of the Earth. Calculate the gravitational force between the Earth and the planet. [2 marks]\n\n2.\n    1. Calculate the magnitude of the resultant gravitational field strength between the planet and the Earth at point $X$, which is located $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the total distance between the centers of the planets when measured from the Earth. [3 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/PH0502.png\" alt=\"PH0502\">\n\n    2. Suggest a reason why your answer above is only an estimate. [1 mark]\n\n3.\n    1. The planet has a moon with an orbital radius of $1.20 \\times 10^7 \\, $m. Show that the acceleration of this moon is approximately $1.9 \\ $m s$^{-2}$.   [1 mark]\n\n    2. Calculate the angular speed of this moon. [1 mark]\n\n    3. Determine the orbital period of this moon, in hours. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. From  $g=\\dfrac{GM}{r^2}$  , $\\hspace{1em}$   $g\\propto M$\n\n        therefore $\\dfrac{m_{planet}}{m_{earth}} = \\dfrac{6.76}{9.81}$\n\n        Rearranging gives $m_{planet} = 4.12\\times10^{24} \\ \\textrm{kg}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        (an extra significant figure from the calculation must be shown here)\n\n         $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n         Showing more detail,\n\n         $\\dfrac{Gm_{earth}}{r_{earth}^2}=6.76$ and $\\dfrac{Gm_{planet}}{r_{planet}^2}=9.81$ , where $r_{planet}=r_{earth}$\n\n         $\\dfrac{\\frac{Gm_{planet}}{r_{planet}^2}}{\\frac{Gm_{earth}}{r_{earth}^2}} = \\dfrac{6.76}{9.81}$\n\n         after cancellations, this gives\n\n         $\\dfrac{m_{planet}}{m_{earth}} = \\dfrac{6.76}{9.81}$\n\n         and\n\n         $m_{planet} = 4.12\\times10^{24} \\ \\textrm{kg}$\n\n    2. Volume = $\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi{(6.37 \\times {10}^6)}^3 = 1.08 \\times {10}^{21} \\, \\ce{m}^3$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Density = $\\dfrac{mass}{volume}$ = $\\dfrac{4.12 \\ \\times \\ {10}^{24}}{1.08 \\ \\times \\ {10}^{21}} = 3.81 \\times 10^3\\  \\textrm{kg}\\ \\textrm{m}^{-3}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        The radius given in $km$ must be converted to $m$.\n        $6.37 \\times {10}^3\\,\\textrm{km} = 6.37 \\times {10}^6\\,\\textrm{m}$\n\n    3. :br\n    $\\begin{aligned}\n    \\textrm{Use of }F&=\\frac{GMm}{r^2}\\\\\n    \\hspace{1em}\\\\\n    &=\\frac{(6.67 \\ \\times \\ {10}^{-11}) (5.98 \\ \\times \\ {10}^{24}) (4.12 \\ \\times \\ {10}^{24})}{{(1.43 \\ \\times \\ {10}^{11})}^2}  \\checkmark\\\\\n    \\hspace{1em}\\\\\n    &= 8.04 \\times 10^{16}\\, \\textrm{N}\\,\\checkmark\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}$\n\n2.\n    1. :br\n    $\\begin{aligned}\n    g_{Earth}&=\\frac{GM}{r^2}\\\\\n    \\hspace{1em}\\\\\n    &=\\frac{(6.67 \\ \\times \\ {10}^{-11}) (5.98 \\ \\times \\ {10}^{24})} {(\\frac{1.43\\times{10}^{11}}{3})^2}\\\\\n    \\hspace{1em}\\\\\n    &=175.5 \\ \\times \\ {10}^{-9}\\ \\textrm{N\\ {kg}}^{-1}\\checkmark\\\\\n    \\hspace{1em}\\\\\n    g_{planet}&=\\frac{GM}{r^2}\\\\\n    \\hspace{1em}\\\\\n    &=\\frac{(6.67 \\ \\times \\ {10}^{-11}) (4.12 \\ \\times \\ {10}^{24})}{{(\\left(1.43 \\ \\times \\ {10}^{11}\\right)(\\frac{2}{3}))}^2}\\\\\n    \\hspace{1em}\\\\\n    &=30.2 \\times {\\ 10}^{-9}\\ \\textrm{N\\ {kg}}^{-1}\\checkmark\\\\\n    \\hspace{1em}\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}$\n\nResultant $g = (175.5 \\times {10}^{-9})-(30.2 \\times {10}^{-9})= 1.45 \\times {\\ 10}^{-7}\\ \\textrm{N\\ {kg}}^{-1}$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        Each calculation should be shown for full marks. Since gravitational field strength is a vector quantity, the two vectors have opposite directions and the numbers need to be subtracted.\n\n    2. Any of the following:     $\\checkmark$\n\n    - Assumed that there is no interference from other stars or planets\n    - Planets are treated as point masses/all planet mass is assumed to be located at the center\n    - Planets are assumed to be spherically symmetric\n\n3.\n    1. Equating $F= ma$ and   $\\,F=\\dfrac{GMm}{r^2}$ giving\n\n        $a=\\dfrac{GM}{r^2}=\\dfrac{(6.67 \\ \\times \\ {10}^{-11})\\ (4.12\\ \\times \\ {10}^{24})}{\\left(1.20 \\, \\times \\, {10}^7\\right)^2}=1.908\\ \\textrm{m s}^{-2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        Since the answer is already given, the correct substitution must be shown, or answer to at least 3 significant figures. Use of $4 \\times 10^{24}$ gives 1.85 m s$^{-2}$ (also accepted).\n\n    2. Use of $a=\\omega^2r$\n\n        $\\omega=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1.908}{1.20 \\ \\times \\ {10}^{7\\ \\ }}}= 3.99 \\ \\times \\ {10}^{-4}\\ \\textrm{rad\\ s}^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    3. $T=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\omega}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $T=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3.99 \\ \\times \\ {10}^{-4}}=1.579 \\times {10}^4 \\ \\textrm{s}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Converting to h: $\\dfrac{15747}{3600}= 4.4 \\ \\textrm{h}$ $\\checkmark$"},{"ref":"PH0595","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0595\n\nA proton and another object with charge +e exert electrical and gravitational forces on one another. To the nearest order of magnitude, what mass must the object have to be so that the net force acting on it is zero?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/PH0595A.png\" alt=\"PH0595A\">\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}   10^1\\,$kg\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  10^3\\,$kg\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}   10^6\\,$kg\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}   10^9\\,$kg","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nEquate $F=G\\dfrac{Mm}{r^2}$ and $F=k\\dfrac{q_1q_2}{r^2}$ from the data booklet so that\n\n- $GMm_p=k\\left(e\\right)\\left(e\\right)$\n\nSolving for M,\n\n- $M=\\dfrac{ke^2}{Gm_p}$\n\nSubstituting in for the known values,\n\n- $M=\\dfrac{(8.99\\times{10}^9)(1.6\\times{10}^{-19})^2}{(6.67\\times{10}^{-11})(1.67\\times{10}^{-27})}$\n\nRounding each input to the nearest order of magnitude\n\n- $M=\\dfrac{{(10}^{10})({10}^{-38})}{({10}^{-10})({10}^{-27})}={10}^9$ kg"},{"ref":"PH0669-1","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0669-1\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nGeostationary satellites are satellites that complete one circular orbit in the same amount of time it takes for the Earth to rotate once on its axis. A geostationary satellite is placed in an orbit at $3.6\\times{10}^7$ m above the Earth\\'s surface, at the equator.  The radius of the Earth is $6.4\\times{10}^3$ km.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/PH0669aabb.png\" alt=\"PH0669aabb\">\n\nThe satellite undergoes uniform circular motion.\n\n1. Define gravitational field strength. [2 marks]\n\n2. Show that the linear speed of the satellite is approximately $3.1\\times{10}^3\\, \\ce{m\\,s^{-1}}$. [3 marks]\n\n3. Calculate the gravitational field strength at the point in the orbit of the satellite. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Force per unit mass $\\checkmark$\n\n    experienced by a small point mass at a location. $\\checkmark$\n\n2. ***convert km to m to give the radius of Earth to be $\\bold{6.4 \\times 10^6 \\ \\ce{m}}$***\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $d=2\\pi r=\\left(2\\right)\\pi\\left(3.6\\times{10}^7+6.4\\times{10}^6\\right)$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $t=\\left(24\\right)\\left(60\\right)\\left(60\\right)=8.6\\times{10}^4$ s $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $v=\\dfrac{d}{t}=\\dfrac{\\left(2\\right)\\pi\\left(3.6\\times{10}^7\\ +6.4\\times{10}^6\\right)\\ }{8.6\\times{10}^4}$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ = :answer[$3.10\\times{10}^3\\ \\ce{m\\,s^{-1}}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    (*extra significant figures or full substitution must be shown to receive 3rd mark*)\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The radius $r$ of the orbit from the centre of the Earth (converting km to m):\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $r=3.6\\times{10}^7\\ \\ce{m}+6.4\\times{10}^6$ m\n\n    The distance $d$ travelled by the satellite in one revolution:\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $d=2\\pi r=\\left(2\\right)\\pi\\left(3.6\\times{10}^7\\ +6.4\\times{10}^6\\right)$ m\n\n    The time $t$ of one revolution:\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $t=\\left(24\\right)\\left(60\\right)\\left(60\\right)=8.6\\times{10}^4$ s\n\n    The linear speed $v$ of the satellite:\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $v=\\dfrac{d}{t}=\\dfrac{\\left(2\\right)\\pi\\left(3.6\\times{10}^7\\ +6.4\\times{10}^6\\right)\\ }{8.6\\times{10}^4}$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $=3.10\\times{10}^3\\, \\ce{m\\,s^{-1}}$\n\n3. Any evidence linking gravitational field strength and the centripetal acceleration $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{v^2}{r}=\\dfrac{{(3.1\\times{10}^3)}^2}{(3.6\\times{10}^7\\ +6.4\\times{10}^6)}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ :answer[$2.3\\times{10}^{-1}\\, \\ce{m\\,s^{-2}}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nThe force $F$ acting on the satellite at that point should be the centripetal force that causes the satellite to orbit the Earth:\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $F=mg=\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}$\n\n    Where $m$ is the mass of the satellite, $v$ is the linear speed of the satellite, and $r$ is the separation between the centre of the Earth and the satellite.\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $g=\\dfrac{v^2}{r}=\\dfrac{{(2.6\\times{10}^3)}^2}{(3.6\\times{10}^7\\ +6.4\\times{10}^6)}=2.3\\times{10}^{-1}\\, \\ce{m\\,s^{-2}}$"},{"ref":"PH0674","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0674\n\nTwo point masses 4m and m separated by 30 cm as shown.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/Screenshot%202026-04-06%20at%207.54.57%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-04-06 at 7.54.57 AM\">\n\nAt point **A**, the net gravitational field due to the point masses is zero.\n\nWhat is the distance between 4m and **A**?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 10 cm\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$  15 cm\n\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$  20 cm\n\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 30 cm","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe net gravitational field is zero when the two gravitational fields of the two masses are equal in magnitude\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\begin{aligned}\ng_1 &= g_2\\\\\nG\\dfrac{4m}{{r_1}^2} &= G\\dfrac{m}{{r_2}^2}\\\\\n\\dfrac{4}{x^2} &= \\dfrac{1}{{(30-x)}^2}\\\\\n4\\left(30-x\\right)^2 &= x^2\\\\\n2\\left(30-x\\right) &= x\\\\\n60-2x &= x\\\\\nx &= 20\\ \\ce{cm}\n\\end{aligned}$"},{"ref":"PH0675","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0675\n\nAn astronaut in a space station orbiting the Earth experiences weightlessness.\n\nThe following are three statements about the astronaut and the space station:\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$ The astronaut experiences no gravitational force.\n- II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ The centripetal acceleration of the space station is equivalent to the gravitational field strength at that location\n- III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ Both the astronaut and the space station have the same acceleration.\n\nWhich of these statements is correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nStatement **I** is incorrect. This is due to Newton’s Law of Gravitation. The astronaut will experience gravitational force from the Earth that acts as the centripetal force.\n\nSince the centripetal force is the gravitational force, then the centripetal acceleration is equal to the gravitational field strength at that point. Therefore both the astronaut and the space station experience the same acceleration. Then statements **II** and **III** are correct."},{"ref":"PH0677","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0677\n\nThe diagram shows the gravitational field between two masses $m_1$ and $m_2$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/PH0677zzzz.png\" alt=\"PH0677zzzz\">\n\nWhich of the following is correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The two masses are equal\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The two masses repel each other\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $m_1<m_2$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $m_2<m_1$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\\\nThe diagram shows position of zero gravitational field near $m_2$.\n\nAt that location, $r_1>r_2$\n\nThen,\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $g_1=g_2$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $G\\dfrac{m_1}{{r_1}^2}=G\\dfrac{m_2}{{r_2}^2}$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{m_1}{m_2}=\\dfrac{{r_1}^2}{{r_2}^2}$\n\nThis means that\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $m_2<m_1$"},{"ref":"PH0678","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0678\n\nWhich of the following is correct about the gravitational field strength at a point near a planet?\n\n-    A. $\\hspace{1em}$ It is the force of gravity acting on an object.\n\n-    B. $\\hspace{1em}$ It is the force per unit mass acting on a point mass at that point\n\n-    C. $\\hspace{1em}$ It is inversely proportional to the distance between the centre of the planet and that point\n\n-    D. $\\hspace{1em}$ It is independent of the mass of the planet.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\\\nChoice **A** is incorrect as it equates the gravitational field strength with the gravitational force and these two are two different physical quantities.\n\nChoice **B** is the correct answer, since the gravitational field strength equals to force per unit mass;\n$g=\\dfrac{F}{m}$.\n\nChoice **C** is incorrect, because the gravitational field strength is inversely proportional to the square of the distance between the center of mass of the planet and that point;\n$g=G\\dfrac{M}{r^2}$.\n\nChoice **D** is incorrect, because the gravitational field strength depends on the mass of the planet;\n$g=G\\dfrac{M}{r^2}$."},{"ref":"PH0679","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0679\n\nWhich of the following graphs shows the variation of the gravitational field strength of a planet with distance $r$ from the surface of a planet?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n|| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/PH0679aa.png\" alt=\"PH0679aa\"> |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/PH0679bb.png\" alt=\"PH0679bb\">|\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/PH0679cc.png\" alt=\"PH0679cc\"> |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/PH0679dd.png\" alt=\"PH0679dd\">|\n\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra https://www.geogebra.org/m/CtfF7SBc]}}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe gravitational field strength varies inversely with the square of the distance from the center of the planet ($R$) to a given point.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $g=G\\dfrac{M}{{(R)}^2}$\n\nThis means the variation of the gravitational field strength with $R$ is an inverse square law. Note, however, that the question asks for the relationship from the distance from the surface of the planet, therefore the graph starts at a finite value of field strength at the surface ($r=0$), then follows the shape of an inverse square curve."},{"ref":"PH0680","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0680\n\nTwo objects **A** and **B** are separated by a distance $r$. The mass of **A** is much larger than that of **B**.\n\nWhich of the following is correct about magnitude and direction of the gravitational force exerted by **A** on **B** compared to that exerted by **B** on **A**?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Magnitude | Direction |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| Same | Same |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Same | Opposite |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Bigger | Same |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Bigger | Opposite |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\\\nAccording to Newton\\'s third law, the two objects exert equal and opposite forces on each other.\n\nThe equation\n\n- $F_g = \\dfrac{GMm}{r^2}$\n\nalso shows that the forces on the planets are equal in magnitude."},{"ref":"PH0683","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0683\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nA space station **X** orbits Earth with orbital radius $R$.\n\n1. State Newton\\'s universal law of gravitation. [2 marks]\n\n2. Explain why an astronaut in the space station feels weightless. [3 marks]\n\n3. The space station changed its position so that its orbital radius is $2R$.\n\n    Explain how the gravitational field strength of Earth at this new position compares to that at the orbital radius $R$. [2 marks]\n\n4. Another space station **Y** with a smaller mass orbits Earth at the same orbital radius $R$.\n\n    Compare the orbital speed of **Y** \\ to the orbital speed of **X**. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Force of gravity between two objects is inversely proportional to the square of the separation of their centers. $\\checkmark$\n\n    Force is directly proportional to the product of their masses. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nHere it would also be acceptable to state the formula and define all terms within the formula\n\n2. The centripetal force is the gravitational force between Earth and the space station $\\checkmark$\n\n    Both the astronaut and the space station have the same acceleration $\\checkmark$\n\n    The acceleration is the gravitational field strength at that position. $\\checkmark$\n\n3. Gravitational field strength is inversely proportional to the square of the distance. $\\checkmark$\n\n    The gravitational field strength will be decreased to quarter of its value at the first position. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nThe gravitational field strength is given by\n$g=\\dfrac{GM}{R^2}$\n\n    This means that the gravitational field strength is inversely proportional to the square of the distance.\nThen the gravitational field strength will be decreased to quarter of its value at the first position.\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $g_2=\\dfrac{GM}{\\left(2R\\right)^2}$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $g_2=\\dfrac{GM}{{4R}^2}$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $g_2=\\dfrac{g}{4}$\n\n4. Same $\\checkmark$\n\n    Orbital speed is independent of mass of the space station/ only depends on the mass of the planet and the orbital radius $v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{GM}{r}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nThe centripetal force is the gravitational force,\n\n    Then\n$\\dfrac{v^2}{r}=\\dfrac{GM}{r^2}$\n\n    Then the orbital speed is\n$v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{GM}{r}}$\n\n    The orbital speed is independent of the mass of the space station; it only depends on the mass of the planet and the orbital radius.\n\n    Since both **X** and **Y** have the same orbital radius and they both orbit the same planet, then they both have the same orbital speed"},{"ref":"PH0824","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0824\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\n1. State Kepler's first law of planetary motion. [1 mark]\n\n2. 1. Asteroids often follow highly elliptical orbits around the Sun. The following diagram shows the orbit of an asteroid around the Sun.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n        On the diagram above, mark the point at which the asteroid will be travelling at its lowest angular speed. Label this point P. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Use Kepler's second law to explain your answer to part b. [3 marks]\n\n3. 1. Kepler's third law states that the orbital period of a planet $T$ around a star is related to the separation between the star and the planet $r$ by the following equation:\n\n        $T^2 = kr^3$\n\n        Show that $k=\\dfrac{4\\pi ^2}{GM}$, where $M$ is the mass of the star. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Below is a graph of $T^2$ against $r^3$ for three planets in our solar system.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n        It is given that the planet Venus has an orbital period of 225 Earth days. Use the graph to estimate the orbital radius of Venus in AU. [2 marks]\n\n        $1\\, \\ce{AU}$ (average distance between the Earth and the Sun) $= 1.5 \\times10^{11}\\, \\ce{m}$\n\n    3. Use the graph to determine the mass of the Sun. Give your answer in kg. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. The orbit of every planet is an ellipse with the star at one of two foci/one focus.  $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n    Planets generally follow elliptical orbits but many planets including Earth follow orbits that are nearly circular (elliptical orbits of low eccentricity).\n\n2. 1. Point P marked on the orbit at furthest possible point from sun.   $\\checkmark$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        Points closest and furthest from sun lie along the longest central axis of the ellipse.\n\n    2. Line segment joining the asteroid and the Sun sweeps out equal areas in equal time intervals.  $\\checkmark$\n\n        The area swept out per radian/per degree/per unit angle is greater for points further from the sun / lower for points closer to the sun.  $\\checkmark$\n\n        Points furthest from the sun have the lowest angular speed.  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        Area swept out per radian is greater for points further from the sun. Kepler's second law tells us that the time taken per radian must also be greater. So, radians covered per unit time (angular speed) must be lower.\n\n3. 1. $\\dfrac{GmM}{r^2}=\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}$ or $\\dfrac{GmM}{r^2} =m{\\omega}^2 r$.   $\\checkmark$\n\n        Substitution of $\\omega=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{T}$ or $v=\\dfrac{2\\pi r}{T}$    $\\checkmark$\n\n        Clear rearranging leading to $T^2=\\dfrac{4{\\pi}^2}{GM} r^3$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        Gravitational force is equal to centripetal force so\n\n        - $\\dfrac{GmM}{r^2} = m{\\omega}^2 r$\n\n        Where $m$ is the mass of the planet.\n\n        Cancelling $m$'s gives:\n\n        - $\\dfrac{GM}{r^2} = {\\omega}^2 r$\n\n        $\\omega$ can be written in terms of the time period $\\omega=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{T}$, substituting this into the equation above gives:\n\n        - $\\dfrac{GM}{r^2} = \\Bigl(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{T}\\Bigr)^2 r $\n\n        - $\\dfrac{GM}{r^2} = \\dfrac{4{\\pi}^2 r}{T^2}$\n\n        - $T^2=\\dfrac{4{\\pi}^2}{GM} r^3$\n\n    2. 225 days $\\approx$ 0.62 years\n\n        And\n\n        $T^2≈0.37$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $r^3≈0.38$\n\n        $r \\approx 0.72\\, \\ce{AU}$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        Allow answers in range 0.71-0.73. Correct answer alone gains 2 marks.\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n        - 225 days $= \\dfrac{225}{365} \\approx$ 0.62 years.\n\n        - $T^2≈0.38 $\n\n        Reading from graph\n\n        - $r^3≈0.38$\n\n        - $r \\approx 0.72\\, \\ce{AU}$\n\n    3. gradient $= \\dfrac{4{\\pi}^2}{GM}$   $\\checkmark$\n\n        Expression or numerical value of gradient in SI units.\n\n        gradient $= 1\\times \\dfrac{((365)(24)(60)(60))^2}{(1.5\\times10^{11})^3} = 2.94…\\times 10^{-19}\\,\\ce{s^2}\\,\\ce{m^{-3}}$   $\\checkmark$\n\n        (allow values in range 1-1.02 for gradient before change of units)\n\n        $M=\\dfrac{4{\\pi}^2}{(6.67\\times10^{-11})(2.94…\\times 10^{-19})}=2.0\\times 10^{30}\\, \\ce{kg}$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n        - $T^2=\\dfrac{4{\\pi}^2}{GM} r^3$\n\n        Comparing this to $y=mx$ tells us that $\\dfrac{4{\\pi}^2}{GM}$ is the gradient of a graph of $T^2$ plotted against $r^3$.\n\n        Where $G$ is the gravitational constant (found in your formula book) and $M$ is the mass of the sun.\n\n        The gradient of the graph is approximately 1 in units $\\text{(Earth years)}^2 (\\ce{AU})^{-3}$ but we must convert into SI units to find the mass of the sun in kg.\n\n        - 1 Earth year = (365)(24)(60)(60)  seconds\n\n        Therefore\n\n        -  $\\text{(1 Earth year)}^2 (\\ce{AU})^{-3}=((365)(24)(60)(60))^2 (1.5\\times 10^{11})^{-3}\\, \\ce{s^2}\\, \\ce{m^{-3}}=2.94\\times 10^{-19}\\, \\ce{s^2}\\, \\ce{m^{-3}}$\n\n        - gradient $=\\dfrac{4{\\pi}^2}{GM}$\n\n        - $M=\\dfrac{4{\\pi}^2}{(G)(\\text{gradient})}= \\dfrac{4{\\pi}^2}{(6.67\\times 10^{-11})(2.94\\times 10^{-19})}=2.0\\times 10^{30}\\, \\ce{kg}$"},{"ref":"PH0836","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0836\n\nWhich of the following is $\\bold{not}$ one of Kepler’s laws of planetary motion?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ The square of a planet’s orbital period around a star is directly proportional to the cube of the distance between the planet and the star.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ The force of gravity between two objects is inversely proportional to the square of the separation of their centers and directly proportional to the product of their masses.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ The orbit of every planet is an ellipse with the sun at one focus.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ A line segment joining a planet and star that it orbits sweeps out equal areas in equal time intervals.","markScheme":"**答案：B**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nA is Kepler’s third law.\n\nB is Newton’s law of gravitation.\n\nC is Kepler’s first law.\n\nD is Kepler’s second law."},{"ref":"PH0837","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0837\n\nPlanet X and planet Y orbit the same star. Planet X has three times the orbital period and three times the mass of Planet Y.\n\nWhat is $\\dfrac{\\text{Orbital radius of planet X}}{\\text{Orbital radius of planet Y}}$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{27}}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{9}}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\sqrt[3]{9}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\sqrt{27}$","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nKepler's third law tells us that $T^2  \\propto r^3$.\n\nSo $\\dfrac{T^2}{r^3}=$ constant\n\nWhere $T_x$ and $T_y$ are the orbital periods of planet X and Y respectively and $r_x$ and $r_y$ are the orbital radii of planet X and planet Y respectively.\n\nWe want $\\dfrac{r_x}{r_y}$.\n\n- $\\dfrac{T_x^2}{r_x^3}=\\dfrac{T_y^2}{r_y^3}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{r_x^3}{r_y^3}=\\dfrac{T_x^2}{T_y^2}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{r_x}{r_y} = \\sqrt[3]{\\Bigl(\\dfrac{T_x}{T_y}\\Bigr)^2}=\\sqrt[3]{\\Bigl(\\dfrac{3T_y}{T_y}\\Bigr)^2}=\\sqrt[3]{9}$\n\nNote that the orbital period of a planet is independent of the planet's mass."},{"ref":"PH0838","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0838\n\nPlanet A orbits a star with orbital radius $r_A$ and orbital speed $v_A$. Planet B orbits the same star with an orbital radius $4r_A$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\nWhich of the following shows a correct expression for the speed and angular velocity of planet B?\n\n| | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Speed of Planet B$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Angular velocity of Planet B$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\dfrac{v_A}{2}$ | $\\dfrac{v_A}{8r_A}$ |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{v_A}{2}$| $\\dfrac{v_A}{2r_A}$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $4v_A$ | $\\dfrac{v_A}{r_A}$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $4v_A$ | $\\dfrac{4v_A}{r_B}$ |","markScheme":"**答案：A**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n$v=\\dfrac{2\\pi r}{T}$ where $T$ is the orbital period.\n\n$v_A=\\dfrac{2\\pi r_A}{T_A}$ and $v_B=\\dfrac{2\\pi r_B}{T_B}$\n\nWe can use Kepler's third law ($T^2 \\propto r^3$) to find the orbital period of planet B, $T_B$, in terms of the orbital period of planet A, $T_A$.\n\n- $\\dfrac{(T_A)^2}{(r_A)^3}=\\dfrac{(T_B)^2}{(r_B)^3}$\n\n$r_B=4r_A$ so\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1em} \\dfrac{(T_A)^2}{(r_A)^3} &= \\dfrac{(T_B)^2}{(4r_A)^3}\\\\\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\\dfrac{(T_B)^2}{4^3} &= (T_A)^2\\\\\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n(T_B)^2 &= 64(T_A)^2\\\\\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\nT_B &= 8T_A\\\\\n\\end{aligned}$\n\nWe may therefore write:\n\n- $v_B = \\dfrac{2\\pi (4r_A)}{8T_A} = \\dfrac{\\pi r_A}{T_A}=\\dfrac{v_A}{2}$\n\n- ${\\omega}_A = \\dfrac{v_A}{r_A}$\n\n- ${\\omega}_A = \\dfrac{v_B}{r_B} = \\dfrac{(\\frac{v_A}{2})}{4r_A} = \\dfrac{v_A}{8r_A}$\n\nAlternatively, we can find the necessary proportionality relations more directly by equating the gravitational force and the centripetal force.\n\nFor $v$:\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1em} \\dfrac{GmM}{r^2} &= \\dfrac{mv^2}{r}\\\\\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\\dfrac{GM}{r^2} &= \\dfrac{v^2}{r}\\\\\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\nGM\\dfrac{1}{r} &= v^2\\\\\n\\end{aligned}$\n\nNote: this formula is in your formula booklet ($v_{orbital}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{GM}{r}}$) under 'additional higher level'.\n\nWhere $G$ and $M$ are constant so $v^2 \\propto \\dfrac{1}{r}$\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1em} (v_A)^2 r &= (v_B)^2 (4r)\\\\\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\\dfrac{(v_A)^2}{4} &= (v_B)^2\\\\\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\nv_B &= \\dfrac{v_A}{2}\\\\\n\\end{aligned}$\n\nFor $\\omega$ we can use\n\n- $\\omega_B=\\dfrac{v_B}{r_B}=\\dfrac{v_A}{2(4r_A)}=\\dfrac{v_A}{8r_A}$"},{"ref":"PH0839","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0839\n\nSome data is collected regarding the orbital period $T$ and orbital radius $r$ of some planets orbiting a nearby star of mass $M$.\n\nWhich of the following shows a correct graph relating $T$ and $r$ that will have a gradient of $\\dfrac{GM}{4\\pi ^2}$ ?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n|| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">  |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">|\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">|","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nGravitational force is equal to centripetal force so\n\n- $\\dfrac{GmM}{r^2}=m{\\omega}^2 r$\n\nWhere $m$ is the mass of the planet.\n\nCancelling $m$ gives:\n\n- $\\dfrac{GM}{r^2} = {\\omega}^2 r$\n\n$\\omega$ can be written in terms of the time period $\\omega = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{T}$, substituting this into the equation above gives:\n\n- $\\dfrac{GM}{r^2} = \\Bigl(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{T}\\Bigr)^2 r$\n\n- $\\dfrac{GM}{r^2} = \\dfrac{4\\pi ^2 r}{T^2}$\n\n- $r^3=\\dfrac{GM}{4\\pi ^2} T^2$\n\nComparing this to $y=mx$ tells us that a graph of $r^3$ ($y$ axis) plotted against $T^2$ ($x$ axis) will have a gradient of $\\dfrac{GM}{4\\pi ^2}$."},{"ref":"PH0840","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0840\n\nWhich of the following diagrams best illustrates Kepler's second law?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n|| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">  |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">|\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">|","markScheme":"**答案：B**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nKepler's second law states:\n\nA line segment joining a planet and star that it orbits sweeps out equal areas in equal time intervals.\n\nThis is illustrated in B.\n\n(Note that A illustrates Kepler's first law:\nThe orbit of every planet is an ellipse with the Sun at one focus.)"},{"ref":"PH0841","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0841\n\nA planet orbits a star of mass $M$ with an orbital radius $r$. During a time $t$, an area $A$ is swept out by a line segment joining the planet and the star as shown in the diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\nWhich of the following is a correct expression for the area $A$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{t}{4}\\sqrt{GMr}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{t}{2}\\sqrt{GMr}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{t}{4\\pi}\\sqrt{GMr}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{t}{2\\pi}\\sqrt{GMr}$","markScheme":"**答案：B**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nArea of entire circular orbit $=\\pi r^2$.\n\nThe velocity of the planet is constant so in time $t$ an area of $\\dfrac{t}{T} \\pi r^2$ is swept out. Where $T$ is the orbital period of the planet.\n\nWe wish to eliminate $T$ so let us write $r$ in terms of $T$.\n\nGravitational force is equal to centripetal force so\n\n- $\\dfrac{GmM}{r^2}=m\\omega^2 r$\n\nWhere $m$ is the mass of the planet.\n\nCancelling $m$'s gives:\n\n- $\\dfrac{GM}{r^2}=\\omega^2 r$\n\n$\\omega$ can be written in terms of the time period $\\omega = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{T}$, substituting this into the equation above gives:\n\n- $\\dfrac{GM}{r^2}=\\Bigl( \\dfrac{2\\pi}{T} \\Bigr)^2 r$\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{GM}{r^3}}=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{T}$\n\n- $T=2\\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{r^3}{GM}}$\n\nNow substituting this into $A = \\dfrac{t}{T} \\pi r^2$ gives:\n\n- $Area = \\dfrac{t}{2\\pi \\sqrt{\\frac{r^3}{GM}}}\\pi r^2 = \\dfrac{t}{2} r^2 \\sqrt{\\dfrac{GM}{r^3}} = \\dfrac{t}{2} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{GMr^4}{r^3}} = \\dfrac{t}{2} \\sqrt{GMr}$"},{"ref":"PH0883","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0883\n\nPlanet $X$ has five times the mass and eight times the volume of Earth.\n\nWhat is the gravitational field strength on the surface of planet $X$?\n\nAssume that both the Earth and planet $X$ are spherical.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{5}{8}\\ g$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{5}{4}\\ g$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{5}{2}\\ g$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $5\\ g$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nGravitational field strength is given by:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $g\\ =\\ \\dfrac{G\\ M}{r^{2}}$\n\nSo, we must find the relationship between the radii of the two planets. Planets are spherical so:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $V\\ =\\ \\dfrac{4}{3}\\ \\pi\\ r^{3}$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{V}{r^{3}}\\ =\\ constant$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{V_{Earth}}{r^{3}_{Earth}}\\ =\\ \\dfrac{V_{X}}{r^{3}_{X}}$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{ V_{Earth}}{ r^{3}_{Earth}}\\ =\\ \\dfrac{8\\ V_{Earth}}{r^{3}_{X}}$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $r^{3}_{X}\\ =\\ 8 r^{3}_{Earth}$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $r_{X}\\ =\\ 2 r_{Earth}$\n\nNow:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $g\\ =\\ \\dfrac{G M_{Earth}}{r^{2}_{Earth}}$\n\nWhere $g$ is the acceleration of freefall on Earth's surface.\n\nNow let the gravitational field strength on the surface of planet $X$ be $g_{X}$:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $g_{X}\\ =\\ \\dfrac{G\\ (5 M_{X})}{(2 r_{X})^{2}}\\ =\\ \\dfrac{G\\ (5 M_{Earth})}{(2 r_{Earth})^{2}}\\ =\\ \\dfrac{5 G M_{Earth}}{4 r^{2}_{Earth}}\\ =\\ \\dfrac{5}{4}\\ g$"},{"ref":"PH0893-2","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0893-2\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe moon orbits the Earth at a constant speed of about $1022\\, \\ce{m\\,s^{-1}}$. The moon's orbit can be approximated as circular. The ratio of the mass of the earth to the mass of the moon is 81 and the average distance between the centre of the earth and the moon is around $3.8 \\times 10^8$ m.\n\n1. Deduce the mass of the Earth. [3 marks]\n\n2. On the graph below, sketch the variation of the gravitational field strength\ng with the position $x$ from the surface of the Earth to the surface of the Moon. Take the surface of the earth as the origin of the graph. [3 marks]\n\n    *(No need to add values to the axes)*\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/PH2893a.png\" alt=\"PH2893a\">","markScheme":"1. Identification of gravitational attraction as the centripetal force $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $M = \\dfrac{rv^2}{G}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ :answer[$6.0 \\times 10^{24}$ kg] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The force responsible for the circular motion of the moon around the Earth is the gravitational attraction between Earth and the Moon:\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{GMm}{r^2} = m \\dfrac{v^2}{r}$\n\n    Where $M$ and $m$ are the mass of the Earth and the Moon respectively.\n\n    When the relationship is simplified and rearranged to make $M$ as the subject:\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $M = \\dfrac{rv^2}{G}$\n\n    By substituting the given values:\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $M = \\dfrac{(3.8 \\times 10^8)(1022)^2}{6.67 \\times 10^{-11}} = 6.0 \\times 10^{24}$ kg\n\n    2. $g$ = zero at point closer to the moon $\\checkmark$\n\n        graviational strength on the surface of the Earth is bigger than the Moon surface $\\checkmark$\n\n        gravitational field directions are opposite in both side of the zero point $\\checkmark$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/PH2893MS.png\" alt=\"PH2893MS\">\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        The gravitational fields of the Earth and the Moon point in opposite directions. Therefore, they should cancel each other at a point between Earth and the Moon. Since Earth’s mass is bigger, this point should be closer to the moon. Additionally, bigger mass also results in bigger gravitational field strength on the surface of the Earth. However, the effect of the Earth's larger radius is negligible compared to the effect of its larger mass."},{"ref":"PH0982","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0982\n\nPlanet $Y$ has a mass three times greater than planet $X$. The gravitational field strength at the surface of planet $Y$ is six times greater than the gravitational field strength on planet $X$. The radius of planet $X$ is $r$. What is the radius of planet $Y$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{r}{2}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{r}{\\sqrt{2}}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $r$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $r\\sqrt{2}$","markScheme":"**答案：B**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\nKnowing that\n\n- $g = \\dfrac{GM}{r^2} \\hspace{3em}$ gives $\\hspace{3em} g \\propto \\dfrac{M}{r^2}$\n\nAnd solving for $r$ leads to\n\n- $r \\propto \\sqrt{\\dfrac{M}{g}} \\hspace{3em}$ and $ \\hspace{3em}r\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g}{M}} = constant$\n\nUsing $X$ and $Y$ to represent the different planets,\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1.5em} r_X\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g_X}{M_X}} &= r_Y\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g_Y}{M_Y}}\\\\\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\nr_X\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g_X}{M_X}} &= r_Y\\sqrt{\\dfrac{6g_X}{3M_X}}\\\\\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\nr_X\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g_X}{M_X}} &= r_Y\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2g_X}{M_X}}\\\\\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\nr_X\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g_X}{M_X}} &= r_Y\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g_X}{M_X}}\\\\\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\nr_X &= \\sqrt{2} r_Y\\\\\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\nr_Y &= \\dfrac{r_X}{\\sqrt{2}}\\\\\n\\end{aligned}$"},{"ref":"PH1006","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1006\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n1. State Newton\\'s law of Gravitation. [2 marks]\n\n2. A geostationary orbit is a special orbit in which satellites follow a circular path and remain above a fixed point on the Earth\\'s surface. A satellite orbits in geostationary orbit at a constant speed of $v$ and orbital radius of $R$. The mass of the Earth is $M$.\n\n    1. Show that $v = \\sqrt{\\frac{GM}{R}}$ [2 marks]\n\n    2. The radius of the satellite\\'s orbit is 4.23 $\\times 10^7\\, \\ce{m}$. Use this information to estimate mass of the Earth. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. The attractive force is proportional to the product of masses $\\checkmark$\n\n    Inversely proportional to the square of the distance between masses $\\checkmark$\n\n    *Accept formula if all terms are defined*\n\n2.\n    1. Statement that centripetal force is equal to gravitational force $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $Fc\\ =Fg$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{mv^2}{R}=\\ \\dfrac{GMm}{R^2}$\n\n        With correct rearrangement to obtain result $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        When in orbit, the centripetal force on the satellite is provided by the gravitational force from the planet. To find the orbital speed, the two quantities can be set equal to one another and rearranged.\n\n        - $\\dfrac{mv^2}{R}=\\ \\dfrac{GMm}{R^2}$\n\n        Giving\n\n        - $v = \\sqrt{\\frac{GM}{R}}$\n\n    2. The period of the satellite must be 1 day $\\ll$ to stay above a fixed point $\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $v = \\dfrac{circumference}{period}$ or $\\dfrac{2\\pi R}{T}$ or $\\dfrac{2\\ \\pi\\ (4.23\\ \\times\\ {10}^7) }{(24)(60)(60)}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\ll M = \\dfrac{v^2R}{G} = \\dfrac{(3076)^2(4.23\\times10^7)}{\\ 6.67\\ \\times\\ {10}^{-11}} \\gg$\n\n        - $6.00 \\times {10}^{24} \\ll \\ce{kg} \\gg $ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The period of the satellite\\'s orbit must match that of the Earth\\'s rotation, therefore the period is 24 hours.\n\n        One day can be converted to seconds through $24 \\times 60 \\times 60$\n\n        The speed of the orbit is\n\n        - $Speed\\ =\\ \\dfrac{distance}{time}=\\dfrac{circumference}{period}=\\dfrac{2\\pi R}{T}$\n\n        $\\hspace{4.7em} =\\dfrac{2\\ \\times\\ \\pi\\ \\times\\ \\ 4.23\\ \\times\\ {10}^7\\ }{24\\ \\times\\ 60\\ \\times60}=3076\\ \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$\n\n        Rearranging the formula\n\n        - $v = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{GM}{R}}$\n\n        to solve for mass gives\n\n        - $M = \\dfrac{v^2R}{G} = \\dfrac{{3076}^2\\ \\times\\ {4.23\\ \\times10}^7}{\\ 6.67\\ \\times\\ {10}^{-11}} = 6.00 \\times {10}^{24}\\ \\ce{kg}$"},{"ref":"PH1020","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1020\n\nPlanet Y has a mass three times greater than planet X. The gravitational field strength on planet Y is six times greater than the gravitational field strength on planet X. If the density of planet X is $\\rho$, what is the density of planet Y?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2\\sqrt2 \\rho$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $3 \\rho$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $6\\sqrt2 \\rho$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $12 \\rho$","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n- $g=\\dfrac{GM}{r^2}$\n\n- $g\\ \\propto\\dfrac{M}{r^2}$\n\n- $r\\ \\propto\\ \\sqrt{M/g}$\n\n- $r\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g}{M}}=constant$\n\n- $r_X\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g_X}{M_X}}=r_Y\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g_Y}{M_Y}}$\n\n- $r_X\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g_X}{M_X}}=r_Y\\sqrt{\\dfrac{6g_X}{3M_X}}$\n\n- $r_X\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g_X}{M_X}}=r_Y\\sqrt{\\dfrac{{2g}_X}{M_X}}$\n\n- $r_X\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g_X}{M_X}}=r_Y\\sqrt2\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g_X}{M_Y}}$\n\n- $r_X=\\sqrt2r_Y$\n\nNow density $\\rho=\\dfrac{m}{V}$ and $V=\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi\\ r^3$ giving $V\\ \\propto\\ r^3$ so we may write\n\n- $\\rho\\ \\propto\\dfrac{m}{r^3}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\rho r^3}{m}=constant$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\rho_Xr_X^3}{M_X}=\\ \\dfrac{\\rho_Yr_Y^3}{M_Y}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\rho_X\\left(\\sqrt2r_Y\\right)^3}{M_X}=\\dfrac{\\rho_Yr_Y^3}{{3M}_X}$\n\n- $\\left(3\\right)\\left(2\\sqrt2\\right)\\dfrac{\\rho_Xr_Y^3}{M_X}=\\dfrac{\\rho_Yr_Y^3}{M_X}$\n\n- $6\\sqrt2\\rho_X=\\rho_Y$\n\nDensity of planet Y = $6\\sqrt2\\rho$"},{"ref":"PH1808","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1808\n\nThe minimum distance between two planets $X$ and $Y$ is $d$. Planet $Y$ has an orbital period eight times that of planet $X$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/Screenshot%202025-03-11%20at%2010.27.15%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-03-11 at 10.27.15 AM\">\n\nWhat is the orbital radius of planet $X$ in terms of $d$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{d}{5}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{d}{4}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{d}{3}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{d}{2}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nKepler's third law states that orbital period squared $T^2$ is directly proportional to orbital radius cubed $r^3$:\n\n- $T^2\\propto r^3$\n\nSo, we have:\n\n- $\\dfrac{(T_Y)^2}{(T_X)^2}=\\dfrac{(r_Y)^3}{(r_X)^3}$\n\nSubstituting $r_Y=r_X+d$ and $T_Y=8T_X$:\n\n- $\\dfrac{(8T_X)^2}{(T_X)^2}=\\dfrac{(r_X+d)^3}{(r_X)^3}$\n\n- $64=\\left(\\dfrac{r_X+d}{r_x}\\right)^3$\n\n- $4=\\dfrac{r_X+d}{r_X}$\n\n- $4r_X=r_X+d$\n\n- $3r_X=d$\n\n- $r_X=\\dfrac{d}{3}$"},{"ref":"PH1846","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1846\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n1. Europa is one of the moons orbiting Jupiter.\n\n    The following data are given:\n\n    - Mass of Europa = 4.3 $\\times$ 10$^{24}$ kg\n    - Mass of Jupiter = 2.0 $\\times$ 10$^{27}$ kg\n    - Orbital radius of Europa = 680 $\\times$ 10$^3$ km\n\n    Calculate the centripetal force acting on Europa. [2 marks]\n\n2. 1. Point $P$ is between Jupiter and Europa. The distance between point $P$ and Jupiter is $x$.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/sScreenshot%202025-09-05%20at%209.46.07%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"sScreenshot 2025-09-05 at 9.46.07 AM\">\n\n        Sketch on the diagram **two** arrows to show the directions of gravitational fields due to Jupiter and Europa at point $P$. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Calculate the distance $x$ at which the net gravitational field strength is zero. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. $\\hspace{1.8em}$ $F=6.67\\times10^{-11}\\dfrac{(2.0\\times10^{27})(4.3\\times10^{24})}{(680\\times10^6)^2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $F=$ :answer[$1.2\\times10^{24}$ $\\ll\\textrm{N}\\gg$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The centripetal force is the gravitational force between Jupiter and Europa is provided by the gravitational force. This can be found from the formula\n\n    - $F=G\\dfrac{Mm}{r^2}$\n\n    For the radius values, we must convert to metres, which gives\n\n    - $r=680\\times10^6$ m\n\n    Solving for gravitational force gives\n\n    - $F=6.67\\times10^{-11}\\dfrac{(2.0\\times10^{27})(4.3\\times10^{24})}{(680\\times10^6)^2}=1.2\\times10^{24}$ N\n\n2. 1. An arrow to the left **AND** an arrow to the right $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The gravitational field strength of Jupiter is directed towards Jupiter and that of Europa is towards Europa.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Gravitational%20Fields/images/ph1846abi.png\" alt=\"ph1846abi\">\n\n    2. $\\hspace{1.8em}$ $\\dfrac{2.0\\times10^{27}}{x^2}=\\dfrac{4.3\\times10^{24}}{(680\\times10^6-x)^2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $x=$ :answer[$6.5\\times10^8$ $\\ll\\textrm{m}\\gg$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The net gravitational field strength is zero, then the two gravitational fields are equal in magnitude and opposite in direction at this point.\n\n        - $g_J=g_E$\n\n        - $G\\dfrac{M_J}{(r_J)^2}=G\\dfrac{M_E}{(r_E)^2}$\n\n        The constant $G$ cancels here. Substituting the values, and recognising that if the distance to Jupiter is $x$ then the distance to Europa is $680\\times10^6-x$ gives\n\n        - $\\dfrac{2.0\\times10^{27}}{x^2}=\\dfrac{4.3\\times10^{24}}{(680\\times10^6-x)^2}$\n\n        - $x=6.5\\times10^8$ m"},{"ref":"PH1847","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1847\n\nThe period of a planet's orbit about the Sun is approximately 2 years. If the radius of the Earth's orbit is $R$, what is the orbital radius of the other planet in terms of $R$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\sqrt[3]{2}R$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\sqrt{2}R$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\sqrt[3]{4}R$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\sqrt{8}R$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFrom Kepler's Third Law\n\n- $T^2\\propto r^3$\n\nThen\n\n- $\\dfrac{(T_{planet})^2}{(T_{Earth})^2}=\\dfrac{(r_{planet})^3}{(r_{Earth})^3}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{2^2}{1}=\\dfrac{(r_{planet})^3}{R^3}$\n\n- $r_{planet}=\\sqrt[3]{4}R$"}]},{"name":"Motion in Electromagnetic Fields","questions":[{"ref":"PH0051","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0051\n\nA wire carrying a current $I$ is placed in a region of uniform magnetic field $B_1$. The magnitude of the magnetic field is $B$, and its direction is into the page. The force acting on the wire is $F$, as shown in the diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0051A.png\" alt=\"PH0051A\">\n\nThe wire, carrying the same current $I$, is now placed with the orientation shown into another region of uniform magnetic field $B_2$. The magnitude of the magnetic field is $B$, and its direction is out of the page.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0051-2.png\" alt=\"PH0051-2\">\n\nWhat is the correct direction and magnitude of the force acting on the wire in field $B_2$?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Direction | Magnitude |\n| --- | --- | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0051CA.png\" alt=\"PH0051CA\"> | $F$ |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0051CB.png\" alt=\"PH0051CB\">| $F$ |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0051CC.png\" alt=\"PH0051CC\"> | $F\\sin\\theta$ |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0051CD.png\" alt=\"PH0051CD\"> | $F\\sin\\theta$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe direction of the current is perpendicular to the magnetic field in both regions and the magnitude of the field is unchanged. So the magnitude of the force is $F$.\n\nUsing Fleming's left hand rule, direction of the force is\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0051CA.png\" alt=\"PH0051-2\">"},{"ref":"PH0068","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0068\n\nAn object of mass $m$ and charge $q$ is dropped vertically between two parallel plates, $K$ and $L$. A magnetic field $B$ can be created perpendicular to the plane of the paper. The electric field between plates is $E$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0068.png\" alt=\"PH0068\">\n\nWhich of the following is correct about the magnitude of magnetic and electric fields when the object falls vertically at a constant velocity $v$?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\qquad\\underline{\\mathbf{E}}\\qquad$ | $\\qquad\\underline{\\mathbf{B}}\\qquad$ |\n| --- | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\dfrac{mg}{q}\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{5mm}$ | $\\dfrac{mg}{qv}\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{5mm}$ |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | ${m}{v}\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{5mm}$ | $0$ |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $mg$ | $\\dfrac{m}{qv}\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{5mm}$ |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\dfrac{mg}{q}\\rule[-4mm]{0pt}{5mm}$ | $0$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nSince the object moves vertically without deflection, the magnetic field in the region is zero.\n\nSince object moves at a constant velocity, vertical forces are balanced and $F_e = mg$, where $F_e = Eq$.\n\nSo, $B = 0$  and $E = \\dfrac{mg}{q}$."},{"ref":"PH0107","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0107\n\nTwo sections of current-carrying wires, $X$ and $Y$, that lie in the plane of the paper are shown below. The arrow in wire $X$ shows the direction of conventional current. Which response gives possible directions of current in wire $Y$ and electromagnetic force acting on $Y$?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0107.jpeg\" alt=\"PH0107\">\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\quad$Conventional current in $Y\\quad$ | $\\quad$Force acting on $Y\\quad$ |\n| --- | :---: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\rightarrow$ | $\\rightarrow$ |\n| B.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\rightarrow$ | $\\uparrow$ |\n| C.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\leftarrow$ | $\\uparrow$ |\n| D.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\leftarrow$ | $\\rightarrow$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nRecall that magnetic force acts perpendicular to current flow according to the Fleming's left-hand rule. That immediately eliminates option A and D. Then using the right hand grip rule, we can determine that the magnetic field experienced by wire $Y$ points into the page. The force direction and magnetic field direction allows current direction to be found with Fleming’s left-hand rule."},{"ref":"PH0117","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0117\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0117.png\" alt=\"PH0117\">\n\nAn alpha particle of mass $m$ and charge $q$, is accelerated through a potential difference $V$ into a magnetic field of flux density $B$ that is at right angles to the direction of motion. Which of the sketches below best describes the relationship between the radius of path $r$ and the potential difference of the accelerating field?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n| |  <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0117A.png\" alt=\"PH0117A\"> |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0117B.png\" alt=\"PH0117B\">|\n|$\\hspace{1cm}$\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0117C.png\" alt=\"PH0117C\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0117D.png\" alt=\"PH0117D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe path of a particle in the $B$-Field is a circle of radius\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{mv}{Bq}\n$$\nSince kinetic energy is conserved in the field,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{kinetic energy} &= \\text{work done by the field on the alpha particle.} \\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{1}{2} mv^2 &= qV \\\\[6pt]\nv &= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2qV}{m}} \\\\[6pt]\n\\therefore \\quad r &= \\dfrac{m}{Bq} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2qV}{m}} \\\\[9pt]\nr&= \\sqrt\\dfrac{2Vm}{B^2q} \\\\[10pt]\nr & \\varpropto \\sqrt{V}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the radius varies directly as the square root of the applied potential."},{"ref":"PH0230","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0230\n\nA current-carrying wire is at right angles to a uniform magnetic field. The magnetic force per unit length on the wire is $\\dfrac{F}{L}$. The length of the wire, the magnitude of the magnetic field strength, and the current of the wire are all doubled. Which of the following is the magnetic force per unit length on the wire after these changes?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{F}{L}$\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{2F}{L}$\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{4F}{L}$\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{8F}{L}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe force on a current-carrying wire under the effect of a magnetic field that is perpendicular to the current;\n\n$F = Bil$ which gives $\\dfrac{F}{l} = Bi$\n\nBoth the magnetic field and the current are increased to twice their initial values;\n\n$\\text{Force/unit length} = (2B)(2i) = 4Bi = 4 \\dfrac{F}{l}$\n\nTherefore the answer is C.\n\nNote that the changing of the length does not affect the force per unit length here."},{"ref":"PH0271","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0271\n\nThe diagram below shows a circuit connected to an iron rod $XY$. The connecting wires are flexible. The rod is placed in a magnetic field directed perpendicularly into the page, away from the observer, as shown.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0271.png\" alt=\"PH0271\">\n\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Created with Geogebra, by Tom Walsh (https://www.geogebra.org/m/JbAKa82Y)]}}$\n\nIn what direction will the resultant force act on the rod when the current is switched on in the circuit?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Upwards\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Downwards\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Away from the observer\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Towards the observer","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nConventional current will flow anticlockwise due to the orientation of the cell. To determine the force experienced by a current carrying conductor in a magnetic field, use (any appropriate hand-rule convention or) Fleming's left hand rule which states that if we arrange our thumb, forefinger and middle finger of the left-hand perpendicular to each other, then the thumb points towards the direction of the force experienced by the conductor, the forefinger points towards the direction of the magnetic field and the middle finger points towards the direction of the electric current."},{"ref":"PH0290","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0290\n\nA conductor carrying electric current out of the plane of the paper is placed between two magnets as shown in the diagram.\n\nAt which location is the magnitude of the resultant magnetic field strength largest due to the conductor and the magnets?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0290.png\" alt=\"PH0290\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe magnetic field between the magnets is from N to S, therefore towards the left.\n\nThe magnetic field of the current carrying conductor is towards the left at A, towards the right at B (right-hand rule)\n\nAt B and C, the two fields will be perpendicular to each other.\n\nAt D they will be opposite to each other and therefore partially cancel out.\n\nSince they are in the same direction at A, the resultant will be greatest at A."},{"ref":"PH0291","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0291\n\nAn electron enters a region of magnetic field with a velocity as shown in the diagram. The path followed by the electron is shown by the dotted line.\nWhat is the direction of the magnetic field in this region?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0291.png\" alt=\"PH0291\">\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Out of the plane of the paper\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ Into the plane of the paper\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ Towards the right\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Towards the left","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe force acting on the electron is downward at entry and perpendicular to the velocity, and the electron has negative charge. Using conventional methods (right-hand/left-hand) the magnetic field vector points into the plane of the paper"},{"ref":"PH0357","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0357\n\nA source of radiation is near a magnetic field that is directed into the page. A sheet of paper is placed in the path of the radiation before it enters the magnetic field.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0357.png\" alt=\"PH0357\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Created with Chemix (https://chemix.org)]}}$\n\nThe gamma radiation is not deflected when entering the magnetic field. Which of the following is correct about the other type of radiation that penetrates the paper sheet and the direction of its deflected path in the magnetic field?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\quad$Type of radiation$\\quad$ | $\\quad$Direction of deflected path$\\quad$ |\n| --- | :---: | :---: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\alpha$ | Upwards |\n| B. | $\\alpha$ | Downwards |\n| C. | $\\beta^-$ | Upwards |\n| D. | $\\beta^-$ | Downwards |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe alpha, beta and gamma radiation have different penetrating powers, where alpha is the one with the least penetrating power and won’t pass through the paper sheet, while gamma and beta radiations will pass through the paper sheet.\n\nWhen entering the magnetic field, gamma radiation will continue in its path without deflection because they have no charge. On the other hand, beta particles have negative charges, so they will be affected by a magnetic force that causes deflection in their path.\n\nTo determine the direction of the magnetic force, the right hand rule is used keeping in mind that the velocity direction in the right hand rule is that of a positive charge. So we have the direction of velocity is to the left, the magnetic field is into page, thus the magnetic force will be directed downwards.\n\n*Any other hand-rule convention can also be used."},{"ref":"PH0455","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0455\n\nA charged particle is moving horizontally when it enters an electric field between two parallel plates. The path followed by the particle when it passes through the plates is shown. Assume gravitational effects to be negligible.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0455.png\" alt=\"PH0455\">\n\nConsider the following statements about the charged particle.\n\n- I.$\\hspace{1em}$ The particle is positively charged.\n\n- II.$\\hspace{1em}$ A magnetic field in a direction into the page could cause the charged particle to move horizontally without deflection.\n\n- III.$\\hspace{1em}$ A magnetic field in a direction out of the page could cause the charged particle to move horizontally without deflection.\n\nWhich statements are correct?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Only II\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Neither","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n- I.$\\hspace{1em}$ Correct: Since the particle is deflected toward the negative plate it is positively charged.\n\n- II.$\\hspace{0.7em}$ Correct: In order not to be deflected magnetic force acting on the particle should be upwards. According to the right hand rule the magnetic field should be into the page.\n\n- III.$\\hspace{0.45em}$ Incorrect: In order not to be deflected the magnetic force acting on the particle should be upwards. According to the right hand rule magnetic field should be into the page."},{"ref":"PH0457","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0457\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nThe diagram shows a cross-sectional view of a current carrying wire. The current direction is out of the plane of the paper.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0457-1.png\" alt=\"PH0457-1\">\n\n1. Draw the magnetic field lines due to this wire.    [2 marks]\n\nA second wire carrying current $B$ is placed near wire $A$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0457-3.png\" alt=\"PH0457-3\">\n\nThe current in wire $A$ is 10 A, while in wire $B$ is 12 A. The length of wire $B$ within the magnetic field of $A$ is 1 m.\n\n2. 1. Draw an arrow representing the direction of the force $F$ on wire $B$ due to the magnetic field of wire $A$.    [1 mark]\n\n    The magnetic field $B$ of wire $A$ can be found using the following formula\n    $$\n        B = \\dfrac{\\mu_0 I_A}{2\\pi r}\n    $$\n\n    where $r$ is the distance from the wire.\n\n    2. If the distance between wires $A$ and $B$ is 0.1 cm, calculate the magnitude of the magnetic force on wire $B$ due to the magnetic field of wire $A$. State an appropriate unit for your answer.      [3 marks]\n\n3. A third wire $C$ is placed between wires $A$ and $B$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0457-5.png\" alt=\"PH0457-5\">\n\nThe total force on wire $C$ due to $A$ and $B$ makes it move to the right as shown.\n\nDetermine the direction of the current of wire C. Justify your answer.    [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Concentric circles with increasing spacing.$\\,\\checkmark$\n\n    Anti-clockwise direction.$\\,\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$\n    The magnetic field due to a wire carrying current is concentric circles with the wire at the centre of each circle.\n    The direction of the magnetic field is determined from the right-hand grip rule.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0457-2.png\" alt=\"PH0457-2\">\n\n2. 1. Arrow directed to the right.$\\,\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$ \\\n        The magnetic field of wire $A$ at wire $B$ is upwards. The direction of the current in wire $B$ is into page.\n\n        Using the right hand rule, the force on $B$ due to the magnetic field of $A$ is to the right.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0457-4.png\" alt=\"PH0457-4\">\n\n        This leads to the fact that wires with parallel currents attract while those with anti-parallel currents repel.\n\n    2. $B = \\dfrac{(4\\pi\\times10^{-7})(10)}{(2\\pi)(0.1\\times10^{-2})}\\,\\checkmark$\n\n        $B = \\ll (2.0\\times10^{-3}) \\, \\ce{T}\\gg$\n\n        $F = (2\\times10^{-3})(12)(1)\\,$\n\n        $F = 0.024\\, $ $\\checkmark$  :answer[N] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$ \\\n        The magnetic field of wire $A$ is calculated from the formula\n\n        $\\begin{aligned}\n        B &= \\dfrac{\\mu_0 I_A}{2\\pi r} \\\\\n        B &= \\dfrac{(4\\pi\\times10^{-7})(10)}{(2\\pi)(0.1\\times10^{-2})} = (2.0)(10^{-3})\\, \\ce{T}\n        \\end{aligned}$\n\n        The magnetic force on $B$ due to magnetic field of $A$\n\n        $F = B_A I_B L_B = (2\\times10^{-3}))(12)(1) = 0.024$ N\n\n3. Current of wire $C$ is into the page. $\\checkmark$\n\n   Current opposite direction of A / anti-parallel currents repel/ Current same as B / parallel currents attract/explanation with use of right hand or left hand rule $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$\n    For wire $C$ to move to the right, it must be repelled by wire $A$ and attracted by wire $B$.\n\n    To have a repelling force from wire $A$, the two currents must be in opposite directions. Since the current in wire $A$ is out of page, so current in wire $C$ is into page.\n\n    Also for wire $B$ to attract wire $C$, both must have the same direction of current, which means that current in wire $C$ is into the page."},{"ref":"PH0458","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0458\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nA charged particle $x$ enters a region of magnetic field $B$ of 2.0 T with a speed of $3.5 \\times 10^6\\, \\ce{m\\, s^{-1}}$ in the shown direction. The magnitude of the charge on the particle is $1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ C.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0458a.png\" alt=\"PH0458a\">\n\n1. Determine whether the particle is positive or negative. Justify your answer.  [2 marks]\n\n2. Calculate the magnitude of the force $F$ on this particle when it enters the magnetic field.  [2 marks]\n\nA particle $y$ with an equal but opposite charge enters the same magnetic field $B$ as shown.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0458b.png\" alt=\"PH0458b\">\n\n3.\n    1. If the ratio $\\dfrac{v_x}{v_y}=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, calculate the magnitude of the magnetic force on charge $y$.    [1 mark]\n\n    2. Draw an arrow indicating the direction of the magnetic force on this particle when it just entered the magnetic field.    [1 mark]\n\n    3. Particle $y$ has the same mass as particle $x$, draw the path of particle y as it moves through the field.   [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Positive. $\\checkmark$\n\n    Using the right hand rule/ force is directed upwards. $\\checkmark$\n\n2. $F = \\ll qvB \\gg$\n\n    $F =  (1.6 \\times 10^{-19})(3.5\\times 10^6) (2)\\,\\checkmark $\n\n    $F = 1.12 \\times 10^{-12} \\ll \\ce{N} \\gg\\,\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$\n    The magnetic force on a charged particle $F$\n\n    $\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    F &= qvB \\\\\n    F &= (1.6 \\times 10^{-19})(3.5\\times 10^6)(2)\\\\\n    &= 1.12 \\times 10^{-12}\\, \\ce{N}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    $\n\n3. 1. $F=(2)(1.12 \\times 10^{-12})$\n\n        $F = 2.24 \\times 10^{-12}\\ll \\ce{N} \\gg \\,\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$ \\\n        Since the velocity $v_y$ is double the velocity $v_x$, then the magnitude of the force on particle $y$ is double that on particle $x$.\n\n        $F = (2)(1.12 \\times 10^{-12}) = 2.24 \\times 10^{-12}$ N\n\n    2. .\n\n       <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0458c.png\" alt=\"PH0458c\">\n\n        Arrow directed to the right. $\\checkmark$\n\n    3. Part of a circle path with the force in the direction of its center. $\\checkmark$\n\n        A larger radius of the circular path. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$\n\n        $r= \\dfrac{mv}{qb}$. Because $v$ has doubled and all other quantities remain the same, the radius will double."},{"ref":"PH0461","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0461\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nA solenoid is carrying a current of 1.2 A in the shown direction.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0461-1.png\" alt=\"PH0461-1\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with chemix - https://chemix.org/]}}$\n\n1. On the diagram above, draw the magnetic field lines produced by the current in this solenoid.      [2 marks]\n\nA proton moving with speed of $5.6 \\times 10^6 \\,\\ce{m \\,s^{-1}}$ is moving in the magnetic field of a different solenoid with an angle $35\\degree$ to the field as shown.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0461-11.png\" alt=\"PH0461-11\">\n\n2.\n    1. Determine the direction of the force on this proton due to the magnetic field of the solenoid.     [1 mark]\n\n    2.  The magnetic field of the solenoid is $4.2\\times 10^{-3}$ T.  Calculate the magnitude of the magnetic force on the proton.     [2 marks]\n\n    3. State and explain the shape of the path that the proton takes as it travels through the field. [3 marks]\n    4. Another proton with the same speed enters the same magnetic field as shown.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0461-2.png\" alt=\"PH0461-2\">\n\n        Discuss whether it will experience the same force as the proton in part b(ii).   [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Curved field lines entering and passing through the solenoid and looping around the outside.  $\\checkmark$\n\n    Field line direction shown to be to the left within the solenoid and to the right when looping back.  $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0461a.png\" alt=\"PH0461a\">\n    $\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with chemix - https://chemix.org/]}}$\n\n    Using the right hand solenoid rule: when the fingers of the right hand are wrapped around the coil to match the direction of current flow, the right thumb will point in the direction of the field lines within the solenoid.\n\n2. 1.  Into the page. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$\n    The velocity of the charge can be split into two components, perpendicular and parallel to the field. The charge experiences no force due to the interaction of the parallel component of the velocity of the charge with the magnetic field, but does experience a force due to the interaction of the perpendicular component of the velocity with the field. As this direction is up the page, we can apply the right hand rule to determine that the magnetic force is into the page.\n\n    2.  $\\mbox{}$\n        $\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        F &= (1.6\\times 10^{-19})(5.6 \\times 10^6)(4.2\\times 10^{-3})(\\sin 35) \\,\\checkmark \\\\[6pt]\n        F &= 2.16 \\times 10^{-15} \\,\\ll \\ce{N} \\gg \\\\[6pt]\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$\n        $\n        \\begin{aligned}\n        F &= qvb \\sin\\theta  \\rule[-0pt]{0pt}{4mm}\\\\\n        F &= (1.6\\times 10^{-19})(5.6 \\times 10^6)(4.2\\times 10^{-3})(\\sin 35) \\\\\n        F &= 2.16 \\times 10^{-15}\\, \\ce{N}\n        \\end{aligned}\n        $\n\n    3. Helix/$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$spring-like” shape $\\checkmark$\n\n        (Component of) velocity parallel to the direction of the field is unchanged $\\checkmark$\n\n        (Component of) velocity perpendicular to the direction of the field causes the proton to move in a circular path. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The component of the particle's velocity along the direction of the field lines is unaffected by the field, however the field does cause a circular motion in the plane perpendicular to the page. These combine to form a helical shape, where the circular path is “stretched out” due to the parallel velocity component. Note that “spiral” is an incorrect description here, as spirals have decreasing radius.\n\n    3. No. $\\checkmark$\n\n        No force/ velocity parallel to the magnetic field/ velocity is in the same direction as the magnetic field. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$\n        The proton in this case will experience zero magnetic force, because it is parallel to the magnetic field.\n\n        Also using the equation and setting $\\theta = 0\\degree$ gives $F = 0$."},{"ref":"PH0474","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0474\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\n1. A mass spectrometer is a tool that uses a magnetic field to determine the mass of a moving charged particle.\n\n    An alpha particle with a speed of $2.0\\times10{^7}$ m s$^{-1}$ enters a region of magnetic field directed into the plane of the paper.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/Screenshot%202025-05-29%20at%201.33.51%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-29 at 1.33.51 PM\">\n\n    1. Suggest why the alpha particle undergoes circular motion inside the magnetic field. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Explain why the centripetal force does no work on the alpha particle. [2 marks]\n\n2. 1. On the diagram, draw an arrow to represent the path of the alpha particle inside the magnetic field. [2 marks]\n\n    2. State the charge on the alpha particle. [1 mark]\n\n    3. The radius of the path of the alpha particle is determined to be 15 cm, and the magnetic field strength is 2.77 T.\n\n        Calculate the mass of the alpha particle, expressing your answer to the appropriate number of significant figures. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. The alpha particle experiences a (magnetic) force $\\checkmark$ \\\n    The force is perpendicular to the velocity (which is the condition for circular motion) $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Recall that a moving charge in a magnetic field experiences a magnetic force in a perpendicular direction to the velocity and the magnetic field.\n\n        Because the magnetic force is perpendicular to the velocity at all times, it cannot either speed it up or slow it down - only change its direction. Because the magnitude of the magnetic force is proportional to the velocity, it also remains constant - and so the direction of the particle changes at a constant rate. This produces circular motion.\n\n    2. Work = (Force)(Displacement)($\\cos(\\theta)$) $\\checkmark$\n\n        Force vector and displacement vector are perpendicular, therefore $\\theta=90\\degree$ and $\\cos(\\theta)$ = 0 $\\checkmark$\n\n        **OR**\n\n        Work = $\\Delta$ kinetic energy $\\checkmark$\n\n        Speed / kinetic energy of the alpha particle is constant (hence no work done) $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        At any instant, the magnetic force vector is perpendicular to the velocity vector, and is thus perpendicular to the displacement vector. Thus $\\theta=90\\degree$ and $\\cos(\\theta)=0$, giving a value of zero for work done.\n\n        We could also note that if work is done in the particle, we would expect its kinetic energy to change. Because the speed of the particle is constant in uniform circular motion, there is no change in kinetic energy and therefore no work done on the particle.\n\n2. 1. The path must be a circular shape $\\checkmark$\n\n        Curving upwards and exiting the magnetic field at some point (cannot show a full circle) $\\checkmark$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/Screenshot%202025-05-29%20at%201.38.35%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-29 at 1.38.35 PM\">\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Use Fleming's or other rule to determine the force on the particle to be upwards as it enters the magnetic field. Path needs to be circular, and not parabolic in shape. Note that a full circle cannot be drawn—for small radii a semicircular path will result, with the particle exiting parallel to its entry path.\n\n    2. :answer[$3.2\\times10^{-19}$ C] $\\checkmark$\n\n        Allow $+2e$. Award no mark for $+2$.\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        This is a factual question. An alpha particle is a Helium nucleus which has 2 neutrons and 2 protons, therefore +2 elementary charge.\n\n    3. $\\hspace{1.8em}$ $m=\\dfrac{qrB}{v}=\\dfrac{(3.2\\times{10}^{-19})(15\\times{10}^{-2})(2.77)}{2.0\\times{10}^7}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $m=$ :answer[$6.6\\times{10}^{-27}\\textrm{ kg}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        Answer in 2 significant figures, correct rounding $\\checkmark$\n\n        Allow error carried forward for use of charge given in b (ii)\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The magnetic force acts as the centripetal force, therefore:\n\n        - $\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}=qvB$\n\n        Rearranging gives\n\n        - $m=\\dfrac{qrB}{v}$\n\n        - $m=\\dfrac{(3.2\\times{10}^{-19})(15\\times{10}^{-2})(2.77)}{2.0\\times{10}^7}$\n\n        - $m=6.6\\times{10}^{-27}$ kg"},{"ref":"PH0780","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0780\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\n1. An electron travels into an evacuated box where a uniform electric field acts vertically downwards. The electron is travelling perpendicular to the field at a speed $v$ at the moment it enters the box.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0780.png\" alt=\"PH0780\">\n\n    Deduce the motion of the electron inside the box. Ignore any gravitational forces. [2 marks]\n\n2. Once the electron has exited the box, a uniform magnetic field is added to the box in addition to the electric field. The magnetic field acts into the page, perpendicular to the plane of the paper and the electric field.\n\n    Another electron travels horizontally into the box at a speed v (initially moving perpendicular to both fields).\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0780bb.png\" alt=\"PH0780bb\">\n\n    The electron is found to exit the box along the same straight line which it entered, with the same speed, $v$. Explain this observation.      [2 marks]\n\n3. The electric field is now removed such that the box contains the magnetic field only. Another electron enters perpendicular to the field at a speed $v$.\n\n    This electron is found to exit the box at the same speed $v$, travelling vertically downwards.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0780C.png\" alt=\"PH0780C\">\n\n    Explain why the speed of the electron is unchanged. [2 marks]\n\n4. It is known that the electron in part (c) was inside the box for a time of 1.0 $\\mu $s. Show that the magnetic flux density inside the box is around 9 $\\mu $T.  [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. Electron accelerates/experiences a net force upwards while horizontal velocity (component) remains the same  $\\checkmark$\n\n    The electron follows a parabolic path (upwards) $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Electric force acts on a positive charge in the same direction as field (downwards).\n\n    Electron is negatively charged so force acts upwards causing an upward acceleration.\n\n    Electron experiences no horizontal force so horizontal component of velocity remains unchanged\n\n2. Magnetic field causes the electron to experience a force downwards. \\<<Fleming’s left hand rule\\>> $\\checkmark$\n\n    Upward electric force and downward magnetic force have the same magnitude/ Electric and magnetic forces are equal and opposite. So net force acting on the electron in the box is zero. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Fleming’s left hand rule - electron is negatively charged so acts as a negative current to the right/ positive current to the left.\n\n    Net force = 0 implies acceleration = 0  (Newton’s 1st law) so the electron remains at constant velocity.\n\n3. Magnetic force is \\<<constantly\\>> perpendicular to the velocity of the electron. $\\checkmark$\n\n    So acceleration causes velocity to change direction while speed/ magnitude of velocity remains constant. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Magnetic field causes a moving charge to experience a force perpendicular to both the field and the velocity of the charge (direction can be determined using Fleming's left hand rule).\n\n    These three vectors (velocity, magnetic field and force) remain perpendicular.\n\n    When force is perpendicular to velocity, circular motion occurs – direction of the velocity changes while its magnitude remains the same.\n\n4. Magnetic force = mass $\\times$  acceleration\n\n    - $\\ Bqv=m\\,\\omega^2\\,r$\n\n    -  **OR**\n    - $Bqv=\\dfrac{m\\,v^2}{r}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    Substitution of $v=\\omega\\ r$ and \\\n    Rearranging for $B$:\n\n    - $B=\\dfrac{m_e\\,\\omega}{e}$\n\n    **OR**\n\n    Equation in terms of Time period:\n\n    - $B=\\dfrac{m_e\\,2\\pi}{T\\,e}$\n\n    - Time period = $4.0\\times{10}^{-6}\\,$s\n\n    **OR**\n\n    - $\\omega=1.57\\times{10}^6\\ \\ce{s}^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    Final answer:\n\n    - $B=$ :answer[$8.9\\ \\mu \\ce{T}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    (extra significant figures must be shown for MP2)\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Magnetic force = mass $\\times$ centripetal acceleration (as the electron is moving in a circle): $B\\,q\\,v=m\\,\\omega^2\\,r$ or $Bqv=\\dfrac{m\\,v^2}{r}$\n\n    ($q$ is elementary charge, $e. m$ is mass of electron $m_e$)\n\n    Substitution of $v=\\omega\\ r$ gives: $Be\\,\\omega\\ r=m_e\\,\\omega^2\\,r$\n\n    Rearranging for $B$ gives: $B=\\dfrac{m_e\\,\\omega}{e}$\n    **OR**\n    Since $\\omega=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{T}$      In terms of Time period equation may be written $B=\\dfrac{m_e\\,2\\pi}{Te}$\n\n    The only possible trajectory for this particular entry and exit of the box is a $^1/_4$ circle. So time given is $^1/_4$ of the time period for the circular path.\n\n    Time period is $(4)\\left(1.0\\times{10}^{-6}\\right)=4.0\\times{10}^{-6}\\ $s \\\n    **OR**\n\n    - $\\omega=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{(4)(1\\times{10}^{-6})}=1.57\\times{10}^6{\\ \\ce{s}}^{-1}$\n\n    Final answer:\n\n    - $B=\\dfrac{{2\\pi\\, m}_e}{T\\,e}$\n\n    - $=\\dfrac{(2\\pi)(9.11\\times{10}^{-31})}{(4\\times{10}^{-6})(1.6\\times{10}^{-19})}$\n\n    - $=8.94\\times{10}^{-6}\\, $T or  $8.9\\, \\mu $T"},{"ref":"PH0784","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0784\n\nTwo parallel wires, each carrying a current $I$, experience an electromagnetic force denoted as $F$, and they have a length $L$ with a separation distance of $r$ between them. The force between the wires is doubled when\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Only $I$ is doubled\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Only $r$ is doubled\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Only $L$ is doubled\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $L$ and $r$ are both doubled","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe force F between two parallel wires:\n\n- $F=L\\mu_0\\dfrac{I_1I_2}{2\\pi r}$\n\nWhen given values are substituted:\n\n- $F=L\\mu_0\\dfrac{I^2}{2\\pi r}$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- $F\\propto\\ L\\ $\n\n- $F \\propto\\ I^2\\ $\n\n- $F\\propto\\ \\dfrac{1}{r}$\n\nTherefore, when length is doubled force is also doubled."},{"ref":"PH0785","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0785\n\nA wire and a region of uniform magnetic field lie in the same plane. The wire is positioned in two distinct ways, as illustrated in Figure **A** and Figure **B**, resulting in different angles between the direction of the magnetic field and the direction of the current in the wire\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0785aa.png\" alt=\"PH0785aa\">\n\nWhich of the following is correct in terms of the magnitude of the force and the direction of the force acting on the wire?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Magnitude of the force | The direction of the force |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| Same | Same |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Same| Opposite |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Greater in **A** | Same |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Greater in **A** | Opposite |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe force $F$ acting on the wire:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $F=BIL\\sin\\theta$\n\nWhere $B$ is magnetic flux density, $I$ is the current passing through the wire, $L$ is the length of the wire in the field and $\\theta$ is the angle between the direction of the current and magnetic field.\n\nSince $L\\sin\\theta$ represents the vertical component of the wire's length within the magnetic field, the value of $L\\sin\\theta$ is the same for both scenarios. Additionally, there is no alteration in the values of $B$ and $I$ in either position. Consequently, it follows that the magnitude of the force remains identical in both configurations.\n\nAnother way of thinking of this is that as $\\theta$ increases the length of wire in the field $L$ increases. But as $\\theta$ increases, $\\sin\\theta$ decreases and the changes to $L$ and $\\sin\\theta$ cancel out.\n\nWhen the current in the wire is resolved into two components, one vertical and one horizontal, the horizontal component does not experience any force because it is parallel to the magnetic field. However, the vertical component does experience a force because it is perpendicular to the magnetic field. In both scenarios, the current is directed upwards and the magnetic field is directed leftwards. Therefore, the force on the wire should be the same for both."},{"ref":"PH0787","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0787\n\nA proton with a speed of $v$ enters a region in a vacuum where there is a magnetic field perpendicular to the page and an electric field due to oppositely charged plates. The proton does not deflect.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0787aa.png\" alt=\"PH0787aa\">\n\nConsider the following particles travelling in the same region in the same direction.\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | A neutron with the speed of $2v$   |\n  | II. | A proton with the speed of $2v$ |\n  | III. | An electron with the speed of $v$ |\n\nWhich of the particles would not be deflected?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAs the proton passes through the region, it experiences two forces: one magnetic $F_B$ and one electric $F_E$. To enable the proton to travel without deviating from its path, these two forces must have equal magnitudes but act in opposite directions, effectively cancelling each other out:\n\n- $F_B=F_E$\n\n- $qvB=qE$\n\n- $v=\\dfrac{E}{B}$\n\nThe deduced relationship shows that the path followed by a charged particle does not depend on its charge. Moreover, the magnitude of the electric field and the magnetic flux density are constant. Hence, the only criteria for the charged particle to follow a specific path is that it must be moving with a specific speed, $v$.\n\nTherefore, while an electron with the speed of $v$ satisfies this criterion, a proton with the speed of $2v$ does not. A neutron is a chargeless particle. Hence, it will not experience any force on itself due to electric and magnetic fields. It is also undeflected."},{"ref":"PH0798","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0798\n\nTwo parallel wires are separated by a distance of 0.015 m and each carry a current of 3.0 A in opposite directions. Each wire has a mass per unit length of $0.2\\, \\ce{g\\, cm^{-1}}$.\n\nWhat is the magnitude and direction of the acceleration of the wires?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Magnitude / ${10}^{-3}\\ \\ce{m\\, s^{-2}}$  | Direction |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| 6 | Towards each other |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 60 | Towards each other |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 6 | Away from each other |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 60 | Away from each other |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nConverting to correct units:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $0.2\\ \\ce{g\\ cm^{-1}} =\\left(0.2\\times{10}^{-3}\\ \\ce{kg}\\right)\\left({10}^{-2}\\ \\ce{m}\\right)^{-1}=0.02\\ \\ce{kg\\ m^{-1}}$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{F}{L}=\\mu_0\\dfrac{I_1I_2}{2\\pi r} $\n\nSubstituting $F=ma$ gives\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{ma}{L}=\\mu_0\\dfrac{I_1I_2}{2\\pi r}$\n\nSubstituting values  $(\\dfrac{m}{L}=0.02\\ \\ce{kg\\ m^{-1}})$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $(0.02)a=\\dfrac{(4\\pi\\times{10}^{-7})(3)(3)}{(2\\pi)(0.015)}$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $a=\\dfrac{(4\\pi\\times{10}^{-7})(3)(3)}{(2\\pi)(0.015)(0.02)}=0.006\\ \\ce{ms^{-2}}$\n\nThe wires feel a repulsive force. This can be determined by considering how the magnetic field from one of the wires affects the other. Using the right-hand rule to determine the direction of the field from wire 1. Then using the left-hand rule to determine the direction of the force on wire 2 caused by this field and the current in wire 2."},{"ref":"PH0799","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0799\n\nThe diagram below shows the path of a charged particle as it travels through a magnetic field directed into the plane of the page.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0799.png\" alt=\"PH0799\">\n\nBelow are three statements about the particle inside the magnetic field.\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | The kinetic energy of the particle remains constant.   |\n  | II. | The force acting on the particle does not change. |\n  | III. | The particle is negatively charged. |\n\nWhich of the statements are correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"**答案：B**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nI is correct as the speed of the particle is constant (uniform circular motion) the mass is also unchanged so $E_k=\\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2$ remains constant.\n\nII is incorrect as the force changes direction as the velocity changes. The force remains perpendicular to velocity.\n\nIII is correct. Using Fleming's left-hand rule it can be deduced that the direction of the current (rate of flow of positive charge) is in the opposite direction to the path shown meaning the charge must be negative."},{"ref":"PH0800","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0800\n\nA wire of length 0.4 m lies in a horizontal magnetic field of magnetic flux density $50\\, \\mu$ T as shown. The wire is in the same plane as the paper and makes an angle of 30$\\text{\\textdegree}$ with the field.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0800.png\" alt=\"PH0800\">\n\nA current of 0.7 A is passed through the wire. What is the magnitude of the magnetic force acting on the wire?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $3.8\\times{10}^{-6}$ N\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $7.0\\times{10}^{-6}$ N\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $1.2\\times{10}^{-5}$ N\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $1.4\\times{10}^{-5}$ N","markScheme":"**答案：B**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n$F=BIL\\sin\\theta$ where $\\theta$ is the angle between the wire and the field lines (so that $I(\\sin\\theta)$  gives the component of the current traveling perpendicular to the field)\n\nGiven that $\\sin \\left(30\\right)=0.5$\n\n- $F=\\left(50\\times{10}^{-6}\\right)\\left(0.7\\right)\\left(0.4\\right)\\sin \\left(30\\right)\\ $\n\n- $\\hspace{1em} =\\left(50\\times{10}^{-6}\\right)\\left(0.7\\right)\\left(0.4\\right)\\left(0.5\\right)$\n\n- $\\hspace{1em} =7.0\\times{10}^{-6}$ N"},{"ref":"PH0801","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0801\n\nTwo parallel wires A and B are separated by a distance, $d$ and carry currents in opposite directions. The current in wire B is ten times greater than the current in wire A.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0801xxx.png\" alt=\"PH0801xxx\">\n\nWhich graph correctly identifies how the magnetic flux density, B varies with displacement traveling away from A towards B?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    | $\\hspace{2cm}$  |B.   ||\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0801EditA.png\" alt=\"PH0801EditA\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0801B.png\" alt=\"PH0801B\">|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0801C.png\" alt=\"PH0801C\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0801D.png\" alt=\"PH0801D\">|","markScheme":"**答案：A**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe wires carry current in opposite directions. Using the right-hand rule tells us that the fields of A and B are always pointing in the same direction along the line connecting the wires. Therefore, there is no change in the sign of the magnetic field so C and D can be disregarded.\n\nWire B has a greater current, so we expect a greater magnetic field close to B (compared with magnetic field close to A).\n\nTherefore, the correct graph is A.\n\nNote: C shows what the graph would look like if the current in wire B were reversed."},{"ref":"PH0802","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0802\n\nAn alpha particle is accelerated horizontally from rest through a potential difference $V$. Following its acceleration, the alpha particle immediately enters a magnetic field of magnetic flux density $B$. The magnetic field lies in the same plane as the paper and makes an angle $\\theta$ with the vertical. The alpha particle is seen to follow a helical path of radius $r$ in the magnetic field.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0802.png\" alt=\"PH0802\">\n\nWhich of the following is a correct expression for the angle $\\theta$?\n\n($u$ is the unified atomic mass unit and $e$ is the elementary charge)\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ ${\\sin}^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{2}{Br}\\sqrt{\\dfrac{uV}{e}\\ }\\right)$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ ${\\sin}^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{4}{Br}\\sqrt{\\dfrac{uV}{2e}\\ }\\right)$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ ${\\cos}^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{2}{Br}\\sqrt{\\dfrac{uV}{e}\\ }\\right)$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ ${\\cos}^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{4}{Br}\\sqrt{\\dfrac{uV}{2e}\\ }\\right)$","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAn alpha particle has a mass of $4u$ and a charge of $2e$.\n\nAs the alpha particle accelerates electric potential energy is converted into kinetic energy.\n\nWork done by the electric field as alpha particle is accelerated across the potential difference is equal to kinetic energy gained by the alpha particle:\n\n- $q\\,V=\\dfrac{1}{2\\,}mv^2$\n\n- $2\\,e\\,V=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(4u\\right)v^2 $\n\nWhere $v$ is the velocity of the alpha particle as it exits the electric field and enters the magnetic field.\n\nSimplifying gives:\n\n- $eV=uv^2$\n\nRearranging for $v$ gives:\n\n- $v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{e\\,V}{u}\\ }$\n\nThe angle $\\theta$ is such that $B\\,\\cos\\,\\theta$ gives us the component of the magnetic force acting perpendicular to the velocity of the alpha particle (upwards). So magnetic force acting on the alpha particle is given by $Bqv\\cos\\theta$.\n\n(The formula in your formula book $F=Bqv\\sin\\theta$ refers to the case where the angle $\\theta$ is the angle between the velocity and the field lines)\n\nAs the alpha particle enters the magnetic field it undergoes circular motion. The magnetic force is equal to centripetal force:\n\n- $Bqv\\cos\\,\\theta=\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}$\n\n- $Bq\\cos\\,\\theta=\\dfrac{mv}{r}$\n\n- $B\\left(2e\\right)\\cos\\,\\theta=\\dfrac{\\left(4u\\right)v}{r}$\n\nSubstituting expression for $v$ from above gives:\n\n- $Be\\,\\cos\\,\\theta=\\dfrac{2u}{r}\\sqrt{\\dfrac{eV}{u}\\ }$\n\n- $\\cos\\, \\theta=\\dfrac{2u}{rBe}\\sqrt{\\dfrac{eV}{u}\\ }$\n\nTaking $\\dfrac{u}{e}$ inside the square root gives:\n\n- $\\cos\\, \\theta=\\dfrac{2}{Br}\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{u^2}{e^2}\\right)\\dfrac{eV}{u}}$\n\n- $\\cos\\, \\theta=\\dfrac{2}{Br}\\sqrt{\\dfrac{uV}{e}}$\n\n- $\\theta={\\cos}^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{2}{Br}\\sqrt{\\dfrac{uV}{e}}\\right)$"},{"ref":"PH0803","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0803\n\nA charged particle travels at a constant horizontal velocity, $v$ inside a region where electric and magnetic fields that are oriented perpendicular to one another are present as shown.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/Screenshot%202025-01-29%20at%209.38.44%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-01-29 at 9.38.44 AM\">\n\nGravitational forces are negligible. Which of the following is a correct expression for the velocity, $v$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $v=\\dfrac{E}{B}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $v=\\dfrac{B}{E}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $v=\\dfrac{B}{E^2}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{E}{B}}$","markScheme":"**答案：A**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nElectric and magnetic fields exert forces in opposite directions. (Electric force is up and magnetic force is down if the charge is positive and vice versa.)\n\nNo deflection implies net force = 0. Magnitude of magnetic and electric forces are equal.\n\n- $F_E=E\\,q$\n- $F_B=Bqv\\,\\sin\\,\\theta=Bqv$  (Field is perpendicular to velocity so $\\sin\\,\\theta=\\sin\\,90=1$)\n\n- $Eq=Bqv$\n\nCanceling $q$’s gives\n\n- $E=B\\,v$\n\n- $v=\\dfrac{E}{B}$"},{"ref":"PH0804","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0804\n\nTwo parallel wires **A** and **B** are separated by a distance of $1.2\\ $cm as shown in the diagram. Both wires have a length of $80$ cm and wire **A** carries a current of $10\\, $A . It is observed that the wires experience a force of $1.6 \\times{10}^{-3}\\, $N towards each other.\n\nWhat is the magnitude of the current in wire **B**?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/Screenshot%202024-12-26%20at%2010.42.25%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2024-12-26 at 10.42.25 AM\">\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $6.0\\, $A\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $8.0\\,$A\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $10\\, $A\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $12\\, $A","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{F}{L}=\\mu_0\\dfrac{I_1I_2}{2\\pi r}$\n\nRearranging for $I_2$ gives:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $I_2=\\dfrac{2\\pi rF}{I_1\\mu_0L}$\n\nSubstituting values gives:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $I_2=\\dfrac{2\\pi(0.012)(1.6\\times{10}^{-3})}{(10)(4\\pi\\times{10}^{-7})(0.8)}=\\ 12\\, $A"},{"ref":"PH0805","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0805\n\nA wire PQ, is placed between the poles of a large magnet that is suspended freely from a Newton-meter as shown in the diagram. The wire is perpendicular to the magnetic field and 10 cm of the wire lies within the magnetic field. The magnetic flux density between the poles is 0.5 T. The newton-meter is held in place.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/805aaa.png\" alt=\"805aaa\">\n\nInitially, a current $I_1$ flows through the wire and the newton-meter reads $29.8\\ $N.\n\nThe current is then changed to $I_2$\n\n$I_{2\\ }$ has three times the magnitude of $I_1$ and flows in the opposite direction. The newton-meter now reads $30.2\\ $N.\n\nWhat is the magnitude and direction of the initial current, $I_1$?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Magnitude / A  | Direction |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| 2 | P to Q  |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 2 | Q to P |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 3  | P to Q |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 3  | Q to P |","markScheme":"**答案：B**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n**Direction**\n\nUsing Fleming’s left-hand rule:\n\nA current in the direction P to Q will cause an upward force on the wire. Newton’s third law (when two bodies A and B interact the force that A exerts on B is equal and opposite to the force that B exerts on A) tells us that the magnet will experience an equal and opposite force. Therefore, the magnet will experience a downward force and the reading on the newton-meter will be higher than if there were no current (if there were no current the newton-meter would simply read the weight of the magnet).\n\nA current in the direction Q to P will cause a downward force on the wire. Newton’s third law tells us that the magnet will experience an equal and opposite force. Therefore, the magnet will experience an upward force and the reading on the newton-meter will be lower than if there were no current.\n\n$I_1$ is the smaller force reading so must be flowing in the direction Q to P.\n\n$I_1$ and $I_2$ flow in different directions so the smaller current must correspond to a newton-meter reading smaller than the weight of the magnet; and the larger current must correspond to a newton-meter reading larger than the weight of the magnet.\n\n**Magnitude**\n\nForce reading on the newton-meter is the weight of the magnet ($W_m)\\, \\pm$ magnetic force.\n\n$30.2=W_m+BI_2L$   (Positive sign as magnetic force in the same direction as weight for the larger newton-meter reading)\n\n$29.8=W_m-BI_1L$  (Negative sign as magnetic force in the opposite direction of weight for the smaller newton-meter reading)\n\nSubstituting in values gives:\n\n- $30.2=W_m+\\left(0.5\\right)(3I_1)\\left(0.1\\right)=W_m+0.15I_1$\n\n- $29.8=W_m-\\left(0.5\\right)\\left(I_1\\right)\\left(0.1\\right)=W_m-0.05I_1$\n\nWe now have simultaneous equations in $W_m$ and $I_1$.\n\nSubtracting the above equations gives\n\n- $30.2-29.8=\\left(W_m+0.15I_1\\right)-(W_m-0.05I_1)$\n\n- $0.4=0.2I_1$\n\n- $I_1=2\\ $A"},{"ref":"PH0806","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0806\n\nA proton is traveling at a constant velocity downwards as shown.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0806.png\" alt=\"PH0806\">\n\nWhich combinations of directions of electric and magnetic fields could **not** cause a component of the proton's deflection to be to the right?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Electric field| Magnetic Field |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| To the left | To the right |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | To the left | Into the page |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | To the right | To the left |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | To the right| Out of the page |","markScheme":"**答案：A**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nIf either field can cause a force to the right, then we can disregard this option (as it will cause some deflection to the right).\n\nAn electric field to the right will cause a force to the right so we can disregard C and D.\n\nFleming’s left-hand rule tells us that a magnetic field into the page will cause a force to the right (current is out of the page) so we can disregard B.\n\nHence the correct answer is A."},{"ref":"PH0807","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0807\n\nA wire is placed between the poles of a large magnet and sits on a mass balance as shown in the diagram. The wire is perpendicular to the magnetic field. The mass of the magnet is $M$,  the magnetic flux density between the poles is $B$ and the length of wire that lies in the field is $L$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0807aaa.png\" alt=\"PH0807aaa\">\n\nThe graph below shows how the mass reading $m$ varies as the current in the wire $I$ is varied.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0807bbb.png\" alt=\"PH0807bbb\">\n\nWhich row gives the correct expressions for the gradient and y-intercept of the graph?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Y-intercept | Gradient|\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| Mg  | $\\dfrac{BL}{g}$ |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Mg  | $BL$  |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $M$ | $\\dfrac{BL}{g}$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $M$ | $BL$   |","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe reading shown on the mass balance multiplied by $g$ (acceleration due to gravity) is the force exerted on the balance by the magnet.\n\nForce exerted on the balance (by magnet) = weight of magnet + Magnetic force exerted on magnet by wire\n\n- $mg=Mg+B\\,I\\,L$\n\n- $m=M+\\dfrac{B\\,I\\,L}{g}$\n\nComparing this to the equation of a straight line. $m$ is analogous to $y$ as it is the variable on the vertical axis and $I$ is analogous to $x$ as it is the variable on the horizontal axis.\n\n- $y=\\left(gradient\\right)x+y\\ intercept$\n\n- $m=\\left(\\dfrac{BL}{g}\\right)I+\\ M$\n\nWe can see that $\\dfrac{BL}{g}$ is analogous to the gradient and M is analogous to the y-intercept."},{"ref":"PH0863","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0863\n\nTwo square coils, coil **A** and coil **B** are placed in a magnetic field.  Both coils carry a current. The plane of coil **A** is perpendicular to the magnetic field and the plane of coil **B** is parallel to the magnetic field as shown in the diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0863ccc.png\" alt=\"PH0863ccc\">\n\nWhich row correctly describes the motion of each of the coils due to their interaction with the magnetic field?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Coil A | Coil B\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n$\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Rotation | Linear acceleration\n$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Linear acceleration | Rotation\n$\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | No acceleration | Linear acceleration\n$\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | No acceleration | Rotation","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nCurrent will flow around the loop and have the same magnitude everywhere.\n\nUsing Fleming's left-hand rule $\\text{\\textendash}$ the force on each side of coil **A** is such that the net force and net torque on the coil are zero. Therefore there is no acceleration. The forces acting on coil **A** are illustrated below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0863msaa.png\" alt=\"PH0863msaa\">\n\nThere is no force acting on the sides of coil B where the current is parallel to the field.\n\nUsing Fleming's left-hand rule $\\text{\\textendash}$ the force on the sides of coil **B** that are perpendicular to the field are equal and opposite so net force is zero meaning no linear acceleration. However, the lines of action of the forces do not meet so there is a net torque on the coil $\\text{\\textendash}$ coil will rotate. The forces acting on coil **B** are illustrated below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0863msbb.png\" alt=\"PH0863msbb\">"},{"ref":"PH0920","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0920\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\n1. In a Bainbridge mass spectrometer, a charged particle passes through two regions. First it enters Region 1, which has combined magnetic and electric fields. After that, it proceeds into Region 2 where only a magnetic field exists.\n\n    During its journey through Region 1, the charge maintains its original path without any deviation. However, once it enters Region 2, it follows a semi-circular trajectory with a radius of $r$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0920.png\" alt=\"PH0920\">\n\n    In Region 1, the strength of the electric field is $E$ and the strength of the magnetic flux density is $B$. Express the speed $v$ of the particle in terms of $E$ and $B$.          [2 marks]\n\n2. Deduce how the kinetic energy of the particle changes in Region 2.    [2 marks]\n\n3. The following data is available:\n\n    - $v=1.0\\times{10}^5\\, \\ce{m\\,s^{-1}}$\n\n    - $E=1.0\\times{10}^4\\, \\ce{V\\,m^{-1}}$\n\n    - $B=0.10$ T\n\n    - $r=0.25$ m\n\n    Determine the charge-to-mass ratio of the charged particle.      [3 marks]\n\n4. In another experiment,  Helmholtz coils are used to create a uniform magnetic field. A Helmholtz coil consists of two identical circular coils with the same magnitude of current, separated by a specific distance and aligned coaxially as shown below.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0920d.png\" alt=\"PH0920d\">\n\n    A leftward magnetic field is obtained at point A, the midpoint between the coils. Deduce the direction of the force acting on coil B.      [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. $q\\,v\\,B=q\\,E$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $v=\\dfrac{E}{B}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    As the charge passes through the first region, it experiences two forces: one magnetic $F_B$ and one electric $F_E$. To enable the charge to travel without deviating from its path, these two forces must have equal magnitudes but act in opposite directions, effectively cancelling each other out:\n\n    - $F_B=F_E$\n\n    - $q\\,v\\,B=q\\,E$\n\n    - $v=\\dfrac{E}{B}$\n\n2. No change/constant\n\n    The resultant force/magnetic force is perpendicular to the motion of the particle $\\checkmark$\n\n    No work done\n\n    **OR**\n\n    No increase in speed $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    There is only one force acting on the charge, magnetic force. This force always acts at a right angle (perpendicular) to the direction of the charge's movement at any given moment. Consequently, no work is being done on the charge, meaning there's no change in its kinetic energy. Alternatively, there is no component of the resultant force on the direction of the motion of the particle. Therefore, there is no acceleration or deceleration.\n\n3. $q\\,v\\,B=m\\,\\frac{v^2}{r}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\dfrac{q}{m}=\\dfrac{1.0\\times{10}^5}{(0.25)(0.1)}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    :answer[$4.0\\times{10}^6\\ \\ce{C\\,{kg}^{-1}}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The magnetic force $F_B$ acting on the charge is the provider of centripetal force in the second region:\n\n    - $F_B=q\\,v\\,B=m\\,\\dfrac{v^2}{r}$\n\n    - $\\dfrac{q}{m}=\\dfrac{v}{r\\,B}$\n\n    When the given values are substituted:\n\n    - $\\dfrac{q}{m}=\\dfrac{1.0\\times{10}^5}{(0.25)(0.1)}=4.0\\times{10}^6\\ \\ce{C\\,{kg}^{-1}}$\n\n4. The current directions must be the same to avoid cancellation $\\checkmark$\n\n    parallel currents attract each other/to  the left $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Because the currents in both coils have equal magnitudes, and the distance between point A and the coils is the same, the current directions must be the same to avoid cancellation of the magnetic field at point A. With parallel currents, the coils should experience an attractive force. This implies that the force acting on coil B is directed to the left."},{"ref":"PH0945","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0945\n\nParticle A is travelling in a magnetic field with a speed $v$. Particle A makes an angle of 30$\\text{\\textdegree}$ to the field. Particle B is travelling in the same field and makes an angle of 60$\\text{\\textdegree}$ to the field. This is illustrated below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0945.png\" alt=\"PH0945\">\n\nIt is given that both particles have the same charge and experience the same force. What is the speed of particle B?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $v\\,\\sin (30\\text{\\textdegree})$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $v\\,\\sin (60\\text{\\textdegree})$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$  $\\dfrac{v\\,\\sin\\, (60\\text{\\textdegree})}{\\sin\\, (30\\text{\\textdegree})}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{v\\,\\sin\\, (30\\text{\\textdegree})}{\\sin\\, (60\\text{\\textdegree})}$","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n$F=Bqv \\sin\\,\\theta$\n\nWhere $\\theta$ is the angle between the velocity of the particle and the field (such that $v\\,\\sin\\,\\theta$ is the component of the velocity that is perpendicular to the field).\n\n- $v\\,\\sin\\,\\theta=\\dfrac{F}{q\\,B}$\n\n$F,\\ B$ and $q$ are constant so we may write.\n\n- $v\\,\\sin\\,\\theta=constant$\n\nTherefore\n\n- $v_A\\,\\sin\\,\\theta_A=v_B\\,\\sin\\,\\theta_B$\n\n- $v\\,\\sin(30\\text{\\textdegree})=v_B\\,\\sin(60\\text{\\textdegree})$\n\n- $v_B= \\dfrac {v\\,\\sin(30\\text{\\textdegree})}{\\sin(60\\text{\\textdegree})}$"},{"ref":"PH0960","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0960\n\nThe diagram below shows a square coil ABCD in a magnetic field. The plane of the coil is parallel to a magnetic field of magnetic flux density $1$ mT. The area of the coil is $25\\, \\ce{cm^2}$. A clockwise current of 1 A flows through the coil.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH0960aa2.png\" alt=\"PH0960aa2\">\n\nWhat is the magnitude and direction of the force acting on side AB?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Magnitude of force / N | Direction of force on AB|\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $2.5\\times{10}^{-6}$  | Into the page |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $$2.5\\times{10}^{-6}$$   | Out of the page |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $5\\times{10}^{-5}$    | Into the page |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $5\\times{10}^{-5}$   | Out of the page |","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n$F=BIL\\,\\sin\\theta=BIL$ (as field and AB are perpendicular)\n\n- $B=1\\times{10}^{-3}$ T\n- $I=1$ A\n\nLength of one side of the coil = $\\sqrt{25\\ \\ce{cm^2}}=5\\ \\ce{cm=0.05}\\ \\ce{m}$\n\n- $L=0.05$ m\n\n- $F=BIL=\\left(1\\times{10}^{-3}\\right)\\left(1\\right)\\left(0.05\\right)=\\ 5\\times{10}^{-5}$ N\n\nUsing Fleming's left-hand rule:\n\nCurrent flows from B to A - magnetic field is to the right - so force is into the page."},{"ref":"PH1062","marks":"11","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1062\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\n1. A charged particle is in an electric field. Describe how the magnitude and direction of the electric force on the particle is determined from the electric field. [2 marks]\n\n2. 1. Electric field strength is typically measured in $\\ce{N\\,C^{-1}}$. Show that an equivalent unit for electric field strength is $\\ce{V\\,m^{-1}}$. [1 mark]\n\n    2. An electron is accelerated between two parallel charged plates from rest to $8.3\\times 10^6\\, \\ce{m\\,s^{-1}}$. Determine the potential difference between the plates. [3 marks]\n\n3. A mass spectrometer is an instrument that is used to identify charged particles, such as ions. Charged particles are emitted from a source, and accelerated to a pre-determined speed ($v$) by an electric field. The resulting high-velocity beam enters a magnetic field ($B$) at right angles, and is then deflected into a circular path onto a detector, as shown in the diagram below:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH1062aaa.png\" alt=\"PH1062aaa\">\n\n    Particles are identified by their mass-charge ratio $\\Bigl(\\dfrac{m}{q}\\Bigr)$.\n\n    Determine the mass-charge ratio for an ion that entered the magnetic field ($B = 0.400\\, \\ce{T}$) of a mass spectrometer at $3.2\\times 10^4\\, \\ce{m\\,s^{-1}}$ and has been deflected into a circular path of diameter $d = 6.2$ cm. [3 marks]\n\n4. 1. As above, a mass spectrometer is used to determine the mass-charge ratio $\\Bigl(\\dfrac{m}{q}\\Bigr)$ of a charged particle. Describe the relationship between the radius of the observed circular path and the mass-charge ratio. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Referring to the diagram above, suggest a way to determine the nature of the charge on the particle. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. The electric force ($F$) is equal to the product of the electric field strength ($E$) and the charge of the particle ($q$) $\\checkmark$\n\n    Note: Accept $F = Eq$ as long as variables are specified.\n\n    A positive charge has the force in the same direction as the electric field / a negative charge has the force in the opposite direction to the electric field. OWTTE $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    By convention, the direction of the electric field is defined as the direction of the force acting on a positive test charge.\n\n2. 1. One volt is a joule per coulomb and a joule is a newton metre\n\n        ($1\\ce{V} = 1\\,\\dfrac{\\,\\ce{J}}{\\ce{C}}\\,/\\,1\\,\\ce{C} = 1\\,\\dfrac{\\ce{J}}{\\ce{V}}$) and ($1\\,\\ce{J} = 1\\,\\ce{N}\\,\\ce{m}$ / $1\\, \\ce{N} = 1\\,\\dfrac{\\ce{J}}{\\ce{m}}$)  $\\checkmark$\n\n        Substituting into $\\dfrac{\\ce{N}}{\\ce{C}}$ Gives, $(\\dfrac{\\ce{J}}{\\ce{m}})(\\dfrac{V}{\\ce{J}}) = \\ce{V\\,m^{-1}}$.\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        Electric field strength, $E$, is defined as the size of the electric force, $F$, acting on a unit charge, $q$:\n\n        - $E=\\dfrac{F}{q}$\n\n        Resulting in the units of $\\ce{N\\,C^{-1}}$ for electric field strength.\n\n        Work done can be found from $W=Fs=qEs$\n\n        And work done on a charge is also $W=q\\Delta V$\n\n        Setting the two equal gives\n\n        - $qEs=q\\Delta V$\n\n        - $E=\\dfrac{\\Delta V}{s}$\n\n        which shows that the units on electric field strength can also be expressed as $\\ce{V\\,m^{-1}}$.\n\n    2. $<\\Delta E_k = -\\Delta E_p=W>$\n\n        $\\dfrac{1}{2}m v^2=q \\Delta V$\n\n        OR\n\n        $\\Delta V=\\dfrac{mv^2}{2q}$   $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\dfrac{1}{2}(9.110\\times 10^{-31})(8.3\\times 10^6)^2=(1.60\\times 10^{-19})\\Delta V$\n\n        OR\n\n        $\\Delta V= \\dfrac{(9.110\\times 10^{-31})(8.3\\times 10^6)^2}{2(1.60\\times 10^{-19})}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\Delta V= 196\\,(\\ce{V})= \\ll 200\\,\\ce{V} \\gg$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        Note: Must show unrounded value for MP3.\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        The electric force, $F$, acting on an electron of charge $q$ in an electric field with field strength, $E$,\n\n        - $F=Eq$\n\n        The electric field, $E$, between two parallel charged plates is created by a high voltage $V$, applied across a small distance, $d$,\n\n        - $E = \\dfrac{\\Delta V}{d}$\n\n        Substituting into the expression for electric force,\n\n        - $F=\\dfrac{\\Delta V}{d}q$\n\n        The work, $W$, done by the electric field and further substitution shows,\n\n        - $W=Fd= \\dfrac{\\Delta Vq}{d}d=q\\Delta V$\n\n        Conservation of Energy means that the kinetic energy gained by the accelerated electron $(E_k=\\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2)$ equals the work done by the electric field.\n\n        - $\\Delta E_k= -\\Delta E_p=W$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2=q\\Delta V$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{1}{2}(9.110\\times 10^{-31})(8.3\\times 10^6)^2=(1.60\\times 10^{-19})\\Delta V$\n\n        - $\\Delta V=\\dfrac{(9.110\\times 10^{-31})(8.3\\times 10^6)^2}{2(1.60\\times 10^{-19})}$\n\n        - $\\Delta V= 196\\,(\\ce{V})= 200\\, \\ce{V}$(2sf)\n\n        It is assumed that that the only force acting on the electron is the electric force, or all other forces are negligible.\n\n3. Use of\n\n    - $F_B= F_C$\n\n    OR\n\n    - $qvB=\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    Substitution with correct values,\n\n    - $<\\dfrac{m}{q}=\\dfrac{Br}{v}> $\n\n    - $\\dfrac{m}{q}=\\dfrac{0.400(3.1\\times 10^{-2})}{(3.2\\times 10^4)}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $\\dfrac{m}{q}=3.9\\times 10^{-7}\\, \\ce{kg\\,C^{-1}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    Since the ion enters the magnetic field at right angles to the field, the magnetic force acts perpendicular to the velocity of the particle ($\\theta=90\\degree$).\n\n    The magnetic force is given by $F_B=qvB\\sin{\\theta}$ which simplifies to $F_B=qvB$ since $\\sin{(90\\degree)}=1$.\n\n    This perpendicular magnetic force, $F_B$, acts as a centripetal force, $F_C$, to produce the circular path.\n\n    Therefore,\n\n    - $F_B= F_C$\n\n    Centripetal force is given by,\n\n    - $F_C=\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}$\n\n    So,\n\n    - $qvB= \\dfrac{mv^2}{r}$\n\n    Rearrange to determine mass-charge ratio, $\\dfrac{m}{q}$, and substitute values,\n\n    - $\\dfrac{m}{q}= \\dfrac{Br}{v}$\n\n    - $\\dfrac{m}{q}=\\dfrac{0.400(3.1\\times 10^{-2})}{3.2\\times 10^4}$\n\n    - $\\dfrac{m}{q}=3.9\\times 10^{-7}\\, \\ce{kg\\,C^{-1}}$\n\n4. 1. mass-charge ratio $\\Bigl(\\dfrac{m}{q}\\Bigr)$ is $\\underline{\\text{proportional}}$ to radius ($r$) $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        From part c,\n\n        - $\\dfrac{m}{q}=\\dfrac{Br}{v}$\n\n        Therefore,\n\n        - $\\dfrac{m}{q} \\propto r$\n\n    2. Magnetic field into page detects positive charge.  Reverse magnetic field direction to out of page to detect negative charge   $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        Applying Fleming's left-hand rule (or right-hand slap rule), the counter-clockwise motion shown in the diagram applies to a positive charge.\n\n        Reversing the direction of the field (i.e. out of the page) will mean that a negative charge will also follow a counter-clockwise motion and so be detected."},{"ref":"PH1822","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1822\n\nThe diagram shows the cross section of a current carrying wire in a magnetic field of flux density $0.5$ T. The only force acting on the wire is the force due to the magnetic field which causes it to accelerate to the right at a rate of $0.4$ m s$^{-2}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/PH1822q.JPEG\" alt=\"PH1822q\">\n\nThe mass per unit length of the wire is $0.02$ kg m$^{-1}$. Which of the following correctly shows the magnitude and direction of the current in the wire?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Magnitude / $\\textrm{mA}$ | Direction |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| 16 | Into the page |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 16 | Out of the page |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 64 | Into the page |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 64 | Out of the page |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n**Magnitude**\n\nThe force $F$ experienced by a current carrying wire in a magnetic field is given by:\n\n- $F=BIL\\textrm{sin}\\,\\theta$\n\nWhere $B$ is the magnetic flux density of the field, $I$ is the current in the wire, $L$ is the length of the wire and, $\\theta$ is the angle between $B$ and $I$.\n\nHere $B$ and $I$ are perpendicular so we have $\\textrm{sin}\\,\\theta=\\textrm{sin}\\,90\\degree=1$\n\n- $F=BIL$\n\nSubstituting $F=ma$ gives:\n\n- $ma=BIL$\n\n- $\\dfrac{m}{L}a=BI$\n\n$\\dfrac{m}{L}$ is mass per unit length. Substituting our values and rearranging for $I$, we have:\n\n- $(0.02)(0.4)=(0.5)I$\n\n- $I=0.016$ A $=16$ mA\n\n**Direction**\n\nWe can deduce the direction of the current using Fleming's left hand rule. According to the rule, align your left hand such that the index finger points in the direction of the magnetic field (upward), and your thumb in the direction of the force (to the right - the same as the acceleration). Your middle finger then points in the direction of the current (into the page)."},{"ref":"PH1870","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1870\n\nA charged particle moves at a constant velocity through a region where the electric and magnetic fields are perpendicular to each other and to the direction of motion. The magnitude of the electric field strength in N C$^{-1}$ is 1000 times greater than that of the magnetic field strength in T.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/ph1870.png\" alt=\"ph1870\">\n\nWhat is the magnitude of the particle's velocity?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.001 m s$^{-1}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1 m s$^{-1}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1000 m s$^{-1}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ More information is needed","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nIn order for the particle to travel at a constant velocity (constant speed and no deflection) the net force acting on it must be zero. This means that the magnetic and electric forces must be equal and opposite.\n\nEquating the electric and magnetic forces we have:\n\n- $Bqv=Eq$\n\nCancelling a factor of $q$ and rearranging for $v$ we have:\n\n- $Bv=E$\n\n- $v=\\dfrac{E}{B}$\n\nThe question tells us that $E$ (in N C$^{-1}$) is 1000 times greater than $B$ (in T) so we have:\n\n- $v=\\dfrac{E}{B}=1000$ m s$^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH1872","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1872\n\nA current $I$ flows around a square coil of side lengths $l$. The two opposite sides of the coil are labelled $X$ and $Y$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/Screenshot%202025-08-26%20at%203.00.06%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-08-26 at 3.00.06 PM\">\n\nWhich of the following correctly states the magnitude of the force acting on $X$ and the nature of the force between $X$ and $Y$?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Magnitude of force on side $X$ $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Nature of force between $X$ and $Y$ $\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\dfrac{\\mu_0 I^2}{2\\pi}$ | Attractive |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{\\mu_0 I^2}{2\\pi}$ | Repulsive |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{\\mu_0 I^2}{2\\pi l}$ | Attractive |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{\\mu_0 I^2}{2\\pi l}$ | Repulsive |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe force $F$ per unit length $l$ acting on a pair of parallel wires of separation $r$ carrying currents $I_1$ and $I_2$ is given by:\n\n- $\\dfrac{F}{l}=\\dfrac{\\mu_0I_1I_2}{2\\pi r}$\n\nWhere $\\mu_0$ is the permeability of free space $=1.26\\times10^{-6}$ N A$^{-2}$.\n\nEach wire carries a current $I$ and each pair of wires is separated by a distance $l$ so we have:\n\n- $\\dfrac{F}{l}=\\dfrac{\\mu_0(I)(I)}{2\\pi l}$\n\n- $F=\\dfrac{\\mu_0 I^2}{2\\pi}$\n\nNow let us determine whether the force between an adjacent pair of wires is repulsive or attractive.\n\nConsider the wire $X$, with a current flowing upwards. The wire $Y$ has a current flowing downwards.\nWe can determine the magnetic field produced by $X$ using the right-hand grip rule. Point your right thumb upwards in the direction of the current in $X$. Your fingers curl around the wire, showing the direction of the magnetic field. To the right of wire $X$, where wire $Y$ sits, the magnetic field points into the page.\n\nNow apply Fleming’s left-hand rule to $Y$. The magnetic field at the position of $Y$ is into the page, and the current in $Y$ flows downwards. With the first finger pointing out of the page and the second finger pointing downwards, the thumb points to the right. This means $Y$ experiences a force pushing it away from $X$.\n\nBy the same reasoning, the left wire experiences a repulsive force from the right wire."},{"ref":"PH1901","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1901\n\nThe diagram below shows an electron of mass $m_e$ entering a region where a constant magnetic field of magnitude $B$ is present.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Fields/Motion%20in%20Electromagnetic%20Fields/images/ph1901q.png\" alt=\"ph1901q\">\n$\\small{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra, - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\nGiven that the electron is moving at a speed $v$, which of the following correctly describes the direction and magnitude of the acceleration experienced by the electron?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Direction | Magnitude |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| up | $\\dfrac{m_evB}{e}$ |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | down | $\\dfrac{m_evB}{e}$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | up | $\\dfrac{evB}{m_e}$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | down | $\\dfrac{evB}{m_e}$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAccording to Fleming's left-hand rule, a positively charged particle moving to the right (middle finger) in a magnetic field directed into the screen (index finger) will experience a force acting upwards (thumb).\n\nSince the particle in question is an electron, which carries a negative charge, the force direction is reversed and will therefore act downwards. Consequently, the acceleration of the electron will also be directed downwards.\n\nTo determine the magnitude of the acceleration we can use Newton's second law.\n\n- $F=m_ea$\n\nThe electron is initially moving with a velocity that is perpendicular to the magnetic field. The magnitude of the force will then be given by $F=eVB$.\n\nCombining these two expressions leads to:\n\n- $eVB=m_ea$\n\nWhich can be solved to find the magnitude of the acceleration:\n\n- $a=\\dfrac{{eV}B}{m_e}$"}]},{"name":"Unassigned","questions":[{"ref":"PH0590","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0590\n\nEstimate the speed of an alpha particle that has accelerated from rest in a uniform electric field $E = 50\\,$N C$^{-1}$ over a distance of 2.0 m.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $10^3 \\ $m s$^{-1}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $10^4 \\ $m s$^{-1}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $10^5 \\ $m s$^{-1}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $10^9 \\ $m s$^{-1}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nUsing $E=\\ \\dfrac{F}{q}$ from the data booklet, as well as $v^2=u^2+2as$ and $F=ma$,\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2em}v^2&=u^2+2as \\\\\nv&=\\sqrt{2as} \\\\\n\\hspace{2em}\\\\\nF&=ma \\\\\na&=\\ \\frac{F}{m} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}$\n\nGiving\n$v=\\sqrt{2\\dfrac{F}{m}s}$\n\nAnd $F=qE$\n\nTherefore, $v=\\sqrt{2\\dfrac{qE}{m}s}$\n\nthe speed of the alpha particle is\n\n- $v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2Eqs}{m}}\\approx\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2(50\\,\\ce{N C}^{-1})(3.2 \\times  {10}^{-19}\\,\\ce{C})(2\\ \\ce{m})}{6.4 \\times  {10}^{-27}\\,\\ce{kg}}}$\n\nTherefore\n\n- $v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{6.4\\ \\times \\ {10}^{-17}\\ \\ce{ N m}}{6.4\\ \\times \\ {10}^{-27}\\ \\ce{kg}}}=\\sqrt{{10}^{10}\\ \\dfrac{\\ce{m}^2}{\\ce{s}^2}}={10}^5\\ $m s$^{-1}$"}]}]},{"name":"Nuclear & Quantum Physics","subtopics":[{"name":"Atomic Structure","questions":[{"ref":"PH0054","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0054\n\nThe diagram gives energy levels of a single-electron atom. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/Screenshot%202026-01-06%20at%206.02.39%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-01-06 at 6.02.39 PM\">\n\nHow many different wavelengths can be detected in the emission spectrum of the atom?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 4\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 5\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 9\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 10","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nWith five energy levels there are 10 possible transitions, but in this case the transitions from $n=5$ to $n=3$ and $n=3$ to $n=2$ are identical in energy difference (1.3 eV). Therefore, there are 9 different transitions with different wavelengths."},{"ref":"PH0090","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0090\n\nAn electron in a hydrogen atom is excited from the ground state of $n=1$ to the $n=4$ level. What is the total number of frequencies that can be observed from the emission spectrum as the electron returns to ground state?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 12\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 6\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 4\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 3","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nCount all possible de-excitations below $n=4$. \\\n\nFrom $n = 4$ electron can de-excite to $n = 3, 2, 1$; 3 possible frequencies. \\\n\nFrom $n = 3$ electron can de-excite to $n = 2, 1$; 2 possible frequencies. \\\n\nFrom $n = 2$ electron can de-excite to $n = 1$; 1 possible frequency. \n\nTotal of 6 frequencies."},{"ref":"PH0111","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0111\n\nA student investigates the scattering of alpha particles. Initially, she uses a gold foil ($Z$ = 79) and next she repeats the experiment using an aluminum ($Z$ = 13) foil of the same thickness. The scattered alpha particles are observed on a detector that fluoresces.\n\nBelow are three statements about the switch from the gold foil to the aluminum foil.\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | The alpha particles deflect more from the gold nucleus than the aluminum.   |\n  | II. | The alpha particles approach the gold nucleus closer than the aluminum nucleus. |\n  | III. | There is no change in the observed scattering pattern. |\n\nWhich of the statements are correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ II and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe aluminum nucleus has fewer protons than the gold nucleus hence the effects of repulsion are less for the aluminum nucleus when compared with the gold.\n\nThe particles will be able to have a closer approach to the aluminum, and the gold will cause more scattering than the aluminum due to its stronger charge."},{"ref":"PH0233","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0233\n\nSome of the energy levels of electrons of a mercury atom are as shown. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/Screenshot%202026-01-06%20at%206.00.27%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-01-06 at 6.00.27 PM\">\n\nElectrons and photons are incident on a mercury vapour causing energy level transitions. Photons emitted from the vapour due to the transition of electrons between energy levels have an energy of 4.9 eV. \n\nWhich of the following could be incident into mercury vapour? \n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n\n  | | |\n\n  | :-- | :-- |\n\n  | I. | An electron with an energy of 5.1 eV |\n\n  | II. | A photon with an energy of 4.9 eV |\n\n  | III. | A photon with an energy of 5.1 eV |\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe energy difference between the ground state and the first excited state of an electron is given by \n\n- $-5.5 - (-10.4) = 4.9$ eV\n\nIn order to have emitted photons with an energy of 4.9 eV, electrons must be excited. Incident electrons can excite electrons in orbits with more than sufficient energy while photons need to have the exact amount of energy for moving an electron from one state to another. Therefore a photon with an energy of 5.1 eV cannot excite the electrons but others can. So, the answer is C."},{"ref":"PH0299","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0299\n\nThe diagram shows atomic energy levels of an element, and wavelengths are labeled corresponding to emission for electron transition between certain energy levels. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/Screenshot%202026-01-05%20at%2012.29.46%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-01-05 at 12.29.46 PM\">\n\nWhich of the following shows these wavelengths in decreasing order?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\lambda_1,\\,\\lambda_2,\\,\\lambda_3,\\,\\lambda_4$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\lambda_4,\\,\\lambda_3,\\,\\lambda_2,\\,\\lambda_1$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\lambda_1,\\,\\lambda_4,\\,\\lambda_3,\\,\\lambda_2$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\lambda_2,\\,\\lambda_3,\\,\\lambda_4,\\,\\lambda_1$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe lengths of the arrows represent the amount of energy released in the transition. \n\nFrom the formula \n\n- $E = \\dfrac{hc}{\\lambda}$\n\nIt is seen that the energy of a photon is inversely proportional to its wavelength, therefore the decreasing order of wavelengths will be the increasing order of energies, which is given in C. \n\nThe lowest energy transition is shown as $\\lambda_1$, therefore the $\\lambda_1$ photon will have the longest wavelength.\n\n$\\lambda_2$ has the largest energy and therefore will have the shortest wavelength."},{"ref":"PH0341","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0341\n\nThe electron in the ground state of a hydrogen atom absorbs a certain amount of energy. The diagram shows some of the energy levels of the hydrogen atom. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/PH0341.png\" alt=\"PH0341\">\n\nAt which state would the electron be if it absorbs 12.09 eV of energy?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ At ground state.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ First excited state.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Second excited state.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Third excited state.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe electron is in ground state, which is $n=1$. \\\n\nIt absorbs 12.09 eV of energy, which is the exact difference between $n=1$ and $n=3$ on the diagram. Therefore, the electron will be in $n=3$, which is the second excited state."},{"ref":"PH0379","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0379\n\nWhich of the following correctly states the conclusion drawn from the Rutherford-Geiger-Marsden experiment?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The nucleus of an atom contains protons and neutrons.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Protons and electrons have equal but opposite charges.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ The nucleus contains nearly the entire mass of the atom.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Gold atoms absorb alpha particles.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nA. $\\hspace{1em}$ At the time of this experiment (1911), neither protons nor neutrons had been discovered. \n\nB. $\\hspace{1em}$ This experiment did not reveal anything about electrons. \n\nC. $\\hspace{1em}$ This is the correct answer. The conclusion from the alpha particle scattering experiment was that there are high concentrations of positive charge and mass in the middle of an atom, with most of the atom being empty space. \n\nD. $\\hspace{1em}$ This experiment was not specific to gold. Gold foil was used in the experiment but the experiment was about the structure of matter in general. There was also no absorption observed."},{"ref":"PH0456","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0456\n\nThe nuclear energy levels of a nucleus are shown below. K, L, and M are transitions between energy levels.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/Screenshot%202025-09-30%20at%203.20.21%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-09-30 at 3.20.21 PM\">\n\nConsider the following statements about photons emitted during transitions K, L, and M?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | The wavelength of the photon emitted by transition K is greater than the wavelength of the photon emitted by transition L.   |\n  | II. | The frequency of the photon emitted by transition K is equal to the frequency of the photon emitted by transition M. |\n  | III. | The wavelength of the photon emitted by transition L is greater than the wavelength of the photon emitted by transition M. |\n\nWhich statements are correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I and II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe energy of the photon emitted = $hf$\n\nDuring transition K the energy released = $5.1- 3.1 = 2.0$ MeV\n\nDuring transition L the energy released = $4.7 - 1.1 = 3.6$ MeV\n\nDuring transition M the energy released = $3.1 - 1.1 = 2.0$  MeV\n\nTherefore,\n\n- $f_L > f_k = f_m$\n\nand\n\n- $\\lambda_M=\\lambda_K>\\lambda_L$"},{"ref":"PH0467","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0467\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe diagram shows six energy levels of hydrogen.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/Screenshot%202025-09-30%20at%203.24.20%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-09-30 at 3.24.20 PM\">\n\n1. Describe how an emission spectrum is evidence of discrete energy levels in an atom. [2 marks]\n\n2. \n\n    1. On the energy level diagram, draw an arrow to represent the transition that emits the shortest wavelength. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Calculate the shortest emitted wavelength. [3 marks]\n\n3. Emitted red light has a frequency estimation of 460 THz. Determine which energy level transition emits this red light.    [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Photons are emitted in transitions of electrons from higher energy levels to lower ones. $\\checkmark$ \\\n\n    Because only specific wavelengths/energies are emitted, this indicates that there are a finite number of energy levels that exist $\\checkmark$ \\\n\n    (1 mark max awarded if absorption spectrum is described)\n\n2. \n\n    1. Downward arrow. $\\checkmark$ \\\n\n        From the sixth energy level to ground level. $\\checkmark$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/Screenshot%202025-09-30%20at%203.29.45%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-09-30 at 3.29.45 PM\">\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        To have the shortest wavelength this means the highest energy photon.\n\n        - $\\lambda=\\dfrac{hc}{E}$\n\n        To emit such a photon, the electron will go from the last energy level to the ground state.\n\n    2. ::indent\n\n        - $E=\\ll -0.38-(-13.60)\\gg$\n\n        - $E=13.22\\ll \\text{eV}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\lambda=\\ll \\dfrac{hc}{E}\\gg$ \n\n        - $\\lambda =\\dfrac{(6.63\\, \\times\\, 10^{-34})(3\\, \\times\\, 10^8)}{(13.22)(1.6\\, \\times\\, 10^{-19})}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - :answer[$\\lambda =9.40 \\times 10^{-8}\\ll \\text{m}\\gg$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The energy difference from $n=6$ to $n=1$\n\n        - $E=-0.38-(-13.60)=13.22$ eV\n\n        To calculate the shortest wavelength\n\n        \n\n        - $\\lambda=\\dfrac{hc}{E}$\n\n        Remember to convert eV to joules.\n\n        - $\\lambda =\\dfrac{(6.63 \\,\\times\\, 10^{-34})(3 \\,\\times \\,10^8)}{(13.22)(1.6\\, \\times\\, 10^{-19})} = 9.40 \\times 10^{-8}$ m\n\n3. ::indent\n\n    - $\\ll E= hf\\gg$\n\n    - $E=\\dfrac{(6.63\\, \\times\\, 10^{-34})(460 \\,\\times \\,10^{12})}{(1.6\\, \\times\\, 10^{-19})}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $E=1.9\\ll \\text{eV}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - From $n=3$ to $n=2$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    We need to calculate the energy required to emit a red light,\n\n    - $\\begin{aligned}\n\n    E&=hf\\\\\n\n    &=(6.63 \\times 10^{-34})(460 \\times 10^{12})\\\\\n\n    &=3.05 \\times 10^{-19}\\,\\text{J}\\\\\n\n    &=1.9\\,\\text{eV}\n\n    \\end{aligned}$\n\n    Investigating the energy differences, we can tell that the transition from $n = 3$ to $n = 2$ gives that energy difference\n\n    \n\n    - $E=-1.51-(-3.40)=1.89$ eV"},{"ref":"PH0696-2","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0696-2\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nOn a sunny day, a house is powered by a solar panel. In order to run home appliances, the incident power of sunlight on the solar panel must be 10 kW.\n\n1. The average surface temperature of the Sun is 5850 K. Show that the peak wavelength of the emission of the sun is around 500 nm. [2 marks]\n\n2. It can be assumed that 7.5$\\%$ of the energy from incident sunlight consists of peak wavelength photons that are converted to electrical energy by the panel. Calculate the number of 500 nm photons that must strike the solar panel per second in order to meet the power requirement of the appliances. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{2.90\\times{10}^{-3}}{5850}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $496\\, \\ll$nm$\\gg\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nBy using Wien\\'s displacement law,\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\lambda_{max}=\\dfrac{2.90\\times{10}^{-3}}{5850}=496\\, $nm\n\n2. Energy of the 500 nm photons in one second\n$=\\left(10000\\right)\\left(0.075\\right)\\left(1\\right)=750\\, $J $\\checkmark$\n\n    Energy per 500 nm photon=$\\dfrac{(6.63\\times{10}^{-34})(3.0\\times{10}^8)}{500\\times{10}^{-9}}=3.98\\times{10}^{-19}\\, $J $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $n=\\dfrac{750}{(3.98\\times{10}^{-19})}=$ :answer[$1.9\\times{10}^{21}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Total power of the 500 nm photons = $\\left(10000\\right)\\left(0.075\\right)=750\\, $W\n\n    The energy $E$ received by the solar panel due to 500 nm photons in one second:\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $E=Pt=\\left(750\\right)\\left(1\\right)=750\\, J$\n\n    The energy $E_p$ of one 500 nm photon:\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $E_p=\\dfrac{hc}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{(6.63\\times{10}^{-34})(3.0\\times{10}^8)}{500\\times{10}^{-9}}=3.98\\times{10}^{-19}\\ $J\n\n    The number $n$ of 500 nm photons:\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $n=\\dfrac{750}{(3.98\\times{10}^{-19})}=19\\times{10}^{20}$"},{"ref":"PH0886","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0886\n\nThe diagram below shows three energy levels within a hydrogen atom. An electron in energy state $E_1$ makes a transition to one of the other energy states shown. This transition involves a photon of the lowest possible frequency.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/Screenshot%202026-01-05%20at%2012.31.21%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-01-05 at 12.31.21 PM\">\n\nWhich row is correct about the photon associated with this transition?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1.5em}$ Frequency of the photon $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$ Photon absorbed/emitted $\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\dfrac{(E_2-E_1)}{h}$ |Absorbed |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{(E_2-E_1)}{h}$ | Emitted |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{(E_3-E_1)}{h}$ | Absorbed |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{(E_3-E_1)}{h}$ | Emitted |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe energy of the photon is given by $E=hf $\n\n$E \\propto f$ so, the lowest frequency transition will be one with lowest energy difference involving $E_1$. This is the difference between $E_1$ and $E_2$. All energy states are negative and $E_1$ is the lowest. So $E_2-E_1$ gives a positive value.\n\n- $f=\\dfrac{(E_2-E_1)}{h}$\n\nElectron jumps to a higher energy level, so photon is absorbed (energy of the photon is converted into electric potential energy of electron)."},{"ref":"PH0903","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0903\n\nThe diagram below shows some of the discrete energy levels in the Bohr model. The energies shown are of the fundamental and first three excited states for an electron in the hydrogen atom.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/Screenshot%202026-01-05%20at%2011.29.54%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-01-05 at 11.29.54 AM\">\n\nA photon with an energy of 2.0 eV is incident on a hydrogen atom. Considering only the energy levels shown in the diagram above, which of the following correctly describes the transitions between states that would be possible for an electron?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ No transitions possible\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $n=3$ to $n=4$ only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $n=2$ to $n=3$ only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Both from $n=2$ to $n=3$ and $n=3$ to $n=4$","markScheme":"**答案：A**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nIn Bohr's model for the hydrogen atom, an electron can transition between two states only if the photon has the exact energy required for the transition.\n\nGiven that the energy of the photon is 2.0 eV and that there are no two energy states with a difference of 2.0 eV, no transitions between energy levels shown in the diagram are possible for an electron."},{"ref":"PH0998","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0998\n\nBelow are three statements about the Rutherford-Geiger-Marsden experiment:\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | It provided evidence that the atom is mostly empty space   |\n  | II. | It provided evidence that atoms have a small positively charged center  |\n  | III. | It provided evidence that electrons exist in discrete energy levels |\n\nWhich of the statements are correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nIn this experiment, alpha particles were fired at a thin sheet of gold to investigate the structure of the atom.\n\nI is correct $-$ Most alpha particles passed straight through the gold sheet providing evidence that the atom is mostly empty space.\n\nII is correct $-$ Some alpha particles (which were known to be positively charged) were deflected at large angles, providing evidence the atom contains a small concentration of positive charge.\n\nIII is incorrect $-$ The electron had not been discovered at the time of this experiment, work involving energy levels came later."},{"ref":"PH1015-2","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1015-2\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nThe diagram shows the emission spectrum of hydrogen atoms when electrons make a transition from different excited states to the second excited state.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/1015aa%20copy.png\" alt=\"1015aa copy\">\n\n1. Outline how the emission spectrum of hydrogen provides evidence for the discrete energy levels of electrons.[2 marks]\n\n2. Determine, from the information given in the diagram,  the energy difference between the 3rd  and 2nd  excited states of the electron. Give your answer in units of joules. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Emitted photons reflect the energy difference between electron energy levels $\\checkmark$\n\n   The wavelengths and therefore energy of emitted photons is discrete $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Photons are emitted when electrons transition between energy levels. The wavelength of the emitted photon is directly related to its energy. Because the emitted wavelengths are quantized, the energy of a photon is also quantized. Therefore, there are distinct energy differences between energy levels.\n\n2. The energy difference should be smallest\n\n    $656\\,nm$ is the longest wavelength that gives lowest energy difference $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\dfrac{(6.63\\times 10^{-34})(3.00\\times 10^8)}{656\\times 10^{-9}}=3.03\\times 10^{-19}\\,J$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The energy difference between the excited states corresponds to the energy of the emitted photon when electrons transition between those states. Energy $E$ of the electron:\n\n    - $E=hf=\\dfrac{hc}{\\lambda}$\n\n    The photon possesses its minimum energy when the wavelength is at its maximum. Specifically, the energy difference between the 3rd  and the 2nd energy states should be the smallest among all other transitions from various excited states to the 2nd state. Consequently, a photon with a wavelength of $656\\,nm$ is expected to be emitted during this particular transition.\n\n    - $E=\\dfrac{hc}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{(6.63\\times 10^{-34})(3.00\\times 10^8)}{656\\times 10^{-9}}=3.03\\times 10^{-19}\\,J$"},{"ref":"PH1015","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1015\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\n1. The diagram shows the emission spectrum of hydrogen atoms when electrons make a transition from different excited states to the second excited state.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/Screenshot%202026-01-06%20at%205.53.47%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-01-06 at 5.53.47 PM\">\n\n    1. Outline how the emission spectrum of hydrogen provides evidence for the discrete energy levels of electrons.[3 marks]\n\n    2. Determine, from the information given in the diagram,  the energy difference between the 3rd and 2nd excited states of the electron. Give your answer in units of joules. [3 marks]\n\n2. 1. The observed light emitted from hydrogen atoms in a remote galaxy is analyzed. This galaxy is moving away from Earth. Sketch the observed spectral lines of the hydrogen in the galaxy.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/Screenshot%202026-01-06%20at%205.55.07%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-01-06 at 5.55.07 PM\">\n\n        The dashed lines represent spectral lines of hydrogen atoms on Earth. There is no need to add values to the lines you draw. [2 marks]\n\n    2. One of the wavelengths of light emitted by a galaxy is measured to be 420 nm, while the wavelength of light from the source is known to be 410 nm. Determine the velocity of the galaxy relative to Earth. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. The energy of a photon is related to its wavelength $\\checkmark$\n\n        Emitted wavelength is quantized/discrete $\\checkmark$\n\n        Photon energies correspond to differences in electron energy levels (therefore electron energy levels are discrete) $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Photons are emitted when electrons transition between energy levels. The wavelength of the emitted photon is directly related to its energy. Because the emitted wavelengths are quantized, the energy of a photon is also quantized. Therefore, there are distinct energy differences between energy levels.\n\n    2. The energy difference should be smallest\n\n        656 nm is the longest wavelength that gives lowest energy difference\n\n        - $\\dfrac{(6.63\\times 10^{-34})(3.00\\times 10^8)}{656\\times 10^{-9}}=3.03\\times 10^{-19}\\,\\text{J}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The energy difference between the excited states corresponds to the energy of the emitted photon when electrons transition between those states. Energy $E$ of the electron:\n\n        - $E=hf=\\dfrac{hc}{\\lambda}$\n\n        The photon possesses its minimum energy when the wavelength is at its maximum. Specifically, the energy difference between the 3rd  and the 2nd energy states should be the smallest among all other transitions from various excited states to the 2nd state. Consequently, a photon with a wavelength of 656 nm is expected to be emitted during this particular transition.\n\n        - $E=\\dfrac{hc}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{(6.63\\times 10^{-34})(3.00\\times 10^8)}{656\\times 10^{-9}}=3.03\\times 10^{-19}\\,\\text{J}$\n\n2. 1. shift to the right $\\checkmark$\n\n        The greater shift for longer wavelengths $\\checkmark$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/Screenshot%202026-01-06%20at%205.56.24%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-01-06 at 5.56.24 PM\">\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Due to the Doppler effect, the observed wavelength of the receding sources is getting longer than the wavelength of the source. Due to the following formula:\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta \\lambda}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{v}{c}$\n\n        Change in wavelength depends on the wavelength of the source. For the longer wavelengths, change is greater. Therefore, the spectral lines should be shifted to the right with an increasing amount of shift.\n\n    2. $v=\\dfrac{c\\Delta \\lambda}{\\lambda}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\dfrac{(3.00\\times 10^8)(10\\times 10^{-9})}{410\\times 10^{-9}}=$ :answer[$7.3\\times 10^6\\,\\text{m}\\,\\text{s}^{-1} \\hspace {1em} \\textrm{away from Earth} $] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The change in wavelength:\n\n        - $\\Delta \\lambda = 420 \\times 10^{-9}-410\\times 10^{-9}=10\\times 10^{-9}\\,\\text{m}$\n\n        The speed of the galaxy can be found by the following formula:\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta \\lambda}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{v}{c}$\n\n        - $v=\\dfrac{c\\Delta \\lambda}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{(3.00\\times 10^8)(10\\times 10^{-9})}{410\\times 10^{-9}}=7.3\\times 10^6 \\, \\text{m}\\,\\text{s}^{-1}$\n\n        Because there is an increase in the wavelength, the star must be moving away from the Earth."},{"ref":"PH1069","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1069\n\nThe diagram shows the scattering of alpha particles that are directed to a thin gold foil. Which of the following scattering patterns cannot be explained by the model of the atom that existed prior to this experiment?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/Screenshot%202026-01-01%20at%2012.17.57%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-01-01 at 12.17.57 PM\">","markScheme":"**答案：A**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\n\nThe previous model (Thomson's “plum pudding model”) assumed the atom to consist of a mass of positively charged matter with embedded electrons. According to the Thomson model, when alpha particles are directed towards an atom, the expectation is that most of them will undergo no deflection. Additionally, minor deflections can be accounted for by the interaction of alpha particles with the positive charges within the atom. However, the significant deflection of alpha particles at large angles can only be explained by the existence of a nucleus, a component not considered in the Thomson model."},{"ref":"PH1070","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1070\n\nA non-naturally occurring element has a nuclear notation of $\\ce{^{180}_{60}X}$.  Below are three statements about the element.\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | The neutron number is twice the proton number.    |\n  | II. | The nucleon number is 180.  |\n  | III. | The number of protons is 60.  |\n\nWhich of the statements are correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Only III\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Only I and II\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Only II and III\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I, II and III","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\n\n180 is the nucleon number while 60 is the proton number of the element X.  Therefore, the neutron number $n$ of the element:\n\n- $n=180-60=120$\n\nHence, the neutron number is twice the number of protons and all three statements are correct."},{"ref":"PH1071","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1071\n\nWhich of the following is the evidence for discrete atomic energy levels?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Discrete energy of alpha emission from a nucleus.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Discrete X-ray lines are produced by high-speed electrons striking a metal target.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Emission spectra of gases.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ The blackbody spectrum produced by incandescent bulbs.","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\n\nThe presence of discrete energy levels in alpha particles emitted from the nucleus is evidence of the quantized energy levels within the nucleus.\n\nDiscrete X-ray lines are generated, among other processes, by the sudden deceleration of electrons during collisions.\n\nIncandescent bulbs produce a blackbody spectrum due to the thermal  radiation from heated filaments, covering a broad range of wavelengths.\n\nConversely, the emission spectrum of gases is a direct result of electrons transitioning between quantized energy levels within atoms. Therefore, C is the correct answer here."},{"ref":"PH1072","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1072\n\nAn electron absorbs a photon and makes a transition between energy levels that have an energy difference of $E$. Then it relaxes to its initial state. Which is correct for absorbed and emitted photons during this transition?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1.5em}$ Energy of Absorbed Photon $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$ Energy of Emitted Photon $\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| Greater than E | Equal to E |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Greater than E | Less than E |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Equal to E | Equal to E |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Equal to E | Less than E |","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\n\nAn electron can absorb photon energy only when it matches the energy difference of the energy levels it transitions between. In the relaxation period, a photon is emitted with the same energy as the difference between the energy levels."},{"ref":"PH1074","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1074\n\nElectrons of an atom are excited to the third excited state. How many different photon wavelengths can be emitted into the surrounding environment as the electrons transition back to the ground state?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 3\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 5\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 6\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 7","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\n\nIn the diagram below, all possible transitions are shown. Note that the third excited state corresponds to the $n = 4$ level for the atom.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/Screenshot%202026-01-01%20at%2012.24.01%E2%80%AFPM1.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-01-01 at 12.24.01 PM1\">"},{"ref":"PH1075","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1075\n\nIn the diagram below, arrows indicate the transitions made by electrons, and corresponding wavelengths of the emitted photons are also marked.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/Screenshot%202026-01-01%20at%2012.28.46%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-01-01 at 12.28.46 PM\">\n\nWhat is the ratio $\\dfrac{\\lambda_1}{\\lambda_2}$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{1.7}{0.8}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{0.8}{1.7}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{1.3}{0.8}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{0.8}{1.3}$","markScheme":"**答案：A**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\n\nThe energy $E_1$ emitted  by the photon that has a wavelength of $\\lambda_1$:\n\n- $E_1=\\dfrac{hc}{\\lambda_1} =-0.2E-(-E)$\n\n- $\\lambda_1=\\dfrac{hc}{-0.2 E+E}=\\dfrac{hc}{0.8 E}$\n\nThe energy $E_2$ emitted  by the photon that has a wavelength of $\\lambda_2$:\n\n- $E_2=\\dfrac{hc}{\\lambda_2} =-0.2E-(-1.9E)$\n\n- $\\lambda_2=\\dfrac{hc}{-0.2E+1.9 E}=\\dfrac{hc}{1.7 E}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\lambda_1}{\\lambda_2} =\\dfrac{\\frac{hc}{0.8 E}}{\\frac{hc}{1.7 E}}=\\dfrac{1.7}{0.8}$"},{"ref":"PH1076","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1076\n\nWhich of the following is directly used in identifying the chemical composition of a star in astronomy?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Doppler shift in spectral lines\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Emission spectra\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Wien's law\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Luminosity of stars","markScheme":"**答案：B**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\n\nEach element has a unique emission spectrum, like a fingerprint. By comparing the emission spectrum of a star to the emission spectra of elements on Earth, astronomers can determine the composition of the star.\n\nDoppler shift makes use of spectral lines, but is used to determine the motion of a star relative to the Earth.\n\nWien's law is used to determine the temperature of a star using its blackbody spectrum.\n\nStellar luminosity is a measure of the power emitted by a star, but is not a direct  indicator of the chemical composition."},{"ref":"PH1116","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1116\n\nInfra-red radiation is absorbed by a gas molecule, causing it to transition from energy level $E_1$ to energy level $E_2$. The absorptivity of the molecule is tested with infra-red radiation of energy $E_{in}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/Screenshot%202026-01-05%20at%2011.27.46%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-01-05 at 11.27.46 AM\">\n\nWhich of the following conditions maximises absorption of the test radiation?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $E_{in}$ is equal to $E_2$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $E_{in}$ is equal to $E_2-E_1$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $E_{in}$ is much less than $E_2-E_1$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $E_{in}$ is much greater than $E_2-E_1$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe molecule absorbs infra-red radiation of energy that exactly matches the energy difference between levels $E_1$ and $E_2$."},{"ref":"PH1151","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1151\n\nTwo different gases A and B have the same pressure and temperature conditions.\nThe diagram shows the emission spectrum of gas A.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/Screenshot%202026-01-01%20at%2012.31.45%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-01-01 at 12.31.45 PM\">\n\nWhich of the following is correct about the atomic energy levels of gas A and the emission spectrum of gas B?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Atomic energy levels of gas A$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Emission spectrum of gas B$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/PH1151A.png\" alt=\"PH1151A\"> | Same as A |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/PH1151B.png\" alt=\"PH1151B\"> | Different from A |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/PH1151C.png\" alt=\"PH1151C\"> | Same as A |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/PH1151D.png\" alt=\"PH1151D\"> | Different from A |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe emission spectrum is unique for each element. This means that no two elements can have the same emission spectrum. Therefore the spectrum of B must be different from that of A.\n\nThe wavelengths produced depend on the differences between atomic energy levels.\n\n- $E=\\dfrac{hc}{\\lambda}$\n\nThe higher the energy difference, the lower the wavelength.\n\nBy investigating the wavelengths of the emission spectrum, we can conclude that the corresponding energy differences between energy levels varies in a way that matches the diagram in A and B.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/PH1151ms1.png\" alt=\"PH1151ms1\">\n\nAs shown in the diagram above, the energy differences will emit three lines with short wavelengths and three lines with long wavelengths.\n\nFor the diagram in C and D, the energy differences for the transitions will lead to an emission spectrum different from that given in the question.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/PH1151ms2.png\" alt=\"PH1151ms2\">\n\nFor example, the three transitions shown by the three arrows to the right will have almost the same wavelength."},{"ref":"PH1152","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1152\n\nThe diagram shows the emission spectrum received from a star and the emission spectra of hydrogen, helium, oxygen, and carbon.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/Screenshot%202026-01-01%20at%2012.39.32%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-01-01 at 12.39.32 PM\">\n\nWhich of the following is correct about the chemical composition of this star?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Hydrogen and helium only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Hydrogen, helium and carbon\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Hydrogen, carbon and oxygen\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Helium, carbon and oxygen","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nWhen we examine the pattern of the emission spectra of the star and compare it to the individual spectrum of each element, we’ll find that it matches the composition of hydrogen and helium only.\n\nFor example, if we consider the smallest wavelength of the spectrum coming from the star, we’ll find that it’s the smallest wavelength of hydrogen. And the largest wavelength coming from the star is the same as the largest wavelength of helium.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/Screenshot%202026-01-06%20at%205.26.08%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-01-06 at 5.26.08 PM\">"},{"ref":"PH1153","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1153\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nScientists are investigating the composition of a star. The spectrum of light emitted by the star is analyzed. The diagram shows a section of the emission spectrum with the longest shown wavelength 654 nm.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/Screenshot%202026-01-01%20at%201.09.48%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-01-01 at 1.09.48 PM\">\n\n1. Differentiate between emission and absorption spectra in terms of electron transitions. [2 marks]\n\n2. Explain how the emission and absorption spectra can be used to deduce the chemical composition of this star. [3 marks]\n\nThis emission spectrum could be produced from element A or B.\nThe diagram below shows some energy levels of both elements\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Atomic%20Structure/images/Screenshot%202026-04-14%20at%208.16.19%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-04-14 at 8.16.19 AM\">\n\n3. Determine the element that produces this emission spectrum. Justify your answer. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Emission spectrum is when electrons transition from a higher energy level to a lower one. $\\checkmark$\\\n    Absorption spectrum is when electrons transition from a lower energy level to a higher one. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    When white light hits an atom, electrons will absorb energy that’s equivalent to the difference between the ground state and another energy level. The electron will transition to the higher energy level.\\\n    Since\n\n    - $E=hf$\n\n    The energy gained by the electron corresponds to a frequency of light which represents a certain color. This will leave a dark region at the position of the absorbed frequency.\\\n    The white light leaving the atom will represent an absorption spectrum.\n\n    On the other hand, when an excited electron transitions to a lower energy level, it will emit a photon with energy equal to the difference between the two energy levels.\\\n    The frequency of this photon depends on this energy difference.\\\n    The spectrum emitted from this atom is an emission spectrum.\n\n2. Atoms absorb/emit energy that is exactly equal to the difference of energies between its energy levels. $\\checkmark$\\\n    Wavelengths of absorbed/emitted photons have discrete values. $\\checkmark$\\\n    The wavelengths emitted are unique for each element. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    Energy levels have discrete values that are unique for each element.\n\n    An electron transitions from one energy level to another by absorbing or emitting energy that is equal to the energy difference between the two energy levels.\n\n    This energy difference corresponds to a certain wavelength of a photon.\n    The wavelengths emitted or absorbed are unique for each element.\n\n    This means that we can identify an element from its absorption or emission spectrum.\n\n3.  ::indent\n    - A $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $E=\\ll \\dfrac{hc}{\\lambda}=\\gg\\dfrac{(6.63\\times10^{-34})(3\\times10^8)}{(654\\times10^{-9})(1.6\\times10^{-19})}=1.9\\ll \\,\\text{eV}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - Energy difference between $n = 5$ and $n = 6$\\\n    $E=(-1.5)-(-3.4)=1.9\\,\\ll \\text{eV}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    The longest shown wavelength emitted from the star is 654 nm\\\n    The energy difference that will produce this wavelength is\n\n    - $E=\\dfrac{hc}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{(6.63\\times10^{-34})(3\\times10^8)}{(654\\times10^{-9})(1.6\\times10^{-19})}=1.9$ eV\n\n    This energy difference can only be found in the energy level diagram of element A between $n = 4$ and $n = 6$\n\n    - $E=(-1.5)-(-3.4)=1.9$ eV"},{"ref":"PH1886","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1886\n\nIn the Geiger-Marsden-Rutherford experiment alpha particles were directed at a thin gold foil to investigate atomic structure.\n\nBelow are three statements about the experiment.\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | Most of the alpha particles passed straight through the foil because the atom is mostly empty space. |\n  | II. | Most of the alpha particles that did not pass through the foil were absorbed by the gold atoms. |\n  | III. | Some of the alpha particles were deflected as they were repelled by the electrons in the gold atoms. |\n\nWhich of the statements is correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n**Statement I is correct.**\nIn the Geiger-Marsden-Rutherford experiment, most alpha particles passed straight through the gold foil, which led to the conclusion that atoms are mostly empty space.\n\n**Statement II is incorrect.**\nAlpha particles are massive and carry a positive charge, and they were not absorbed by the gold atoms. Instead, the few that didn't pass through were scattered, often at large angles due to repulsion from the dense, positively charged nucleus.\n\n**Statement III is incorrect.**\nDeflections were caused by the strong electrostatic repulsion from the nucleus, not the electrons."},{"ref":"PH1903","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1903\n\nThe table below shows the lowest four energy levels for the electrons around an atom.\n\n$\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\textrm{Level} & \\textrm{Energy / eV} \\\\\n\\hline\n1 & -10 \\\\\n\\hline\n2 & -4.0 \\\\\n\\hline\n3 & -2.8 \\\\\n\\hline\n4 & -1.6 \\\\\n\\end{array}$\n\nThe energy of a violet photon is approximately 3.1 eV.\n\nGiven that the wavelength of a red photon is about twice the wavelength of a violet photon, which of the following electronic transitions of an electron in the atom shown would result in the emission of a visible photon?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $4\\rightarrow3$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $4\\rightarrow2$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $3\\rightarrow2$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2\\rightarrow1$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nIt is given that the wavelength of a red photon is about twice that of a violet one. This means that its frequency (and thus its energy) must be half that of a violet photon.\n\nTherefore, visible photons will have energies ranging between 1.5 eV (red) and 3.1 eV (violet).\n\nThe only transition that falls between these values is the one between the energy levels 4 to 2.\n\n- $E_{4\\rightarrow2}=E_2-E_4=(-4.0\\textrm{ eV})-(-1.6\\textrm{ eV})=-2.4$ eV\n\nWhen an electron transitions between the 4$^\\textrm{th}$ energy level to the 2$^\\textrm{nd}$ energy level, a photon with 2.4 eV will be emitted.\n\nThis photon will be a visible photon.\n\nNo other transitions shown fall within the energy range for visible light."}]},{"name":"Nuclear Fission","questions":[{"ref":"PH0091","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0091\n\nWhich of the following correctly shows the change in total binding energy per nucleon in nuclear fission and nuclear fusion?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$ Nuclear fission $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Nuclear fusion$\\hspace{1em}$  |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Increases | Increases |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Increases | Decreases |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Decreases | Increases |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Decreases | Decreases |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nEnergy is released in both nuclear fission and nuclear fusion. Hence the total nuclear mass in both cases decreases, which corresponds to greater mass defect and greater binding energy per nucleon."},{"ref":"PH0119","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0119\n\nIn the design of a nuclear fission reactor, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n\n  | | |\n\n  | :-- | :-- |\n\n  | I. | Control rods can increase the rate of fission when inserted into the reactor |\n\n  | II. | The moderator blocks neutrons to ensure the chain reaction is controlled |\n\n  | III. | Enrichment of uranium increases the probability of a chain reaction taking place |\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nI is incorrect because control rods block neutrons and inhibit the rate of reaction.\n\nII is incorrect because the moderator slows neutrons to increase the chance of being absorbed by a uranium nucleus and result in a fission.\n\nIII is correct."},{"ref":"PH0302","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0302\n\nIn a nuclear reactor, what is the name given to the material used to slow down neutrons in order to cause successful fissions?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Control rod\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Moderator\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Heat exchanger\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Chain reaction","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe moderator slows down neutrons so that they can cause successful fissions. Without a moderator, neutrons that are the product of other fissions have too much energy to interact with new nuclei such that they cause further fissions to occur.\n\nControl rods block the neutrons to reduce the number of chain reaction fissions.\n\nThe heat exchanger transfers thermal energy to the external steam generator circuit.\n\nA chain reaction is a process, not a specific material."},{"ref":"PH0316","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0316\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nA nuclear power plant operating at an overall efficiency of 40% produces energy at a rate of 950 MW. The reaction responsible for generating this energy is given by:\n\n$\\ce{_92^235U + _0^1n -> _56^141Ba + _36^92Kr + 3 _0^1n}$ \n\nThe atomic masses of the different nuclides are:\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\ce{_92^235U} = 235.0444u\\ \\hspace{5em}\n\n\\ce{_56^141Ba} &= 140.9144u\\ \\hspace{5em}\n\n\\ce{_36^92Kr} = 91.9262u\\\\\n\n\\end{aligned}$\n\n1. \n\n    1. Show that approximately 170 MeV of energy is released in one fission reaction. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Using the information given, determine the energy density of uranium-235 in units of J kg$^{-1}$. [3 marks]\n\n    3. Assuming that all of the uranium-235 undergoes fission, calculate the mass of uranium needed to operate the plant for one year. [3 marks]\n\n2. The nuclear reactor consists of several features responsible for the smooth functioning of the reactor. State the function of the following features  in the reactor:\n\n    \n\n    1. The control rods [1 mark]\n\n    2. The moderator [1 mark]\n\n    3. The heat exchanger [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. \n\n    1.  $\\hspace{1em}$ \n\n        $\\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\Delta m&=\\textrm{Mass}_{Reactants} -\\textrm{Mass}_{Products}\\\\\n\n        \\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n        &=(1.008665u+235.0444u)-(140.9144u+91.9262u+3.026u)\\\\\n\n        \\hspace{1em}\\\\    \n\n        &=0.18647u \\,\\checkmark\n\n        \\end{aligned}$\n\n        - $E=0.18647u\\Bigl(\\dfrac{931.5\\,\\ce{MeV}}{1u}\\Bigr)=173.7\\,\\ce{MeV}$  $\\checkmark$ $\\approx 170\\,\\ce{MeV}$ \n\n        \n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n\n        The mass difference between the reactants and the products is given by:\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$\n\n        $\\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\Delta m&=\\textrm{Mass}_\\textrm{Reactants} -\\textrm{Mass}_\\textrm{Products}\\\\\n\n        \\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n        &=(1.008665u+235.0444u)-(140.9144u+91.9262u+3(1.008665u))\\\\\n\n        \\hspace{1em}\\\\    \n\n        &=0.18647u \n\n        \\end{aligned}$\n\n        Converting from $u$ to MeV\n\n        - $E=0.18647u\\Bigl(\\dfrac{931.5\\,\\ce{MeV}}{1u}\\Bigr)=173.7$ MeV\n\n        At this point the extra significant figures need to be shown since it's a proof. \n\n        $\\approx$ 170 MeV\n\n    \n\n    2. ::indent\n\n    \n\n        - $m=235.0444u = (235.0444)(1.661 \\times 10^{-27}) = 3.90 \\times 10^{-25}$ kg $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $E = 173.7\\,\\ce{MeV} = 2.78 \\times 10^{-11}\\,\\ce{J}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $E_S$ = $\\dfrac{E}{m} = \\dfrac{2.78\\, \\times \\,10^{-11}}{3.90 \\,\\times\\, 10^{-25}} = $ :answer[$7.13 \\times 10^{13}\\,\\ce{J kg}^{-1}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The specific energy of the uranium atom is the energy released per unit mass. \n\n        Mass of one uranium atom = $235.0444u = (235.0444)(1.661 \\times 10^{-27}) = 3.90 \\times 10^{-25}\\,\\ce{kg}$ \n\n        Energy from one atom = $(173.7 \\times 10^6 $ eV$)(1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$ J eV$^{-1}$) = $2.78 \\times 10^{-11}$ J \n\n        Specific energy = $\\dfrac{\\textrm{Energy released}}{\\textrm{mass}} = \\dfrac{2.78 \\times 10^{-11}}{3.90 \\times 10^{-25}} = 7.13 \\times 10^{13}$ J kg$^{-1}$\n\n    3. ::indent\n\n        - $P_{in}=\\dfrac{950\\,\\ce{MW}}{0.4}=2375\\,\\ce{MW}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $E_{year}=(2375\\times 10^6)(365)(24)(60)(60) = 7.49\\times 10^{16}\\,\\ce{J}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $m = \\dfrac{7.49\\,\\times\\, 10^{16}\\,\\ce{J}}{7.13 \\times 10^{13}\\,\\ce{J}\\,\\ce{kg}^{-1}}= $ :answer[1050 kg] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n\n        $Power_{in}=\\dfrac{950\\,\\ce{MW}}{0.4}=2375$ MW\n\n        Energy in 1 year, $E_{year}=(2375\\times 10^6)(365)(24)(60)(60) = 7.49\\times 10^{16}$ J \n\n        Mass needed = $\\dfrac{\\textrm{Yearly energy}}{\\textrm{specific energy}} = \\dfrac{7.49\\,\\times \\,10^{16}\\,\\ce{J}}{7.13 \\times 10^{13}\\,\\ce{J}\\,\\ce{kg}^{-1}}=1052$ kg\n\n2. \n\n    1. The control rods are used to absorb neutrons to control the rate of the chain reaction. $\\checkmark$\n\n    2. The moderator slows fission neutrons (to cause a chain reaction) $\\checkmark$\n\n    3. The heat exchanger removes excess thermal energy from the moderator / transfers heat to the external circuit to turn turbines and produce electricity. $\\checkmark$"},{"ref":"PH0334","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0334\n\nA nuclear reactor converts nuclear energy into electrical energy. Which of the following reactions best describes the primary nuclear reaction in a nuclear reactor?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{^{234}_{90}Th}$ $\\hspace{0.1em}$ $\\rightarrow$ $\\hspace{0.1em}$ $\\ce{^{0}_{-1}\\beta}$ $\\hspace{0.1em}$ + $\\hspace{0.1em}$ $\\ce{^{234}_{91}Pa}$ + $\\ce{^{0}_{0}\\overline{\\nu}}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{^{232}_{92}U}$ $\\hspace{0.1em}$ $\\rightarrow$ $\\hspace{0.1em}$ $\\ce{^{228}_{90}Th}$ $\\hspace{0.1em}$ + $\\hspace{0.1em}$ $\\ce{^{4}_{2}\\alpha}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{^{235}_{92}U}$ $\\hspace{0.1em}$ + $\\hspace{0.1em}$ $\\ce{^{1}_{0}\\textit{n}}$ $\\hspace{0.1em}$ $\\rightarrow$ $\\hspace{0.1em}$ $\\ce{^{90}_{36}Kr}$ $\\hspace{0.1em}$ + $\\hspace{0.1em}$ $\\ce{^{143}_{56}Ba}$ $\\hspace{0.1em}$ + $\\hspace{0.1em}$ 3 $\\ce{^{1}_{0}\\textit{n}}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{^{14}_{6}C}$ $\\hspace{0.1em}$ $\\rightarrow$ $\\hspace{0.1em}$ $\\ce{^{7}_{3}Li}$ $\\hspace{0.1em}$ + $\\hspace{0.1em}$ $\\ce{^{7}_{3}Li}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nA is a simple beta decay reaction.\n\nB is an alpha decay reaction.\n\nC is the type of fission reaction that occurs in a nuclear reactor for the generation of electricity with uranium as a fuel.\n\nD shows a fission reaction that would require an input of energy to occur. It does not take place in a fission reactor."},{"ref":"PH0471","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0471\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nUranium-235 ($\\ce{_{92}^{235}U}$) has a nuclear mass of $235.044u$.\n\n1. 1. State what is meant by mass defect. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Calculate the mass defect of uranium-235. [2 marks]\n\n2.\n    1. Calculate the binding energy per nucleon of uranium-235. [2 marks]\n\n    2. On the axes given, sketch the variation of the binding energy per nucleon with the nucleon number. Label the position of uranium-235. There is no need to add numbers to the axes. [3 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Nuclear%20Fission/images/PH0471_Q.png\" alt=\"PH0471_Q\">\n    $\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra (https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en)]}}$\n\nUranium-235 ($\\ce{_{92}^{235}U}$) decays into thorium-231 ($\\ce{_{90}^{231}Th}$).\n\n3. Determine which nuclide has a higher binding energy per nucleon. Use the graph to justify your answer.  [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. The mass of a nucleus is always less than the total mass of its constituent nucleons. The difference in mass is called the mass defect. $\\checkmark$\n\n    2. ::indent\n        - $\\delta=\\ll Zm_p+\\left(A-Z\\right)m_n-M_{nucleus}\\gg$\n\n        - $\\delta=(92)(1.007276)+(143)(1.008665)-235.044$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\delta=$ :answer[1.86 $\\ll u\\gg$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$ \\\nHere the proton number (92) and the neutron number (143) are multiplied by their respective particle masses. The mass of the nucleus is then subtracted from this number.\n\n        The non-rounded value is 1.8644487.\n\n2.\n    1. ::indent\n        - $\\ll \\textrm{binding energy}=\\delta\\ c^2\\gg$\n\n        - binding energy $=(1.86)(931.5) =1737\\ \\ll\\ \\ce{MeV}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - binding energy per nucleon $=\\ll\\dfrac{1737}{235}=\\gg$ :answer[7.39 $\\,\\ll \\ce{MeV}\\gg$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$ \\\n        The binding energy will be the mass defect in amu multiplied by the mass-energy equivalence of an atomic mass unit.\n\n        - binding energy $=(1.864487)(931.5)=1737$\n\n        (Using the rounded value gives 1732.59)\n\n        To find binding energy per nucleon we divide by 235.\n\n        - binding energy per nucleon $=\\dfrac{1737}{235}=7.39$ MeV\n\n        (Using the rounded value gives 7.37 MeV)\n\n    2. Curve starts at 0 and rises sharply to a maximum point. $\\checkmark$ \\\n    The curve then drops gently. $\\checkmark$ \\\nCorrect positioning of U-235. $\\checkmark$ \\\nAllow smooth curve at low nucleon number.\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Nuclear%20Fission/images/PH0471.png\" alt=\"PH0471\">\n        $\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra (https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en)]}}$\n\n    3. Thorium-231 $\\checkmark$ \\\nAccording to its position in the graph/ lower nucleon number. $\\checkmark$ \\\n(In the event of an incorrect graph, allow for full marks if answer and justification correlates with shape of the drawn graph.)\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$\\\nBecause of its lower nucleon number, thorium will appear to the left of uranium on the graph. This shows that it has a higher binding energy per nucleon."},{"ref":"PH0472","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0472\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe following reaction takes place in a nuclear reactor:\n\n$\\hspace{4em}$ $\\ce{^{235}_{92}U}$ + $\\ce{^{1}_{0}\\textit{n}}$ $\\rightarrow$ $\\ce{^{144}_{56}Ba}$ + $\\ce{^{89}_{36}Kr}$ + 3$\\ce{^{1}_{0}\\textit{n}}$\n\n1. 1. Differentiate between fission and fusion reactions. [3 marks]\n\n    2. Determine whether this reaction is a nuclear fission or nuclear fusion. [1 mark]\n\nThe following data is given for this reaction:\n\n- The binding energy per nucleon of $\\ce{^{235}U}$ = 7.59 MeV\n- The binding energy per nucleon of $\\ce{^{144}Ba}$ = 8.27 MeV\n- The binding energy per nucleon of $\\ce{^{89}Kr}$ = 8.62 MeV\n\n2. Calculate the energy released from this reaction. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. Fission is the process of splitting a large nucleus into smaller nuclei. $\\checkmark$ \\\nFusion is the joining together of small nuclei to give larger ones. $\\checkmark$ \\\nEnergy is released in both. $\\checkmark$ \\\n\n    2. Fission $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n    The larger uranium nucleus breaks into two smaller daughter nuclei.\n\n2. Binding energy of $\\ce{^{235}U}$ = 1783.65 MeV\\\n    **AND** \\\n    Binding energy of $\\ce{^{144}Ba}$ = 1190.88 MeV \\\n    **AND** \\\n    Binding energy of $\\ce{^{89}Kr}$ = 767.18 MeV $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $E=1783.65-\\left(1190.88+767.18\\right)$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - Energy released =:answer[($-$)174.41 $\\ll\\ \\ce{MeV}\\gg$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    To find the binding energies of the nuclides involved, multiply the binding energy per nucleon by the number of nucleons\n\n    - Binding energy of $\\ce{^{235}U}$ = (235)(7.59) = 1783.65 MeV\n    - Binding energy of $\\ce{^{144}Ba}$ = (144)(8.27) = 1190.88 MeV\n    - Binding energy of $\\ce{^{89}Kr}$ = (89)(8.62) = 767.18 MeV\n\n    - $\\Delta$E = Binding energy of original nucleus $-$ Sum of binding energy of products\n\n    - $\\Delta E=1783.65-\\left(1190.88+767.18\\right)$\n\n    - $\\Delta E = -174.41$ MeV\n\n    This negative result shows that the binding energy of the products is greater than that of the original uranium nucleus. As there is an increase in total binding energy, there is a release of energy in this reaction."},{"ref":"PH0719","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0719\n\nWhich of the following incorrectly matches a component of a nuclear power plant with its function?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Component | Function |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\hspace{1.5em}$Heat Exchanger$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |Prevents overheating |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Moderator | Slows down electrons |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Control rods | Absorbs neutrons |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Shielding | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Prevents any leakage of radiation$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nModerators slow down the speed of the neutrons (not electrons) so that they can be captured by nuclei of fuel and initiate further fission reactions."},{"ref":"PH0720","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0720\n\nWhich of the following is the most environmentally responsible method for managing nuclear waste?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $Burying in the soil at shallow depths\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $Disposal into the ocean\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $Storage in a disused mine\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $Burning in controlled incinerators","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nEffective management of nuclear waste requires preventing contamination of the soil, water supply, and atmosphere. Burying nuclear waste in the soil can lead to soil and water supply contamination. Similarly, disposing of it in the ocean poses numerous hazards to marine life. Burning nuclear waste can also result in contamination of the atmosphere.\n\nThe most suitable method for long-term management is storing the waste in a disused mine with appropriate containment measures."},{"ref":"PH1033","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1033\n\nEnergy is released in nuclear reactors by\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Neutron-induced fission\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Spontaneous fission\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Random fission\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Fusion","markScheme":"**答案：A**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nNuclear power plants harness the energy released through nuclear fission. Fission can occur spontaneously, without any external stimulus, but this is a rare and inefficient process that is not utilized in nuclear power plants. Instead, nuclear power plants employ neutron-induced fission, which involves bombarding a heavy nucleus with a neutron, causing it to split and release energy. This induced fission can be controlled in nuclear reactors by using control rods to block given proportions of neutrons, allowing for a sustained release of energy that can be used to generate electricity."},{"ref":"PH1034","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1034\n\nWhich of the following conditions is/are necessary to sustain a chain reaction in a nuclear reactor?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | The number of neutrons released from reactions per second is less than the number of neutrons absorbed by control rods per second.   |\n  | II. | The number of neutrons released from reactions per second is equal to the number of neutrons absorbed by control rods per second. |\n  | III. | The number of neutrons released from reactions per second is more than the number of neutrons absorbed by control rods per second. |\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Only I and II\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Only I and III\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Only II and III\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Only III","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nA self-sustaining chain reaction in nuclear fission requires the release of neutrons that can initiate subsequent fission events. For each fission, two to three neutrons are released. If at least one neutron is not absorbed or escapes, it can trigger another fission, maintaining the chain reaction. The only option that satisfies this requirement is statement III, so option D is correct."},{"ref":"PH1035","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1035\n\nIn a nuclear reactor, the purpose of moderators is\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Exchanging heat\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Slowing down neutrons\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Absorbing neutrons\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Shielding","markScheme":"**答案：B**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe neutrons released during nuclear fission are extremely fast, travelling at speeds that are too high to be captured by the nuclei of the fuel atoms. To make these neutrons more likely to be captured and sustain a chain reaction, they need to be slowed down. This is achieved by passing the neutrons through a material called a moderator, which is typically a light element like water, graphite, or heavy water."},{"ref":"PH1036","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1036\n\nWhich of the following methods is suitable for the management of waste produced from fuel rods in a nuclear reactor?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | Storing them in well-suited containers at ground level.   |\n  | II. | Storing them in underground vaults such as disused mines. |\n  | III. | Burying them in the ground. |\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Only I and II\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Only I and III\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Only II and III\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I, II, and III","markScheme":"**答案：A**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nHigh-level radioactive waste from nuclear reactors poses a unique environmental hazard due to its long-lasting radioactivity and potential to contaminate the food chain. Unlike low-level waste, it cannot be buried safely, requiring specialized disposal methods to prevent environmental harm."},{"ref":"PH1037","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1037\n\nIn a fission reactor, which of the following is correct for the main role of the control rods and the heat exchangers?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Control Rods  | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Heat Exchangers$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\hspace{1.5em}$Absorb neutrons$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |$\\hspace{1.5em}$Transfer heat from moderator to boiler$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Absorb neutrons | Transfer heat from boiler to moderator |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Slow down neutrons$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Transfer heat from moderator to boiler |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Slow down neutrons | Transfer heat from boiler to moderator |","markScheme":"**答案：A**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nIn a fission reactor, control rods regulate the rate of the nuclear chain reactions by absorbing neutrons while heat exchangers transfer the heat stored in the moderator (mostly water) to the boiler for generating electricity."},{"ref":"PH1038","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1038\n\nThe diagram shows a pressurized water reactor. Which part of the diagram is responsible for absorbing neutrons to control the reaction rate?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Nuclear%20Fission/images/PH1039%20copy.png\" alt=\"PH1039 copy\">","markScheme":"**答案：A**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nControl rods must be positioned to allow for their insertion or removal from the reactor core. Option A is the only option that meets this requirement."},{"ref":"PH1039","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1039\n\nThe diagram shows a pressurized water reactor. Which part of the diagram is responsible for protecting workers in a nuclear power station?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Nuclear%20Fission/images/PH1039.png\" alt=\"PH1039\">","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nA reactor must be equipped with a protective shield that effectively absorbs all the harmful radiation emanating from its core. This shield must be sufficiently thick and encompassing to effectively prevent radiation leakage. Option C is the only option that meets this requirement."},{"ref":"PH1041","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1041\n\nWhich of the following is correct for neutron-induced fission?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Heavier nuclei split into smaller nuclei on its own\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Lighter nuclei combine into a heavier nucleus on its own\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Heavier nuclei split into smaller nuclei after absorption of a particle\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Lighter nuclei combine into a heavier nucleus after absorption of a particle","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nNeutron-induced fission occurs when a heavy nucleus absorbs a neutron, triggering its disintegration into smaller fragments accompanied by the release of a tremendous amount of energy."},{"ref":"PH1042","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1042\n\nWhich of the following, on its own, would be least likely to lead to a meltdown in a nuclear reactor?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Absence of heat exchanger\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Absence of moderator\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Absence of control rods\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Absence of coolant","markScheme":"**答案：B**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nIn the absence of a moderator, the chain reaction becomes difficult to sustain, leading to the reactor's shutdown without achieving a high temperature."},{"ref":"PH1155","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1155\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nIn a nuclear reactor, a fuel rod of U-235 is partially covered by boron control rods.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Nuclear%20Fission/images/PH1155.png\" alt=\"PH1155\">\n\nThe decay of U-235 proceeds according to the equation\n\n$\\ce{_92^235{U}}+\\ce{_0^1{n}} \\rightarrow \\ce{_56^144{Ba}}+\\ce{_36^89{Kr}}+3\\ce{_0^1{n}}$\n\n1. With reference to the equation, explain the role of the control rods. [3 marks]\n\nEach fuel rod contains 500 kg of U-235 and releases $2.23\\times10^{29}$ MeV of energy if all the U-235 undergoes fission. The binding energy per nucleon of U-235 and Ba-144 are 7.59 MeV and 8.27 MeV, respectively.\n\n2. 1. Show that one fuel rod contains approximately $1.3\\times10^{27}$ nuclei. Assume that the mass of U-235 is $235u$. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Calculate the binding energy per nucleon of Kr-89. [3 marks]\n\n3. Show that the Kr-89 fragment receives a greater share of the released energy than the Ba-144 fragment upon fission. Assume that the U-235 nucleus is initially at rest, and that negligible energy is imparted to neutrons released in the fission reaction. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Each neutron released can lead to another fission reaction $\\checkmark$\n\n    Each fission reaction produces three times more neutrons than it uses $\\checkmark$\n\n    The control rod absorbs neutrons to prevent an uncontrolled chain reaction $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    The fission of U-235 is induced when it absorbs a single neutron to yield an unstable U-236 nucleus, which subsequently breaks apart to release three neutrons and two daughter nuclei.\n\n    The three neutrons may each induce further fission reactions, resulting in a chain reaction that could lead to a dangerous, uncontrolled increase in generated power.\n\n    The purpose of the control rod is to absorb some of the released neutrons, preventing them from inducing fission reactions. In this way, the control rod ensures that the rate of fission reactions is maintained at a constant value.\n\n2. 1. ::indent\n        - Nuclei = $N_A\\Bigl(\\dfrac{\\textrm{Fuel rod mass}}{\\textrm{Molar mass }(\\ce{_92^235{U})}}\\Bigr)$\n\n        - $\\hspace{3em} =(6.02\\times10^{23}\\textrm{mol}^{-1})\\Bigl(\\dfrac{5\\times10^5\\,\\ce{g}}{235\\,\\ce{g}\\,\\textrm{mol}^{-1}}\\Bigr)=1.28\\times10^{27}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        The molar quantity of nuclei in each fuel rod is equal to the mass divided by the molar mass of U-235:\n\n        - Moles = $\\dfrac{\\textrm{Fuel rod mass}}{\\textrm{Molar mass } (\\ce{_92^235{U}})}$\n\n        Multiplying this value by Avogadro’s constant ($N_A$) gives the total number of nuclei:\n\n        - Nuclei = $N_A\\Bigl(\\dfrac{\\textrm{Fuel rod mass}}{\\textrm{Molar mass } (\\ce{_92^235{U}})}\\Bigr)$\n\n        Substituting the values provided into the equation yields $1.28\\times10^{27}$ nuclei.\n\n        - $=(6.02\\times10^{23}\\textrm{mol}^{-1})\\Bigl(\\dfrac{5\\times10^5\\,\\ce{g}}{235\\,\\ce{g}\\,\\textrm{mol}^{-1}}\\Bigr)=1.28\\times10^{27}$\n\n    2. ::indent\n        - $\\Delta BE$ per nucleus = $\\dfrac{\\textrm{Energy released}}{\\textrm{ number of } \\ce{_92^235{U}}\\textrm{ nuclei}} = 174$ MeV $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\Delta BE \\textrm{ per nucleus} = 89BE(\\ce{_36^89{Kr}}) + 144BE(\\ce{_56^144{Ba}})-235BE(\\ce{_92^235{U}})$\n\n        - $89BE(\\ce{_36^89{Kr}})+(144)(8.27\\,\\ce{MeV})-(235)(7.59\\,\\ce{MeV})=174$ MeV  $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $BE(\\ce{_36^89{Kr}})=\\dfrac{174+1.784\\times10^3-1.191\\times10^3}{89}\\,\\ce{MeV}=8.62\\,\\ce{MeV}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        The change in binding energy per U-235 nucleus is given by the total energy released by a fuel rod divided by the number of U-235 nuclei:\n\n        - $\\Delta BE \\textrm{ per nucleus}=\\dfrac{\\textrm{Energy released}}{\\ce{_92^235{U}} \\textrm{ nuclei}}=\\dfrac{2.23\\times10^{29}\\,\\ce{MeV}}{1.28\\times10^{27}}=174$ MeV\n\n        Defining $BE$(X) as the binding energy per nucleon in nucleus X, one may write\n\n        - $\\Delta BE \\textrm{ per nucleus}=89BE(\\ce{_36^89{Kr}})+144BE(\\ce{_56^144{Ba}})-235BE(\\ce{_92^235{U}})$\n\n        Substituting for known values yields\n\n        - $89BE(\\ce{_36^89{Kr}})+(144)(8.27\\,\\ce{MeV})-(235)(7.59\\,\\ce{MeV})=174$ MeV\n\n        Rearranging the equation to make the binding energy per nucleon of Kr-89 the subject, one obtains\n\n        - $BE(\\ce{_36^89{Kr}})=\\dfrac{174+1.784\\times10^3-1.191\\times10^3}{89}\\,\\ce{MeV}=8.62$ MeV\n\n3. ::indent\n    - $m_{Kr}v_{Kr}=m_{Ba}v_{Ba}$   $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $E=\\dfrac{p^2}{2m}$, $E\\propto\\dfrac{1}{m}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    Kr-89 has the lower mass, therefore the greater kinetic energy $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    It is assumed that all the energy is converted to kinetic energy of the daughter nuclei. Conservation of momentum demands that these fragments possess equal and opposite momenta:\n\n    - Momentum of $\\ce{_36^89{Kr}}$ = Momentum of $\\ce{_56^144{Ba}}$\n\n    From the data booklet, $E=\\dfrac{p^2}{2m}$\n\n    We can see that because the momenta are equal, the kinetic energy is inversely proportional to the mass.\n\n    Comparing the masses of Kr-89 and Ba-144, we see that the Kr will have the greater kinetic energy because it has the lower mass."},{"ref":"PH1193","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1193\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nA 20 g pellet of Pu-239 is held in a light, closed vessel suspended from a spring. The spring constant of the spring is 0.30 N m$^{-1}$. Neutrons are fired through the wall of the vessel to initiate fission of the Pu-239 to produce Xe-134 and Zr-103. After the fission, the daughter nuclei remain within the pellet.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Nuclear%20Fission/images/PH1193.png\" alt=\"PH1193\">\n\n1. Explain why the mass of the pellet decreases during the fission reaction.[3 marks]\n\nThe binding energy per nucleon of Pu-239, Xe-134 and Zr-103 are as follows:\n\nBinding energy per nucleon of Pu-239 = 7.56 MeV\\\nBinding energy per nucleon of Xe-134 = 8.41 MeV\\\nBinding energy per nucleon of Zr-103 = 8.43 MeV\n\n2. 1. Show that the fission reaction yields a mass loss per Pu-239 nucleus of approximately $2u$. [3 marks]\n\n    2. Show that the total mass of the pellet changes by about 190 mg when all the Pu-239 has undergone fission. [2 marks]\n\n    3. All neutrons produced in the fission escape the vessel. Estimate the upward displacement of the vessel by the end of the fission reaction. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. The sum of nucleons in the daughter nuclei is lower than the nucleon number of Pu-239 $\\checkmark$\n\n    OR\n\n    There is a net loss of two neutrons from the pellet per nuclear fission $\\checkmark$\n\n    Daughter nuclei have higher binding energy per nucleon than parent $\\checkmark$\n\n    Released energy yields an equivalent mass defect (Einstein relation) $\\checkmark$`\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    When a Pu-239 nucleus undergoes neutron-induced fission, it loses mass because the total mass number of its daughter nuclei ($134+103 = 237$) is lower than the initial mass number (239). This difference corresponds to the net loss of two neutrons during the fission reaction (three neutrons are released, whereas just one initiates the reaction).\n\n    In addition to the decreased nucleon number, mass is lost as kinetic energy due to the daughter nuclei possessing higher binding energies per nucleon than the parent. This increase in binding energy is a necessary condition for the parent nucleus to undergo spontaneous fission after absorbing an incident neutron.\n\n    Through Einstein’s equivalence relation between energy and mass ($E=mc^2$), the energy released during fission yields a corresponding mass decrease, or “mass defect”.\n\n2. 1. ::indent\n        - $\\Delta BE = 188.39$ MeV $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\Delta m(BE)=\\dfrac{\\Delta BE}{c^2}=\\dfrac{(188.39\\times10^6\\,\\textrm{eV})(1.60\\times10^{-19}\\,\\ce{C})}{(3.00\\times10^8\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1})^2(1.661\\times10^{-27}\\,\\ce{kg})}u \\approx 0.20 u$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\Delta m=2.0 u+\\Delta m(BE) \\approx 2.2u$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        To calculate the mass defect per Pu-239 nucleus, one must first calculate the binding energy change during its fission. Using the values provided to sum the total binding energy of the daughter nuclei, then subtracting the total binding energy in the parent nucleus,\n\n        - $\\Delta BE=(134)(8.41\\,\\ce{MeV}) + (103)(8.43\\,\\ce{MeV}) - (239)(7.56\\,\\ce{MeV})$\n\n        - $\\Delta BE= 1,126.94\\,\\ce{MeV}+868.29\\,\\ce{MeV}-1,806.84\\,\\ce{MeV}$\n\n        - $\\Delta BE= 188.39\\,\\ce{MeV}$\n\n        Using Einstein’s relation and converting to SI units,\n\n        - $\\Delta m(BE)=\\dfrac{\\Delta BE}{c^2}=\\dfrac{(188.39 \\times 10^6\\,\\textrm{eV})(1.60 \\times 10^{-19}\\,\\ce{C})}{(3.00 \\times 10^8\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1})^2}$\n\n        - $\\Delta m(BE)=\\dfrac{3.03 \\times 10^{-11}\\,\\ce{J}}{9.00\\times10^{16}\\,\\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}} \\approx 3.349\\times10^{-28}\\,\\ce{kg}=0.202 u$\n\n        However, this value only accounts for the mass defect caused by the increased binding energy of nucleons; in addition, it is necessary to include the decrease in nucleon mass by two neutrons, or $2.017u$:\n\n        - $\\Delta m=2.017 u+0.202 u\\approx2.22 u$\n\n        Or, in SI units,\n\n        - $\\Delta m \\approx 3.69\\times10^{-27}\\,\\ce{kg}$\n\n    2. Number of nuclei $=N_A\\dfrac{20\\,\\ce{g}}{239\\,\\ce{g}\\,\\textrm{mol}^{-1}}=5.04\\times10^{22}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\Delta m_{tot}=N\\Delta m=1.86\\times10^{-4}\\,\\ce{kg}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        The number of nuclei in the Pu-239 pellet is given by dividing the pellet’s mass (210 g) by the molar mass of Pu-239 (approximately 239 g mol$^{-1}$), then multiplying by Avogadro’s constant, $N_A$:\n\n        - $N=N_A \\dfrac{20\\,\\ce{g}}{239\\,\\ce{g}\\,\\textrm{mol}^{-1}}=6.02\\times10^{23}\\,\\textrm{mol}^{-1}\\dfrac{20\\,\\ce{g}}{239\\,\\ce{g}\\,\\textrm{mol}^{-1}}$\n\n        - $N = 5.04\\times10^{22}$\n\n        The total mass defect is equal to the mass loss per Pu-239 nucleus multiplied by the number of Pu-239 nuclei:\n\n        - $\\Delta m_{tot}=N\\Delta m=(5.04\\times10^{22})(3.69\\times10^{-27}\\,\\ce{kg})=1.86\\times10^{-4}\\,\\ce{kg}$\n\n        - $\\Delta m_{tot} = 186$ mg\n\n    3. ::indent\n        - $\\Delta m_{tot}g=-k\\Delta x$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\Delta x = 6.1\\,\\ce{mm}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        The change in displacement of the spring is related to the change in applied force by Hooke’s law:\n\n        - $\\Delta F=-k\\Delta x$\n\n        Substituting for the value of the mass loss yields\n\n        - $\\Delta F=\\Delta m_{tot}g=(-1.86\\times10^{-4}\\,\\ce{kg})(9.81\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-2})$\n\n        - $\\Delta F=-1.82\\times10^{-3}$ N\n\n        Finally, rearranging the equation gives\n\n        - $\\Delta x=-\\dfrac{\\Delta F}{k}=\\dfrac{1.82\\times10^{-3}\\,\\ce{N}}{0.3\\,\\ce{N}\\,\\ce{m}^{-1}}=6.07\\times10^{-3}\\,\\ce{m} = 6.1\\,\\ce{mm}$"},{"ref":"PH1819","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1819\n\nWhat is the primary role of the moderator in a uranium-235 nuclear reactor?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ To absorb excess neutrons\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ To protect workers from radiation\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ To transfer heat away from the reactor core\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ To slow down neutrons to sustain the chain reaction","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nWhen uranium-235 undergoes fission, it releases fast-moving neutrons. However, these neutrons have too much kinetic energy to efficiently cause further fission in other uranium-235 nuclei.\n\nThe moderator is a material (such as water, heavy water, or graphite) that slows down these fast neutrons through elastic collisions with its atoms. Slower neutrons (called thermal neutrons) have a much higher probability of being absorbed by uranium-235, allowing the chain reaction to continue at a controlled rate.\n\nA is incorrect. This is the role of control rods, which absorb neutrons to regulate the reaction.\n\nB is incorrect. Radiation shielding (such as thick concrete and lead layers) provides protection, not the moderator.\n\nC is incorrect. This is the job of the coolant, such as water or liquid sodium."}]},{"name":"Radioactive Decay","questions":[{"ref":"PH0210","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0210\n\nConsider the following radioactive decays:\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n\n  | | |\n\n  | :-- | :-- |\n\n  | I. | $\\alpha$ decay |\n\n  | II. | $\\beta$ decay |\n\n  | III. | $\\gamma$ decay |\n\nWhich of the decays produce a daughter nucleus of a different element from the parent nucleus?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nA change in the proton composition of a nucleus is needed to label it as a different nucleus. Both alpha ($\\alpha$) and beta ($\\beta$) decay change the proton and neutron number of the parent nucleus therefore a different daughter will be produced. On the other hand, gamma ($\\gamma$) decay involves only the loss of excess energy, not the change in nucleon composition; therefore the daughter is the same as the parent.\n\nTherefore, the answer is C."},{"ref":"PH0234","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0234\n\nAn element X is bombarded with alpha particles and a new element Y is formed with a neutron emitted. The following equation can be written for the nuclear reaction. \n\n$\\Large{{}^{4}_{2}\\mathrm{He} + {}^{\\square}_{A}\\mathrm{X} \\rightarrow {}^{\\square}_{B}\\mathrm{Y} + \\mathrm{n}}\n\n$\n\nThe atomic numbers of X and Y are $A$ and $B$ respectively. Which of the following is correct for $A$ and $B$? \n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $A-1=B$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $A+1=B$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $A-2=B$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $A+2=B$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nThe number of protons on both sides of the equation must be the same. \n\n- $2 + A = B + 0$\n\nTherefore the answer is D."},{"ref":"PH0235","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0235\n\nThe binding energy per nucleon of a $_{Z}^{A}\\textrm{X}$ nucleus is $E$. What is the mass defect for the formation of X  from its constituent nucleons?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{AE}{c^2}$\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{ZE}{c^2}$\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{E}{Ac^2}$\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{E}{Zc^2}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nBinding energy of X:\n\n- $(A)(E) = AE$\n\nBy using $E = mc^2$, mass defect:\n\n- $m =$ $\\dfrac{AE}{c^2}$\n\nTherefore the answer is A."},{"ref":"PH0301","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0301\n\nA nucleus $_{}^{A}\\textrm{X}$ has a mass defect of $N$ unified atomic mass units, $Nu$. What is the correct expression for its binding energy per nucleon?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ $931.5\\frac{\\ N}{A}$ MeV\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ $931.5\\frac{N}{A}$ MeV $c^{-2}$\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ $931.5\\frac{\\ A}{N}$ MeV\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ $931.5\\frac{\\ A}{N}$ MeV $c^{-2}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nFor one atomic mass unit ($u$), the mass defect is 931.5 MeV $c^{-2}$ \\\nTotal mass defect = $Nu$ = 931.5 N MeV $c^{-2}$ \\\nThis means the binding energy is 931.5 MeV\n\nFor binding energy per nucleon, dividing by nucleon number A gives $931.5\\frac{\\ N}{A}$ MeV. \\\nNote that the units of MeV $c^{-2}$ are mass units and not energy units."},{"ref":"PH0332","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0332\n\nThe diagram below shows the graph of binding energy per nucleon vs. the mass (nucleon) number of some elements.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Radioactive%20Decay/images/Screenshot%202026-04-27%20at%207.02.35%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-04-27 at 7.02.35 AM\">\n\nWhich is the most stable nucleus?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $_{}^{238}\\textrm{U}$\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $_{}^{2}\\textrm{H}$\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $_{}^{4}\\textrm{He}$\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $_{}^{32}\\textrm{S}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe binding energy per nucleon indicates the energy needed to remove a particle from the nucleus. The more energy needed to remove a particle from a nucleus, the more stable a nucleus is.\n\nOf the choices listed, $_{}^{32}\\textrm{S}$ has a binding energy per nucleon slightly greater than 8 MeV. $_{}^{238}\\textrm{U}$ is slightly less than 8 MeV. The values for $_{}^{2}\\textrm{H}$ and $_{}^{4}\\textrm{He}$ are much lower."},{"ref":"PH0335","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0335\n\nConsider the following statements about nuclear binding energy:\n\nThe nuclear binding energy is:\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$proportional to the mass defect from nucleus formation.\n- II. $\\hspace{0.7em}$the energy released when a nucleus is formed from its nucleons.\n- III. $\\hspace{0.4em}$the energy required to remove a proton from the nucleus.\n\nWhich of the statements are correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nStatement I is true. The nuclear binding energy is the energy released when a nucleus is formed from its constituent nucleons. The final nucleus will have less mass than the separately considered nucleons, and the difference in that mass is known as the mass defect. The mass defect is related to the binding energy of the nucleus by $\\Delta E = \\Delta mc^2$.\n\nStatement II is true, as it gives the definition of nuclear binding energy.\n\nStatement III is not true. The energy required to remove a particle from a nucleus is similar to the binding energy per nucleon, whereas nuclear binding energy refers to the total energy required to completely separate all nucleons in a nucleus."},{"ref":"PH0337","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0337\n\nA nuclide of a radioactive element Q can be represented as $\\ce{^{234}_{90}Q}$.  The nuclide undergoes two negative beta decays and one alpha decay. Which of the following configurations correctly represents the daughter nuclide R?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{^{234}_{92}R}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{^{232}_{92}R}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{^{230}_{90}R}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\ce{^{230}_{92}R}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nQ has a mass number of 234 and atomic number of 90. This means that Q has 234 nucleons, and 90 protons.\n\nSince beta particles have mass number zero and charge number $-1$, the nucleus will gain a $+2$ charge while the mass number will remain unchanged. This gives a mass number of 234 and the charge number will become 92. \n\nIn the next step, the emission of 1 alpha particle will diminish the mass number by 4 and the charge number by 2 resulting in R having a mass number of 230 and a charge number of 90."},{"ref":"PH0338","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0338\n\nA 1.20 kg sample of a radioactive isotope decaying via beta decay with a half-life of 30 minutes is observed in a closed container in a laboratory. The decay results in a daughter nucleus that is also a solid. What is the approximate total mass measured after 90 minutes?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.00 kg\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.15 kg\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.60 kg\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1.20 kg","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nFor each half-life, half of the remaining nuclides in the sample decay to a different nuclide. In this situation, three half lives occur in the 90 minutes, so only one eighth of the original isotope remains. Because the daughter nuclides are also solid, however, they remain in the sample.\n\nThe decay products of beta decay include the daughter nuclide, the beta particle (an electron), and an anti-neutrino. The mass of the electron and the anti-neutrino are negligible compared to the parent nuclide.\n\nBecause the daughter nuclides contain the same number of nucleons as the parent nuclides, there is effectively no change in mass of the sample."},{"ref":"PH0345","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0345\n\nA stationary nucleus of uranium-238 undergoes alpha decay to form thorium-234. The initial speed of the alpha particle is $v$. \n\nWhat is correct about the magnitude of the thorium-234 velocity and its direction?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Magnitude | Direction |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{4}{234}v$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Same as the alpha particle$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{234}{238}v$ | Opposite to the alpha particle |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{234}{238}v$ | Same as the alpha particle |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{4}{234}v$ | Opposite to the alpha particle |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe uranium-238 is stationary; this means its velocity is zero. \\\n\nThe alpha particle has an atomic mass 4.\n\nUsing the law of conservation of momentum,\n\n \n\nMomentum before = momentum after\n\n- $m_U v_U=m_{Th} v_{Th}+m_\\alpha v_\\alpha$\n\n- $0=234v_{Th}+4v$\n\n- $v_{Th}=\\dfrac{-4}{234}v$\n\nThe negative sign indicates that the direction of thorium-234 velocity is opposite to that of the alpha particle."},{"ref":"PH0360","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0360\n\nThe binding energy of $_{2}^{4}\\textrm{He}$ is 28 MeV. What is its binding energy per nucleon?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 45 $\\times$ $10^{-13}$ J\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 22 $\\times$ $10^{-13}$ J\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 11 $\\times$ $10^{-13}$ J\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 7.5 $\\times$ $10^{-13}$ J","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nTo get the value of the binding energy per nucleon, the binding energy is divided by the number of nucleons\n\n$\\hspace{3em}$ $\\dfrac{28}{4}=7$ MeV\n\nSince 1 eV $= 1.6 \\times$ $10^{-19}$ J, converting to joules gives\n\n$\\hspace{3em}$ ($7\\times{10}^6)(1.6\\times{10}^{-19})=(7)(1.6)\\times{10}^{-13}=11.2\\times{10}^{-13}$ J\n\nTo estimate such a value without calculator, first add the powers of 10: $6 -19= -13$ \\\nThen, $(7)(1.6)$ = 11.2 is less than $(7)(2)=14$, so the nearest answer to this from the choices will only be C."},{"ref":"PH0465","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0465\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe plutonium isotope $\\ce{^{238}_{94}Pu}$  undergoes alpha decay.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Radioactive%20Decay/images/PH0465a%20.png\" alt=\"PH0465a \">\n\n1. Define isotope. [2 marks]\n\n2. Rewrite this decay equation, filling in any missing information. [3 marks]\n\n3. Plutonium undergoes a decay series. The graph below shows a part of this decay series. \n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Radioactive%20Decay/images/PH0465.png\" alt=\"PH0465\">\n\n    $\\small{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra, - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\n    1. One type of decay cannot be represented on this diagram. Outline this type of decay and justify your answer. [2 marks]\n\n    2. State the type of decay of $\\ce{^{230}_{90}Th}$ that is represented on the diagram above. Justify your answer. [2 marks]\n\n    \n\n    3. Isotope X undergoes an alpha decay followed by a beta minus decay to produce isotope Y.  Label the position of isotope Y on the diagram. [1 mark]\n\n4. Compare the penetrating powers of alpha, beta and gamma radiation. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Nuclei having same proton number with different nucleon (mass) number / nuclides that have the same proton number but different nucleon number / same number of protons different number of neutrons. $\\checkmark$\n\n2. $\\hspace{1em}$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Radioactive%20Decay/images/PH0465B.png\" alt=\"PH0465B\">\n\n    Correct symbol of alpha **AND** correct mass number of alpha **AND** correct number of charges of alpha $\\checkmark$ \\\n\n    Conservation of mass number $\\checkmark$ \\\n\n    Conservation of charge $\\checkmark$\n\n3. \n\n    1. Gamma. $\\checkmark$ \\\n\n        Photon emission at distinct energies/ doesn't change the nucleus/ no changes in mass or atomic numbers. $\\checkmark$\n\n    \n\n    2. Beta minus decay. $\\checkmark$ \\\n\n        Atomic number increases by 1 and no change in mass number. $\\checkmark$\n\n    3. Y at mass number of 224 and atomic number of 88. $\\checkmark$\n\n       \n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Radioactive%20Decay/images/PH0465-2.png\" alt=\"PH0465-2\">\n\n        $\\small{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra, - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\n4. Penetrating power of alpha is weak and beta is moderate and gamma is strong / alpha is stopped by a sheet of paper and beta is stopped by an aluminum sheet and gamma is stopped by a thick sheet of lead. $\\checkmark$"},{"ref":"PH0466","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0466\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nThe graph shows the variation of the measured activity of an isotope sample with time.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Radioactive%20Decay/images/PH0466-1.png\" alt=\"PH0466-1\">\n\n$\\small{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra, - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\n1. State what is meant by activity. [1 mark]\n\n2. Explain why the activity graph doesn’t approach zero as time increases. [2 marks]\n\n3. The activity graph is corrected so as to reach zero as time increases as shown below.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Radioactive%20Decay/images/PH0466-2.png\" alt=\"PH0466-2\">\n\n    $\\small{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra, - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\n    1. Determine the half-life of the sample. [1 mark]\n\n    \n\n    2. The initial mass of this sample was 248 g. Determine the time that would elapse before 31 g of the sample remained. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. The number of radioactive decays per unit time. $\\checkmark$\n\n2. Due to background radiation. $\\checkmark$ \\\n\n    Background radiation is radioactive ionizations detected when there is no radioactive source (OWTTE). $\\checkmark$\n\n    \n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    Background radiation is always present, with radioactive sources in the environment and also in cosmic rays.\n\n3. \n\n    1. :answer[10 $-$ 12 days] $\\checkmark$\n\n    \n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        To determine the half-life from the graph, determine the maximum activity value which is 300 Bq \\\n\n        Then the half-life is the time where the activity has halved which is 150 Bq \\\n\n        From the graph find the time corresponds to this activity\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Radioactive%20Decay/images/PH0466-3.png\" alt=\"PH0466-3\">\n\n        $\\small{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra, - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\n    2. 3 half-lives. $\\checkmark$ \\\n\n        :answer[30 $-$ 36 days] $\\checkmark$\n\n        \n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The remaining mass ratio to the initial mass is\n\n        - $\\dfrac{31}{248}=\\dfrac{1}{8}$\n\n        Which corresponds to 3 half-lives \n\n        - $\\dfrac{1}{8}=\\Bigl(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\Bigr)\\Bigl(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\Bigr)\\Bigl(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\Bigr)$\n\n        Since the half-life is 12 days, so the time taken is $(3)(12) = 36$ days."},{"ref":"PH0473","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0473\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe graph shows the variation of the binding energy per nucleon with the mass number $A$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Radioactive%20Decay/images/PH0473-1.png\" alt=\"PH0473-1\">$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\n1.  State what is meant by binding energy. [1 mark]\n\nNickel-62 is energetically stable; it doesn’t release energy by fission or fusion.\n\n2. Label the position of Nickel-62 on the graph above. [1 mark]\n\n3. The following reaction represents a nuclear fusion reaction:\n\n    $\\hspace{3em}$ $_{1}^{2}$H + $_{1}^{2}$H $\\rightarrow$ $_{2}^{3}$He + $_{Z}^{A}$X\n\n    1. State the nature of X along with its values for $A$ and $Z$. [2 marks]\n\n    The following data is given about the reaction:\n\n    $\\hspace{3em}$ The nuclear mass of H-2 = 2.014 u \\\n    $\\hspace{3em}$ The nuclear mass of He-3 = 3.016 u\n\n    2. Calculate the energy released from the reaction. [3 marks]\n\n    3. All the energy released from the reaction goes into kinetic energy. Assume that both of the $_{1}^{2}$H particles are initially at rest.  Determine the speed of X. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Energy released when a nucleus is formed from its constituent nucleons $\\checkmark$\\\n    **OR** \\\n    The minimum energy needed to break a nucleus up into its constituent nucleons. $\\checkmark$\n\n2. At the maximum point of the curve. $\\checkmark$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Radioactive%20Decay/images/PH0473_2.png\" alt=\"PH0473_2\">    $\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nNuclear reactions occur such that there is an increase in binding energy per nucleon. The most stable nuclide will have the highest binding energy per nucleon.\n\n3.\n    1. X is a neutron $\\checkmark$ \\\nMass number $A=1$ **AND** atomic number $Z=0$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Charge and mass must be conserved.\n        Therefore the mass numbers on each side must total 4, and the charge numbers must total 2.\n\n        $\\hspace{2em}$ $_{1}^{2}$H + $_{1}^{2}$H $\\rightarrow$ $_{2}^{3}$He + $_{0}^{1}$n\n\n    2. ::indent\n        - $\\Delta m=(2)(2.014)-(3.016+1.008665)=0.0033\\ll \\ce{u}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n        - $E=\\ll\\Delta mc^2\\gg$\n        - $E=(0.0033)(931.5)$ $\\checkmark$\n        - $E=3.07\\ll\\ \\ce{MeV}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    3. ::indent\n        - $p_{He}=p_n$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $E_n=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\times\\ E$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $v_n=\\ll\\sqrt{\\dfrac{(\\frac{3}{4})(3.07\\times{10}^6)(1.6\\times{10}^{-19})}{(\\frac{1}{2})(1.675\\times{10}^{-27})}}\\gg=2.09\\times{10}^7\\ll\\ $m s$^{-1}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        **OR**\n\n        - $p_{He}=p_n$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $p=\\ll\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2E}{\\left(\\frac{1}{m_{He}}+\\frac{1}{m_n}\\right)}}\\gg=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{(2)(3.07\\times{10}^6)(1.6\\times{10}^{-19})}{\\left(\\frac{1}{(3.016)(1.661\\times{10}^{-27})}+\\frac{1}{1.675\\times{10}^{-27}}\\right)}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $v_n=\\ll\\dfrac{p}{m_n}\\gg=2.09\\times{10}^7\\ll \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Energy released is the sum of kinetic energies of He-3 and the neutron.\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $E=E_{He}+E_n$\n\n    Due to conservation of momentum, and since the initial momentum before the reaction is zero, then the momentum of He-3 is equal in magnitude to the momentum of the neutron and opposite in direction\n\n    - $p_{He}=p_n$\n\n    Because $E=\\dfrac{p^2}{2m}$, the ratio between the kinetic energy of the neutron to that of the He-3 is:\n\n    - $\\dfrac{E_n}{E_{He}}=\\dfrac{m_{He}}{m_n}=\\dfrac{3.016}{1.008665}\\approx\\dfrac{3}{1}$\n\n    To calculate the velocity,\n\n    - $E_n=\\dfrac{3}{4}E=\\dfrac{1}{2}m_n{v_n}^2$\n\n    - $\\dfrac{3}{4}(3.07\\times{10}^6)(1.6\\times{10}^{-19})=\\dfrac{1}{2}(1.675\\times{10}^{-27}){v_n}^2$\n\n    - $v_n=2.09\\times{10}^7\\,$m s$^{-1}$\n\n    **OR**\n\n    - $E=\\dfrac{p^2}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{m_{He}}+\\dfrac{1}{m_n}\\right)$\n\n    - $(3.07\\times{10}^6)(1.6\\times{10}^{-19})=\\dfrac{p^2}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{(3.016)(1.661\\times{10}^{-27})}+\\dfrac{1}{1.675\\times{10}^{-27}}\\right)$\n\n    - $p=m_nv_n$\n\n    therefore,\n\n    - $v_n=2.09\\times{10}^7$ m s$^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH0505","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0505\n\nThe activity of a 100\\% pure radioactive sample is ${10}^6$ Bq. The activity of the sample after 20\\% of nuclei have decayed is:\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}      0.2\\ \\times{\\ 10}^6$ Bq\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}    0.4\\ \\times\\ {10}^6$ Bq\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}      0.6\\ \\times{\\ 10}^6$ Bq\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}      0.8\\ \\times{\\ 10}^6$ Bq","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe activity of a sample is proportional to the number of radioactive nuclei: $A\\propto N$.\n\nLet $\\lambda$ be the decay constant, and $N$ be the initial number of nuclei.\n\nThen,\n\n- $\\lambda N={10}^6$ Bq\n\nIf 20\\% decay, then 80\\% remain and the new activity will be\n\n- $A= \\lambda(0.80N)=0.80 \\lambda N = 0.8 \\times 10^6$ Bq"},{"ref":"PH0590","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0590\n\nEstimate the speed of an alpha particle that has accelerated from rest in a uniform electric field $E = 50\\,$N C$^{-1}$ over a distance of 2.0 m.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $10^3 \\ $m s$^{-1}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $10^4 \\ $m s$^{-1}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $10^5 \\ $m s$^{-1}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $10^9 \\ $m s$^{-1}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nUsing $E=\\ \\dfrac{F}{q}$ from the data booklet, as well as $v^2=u^2+2as$ and $F=ma$,\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2em}v^2&=u^2+2as \\\\\nv&=\\sqrt{2as} \\\\\n\\hspace{2em}\\\\\nF&=ma \\\\\na&=\\ \\frac{F}{m} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}$\n\nGiving\n$v=\\sqrt{2\\dfrac{F}{m}s}$\n\nAnd $F=qE$\n\nTherefore, $v=\\sqrt{2\\dfrac{qE}{m}s}$\n\nthe speed of the alpha particle is\n\n- $v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2Eqs}{m}}\\approx\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2(50\\,\\ce{N C}^{-1})(3.2 \\times  {10}^{-19}\\,\\ce{C})(2\\ \\ce{m})}{6.4 \\times  {10}^{-27}\\,\\ce{kg}}}$\n\nTherefore\n\n- $v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{6.4\\ \\times \\ {10}^{-17}\\ \\ce{ N m}}{6.4\\ \\times \\ {10}^{-27}\\ \\ce{kg}}}=\\sqrt{{10}^{10}\\ \\dfrac{\\ce{m}^2}{\\ce{s}^2}}={10}^5\\ $m s$^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH0867","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0867\n\nThe count rate is measured at a point near a radioactive sample. The average background count rate is constant throughout. The graph below shows how the count rate varies with time.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Radioactive%20Decay/images/Screenshot%202026-04-06%20at%208.01.12%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-04-06 at 8.01.12 AM\">\n\nWhich row correctly shows the half-life of the radioactive sample and the background count rate?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$ Half-life of sample / s $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Background count rate / s$^{-1}$ $\\hspace{1em}$\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n$\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 0.4 | 0.5\n$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 0.4 | 0.75\n$\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 0.8 | 0.5\n$\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 0.8 | 0.75","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe graph trends towards the background rate as the count rate from the sample decays exponentially. The graph levels out at 0.5 s$^{-1}$, therefore the background count rate is 0.5 s$^{-1}$.\n\nThe half-life of the sample is independent of the background count rate. To find the initial count rate of the sample we must subtract the background count rate from the total initial count rate: $1.5-0.5=1\\, \\ce{s}^{-1}$.\n\nAfter one half-life the count rate of the sample will have halved $\\text{\\textendash}$ so the sample count rate will be 0.5 s$^{-1}$ $\\text{\\textendash}$ the background count rate is constant so will still be 0.5 s$^{-1}$.\n\nTherefore the total count rate after one half-life is 1 s$^{-1}$.\n\nUsing the graph, we can see that when the count rate is 1 s$^{-1}$ the time is 0.4 s so the half-life is 0.4 s.\n\nThe answer is **A**."},{"ref":"PH0884","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0884\n\nNuclide X decays with a half-life of $T$ and nuclide Y decays with a half-life of $3T$. At $t = 0$, a sample of nuclide X has eight times the activity of a sample of nuclide Y.\n\nWhen $t = 6 T$, the activity of the sample of nuclide X is $A$. What is the activity of the sample of nuclide Y at this time?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{1}{16} A$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{1}{2} A$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2 A$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $16 A$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\n\nLet the initial activity of nucleus Y be $A_{0}$. The initial activity of nucleus X is then $8A_{0}$.\n\nFor sample X, six half-lives have passed at $6T\\left( \\dfrac{6T}{T} =6 \\right)$. Activity will halve six times so the activity of sample X at $t = 6 T$ is given by:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $8 A_{0}\\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) ^{6} = \\dfrac{8 A_{0}}{64} = \\dfrac{A_{0}}{8}$\n\nFor sample Y, two half-lives have passed at $6 T \\left( \\dfrac{6 T}{3 T}\\ =\\ 2 \\right)$. Activity will halve twice, so the activity of sample Y at $t = 6 T$ is given by:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $A_{0} \\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) ^{2}\\ =\\ \\dfrac{A_{0}}{4}$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{\\textrm{Activity of sample X}}{\\textrm{Activity of sample Y}} = \\dfrac{A_{0}}{8} \\div \\dfrac{A_{0}}{4} = \\left( \\dfrac{A_{0}}{8} \\right) \\left( \\dfrac{4}{A_{0}} \\right) = \\dfrac{4}{8} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$\n\nTherefore, the activity of sample Y is two times that of sample X, which gives $2A$."},{"ref":"PH0948","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0948\n\nWhich of the following defines the term “isotope”?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ A species of atom whose nucleus contains a specified number of protons and a specified number of neutrons.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ A species of atom containing a specified number of protons and a specified number of electrons.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Different forms of the same element having same nucleon number and a different proton number.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Different forms of the same element having same proton number and a different nucleon number.","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nA defines the term $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$nuclide”.\n\nB and C are slight variations on correct definitions that do not define anything in particular.\n\nD is the definition of an isotope. An example of two isotopes would be carbon-12 ($\\ce{^{12}_{6}C}$) and carbon-14 ($\\ce{^{14}_{6}C}$)."},{"ref":"PH0949","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0949\n\nThe graph below shows a graph of binding energy per nucleon against mass number for some nuclei. Nuclei P, Q, R, S, T and U are labelled.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Radioactive%20Decay/images/PH0949.png\" alt=\"PH0949\">\n\nWhich of the following nuclear reactions involving nuclei P, Q, R, S, T and U will result in the largest amount of energy released per reaction?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ P + Q $\\rarr$ R\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ R $\\rarr$ P + Q\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ S + T $\\rarr$ U\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ U $\\rarr$ S + T","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nEnergy will be released when binding energy per nucleon increases so we can disregard B and C (these require the input of energy, as binding energy per nucleon decreases). Note that B represents the fission of a small nucleus and C represents the fusion of large nuclei.\n\nComparing the difference in binding energy per nucleon (difference in height on graph), we can see that it is slightly greater for reaction P + Q $\\rarr$ R compared to U $\\rarr$ S + T. So energy released per nucleon is greater for the reaction P + Q $\\rarr$ R.\n\n**However, we have been asked for energy released per reaction.**\n\nEnergy released per reaction = (Difference in binding energy per nucleon) $\\times$ (number of nucleons per reaction)\n\nThe number of nucleons involved in each reaction U $\\rarr$ S + T is much greater than the number of nucleons involved in each reaction P + Q $\\rarr$ R (around 10 times greater) So energy released per reaction is greater for U $\\rarr$ S + T."},{"ref":"PH0963","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0963\n\n$\\ce{^{10}_{6}C}$ decays according to the equation\n\n$\\ce{^{10}_{6}C}\\rarr \\ce{X} + \\ce{^{0}_{1}\\beta}^+ + \\ce{Y} $\n\nWhich row correctly identifies X and Y?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1.5em}$ X $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$ Y $\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\ce{^{10}_{5}B}$ | ${\\nu}_e$ |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\ce{^{10}_{5}B}$ | $\\overline{{\\nu}_e}$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\ce{^{10}_{7}N}$ | ${\\nu}_e$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\ce{^{10}_{7}N}$ | $\\overline{{\\nu}_e}$ |","markScheme":"**答案：A**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nCharge is conserved so 6 = Proton number of element X + 1.\nProton number of element X = 5. Element X must be $\\ce{^{10}_{5}B}$.\n\nLepton number is conserved. ${\\beta}^+$ has a lepton number  of $-1$ and both nuclei have a lepton number of 0 so. $0 = -1 +$ lepton number of neutrino. Lepton number of neutrino = 1 so neutrino is an electron neutrino (${\\nu}_e$).\n\n${\\beta}^+$ decay involves a proton inside the nucleus decaying into a neutron, a positron (${\\beta}^+$) and an electron neutrino."},{"ref":"PH0983","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0983\n\nAn atom of $\\ce{^{A}_{Z}X}$ follows a sequence of three decays: beta plus, alpha, beta plus.\n\nWhat is the number of neutrons in the final nucleus produced?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $A$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $A-Z$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $A-4$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $A-Z-4$","markScheme":"**答案：B**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nDuring beta plus decay a proton in the nucleus decays into a neutron (a positron and an electron neutrino). Neutron numbers increase by 1.\n\nDuring alpha decay the nucleus loses 2 protons and 2 neutrons (an alpha particle), so neutron number decreases by 2.\n\n$A$ is the nucleon number (number of protons + neutrons) and $Z$ is the proton number.\n\nInitially the nucleus has $A-Z$ neutrons.\n\n2 neutrons are lost (in alpha decay) and 2 are gained (one in each beta decay) so overall neutron number does not change. Final neutron number is $A-Z$.\n\nThis can be also shown by looking at each stage of the decay process:\n\n- After decay 1 (beta plus):\nNeutron number $= A-Z+1$\n\n- After decay 2 (alpha):\nNeutron number $= A-Z+1-2=A-Z-1$\n\n- After decay 3 (beta plus):\nNeutron number $= A-Z-1+1=A-Z$"},{"ref":"PH0984","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0984\n\nThe total mass of a small number of completely separated nucleons is $m_1$. All of the nucleons join together to form a nucleus of mass $m_2$.\n\nBelow are three statements.\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | $m_1$  is greater than $m_2$   |\n  | II. | The difference in energy between the nucleus and the total mass of the separated nucleons is the difference of $m_1$  and $m_2$ multiplied by the speed of light squared |\n  | III. | Energy is released when the nucleus is formed from the separated nucleons |\n\nWhich of the statements are correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nI is correct $-$ The mass of a nucleus is always less than the mass of its completely separated constituent nucleons.\n\nII is correct $-$ The $\\text{\\textquoteleft}$extra' mass of the constituent nucleons $(m_1-m_2)$ is converted into energy when the nucleus is formed. $\\Delta E=\\Delta mc^2$, i.e. energy released = difference of $m_1$ and $m_2$ multiplied by the speed of light squared.\n\nIII is correct $-$ Energy is released (mass is converted into energy)."},{"ref":"PH1032-1","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1032-1\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n1. 1. A single nucleus of $\\ce{^{210}_{84}Po}$ has a mass of $3.4203\\times 10^{-25}\\,kg$. Calculate the mass defect on formation of $\\ce{^{210}_{84}Po}$. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Polonium $\\ce{^{210}_{84}Po}$ decays into a nuclide of lead (Pb) through the emission of an alpha particle. Give the equation for this decay. [1 mark]\n\n    3. Explain, in terms of binding energy, why energy is released in this decay process. [1 mark]\n\n2. The graph below shows the number of nuclei against time in years for a small sample of $\\ce{^{210}_{84}Po}$. Initially the sample contains $N_0$ polonium nuclei and zero lead nuclei.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Radioactive%20Decay/images/2032aaa.png\" alt=\"2032aaa\">\n\n    On the graph above, sketch how the number of lead nuclei varies with time. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. Determination of 126 neutrons  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $84m_p+126m_n-3.4203\\times 10^{-25}=84(1.673\\times 10^{-27})+126(1.675\\times 10^{-27})-3.4203\\times 10^{-25}=9.552\\times 10^{-27}\\,kg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    The mass of a nucleus is always less than the total mass of its constituent nucleons, the difference in mass is called the mass defect.\n\n        $\\ce{^{210}_{84}Po}$ has 84 protons and $(210-84=)126$ neutrons so we may write:\n\n        - Mass defect $=84m_p+126m_n-3.4203\\times 10^{-25}$\n\n        The masses of a proton and a neutron can be found in the data booklet.\n\n        - $84(1.673\\times 10^{-27})+126(1.675\\times 10^{-27})-3.4203\\times 10^{-25}=9.552\\times 10^{-27}\\,kg$\n\n    2. $\\ce{^{210}_{84}Po} \\rightarrow \\ce{^{206}_{82}Pb} + \\ce{^{4}_{2}\\alpha}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Allow He for $\\alpha$ \\\n        Ignore gamma\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        An alpha particle is a nucleus of helium. Comprised of two protons and two neutrons.\n\n        The top left number (mass number) and bottom left number (atomic number) are conserved.\n\n        $\\ce{^{210}_{84}Po}$ nucleus loses 4 nucleons so mass number decreases by 4: $210-4=206$\n\n        $\\ce{^{210}_{84}Po}$ nucleus loses 2 protons so atomic number decreases by 2: $84-2=82$\n\n    3. $\\ce{^{206}_{82}Pb}$/ Lead has a higher binding energy $\\bold{per\\,\\,nucleon}$ than $\\ce{^{210}_{84}Po}$ / Polonium\n\n        OR\n\n        $\\ce{^{206}_{82}Pb}$/ Lead and alpha combined have a higher binding than that of $\\ce{^{210}_{84}Po}$ / Polonium  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        Binding energy is the energy required to separate a nucleus into its constituent nucleons. It is also the energy released when the completely separated nucleons combine to form that nucleus.\n\n        Energy is released when the total binding energy of the decay products is greater than that of the original nucleus.\n\n        Below is a curve showing binding energy per nucleon against mass number.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Radioactive%20Decay/images/2032ccc.png\" alt=\"2032ccc\">\n\n        $\\ce{^{206}_{82}Pb}$ has a greater binding energy per nucleon than $\\ce{^{210}_{84}Po}$. So total binding energy of $\\ce{^{206}_{82}Pb}$ and alpha is greater than that of $\\ce{^{210}_{84}Po}$ alone.\n\n2. correct shape - increasing from 0 to about $0.9 N_0$  $\\checkmark$\n\n    crosses given line at $0.50 N_0$  $\\checkmark$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Radioactive%20Decay/images/2032bbb.png\" alt=\"2032bbb\">\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    For every polonium nucleus that decays, a lead nucleus forms. The total number of lead and polonium nuclei will add to $N_0$ at all times."},{"ref":"PH1877","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1877\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\n1. 1. Molybdenum-99 $\\big( \\ce{^{99}_{42}Mo} \\big)$ decays into Technetium-99 $\\big( \\ce{^{99}_{43}Tc} \\big)$. State the names of the particles emitted during this decay. [1 mark]\n\n    2. A radiation detector is placed near a small sample of Molybdenum-99. The initial count rate of the detector is 8.5 Bq. After three half-lives have passed, the count rate falls to 1.5 Bq. Assuming a constant flux of background radiation, determine the background count rate. [2 marks]\n\nThe graph shows how the activity of the sample $A$ varies with time $t$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Radioactive%20Decay/images/ph1877q.png\" alt=\"ph1877q\">\n\n2. Determine the half-life of the sample. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. 1. $\\beta^-$/Beta minus **AND** antineutrino $\\checkmark$\n\n        Allow electron / $e^-$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        During this decay the nucleon number stays the same while the proton number increases by 1. This means that the neutron has decayed into a proton.\n\n        During this process a $\\beta^-$ particle (a fast-moving electron) is emitted from the nucleus, conserving electric charge.\n\n        Whenever a $\\beta^-$ particle is produced, an antineutrino is also produced.\n\n        The full equation for $\\beta^-$ decay includes the emission of a neutral electron antineutrino:\n\n        - $\\ce{^{1}_{0}n}\\rightarrow\\ce{^{1}_{1}p}+\\ce{^{0}_{-1}\\beta}+\\ce{^{0}_{0}\\bar{\\nu}}$\n\n    2. One correct equation for background count e.g.,\n\n        - 8.5 = background count + initial count from source\n\n        - 1.5 = background count + final count from source\n\n        **OR**\n\n        - Initial count rate from the source = 8 Bq\n\n        **OR**\n\n        - Final count rate from source = 1 Bq $\\checkmark$\n\n        - Background count rate = :answer[0.5 Bq] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The count total rate of the detector $C_T$ is the sum of the count rate due to background radiation $C_B$ and the count rate due to the radioactive source $C_S$:\n\n        - $C_T=C_B+C_S$\n\n        $C_B$ remains constant while $C_S$ varies with time. Specifically, $C_S$ will half with each half life.\n\n        If we let the initial count rate from the source be $C_S$, then the count rate after 3 half lives is given by:\n\n        - $C_S\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)=C_S\\left(\\dfrac{1}{8}\\right)=\\dfrac{C_S}{8}$\n\n        The initial total count rate is given by:\n\n        - $8.5=C_B+C_S$\n\n        The total count rate after 3 half lives is given by:\n\n        - $1.5=C_B+\\dfrac{C_S}{8}$\n\n        Taking these equations away from each other, we have:\n\n        - $8.5-1.5=C_B-C_B+C_S-\\dfrac{C_S}{8}$\n\n        - $7=\\dfrac{7}{8}C_S$\n\n        Therefore, the original rate emitted by the source is:\n\n        - $C_S=8$ Bq\n\n        Background count rate is therefore:\n\n        - $C_B=8.5-8=0.5$ Bq\n\n2. :answer[$2.4\\times10^5$ s] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Radioactive%20Decay/images/Screenshot%202025-08-28%20at%208.50.21%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-08-28 at 8.50.21 AM\">\n\n    Half-life $T_\\frac{1}{2}$ can be defined as the time taken for the activity of a sample to halve. To find the half-life of this sample we can read the value of $t$ from the graph when $A=\\dfrac{2\\times10^5}{2}=1\\times10^5$ Bq.\n\n    We can see that the half-life of the sample is around $2.4\\times10^5$."},{"ref":"PH1881","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1881\n\nOne mole of deuterium nuclei $\\ce{^{2}_{1}H}$ undergoes the following fusion reaction.\n\n$\\ce{^{2}_{1}H}+\\ce{^{2}_{1}H}\\rightarrow\\ce{^{4}_{2}He}$\n\nThe total energy released in the fusion reaction is $Y$ and the binding energy per mole of deuterium is $X$.\n\nWhat is the binding energy per mole of $\\ce{^{4}_{2}He}$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $X-Y$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2X-2Y$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $X+Y$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2X+2Y$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nBinding energy per mole is the energy required to break all of the nuclei in one mole of a substance into its constituent nucleons.\n\nOne $\\ce{^{4}_{2}He}$ nucleus is formed for every two $\\ce{^{2}_{1}H}$ nuclei, so if one mole of $\\ce{^{2}_{1}H}$ nuclei react, the amount of helium produced is 0.5 mol.\n\nThe energy required to break one mole of $\\ce{^{2}_{1}H}$ nuclei into its constituent nucleons is $X$.\n\nThe energy **released** in the reaction is $Y$ so the energy required to break apart one mole of helium formed in the reaction is $X+Y$.\n\nTherefore, the amount of energy required to break apart one mole of $\\ce{^{4}_{2}He}$ (i.e., the binding energy per mole of $\\ce{^{4}_{2}He}$) is given by:\n\n- $\\dfrac{X+Y}{0.5}=2X+2Y$"},{"ref":"PH1887","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1887\n\nA radioactive source emits two types of radiation. Most of the radiation is stopped by a thin sheet of paper and all of it is stopped by around a 0.5 cm sheet of aluminum. Which best describes the nature of the radiation?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Mostly alpha with some beta\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Mostly alpha with some gamma\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Mostly beta with some alpha\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Mostly gamma with some alpha","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAlpha radiation is easily stopped by a thin sheet of paper (or even a few centimetres of air), so if most of the radiation is blocked by paper, the source must be emitting mostly alpha particles.\n\nBeta radiation can pass through paper but is stopped by materials like aluminium a few millimetres thick. All of the remaining radiation is stopped by a 0.5 cm sheet of aluminium, the second type must be beta particles.\n\nGamma radiation, on the other hand, is much more penetrating and would not be completely stopped by such a thin aluminium sheet.\n\nTherefore, the source is emitting mostly alpha radiation, with some beta radiation."}]},{"name":"Stellar Properties & Processes","questions":[{"ref":"PH0333","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0333\n\nWhich statement describes a possible nuclear fusion reaction?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrogen nuclei join together to form a helium nucleus.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ A helium nucleus joins with a hydrogen nucleus to form an alpha particle.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Uranium nuclei split and produce high energy neutrons causing a chain reaction.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Helium nuclei join to form hydrogen nuclei.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nA represents the best conditions for this, in that hydrogen nuclei are smaller than helium nuclei. \\\nB shows at least one reactant particle identical to the only product particle. This is not balanced. \\\nC shows a larger particle splitting into smaller ones. This is called fission. \\\nD is also unbalanced because 2 larger atoms would not combine to form a smaller one."},{"ref":"PH0699","marks":"14","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0699\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\n1. VV Cephei is a binary star system composed of two stars referred to as VV Cephei A and VV Cephei B.\n\n    1. The measured wavelength of the peak spectral line of VV Cephei A is 760 nm. Show that the surface temperature of VV Cephei A is approximately 3800 K.        [2 marks]\n\n    2. The surface temperature of the Sun is $T_\\odot=5800$ K. The luminosity of the VV Cephei A is $2.7 \\times 10^5$  times the Sun's luminosity $L_\\odot$. Determine the radius of  VV Cephei A in terms of the Sun's radius $R_\\odot$.     [2 marks]\n\n    A Herzsprung-Russell (HR) diagram is shown.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Stellar%20Properties%20%26%20Processes/images/PH0699.png\" alt=\"PH0699\">\n\n    3. Plot the current position of VV Cephei A on the HR diagram and identify the classification of VV Cephei A.  [2 marks]\n\n2.  The mass of VV Cephei B is about 11 times the mass of the Sun and it is not on the main sequence.\n\n    1.  In terms of forces, explain the condition that causes a star to first leave the main sequence.  [2 marks]\n\n    2. Explain why VV Cephei B is likely to remain on the main sequence for a shorter time than the Sun.          [2 marks]\n\n    3. The parallax angle for VV Cephei B is 0.31 arcseconds. Calculate, in light years, the distance between the Earth and VV Cephei B.      [2 marks]\n\n    4. State why the parallax method is not suitable for measuring the distance of very distant stars.        [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1. ::indent\n        - $T=\\dfrac{2.9\\times 10^{-3}}{760\\times 10^{-9} }$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - = :answer[$3820\\,\\ce{K}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        By using Wien's Displacement Law:\n\n        - $T=\\dfrac{2.9\\times10^{-3}}{\\lambda_\\textrm{max}}$\n\n        - $T=\\dfrac{2.9\\times 10^{-3}}{760\\times 10^{-9} } =3820$ K\n\n    2. ::indent\n        - $R=\\dfrac{R_\\odot 5800^2}{3 800^2}  \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2.7\\times10^5 L_\\odot}{L_\\odot }}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\hspace{1em}= 1200R_\\odot$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        By using the Stefan-Boltzmann constant for a black body:\n\n        - $L=\\sigma AT^4$\n\n        The following relation can be obtained for the ratio of the luminosities of two stars:\n\n        - $\\dfrac{L_1}{L_2} =\\dfrac{\\sigma A_1 (T_1)^4}{\\sigma A_2 (T_2)^4 }$\n\n        - $\\hspace{1.6em} =\\dfrac{\\sigma 4\\pi (R_1)^2 (T_1)^4}{\\sigma 4\\pi (R_2)^2 (T_2)^4 }$\n\n        - $\\hspace{1.6em} =\\dfrac{(R_1)^2 (T_1)^4}{(R_2)^2 (T_2)^4} $\n\n        - $R_2=\\dfrac{R_1 (T_1)^2}{(T_2)^2}  \\sqrt{\\dfrac{L_2}{L_1 }}$\n\n        - $R=\\dfrac{R_\\odot (T_\\odot)^2}{T^2}  \\sqrt{\\dfrac{L}{L_\\odot}}$\n\n        - $R=\\dfrac{R_\\odot 5800^2}{3 800^2 } \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2.7\\times10^5 L_\\odot}{L_\\odot }}$\n\n        - $R=1200R_\\odot$\n\n    3. $\\hspace{1em}$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Stellar%20Properties%20%26%20Processes/images/PH0699a.png\" alt=\"PH0699a\">\n\n        Any point within the region shown above  $\\checkmark$\n\n        Red supergiant $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        From the information given in the previous parts of the question:\n\n        - $T=3800$ K and $L=2.7\\times10^5 L_\\odot$\n\n        The region it is cooler and brighter than main sequence stars, which defines a red supergiant.\n\n2.  1. Nuclear fusion generates an outward force/radiation pressure. $\\checkmark$\n\n       The gravitational inward pull is more than the outward force. $\\checkmark$\n\n       $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Nuclear fusion generates an outward force/radiation pressure, which is counterbalanced by the inward force of gravity. When a star exhausts a lot of its hydrogen fuel, the outward force can no longer counteract gravity's inward pull. As a result, the star's core begins to contract and heat up, causing the outer layers of the star to expand and cool down.\n\n    2. Higher mass stars have greater luminosity.  $\\checkmark$\n\n        They burn hydrogen at a faster rate.  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The stars that have greater mass have greater luminosity. So, they produce energy at a quicker pace. Therefore, they burn hydrogen at a faster rate.\n\n    3. ::indent\n        - $d(\\ce{pc})=\\dfrac{1}{p}=\\dfrac{1}{0.31}=3.2$ pc $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $d=(3.2)(3.26)=10.5\\,\\ce{ly}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The distance $d$ in parsec:\n\n        - $d(\\ce{pc})=\\dfrac{1}{p}=\\dfrac{1}{0.31}=3.2$ pc\n\n        Since 1 pc = 3.26 ly\n\n        - $d=(3.2)(3.26)=10.5$ ly\n\n    4. Very small parallax angle for the distant stars $\\checkmark$\n\n        It is very hard to measure small angles accurately $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        As the distance of star increases, the parallax angle gets smaller and is very difficult to measure accurately."},{"ref":"PH0708-3","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0708-3\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n1. A star X is burning carbon in its core. The reaction is given by:\n\n    - $\\ce{^{12}_{6}C}+\\ce{^{12}_{6}C}\\rightarrow{_{12}^{24}}Mg  $\n\n    The following data are available for the reaction.\n\n    - Rest mass of the $\\ce{^{12}_{6}C}                     =12.0111507 \\,u$\n\n    - Rest mass of the ${_{12}^{24}}Mg                    =23.9909630\\, u$\n\n    Determine, in MeV, the energy released in the reaction. Be sure to express your answer to the correct number of significant figures                            [3 marks]\n\n2.  The star $Y$ is a red supergiant.  Describe the next stage in the evolutionary sequence of the star $Y$. [2 marks]\n\n3. Outline why the Sun is not expected to undergo carbon fusion.                                     [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  ::indent\n    - $\\Delta m=(2)(12.0111507 u)-23.9909630 u=0.0313384 u$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $(0.0313384)(931.5)= $ :answer[$29.19\\, MeV$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    - 4 s.f. on answer    $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The mass defect $\\Delta m$ in the reaction:\n\n    - $\\Delta m=(2)(12.0111507 u)-23.9909630 u=0.0313384 u$\n\n    The energy $\\Delta E$ is released from the reaction:\n\n    - $\\Delta E=\\Delta mc^2=(0.0313384)(931.5)=29.19\\, MeV$\n\n    Because there are six significant figures on the mass defect and four significant figures on the mass to energy conversion, the final answer will have the lesser of the two, which results in four significant figures on the answer.\n\n2.  It undergoes Supernova. $\\checkmark$\n\n    Becoming a neutron star or black hole. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    A red supergiant star undergoes a supernova explosion, leaving behind its core. Depending on the mass of the core, it can either form a neutron star or a black hole.\n\n3.  To initiate carbon fusion, there is not enough pressure and temperature in the core $\\checkmark$\n\n    Due to having not enough mass $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    To have carbon fusion, there must be more pressure and temperature inside the core. To make it happen, the Sun should be more massive."},{"ref":"PH0709","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0709\n\nIn the core of a star, a proton-proton chain reaction takes place.\n\n$4\\ce{^{1}_{1}H}\\rightarrow\\ce{^{4}_{2}He}+2\\ce{^{0}_{+1}\\beta}^{+} +2\\nu_e+2\\gamma$\n\nAfter the initial stage of the reaction, two positrons annihilate with electrons present in the surroundings. Which of the following correctly shows the nature of the chain reaction and the total energy released due to annihilation?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Nature of the chain reaction$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Energy released$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| Fusion |$\\left(2\\right)\\left(0.511\\right)$ MeV |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Fusion| $\\left(4\\right)\\left(0.511\\right)$ MeV |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Fission | $\\left(2\\right)\\left(0.511\\right)$ MeV |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Fission | $\\left(4\\right)\\left(0.511\\right)$ MeV |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nIn the given reaction, four protons combine to form the nucleus of a helium atom. This process is known as fusion, where smaller nuclei merge to create a larger one. During the annihilation process, two positrons and two electrons interact and transform into gamma rays. Therefore, the energy they stored due to their masses is released. The energy released can be calculated by using the following formula:\n\n- $E=mc^2$\n\nSince the mass of the positron and electron are the same:\n\n- $E=4m_ec^2=\\left(4\\right)\\left(\\dfrac{0.511\\,\\ce{MeV}}{c^2}\\right)c^2=\\left(4\\right)\\left(0.511\\right)\\,\\ce{MeV}$"},{"ref":"PH0710","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0710\n\nWhich of the following is correct for a stable star?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$  Inward force due to radiation pressure equals the outward force due to gravitational attraction\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em} $ Inward force due to radiation pressure is greater than the outward force due to gravitational attraction\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Inward force due to gravitational attraction equals the outward force due to radiation pressure\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Inward force due to gravitational attraction is greater than the outward force due to radiation pressure","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nTo be a stable star, outwards and inwards forces must be balanced. Outwards force is the result of the pressure of radiation and temperature while the inwards force is formed due to gravitational attraction."},{"ref":"PH0711","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0711\n\nFor fusion reactions to occur in a cloud of stellar dust, a combination of high temperature and high density among hydrogen nuclei is essential. Which of the following best describes why high temperature and high density are needed?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | High temperature | High density |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| To get hydrogen nuclei sufficiently close |To get a high rate of collision |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | To get a high rate of collision | To get hydrogen nuclei sufficiently close |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$To get hydrogen nuclei sufficiently far away$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | To get a low rate of collision |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | To get a low rate of collision | $\\hspace{1.5em}$To get hydrogen nuclei sufficiently far away$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nTo initiate hydrogen fusion, the protons in two hydrogen nuclei must be brought within the range of the strong nuclear force. This can be achieved by raising the temperature of the nuclei so that their kinetic energy is high enough. However, even if the nuclei are close enough, the majority of collisions will not result in fusion.\n\nTherefore, the number of collisions must be high enough to ensure that a significant number of fusion reactions occur. This can be achieved by increasing the density and pressure of the nuclei."},{"ref":"PH0712","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0712\n\nA main sequence star has the following possible fates in its evolution.\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | White dwarf   |\n  | II. | Neutron star |\n  | III. | Black hole |\n\nWhich is the possible fate of a star with a mass greater than 12 times the mass of the Sun?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I and II only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ II and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nStars with a main sequence mass up to 8 to 10 times the mass of the Sun will form a white dwarf. If their mass is between roughly 10 and 40 solar masses, they will form a neutron star. If the mass is greater than roughly 40 solar masses, they will form a black hole. We are told that the mass of the star is greater than 12 solar masses, so we do not know the mass of the star, only that it will not form a white dwarf $-$ depending on its mass, it could form either a neutron star or a black hole.\n\nNote that the Chandrasekhar limit and Oppenheimer-Volkoff limit refer to the mass of the stellar remnant, the mass of the star remaining after the star has left the main sequence."},{"ref":"PH0713","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0713\n\nA star with a mass and radius similar to the Sun and a temperature twenty times hotter than the Sun, would be found in which region of the Hertzsprung-Russell diagram?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Stellar%20Properties%20%26%20Processes/images/PH0713B.png\" alt=\"PH0713B\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe star's position on the H-R diagram is determined by its temperature and luminosity. The star is hotter than the Sun, it should fall to the left of the Sun’s position on the H-R diagram.\n\nFurthermore, the star has a similar radius and hotter temperature compared to the Sun, resulting in higher luminosity according to the equation\n\n- $L=\\sigma AT^4$\n\n Consequently, it would fall above the Sun's position on the H-R diagram. By combining these two pieces of information, it can be deduced that the most probable position of the star is C."},{"ref":"PH0714","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0714\n\nThe luminosity of the Sun is $L_\\odot$. A main sequence star with twice the radius of the Sun and twice the temperature of the Sun has a luminosity of $L$. What is the ratio of $\\dfrac{L}{L_\\odot}$ ?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  4$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  16$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  32$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  64$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe luminosity of a star:\n\n- $L=\\sigma AT^4=\\sigma 4 \\pi r^2T^4$\n\nLuminosity $L$ of a star is directly proportional to the product of the square of the radius and the fourth power of temperature.\n\nFor the Sun\n\n- $L_\\odot\\propto{r_\\odot}^2{T_\\odot}^4$\n\nFor the star\n\n- $L\\propto\\left(2r_\\odot\\right)^2\\left(2T_\\odot\\right)^4=64{r_\\odot}^2{T_\\odot}^4$\n\n- $\\dfrac{L}{L_\\odot}=\\dfrac{64{r_\\odot}^2{T_\\odot}^4}{{r_\\odot}^2{T_\\odot}^4}=64$"},{"ref":"PH0715","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0715\n\nA star with a mass of twice the mass of the Sun is a main sequence star. The time the Sun is expected to spend in the main sequence is $T$. Which of the following is correct for the time the star is expected to spend in the main sequence and the next stage in its evolution?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Time | Stage |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\hspace{1.5em}$More than T$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |$\\hspace{1.5em}$Red giant $\\hspace{1.5em}$|\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | More than T | Supergiant |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Less than T | Red giant |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Less than T | Supergiant |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe stars that have greater mass have higher luminosity which results in a shorter time to run out of hydrogen. Therefore, the lifetime of a star on the main sequence is roughly inversely proportional to the mass of the star. Since the star has greater mass than the Sun, time is less than $T$.\n\nMoreover, the mass of the star is less than eight times the solar mass. Hence, it is a moderate-mass star and it will become a red giant in its next evolutionary stage."},{"ref":"PH0716","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0716\n\nWhich of the following is correct about a red giant star?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | The orbits of the inner planets of the solar system could fit into its volume   |\n  | II. | Cooler than the Sun |\n  | III. | Have higher luminosity than the Sun |\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I and II only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nBy considering the position of the red giants and the Sun on an H-R diagram, it is easy to say that red giants have higher luminosity and lower temperature than the Sun. They can also be large enough to have a volume that could include the Sun and inner planets inside."},{"ref":"PH1135","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1135\n\nIn the second stage of the proton-proton cycle a nucleus of hydrogen fuses with deuterium to form $\\ce{^{3}_{2}He}$. The equation below describes this process:\n\n$\\ce{^{1}_{1}H} + \\ce{^{2}_{1}H \\rightarrow \\ce{^{3}_{2}He^} + \\gamma}$\n\nIt is given that the binding energy per nucleon for $\\ce{^{2}_{1}H}$ is $1.112$ MeV and for $\\ce{^{3}_{2}He}$ is $2.573\\,$MeV.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the energy released in the reaction?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $1.461\\,$MeV\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $2.573\\,$MeV\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $5.495\\,$MeV\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $7.719\\,$MeV","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nTo split the reactants into its constituent parts, the energy required is given by:\n\n- $2(1.112\\,\\ce{MeV})=2.224\\,\\ce{MeV}$\n\nThen, when $\\ce{^{3}_{2}He}$ is formed the energy released can be calculated as follows:\n\n- $3(2.573\\,\\ce{MeV})=7.719\\,\\ce{MeV}$\n\nThe energy released is then:\n\n- $7.719\\,\\ce{MeV}-2.224\\,\\ce{MeV}=5.495\\,\\ce{MeV}$"},{"ref":"PH1136","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1136\n\nThe third stage of the proton-proton cycle is described by the equation below:\n\n$\\ce{^{3}_{2}He}+\\ce{^{3}_{2}He} \\rightarrow \\ce{^{4}_{2}He}+2\\ce{^{1}_{1}H}$\n\nThe binding energies per nucleon are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\hspace{1em}$Nuclide$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$Binding energy per nucleon$\\hspace{1em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| $\\ce{^{3}_{2}He}$ | 2.573 MeV |\n| $\\ce{^{4}_{2}He}$ | 7.074 MeV |\n\nWhich of the following best describes the energy released in this stage?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 4.50 MeV\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 5.50 MeV\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 7.95 MeV\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 12.9 MeV","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe energy released can be calculated as follows:\n\nEnergy to split two $\\ce{^{3}_{2}He}$ into its constituent nucleons: $(2)(3)(2.573\\,\\ce{MeV})=15.438\\,\\ce{MeV}$\n\nEnergy released when $\\ce{^{4}_{2}He}$ is formed: $(4)(7.074\\,\\ce{MeV})=(28.296\\,\\ce{MeV})$\n\n- $E=28.296\\,\\ce{MeV}-15.438\\,\\ce{MeV}=12.858$ MeV"},{"ref":"PH1137","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1137\n\nStar X has a surface temperature that is twice that of the Sun ($T_X=2T_\\odot$) and its luminosity is 49 times greater than the luminosity of the Sun ($L_X=49L_\\odot$).\n\nWhich of the following best describes the ratio $R_X:R_\\odot$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 1.8\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 3.5\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 8.4\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 9.1","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAccording to Stefan-Boltzmann law the luminosity is given by:\n\n- $L=4\\pi\\sigma R^2T^4$\n\nUsing ratios we get:\n\n- $\\dfrac{L_X}{L_\\odot}=\\Bigl(\\dfrac{R_X}{R_\\odot}\\Bigr)^2\\Bigl(\\dfrac{T_X}{T_\\odot}\\Bigr)^4$\n\nWhich can be rearranged into:\n\n- $\\Bigl(\\dfrac{R_X}{R_\\odot}\\Bigr)^2=\\Bigl(\\dfrac{L_X}{L_\\odot}\\Bigr)\\Bigl(\\dfrac{T_\\odot}{T_X}\\Bigr)^4$\n\nLeading to:\n\n- $\\dfrac{R_X}{R_\\odot}=\\sqrt{\\Bigl(\\dfrac{L_X}{L_\\odot}\\Bigr)\\Bigl(\\dfrac{T_\\odot}{T_X}\\Bigr)^4} = \\Bigl(\\sqrt{\\dfrac{L_X}{L_\\odot}}\\Bigr)\\Bigl(\\dfrac{T_\\odot}{T_X}\\Bigr)^2 = (\\sqrt{49})\\Bigl(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\Bigr)^2 = 1.75 \\approx 1.8$"},{"ref":"PH1138","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1138\n\nThe equation below describes the fusion of Deuterium $\\ce{^{2}_{1}H}$ with Tritium $\\ce{^{3}_{1}H}$.\n\n$\\ce{^{2}_{1}H}+\\ce{^{3}_{1}H}\\rightarrow \\ce{^{4}_{2}He} + \\ce{^{1}_{0}n}$\n\nDuring this reaction 17.59 MeV of energy are released.\n\nConsider the following statements for this reaction:\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | The total binding energy increases.   |\n  | II. | The total mass increases. |\n  | III. | Binding energy per nucleon remains constant. |\n\nWhich of the statements are true?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Only I\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Only II\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I and II\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nWhen two small nuclei fuse together energy is released, this process results in a loss of mass in the system.\\\nThe product of this reaction will then reach a lower energy state which correspond to a higher magnitude of the total binding energy.\\\nThe binding energy per nucleon will also increase."},{"ref":"PH1139","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1139\n\nAt the core of stars P and Q different elements are fused together.\n- Star P fuses Hydrogen creating Helium at its core.\n- Star Q fuses Helium nuclei to produce Beryllium.\n\nWhich row correctly describes the relative temperature and pressure at the core of these stars?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$Temperature is higher$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$Pressure is higher$\\hspace{1em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| P | P |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | P| Q |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Q | P |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Q | Q |","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAt the core of star Q larger nuclei are fused. The electrostatic repulsion between these nuclei is higher due to the larger number of positively charged protons.\n\nFor these nuclei to fuse and to overcome the Coulomb repulsion, higher kinetic energies  will be required which represents higher temperatures. The pressure at its core will also be higher given that stronger forces are needed.\n\nHydrogen fusing stars do not need such high temperatures and pressures, given that the Coulomb repulsion between hydrogen nuclei is, in comparison, relatively small."},{"ref":"PH1140","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1140\n\nStar M has a radius that is about 640 times that of the Sun and its surface temperature is about 3100 K.\n\nGiven that the surface temperature of the Sun is about 5800 K, which of the following best describes the luminosity of Star M?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $1700L_\\odot$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $33000L_\\odot$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $42000L_\\odot$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $740000L_\\odot$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAccording to Stefan-Boltzmann law the luminosity is given by:\n\n- $L=4\\pi\\sigma R^2T^4$\n\nUsing ratios we get:\n\n- $\\dfrac{L_M}{L_\\odot}=\\Bigl(\\dfrac{R_M}{R_\\odot}\\Bigr)^2\\Bigl(\\dfrac{T_M}{T_\\odot}\\Bigr)^4$\n\n- $\\dfrac{L_M}{L_\\odot}=(640)^2\\Bigl(\\dfrac{3100\\,\\ce{K}}{5800\\,\\ce{K}}\\Bigr)^4\\approx33426$\n\nLeading to:\n\n- $L_M\\approx33000L_\\odot$"},{"ref":"PH1141","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1141\n\nThe Hertzsprung-Russell diagram shown below shows the position of four different stars\nA, B, C and D.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Stellar%20Properties%20%26%20Processes/images/Screenshot%202024-08-20%20at%2010.24.08%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2024-08-20 at 10.24.08PM\">\n\nWhich star is likely a red giant?","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nRed giants are usually relatively cool stars. Due to their large size, their luminosity is great."},{"ref":"PH1142","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1142\n\nThe Hertzsprung-Russell diagram shown below represents the surface temperature and luminosity for different stars.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Stellar%20Properties%20%26%20Processes/images/PH1142.png\" alt=\"PH1142\">\n\nOne of the stars is labelled X. Which of the following best describes star X?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Red giant\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Red supergiant\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ White dwarf\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Main sequence star","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nStar X is located within the group of stars with higher temperatures and low luminosities on the HR diagram.\n\nThese stars must then be relatively small so that their luminosity is low and are known as white dwarfs."},{"ref":"PH1144","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1144\n\nThe parallax angle for the star Proxima Centauri is 0.772 arcsec.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the distance to this star?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 1.30 ly\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 2.32 ly\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 3.81 ly\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 4.22 ly","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n- $d=\\dfrac{1}{p(\\textrm{arc seconds})}$\n\n- $d=\\dfrac{1}{0.772}=1.295$\n\nThe calculated value corresponds to the distance in parsecs (pc). We now need to convert this value:\n\n- $d=1.295\\,\\ce{pc}\\times\\dfrac{3.26\\,\\ce{ly}}{1\\,\\ce{pc}}=4.22\\,\\ce{ly}$"},{"ref":"PH1820","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1820\n\nThe diagram below is a Hertzsprung-Russell diagram:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Stellar%20Properties%20%26%20Processes/images/PH1820q.JPEG\" alt=\"PH1820q\">\n\nWhat are the quantities represented by X and Y?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | X | Y |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\hspace{1.5em}$Luminosity $\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\hspace{1.5em}$Temperature$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Temperature | Luminosity |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Mass | Temperature |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Temperature | Mass |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe Hertzsprung-Russell (H-R) diagram is a fundamental tool in astrophysics used to classify stars based on their luminosity and temperature.\n\nThe horizontal axis of the diagram represents the temperature of the star, but it is plotted in a decreasing order, meaning hotter stars (higher temperature) are on the left, and cooler stars (lower temperature) are on the right.\n\nThe vertical axis represents the luminosity, which is the total energy output of a star per second. Stars with higher luminosities are positioned higher on the diagram, while stars with lower luminosities are lower."},{"ref":"PH1827","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1827\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe star Betelgeuse is a red supergiant in the Orion constellation. Betelgeuse has a surface temperature of approximately 3500 K and a diameter of $1.2\\times10^9$ km.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Stellar%20Properties%20%26%20Processes/images/PH1827q.JPEG\" alt=\"PH1827q\">\n\n1. Calculate the luminosity of Betelgeuse. [2 marks]\n\n2. Betelgeuse will continue to fuse heavier elements together until iron is produced. Explain why iron production signifies the end of energy production in the star. [2 marks]\n\n3. Explain the next stages in the evolution of Betelgeuse after the production of iron in the star's core. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. ::indent\n\n    - $(1.2\\times10^9$ km $ =1.2\\times10^{12}$ m)\n    - $A=4\\pi r^2=4\\pi\\left(\\dfrac{1.2\\times10^{12}}{2}\\right)^2=4.5\\times10^{24}$ m$^2$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $L=\\sigma AT^4=(5.67\\times10^{-8})(4.5\\times10^{24})(3500)^4= $ :answer[$3.8\\times10^{31}$ W] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    To find the luminosity of the star, we use the Stefan-Boltzmann law:\n\n    - $L=\\sigma AT^4$\n\n    The diameter of Betelgeuse is:\n\n    - $d=1.2\\times10^9$ km $ =1.2\\times10^{12}$ m\n\n    Now, we can calculate the surface area, $A$, by dividing the diameter by two in order to find the radius:\n\n    - $A=4\\pi r^2=4\\pi\\left(\\dfrac{1.2\\times10^{12}}{2}\\right)^2=4.5\\times10^{24}$ m$^2$\n\n    Substituting the values into the equation for $L$:\n\n    - $L=\\sigma AT^4=(5.67\\times10^{-8})(4.5\\times10^{24})(3500)^4=3.8\\times10^{31}$ W\n\n2. Fusion of elements lighter than iron releases energy due to increase in binding energy per nucleon $\\checkmark$\n\n    Fusion of elements heavier than iron consumes energy due to decrease in binding energy per nucleon $\\checkmark$\n\n    OWTTE\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Fusing elements lighter than iron together into heavier ones releases energy due to the resulting nucleus having a higher binding energy per nucleon. Elements heavier than iron have a lower binding energy per nucleon and so energy is required (no energy would be released) to fuse these heavier elements together. This is an unsustainable energy source for the star which is why iron production signifies the end of energy production.\n\n3. Gravity causes core to collapse due to lack of outward pressure from fusion and so core temperature will rise $\\checkmark$\n\n    Outer layers collapse into the core and increase the temperature, causing a type-II supernova $\\checkmark$\n\n    Resulting in a neutron star or a black hole $\\checkmark$\n\n    OWTTE\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Once iron is produced in the core and there is no nuclear fuel left, gravity will cause the core to collapse in on itself due to the lack of outward pressure from fusion. The temperature of the core will increase and allow for iron to fuse and consume more energy. The collapse causes protons to combine with electrons to produce neutrons. This process will continue until the core contains only neutrons. The collapse occurs so quickly that a gap is left between the core and outer layers. The outer layers fall into the gap and cause a rapid increase in temperature, resulting in a large explosion that blows everything away except the core — this is known as a type-II supernova. The result is either a neutron star or a black hole."},{"ref":"PH1828","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1828\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n1. A distant star of radius $1.4\\times10^{11}$ m is measured to have a peak wavelength in its spectrum of 725 nm. Calculate the surface temperature of the star. [2 marks]\n\n2. The luminosity of the Sun is $L_\\odot=3.8\\times10^{26}$ W. Calculate the luminosity of the star in terms of the Sun's luminosity. [3 marks]\n\n3. On the Hertzsprung-Russell diagram below, plot the location of the star and state the classification of this star. [2 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Stellar%20Properties%20%26%20Processes/images/Screenshot%202025-03-12%20at%209.55.20%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-03-12 at 9.55.20 AM\">","markScheme":"1. Use of $\\lambda_\\textrm{max}=\\dfrac{2.9\\times10^{-3}}{T}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $T=$ :answer[4000 K] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    To find the surface temperature, we use Wien's law:\n\n    - $\\lambda_\\textrm{max}=\\dfrac{2.9\\times10^{-3}}{T}$\n\n    Rearranging for $T$ and substituting in the values:\n\n    - $T=\\dfrac{2.9\\times10^{-3}}{725\\times10^{-9}}=4000$ K\n\n2. ::indent\n    - $A=4\\pi r^2=4\\pi(1.4\\times10^{11})^2=2.5\\times10^{23}$ m$^2$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $L=\\sigma AT^4=(5.67\\times10^{-8})(2.5\\times10^{23})(4000)^4=3.6\\times10^{30}$ W $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $\\dfrac{3.6\\times10^{30}}{3.8\\times10^{26}}=$ :answer[$9.4\\times10^3\\,L_\\odot$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    To find the luminosity of the star, we must first find its surface area:\n\n    - $A=4\\pi r^2=4\\pi(1.4\\times10^{11})^2=2.5\\times10^{23}$ m$^2$\n\n    We can now calculate the luminosity using:\n\n    - $L=\\sigma AT^4=(5.67\\times10^{-8})(2.5\\times10^{23})(4000)^4=3.6\\times10^{30}$ W\n\n    The star's luminosity as a fraction of the Sun's luminosity is given by:\n\n    - $\\dfrac{3.6\\times10^{30}}{3.8\\times10^{26}}=9.4\\times10^3\\,L_\\odot$\n\n    Use of rounded values gives $9.5\\times10^3\\,L_\\odot$.\n\n3. Star plotted correctly at $T=4000$ K and $L\\approx10^4\\,L_\\odot$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Stellar%20Properties%20%26%20Processes/images/Screenshot%202025-03-12%20at%2010.00.36%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-03-12 at 10.00.36 AM\">\n\n    Red giant $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The star has a temperature of 4000 K and a luminosity of $9.4\\times10^3\\,L_\\odot\\approx10^4\\,L_\\odot$.\n\n    Therefore, the location of the star on the diagram is in the upper right quadrant:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Stellar%20Properties%20%26%20Processes/images/PH1828ac2.JPEG\" alt=\"PH1828ac2\">\n\n    A star located in this area of the Hertzsprung-Russell is classified as a **red giant**."},{"ref":"PH1829","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1829\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nThe star Proxima Centauri is the closest star to the Sun. It is located 4.24 light years away from Earth.\n\n1.    Calculate the parallax angle of Proxima Centauri when viewed from Earth. [3 marks]\n\n2. Proxima Centauri is a red dwarf star that is currently in the main sequence stage of its life cycle. Describe the process by which a star like Proxima Centauri forms, from its birth to reaching the main sequence. [4 marks]\n\n3. Proxima Centauri is currently a red dwarf star and will eventually become a white dwarf. Label on the Hertzsprung-Russell diagram below the approximate location of a red dwarf (A) and a white dwarf (B). [2 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Stellar%20Properties%20%26%20Processes/images/Screenshot%202025-03-12%20at%2010.05.57%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-03-12 at 10.05.57 AM\">","markScheme":"1. ::indent\n    - $d=\\dfrac{4.24}{3.26}=1.3$ pc $\\checkmark$\n\n    Use of $d(\\textrm{parsec})=\\dfrac{1}{p(\\textrm{arcsecond})}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $p=$ :answer[0.77 arcseconds] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Since 1 pc $=3.26$ ly, then the distance to Proxima Centauri in parsecs is:\n\n    - $d=\\dfrac{4.24}{3.26}=1.3$ pc\n\n    To find the parallax angle, we use:\n\n    - $d(\\textrm{parsecs})=\\dfrac{1}{p(\\textrm{arcsecond})}$\n\n    Rearranging and substituting in the values:\n\n    - $p=\\dfrac{1}{d}=\\dfrac{1}{1.3}=0.77$ arcseconds\n\n2. Star begins as a giant molecular cloud of gas/nebula $\\checkmark$\n\n    Gravity causes the nebula to collapse and form a hot dense core known as a protostar $\\checkmark$\n\n    Temperature and pressure increase until hydrogen begins to fuse $\\checkmark$\n\n    Fusion creates outward radiation pressure that balances gravity and prevents further collapse – the star is now in its main sequence stage $\\checkmark$\n\n    OWTTE\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Proxima Centauri would have started as a cold, dense nebula — a giant molecular cloud consisting of gas and dust left over from the formation of galaxies. Nebulae are held together by gravity but are prevented from collapsing due to the outward pressure of the molecules moving in random directions. Gravity can cause a region of the nebula to collapse into a dense core. The temperature and pressure increase, creating a protostar. As the protostar gets hotter and denser, hot hydrogen starts to fuse to create helium, releasing energy. The nuclear fusion reactions create an outward radiation pressure that balances the inward force of gravity, preventing further collapse — the star enters its main sequence stage of life. The star will remain in the main sequence stage until most of the hydrogen is used up.\n\n3. A correctly plotted within the $\\text{\\textquoteleft}$red dwarfs' area $\\checkmark$\n\n    B correctly plotted within the $\\text{\\textquoteleft}$white dwarfs' area $\\checkmark$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Stellar%20Properties%20%26%20Processes/images/Screenshot%202025-03-12%20at%2010.06.45%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-03-12 at 10.06.45 AM\">\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Red dwarf stars are cooler than the Sun and have a lower luminosity and are, therefore, located in the lower right quadrant of the main sequence part of the diagram.\n\n    White dwarf stars have a lower luminosity than the Sun but are much hotter and are, therefore, located in the lower left quadrant of the diagram.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Stellar%20Properties%20%26%20Processes/images/Screenshoot%202025-03-12%20at%2010.06.45%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshoot 2025-03-12 at 10.06.45 AM\">"},{"ref":"PH1841","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1841\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nIn stars, deuterium ($\\ce{^{2}_{1}H}$) and tritium ($\\ce{^{3}_{1}H}$) fuse to form helium and a neutron as shown.\n\n$\\ce{^{2}_{1}H}+\\ce{^{3}_{1}H}\\rightarrow\\ce{^{4}_{2}He}+\\ce{^{1}_{0}n}$\n\n1. Using the table below, calculate the energy released in this fusion reaction. [2 marks]\n\n    $\\\\$\n\n    | | $\\hspace{1em}$Nuclide$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$Mass/u$\\hspace{1em}$ |\n    | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n    | | $\\ce{^{1}_{0}n}$ | 1.008664 |\n    | | $\\ce{^{2}_{1}H}$ | 2.014101 |\n    | | $\\ce{^{3}_{1}H}$ | 3.016049 |\n    | | $\\ce{^{4}_{2}He}$ | 4.002603 |\n\n    $\\\\$\n\n2. The fusion reaction of deuterium and tritium will be used in future fusion reactors such as ITER. Explain the conditions that are necessary in order to sustain fusion reactions. [3 marks]\n\n3. Fusion reactors, such as tokamaks, make use of magnetic fields to confine and control a plasma to allow fusion to occur. Describe two major challenges scientists face when attempting to achieve practical fusion power. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. ::indent\n\n    - $\\Delta m=0.018883$ u $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $E=(0.018883)(931.5)= 17.6\\ \\ce{MeV}\\ \\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The energy released from the reaction is found by subtracting the mass of the products from the mass of the reactants:\n\n    - $\\Delta m=\\textrm{mass(reactants)}-\\textrm{mass(products)}$\n\n    - $\\Delta m=(2.014101+3.016049)-(1.008664+4.002603)=0.018883$ u\n\n    The mass difference is positive i.e. the mass of the reactants is greater than the mass of the products. This mass difference represents the mass than has been converted into energy.\n\n    Since 1 u$=931.5$ MeV, then the energy released is:\n\n    - $E=(0.018883)(931.5)=17.6$ MeV\n\n2. Nuclei repel each other due to being positively charged OWTTE. $\\checkmark$\n\n    High temperatures give nuclei greater kinetic energies to overcome repulsion OWTTE. $\\checkmark$\n\n    High density will increase frequency of collisions, leading to more fusions OWTTE. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Due to the deuterium and tritium nuclei both being positively charged, there will be a repulsive electrostatic force between them. To overcome this force and allow the nuclei to fuse, a fusion reactor needs to bring these nuclei to very high speeds under very high pressures for the nuclei to become close enough to experience the strong nuclear force and fuse. High temperatures are used in fusion reactors to supply the nuclei with large amounts of kinetic energy, allowing them to travel at very high speeds and overcome the repulsive force. Since the nuclei need to be in very close proximity to one another to fuse, fusion reactors also use very high pressures to create high density, bringing the nuclei closer together and increase the frequency of collisions between the nuclei. This results in a greater number of fusion reactions occurring.\n\n3. Any two of the following\n\n    Confining the plasma is difficult due to the extreme temperatures (millions of degrees) required for fusion and the need for strong magnetic fields OWTTE. $\\checkmark$\n\n    Current reactors require more energy input than they output, making the reactors inefficient OWTTE. $\\checkmark$\n\n    Tritium as a fuel source is rare and difficult to produce OWTTE. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Confining and controlling the plasma that exists within tokamak fusion reactors is very difficult for several reasons.\n\n    To sustain the plasma and allow fusion to occur, the plasma must be heated to very high temperatures (approximately 150 million degrees Celsius).\n\n    The plasma must also be confined and shaped to prevent the plasma making contact with the vessel walls, which would damage the fusion reactor and cool the plasma down. Today's current fusion reactors require more energy input than they output, and thus, they are inefficient. Fusion reactors of the future, such as ITER, will produce more energy that is required to be put in, making them efficient and suitable for power production.\n\n    One of the main fuels used in fusion reactors is radioactive tritium. Tritium is rare (approximately only 10 kg exists on Earth at any given time), and so it must be created to supply future fusion reactors. One of the methods for this involves absorbing the neutrons produced from fusion via a lithium blanket surrounding the reactor. When lithium is combined with a neutron, tritium is produced which can be used as fuel."},{"ref":"PH1851","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1851\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\n1. 1. Alpha Centauri A is a main sequence star with radius $8.5\\times10^8$ m and surface temperature of 5790 K. Calculate the luminosity of Alpha Centauri A. [2 marks]\n\n    2. A planet at a distance of 0.9 AU from Alpha Centauri A has an albedo of 0.3. Calculate the average intensity of radiation from Alpha Centauri A absorbed by the surface of the planet. [3 marks]\n\n2. Alpha Centauri A maintains a constant radius because it is in equilibrium. Explain how this equilibrium occurs. [2 marks]\n\n3. Star X has the same luminosity as Alpha Centauri A and it is located to the right of Alpha Centauri A on a Hertzsprung-Russell diagram. Deduce how the radius of Star X compares to that of Alpha Centauri A. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. $\\hspace{1.9em}$ $L=(5.67\\times10^{-8})(4)(\\pi)(8.5\\times10^8)^2(5790)^4$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $L=$ :answer[$5.8\\times10^{26}$ $\\ll\\textrm{W}\\gg$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The luminosity is given by\n\n        - $L=\\sigma AT^4$\n\n        The surface area of a sphere can be calculated from the formula\n\n        - $A=4\\pi r^2$\n\n        Substituting this value and all formulae gives\n\n        - $L=(5.67\\times10^{-8})(4)(\\pi)(8.5\\times10^8)^2(5790)^4=5.8\\times10^{26}$ W\n\n    2. $\\hspace{1.9em}$ $b=\\dfrac{L}{4\\pi r^2}=\\dfrac{5.8\\times10^{26}}{4\\pi(0.9(1.5\\times10^{11}))^2}=2526$ W m$^{-2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $b_\\textrm{avg}=\\dfrac{2526}{4}=632$ W m$^{-2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $I_\\textrm{abs}=(1-0.3)(632)=$ :answer[$442$ W m$^{-2}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        Allow ECF from (a)(i)\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        First we can find the intensity of the light from Alpha Centauri A at a distance of 0.9 AU, where the value for 1 AU can be found in the data booklet.\n\n        - $b=\\dfrac{L}{4\\pi r^2}=\\dfrac{5.8\\times10^{26}}{4\\pi(0.9(1.5\\times10^{11}))^2}=2526$ W m$^{-2}$\n\n        The average intensity over the entire surface of the planet is given by\n\n        - $b_\\textrm{avg}=\\dfrac{b}{4}=\\dfrac{2526}{4}=632$ W m$^{-2}$\n\n        The albedo gives the fraction of the intensity that is reflected, so the fraction absorbed can be found from\n\n        - Fraction absorbed $=(1-\\textrm{albedo})=1-0.3=0.7$\n\n        Therefore the absorbed intensity is\n\n        - $I_\\textrm{abs}=(1-0.3)(632)=442$ W m$^{-2}$\n\n2. Outward radiation/thermal pressure/force due to fusion reactions $\\checkmark$\n\n    is balanced by inward gravitational pressure/force. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    There is an inward gravitational force due to the mass of the star and an outward force due to the pressure of radiation/thermal energy released from the fusion reactions taking place in the core. In a main sequence star, these forces/pressures balance each other out an an equilibrium is maintained.\n\n3. $T_X<T_A$ / Temperature of Star X is lower\n\n    AND\n\n    $\\dfrac{R_X}{R_A}=\\dfrac{(T_A)^2}{(T_X)^2}$ / radius is inversely proportional to the square of temperature for the same luminosity $\\checkmark$\n\n    Bigger radius $\\checkmark$\n\n    **OR**\n\n    Lines of constant radius slope upwards to the left   $\\checkmark$\n\n    X lies on a line of larger radius $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Since Star X is to the right of Alpha Centauri A, then it has a lower surface temperature.\n\n    Since the luminosity is given by\n\n    - $L=\\sigma AT^4$\n\n    And both stars have the same luminosity, then\n\n    - $\\begin{aligned}\n    \\ L_X&=L_A\\\\\n    \\ \\\\\n    \\ \\sigma A_X(T_X)^4&=\\sigma A_A(T_A)^4\\\\\n    \\ \\\\\n    \\ 4\\pi (R_X)^2(T_X)^4&=4\\pi (R_A)^2(T_A)^4\\\\\n    \\ \\\\\n    \\ (R_X)^2(T_X)^4&=(R_A)^2(T_A)^4\\\\\n    \\ \\\\\n    \\ \\dfrac{(R_X)^2}{(R_A)^2}&=\\dfrac{(T_A)^4}{(T_X)^4}\\\\\n    \\ \\\\\n    \\ \\dfrac{R_X}{R_A}&=\\dfrac{(T_A)^2}{(T_X)^2}\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}$\n\n    Since\n\n    - $T_X<T_A$\n\n    Then\n\n    - $R_X>R_A$\n\n    The radius of Star X is bigger than that of Alpha Centauri A.\n\n    Alternatively we can consider an H-R diagram and the lines of constant radius. The lines of constant radius are angled upwards to the left, with the radius increasing as we move upwards on the graph. Thus X lies on a line of greater radius than Y.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Stellar%20Properties%20%26%20Processes/images/Screenshot%202025-11-06%20at%2010.21.49%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-11-06 at 10.21.49 AM\">"},{"ref":"PH1876","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1876\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nIn the early universe, the following fusion reaction occurred:\n\n$\\ce{^{1}_{1}H}+\\ce{^{1}_{0}n}\\rightarrow\\ce{^{2}_{1}H}+\\ce{^{0}_{0}\\gamma}$\n\nThe following data is provided:\n\n- The mass of $\\ce{^{1}_{1}H}=1.007276$ u\n\n- The mass of $\\ce{^{1}_{0}n}=1.008665$ u\n\n1.    The energy released in this reaction is 1.713 MeV. Determine the mass of $\\ce{^{2}_{1}H}$. [3 marks]\n\n2. State and explain the conditions needed for fusion to occur. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. $\\hspace{1.85em}$ mass defect $=0.001839$ u $\\checkmark$\n\n    - mass of the reactants $=2.015941$ u $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $2.015941\\textrm{ u}-0.001839\\textrm{ u}=$ :answer[$2.014\\textrm{ u}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The mass defect of the reaction $\\Delta m$ in u:\n\n    - $\\Delta m=\\dfrac{1.713}{931.5}=0.001839$ u\n\n    The mass of the reactants $m_r$:\n\n    - $\\begin{aligned}\n    \\ m_r&=1.007276+1.008665\\\\\n    \\\\\\\n    \\ &=2.015941\\textrm{ u}\\\\\n    \\\\\\\n    \\ \\textrm{mass of }\\ce{^{2}_{1}H}&=m_r-\\Delta m\\\\\n    \\\\\\\n    \\ &=2.015941\\textrm{ u}-0.001839\\textrm{ u}\\\\\n    \\\\\\\n    \\ &=2.014102\\textrm{ u}\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}$\n\n2. high temperature $\\checkmark$\n\n    high density of the nuclei $\\checkmark$\n\n    bring nuclei close enough to overcome (electrostatic) repulsion and experience strong nuclear force $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Nuclear fusion, the process powering stars and proposed fusion reactors, requires overcoming the strong electrostatic repulsion between positively charged atomic nuclei. This is achieved by providing the nuclei with extremely high temperatures (millions of Kelvin), which gives them sufficient kinetic energy to move at speeds necessary to overcome the repulsion. Additionally, a high density of nuclei is crucial, as it increases the probability of these high-energy collisions occurring frequently enough for sustained fusion reactions. The combination of these extreme conditions allows the nuclei to get close enough for the strong nuclear force to bind them together, releasing a tremendous amount of energy."},{"ref":"PH1905","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1905\n\nThe HR diagram below shows the evolutionary track of a star starting from the main sequence (point X). The star's initial position on the main sequence corresponds to a luminosity of about $0.5L_\\odot$ and a surface temperature of about 5200 K.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Stellar%20Properties%20%26%20Processes/images/Screenshot%202025-09-07%20at%2011.53.51%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-09-07 at 11.53.51 AM\">\n\nWhich of the following correctly describes the mass of the star and the final stage of its evolution?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Mass$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Final stage$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\lt12M_\\odot$ | White dwarf |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\lt12M_\\odot$ | Neutron star |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\gt12M_\\odot$ | White dwarf |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\gt12M_\\odot$ | Neutron star |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe star's initial position on the HR diagram corresponds to a luminosity $L=0.5L_\\odot$ and a surface temperature $T$ = 5200 K.\n\nBecause the star's luminosity and temperature are less than that of the Sun, it must be less massive than the Sun and therefore **much less than** $12M_\\odot$ which is approximately the limit above which neutron stars can be formed from the remaining core.\n\nFrom its track on the HR diagram, the star evolves off the main sequence into the red giant branch, with luminosity increasing and surface temperature decreasing. Once helium fusion ends, the outer layers are ejected as a planetary nebula, leaving behind the hot core.\n\nBecause the star's initial mass is less than twelve solar masses, the core mass is insufficient to overcome electron degeneracy pressure and undergo further collapse. The remnant is a white dwarf, located in the lower left of the HR diagram.\n\nTherefore, the star's mass is lower than 12 solar masses and its final stage is a white dwarf."},{"ref":"PH1906","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1906\n\nIn 1928, Harold L. Aiden measured Proxima Centauri to have a parallax angle of approximately 0.783 arcseconds.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the distance to Proxima Centauri?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2.55 ly\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 4.16 ly\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 13.8 ly\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 16.1 ly","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nTo calculate the distance from the Earth to Proxima Centauri we can use the equation from the Data Booklet as follows:\n\n- $d(\\textrm{parsec})=\\dfrac{1}{p(\\textrm{arcsecond})}$\n\n- $d=\\dfrac{1}{0.783\\textrm{ arcsecond}}\\approx1.277$ pc\n\nThis distance can now be converted to ly using the unit conversions provided in the Data Booklet:\n\n- $d=1.277\\textrm{ pc}\\times\\dfrac{3.26\\textrm{ ly}}{1\\textrm{ pc}}=4.16$ ly"}]},{"name":"Unassigned","questions":[{"ref":"PH0357","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0357\n\nA source of radiation is near a magnetic field that is directed into the page. A sheet of paper is placed in the path of the radiation before it enters the magnetic field.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Nuclear%20%26%20Quantum%20Physics/Unassigned/images/PH0357.png\" alt=\"PH0357\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Created with Chemix (https://chemix.org)]}}$\n\nThe gamma radiation is not deflected when entering the magnetic field. Which of the following is correct about the other type of radiation that penetrates the paper sheet and the direction of its deflected path in the magnetic field?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\quad$Type of radiation$\\quad$ | $\\quad$Direction of deflected path$\\quad$ |\n| --- | :---: | :---: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\alpha$ | Upwards |\n| B. | $\\alpha$ | Downwards |\n| C. | $\\beta^-$ | Upwards |\n| D. | $\\beta^-$ | Downwards |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe alpha, beta and gamma radiation have different penetrating powers, where alpha is the one with the least penetrating power and won’t pass through the paper sheet, while gamma and beta radiations will pass through the paper sheet.\n\nWhen entering the magnetic field, gamma radiation will continue in its path without deflection because they have no charge. On the other hand, beta particles have negative charges, so they will be affected by a magnetic force that causes deflection in their path.\n\nTo determine the direction of the magnetic force, the right hand rule is used keeping in mind that the velocity direction in the right hand rule is that of a positive charge. So we have the direction of velocity is to the left, the magnetic field is into page, thus the magnetic force will be directed downwards.\n\n*Any other hand-rule convention can also be used."}]}]},{"name":"Scientific Skills & Tools","subtopics":[{"name":"Scientific Skills & Tools","questions":[{"ref":"PH0031","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0031\n\nA rectangular prism is obtained by combining two identical cubes as shown in the figure.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/PH0031.png\" alt=\"PH0031\">\n\nThe length of one side of a cube is $10.0 \\pm 0.2$ cm.\n\nWhat is the ratio\n\n $\\dfrac{\\textrm{absolute uncertainty of volume of the rectangular prism}}{\\textrm{absolute uncertainty of volume of one cube}}$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{1}{2}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 5","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFor the rectangular prism\n\n$V = l \\times w \\times h$ where $l = 20.0 \\pm 0.4$, $w = 10.0 \\pm 0.2$, and $h = 10.0 \\pm 0.2$\n\nThe uncertainty on the volume is found from adding the fractional uncertainties of the three different dimensions.\n\n$\\Delta V=V(\\dfrac{\\Delta l}{l}+\\dfrac{\\Delta w}{w}+\\dfrac{\\Delta h}{h})$\n\n$\\Delta V=2000(\\dfrac{0.4}{20.0}+\\dfrac{0.2}{10.0}+\\dfrac{0.2}{10.0})=\\pm 120\\,\\ce{cm^3}$\n\nFor the cube\n\n$V=l^3 \\hspace{1em}$ where $l = 10.0 \\pm 0.2$\n\n$\\Delta V=(V)(3)\\dfrac{\\Delta l}{l}$\n\n$\\Delta V=(1000)(3)(\\dfrac{0.2}{10.0})=\\pm 60\\,\\ce{cm^3}$\n\nTherefore the ratio is $\\dfrac{120}{60} = 2$"},{"ref":"PH0078","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0078\n\nExpress 0.04758 to 3 significant figures.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$0.04758\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$0.0476\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$0.048\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$0.05","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nLeading zeros do not count in the number of significant figures (s.f.). Therefore, option A is 4 s.f.; option B is 3 s.f.; option C is 2 s.f.; option D is 1 s.f."},{"ref":"PH0079","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0079\n\nThe volume of a cylinder is calculated from the product of its base area and height. The measurement of the height has an uncertainty of 5% and the uncertainty of the diameter is 1%. What is the uncertainty of the volume of the cylinder?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1%\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 5%\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 6%\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 7%","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe volume of a cylinder is calculated by Base Area $\\times$ Height\n\n$V=AH=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}(D)(D)(H)$\n\nThe uncertainty of volume can be calculated as the following, where the relative uncertainties of each term are added when quantities are multiplied together\n\n$\\cfrac{\\Delta V}{V}=\\cfrac{\\Delta D}{D}+\\cfrac{\\Delta D}{D}+\\cfrac{\\Delta H}{H}$\n\nHence the percentage of the volume uncertainty is just the sum of the percentage uncertainty of base area and height.\n\n$1\\%+1\\%+5\\%=7\\%$."},{"ref":"PH0097","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0097\n\nAn electronic balance has a button with “tare” written on it. The reading of the balance is reset to zero when the “tare” button is pressed. Which of the following gives a reason why use of the “tare” button improves the measurement?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Reduce systematic error\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Reduce random error\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Increase precision\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Reduce percentage uncertainty of the measurement","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe “Tare” button resets the balance display to zero. It avoids zero error when measuring weight with a container. It does not change the precision of the balance or the absolute uncertainty of the measurement. Without the use of the Tare button, all values will be wrong by the same amount, which is a systematic error."},{"ref":"PH0143","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0143\n\nAn experiment requires the measurement of the radius $R$ and height $H$ of a cylindrical battery to determine its volume. The uncertainty in $R$ is $\\Delta R$ and the uncertainty in $H$ is $\\Delta H$.  \n\nWhat is the fractional uncertainty in the volume of the battery? \n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2(\\dfrac{\\Delta R}{R}) \\times (\\dfrac{\\Delta H}{H})$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2(\\dfrac{\\Delta R}{R}) + (\\dfrac{\\Delta H}{H})$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $(\\dfrac{\\Delta R}{R}) + (\\dfrac{\\Delta H}{H})$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $(\\dfrac{\\Delta R}{R}) \\times (\\dfrac{\\Delta H}{H})$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe formula for the volume of a cylinder is $\\pi R^2H$\n\nThe fractional uncertainty in $R^2$ is $2(\\dfrac{\\Delta R}{R})$\n\nThe fractional uncertainty in $H$ is $(\\dfrac{\\Delta H}{H})$\n\nIn multiplication, the fractional uncertainties are added together\n\nGiving $2(\\dfrac{\\Delta R}{R}) + (\\dfrac{\\Delta H}{H})$"},{"ref":"PH0147","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0147\n\nAs a truck is brought to rest, the brakes apply a force of $5.1 \\times 10^3$ N. Which answer best describes the magnitude of the change in momentum of the truck if the brakes are applied for 3.0 s?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $15$ kN s\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $15000$ N s\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $15.3$ kN s\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $15300$ N s","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\n**NOTE** this question requires students to use their judgement to determine the best answer, using standard scientific conventions.\n\n- Impulse = change in momentum =\n\n$F \\,t $\n\nSo the magnitude of the change in momentum:\n\n- $\\Delta p= (5.1 \\times 10^3$)(3.0) = 15300 N s\n\nAll responses match this quantity but the number of significant figures needs to be considered. As both of the values used in the calculation have two significant figures, there should be two significant figures in the answer.\n\nIn the answer $15000$ N s, the number of significant figures is unclear because expressed this way it could be accurate to the nearest 1000 N s, 100 N s, 10 N s or 1 N s. It could be 2, 3, 4 or 5 significant figures. The response $15$ kN s clearly has two significant figures, so it is the best answer."},{"ref":"PH0154","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0154\n\nA point $P$ is subjected to three simultaneous forces of magnitudes $F_A > F_B > F_C$. Point $P$ is in equilibrium. Consider the following statements.\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | $F_A = F_B + F_C$ |\n  | II. | $F_A \\leq F_B + F_C$ |\n  | III. | $F_A > F_B - F_C$ |\n\nWhich of the statements is **not always true** about the magnitudes of the forces?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nVectors of forces in equilibrium form a triangle or a straight line.\n\nThe sum of two sides of a triangle is always greater than the third (II is always true).\n\nThe difference between two sides of a triangle is always less than the third (III is also always true).\n\nI is true **only when** the three forces are in a straight line and $F_B$ and $F_C$ are both opposing $F_A$(not always true, as required by the question)."},{"ref":"PH0159","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0159\n\nThe mass and circumference of a standard volleyball are $270 \\pm 10$ g and $66 \\pm1$ cm respectively. What is the percentage uncertainty in the density of the ball calculated from these values?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $(\\dfrac{10}{270} + (3)(\\dfrac{1}{66})) \\times 100\\%$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $(3)(\\dfrac{1}{66}) \\times 100\\%$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $(\\dfrac{10}{270} - (3)(\\dfrac{1}{66})) \\times 100\\%$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $(\\dfrac{10}{270} - (\\dfrac{1}{3})(\\dfrac{1}{66})) \\times 100\\%$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nGiven, $C=2\\pi r$\n\n$\\dfrac{\\Delta r}{r}=\\dfrac{\\Delta C}{C} = \\dfrac{1}{66}$\n\nAnd knowing that $V=\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^3$, the relative uncertainty on volume will be three times that of the radius\n\n$\\dfrac{\\Delta V}{V} = 3 \\dfrac{\\Delta r}{r}=3(\\dfrac{1}{66}) =(3)(\\dfrac{1}{66})$\n\nUsing the equation for density,\n\n$\\rho =\\dfrac{m}{V}$, the uncertainty is found by adding the relative uncertainties $\\dfrac{\\Delta \\rho}{\\rho}=\\dfrac{\\Delta m}{m}+\\dfrac{\\Delta V}{V}$\n\nAs percentage, that would be: $\\dfrac{\\Delta\\rho}{\\rho}=(\\dfrac{\\Delta m}{m}+\\dfrac{\\Delta V}{V}) \\times 100\\%$\n\n$=(\\dfrac{10}{270}+(3)(\\dfrac{1}{66})) \\times 100\\%$"},{"ref":"PH0258","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0258\n\nIn finding the area of a circular metal plate, a student measures the radius $R$ of the plate and calculates the area to be $A \\pm a$, where $a$ is the absolute uncertainty on the area. Which of the following would give the fractional uncertainty on $R$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{a}{A}}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{a}{2A}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{a}{2\\pi A}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{a}{2}}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nAssuming the measured radius was $R ± r$, \n\nBecause $A = \\pi r^2$\n\nThe fractional uncertainty on $A$ will be twice the fractional uncertainty on $r$, \n\nThus\n\n$\\dfrac{a}{A} = 2(\\dfrac{r}{R})$\n\nGiving the absolute uncertainty on $A$ to be $a = 2(\\dfrac{r}{R})A$\n\nThe question is asking for the fractional uncertainty on $R$ which is ($\\dfrac{r}{R}$)\n\nSolving the equation above gives the answer $\\dfrac{r}{R} = \\dfrac{a}{2A}$"},{"ref":"PH0279","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0279\n\nA student models the relationship between velocity v of a moving charge in a magnetic field, and the force $F$ acting on it as $F = kv$, with $k$ being a constant\n\nWhat are the units for $k$ expressed in fundamental SI units?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ m s\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em} \\ce{kg \\,s^{-1}}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em} \\ce{kg \\,s^{-2}}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em} \\ce{m \\,s^{-2}}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe units for $k$ must be the unit of force divided by the unit of velocity, ($\\dfrac{\\ce{kg \\,m \\,s^{-2}} }{ \\ce{m \\,s^{-1}}}$ ) therefore  $\\ce{kg \\,s^{-1}}$"},{"ref":"PH0327","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0327\n\nAn object starting from rest travels a distance $d$ after a time $t$. If $d = (20\\,\\pm\\,1)$ m, and the time is measured as $t = (2.0\\,\\pm\\,0.4)$ s. Which of the following gives the correct values of the fractional and absolute uncertainties of acceleration?\n\nAssume constant acceleration.\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$ Fractional $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Absolute $\\bf{/m\\,s^{-2}}$ $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ A. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 0.25 | $\\pm$ 2.5 |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ B. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 0.30 | $\\pm$ 3.0 |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ C. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 0.45 | $\\pm$ 4.5 |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ D. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 1.4 | $\\pm$ 14 |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nAcceleration is calculated from rearranging the equation\n\n$d = \\dfrac{1}{2} at^2$ to get $a = \\dfrac{2d}{t^2}$\n\nTherefore $a = \\dfrac{2(20)}{2^2} = 10\\,\\ce{m\\,s^{-2}}$\n\nFractional uncertainty of $t$; $\\dfrac{\\Delta t}{t}=\\dfrac{0.4}{2.0}= 0.2$\n\nFractional uncertainty of $d$; $\\dfrac{\\Delta d}{d}=\\dfrac{1}{20}= 0.05$\n\nIn this calculation, the  fractional uncertainties are added\n\nFor the fractional uncertainty of $a$; $\\dfrac{\\Delta d}{d}+ 2(\\dfrac{\\Delta t}{t}=0.05\\%+ 2(0.2) =0.45$\n\nFor the absolute uncertainty of $a$; $\\Delta a=0.45(10)= 4.5\\,\\ce{m\\,s^{-2}}$"},{"ref":"PH0483","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0483\n\nWhich of the following is **not** a fundamental SI unit?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ampere\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ kelvin\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$newton\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ mole","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe seven fundamental SI units are the ampere, mole, kelvin, kilogram, second, meter and candela. All others are considered derived units."},{"ref":"PH0484","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0484\n\nA body moves a distance of 1.5 km in 10 ks.\n\nWhich of the following gives the correct magnitudes of both distance and time?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Distance | Time |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\hspace{1.5em}1.5 \\times 10^{-4}\\, \\ce{dm}\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}10^{16}\\,  \\ce{ps}\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n| B. | $\\hspace{1.5em}1.5 \\times 10^{4}\\,  \\ce{dm}\\hspace{1.5em}$  | $\\hspace{1.5em}10^{16} \\, \\ce{ps} \\hspace{1.5em}$|\n| C. | $\\hspace{1.5em}1.5 \\times 10^{9} \\, \\mu \\ce{m} \\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}10^{-16}\\,  \\ce{ps}\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n| D. | $\\hspace{1.5em}1.5 \\times 10^{-9}\\,  \\mu \\ce{m} \\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}10^{-16}\\,  \\ce{ps}\\hspace{1.5em}$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe distance is 1.5 km which is $1.5 \\times10^3$ m, to convert it to dm, it will be $1.5 \\times 10^4$ dm.\n\nIf we convert it to micrometers, it will be $1.5 \\times 10^9\\,\\mu$m.\n\nThis eliminates answers A and D.\n\nTo convert the time from 10 ks to ps; it will be $10 \\times 10^{15}\\,\\ce{ps} = 10^{16}\\,\\ce{ps}$\n\ntherefore, the only correct answer for both of distance and time will be B."},{"ref":"PH0485","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0485\n\nWhich of the following gives the unit for work in fundamental SI units?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em} \\ce{m^2\\,s^2}$\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em} \\ce{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em} \\ce{kg\\,m^2\\,s^{-1}}$\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em} \\ce{kg\\,m^2\\,s^{-2}}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nSince   $W=Fs$,\n\nThe unit of work is equivalent to Nm.\n\nBecause, $F=ma$\n\nTherefore the newton is equivalent to  $\\ce{kg\\, m \\,s^{-2}}$\n\nThen, the unit of work is $\\ce{kg\\, m^2\\, s^{-2}}$"},{"ref":"PH0486","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0486\n\nWhich of the following is an estimate of the order of magnitude of the radius of the Earth?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}10^{4}$ m\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}10^{7}$ m\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}10^{10}$ m\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}10^{13}$ m","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe radius of the earth is approximately 10 000 km, which is $10^7 \\,$m. All other values are significantly different from this value."},{"ref":"PH0487","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0487\n\nThe diagram shows a cube of side length 0.010 m and a metal sphere of radius 0.5 cm. The image below is not to scale.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/PH0487.png\" alt=\"PH0487\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: OpenClipart-Vectors from Pixabay https://pixabay.com/vectors/ball-balls-glass-glow-glowing-1293319/ ]}}$\n\nIf there is a set of these spheres that could be melted down, what is the estimated number of spheres required to construct the cube from the material?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 20\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$10\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 4\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 2","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe side length of the cube is 0.010 m which can be written as $1\\times10^{-2}$ m\n\nThe volume of the cube is\n\nVolume of cube = side$^3$ = $(1\\times10^{-2})^3 = 1\\times10^{-6}\\,\\ce{m^3}$\n\nThe radius of the sphere is 0.5 cm which is equivalent to $0.5\\times10^{-2}$ m = $(\\frac{1}{2} )\\times 10^{-2}$ m\n\nVolume of the sphere = $\\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^3$\n\n$\\approx (\\frac{4}{3} )( 3 ) (\\frac{1}{2} \\times 10^{-2})^3 \\approx (4 )(\\frac{1}{8} \\times 10^{-6}) \\approx \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10^{-6}\\, \\ce{m^3}$\n\n*Number of spheres in the cube* $\\approx \\dfrac{1\\times10^{-6}}{\\frac{1}{2} \\times 10^{-6}} \\approx$ 2 spheres"},{"ref":"PH0488","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0488\n\nHow many significant figures are there in 0.000017000?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 2\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 5\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 6\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$9","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nIn a decimal number, the zeroes to the left are not counted as significant figures, but the zeroes to the right are counted.\n\nTherefore, the number of significant figures is 5 as only the bold part will be considered.\n\n0.0000$\\bf{17000}$"},{"ref":"PH0489","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0489\n\nVectors $X$ and $Y$ are in the shown directions.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/PH0489-Q.png\" alt=\"PH0489-Q\">\n\nWhich of the following shows the sum of the two vectors? \n\n| | | | | |\n\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---| \n\n| A.  |    |   |  B. ||\n\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/PH0489QA.png\" alt=\"PH0489QA\">  | $\\hspace{2cm}$ |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/PH0489QB.png\" alt=\"PH0489QB\">|\n\n| $\\hspace{1cm}$ |\n\n| $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n|C. |   |   | D. | |\n\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/PH0489QC.png\" alt=\"PH0489QC\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/PH0489QD.png\" alt=\"PH0489QD\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nTo get the direction of the sum, we have to rearrange these vectors. \n\nWe can arrange them to start from the same point and complete the parallelogram, and then the diagonal from the starting point will be the sum vector.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/PH0489MS.png\" alt=\"PH0489MS\">\n\nUsing any method will give the same direction of the vector, which is in the same direction as choice A."},{"ref":"PH0490","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0490\n\nTwo trucks X and Y are pulling a car out of thick mud along a straight path. The only other horizontal force acting on the car is a drag force opposite to the direction of motion. The diagram shows a top view of the direction of the force applied by truck $X$ and the direction of motion of the car.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/Screenshot%202025-06-30%20at%205.26.09%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-30 at 5.26.09 PM\">\n\nWhich of the following vectors shows the direction of the force applied by truck $Y$? \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/Screenshot%202025-06-30%20at%205.26.19%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-30 at 5.26.19 PM\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe motion of the car here is in the direction of the sum of the two forces vectors from $X$ and $Y$. \\\n\nThe only vector that will add to vector $X$ to produce a net force vector to the right is one in the direction of C.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/Screenshot%202025-06-30%20at%205.26.14%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-30 at 5.26.14 PM\">"},{"ref":"PH0491","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0491\n\nVector $d$ makes an angle $\\theta$ with the horizontal as shown. Its magnitude is $5\\,m$ and the magnitude of its horizontal component $d_x$ is $3\\,m$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/PH0491.png\" alt=\"PH0491\">\n\nWhich of the following is correct about the magnitude and direction of its vertical component?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$ Magnitude $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Direction $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ A. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 4 $m$ | Up |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ B. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\sqrt{34}\\,m$ | Up |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ C. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\sqrt{34}\\,m$ | Down |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ D. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 4 $m$ | Down |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nTo calculate the magnitude of the vertical component of vector $d$\n\n$d_y=\\sqrt{d^2 - d_x\\textrm{}^2} = \\sqrt{5^2 - 3^2}=4 \\,m$\n\nIts direction is vertically downwards, since the vector $d$ is below the horizontal."},{"ref":"PH0493","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0493\n\nWhich of the following is a vector quantity?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Speed\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Electric force\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Electric current\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Temperature","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nFor any force, the direction is an essential aspect of the quantity. All other options here are scalar quantities."},{"ref":"PH0498","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0498\n\nA rectangle has length $L = (2\\,\\pm\\,0.1)\\,$cm and width $W = (3\\,\\pm\\,0.2)\\,$cm. \n\nWhat is the uncertainty of the perimeter of the rectangle?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.08 cm\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.2 cm\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.3 cm\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.6 cm","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nPerimeter = 2$(L+W)$\n\nuncertainty of perimeter will be calculated by adding the uncertainties of the four sides of the rectangle which  gives $(0.1+0.1+0.2+0.2)=  0.6 $ cm"},{"ref":"PH0526-1","marks":"10","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH0526-1\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\n1.\n    Young\\'s double slit experiment is used to determine the wavelength of a monochromatic light source.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/Screenshot%202026-08-23%20at%207.45.59%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-08-23 at 7.45.59 AM\">\n\n    The separation between the two slits $d$ = 0.15 $\\pm$ 0.01 mm.\\\n        The distance between the double slit and the screen $D$ = 1.30 $\\pm$ 0.05 m.\\\n        The distance between the bright fringes $s$ = 3.2 $\\pm$ 0.1 mm\n\n    1. Explain why a double slit is used instead of two light sources. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Determine the wavelength of the light, including its uncertainty. [3 marks]\n\n    3. The experiment is repeated with a larger distance $D$ but the same measurement tools were used.\n\n        State and explain the impact of this change on the uncertainty on the calculated value for wavelength. [3 marks]\n\n2. The experiment was repeated by changing the separation between the slits and the screen.\n\n    The graph shows the variation of the distance between the bright fringes $s$ and the distance between the double slit and the screen $D$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/PH0526%20Graph.png\" alt=\"PH0526 Graph\">\n\n    Describe how the wavelength can be determined using the graph. You do not need to do the calculations.          [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. coherent waves $\\checkmark$\n\n        to have interference pattern/phase difference affects the path difference $\\checkmark$\n\n    2. $\\lambda=\\dfrac{(3.2\\times{10}^{-3})(0.15\\times{10}^{-3})}{1.3}$\n\n        $\\lambda=369.2\\times{10}^{-9}\\ll\\ \\ce{mm}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\Delta \\lambda = 50.3 \\times 10^{-9}\\ll\\ \\ce{m}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\lambda=$ :answer[$370\\times{10}^{-9}\\ \\pm\\ 50\\ \\times{10}^{-9}$ nm]  $\\checkmark$ ($369\\times{10}^{-9}\\ \\pm\\ 50\\ \\times{10}^{-9}$ also accepted if 2 significant figures are kept on the uncertainty)\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The wavelength can be calculated from the formula\n\n         - $s=\\dfrac{\\lambda D}{d}$\n\n        - $\\lambda=\\dfrac{sd}{D}$\n\n        - $\\lambda=\\dfrac{(3.2\\times{10}^{-3})(0.15\\times{10}^{-3})}{1.3}=369$.$23\\times{10}^{-9}\\ce{m}=369.23 \\ \\ce{nm}$\n\n        To calculate the fractional uncertainty in the wavelength, we add the fractional uncertainty of $s$, $d$, and $D$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta \\lambda}{\\lambda} = \\dfrac{\\Delta s}{s} + \\dfrac{\\Delta d}{d} + \\dfrac{\\Delta D}{D}$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta \\lambda}{\\lambda} = \\dfrac{0.1}{3.2} + \\dfrac{0.01}{0.15} + \\dfrac{0.05}{1.3} = 0.13$\n\n        The uncertainty in wavelength $\\Delta \\lambda$ can be found from\n$\\Delta \\lambda = (0.13) \\lambda =(0.13)(369.23 \\times 10^{-9})=50.3 \\times 10^{-9} \\ \\ce{m} = 50 \\ \\ce{nm}$\n\n        Therefore\n\n         - $\\lambda=370\\pm50 \\ $nm\n\n         where 369 has been rounded to match the 1 significant figure precision of the uncertainty. As 2 significant figures are also permissible on uncertainty,\n\n         - $\\lambda=369\\pm50 \\ $nm\n\n         is also acceptable here.\n\n   3. Decreases $\\checkmark$\n\n        The measurement/absolute uncertainty remains the same $\\checkmark$\n\n        But with larger values of $D$ and of fringe spacing, the relative uncertainty decreases $\\checkmark$\n\n2. Calculate the gradient $\\checkmark$\n\n      $\\lambda=gradient\\times\\ \\dfrac{d}{gradient} \\times 0.15 \\ $mm $\\checkmark$\n\n      $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n      The distance between bright fringes $s$ is given by\n      $s=\\dfrac{\\lambda D}{d}$\\\n      Then the gradient of this graph is\n      $gradient=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{d}$\\\n      The wavelength can be calculated from\n      $\\lambda=gradient\\times\\ d$"},{"ref":"PH0551","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0551\n\nAn object is moving at constant velocity. Which of the following could be the free-body diagram representing the forces acting on the object?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n|| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/PH0551A.png\" alt=\"PH0551A\">|$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/PH0551B.png\" alt=\"PH0551B\">|\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  |  <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/PH0551C.png\" alt=\"PH0551C\">|  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/PH0551D.png\" alt=\"PH0551D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAccording to Newton’s First Law, when the resultant force acting on the object is zero, acceleration will be zero and the velocity of the object is constant.  When head to tail method is applied to add the three vectors of option C, the following is obtained.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/PH0551MS.png\" alt=\"PH0551MS\">\n\nThe closed triangle represents a resultant force of zero.\n\nHence, the correct answer is C."},{"ref":"PH0821","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0821\n\nBelow are three suggested units for viscosity\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | kg m$^{-1}$ s$^{-1}$   |\n  | II. | Pa s |\n  | III. | N s m$^{-1}$ |\n\nWhich of the unit combinations is/are correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I and II\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ II and III","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nDrag:\n\n- $F_d=6\\pi \\eta rv$\n\nBy making viscosity the subject of the formula:\n\n- $\\eta =\\dfrac{F_d}{6\\pi rv}$\n\nUnits are substituted:\n\n- $\\eta = \\dfrac{\\textrm{N}}{(\\textrm{m})(\\textrm{m s}^{-1})}=\\dfrac{\\textrm{N}}{(\\textrm{m}^2\\textrm{ s}^{-1})}=\\textrm{ N s m}^{-2}$\n\nBy replacing N m$^{-2}$ = Pa:\n\n- N s m$^{-2}$ = Pa s\n\nBy replacing N = kg m s$^{-2}$:\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{N}}{\\textrm{m}^2\\textrm{ s}^{-1}}=\\dfrac{\\textrm{kg m s}^{-2}}{\\textrm{m}^2\\textrm{ s}^{-1}}=\\textrm{kg m}^{-1}\\textrm{ s}^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH0848","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0848\n\nA box moves a distance of ($5.0 \\pm 0.5)\\ \\ce{m}$ due to a horizontal force of ($20 \\pm 1)\\ \\ce{N}$.\n\nWhat is the percentage uncertainty in the work done on the box?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $0.5\\%$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $1.5\\%$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $9\\%$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $15\\%$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe work done on the box is given by\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $W=Fs$\n\nThe fractional uncertainty in the work done is\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{\\Delta W}{W_0} = \\dfrac{\\Delta F}{F_0} + \\dfrac{\\Delta s}{s_0} = \\dfrac{1}{20} + \\dfrac{0.5}{5}= \\dfrac{1}{20} + \\dfrac{1}{10} = \\dfrac{3}{20}$\n\nThe percentage uncertainty in the work done is\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ Percentage uncertainty = $\\dfrac{\\Delta W}{W_0} \\times 100= \\dfrac{3}{20} \\times 100 =15\\%$"},{"ref":"PH0849","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0849\n\nThe absolute uncertainty in the radius $r$ of a sphere is $\\Delta r$.\n\nWhat is the fractional uncertainty in the volume of the sphere?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\left ( \\dfrac{\\Delta r}{r} \\right )^3$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $3 \\dfrac{\\Delta r}{r}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi \\left( \\dfrac{\\Delta r}{r} \\right)^3$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $4 \\pi \\dfrac{\\Delta r}{r}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe formula for the volume of a sphere is\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $V=\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3$\n\nThe rule for propagating uncertainty is that the fractional uncertainties are added when quantities are multiplied together.\n\nIn this case, the fractional uncertainty on $r$ will add together three times:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{\\Delta r}{r} + \\dfrac{\\Delta r}{r} + \\dfrac{\\Delta r}{r} = 3\\dfrac{\\Delta r}{r}$"},{"ref":"PH0959","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0959\n\nWhich of the following is $\\textbf{not}$ a unit for magnetic flux density?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ T\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Wb m$^2$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ kg s$^{-2}\\,\\ce{A}^{-1}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ N A$^{-1}\\,\\ce{m}^{-1}$","markScheme":"**答案：B**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nA is correct - Tesla (T) is the unit for magnetic flux density\n\n$\\textbf{B is incorrect}$ - Weber (Wb) is the unit of magnetic flux\n\nMagnetic flux density $= \\dfrac{\\text{magnetic flux}}{\\text{area}}$\n\nSo 1 T = 1 Wb m$^{-2}$\n\nC is correct (C is the SI base unit of magnetic flux density)\n\n- $B=\\dfrac{F}{IL}=\\dfrac{ma}{IL}$\n\n- $T=\\dfrac{\\ce{N}}{\\ce{A}\\,\\ce{m}}=\\dfrac{\\ce{kg}\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}}{\\ce{A}\\,\\ce{m}}=\\ce{kg}\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}\\,\\ce{A}^{-1}$\n\nD is correct\n\n- $B=\\dfrac{F}{IL}$\n\n- $T=\\dfrac{\\ce{N}}{\\ce{A}\\,\\ce{m}}=\\ce{N}\\,\\ce{A}^{-1}\\,\\ce{m}^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH0966","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"?","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0966\n\nWhich of the following quantities has the same units as force per unit charge?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Electric current\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Electric field strength\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Electric potential difference\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Electric potential energy","markScheme":"**答案：B**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n$E=\\dfrac{F}{q}$ so electric field strength has same units as ‘force per unit charge’.\n\nElectric field strength at a point is defined as:\n\nThe force per unit charge exerted on a small positive test charge at that point."},{"ref":"PH1758","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1758\n\nThe velocity $v$ of an object accelerating non-uniformly as a function of time $t$ is given by:\n\n$v=5t^\\frac{5}{2}$\n\nA student plots a graph of $\\ln(v)$ against $\\ln(t)$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/PH1758.JPEG\" alt=\"PH1758\">\n\nWhat is the gradient and $y$-intercept of the graph?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Gradient$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Y-intercept$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $\\ln\\Bigl(\\dfrac{5}{2}\\Bigr)$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $\\ln(5)$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 5 | $\\ln\\Bigl(\\dfrac{5}{2}\\Bigr)$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 5 | $\\ln(5)$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nTaking the natural logarithm of both sides of the equation we have:\n\n- $v=5t^\\frac{5}{2}$\n\n- $\\ln(v) = \\ln\\Bigl(5t^\\frac{5}{2}\\Bigr)$\n\n- $\\ln(v) =\\ln(5) + \\ln\\Bigl(t^\\frac{5}{2}\\Bigr)$\n\n- $\\ln(v) = \\ln(5) + \\dfrac{5}{2}\\ln(t)$\n\nComparing this to the equation of a straight line $y=mx+c$ where $m$ is the gradient and $c$ is the y-intercept we have:\n\n- $y=mx+c$\n\n- $\\ln(v) = \\dfrac{5}{2}(\\ln(t)) + \\ln(5)$\n\n- gradient = $\\dfrac{5}{2}$\n\n- $y$-intercept = $\\ln(5)$"},{"ref":"PH1759","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1759\n\nTwo forces A and B are acting on an object. The magnitude of force A is 3.0 N. Below shows a scale diagram representing forces A and B.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/PH1759.JPEG\" alt=\"PH1759\">\n\nWhat is the net force acting on the object?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 3.0 N\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 4.0 N\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 5.0 N\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 6.0 N","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe net force acting on the object is the vector sum of A and B shown below as F:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/PH1759MS.JPEG\" alt=\"PH1759MS\">\n\nUsing a ruler to measure the sizes of A and F. We see that the length of F is double the length of A. Therefore, the magnitude of F is:\n\n- $F=2(3)=6$ N.\n\nAlternatively, we can see that vector A has components of 2 units in one direction and one unit in the other. Vector F is made of components of 4 units in one direction and two in the other, and therefore is twice the size of vector A."},{"ref":"PH1760","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1760\n\nAt a point in space, the magnitude of the gravitational field strength in units of N kg$^{-1}$ is equal to the magnitude of the electric field strength in units of N C$^{-1}$. A proton is placed at this point.\n\nTo the nearest order of magnitude what is\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{Electric force acting on the proton}}{\\textrm{Gravitational force acting on the proton}}$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $10^7$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $10^8$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $10^9$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $10^{10}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe gravitational force acting on an object is given by:\n\n- $F_g=mg$\n\nWhere $m$ is the mass of the object and $g$ is the gravitational field strength.\n\nThe electric force acting on an object is given by:\n\n- $F_e=qE$\n\nWhere $q$ is the charge of the object and $E$ is the electric field strength.\n\nFor the proton we have:\n\n- $\\dfrac{F_e}{F_g}=\\dfrac{q_pE}{m_pg}$\n\n- $m_p=1.673\\times10^{-27}$ kg and $q_p=1.6\\times10^{-19}$ C\n\nDropping units, we have $E=g$. Therefore:\n\n- $\\dfrac{F_e}{F_g}=\\dfrac{q_p}{m_p}=\\dfrac{1.6\\times10^{-19}}{1.673\\times10^{-27}}\\approx\\dfrac{10^{-19}}{10^{-27}}=10^{27-19}=10^8$"},{"ref":"PH1761","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1761\n\nA cylinder is measured to have a length of 10.0 cm $\\pm$ 0.4 cm and a diameter of 8.0 cm $\\pm$ 0.4 cm.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/Screenshot%202025-02-09%20at%201.21.36%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-02-09 at 1.21.36 PM\">\n\nWhat is the percentage uncertainty in the volume of the cylinder?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 1.2\\%\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 9\\%\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 14\\%\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 24\\%","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe fractional uncertainty of a quantity $y=\\dfrac{ab}{c}$ is given by\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\Delta y}{y}=\\dfrac{\\Delta a}{a}+\\dfrac{\\Delta b}{b}+\\dfrac{\\Delta c}{c}$\n\nWhere $\\Delta y$, $\\Delta a$, $\\Delta b$ and $\\Delta c$ are the absolute uncertainties.\n\nNote that the uncertainty on the radius $\\Delta r$ is the same as the diameter $\\Delta d$.\n\nThe volume of a cylinder $V$ is given by:\n\n- $V=\\pi r^2h=\\pi(r)(r)(h)$\n\nWhere $r$ is the radius of the circular end and $h$ is the height. So the fractional uncertainty in the volume is given by:\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\Delta V}{V}=\\dfrac{\\Delta r}{r}+\\dfrac{\\Delta r}{r}+\\dfrac{\\Delta h}{h}$\n\nThe fractional uncertainty in $h$ is given by:\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\Delta h}{h}=\\dfrac{0.4}{10}=0.04$\n\nThe fractional uncertainty in $r$ is equal to the fractional uncertainty in diameter given by:\n\n- $\\hspace{0.02cm}$ $\\dfrac{\\Delta r}{r}=\\dfrac{\\Delta d}{d}=\\dfrac{0.4}{8}=0.05$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\Delta V}{V}=\\dfrac{\\Delta r}{r}+\\dfrac{\\Delta r}{r}+\\dfrac{\\Delta h}{h}=0.05+0.05+0.04=0.14$\n\nFinally writing this a percentage:\n\n- $(0.14)(100)=14\\%$"},{"ref":"PH1762","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1762\n\nA student investigates the motion of a trolley on a slope by measuring the time, $t$, it takes to travel down the slope. The length of the slope, $s$, is varied, and the student plots a graph of $t^2$ against $s$, as shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/PH1762.JPEG\" alt=\"PH1762\">\n\nWhich of the following best describes the types of error present in the experiment?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Systematic only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Random only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Random and systematic\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ No errors are present","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe graph shows $t^2$ plotted against $s$, with data points scattered around an apparent trend. From the graph:\n\n**Random Error:**\n\nThe data points do not lie perfectly on a straight line, indicating random fluctuations in the measurements of time or length.\n\nThese errors may arise from factors such as reaction time when starting or stopping the timer or slight inconsistencies in releasing the trolley.\n\n**Systematic Error:**\n\nIf the best-fit line is drawn, it does not pass through the origin, which suggests a systematic error in the experiment.\n\nThis could result from a consistent offset in the measurement tools, such as a timer starting too late or an incorrect zeroing of the length.\n\nBoth random and systematic errors are present."},{"ref":"PH1830","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH1830\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nA student investigates the efficiency of a flask in maintaining the temperature $\\theta$  of 1 kg of water over time $t$. The temperature was recorded every 10 minutes for a total duration of 24 h.\n\nThe graph below shows the trend that was recorded during the experiment.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/PH1830qa.JPEG\" alt=\"PH1830qa\">\n\nThe student wishes to investigate how the rate of decrease in temperature $(v_\\theta)$ is affected by the difference $\\Delta\\theta$ between the water temperature and the room temperature, which was recorded as 20°C.\n\n1. State the value of $\\Delta\\theta$ at $t=9$ h. [1 mark]\n\n2. By drawing a suitable tangent, determine the rate $v_\\theta$ at which temperature is decreasing at $t=9$ h. [3 marks]\n\nThe student calculates the value of $v_\\theta$ for different values of temperatures.\n\nThe graph below shows the **magnitude** of $v_\\theta$ for different values of $\\Delta\\theta$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/Screenshot%202025-03-12%20at%2010.41.44%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-03-12 at 10.41.44 AM\">\n\n3. Use your answer to (b) to plot the data point at $\\Delta\\theta=25$°C. [1 mark]\n\nAfter plotting the graph, the student realises that the values of $|v_\\theta|$ were calculated with a relative uncertainty of $\\pm$10% and that the uncertainty in $\\Delta\\theta$ is negligible.\n\n4. On the graph above, draw error bars at the points where $\\Delta\\theta=10$°C and $\\Delta\\theta=70$°C. [2 marks]\n\nThe student suggests that the rate at which temperature of the water decreases is proportional to the temperature difference between the water and the environment.\n\n5. State and explain whether the data supports the student's suggestion. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. $\\Delta\\theta=$ :answer[25 $\\ll$°C$\\gg$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    From the graph, at $t=9$ h water temperature was about 45°C.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/PH1830aa.JPEG\" alt=\"PH1830aa\">\n\n    The difference between this and room temperature (20°C) is then:\n\n    - $\\Delta\\theta=45$°C$ \\ - \\ 20$°C\n\n    - $\\Delta\\theta=25$°C\n\n2. Tangent at $t=9$ h traced. $\\checkmark$\n\n    Choosing (any) two points along the tangent (points at least 8 h apart). $\\checkmark$\n\n    Calculation of the gradient using $v_\\theta=\\dfrac{\\Delta\\theta}{\\Delta t}$\n\n    Calculated gradient must be within -3.5°C h$^{-1}$ and -2.5°C h$^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    To find the instantaneous rate at which temperature is decreasing, we must first trace a tangent to the graph at $t=9$ h.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/PH1830ab1.JPEG\" alt=\"PH1830ab1\">\n\n    Now we must choose two points through which this tangent passes. It is recommended to choose points that are far apart from each other.\n\n    Different students might of course choose different points leading to a slightly different estimate of the rate of decrease in temperature.\n\n    We will use points (4, 60) and (22,5). These are points through which the tangent seems to be passing.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/PH1830ab2.JPEG\" alt=\"PH1830ab2\">\n\n    We can now calculate the gradient of this line as:\n\n    - $v_\\theta=\\dfrac{\\Delta\\theta}{\\Delta t}=\\dfrac{5\\textrm{°C}-60\\textrm{°C}}{22\\textrm{ h}-4\\textrm{ h}}$\n\n    - $v_\\theta=-3.1$ °C h$^{-1}$\n\n3. Data point plotted correctly at (25, 3.1) $\\left(\\pm\\dfrac{1}{2}\\textrm{ square}\\right)$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/Screenshot%202025-03-12%20at%2010.44.02%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-03-12 at 10.44.02 AM\">\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The data that corresponds $\\Delta\\theta=25$°C has a horizontal coordinate which is equal to 25°C and a vertical coordinate that corresponds to the magnitude of our answer to question (b): i.e. 3.1 °C h$^{-1}$.\n\n    Point should then be plotted at (25, 3.1)\n\n4. *Vertical* error bars plotted both points. $\\checkmark$\n\n    Error bars of the correct length for each point. $\\checkmark$\n\n    (Approximately $\\pm0.1$ for the first point and $\\pm0.8$ for the second one)\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/Screenshot%202025-03-12%20at%2010.44.41%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-03-12 at 10.44.41 AM\">\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The relative uncertainty of $\\pm10$% affects only $|v_\\theta|$ . This means that the error bars must be represented vertically. No horizontal error bars are required given that the uncertainty in $\\Delta\\theta$ is negligible.\n\n    As seen on the graph, for the data point at $\\Delta\\theta=$10°C, the vertical coordinate is $|v_\\theta|=1$ °C h$^{-1}$. The relative uncertainty for this point is then:\n\n    - $\\Delta(|v_\\theta|)_{\\textrm{relative}}=0.1(1$ °C h$^{-1})=0.1$ °C h$^{-1}$\n\n    For the data point at $\\Delta\\theta=70$°C, the vertical coordinate is $|v_\\theta|=8$°C h$^{-1}$. The relative uncertainty for this point is then:\n\n    - $\\Delta(|v_\\theta|)_{\\textrm{relative}}=0.1(8$ °C h$^{-1})=0.8$ °C h$^{-1}$\n\n    We must then represent the corresponding error bars with these lengths.\n\n5. The data supports the student's suggestion. $\\checkmark$\n\n    A line that passes through the origin can be traced on the graph that passes through the error bars. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The student's suggestion is that the graph of $|v_\\theta|$ against $\\Delta\\theta$ should be linear and passing through the origin (proportionality).\n\n    The data supports this claim given that a line can be traced from the origin that passes (within the given uncertainties) through every point.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/Screenshot%202025-03-12%20at%2010.45.23%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-03-12 at 10.45.23 AM\">"},{"ref":"PH1831","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH1831\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nA student wishes to investigate how the viscosity of oil is affected by its temperature. An experimental setup is designed by the student to investigate the phenomenon.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/Screenshot%202025-03-25%20at%2010.40.24%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-03-25 at 10.40.24 AM\">\n\nThe apparatus includes an electric heater positioned at the base of a water-filled container. Inside this container, a smaller cylinder containing oil is fully immersed in the water.\n\nA thermometer monitors the water temperature. A small metallic sphere is dropped into the oil-filled cylinder, where it descends to the bottom. The time $t$, required for the sphere to travel between two reference marks, spaced a distance $h$ apart, is measured.\n\n1. Explain, in terms of heat transfer, the experimental reason that requires:\n\n    1. the heating element to be placed at the bottom. [1 mark]\n\n    2. the oil-filled cylinder to be completely immersed in water. [1 mark]\n\n2. The metal sphere is dropped and the time taken to fall at constant speed through a distance $h=62.0\\pm0.2$ cm is measured to be $t=2.0\\pm0.1$ s.\n\n    The student used these values to calculate the average speed as 0.31 m s$^{-1}$.\n\n    Use these values to determine the absolute uncertainty on the calculated value for the speed. [2 marks]\n\n3. The student decides to use a model for the oil viscosity $\\eta$ that suggests that it is inversely proportional to the speed $v$ at which the sphere falls.\n\n    According to this model\n\n    - $\\eta=\\dfrac{k}{v}$\n\n    where $k$ is a constant.\n\n    State the units of $k$ in terms of fundamental SI units. [1 mark]\n\nTo investigate this phenomenon further, another student proposes to use a model for the viscosity $\\eta$ of the oil at a temperature $T$ to be:\n\n- $\\eta=\\alpha T^\\beta$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined.\n\nThe experiment is repeated several times. Each time, the temperature $T$ of the oil is recorded and the velocity at which the ball falls is used to calculate $\\eta$.\n\nThen, a graph of $\\log\\eta$ is plotted against $\\log T$.\n\n4. State and explain which features of such a graph would allow the constants $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ to be obtained. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. To allow for convection currents that will lead to a constant temperature across the volume of water. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Any heating element placed at the lower part of a fluid will allow for convection currents to distribute heat uniformly through the liquid. As water in contact with the heat source increases its temperature it will expand reducing its density. This lower density will force this heated water to rise allowing for cooler water to descend and thus establishing circular flow that facilitates heat transfer by a process named *convection*.\n\n        These currents will contribute to the uniformity of temperature across the entire volume of water within the cylinder.\n\n    2. Conduction of heat from water into the oil will be more efficient to have a constant temperature along the entire volume of oil. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        By having the oil-filled cylinder completely immersed in water, heat transfer by conduction from the water into the oil will be more efficient thus leading to a uniform temperature along the volume of oil.\n\n2. ::indent\n    - $\\Delta v=\\left(\\dfrac{0.002\\textrm{ m}}{0.62\\textrm{ m}}+\\dfrac{0.1\\textrm{ s}}{2.0\\textrm{ s}}\\right)(0.31)$ m s$^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $\\Delta v= 0.02 \\textrm{ m s}^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    (accept 0.016 m s$^{-1}$ or 0.017 m s$^{-1}$, rounded to 2 sf. Any more than 2 sf will not receive the mark)\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Given that the speed $v$ is calculated as:\n\n    - $v=\\dfrac{h}{t}$\n\n    where $h$ is the distance travelled by the metal sphere and $t$ is the time, its absolute uncertainty can be calculated as:\n\n    - $\\dfrac{\\Delta v}{v}=\\dfrac{\\Delta h}{h}+\\dfrac{\\Delta t}{t}$\n\n    This expression leads to:\n\n    - $\\begin{aligned}\n    \\ \\Delta v&=\\left(\\dfrac{\\Delta h}{h}+\\dfrac{\\Delta t}{t}\\right)(v)\\\\\n    \\\\\\\n    \\ \\Delta v&=\\left(\\dfrac{0.002\\textrm{ m}}{0.62\\textrm{ m}}+\\dfrac{0.1\\textrm{ s}}{2.0\\textrm{ s}}\\right)(0.31\\textrm{ m s}^{-1})\\\\\n    \\\\\\\n    \\ \\Delta v&=0.0165\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}\\\\\n    \\end{aligned}$\n\n    Because it is an uncertainty, this value should be rounded to 1 significant figure, leading to:\n\n    - $\\Delta v=0.02$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n3. $[k]=[\\eta v]=$ kg s$^{-2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The given expression leads to the constant $k$ being equal to:\n\n    - $k=\\eta v$\n\n    Which means that the units of $k$ are given as the product of the units of $\\eta$ times the units of $v$.\n\n    We can use the expression for the drag force to solve for $\\eta v$:\n\n    - $F_d=6\\pi\\eta rv$\n\n    According to this expression\n\n    - $[\\eta v]=\\dfrac{[F_d]}{[6\\pi r]}$\n\n    - $[\\eta v]=\\dfrac{\\textrm{N}}{\\textrm{m}}=\\dfrac{\\textrm{kg m s}^{-2}}{\\textrm{m}}=$ kg s$^{-2}$\n\n    Therefore:\n\n    - $[k]=$ kg s$^{-2}$\n\n4. $\\eta=\\alpha T^\\beta$ leads to $\\log\\eta=\\beta\\log T+\\log\\alpha$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\ll$On a graph of $\\log\\eta$ against $\\log T\\gg$ $\\beta$ would be the gradient of the graph $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\log\\alpha$ would be the y-intercept of the graph $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    According to $\\eta=\\alpha T^\\beta$, taking logarithms on each side leads to:\n\n    - $\\log\\eta=\\log\\left(\\alpha T^\\beta\\right)$\n\n    The right-hand side of this equation can be expanded as:\n\n    - $\\log\\eta=\\beta\\log(T)+\\log\\alpha$\n\n    This equation can be interpreted as a linear one of the form $y=mx+b$ in which $\\log\\eta$ is the dependent variable ($y$), and $\\log T$ is the independent variable ($x$).\n\n    The constant $\\beta$ would then be the gradient and $\\log\\alpha$ would be the y-intercept.\n\n    On a graph of $\\log\\eta$ against $\\log T$, $\\beta$ would be the gradient and $\\alpha$ would be equal to $10^{\\textrm{y-intercept}}$."},{"ref":"PH1837","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH1837\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\n1. A student wants to measure the bending of a long plastic ruler. The ruler is fixed at both ends and a mass is added at its midpoint. The student varies the mass and measures the vertical deflection.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/Screenshot%202025-08-27%20at%2012.42.28%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-08-27 at 12.42.28 PM\">\n\n    The following data are different trials to measure deflection of the same mass.\n\n    $\\\\$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/ph1837q1b.JPEG\" alt=\"ph1837q1b\">\n\n    $\\\\$\n\n    1. State why performing several trials can lead to a more accurate experimental result.   [1 mark]\n\n    2. Calculate the average value for the deflection value $d$, making use of proper experimental techniques. [2 marks]\n\n2. The deflection of the ruler is predicted to be directly proportional with mass.\n\n    The graph shows the variation of deflection with mass.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/Screenshot%202025-08-27%20at%2012.47.24%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-08-27 at 12.47.24 PM\">\n\n    1. Referring to  the graph, state the classification of experimental error that has occurred and suggest a reason for this error.   [2 marks]\n\n    2. The relation between deflection and mass is given by\n\n        $d=\\dfrac{mgL^3}{48EI}$\n\n        Where $L$ is the length of the ruler and the unit of $I$ is m$^4$.\n\n        Determine the units of $E$ in SI fundamental units. [2 marks]\n\n    3. The following set of data is given\n\n        $m=(35\\pm0.1)$ kg\n\n        $L=(2.0\\pm0.0005)$ m\n\n        $d=(5.0\\pm0.05)$ cm\n\n        $I=(2.5\\pm0.4)\\times10^{-9}$ m$^4$\n\n        The uncertainty of $g$ is negligible.\n\n        Determine the absolute uncertainty of $E$. [3 marks]\n\n    4. The student suggests that the value of $I$ depends on the cross-sectional area of the ruler.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/images/ph1837qd.JPEG\" alt=\"ph1837qd\">\n\n        $I$ is directly proportional to $h^3$.\n\n        A student uses a measuring tape to find the value of $h$ to be $3.0\\pm0.5$ mm.\n\n        Suggest an instrument that could be used to obtain an improved value for $h$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. Random error can be eliminated through averaging. $\\checkmark$\n\n        **Or**\n\n        Anomalies can be avoided $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        If just one trial is taken, that value could have a random fluctuation from the actual value and there is no way of knowing this. By taking a number of readings and averaging the results, random error will cause the readings to be above and below the actual value, and averaging the readings will give a result closer to the actual value. Also, if there is a significant anomaly, this can be seen when compared to the other readings.\n\n    2. Removes the $1.7$ cm reading as an outlier. $\\checkmark$\n\n        - Average$=\\dfrac{2.4+2.3+2.2+2.3+2.1}{5}= 2.3\\  \\ce{cm}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The outlier is at trial 3\n\n        If we exclude the outlier, then\n\n        - Average$=\\dfrac{2.4+2.3+2.2+2.3+2.1}{5}=2.3$ cm\n\n2. 1. Systematic error $\\checkmark$\n\n        Zero error in measuring devices.\n\n        **Or**\n\n        Beam deflects under its own weight $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The graph is not passing through the origin and yet the points form a straight line. This suggests that all measurements differ from the real value by a fixed amount.\n\n        There is a systematic error.\n\n        This systematic error can be due to zero error in measuring devices.\n\n        It also can be because the beam deflects under its own weight, and so would have non-zero deflection for no mass added.\n\n    2. Substitution of units for quantities $\\dfrac{(\\textrm{kg})(\\textrm{m s}^{-2})(\\textrm{m}^3)}{(\\textrm{m})(\\textrm{m}^4)}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        kg m$^{-1}$ s$^{-2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n        - $E=\\dfrac{mgL^3}{48dI}$\n\n        Unit of $E$ is found by substituting the units for all quantities in the expression. Note that $g$ is an acceleration and the units on $I$ have been given in the question.\n\n        - $\\dfrac{(\\textrm{kg})(\\textrm{m s}^{-2})(\\textrm{m}^3)}{(\\textrm{m})(\\textrm{m}^4)}=\\textrm{kg m}^{-1}\\textrm{ s}^{-2}$\n\n    3. At least one fractional uncertainty is correct $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta E}{E}=\\dfrac{0.1}{35}+(3)\\left(\\dfrac{0.0005}{2}\\right)+\\dfrac{0.05}{5}+\\dfrac{0.4}{2.5}=0.174$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\Delta E=\\dfrac{\\Delta E}{E}\\times E=(0.174)(4.6\\times10^{11})= 8\\times10^{10}\\ll \\ce{kg m}^{-1} \\ce{s}^{-2}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Since\n\n        - $E=\\dfrac{mgL^3}{48dI}$\n\n        Then\n\n        - $E=\\dfrac{(35)(9.8)(2^3)}{(48)(5\\times10^{-2})(2.5\\times10^{-9})}=4.6\\times10^{11}$ kg m$^{-1}$ s$^{-2}$\n\n        The fractional uncertainty in $E$ is given by\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta E}{E}=\\dfrac{\\Delta m}{m}+(3)\\left(\\dfrac{\\Delta L}{L}\\right)+\\dfrac{\\Delta d}{d}+\\dfrac{\\Delta I}{I}$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta E}{E}=\\dfrac{0.1}{35}+(3)\\left(\\dfrac{0.0005}{2}\\right)+\\dfrac{0.05}{5}+\\dfrac{0.4}{2.5}=0.174$\n\n        The absolute uncertainty is given by\n\n        - $\\Delta E=\\dfrac{\\Delta E}{E}\\times E=(0.174)(4.6\\times10^{11})=8\\times10^{10}$ kg m$^{-1}$ s$^{-2}$\n\n    4. Any instrument with lower uncertainty (micrometer/vernier caliper) $\\checkmark$\n\n        Lower fractional uncertainty/more precise $I$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        A micrometer or a vernier caliper has a lower uncertainty than a meter tape, specifically around 0.01 mm for a micrometer and 0.1 mm for a vernier caliper.\n\n        This increases the precision of measuring $h$ and decreases its fractional uncertainty, leading to a more precise calculation of $I$."}]},{"name":"Unassigned","questions":[{"ref":"PH0036","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0036\n\nA spring of negligible mass and length of $l_o$ is attached to a fixed point. When the spring is extended by an external force $F$, the length of the spring increases to $l$.  The graph shows how the length of the spring $L$ varies with the tension $T$ in the spring. The tension in the spring is equal to $k \\Delta l$, where k is a constant and $\\Delta l$ is the extension of the spring.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-03%20at%202.57.48%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-03 at 2.57.48 PM\">\n\nWhat are the **gradient** and **y-intercept** of the graph?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1.5em}$ Gradient $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$ y-intercept $\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ A. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{k}$ | $l$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ B. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{k}$ | $l_o$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ C. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $k$ | $l_o$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ D. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{k}$ | $\\Delta l$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe y-intercept is the length of the spring when there is no external force. Therefore, the y-intercept is $l_o$\n\nGradient is $\\cfrac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}$ on the graph\n\nHooke's Law states that $F = k\\Delta x$ and in this case $\\Delta x = l - l_o$\n\nThe gradient = $\\cfrac{l-l_o}{F} = \\cfrac{\\Delta x}{F}$\n\nTherefore, $gradient = \\dfrac{1}{k}$"},{"ref":"PH0217","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0217\n\nWhich of the following units is not a unit of power?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ W\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ J\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ N m s$^{-1}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ J s$^{-1}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe SI unit of power is W and it is defined as J s$^{-1}$.\n\nAdditionally, power can be calculated by\n\n- $P = Fv$\n\nWhen units of force and speed are substituted in the equation, the units become\n\n$\\hspace{1.5em}$ N m s$^{-1}$\n\nBut J is the SI unit of energy.\n\nTherefore, the answer is B."},{"ref":"PH0259","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0259\n\nA pilot intends to fly a plane from point $A$ to point $B$. Due to strong winds, she points the nose of the plane in the direction shown by the vector $P$. Which of the choices below best represents the direction of the wind encountered by the plane?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/PH0259-1.png\" alt=\"PH0259-1\">\n\n| | | | | |\n\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---| \n\n| A.  |    |  |  B. ||\n\n|<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/PH0259A.png\" alt=\"PH0259A\"> | $\\hspace{2cm}$   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/PH0259B.png\" alt=\"PH0259B\">|\n\n| $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n|C. |   |   | D. | |\n\n|<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/PH0259C.png\" alt=\"PH0259C\"> |   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/PH0259D.png\" alt=\"PH0259D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nIn order for the plane to travel from $A$ to $B$, the resultant vector of the sum of the wind vector and the vector of the orientation of the plane must point in the same direction as the vector from $A$ to $B$, as shown in the diagram below. For any of the other choices, the resultant will not be in the direction $A$ to $B$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/PH0259aaa.png\" alt=\"PH0259aaa\">"},{"ref":"PH0278","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0278\n\nA student is using the equation $pV = nRT$ to solve for $n$ with the following percentage uncertainties in the measurements. \n\n| $\\hspace{1em}$ Quantity $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Uncertainty $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n| :-: | :-: |\n\n| P | $\\pm\\,2\\,\\%$ |\n\n| V | $\\pm\\,6\\,\\%$ |\n\n| T | $\\pm\\,1\\,\\%$ |\n\nWhat is the percentage uncertainty in the calculated value of n?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 3 %\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 7 %\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 9 %\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 12 %","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nTo calculate $n$, the student would rearrange the formula to get\n\n- $n=\\dfrac{PV}{RT}$\n\nIn both operations, the percentage uncertainty must be added, therefore the correct answer is C (2 \\% + 6 \\% + 1 \\% = 9 \\%)"},{"ref":"PH0492","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0492\n\nA stream flows east with a speed $v_s$. A boat points due south and moves with a speed $v_o$ relative to the water.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screensho0t%202024-10-26%20at%203.57.21%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screensho0t 2024-10-26 at 3.57.21 PM\">\n\nThe ratio  $\\dfrac{v_o}{v_s} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$\n\nWhich of the following is correct about the magnitude in m s$^{-1}$ and the angle $\\theta$ between the velocity of the boat and the side of the stream?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$ Magnitude $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{2.5em}$ $\\theta$ $\\hspace{2.5em}$ |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ A. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\sqrt{3}\\,v_o$ | $\\sin^{-1}\\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg)$ |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ B. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\sqrt{3}\\,v_o$ | $\\cos^{-1}\\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg)$ |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ C. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\sqrt{5}\\,v_o$ | $\\tan^{-1}(2)$ |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ D. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\sqrt{5}\\,v_o$ | $\\tan^{-1}\\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg)$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nSince $v_s = 2\\,v_o$\n\nThe magnitude of the net velocity \n\n- $v_{net}=\\sqrt{v_o\\textrm{}^2 +(2v_o)^2} = \\sqrt{5} v_o$\n\nThe direction is determined by the angle it makes with the side of the river\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/PH0492zzz2.png\" alt=\"PH0492zzz2\">\n\n- $\\tan \\theta = \\dfrac{v_o}{2v_o} =\\dfrac{1}{2}$  \n\n- $\\theta = \\tan^{-1}(\\dfrac{1}{2})$\n\nThus the previous answer of $\\sqrt{5} v_o$ combined with $\\theta = \\tan^{-1}(\\dfrac{1}{2})$ corresponds to answer D."},{"ref":"PH0494","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0494\n\nAn object moves 9 m east, then 12 m north. \n\nWhat is the magnitude of the total displacement of this object?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 3 m\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 15 m\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 21 m\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 36 m","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThrough vector addition, \n\nDisplacement = $\\sqrt{12^2+9^2}=$ 15 m\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-07%20at%2011.15.26%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-07 at 11.15.26 AM\">"},{"ref":"PH0495","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0495\n\nA ball is projected with an initial speed of 2 m s$^{-1}$ making an angle of $35\\degree$ with the horizontal as shown.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-07%20at%2011.06.31%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-07 at 11.06.31 AM\">\n\nWhich expression gives the vertical component of the velocity vector?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2\\cos(35\\degree)$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2\\sin(35\\degree)$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2\\tan(35\\degree$) m s$^{-1}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-07%20at%2011.06.37%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-07 at 11.06.37 AM\">\n\n- $\\sin(35\\degree) = \\dfrac{v_y}{2}$\n\nTherefore \n\n- $v_y = 2\\sin(35\\degree)$"},{"ref":"PH0496","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0496\n\nA car moves clockwise in a circular path as shown. The direction of its instantaneous velocity at two different times is shown in the diagram. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/PH0496.png\" alt=\"PH0496\">\n\nWhich of the following vectors shows the direction of the change in velocity?\n\n| | | | | |\n\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---| \n\n| A.  |   |  $\\hspace{3cm}$  | B.  ||\n\n|<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/PH0496A.png\" alt=\"PH0496A\"> |   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/PH0496B.png\" alt=\"PH0496B\">|\n\n|C. |   |   |  D.| |\n\n|<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/PH0496C.png\" alt=\"PH0496C\">|   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/PH0496D.png\" alt=\"PH0496D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe change in velocity = final velocity - initial velocity = $v_2 + (-v_1)$. In a vector diagram, we represent $-v_1$ as in the opposite direction to the $v_1$ direction, and then add the vectors $v_2$ and  $(-v_1)$ as shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/PH0496-1.png\" alt=\"PH0496-1\">"},{"ref":"PH0497","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0497\n\nA spring with spring constant $k$ is extended by a distance $x$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-07%20at%208.19.25%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-07 at 8.19.25 AM\">\n\n$\\small{\\textrm{[Created using Geogebra https://www.geogebra.org/m/vUK7gGkN]}}$\n\nThe uncertainty of $k$ is 2% and that of $x$ is 3%. \n\nWhat is the uncertainty of the potential energy stored in the spring? \n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 8%\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 11%\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 12%\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 18%","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe formula for potential energy stored is \n\n- $E_H=\\dfrac{1}{2}kx^2$\n\nSince $E_H$ is calculated from multiplying $k$ and $x^2$ (ignoring the constant $\\frac{1}{2}$ as there is no uncertainty for it), then the uncertainty of $E_H$ is calculated by adding the fractional uncertainties of $x^2$ and $k$.\n\nThe fractional uncertainty of $x^2$ is double the fractional uncertainty on $x$, which gives \n\n- $2\\times3\\% = 6\\%$\n\nThe uncertainty of $E_P$ therefore \n\n-  2% + 6% = 8%"},{"ref":"PH0509","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH0509\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nA student uses the shown setup to measure the speed of sound.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-16%20at%204.01.15%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-16 at 4.01.15 PM\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Image created using Images by OpenClipart-Vectors and Josy Dom Alexis from Pixabay - https ://pixabay.com]}}$\n\nThe sound source has a frequency of 590 Hz. The student changes the distance between the two microphones *d* and records the time difference $\\Delta$*t* between the received sounds from the microphones.\n\nThe graph below shows the data from this experiment.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-06-04%20at%204.52.12%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-04 at 4.52.12 PM\">\n\n1. Draw the line of best fit. [1 mark]\n\n2. Determine the fractional uncertainty of *d* when the time difference *$\\Delta$t* is 2.2 ms. [2 marks]\n\n3. Showing your working on the graph, calculate the speed of the sound wave.[3 marks]\n\n4. Determine the wavelength of the sound. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. $\\hspace{1em}$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%2010.51.21%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 10.51.21 AM\">\n\n    Any straight line that passes through all uncertainty bars $\\checkmark$\n\n2. correct value and uncertainty for d: $d=0.75\\pm0.04 $ m  $\\checkmark$\n\n    $fractional\\ unceratinty \\ = \\ \\ll \\dfrac{\\Delta d}{d} \\gg \\ = \\ \\ll \\dfrac{0.04}{0.75} \\gg \\ = \\dfrac{4}{75}$  or 5\\% $\\checkmark$\n\n3. Recognises that gradient of the line is $\\dfrac{s}{t} $\n\n    Use of gradient, with selected points indicated on graph. Points must be separated by at least 2 ms to receive mark $\\checkmark$\n\n    Shown calculation of gradient.\n\n    - $v=gradient=\\dfrac{0.9-0.18}{(2.6-0.5)\\times{10}^{-3}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    *If points used do not fall on line of best fit, do not award this marking point*\n\n    - $v=$ :answer[$343\\ll\\ \\ce{m s}^{-1}\\gg$] $\\checkmark$ \\\n    (Values between 320 to 350 m s$^{-1}$ accepted)\n\n4.  $v=f\\lambda$\n\n    $\\lambda=\\dfrac{v}{f}=\\dfrac{343}{590}$ / divide the speed by the given frequency $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\lambda=$ :answer[$0.58\\ll\\ \\ce{m}\\gg$] $\\checkmark$ \\\n(allow ECF for use of value obtained from part (c))"},{"ref":"PH0510","marks":"13","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH0510\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\nIn an experiment, a water-filled beaker is positioned on an electronic balance. An immersion heater with a power rating of $P$ is introduced into the water. Once the water begins to boil, a researcher monitored the mass $m$ of the liquid in the beaker over time $t$ once the boiling commenced.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/PH0510-4.png\" alt=\"PH0510-4\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with chemix - https://chemix.org/]}}$\n\nThe theoretically predicted relationship between time $t$ and $m$ is\n\n- $L_v=\\dfrac{Pt}{C-m}$\n\n$C$ is the initial mass of the water when $t=0\\, $s and $L_v$ is the latent heat of vaporisation of water.\n\n$\\\\$\n\n1.   Give the units of $L_v$ in fundamental SI units. [2 marks]\n\n2. In a particular experiment, the researcher uses a heater of power $65.0\\pm0.1\\ $W and measures $m=0.120\\pm0.001\\, $kg, $C=0.125\\pm0.001\\ $kg, and $t=600\\pm1\\ $s.\n\n    1. Calculate the percentage uncertainty in the measured value of $L_v$. [3 marks]\n\n    2. For this experiment, calculate the value of $L_v$ and its absolute uncertainty. [2 marks]\n\n    3. The accepted value of $L_v$ is $2.3 \\times 10^6 \\, $J kg$^{-1}$. Explain why the experimental value does not match the accepted value. [2 marks]\n\n3. The researcher plots a graph to show how *m* varies with *t*.\n\n    1.     Outline why *P* must be kept constant during the experiment. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Assuming the relationship $L_v=\\dfrac{Pt}{C-m}$ is correct, sketch the expected mass-time relationship on the axes below. (There is no need to put numbers to the axes.) [2 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-07%20at%205.20.22%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-07 at 5.20.22 PM\">\n\n    3. Outline how to obtain the value of $L_v$ from the graph you have drawn  in c(ii). [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. $Unit\\ of\\ Power=\\ll\\dfrac{\\ce{kg} \\ \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}\\,\\ce{m}}{\\ce{s}}=\\gg\\ \\ce{kg} \\ \\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-3}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $Unit\\ of\\ L_v=\\dfrac{\\ce{kg} \\ \\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-3}\\,\\ce{s}}{\\ce{kg}}= \\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}$  $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n    - $Power=\\dfrac{W}{t}=\\dfrac{Fs}{t}=\\dfrac{mas}{t}$\n\n    Substituting in the fundamental units for each of the variables gives\n\n    - $\\textrm{Unit of Power}=\\dfrac{\\ce{kg} \\ \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}\\,\\ce{m}}{\\ce{s}}=\\ce{kg} \\ \\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-3}$\n\n    The unit of power is Watts. It is equal to kg m$^2$ s$^{-3}$.\n\n    Both *m* and *C* have fundamental units of kg, and we are multiplying but time which has units of s.\n\n    Therefore\n\n    - $Unit\\ of\\ L_v=\\dfrac{\\ce{kg} \\ \\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-3}\\,\\ce{s}}{\\ce{kg}}=\\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}$\n\n2.\n    1. The percentage uncertainty of $\\left(C-m\\right)= \\ll \\dfrac{\\Delta \\left(C-m \\right)}{\\left(C-m \\right)}\\times 100\\% = \\dfrac{0.002}{0.005} \\times 100\\%=≫40\\%$\n$\\checkmark$\n\n        Add percentage uncertainty $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\ll \\textrm{The percentage uncertainty of } L_v=0.154\\% + 0.167\\% + 40\\% =\\gg $ :answer[40%] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$\\\n        The $C-m$ with its absolute uncertainty;\n\n        $\\left(C-m\\right)\\pm \\Delta \\left(C-m\\right)= \\left(0.125-0.120\\right)\\pm \\left(0.001+0.001\n        \\right)=0.005\\pm0.002 \\ $kg\n\n        The percentage uncertainty of the given quantities:\n\n        - $The\\ percentage\\ uncertainty\\ of\\ Power=\\dfrac{\\Delta P}{P} \\times 100\\%= \\dfrac{0.1}{65.0} \\times 100\\%=0.154\\%$\n\n        - $The\\ percentage\\ uncertainty\\ of\\ time= \\dfrac{\\Delta t}{t} \\times 100\\%= \\dfrac{1}{600} \\times 100\\%=0.167\\%$\n\n        - $The\\ percentage\\ uncertainty\\ of\\ \\left(C-m\\right)=\\dfrac{\\Delta \\left(C-m\\right)}{\\left(C-m\\right)} \\times 100\\%= \\dfrac{0.002}{0.005} \\times 100\\%=40\\%$\n\n        Using the rules of propagation of uncertainties;\n\n        - $The\\ percentage\\ uncertainty\\ of\\ L_v=0.154\\%+0.167\\%+40\\%=40.3\\%$\n\n    2. $L_v \\ = \\ \\ll\\dfrac{65\\times600}{0.125-0.120}=\\dfrac{39000}{0.005}=\\ \\ \\gg$ :answer[$8\\times{10}^6$] $ \\ll$  J kg$^{-1}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\Delta L_v \\ = \\ \\ll \\dfrac{40.3 \\times 7.8 \\times 10^6}{100} = \\ \\gg $ :answer[$3 \\times 10^6$] $ \\ll $ J  kg$^{-1} \\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        *allow ECF for use of calculated $\\%$ uncertainty from (i)*\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$\\\n        When the given values are substituted in the relationship, the value of $L_v$\n\n        - $L_v=\\dfrac{65\\times600}{0.125-0.120}=\\dfrac{39000}{0.005}=7.8\\times{10}^6\\ $J kg$^{-1}$\n\n        The absolute uncertainty $\\Delta L_v$ of  $L_v$:\n\n        - $\\Delta L_v = \\dfrac{40.3 \\times 7.8 \\times 10^{6}}{100} = 3.14 \\times 10^{6}$ J kg$^{-1}$\n\n    3. Heat lost to the environment $\\checkmark$\n\n        Causes the m value in the equation to be too low/means not all P contributed to the vaporisation of water $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$\\\n        Due to environmental losses, a portion of the heat input is not effectively utilized for boiling, resulting in a lower quantity of water actually boiling compared to the ideal scenario. As a consequence, the observed mass ($m$) is higher and the corrected mass ($C-m$) is lower, leading to a calculated latent heat of vaporization ($L_v$) that exceeds the expected value.\n\n        Alternatively, the equation assumes that all the power supplied by the heater is utilized for boiling the water. However, in reality, there are losses, causing the actual power ($P$) to be lower than the assumed value. This leads to an overestimation of the latent heat of vaporization ($L_v$) in the equation, resulting in a higher value than the accepted value.\n\n3.\n    1. To eliminate variables other than *m* and *t*\\\n    **OR** \\\n    It is a control variable\\\n    **OR** \\\n    To isolate the relationship between *m* and *t* $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$\\\n        To investigate a relationship between two quantities, other variables must be kept constant. In this experiment, It is the power *P*.\n\n    2. Straight line $\\checkmark$ \\\n    With a negative gradient $\\checkmark$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-07%20at%205.21.42%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-07 at 5.21.42 PM\">\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$\\\n        When the relationship is rearranged;\n\n        $\\begin{aligned}\n        L_v&=\\dfrac{Pt}{C-m}\\\\\n        CL_v-mL_v&=Pt\\\\\n        CL_v-Pt&=mL_v\\\\\n    m&=-\\dfrac{P}{L_v}t+C\n\\end{aligned}$\n\n        This relationship is similar to\n\n        - $y=mx+c$\n\n        We can see that this should be a straight line with gradient $-\\dfrac{P}{L_v}$ and a vertical axis intercept of $C$.\n\n        Therefore, it represents a straight line with a negative gradient.\n\n    3. ::indent\n        - $L_v=\\dfrac{P}{\\textrm{the slope of the graph}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$\\\n        According to\n\n        - $y=mx+c$\n\n        and\n\n        - $m=-\\dfrac{P}{L_v}t+C$\n\n        Similarly, the slope of the line equals to $-\\dfrac{P}{L_v}$."},{"ref":"PH0511","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH0511\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nIn an experiment, the free-fall of a ball bearing is used to measure the acceleration due to gravity. The ball bearing is suspended with an electromagnet. Then it is released from a height of $h$ above a table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/PH0511_A.png\" alt=\"PH0511_A\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with chemix - https:// chemix.org/]}}$\n\nThe time *t* needed for the ball bearing to reach the ground after release is measured. The experiment is repeated for different values of $h$. The theoretically predicted relationship between time *t* and *h* is\n\n$t^2=\\dfrac{2h}{g}$\n\n1.   State why there is a need to collect data for a range of values of $h$ to verify this relationship. [1 mark]\n\n2.  The experimental data for $t^2$ and *h* are plotted on the graph below.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-16%20at%209.01.00%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-16 at 9.01.00 AM\">\n\n    1. Draw the line of best fit for the plotted data on the graph. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Suggest whether the data is consistent with the theoretical prediction. [2 marks]\n\n    3.     By using the line of best fit, determine a value for *g* and state the units. [4 marks]\n\n3. 1.     Compare the experimental result to the accepted value of *g* and suggest a reason for the discrepancy. [2 marks]\n\n    2. State and explain whether the source of error in (c)(i) is a systematic or random error. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. To prove the relationship between $t^2$ and *h* different values are needed to find a trend\n\n    **OR**\n\n    to reduce random error $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    To prove a relationship, one piece of data is not enough. The relationship can be generalized by using a graph where multiple points can reveal a trend. More data allows for drawing a graph that can support the relationship. Additionally, using more data and taking the average of them reduces the amount of random error.\n\n2.\n    1. A straight line with roughly equal numbers of points above and below the line $\\checkmark$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-16%20at%209.01.10%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-16 at 9.01.10 AM\">\n\n    2. $\\ll$ According to the theory $\\gg$  $t^2$ is proportional to $h$ **OR** ${t^2}\\propto\\ h$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        A best-fit line that is a straight line that passes through the origin can be drawn $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\ll$  Yes $\\gg$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        According to the predicted relationship;\n\n        - $t^2\\propto h$\n\n        This relationship suggests that the graph should be a straight line which passes through the origin. According to plotted data, that kind of straight line can be drawn.\n\n    3. Rearrangement of the formula to solve for $g$\n\n        - $g=2\\dfrac{h}{t^2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Using a correct  data point on the line\n\n        **OR**\n\n        Correct calculation of  the gradient $\\checkmark$\n\n        *g* is between :answer[9.0 and 9.5] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\ce{m s}^{-2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The given formula is\n\n        - $t^2=\\dfrac{2h}{g}$\n\n        The gradient is therefore equal to $\\dfrac{2}{g}$.\n\n        Finding the gradient gives\n\n        - gradient = $\\dfrac{0.19}{0.9}=0.21$\n\n        Solving for $g$:\n\n        - $0.21 = \\dfrac{2}{g}$\n\n        - $g=\\dfrac{2}{0.21}=9.5\\ \\ce{m s}^{-2}$\n\n3.\n    1. Air resistance acts on the object  $\\checkmark$\n\n        This increases the t value / increases the gradient $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The time *t* should be smaller than the measured value under the frictionless environment. According to the equation,\n\n        - $g=2\\bigg(\\dfrac{h}{t^2}\\bigg)$\n\n        Therefore in the absence of air resistance, the experimental value for $\\dfrac{h}{t^2}$ would be greater than the values obtained in this experiment. Therefore, the calculated value is lower than the accepted value.\n\n    2. Systematic $\\checkmark$\n\n        The result of the experiment is always less than the actual value/results are biased in one direction only $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The error is the result of the design of the experiment and leads to the results always being less than the actual value. Therefore, it is systematic."},{"ref":"PH0512","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH0512\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\n1. In an experiment, a beaker containing liquid of mass $m$ is heated with a heater of power *P*. Then the variation of temperature *T* of the liquid with time *t* is recorded.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-16%20at%204.05.24%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-16 at 4.05.24 PM\">\n    $\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with chemix - https:// chemix.org/]}}$\n\n    The data from the experiment is shown on the graph below.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-06-04%20at%205.04.42%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-04 at 5.04.42 PM\">\n\n     1. On the graph, draw the line of best fit for the data. [1 mark]\n\n     2. Explain why it cannot be concluded that the relationship between temperature and time is directly proportional. [1 mark]\n\n2. Theory suggests that\n\n    - $\\dfrac{T-T_0}{t}=k$\n\n    where $T_0$ is the temperature when $t=0$ s.\n\n    1. Using the data given, calculate the percentage uncertainty of $(T-T_0)$ at time 25 s. [3 marks]\n\n    2.     Use the graph to determine constant *k*. [2 marks]\n\n    3. Theory also suggests that another expression for *k* is given by\n\n        - $k=\\dfrac{P}{mc}$,\nwhere *c* is another constant.\n\n        Determine the units of constant *c*, expressing your answer in fundamental SI units. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. A straight line that passes within all the uncertainty bars $\\checkmark$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-06-04%20at%205.05.56%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-04 at 5.05.56 PM\">\n\n    2. The graph does not pass through the origin.\n\n        **OR**\n\n        Celsius cannot be used for temperature proportionality/Kelvin temperature is proportional to average kinetic energy.  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    To have a proportional relationship between two quantities, the graph should be straight and pass through the origin. Even though a straight line can be drawn on the graph, it does not pass through the origin.\n\n        Furthermore, Celsius units for temperature are not directly related to a physical quantity of a substance so a proportional relationship would have little meaning. If the temperature units were in Kelvin, which is representative of the average kinetic energy of the particles of the substance, proportionality would have significant meaning. With conversion to Kelvin, the data in this experiment would not meet the criteria for proportionality.\n\n2.\n    1. $T_0=20\\pm0.5$ and $T=31 \\pm 0.5$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $T-T_o=11 \\pm 1 \\checkmark$\n\n        $\\ll \\dfrac{\\Delta (T-T_0)}{T-T_0} \\times 100\\%= \\dfrac{1}{11} \\times 100\\% = \\gg $ :answer[$9\\% $] $\\checkmark$\n\n    2. The gradient of the graph = $k$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Correct calculation of the gradient of the graph  reference to (a)(i). *Must use a time range of at least 15s in gradient calculation* $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\ll\\ k=\\dfrac{35-20}{34.5-0}=\\dfrac{15}{34.5}=0.43 \\gg$ accept answers in the range from 0.40 to 0.50\n\n    3. $c=\\ll\\dfrac{Pt}{m\\left(T-T_0\\right)}=\\gg \\dfrac{Pt}{m \\Delta T}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\ll\\dfrac{\\ce{kg}\\,\\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-3}\\,\\ce{s}}{\\ce{kg}\\ \\ce{K}}=\\dfrac{\\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}}{\\ce{K}}=\\gg\\ \\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}\\,\\ce{K}^{-1}$\n(*also accept if students find answer using knowledge of specific heat capacity*) $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The following can be written;\n\n        - $\\dfrac{T-T_0}{t}=k=\\dfrac{P}{mc}$\n\n        When the relationship is rearranged to find c;\n\n        - $c=\\dfrac{Pt}{m\\left(T-T_0\\right)}= \\dfrac{Pt}{m \\Delta T}$\n\n        When units are substituted;\n\n        - $Unit\\ of\\ c=\\dfrac{\\ce{W}\\,\\ce{s}}{\\ce{kg}\\,\\ce{K}}$\n\n        Since\n\n        - $Watts=\\ce{kg} \\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-2} \\ \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}=\\ce{kg} \\ \\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-3}$\n\n        - $SI\\ unit\\ of\\ c=\\dfrac{\\ce{kg}\\,\\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-3}\\ce{s}}{\\ce{kg}\\,\\ce{K}}=\\dfrac{\\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}}{\\ce{K}}=\\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}\\,\\ce{K}^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH0513","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH0513\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nA student investigates the refraction of light. A light beam emitted from the ray box is incident on a transparent block.  The ray trace sheet and the position of the ray box and the transparent block are shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/raybox.png\" alt=\"raybox\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with chemix - https:// chemix.org/]}}$\n\n1. The distances *a* and *b* are measured. Then the measurement is repeated for different values for angle $\\theta$. The variation of *b* with *a* is plotted on the graph below according to obtained data.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%2010.53.50%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 10.53.50 AM\">\n\n    Draw the line of best fit for the plotted data on the graph. [1 mark]\n\n2. Theory suggests that\n\n    - $n=\\dfrac{a}{b}$\n\n    Where $n$ is the refractive index of the material and has a constant value.\n\n    1. Suggest whether the data is consistent with the theoretical prediction. [2 marks]\n\n    2. By using the graph calculate *n* with its absolute uncertainty for the data point when *a* is measured to be $3.0 \\,$cm. [4 marks]\n    3. The accepted value for the refractive index of the transparent block is 1.71. Comment on your result. [1 mark]\n\n3. State why using the line of best fit to find the value for $n$ is better than finding $n$ from an individual data point. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. A straight line passes through within the error bars $\\checkmark$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%2010.54.06%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 10.54.06 AM\">\n\n2.\n    1. $\\ll$ According to the theory $\\gg$, *a* is proportional to $b$ / $a\\propto b$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        A best-fit line that is a straight line that passes through the origin and pass through all uncertainty bars can be drawn $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\ll$ therefore, yes, the data agrees with the theory $\\gg$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        According to the predicted relationship;\n\n        $a\\propto\\ b$\n\n        This relationship suggests that the graph should be a straight line which passes through the origin. According to plotted data, that kind of straight line can be drawn.\n\n    2. Correct calculation of percentage uncertainties of *a* and *b* $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\ll \\dfrac{\\Delta a}{a} \\times 100\\% = \\dfrac{0.1}{3.0}\\times 100 \\% = 3.3\\% \\gg$\n\n        - $\\ll \\dfrac{\\Delta b}{b} \\times 100 \\% = \\dfrac{0.1}{1.8} \\times 100 \\% = 5.6 \\% \\gg$\n\n        The percentage error of n = $\\ll 3.3 \\% + 5.6 \\% \\gg = 8.9\\%$\n\n        - $n=\\ll\\dfrac{3.0}{1.8}=1.67=\\gg1.7$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\Delta n = \\ll \\dfrac{3.0}{1.8} \\times 0.089 = 0.148 = 0.15$ (0.1 and 0.2 also accepted here) $\\checkmark$\n\n        Giving\n\n        - $n=1.67\\pm 0.15$ **OR**  $   n =1.7\\pm 0.2 $  **OR**  $n=1.7 \\pm 0.1   $ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Note that precision (decimal places) on n and ∆n must match\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        According to the graph, values of *a* and *b* with their absolute uncertainties;\n\n        - $a=3.0\\pm0.1\\ $cm \\\n$b=1.8\\pm0.1\\ $cm\n\n        Solving for the percentage uncertainties of *a* and *b*:\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta a}{a} \\times 100\\% = \\dfrac{0.1}{3.0}\\times 100 \\% = 3.3\\%$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta b}{b} \\times 100 \\% = \\dfrac{0.1}{1.8} \\times 100 \\% = 5.6 \\%$\n\n        By using the rules of propagation of percentage uncertainties, the percentage uncertainties are added in multiplication or division operations.\n\n        - % $n=3.3\\%+5.6\\%=8.9\\%$\n\n        The value of *n*:\n\n        - $n=\\dfrac{3.0}{1.8} = 1.67$\n\n        - $n=1.67\\pm (1.67\\times 8.9\\%) =1.67\\pm0.148$\n\n        The uncertainty should be expressed to one significant figure, or two significant figures if the first digit is 1 which is the case here. So the best answer is\n\n        - $n=1.67±0.15$\n\n        But $n=1.7±0.1$  **OR** $n=1.7±0.2$ are also accepted here\n\n    3.\n        1.71 is between the upper and lower value of experimental result ($\\checkmark$) so it matches with the actual value/is accurate ($\\checkmark$)\n\n        **OR**\n\n        1.71 is not between the upper and lower value of experimental result so it does not match with the actual value/is inaccurate (ECF from previous parts)\n\n3. Using the gradient takes into account all data points collected $\\checkmark$\n\n    **OR**\n\n    reducing random error/and individual point may be an anomaly in the data. $\\checkmark$\n\n    reducing random error $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    To prove a relationship, one piece of data is not enough. The relationship can be generalized by using a graph. More data allows for drawing a graph that can support the relationship. Additionally, using more data and taking the average of them reduces the amount of random error."},{"ref":"PH0514","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH0514\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\n1. A student investigates the variation of gas pressure with volume at a constant temperature. Air is trapped inside a gas syringe with a constant cross-sectional area of $A$. The plunger of the gas syringe is tied to a mass holder. The student measures the volume $V$ of the trapped gas for different hanging masses with the external constant air pressure, $P_A$, remaining constant.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%201.17.05%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 1.17.05 PM\">\n    $\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with chemix - https:// chemix.org/]}}$\n\n    1.     After each increase in mass, the student waits for some time to measure the volume of the gas. Outline why this is necessary.  [1 mark]\n\n        The recorded data is plotted on a graph:\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202026-02-23%20at%207.16.06%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-02-23 at 7.16.06 AM\">\n\n    2. Draw the line of best fit for the plotted data. [1 mark]\n\n    3.     Determine the gradient of the graph. [2 marks]\n\n2. Theory suggests that\n\n    - $(P_A-\\dfrac{mg}{A})V=k$;\n\n    where *m* is the total mass of the plunger and hanging masses, *g* is the acceleration of free fall and *k* is a constant.\n\n    1.     Determine the units of *k* in fundamental SI units. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Calculate the percentage uncertainty of the volume *V* when the mass *m* is 700 g. [2 marks]\n\n3. In a particular experiment, the researcher measures\n\n    - $m=0.700\\pm0.005\\ \\ce{kg}$, \\\n $A=0.00010\\pm0.00001{\\ \\ce{m}}^2$ and\\\n $g=9.81\\pm0.01\\ \\ce{m s}^{-2}$.\n\n Calculate the absolute uncertainty of $\\dfrac{mg}{A}$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. To keep the temperature constant\n\n        **OR**\n\n        To allow the system to return to thermal equilibrium $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Temperature is one of the control variables in the experiment. It must be kept constant. During the motion of the plunger, the gas temperature will decrease when the gas expands. Therefore, time is needed for the gas to reach room temperature again.\n\n    2. A straight line passes through within the error bars and has roughly equal numbers of data points on each side $\\checkmark$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202026-02-23%20at%207.14.31%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-02-23 at 7.14.31 AM\">\n\n    3. Calculation of gradient using a minimum mass range of 400 g. $\\checkmark$\n\n        Calculated gradient between $1.8\\times{10}^{-3}{\\ \\ce{cm}}^3\\,\\ce{g}^{-1}$ and $0.7{\\times10}^{-3}{\\ \\ce{cm}}^3\\ce{g}^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$ (units must be included for MP2)\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Gradient of the graph = $\\dfrac{2.9-2.0}{700-0}=1.29\\times{10}^{-3}{\\, \\ce{cm}}^3\\,\\ce{g}^{-1}$\n\n2.\n    1. The unit of $P_A=\\ll\\dfrac{\\ce{kg m s}^{-2}}{\\ce{m}^2}=\\gg\\ \\ce{kg}\\,\\ce{m}^{-1}\\ce{s}^{-2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        The fundamental SI units of $k=  \\ll\\ (\\ce{kg}\\,\\ce{m}^{-1}\\ce{s}^{-2})(\\ce{m}^3) \\gg =\\ \\ce{kg}\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}\\ce{m}^2$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The unit of atmospheric pressure $P_A$ is Pascals. The formula for pressure is\n\n        - $P = \\dfrac{F}{A}$ and also $F=ma$.\n\n        Pascals can then be expressed in SI fundamental units as follows;\n\n        - The unit of $P_A=\\dfrac{\\ce{kg}\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}}{\\ce{m}^2}=\\ce{kg}\\,\\ce{m}^{-1}\\ce{s}^{-2}$\n\n        - The unit of $\\dfrac{mg}{A}$  is the same as the unit of $P_A$\n\n        SI fundamental units of $k$ is therefore pressure units multiplied by volume units:\n\n        - $=\\ (\\ce{kg}\\,\\ce{m}^{-1}\\ce{s}^{-2})(\\ce{m}^3)  =\\ \\ce{kg}\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}\\ce{m}^2$\n\n    2. ::indent\n        - $V=2.9\\pm0.2\\ {\\ce{cm}}^3$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta V}{V} \\times 100 \\% = \\dfrac{0.2}{2.9} \\times 100\\% = $ :answer[$6.9\\%$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nThe volume of the trapped gas  *V* when $m=700$ g;\n\n        - $V=2.9\\pm0.2\\ \\ce{cm}^3$\n\n        Therefore, percentage uncertainty;\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta V}{V} \\times 100 \\% = \\dfrac{0.2}{2.9} \\times 100\\% = 6.9\\%$\n\n3. Correct calculation of individual percentage uncertainties $\\checkmark$\n\n    - for mass = $\\dfrac{\\Delta m}{m} \\times 100 \\% = \\dfrac{0.005}{0.700} \\times 100 \\% = 0.7\\%$\n\n    - for area = $\\dfrac{\\Delta A}{A} \\times 100 \\% = \\dfrac{0.00001}{0.00010} \\times 100 \\% = 10\\%$\n\n    - for gravity = $\\dfrac{\\Delta g}{g} \\times 100 \\% = \\dfrac{0.01}{9.81} \\times 100\\% = 0.1\\%$\n\n    The percentage uncertainty of $\\dfrac{mg}{A} = \\ \\ll \\dfrac{\\Delta m}{m}  + \\dfrac{\\Delta g}{g}  + \\dfrac{\\Delta A}{A}  = 0.7\\% + 0.1\\% + 10\\% \\gg \\ = 10.8\\%$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $\\dfrac{mg}{A}=\\dfrac{(0.700)(9.81)}{0.00010}=68670\\ $Pa\n\n    - $\\Delta (\\dfrac{mg}{A}) = \\dfrac{(0.700)(9.81)}{0.00010} \\times \\dfrac{10.8}{100} = 7416 = $ :answer[$7000 \\ \\ce{Pa}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n    - For $\\dfrac{mg}{A}$ with its absolute uncertainty:\n\n    - $\\dfrac{mg}{A} \\pm \\Delta(\\dfrac{mg}{A}) = \\dfrac{(0.700)(9.81)}{0.00010} \\pm \\dfrac{(0.700)(9.81)}{0.00010} \\times \\dfrac{10.8}{100} = 68670 \\pm 7416$\n\n    With correct rounding of the uncertainty to 1 significant figure and matching precision in the calculated value, it would be $69000 \\pm 7000$ Pa"},{"ref":"PH0515","marks":"11","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH0515\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\n1. A student investigates standing waves in strings. A string is connected to an oscillator where a function generator can adjust the frequency. The other end of the string passes through a pulley and supports a mass.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/PH0515aaa.png\" alt=\"PH0515aaa\">\n    $\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with chemix - https ://chemix.org/]}}$\n\n    The student varied the mass $m$ and recorded the values for frequency $f$ for which the first harmonic was visible on the string. The following graph shows the recorded data.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%2011.23.36%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 11.23.36 AM\">\n\n    The uncertainty of the mass is negligible.\n\n    1. Draw the line of best fit for the plotted data on the graph. [1 mark]\n\n    2. By referring to the plotted data, explain why it cannot be stated that frequency is directly proportional to mass.     [1 mark]\n\n    3. Use the graph to determine the value for $f$ with its absolute uncertainty for $m=1.0\\ $kg [2 marks]\n\n2. The theory suggests that\n\n    $f^2=km$\n\n    where *k* is a constant.\n\n    The variation of $f^2$ with *m* is seen on the graph below.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%2011.31.35%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 11.31.35 AM\">\n\n    1.     Show that the absolute uncertainty of $f^2$ is $\\pm0.01\\ $kHz$^2$ for $m=1.0\\ $kg. [3 marks]\n\n    2. The absolute uncertainty of $f^2$ is $0.03\\ $kHz$^2$ for $m=0.25\\ $kg. Construct error bars for $f^2$ when  $m=0.25\\ $kg and $m=1.00\\ $kg. [1 mark]\n\n    3. State and explain whether the theory is supported by data or not. [2 marks]\n\n3. Determine the unit of *k*, in fundamental SI units. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. A curved line that passes through the uncertainty bars $\\checkmark$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%2011.25.54%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 11.25.54 AM\">\n\n    2. A straight line cannot be drawn\n\n        **OR**\n\n        The line does not pass through the origin $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        To represent direct proportionality in graphs, a straight line passes through the origin is drawn. According to the data, it is not possible.\n\n    3. :answer[530] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\pm10 \\ll \\ce{Hz} \\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        According to the graph, each small square in the y-axis increases the value of frequency by 20 Hz. Since the error bars are drawn as the length of one square, the absolute uncertainty is half of 20 Hz.\n\n2.\n    1. Percentage uncertainty of $f = \\dfrac{\\Delta f}{f} \\times 100\\% = \\dfrac{10}{530} \\times 100 \\% = 1.9\\%$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Percentage uncertainty of $f^2 = \\dfrac{\\Delta (f^2)}{f^2} \\times 100 \\% = 2 \\times \\dfrac{\\Delta f}{f} \\times 100 \\% = 2 \\times 1.9\\% = 3.8\\%$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        The absolute uncertainty of$\\ f^2$=$f^2\\times3.8\\%={530}^2\\times3.8\\%=10674\\,\\ce{Hz}^2=0.01\\ \\textrm{kHz}^2$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The percentage uncertainty of *f* for $m=1.0\\ $kg;\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta f}{f} \\times 100\\% = \\dfrac{10}{530} \\times 100\\% = 1.9\\%$\n\n        By using the rules of propagation of uncertainties;\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta (f^2)}{f^2}\\times 100\\% = 2 \\times \\dfrac{\\Delta f}{f} \\times 100\\% = 2 \\times 1.9\\% = 3.8\\%$\n\n        The absolute uncertainty of $f^2$;\n\n        - $\\Delta (f^2) = f^2 \\times 3.8\\% = 530^2 \\times 3.8\\% = 0.01 \\ \\textrm{kHz}^2$\n\n    2. Error bar added to first point as $\\pm3$ square and to the fourth point as $\\pm1$ square $\\checkmark$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%2011.31.51%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 11.31.51 AM\">\n\n    3. The theory suggests a direct proportionality between $f^2$ and *m* $\\checkmark$\n\n        A straight line that passes within the uncertainty bars and through the origin can be drawn. Therefore it matches the theory.\n\n        **OR**\n\n        A straight line that passes within the uncertainty bars and through the origin cannot be drawn. Therefore it does not match the theory. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Theory suggests a direct proportionality between $f^2$ and *m*. According to the data and error bars of two points, a straight line that passes through the origin can be drawn. Therefore, the theory is supported.\n\n3. SI unit of $k=\\ll \\dfrac{{\\textrm{kHz}}^2}{\\ce{kg}}=\\ \\dfrac{{(\\ce{s}^{-1})}^2}{\\ce{kg}}=\\gg \\ce{s}^{-2}\\,{\\ce{kg}}^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    When the equation is rearranged:\n\n    - $k=\\dfrac{f^2}{m}$\n\n    Substitute SI units of the quantities:\n\n    SI unit of $k=\\dfrac{{(\\ce{s}^{-1}})^2}{\\ce{kg}}=\\ce{s}^{-2}\\,{\\ce{kg}}^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH0519","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH0519\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\n1. The circuit shown is used to measure the variation of the resistance *R* of a wire with the length *L* of the wire. The wire is connected across points $X$ and $Y$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%204.41.11%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 4.41.11 PM\">\n\n    A voltmeter and an ammeter are connected to the circuit in order to investigate the relationship.\n\n    1. Draw the ammeter and voltmeter on the given circuit with their correct connections. [2 marks]\n\n    2. State the purpose of connecting a resistor in series with the wire as shown in the diagram. [1 mark]\n\n2. The diameter *d* of the wire is kept constant at $d=0.58\\pm0.01 \\, $mm. Determine the fractional uncertainty of the cross-sectional area *A* of the wire. [2 marks]\n\n3. The graph shows the variation of resistance of the wire *R* with the wire\\'s length *L*.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%204.28.53%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 4.28.53 PM\">\n\n    1. Draw the line of best fit. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Use the graph to estimate the resistivity of the wire. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. Correct symbol of ammeter connected in series with the wire. $\\checkmark$\n\n        Correct symbol of voltmeter connected parallel to the wire. $\\checkmark$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%204.41.15%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 4.41.15 PM\">\n\n    2. To decrease power dissipated in the wires **OR** to prevent heating of the wire **OR** to reduce current in the wire. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    When a resistor is connected in series with the wire, it increases the overall resistance of the circuit and thus decreases the current. A lower current decreases the power dissipated in the wire as seen in the relationship $P=I^2R$, thus preventing the wire from heating up, which may affect the value of its resistance.\n\n2. Percentage uncertainty of $A=2\\bigg(\\dfrac{0.01}{0.58}\\bigg)(100 \\%)$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\hspace{4.4cm}=$:answer[$0.0344$] OR :answer[$3.44\\%$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n    - $A=\\pi\\ (r^2)=\\pi{(\\dfrac{d}{2})}^2$\n\n    When values with absolute uncertainty are multiplied, the sum of the relative uncertainties is used to find the new uncertainty. In this case, the relative uncertainty of the area is equal to twice the relative uncertainty on the diameter (which is also equal to twice the relative uncertainty of the radius)\n\n    - $A=2(\\dfrac{0.01}{0.58})(100)=0.0344 \\ll=3.44\\% \\gg$\n\n3.\n    1. Straight line passing within all uncertainty bars $\\checkmark$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%204.29.06%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 4.29.06 PM\">\n\n    2.  Evidence of calculating the gradient. $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\rho=\\dfrac{\\left(8.8-1.4\\right)(\\pi)\\left(\\frac{0.58\\times{10}^{-3}}{2}\\right)^2}{\\left(60-10\\right)\\times{10}^{-2}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\rho=$ :answer[$3.9\\times{10}^{-6}\\ll \\Omega\\, $m$ \\gg $] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Since $\\rho=\\dfrac{RA}{L}$\n\n        Then; the gradient of the graph is\ngradient $=\\ \\dfrac{\\rho}{A}$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\rho}{A}=\\dfrac{8.8-1.4}{\\left(60-10\\right)\\times{10}^{-2}}=14.8$\n\n        Solving for $\\rho$ while converting diameter to radius by dividing by two gives\n\n        - $\\rho=14.8(\\pi r^2)=14.8\\pi{\\bigg(\\dfrac{0.58\\times{10}^{-3}}{2}\\bigg)}^2=3.9\\times{10}^{-6}\\, \\Omega \\,$m"},{"ref":"PH0520","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH0520\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe circuit shown is used to measure the internal resistance of a cell. The voltmeter and the ammeter used are ideal.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202026-08-22%20at%208.12.45%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-08-22 at 8.12.45 AM\">\n\n1.\n    1. Differentiate between precise and accurate measurements. [2 marks]\n\n    2. The voltmeter used has a zero error. State the type of uncertainty this will introduce into the voltmeter\\'s measurements. [1 mark]\n\n2.\n    1. The graph shows the variation of the voltmeter\\'s reading $V$ with current $I$.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-06-04%20at%204.31.31%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-04 at 4.31.31 PM\">\n\n        Determine the emf of the cell. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Estimate the internal resistance of the cell including its uncertainty. [3 marks]\n\n    3. Determine whether the zero error in the voltmeter will affect the calculated value of the internal resistance. Justify your answer. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        In precise measurements the random uncertainty is small/the measurements are close to each other/there are many significant figures on the measurements. $\\checkmark$\n\n        Accurate measurements are close to the actual values. $\\checkmark$\n\n    2. Systematic uncertainty. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nA systematic uncertainty affects all recorded points in the same way. The zero error will cause all points to be either too high or too low by the same amount.\n\n2.\n    1. Evidence that emf is the y-intercept. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\varepsilon= $ :answer[$8.9\\ to\\ 9.1$] $\\ll\\ \\ce{V}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-06-04%20at%204.35.14%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-04 at 4.35.14 PM\">\n\n        The formula for the terminal voltage of a cell is given by\n\n        - $V_T = \\varepsilon - ir$\n\n        Thus the line of best fit intercepts the y-axis at the emf.\n\n    2. Gradient=$-r$/evidence of calculating the gradient $\\checkmark$\n\n        $r= $ :answer[$1.0\\,\\Omega$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        uncertainty = :answer[$\\pm\\,0.1\\ll \\Omega\\gg$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        The gradient of this graph is $-r$\n\n        gradient$=\\ \\dfrac{6.75-9}{2.25-0}=-1$\n\n        Then\n\n        - $r=1.0\\, \\Omega$\n\n        To determine the uncertainty in the gradient, we can the line of best fit with the maximum gradient possible with use of the uncertainty bars.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-16%20at%209.08.42%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-16 at 9.08.42 AM\">\n\n        The uncertainty in gradient is:\n\n        - $\\Delta$ gradient  =best  fit  gradient - worst  fit   gradient\n\n        - worst fit gradient=$\\dfrac{6.65-9.2}{2.25-0}=-1.13$\n\n        - $\\Delta$ gradient  $= -1 -(-1.13) = 0.1 \\Omega$\n\n    3. Not affected $\\checkmark$\n\n        Error will cancel out in calculations/gradient remains the same $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        The zero error will raise or lower all the measured values by the same amount, but will not affect the gradient.\n\n        The internal resistance is calculated from the gradient of the graph of voltage variation with current.\n\n        In these calculations, the raised values will cancel out, which means that the value of the internal resistance of the cell will not be affected.\n\n        Alternatively, the systematic error will shift the plotted points upwards or downwards, but will not affect the gradient."},{"ref":"PH0521","marks":"11","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH0521\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nA student investigates gas behaviour. A volume of gas is trapped in a capillary tube by a thin layer of oil. The tube, a thermometer and a ruler are immersed in water.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202026-08-20%20at%2010.06.30%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-08-20 at 10.06.30 AM\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with chemix - https:// chemix.org]}}$\n\nThe student slowly added boiling water and recorded the values of the temperature *T* of the water and the height *h* of the gas trapped inside the capillary tube. The recorded data is plotted on a graph as shown.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%204.03.23%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 4.03.23 PM\">\n\n1. Draw the best fit line for the plotted data. [1 mark]\n\n2. The theory suggests that\n\n    - $h=mT+C$\n\n    where *m* and *C* are constant. Temperature *T* is in $\\degree$C.\n\n    1. Suggest whether the data are consistent with the theory. [2 marks]\n\n    2. By using the graph drawn in (a), determine the constants *m* and *C*. [4 marks]\n\n    3. State the units of the gradient in fundamental SI units. [1 mark]\n\n3. Following the experiment, the student tested their thermometer in an ice bath and in boiling water. The temperatures were 2$\\degree$C and 102$\\degree$C respectively.\n\n    1. Explain how this finding will affect how the student interprets their results. [2 marks]\n\n    2. The student also goes on to find the intercept on the temperature axis. State whether this error causes their intercept value to be higher or lower than expected. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. A straight line passes through the error bars $\\checkmark$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%204.03.38%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 4.03.38 PM\">\\\n\n2.\n    1. The relationship $h = mT + C$ suggests a straight line on the graph $\\checkmark$\n\n        A straight line can be drawn that passes through all of the uncertainty bars (therefore consistent) $\\checkmark$\n\n    2. Any evidence of calculation of gradient $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $m=\\ll\\dfrac{11.80\\ -10.64\\ }{95\\ -60}=\\gg 0.033 \\ll$ cm C$^{-1}$  $\\gg \\checkmark$\\\n        (answers between 0.030 and 0.036 accepted)\n\n        Any evidence of substitution of correct data in the equation $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $C=8.65 \\ll $C $\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\\\n        (answers between 8.60 and 8.70 accepted)\n\n         $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The equation suggests that *m* is the gradient of the graph and *C* is the y-intercept.\n\n        $m=gradient\\ of\\ the\\ graph=\\dfrac{11.80\\ -10.64\\ }{95\\ -60}=0.033$\n\n        When any data is substituted in the equation with the value of *m*;\n\n        - For $h = 10.8$ and $T = 65$;\n\n        - $10.8=0.033\\times65+C$\n\n        - $C=10.8-0.033\\times65=8.65$\n\n    3. SI unit of  $\\ce{m} \\ = \\ \\ll \\dfrac{\\ce{m}}{\\degree \\ce{C}}\\ \\gg = \\ce{m}{ \\ \\ce{K}^{-1}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    When the equation is rearranged;\n\n        $m=\\dfrac{h-C}{T}$\n\n        Substitute SI units of the quantities;\n\n        SI unit of $m=\\dfrac{m}{\\degree C}=\\ce{mK}^{-1}$\n\n3.\n    1. There is a systematic error ($\\checkmark$) where all readings are 2$\\degree$C too high ($\\checkmark$).\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The ice water reading should be 0$\\degree$C and the boiling water 100$\\degree$C. This indicated that the thermometer has a zero error of 2$\\degree$C. As all data points are likely affected by this fixed amount, it is a systematic error.\n\n    2. As all points have been shifted to the right, the temperature intercept reading will be higher than expected. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The graph could be corrected by shifting data points to the left. This will shift the line of best fit to the left, decreasing the value for the temperature intercept."},{"ref":"PH0567","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0567\n\nElectrons are accelerated between two conducting plates that have a potential difference of 100 V. Estimate the total energy that would be delivered by the conducting plates to ${10}^{21}$ electrons.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}      {10}^5\\ $J\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}      {10}^4\\ $J\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}      {10}^3\\ $J\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}      {10}^2\\ $J","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe energy $E$ is given to each electron in J:\n\n- $E=q\\Delta V=(1.6 \\times 10^{-19})(100)=1.6 \\times10^{-17}\\,$J\n\nThe total energy $E_T$ is given to the electrons in J:\n\n- $E_T=({10}^{21})(1.6\\times{10}^{-17})$\n\n- $E_T=1.6\\times{10}^4\\,$ J"},{"ref":"PH0578","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH0578\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nIn an experiment to determine the speed of sound in air, some students used a number of tuning forks of known frequencies and a tube with a moveable piston arranged as shown below.\n\nFor each tuning fork they used the setup shown in the diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-16%20at%204.11.10%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-16 at 4.11.10 PM\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with chemix - https:// chemix.org/]}}$\n\nThe students would sound the tuning fork, then move the piston slowly downwards until a loud sound is first heard. The length $L$ was then measured. This was repeated for different tuning forks.\n\nThe graph shows the variation of $L^{-1}$ with frequency $f$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202026-02-18%20at%209.03.22%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-02-18 at 9.03.22 AM\">\n\n1. Draw the line of best fit. [1 mark]\n\n2. Calculate the fractional uncertainty of $L^{-1}$ when the length is $20\\, $cm. [2 marks]\n\n3.\n    1. Determine the gradient, expressing your answer in fundamental SI units. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Use the gradient calculated in (i) to determine the speed of sound. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. A straight line passing within all error bars $\\checkmark$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202026-02-18%20at%209.04.13%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-02-18 at 9.04.13 AM\">\n\n2. $\\dfrac{1}{L}=0.05\\ll \\ce{cm}^{-1}\\gg$, uncertainty is $0.001\\ \\ce{cm}^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    Fractional uncertainty = :answer[0.02] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    From the graph, the uncertainty on the 0.05 is 0.001.\n\n    The fractional uncertainty is therefore $\\dfrac{0.001}{0.05}=0.02\\ or\\ 2\\%$\n\n3.\n    1. Gradient = $\\left(\\dfrac{480-180}{5.8-2.2}\\right)=$ :answer[83.33] $\\checkmark$. (Accept answers from 82.5 to 86.25)\n\n       $\\ce{m s}^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n       $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n       Note that over half of the line should be used to find the gradient.\n\n       The values on the horizontal axis and therefore the denominator are $\\times 10^{-2}\\, \\ce{cm}^{-1}$. Within the gradient calculation, this becomes $\\times 10^2\\,\\ce{cm}$ which is equvalent to m.\n\n       The values on the vertical axis are Hz, which are equivalent to s$^{-1}$.\n\n       Combined, this leaves the units in m s$^{-1}$.\n\n    2. $\\ll\\ v=4(gradient)\\gg$\n\n        $v=\\left(4\\right)\\left(83.33\\right)$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        v= :answer[333] $\\ll\\ \\ce{m s}^{-1}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\\\n        *Accept answers from 330 to 345*\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        This model is similar to a pipe that is open at one end and closed at the other.\n\n        The frequency in such a pipe is given by\n        $f=\\dfrac{nv}{4L}$\n\n        The first loud sound is heard at the first harmonics at $n=1$\n\n        Then the gradient for this graph is\n\n        $\\begin{aligned}\n        gradient &= \\dfrac{v}{4}\\\\\n        v &=4\\left(gradient\\right)\\\\\n        v &=\\left(4\\right)\\left(83.33\\right)\\\\\n        &=333.32\\, \\ce{m s}^{-1}\\\\\n        \\end{aligned}$"},{"ref":"PH0585","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0585\n\nA 1000 kg car is moving with a kinetic energy of ${10}^5$ J.\n\nEstimate the order of magnitude of its momentum.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}      {10}^3$ kg m s$^{-1}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}      {10}^4$ kg m s$^{-1}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}      {10}^5$ kg m s$^{-1}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}      {10}^6$ kg m s$^{-1}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe relationship between kinetic energy $E_k$ and momentum $p$ of the car is given by the following formula:\n\n- $E_k=\\dfrac{p^2}{2m}$\n\n- $p^2=2mE_k$\n\nUsing the mass of the car as ${10}^3\\, $kg.\n\n- $p^2\\approx 2\\left({10}^3\\right)\\left({10}^5\\right)$\n\nBecause we are only looking for the order of magnitude, we can disregard the 2\n\n- $p^2\\approx{10}^8 $\n\n- $p\\approx{10}^4$"},{"ref":"PH1100","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH1100\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nIn an experiment to investigate Newton's 2nd Law, a student attaches a trolley of mass $M$ to a string that passes over a pulley and is attached to a hanging mass $m$ as shown. The student measures the time taken for the cart to travel between two marks on the table when accelerating from rest.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/PH1100aaaa.png\" alt=\"PH1100aaaa\">\n\nFor one of the trials, the student measured the time taken to be $1.2 ± 0.2\\, $s. The distance between the markings was $1.50 ± 0.03\\, $m. The acceleration can be found from the equation $a=\\dfrac{2s}{t^2}$ .\n\n1. 1. Determine the experimental value for the acceleration, with uncertainty.  [4 marks]\n\n   2. The student increases the distance between the two marks on the table and repeats the experiment. Explain the effect of this change on the uncertainty on the calculated acceleration.    [2 marks]\n\n2.  The student uses the equation\n\n    - $a=\\dfrac{mg}{(M+m)}$\n\n    to predict the acceleration of the trolley. Give two reasons why the experimental value will likely be lower than the result from the calculation based on the masses.  [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1. ::indent\n        - $a=\\dfrac{2(1.5)}{(1.2)^2} =2.08\\, \\ce{m\\, s^{-2}}$    $\\checkmark$\n\n       Relative uncertainty = $\\dfrac{.03}{1.5}+2 \\dfrac{0.2}{1.2}=0.35=35\\% $  $\\checkmark$\n\n       Uncertainty = $(2.08)(0.35)= 0.72$  $\\checkmark$\n\n       $a= $:answer[$(2.1± 0.7) \\, $m s$^{-2}$] $\\hspace{2em}$    OR   $\\hspace{2em} $:answer[$(2.08 ±  0.72) \\, $m s$^{-2}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n       $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n       Substituting the measured values into the given equation\n\n       - $a=\\dfrac{2s}{t^2} =2\\dfrac{1.5}{(1.2)^2}\\ =2.08\\, $m s$^{-2}$\n\n       To find the uncertainty, we will need to add the relative/percentage uncertainties. Because the time is squared, this percentage uncertainty is added twice.\n\n       - Relative uncertainty = $\\dfrac{.03}{1.5}+2 \\left(\\dfrac{0.2}{1.2}\\right)=0.35=35\\% $\n\n       The uncertainty on acceleration is found by multiplying the experimental value by the calculated relative uncertainty.\n\n       Uncertainty = $(2.08)(0.35)= 0.72  $\n\n        For the final statement, the number of decimal places on the acceleration and that on the uncertainty should match.\n\n       - $a=(2.1\\pm 0.7) \\,$m s$^{-2}\\ \\ \\ \\ OR\\ \\ \\ (2.08 \\pm 0.72)\\,$m s$^{-2} $\n\n    2.  Uncertainty on the time and distance measurements are unchanged but the distance and time values increase.   $\\checkmark$\n\n        Relative uncertainties decrease, therefore the uncertainty on acceleration will decrease.   $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nAlthough the marks are moved further apart, we assume that the same measuring technique is used and so the uncertainty on the distance measurement will remain $± 0.03$ m. Similarly, the uncertainty on the time will likely remain $± 0.2$ s. The fractional uncertainties for both will therefore decrease, leading to a decrease in the fractional uncertainty on the acceleration. These changes should not affect the acceleration value itself, therefore the absolute uncertainty on the acceleration will also decrease.\n\n2.  friction/air resistance. $\\checkmark$\n\n    energy goes into the rotation of the pulley/trolley wheels. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Friction will oppose the motion of the cart, reducing the net force and therefore the acceleration. Friction will also exist in the pulley\n\n    The pulley has mass and rotates at an increasing rate as the cart accelerates. This is also true for the wheels on the cart. This takes some energy from the system that would normally go into the kinetic energy of the cart."},{"ref":"PH1101","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH1101\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nIn an experiment the volume occupied by a fixed mass of an ideal gas is varied. The temperature of the gas is kept constant at 293 K throughout the experiment. The pressure of the gas $P$ is measured for five different volumes $V$. Below shows a graph of $P$ against $V^{-1}$. The uncertainty in $V^{-1}$ is much less than the uncertainty in P for all of the data points.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-06-04%20at%204.38.59%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-04 at 4.38.59 PM\">\n\n1.     Determine the relative uncertainty on pressure when $\\dfrac{1}{V} $ is equal to $5.0 \\times 10^2\\ \\ce{m}^{-3}$.   [2 marks]\n\n2.  1. Draw the line of best fit on the graph.    [1 mark]\n\n    2. Use your line of best fit to estimate the number of moles of gas used in the experiment                     [3 marks]\n\n    3.  It is suggested that $P$ and $\\dfrac{1}{V}$ are directly proportional. Explain why the data does not support this hypothesis.                                                [1 mark]\n\n    4.  With reference to the graph, suggest, with a reason, whether a systematic or random error is responsible for the hypothesis not being supported.         [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Absolute uncertainty +-0.2 x 105. $\\checkmark$\n\n    Relative uncertainty $=\\dfrac{0.2}{2.6}=0.08=8\\%.$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Reading from the graph, the uncertainty for the pressure is $0.2\\times{10}^5$ Pa\n\n    The pressure at this point is $2.6\\times{10}^5$ Pa\n\n    Relative uncertainty $=\\dfrac{\\Delta P}{P}=\\dfrac{0.2\\times{10}^5}{2.6\\times{10}^5}=0.077=7.8\\%$\n\n2. 1. Straight line that passes through all uncertainty bars $\\checkmark$\n\n      <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-16%20at%209.35.59%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-16 at 9.35.59 AM\">\n\n    2. gradient $=nRT$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        Gradient of line of best fit $= 440\\ (\\ce{J})$ Allow values in range 420-460  (J). $\\checkmark$\n\n        Number of moles = $\\dfrac{gradient}{RT}=\\dfrac{440}{(8.31)(293)}=0.18\\, \\ce{mol}$ (Allow answers in range 0.17-0.19). $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n        - $PV=nRT$\n\n        Rearranging this equation, we can see\n\n        - $P=nRT\\left(\\dfrac{1}{V}\\right)$\n\n        Comparing this to $y=mx\\ (+c)$ tells us that gradient $=nRT $\n\n        Finding the gradient of the line of best fit.\n\n        - Gradient$=\\dfrac{difference\\ in\\ P}{difference\\ in\\ V^{-1} }=\\dfrac{1.1\\times{10}^5}{2.5\\times{10}^2}=440\\, (\\ce{J})\\ $\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Scientific%20Skills%20%26%20Tools/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-04-16%20at%203.43.33%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-16 at 3.43.33 PM\">\n\n        - $n=\\dfrac{gradient}{RT}=\\dfrac{440}{\\left(8.31\\right)\\left(293\\right)}=0.18\\ $moles\n\n        (Note $R$ is the molar gas constant $=8.31\\, $J K$^{-1}\\, \\textrm{mol}^{-1}$ - this value is given in your data booklet)\n\n    3.   It is not possible to draw a straight line from the origin that passes within all uncertainty bars.  $\\checkmark$\n\n         $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n         The conditions for two variables to be directly proportional are that the graph shows a straight line that passes through the origin. For the given data and uncertainty bars, a straight line can be drawn; however, it crosses the pressure axis above the origin.\n\n    4.  Systematic error.   $\\checkmark$\n\n        All points have been shifted by the same amount.  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The points on the graph show a good amount of linearity, which implies that there is minimal random error.\n\n        Because the line of best fit passes above the origin, it suggests that all pressure measurements are a little higher than the actual amounts, or all $\\dfrac{1}{V}$ measurements are lower than they should be (or all V measurements are higher than the actual amount)"},{"ref":"PH1832","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH1832\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe speed of sound $v$ in a gas is hypothesized to depend on the pressure $P$ and the density of the gas $\\rho$ according to\n\n- $v=kP^a\\rho^b$\n\nwhere $k$ is a dimensionless constant.\n\n1. Determine the values of $a$ and $b$. [3 marks]\n\nA student wishes to test this hypothesis by measuring the speed\n$v$ of sound in a gas at different pressures and maintaining the density $\\rho$ of the gas constant.\n\n2. Identify the independent and dependent variables [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Expressing speed, pressure and density as:\n\n    - $[v]=$ L T$^{-1}$ (or m s$^{-1}$)\n\n    - $[P]=$ F A$^{-1}$ (or kg m$^{-1}$ s$^{-2}$)\n\n    - $[\\rho]=$ M L$^{-3}$ (or kg m$^{-3}$)\n\n    Any 2 of these three magnitudes correctly expressed in terms of the correct dimensions (or fundamental units) $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $a=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $b=-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    According to $v=kP^a\\rho^b$, we start by expressing the dimensional forms of $v$, $P$, and $\\rho$ in terms of dimensions mass $M$ (mass), $L$ (length), and $T$ (time):\n\n    - $[v]=$ L T$^{-1}$ (speed is distance/time)\n\n    - $[P]=$ F A$^{-1}=$ (M L T$^{-2}$)(L$^{-2}$) = M L$^{-1}$ T$^{-2}$ (pressure is force/area))\n\n    - $[\\rho]=$ M L$^{-3}$ (density is mass/volume)\n\n    We can now substitute the dimensions of each magnitude into the equation:\n\n    - L T$^{-1}$ = (M L$^{-1}$ T$^{-2})^a$(M L$^{-3})^b$\n\n    By expanding the exponents we get:\n\n    - L T$^{-1}$ = M$^a$ L$^{-a}$ T$^{-2a}$ M$^b$ L$^{-3b}$\n\n    - L T$^{-1}$ = M$^{a+b}$ L$^{-a-3b}$ T$^{-2a}$\n\n    Since both sides must have the same dimensions for mass $M$ (mass), $L$ (length), and $T$ (time) we can setup equation by comparing exponents:\n\n    - For mass: $a+b=0$\n    - For length: $-a-3b=1$\n    - For time: $-2a=-1$\n\n    From the third equation $a=\\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n\n    Then by substituting this value in the first equation we get $b=-\\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n\n    Thus, $v=kP^{1/2}\\rho^{-1/2}$, which can be written as:\n\n    - $v=k\\sqrt{\\dfrac{P}{\\rho}}$\n\n    This is consistent with the Newton-Laplace equation for the speed of sound in an ideal gas.\n\n2. 1. IV: Pressure / $P$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        DV: Speed of sound in the gas / $v$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Pressure is the variable that would be adjusted independently and its effect of the speed would be studied.\n\n        The speed of sound $v$ is the dependent variable given that its value is assumed to depend on the pressure.\n\n        In this case the density of the gas is a controlled variable and must be kept at a constant value."}]}]},{"name":"Space, Time, and Motion","subtopics":[{"name":"Forces & Momentum","questions":[{"ref":"PH0036","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0036\n\nA spring of negligible mass and length of $l_o$ is attached to a fixed point. When the spring is extended by an external force $F$, the length of the spring increases to $l$.  The graph shows how the length of the spring $L$ varies with the tension $T$ in the spring. The tension in the spring is equal to $k \\Delta l$, where k is a constant and $\\Delta l$ is the extension of the spring.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-04-03%20at%202.57.48%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-03 at 2.57.48 PM\">\n\nWhat are the **gradient** and **y-intercept** of the graph?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1.5em}$ Gradient $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$ y-intercept $\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ A. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{k}$ | $l$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ B. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{k}$ | $l_o$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ C. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $k$ | $l_o$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ D. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{k}$ | $\\Delta l$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe y-intercept is the length of the spring when there is no external force. Therefore, the y-intercept is $l_o$\n\nGradient is $\\cfrac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}$ on the graph\n\nHooke's Law states that $F = k\\Delta x$ and in this case $\\Delta x = l - l_o$\n\nThe gradient = $\\cfrac{l-l_o}{F} = \\cfrac{\\Delta x}{F}$\n\nTherefore, $gradient = \\dfrac{1}{k}$"},{"ref":"PH0052","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0052\n\nAn object with a weight $W$ connected to one end of a rod of negligible mass rotates at constant speed in a vertical plane.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202026-01-30%20at%206.46.14%E2%80%AFAM1.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-01-30 at 6.46.14 AM1\">\n\nWhen the object is at the top position of the circular path, the tension on the rod is zero. What is the magnitude of tension at the bottom position of the circular path?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Equal to $W$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Smaller than $W$\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Equal to $2W$\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Greater than $2W$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAt the top position both the tension and the weight would act downwards, which is the same direction as the centripetal acceleration. We are told there is no tension, so all centripetal acceleration is provided by the weight.\n\n- $T + W=F_{centripetal}$ and $T = 0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n- $F_{centripetal} = W$\n\nAt the bottom, tension works upwards towards the centre and weight acts downwards. Resolving for net force gives\n\n- $T - W  =  F_{centripetal}$\n\nSince speed and radius are constant, the centripetal acceleration at the top and bottom are equal:\n\n- $F_{centripetal} = W$\n\nTherefore, the tension at the bottom\n\n-   $T = 2W$"},{"ref":"PH0083","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0083\n\nThe diagram shows the forces acting on a block as it slides down an inclined plane with constant acceleration. $W$ is the weight of the block, $F$ is the friction force, $\\mu_d$ is the coefficient of dynamic friction and $g$ is the gravitational field strength. The plane is inclined at an angle $\\theta$ to the horizontal.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-07-08%20at%203.55.44%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-08 at 3.55.44 PM\">\n\nWhich expression gives the acceleration of the block?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $g\\sin\\theta - g\\mu_d\\cos\\theta$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $g\\cos\\theta - g\\mu_d\\sin\\theta$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $W\\sin\\theta - W\\mu_d\\cos\\theta$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $W\\cos\\theta - W\\mu_d\\sin\\theta$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nTo solve this, we should resolve the weight into components parallel and perpendicular to the slope.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202026-02-18%20at%207.00.51%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-02-18 at 7.00.51 AM\">\n\nComponent of weight along the slope = $W\\sin \\theta = mg\\sin \\theta$\n\nThe forces perpendicular to the slope are balanced, so the normal reaction force ($F_N$) is equal to the component of gravity opposite to it, given by\n\n- $F_N = W\\cos\\theta=mg\\cos\\theta$\n\nDynamic friction\n\n- $F= \\mu_dF_N = \\mu_d\\,mg\\cos \\theta$\n\nApply Newton's second law:\n\n- $F_{net}=mg\\sin\\theta - \\mu_d\\,mg\\cos\\theta =ma$\n\nDividing both sides by mass gives\n\n- $a=g\\sin\\theta - \\mu_d\\,g\\cos\\theta $"},{"ref":"PH0085","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0085\n\nA rocket ejects hot gas in space in a direction opposite to its travelling direction. What is correct for the magnitude of the momentum and the kinetic energy of the rocket?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  Momentum  |  Kinetic Energy  |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Conserved | Conserved |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Increased | Conserved |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Conserved | Increased |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Increased | Increased |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe rocket is not a closed system. The reaction force from ejecting hot gas will increasingly accelerate the rocket, hence both momentum and kinetic energy will increase."},{"ref":"PH0088","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0088\n\nA roller coaster passes a vertical loop in a constant gravitational field. Friction and air resistance are negligible.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202026-11-04%20at%202.05.09%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-11-04 at 2.05.09 PM\">\n\nHow do the kinetic energy of the roller coaster and the normal reaction force acting on the roller coaster compare at point X and Y?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1.75em}$ **kinetic energy** $\\hspace{1.75em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ **normal reaction force** $\\hspace{1em}$  |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | greater at X than Y | greater at Y than X |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | greater at Y than X | greater at X than Y |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | greater at Y than X | greater at Y than X |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | greater at X than Y | greater at X than Y |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nBy conservation of energy, the roller coaster has greater kinetic energy at the bottom of the loop (X), then kinetic energy is converted to gravitational potential energy at the top of the loop (Y). Hence B and C are rejected.\n\nThe normal reaction force by the track contributes to the centripetal force needed for the roller coaster to move along the loop. Since centripetal force increases with speed, the normal reaction force is larger at the bottom of the loop (X), when speed is greater. At the bottom of the loop, the normal reaction force is also the sum of the centripetal force and the weight of the roller coaster, while at the top of the loop the normal force is equal to the centripetal force minus the gravitational force."},{"ref":"PH0109","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0109\n\nAn object of mass $M$ on a string moves in a circle of radius $r$ at a constant speed.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-14%20at%202.28.54%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-14 at 2.28.54 PM\">\n\nBelow are three statements about the motion of the object:\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1.1em}$ The resultant work done on the object is zero.\n- II. $\\hspace{0.8em}$ The velocity of the object is constant.\n- III. $\\hspace{0.5em}$ The object is accelerating\n\nWhich of the statements is/are true?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nI is correct. \\\nThe accelerating force is always perpendicular to the direction of motion, therefore no work is done.\n\n- $W = Fs \\cos\\theta$, and $\\theta$ (the angle between $F$ and $s$ is always 90$\\degree$, and $\\cos (90 \\degree) =0$\n\nAlso, $W=\\Delta E_k$ and the speed of the object is constant so there is no change in kinetic energy.\n\nII is incorrect. \\\nBecause velocity is a vector quantity and the direction is constantly changing, velocity is not constant.\n\nIII is correct. \\\nBecause velocity is constantly changing, the object is always accelerating."},{"ref":"PH0124","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0124\n\nThe diagram below shows a tennis ball of mass $m$ striking a wall with an initial velocity, $u$, and rebounding with velocity $v$. A second, softer ball of the same mass strikes the wall with the same speed but rebounds with a slower rebound speed.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-14%20at%202.33.06%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-14 at 2.33.06 PM\">\n\nWhich of the following statements is correct about the magnitudes of the impulse and change in momentum applied to the second ball compared to the first?\n\n| |$\\hspace{1em}$  Impulse$\\hspace{1em}$ | Change in momentum |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| Greater | Smaller |\n\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Smaller | Greater |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Greater | Greater |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Smaller | Smaller |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe change in momentum and impulse applied to the ball are equal, because the impulse is defined as the change in momentum. Increasing one is the same as increasing the other and vice-versa. The slower rebound speed of the second ball causes a decrease in the magnitude of the impulse since the change in momentum is smaller if the final speed $v$ is smaller.\n\n- $J = \\Delta p = m(v - -u) = m(v + u)$"},{"ref":"PH0130","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0130\n\nA skydiver of weight $W$ falls through air with a drag force $F_{D}$\n\nIf the skydiver experiences terminal velocity, which of the following best represents the free body diagram of the skydiver?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202026-03-08%20at%208.59.34%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-03-08 at 8.59.34 AM\">  |$\\hspace{1cm}$  || <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202026-03-08%20at%208.59.43%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-03-08 at 8.59.43 AM\"> |\n|C. |   |   |D.  | |\n|  |  <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202026-03-08%20at%208.59.51%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-03-08 at 8.59.51 AM\"> |   ||<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202026-03-08%20at%209.00.00%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-03-08 at 9.00.00 AM\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAt terminal velocity there is no acceleration. Forces must be balanced, therefore the drag force is equal and opposite to the weight."},{"ref":"PH0146","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0146\n\nA football player bounced the ball off their head while diving sideways, as shown:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0146-1.png\" alt=\"PH0146-1\">\n\nWhich of the following gives a Newton's Third Law pair of forces that applies to this situation?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Force 1 | Force 2 |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Force of the ball on the player | Force of the ball on the earth |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Force of the player on the earth | Force of the ball on the player |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Force of the earth on the ball | Force of the ball on the earth |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Force of the earth on the player | Force of the player on the ball |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe only combination of answers which mentions the correct Newton’s Third Law  force pair is C. In Newton’s Third Law force pairs always occur between the same two objects.\n\nIn this case, because the ball and Earth are not in contact, it is the gravitational force that is equal and opposite here."},{"ref":"PH0153","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0153\n\nBlock $A$ of mass $m_A=3.0$ kg rests above the flat horizontal surface of block $B$ of mass $m_B=6.0$ kg, which in turn rests on a smooth horizontal surface as shown.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202026-01-21%20at%201.48.01%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-01-21 at 1.48.01 PM\">\n\nThe coefficients of static and kinetic friction between $A$ and $B$ are $\\mu_s=0.15$ and $\\mu_k=0.1$ respectively. A horizontal force of $F=7.0$ N is applied towards the right on block $A$. Assuming acceleration due to gravity $g \\approx 10$ m s$^{-2}$, what is the acceleration of block $B$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}   \\dfrac{1}{2}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  \\dfrac{3}{4}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  \\dfrac{7}{9}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  \\dfrac{7}{6}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nIf the blocks were to accelerate together with no slipping, the acceleration of the system would be\n\n- $a=\\dfrac{F_{net}}{m}=\\dfrac{7}{(3+6)}=\\dfrac{7}{9}$\n\nFor this to occur, the frictional force that pulls block $B$ along would need to provide the net force. Solving for this force:\n\n- $F_{net}=ma=(6)(\\dfrac{7}{9})=4.7$ N\n\nThe normal contact force on $A$, $F_{N(A)}=m_A g=(3.0$ kg) (10 m s$^{-2})=30$ N\n\n(Contact force from $B$ supports the weight of $A$).\n\nThe maximum friction on between $A$ and $B$ is $F_{s}=\\mu_s F_{N(A)}=(0.15)(30$ N) = 4.5 N\n\nThe static friction force is not enough to accelerate the bottom block at this rate. Therefore block $A$ slides across block $B$\n\nTherefore, block $A$ slides on block $B$, reducing the final frictional force between the blocks to kinetic friction, $F_k=\\mu_k F_{N(A)}=(0.1)(30)$ N = 3.0 N\n\nUsing Newton's second law, the acceleration of $B$, $a_B=\\dfrac{F_{net}}{m_B} = \\dfrac{F_k}{m_B} = \\dfrac{3.0\\textrm{ N}}{6.0\\textrm{ kg}}=\\dfrac{1}{2}$"},{"ref":"PH0156","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0156\n\nA table tennis ball of mass $m$ is dropped on a level surface from a height $H$. The ball rebounds to a height $h < H$ after impact. Air resistance on the ball is negligible. Which of the following is correct about the nature of the collision and magnitude of the change in momentum ($\\Delta p$) of the ball as a result of this impact?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Nature of the collision |Change in momentum $\\Delta p$ |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Elastic | $m(\\sqrt{2gH}-\\sqrt{2gh})$ |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Inelastic | $m(\\sqrt{2gH}-\\sqrt{2gh})$ |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C. $\\hspace{1.5em}$| Elastic | $m(\\sqrt{2gH}+\\sqrt{2gh})$ |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D. $\\hspace{1.5em}$| Inelastic | $m(\\sqrt{2gH}+\\sqrt{2gh})$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe height of rebound is less than the height of release. This is evidence that some energy is dissipated at the point of impact. Hence inelastic. \n\nWe can use conservation of energy to find the velocity upon impact:\n\n- $mgH=\\dfrac{1}{2}mu^2$\n\n- $u^2 =2gH$\n\n- $u=\\sqrt{2gH} $\n\nSimilarly, the velocity just after impact is: $v=-\\sqrt{2gh} \\hspace{2em}$\n\nWhere the negative sign represents the opposite direction to $u$ (upwards)\n\n- $\\therefore \\Delta p=|mv-mu|$\n\n- $=|m(v-u)|$\n\n- $=m|(-\\sqrt{2gh}-\\sqrt{2gH})|$\n\nRemoving the absolute value gives\n\n- $=m(\\sqrt{2gH}+\\sqrt{2gh})$"},{"ref":"PH0205","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0205\n\nAn object of mass $m$ rests on a rough surface. Another object of mass $M$ is connected to it via a frictionless pulley. The coefficient of dynamic friction between the surface and the object of mass $m$ is $\\mu$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-27%20at%201.47.48%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-27 at 1.47.48 PM\">\n\nWhen the object of mass $M$ is released, the objects accelerate. What is the acceleration of the system?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{gM}{M+m}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{gm}{M+m}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{g(M-\\mu m)}{M+m}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{g(\\mu m-M)}{M+m}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe resultant force is the difference between the weight of the object of mass $M$ and the force of friction between the object of mass $m$ and the rough surface.\n\n- $F_{net} = F_g - F_f =m_{total}a$\n\nWeight of the object\n\n- $F_g= Mg$\n\nFriction on mass $m$\n\n- $F_f= \\mu mg$\n\n- $M_{total}=M+m$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation,\n\n-  $Mg - \\mu mg = (M+m)a$\n\nBy rearranging;\n\n- $a = \\dfrac{g(M-\\mu m)}{M+m}$\n\nTherefore, The answer is C."},{"ref":"PH0231","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0231\n\nA stone attached to one end of a rod rotates in a vertical circle at a constant speed. Two positions of the stone at two different instants, X and Y, are shown in the diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0231-1.png\" alt=\"PH0231-1\">\n\n In the table below, which row is correct for the centripetal force acting on the stone at X and Y?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n | |$\\hspace{1em}$ Direction of centripetal force $\\hspace{1em}$| $\\hspace{1em}$Magnitude of centripetal force$\\hspace{1em}$|\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Same | Same |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Same | Different |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Different | Same |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Different | Different |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe centripetal force on an object undergoing uniform circular motion is always towards the centre of the circle therefore the direction is different at the different locations. The magnitude of centripetal force is dependent on the radius of the circular path and the speed of the object. Since both locations have the same radius and speed, the magnitude of centripetal force is constant throughout the motion. Therefore, the answer is C."},{"ref":"PH0243","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0243\n\nAn airplane of weight $W$ is flying horizontally with constant velocity. The total forward thrust of the engines is $3W$. Which of the following gives the magnitude of the total force of the air on the plane?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em} 4W$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em} 3W$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\sqrt{10}W$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\sqrt{8} W$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nDue to the absence of acceleration, the resultant force must be zero. Therefore, both the forward thrust and the weight must be cancelled by forces from the air.\n\nThe force from the air has two components; $3\\, W$ in backward drag and $W$ in upward lift.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-07-03%20at%204.59.56%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-03 at 4.59.56 PM\">\n\nUsing Pythagoras' theorem to find the resultant of these two components:\n\n- R = $\\sqrt{(3W)^2+ W^2 } = \\sqrt{10}\\,  W$"},{"ref":"PH0245","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0245\n\nTwo bodies of masses 2 kg and 3 kg are connected by a thread and pulled in the upward direction with an acceleration of $g$ due to an upward force $F$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0245q.JPEG\" alt=\"PH0245q\">\n\nThe thread between the masses is cut. Which of the following is correct for the bodies just after the cut?\n\n| | Acceleration of 3 kg mass | Acceleration of 2 kg mass |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $g$ | $g$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $4g$ | $g$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $4g$ | $4g$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $g$ | $4g$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n- $F = ma$\n\nBefore the thread is cut,\n\n- $F - W = ma$  for the system\n- $W = mg = 5g    $ and  $\\ ma=5g$\n\nTherefore\n\n- $F = 5g + 5g = 10g$\n\nJust after the cut, the 3 kg mass is only under the influence of gravity, so its acceleration is equal to $g$\n\nThe net force on the 2 kg mass is\n\n- $F - 2g = 10g - 2g= 8g $\n\nAcceleration of the 3 kg mass = $g$\n\nAcceleration of 2 kg mass = $ \\dfrac{8g}{2} = 4g$"},{"ref":"PH0247","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0247\n\nA ball of mass 0.5 kg moving horizontally strikes a vertical wall with a speed of 30 m s$^{-1}$. The magnitude of the change of the momentum of the ball is 22.5 kg m s$^{-1}$. Calculate the speed of the ball just after it rebounds.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0247.png\" alt=\"PH0247\">\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0 m s$^{-1}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 5 m s$^{-1}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 10 m s$^{-1}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 15 m s$^{-1}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nChange in momentum;\n\n- $\\Delta p = p_f - p_i$ \n\nThe momentum in the left direction is regarded as negative while the right is positive. Therefore;\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\hspace{0.8\n\ncm}-\\Delta p &= -p_f - p_i\\\\\n\n-\\Delta p &= -mv - mu\\\\\n\n-22.5 &= -0.5v - (0.5)(30)\\\\\n\n0.5v &= 22.5 - 15\\\\\n\n0.5v &= 7.5\\\\\n\nv &= 15\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}\n\n\\end{aligned}$"},{"ref":"PH0248","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0248\n\nA stationary object explodes and splits into two parts of masses $m$ and $2m$. Mass $m$ moves north with a velocity of $2v$ and mass $2m$ moves south with a velocity of $v$. Calculate the total change in linear momentum of the system as a result of the explosion.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-06-30%20at%205.56.30%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-30 at 5.56.30 PM\">\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Zero\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $1.5mv$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $4mv$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $6mv$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe change in the momentum of the system in the explosion is zero, as the system is isolated and therefore momentum is conserved."},{"ref":"PH0261","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0261\n\nA block of wood is placed on a rough inclined surface. Which of the diagrams below correctly represents the forces acting on the block when it is at rest?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  |  B. ||\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0261A.png\" alt=\"PH0261A\">  |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0261B.png\" alt=\"PH0261B\">|\n$\\hspace{1cm}$\n$\\hspace{1cm}$\n|C. |   |   |  D. ||\n|  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0261C.png\" alt=\"PH0261C\">   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0261D.png\" alt=\"PH0261D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nWeight always acts vertically downwards\n\nNormal reaction force is always normal (perpendicular) to the surface\n\nIn order to remain at rest, friction must act up the incline in order to keep the block from sliding down the incline under the action of the component of weight acting down the incline.\n\nIn diagram B, friction is acting down the incline. This will result in an acceleration down the incline.\n\nIn diagram C, the normal reaction is acting opposite to the weight whereas normal reaction must always be perpendicular to the contact surface.\n\nIn diagram D, weight is shown to act at an angle to the vertical, whereas weight always acts vertically downwards."},{"ref":"PH0263","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0263\n\nA firecracker is at rest just before exploding. Upon exploding, the firecracker splits into two unequal parts which are projected in exactly opposite directions. Which of the following must be true?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The total kinetic energy of the firecracker remains unchanged before and after the explosion.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The kinetic energy of the two parts after the explosion is identical.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ The total momentum of the firecracker is unchanged after the explosion. \n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The velocities of the two parts are exactly equal in magnitude but opposite in direction.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nA. Incorrect. $E_k$ changed from zero to non-zero because the chemical potential energy in the firecracker is converted into kinetic energy which is divided between the two parts after the explosion.\n\nB. Incorrect. Because $E_k=\\left(\\dfrac{p^2}{2m}\\right)$ and the momenta must be equal, the kinetic energies must be unequal as the masses are not equal. \n\nC. Correct. Momentum is always conserved in any process for an isolated system. \n\nD. Incorrect. Because since momentum is conserved, unequal parts must have unequal velocities."},{"ref":"PH0266","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0266\n\nA block of mass $m$, initially at rest, is acted upon by two unequal forces $F_1$ and $F_2$ such that $F_1 > F_2$ as shown in the diagram below. The forces act for a time $t$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0266.png\" alt=\"PH0266\">\n\nWhich of the following correctly represents the magnitude of the final velocity of the block after time $t$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\bigg(\\dfrac{F_1+F_2}{m}\\bigg)(t)$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\bigg(\\dfrac{F_1-F_2}{m}\\bigg)(t)$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\bigg(\\dfrac{F_2-F_1}{m}\\bigg)(t)$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\bigg(\\dfrac{F_1-F_2}{t}\\bigg)(m)$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nSince $F_1$ is greater than $F_2$, net force will be $F_1-F_2$ in the direction of $F_1$. \n\nUsing this value of net force, acceleration $a$ is \n\n- $a=\\dfrac{F_{net}}{m}= \\dfrac{F_1-F_2}{m}$\n\nUsing this value of $a$ in the expression $v=u+at$ and taking $u = 0$, we obtain the final velocity.\n\n**OR**\n\n- $\\Delta p = F_{net}\\Delta t. \\hspace{2em}$\n\n-  $m\\Delta v = F_{net}\\Delta t. \\hspace{2em} $\n\n \n\n-  $\\Delta v = \\bigg(\\dfrac{F_1-F_2}{m}\\bigg)(t)$"},{"ref":"PH0284","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0284\n\nA mass is attached to the mirror of a car by a string. As the car accelerates to the left, the string makes an angle with the vertical as shown in the diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-04-03%20at%202.33.56%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-03 at 2.33.56 PM\">\n\nWhich of the following shows the correct  free-body diagram that represents the forces acting on this mass when it is accelerating?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n|| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0284A.png\" alt=\"PH0284A\"> |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0284B.png\" alt=\"PH0284B\">|\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0284C.png\" alt=\"PH0284C\"> |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0284D.png\" alt=\"PH0284D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe only forces acting are the weight and the tension. These forces must be balanced in the vertical direction, but provide a net horizontal force when added together.\n\nB is the only diagram that shows weight and tension, with the vertical component of tension equal in magnitude to the weight vector. Upwards and horizontal forces on the mass are only provided by the tension force.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202026-01-28%20at%207.54.45%E2%80%AFAM1.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-01-28 at 7.54.45 AM1\">"},{"ref":"PH0298","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0298\n\nA car enters a roundabout with a speed $v$. The dotted line shows the path of the car. The road is slightly banked inward at an angle to the horizontal.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0298-1.png\" alt=\"PH0298-1\">\n\nThe coefficient of friction between the tires of the car and the road is $\\mu$. When $\\mu$ decreases, which of the following changes could prevent the car from slipping away on this roundabout?\n\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$ **Banking angle of the turn** $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{3em}$ **Speed of the car**$\\hspace{3em}$  |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | increase | increase |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | decrease | increase |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | increase | decrease |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | decrease | decrease |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nWhen $\\mu$ decreases, the maximum possible frictional force decreases. Friction acts along with the component of the normal force in the horizontal direction to produce the centripetal force. To account for this change, decreasing the speed will allow the car to require less frictional force due to a lower required centripetal acceleration and safely take the turn.\n\n- $\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}=\\mu mg$\n\nIncreasing the banking angle increases the component of the normal force that points towards the centre of the circle, providing more centripetal force to compensate for the loss in centripetal force contribution from friction. Remember that in this situation friction is acting towards the centre of the circle, and so having an increased component of the normal force acting towards the centre of the circle will $\\lq$help' friction to turn the car."},{"ref":"PH0329","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0329\n\nA piece of metal of weight $W$ is suspended by two identical strings. Each string passes through a pulley and is attached to a block of mass $m$. The system is in equilibrium\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Sc%CF%88reenshot%202025-05-27%20at%205.06.05%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Scψreenshot 2025-05-27 at 5.06.05 PM\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Created with Chemix (https://chemix.org)]}}$\n\nWhat must be true for $m$ such that the two strings attached to the piece of metal are almost horizontal?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $m < \\dfrac{W}{g}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{W}{2g} < m < \\dfrac{W}{g}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $m > \\dfrac{W}{g}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $m < \\dfrac{W}{2g}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFor a string to be almost horizontal, the angle of inclination of the string $\\theta$ will be very small\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-27%20at%205.05.05%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-27 at 5.05.05 PM\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Created with Chemix (https://chemix.org)]}}$\n\nThe piece of metal is stationary, so $W=2T\\sin\\theta$\n\nFor very small inclination angle $\\theta$, $\\sin\\theta$ will be very small\n\nFor the above equation to be valid; the value of the tension T in the string must be very high. T >> W\n\n$T = mg$\n\nWhich means that the mass of the block must be higher than the mass of the metal. So $m > \\frac{W}{g}$"},{"ref":"PH0330","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0330\n\nA block of mass 4.0 kg rests on an inclined plane.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0330.png\" alt=\"PH0330\">\n\nThe coefficient of static friction between the block and the plane $\\mu _s$ is 0.4.\n\nWhich of the following gives the angle of inclination at which the block will start to slide?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\sin^{-1}(0.4)$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\tan^{-1}(0.4)$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\cos^{-1}(0.4)$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\tan^{-1}(0.6)$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-27%20at%205.09.47%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-27 at 5.09.47 PM\">\n\n- $F_N = mg\\cos\\theta$\n\n- $F = \\mu_sF_N$\n\n- $F = mg\\sin\\theta$\n\n- $mg(\\sin\\theta) = 0.4mg(\\cos\\theta$)\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\sin\\theta } {\\cos\\theta}=0.4$\n\n- $\\tan\\theta = 0.4$\n\n- $\\theta=\\tan^{-1}(0.4)$"},{"ref":"PH0344","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0344\n\nA ball of mass $0.1$ kg moves horizontally with a speed $5.0$ m s$^{-1}$, hits a vertical wall and rebounds with speed of $3.0$ m s$^{-1}$. The graph records the variation of force $F$ with time $t$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%209.31.17%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 9.31.17 AM\">\n\nWhat is $F_{max}$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 4 N\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 8 N\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 40 N\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 80 N","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nFor a force time graph; the area under the graph is equal to the magnitude of the change in momentum of the ball.\n\nThe change in momentum; $\\Delta p$\n\n- $\\Delta p = m(v-u)=0.1(-3-5)=-0.8$ kg m s$^{-1}$\n\nThe area under the graph = magnitude of change in momentum\n\n- $\\dfrac{1}{2}(20 \\times 10^{-3})(F_{max}) = 0.8$\n\n- $F_{max} = \\dfrac{(0.8)(2)}{(20 \\times 10^{-3})}=80$ N"},{"ref":"PH0393","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0393\n\nA mass $Y$ attached to a rope is moving in uniform circular motion on a frictionless table as shown on the diagram. The circular path of constant radius $r$ is shown with the dotted line. The mass $X$ is hanging from the lower end of the rope.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%209.44.48%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 9.44.48 AM\">\n\nThe masses of $X$ and $Y$ can be adjusted. What independent adjustments to either mass $X$ or mass $Y$ could produce uniform circular motion at a lower speed $v$, but with the same radius $r$?\n\n| | $\\hspace{2em}$ **Mass $Y$** $\\hspace{2em}$ | $\\hspace{2em}$ **Mass $X$**$\\hspace{2em}$  |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | increase | increase |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | decrease | increase |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | increase | decrease |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | decrease | decrease |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nCentripetal force is provided by the weight of the hanging mass $X$. We can substitute $X$ in to the equation $F_g= mg$ and $Y$ into the centripetal force equation:\n\n- $mg = \\dfrac{mv^2}{r}$\n\n- $Xg=\\dfrac{Yv^2}{r}$\n\nTo maintain the same radius with a decrease in $v$, this equation should hold.\nWhen the linear speed decreases, therefore to keep the equation balanced, the mass $Y$ should increase, or $X$ should decrease."},{"ref":"PH0394","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0394\n\nA toy of mass $m$ is attached to a rod of length $L$ and undergoes circular motion in the vertical plane. The radial force $F$ of the rod is constant.\n\nWhat is the ratio of the linear speed at the top to the linear speed at the bottom of its path, $\\dfrac{v_{top}}{v_{bottom}}$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{F-mg}{F+mg}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{F+mg}{F-mg}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{F-mg}{F+mg}}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{F+mg}{F-mg}}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nAt the top, the centripetal force is $F+mg=\\dfrac{m(v_{top})^2}{r}$\n\nAt the bottom, the centripetal force is $F-mg=\\dfrac{m(v_{bottom})^2}{r}$\n\nRearranging the above equations for the required ratio gives:\n\n$\\hspace{3em}$ $\\dfrac{v_{top}}{v_{bottom}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{F+mg}{F-mg}}$"},{"ref":"PH0395","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0395\n\nA 200 g ball is moving in a horizontal circle shown with the dotted path in the diagram. The diameter of the bowl is 40 cm and the angular speed is $4\\pi$ rad s$^{-1}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%209.56.32%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 9.56.32 AM\">\n\nWhat is the magnitude of the ball's centripetal acceleration?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $0.64\\pi$ rad s$^{-2}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    $3.2\\pi$ rad s$^{-2}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    $6.4\\pi$ rad s$^{-2}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    Cannot be determined with the given information.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe equation for the centripetal acceleration is $a_c=\\omega^2r$.\n\nAlthough $\\omega$ is given in the question, the radius of the circular path is smaller than the radius of the bowl and no information is given to calculate it, therefore answer is D."},{"ref":"PH0396","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0396\n\nA mass $m$ is moving on a circular path at the end of a rope of constant length. The centripetal force acting on the mass is $F$.\nThe frequency of the motion is gradually increased.\n\nWhich graph correctly shows the change of the centripetal force $F_c$ as the frequency $f$ is increased?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |   |  B. ||\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%2010.18.18%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 10.18.18 AM\">   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%2010.18.23%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 10.18.23 AM\">|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%2010.18.28%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 10.18.28 AM\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%2010.18.32%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 10.18.32 AM\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nIn the data booklet, given centripetal acceleration:\n\n- $a=\\dfrac{4\\pi^2r}{T^2}$\n\nwhich can also be expressed as\n\n- $a=4\\pi^2rf^2$\n\nSince $F=ma$,\n\n- $F=m4\\pi^2rf^2$\n\nTherefore the graph of $F$ vs $f$ must be a quadratic as in A."},{"ref":"PH0440","marks":"12","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0440\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\n1. Define Newton's First Law. [2 marks]\n\n2. A block of mass 2.0 kg slides at the constant speed of $v$ on a rough inclined plane. A string attached to the hanging mass of 0.50 kg passes over a pulley and supports the block. The slope of the incline is 25$\\degree$. \n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%2011.46.01%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 11.46.01 AM\">\n\n    $\\small{\\textrm{[Source: Created with Chemix - https://chemix.org/]}}$\n\n    \n\n    1. Show that the direction of the motion of the box is down the slope. [3 marks]\n\n    2. Draw and label a free-body diagram for the forces acting on the block. [2 marks]\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%2011.48.51%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 11.48.51 AM\">\n\n    3. Determine the coefficient of dynamic friction between the block and the inclined plane. [3 marks]\n\n    4. In reality, air resistance will also have an effect on the motion of the block and the calculated coefficient of friction. If air resistance were taken into account for this constant speed scenario, discuss how the calculated value would be affected. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. When the net force acting on the body is zero $\\checkmark$\n\n    The body has constant velocity/does not accelerate $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\textbf{OR}$\n\n    An object at constant velocity $\\ll$and in a state of rest$\\gg$ will remain so $\\checkmark$\n\n    Unless acted on by an external/unbalanced force $\\checkmark$\n\n2. \n\n    1. Tension/Weight of the mass hanger = $\\ll (0.50)(9.81) = \\gg$ $4.9$ N $\\checkmark$\n\n        The component of the weight of the block on the plane of incline = $\\ll (2.0)(9.81)(sin25) = \\gg(2.0)(9.81)(0.42)$ = $8.3$ N $\\checkmark$\n\n        :answer[8.3 > 4.9] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The direction of the friction on the block should be in the opposite direction of the block's motion. \n\n        There is no acceleration. According to Newton's second law, there is no net force either on the object separately or the system. The forces on the block must be balanced. \n\n        Weight $W_T$ of the block: \n\n        - $W_T = mg = (2.0)(9.81) = 20$ N\n\n        The component of the weight of the block $W_{Tx}$ on the plane of the incline: \n\n        - $W_{Tx} = mg\\sin25\\degree = (19.62)(0.42) = 8.3$ N\n\n        Weight $W_M$ of the mass hanger: \n\n        - $W_M = mg = (0.50)(9.81) = 4.9$ N\n\n        The weight of the mass hanger should be the tension of the string and the tension of the spring is less than the component of the weight of the block. Therefore, the friction force acting on the block should be up the slope. The block moves down the slope. \n\n    2. $\\textrm{ }$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%2011.54.23%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 11.54.23 AM\">\n\n        \n\n        Correct directions and labels $\\checkmark$\n\n        Weight is longer than the other three by eye $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        Four forces are acting on the block. They are weight towards the bottom of the page, normal force perpendicular to the plane of incline, tension and frictional force up to the slope. Since weight balances the vertical components of the other three forces, it should be greater in magnitude. This can be represented by longer arrows in the free-body diagram. \n\n    3. Normal reaction force = $\\ll$ Component of the weight perpendicular to the plane of incline $=\\gg 18$ N $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $8.3\\textrm{ N} - 4.9\\textrm{ N} = 3.4\\textrm{ N}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\mu_d = \\dfrac{3.4}{18}$ = :answer[0.19] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The net force acting on the block should be zero. The forces on the plane of incline are the force of dynamic friction, the tension up to the slope and the component of weight down to the slope. The force of dynamic friction $F_f$: \n\n        - $F_f = \\mu_dF_N$\n\n        The component of the weight of the block $W_{Tx}$ on the plane of the incline: \n\n        - $W_{Tx} = mg\\sin25° = (2.0)(9.81)(0.42) = 8.3$ N\n\n        The tension $T$: \n\n        - $T = W_m = mg = (0.50)(9.81) = 4.9$ N\n\n        The forces that are perpendicular to the plane of the incline are normal reaction force and the component of the weight of the block.\n\n        The component of the weight of the block $W_{Ty}$ perpendicular to the plane of the incline: \n\n        - $W_{Ty} = mg\\cos25° = (2.0)(9.81)(0.91) = 18$ N\n\n        $W_{Ty}$ must be equal to $R$.\n\n        So, dynamic friction can be rewritten as: \n\n        - $F_f = \\mu_dW_{Ty}$\n\n        Since $W_{Tx}$ must be balanced with $F_f$ and $T$: \n\n        - $F_f+T=W_{Tx}$\n\n        - $\\mu_d W_{Ty}=W_{Tx}-T$\n\n        - $\\mu_d = \\dfrac{W_{Tx}-T}{W_{Ty}} = \\dfrac{8.3-4.9}{18} = 0.19$\n\n    4. air resistance also contributes a force up the slope, which means there is less force due to dynamic friction. $\\checkmark$\n\n        the calculated value for the coefficient of friction would be lower $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The direction of air resistance is always opposite to the motion of the object. Therefore, it is in the same direction as the force of dynamic friction and tension. Instead of the force of dynamic friction with tension only, the resultant force of air friction, tension and the force of dynamic friction balances the component of weight. Hence, the force of dynamic friction would be lower. This would lead to a lower calculated value for coefficient of friction."},{"ref":"PH0441","marks":"11","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0441\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\n1. State Newton's Third Law. [2 marks]\n\n2. A block of mass 2.0 kg is placed on another block of mass 5.0 kg. The blocks sit on a frictionless surface, but the coefficient of static friction between the blocks is 0.50. The block at the top is pulled with a varying horizontal force $F$. \n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%201.12.55%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 1.12.55 PM\">\n\n    1. Calculate the magnitude of the maximum force $F_{max}$ which results in both of the blocks moving together without slipping. [3 marks]\n\n    2. On the graph, sketch how the force of static friction $F_f$ acting on the bottom block varies with the force $F$. [2 marks]\n\n        (no need to add values on the axis)\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%201.06.47%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 1.06.47 PM\">\n\n        $F_{max}$ is your answer to (b)(i) and draw your graph up to $F_{max}$\n\n    3. According to Newton's Third Law, there must be another force paired with the weight of the top block. Describe this force. [2 marks]\n\n3. On another occasion, the blocks are moved with the help of a force of $12$ N acting on the block at the top. The drag force $D$ on the two blocks is given by $D=0.025v^2$ where $v$ is the speed of the blocks. Calculate the top speed of the blocks for this force. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. when two bodies A and B interact, the $\\ll$action$\\gg$ $\\underline{\\textrm{force}}$ that A exerts on B is equal and $\\checkmark$\n\n    opposite in direction to the $\\ll$reaction$\\gg$ $\\underline{\\textrm{force}}$ that B exerts on A $\\checkmark$\n\n2. \n\n    1. The magnitude of maximum force of friction = $ \\mu_s R =  9.8$ N $\\checkmark$\n\n        The acceleration of both blocks = $\\ll \\dfrac{F_{fmax}}{5} = \\gg = 1.96 =2.0$ m s$^{-2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $ F = ma  = \\ll(5+2)(1.96)=13.7=\\gg$ :answer[14 N]  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        To have the blocks moving together, the acceleration of each block must be the same. The vertical forces on each block cancel each other. Therefore, only horizontal forces create acceleration. The only horizontal force acting on the block with a mass of 5.0 kg is the force due to static friction between the blocks. The acceleration of 5.0 kg mass can be found by Newton's second law ($F_{net}=ma$): \n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%201.16.32%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 1.16.32 PM\">\n\n        The acceleration of 5.0 kg mass can be found by Newton's second law ($F_{net}=ma$): \n\n        - $F_f = 5a \\hspace{2em}$ (first expression)\n\n        Two horizontal forces are acting on the 2.0 kg mass as $F$ and the force of static friction. \n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%201.16.22%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 1.16.22 PM\">\n\n        The acceleration of 2.0 kg mass can be found by: \n\n        - $F - F_f = 2.0a \\hspace{2em}$ (second expression)\n\n        Since maximum $F$ is investigated, the maximum value of $F_f$ should be employed.\n\n        - $F_{fmax}=\\mu_s R \\hspace{1em}$    where $\\mu_s=0.5$ and $R=mg=(2.0)(9.81)=20$ N\n\n        - $F_{fmax} = (0.50)(20) = 9.8$ N\n\n        This is the net force acting on the bottom block. We can use this to find the acceleration. When $F_{fmax}$ is substituted to the first expression: \n\n        - $9.8 = 5.0a$\n\n        - $1.96$ m s$^{-2}=a$\n\n        As both blocks must have the same acceleration, they can be treated as one object of mass $2 + 5  = 7$ kg\n\n        \n\n        The applied force F is also the net force for the two blocks\n\n        \n\n        Therefore,\n\n        - $F_{net} = ma = (7)(1.96) = 13.7 =14$ N\n\n    2. $\\textrm{ }$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%201.08.54%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 1.08.54 PM\">\n\n        a straight line $\\checkmark$\n\n    \n\n        passing through the origin $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        When both of the objects are considered as one block. There is no opposing force due to a lack of friction between the surface and bottom block. Hence, the applied force can not be balanced and the acceleration of the blocks will never be zero. The forces acting on the block at the top are the force of static friction $F_f$  (It is the same as the friction force on the block at the bottom) and applied force F. The net force on the top block: \n\n        - $F - F_f = 2a \\hspace{2em} a \\neq 0$\n\n        Due to this relationship, when $F$ is increased, $F_f$ is also increased. The maximum value for the force of static friction: \n\n        - $F_f = \\mu N = (0.5)(2.0)(9.81) = 9.8\\textrm{ N}$ when the $F_{max}$ is applied.\n\n        Since there is no scaling on the x and y axis, this relation can be expressed with a straight line passing through the origin at any angle.\n\n    3. The gravitational pull of  mass at the top $\\checkmark$\n\n        on the earth $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The paired force should be on the other object and the same type of force. Since weight is the pull of the earth on the mass, paired force should be the pull of the mass on the earth.\n\n3. $\\hspace{2.2em} F = Dv^2$     \n\n    - $12 = 0.025v^{2} \\checkmark$\n\n    - $v = \\ll \\sqrt{\\dfrac{12}{0.025}} = \\gg$ :answer[22 m s$^{-1}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    The acceleration of the blocks must be zero when they are moving at top speed. The resultant force should be zero to have no acceleration. Since two forces acting on the blocks are drag force and 12 N, they must be balanced by each other; \n\n    - $F = D v^2$\n\n    - $12=(2.5 \\times 10^{-2})(v^2)$\n\n    - $\\dfrac{12}{0.025} = v^2$\n\n    - $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{12}{0.025}} = v$\n\n    - $v = 22$ m s$^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH0475","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0475\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nA car enters a horizontal circular roundabout as shown with a speed of 35 km h$^{-1}$. The radius of the roundabout is 15 m.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-29%20at%201.32.11%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-29 at 1.32.11 PM\">\n\n1. State the force which provides the centripetal force for the circular motion of the car. [1 mark]\n\n2. Calculate the minimum coefficient of static friction between the tyres of the car and the road for the car to safely complete its motion around the roundabout. [3 marks]\n\n3. State and explain any change in your answer to part (b) if:\n\n    1. The mass of the car increases. [2 marks]\n\n    2. The road is banked at an angle so that the car is tilted towards the center of the roundabout. [2 marks]\n\n    3. The speed of the car increases. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. **Frictional** force (between the road and the car tyres) $\\checkmark$\n\n    Weight is not accepted as a response here.\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    It is the force of friction between the road and the tires that provides the force towards the centre of the roundabout. Without friction, the car would not be able to make the turn.\n\n2. The speed of the car is ($35\\textrm{ km h}^{-1}$, which is $\\dfrac{35000\\textrm{ m}}{3600\\textrm{ s}})=$ 9.72 m s$^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    Frictional force provides the centripetal force, therefore $\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}=mg\\mu$\n\n    Rearranging gives $\\mu=\\dfrac{v^2}{rg}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    **OR**\n\n    The substitution: $\\mu=\\dfrac{9.72^2}{(15)(9.8)}$ = :answer[0.64] $\\checkmark$\n\n    (2 marks max if speed is not converted to m s$^{-1}$. This gives 8.33)\n\n3.\n    1. No change $\\checkmark$\n\n        The mass of the car impacts the calculation of the frictional force and centripetal force equally/cancels in the calculation. $\\checkmark$\n\n    2. Decrease $\\checkmark$\n\n        The normal force would contribute to the centripetal force, therefore less frictional force would be sufficient. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        On a banked turn, the normal force will have a component that points towards the centre of the circle. As the centripetal force required is the same as on a non-banked turn, less frictional force is required and therefore a lower coefficient of friction is sufficient.\n\n    3. Increase $\\checkmark$\n\n        At a higher speed, a larger centripetal force would be necessary to maintain the circular motion. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        A higher speed will increase the value of $\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}$, resulting in the need for a higher value for the coefficient of friction $\\mu$."},{"ref":"PH0541","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0541\n\nAn object of mass $m$ rests on a rough surface. Another object of mass $M$ is connected to it with a rope.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0541-1.png\" alt=\"PH0541-1\">\n\nThe image below shows the free-body diagram of horizontal forces on the object of mass $m$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0541-2.png\" alt=\"PH0541-2\">\n\nWhich of the following correctly describes the Newton's 3rd Law force paired with $F_2$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $     The pull of mass $m$ upwards on the earth.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $     The upward contact force acting on the mass $m$.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $     The tension force to the right acting on the mass $m$.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ The friction force to the right acting on the surface.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAccording to the given diagram, two horizontal forces are acting on the mass $m$. They are friction and tension. The force acting towards the left, represented by $F_2$, is friction. The friction occurs on the object due to sliding on the surface. Therefore, according to Newton's third law, the pair of the force must be on the surface and in the opposite direction. Therefore, the answer is D."},{"ref":"PH0542","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0542\n\nA brick of weight $100$ N rests on a rough horizontal surface. A varying horizontal force is applied to the brick. The graph shows the variation of the magnitude of the frictional force with the magnitude of the applied force.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-06-30%20at%205.09.59%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-30 at 5.09.59 PM\">\n\nWhat is the coefficient of the dynamic friction between the brick and the rough surface?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 0.2\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 0.3\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 0.4\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 0.5","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe dynamic friction between two surfaces occurs when there is a relative motion between them. It has a fixed value and it can be found by\n\n- $F_f=\\mu_dF_N$\n\nThe graph shows the static friction force increasing linearly to a maxiumum until the object starts to slip and dynamic friction replaces static friction. According to the graph, the magnitude of the dynamic friction (the fixed value on the graph) is $40$ N.\n\nBy considering\n\n- $F_g=F_N$\n\nWe can substitute $F_N=100$ N and $F_f=40$ N into the friction equation as follows:\n\n- $F_f=\\mu_dF_N$\n\n- $40\\,N=\\mu_d$ (100 N)\n\n- $0.40=\\mu_d$\n\nTherefore, the answer is C."},{"ref":"PH0544","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0544\n\nA mass moving with a constant speed $u$ encounters a rough surface and comes to a stop. The mass takes a time $t$ to stop after encountering the rough surface.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%201.43.46%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 1.43.46 PM\">\n\nThe coefficient of dynamic friction between the rough surface and the mass is 0.40. Which of the following expressions gives the initial speed $u$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}      0.2gt$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}      0.4gt$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}      gt$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}      2.5gt$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nWhen the mass is moving on the rough surface, the only horizontal force acting on it is dynamic friction. Due to Newton’s Second Law, the following relationship can be written:\n\n- $F_f = ma$\n\nusing $F_f=\\mu_dF_N$ and $a=\\dfrac{v-u}{t}$;\n\n- $\n\\mu_dF_N=m\\dfrac{v-u}{t}$\n\n$F_N=mg$,  therefore\n\n- $\\mu_dmg=m\\dfrac{v-u}{t}$\n\n- $\\mu_dg=\\dfrac{v-u}{t}$\n\n- $\\mu_dgt=v-u$\n\n- $u=v-\\mu_dgt$\n\nBy substituting the given values\n\n- $u=0-0.4gt$\n\n- $u=-0.4gt$\n\nBecause we are asked for the speed, we can remove the negative sign to get\n\n- $u=0.4gt$"},{"ref":"PH0545","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0545\n\nA ball is projected in an environment where air resistance can be ignored.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-04-07%20at%208.05.30%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-07 at 8.05.30 AM\">\n\nWhich graph shows the variation of the resultant force $R$ acting on the ball with the height $h$ of the object?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n|| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-26%20at%2011.10.29%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-26 at 11.10.29 AM\"> |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-26%20at%2011.10.57%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-26 at 11.10.57 AM\">|\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  |  <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-26%20at%2011.11.10%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-26 at 11.11.10 AM\">|  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshott%202025-05-26%20at%2011.11.16%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshott 2025-05-26 at 11.11.16 AM\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nDue to the absence of air resistance, the only force acting on the ball is weight and its magnitude is constant. Therefore, the resultant force should be constant during the motion. The only graph that represents this situation is the graph in B."},{"ref":"PH0546","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0546\n\nThree identical masses are connected as shown with a string that passes over an ideal pulley. The tension in the string connecting $m_1$ and $m_2$ is $T_1$. The tension of the string attached to $m_3$ is $T_2$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0546.png\" alt=\"PH0546\">\n\n Which of the following is correct for $T_2$ and the acceleration of  $m_1$?\n\n| |$\\hspace{1.5em} \\bf{T_2} \\hspace{1.5em}$ | Acceleration of $\\bf{m_1}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $2T_1$ |$\\dfrac{g}{3}$|\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $2T_1$ | $\\dfrac{g}{2}$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{3T_1}{2}$ | $\\dfrac{g}{3}$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{3T_1}{2}$ | $\\dfrac{g}{2}$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nLet all masses be equal to $m$.\n\nFor the acceleration, we can assume all masses to be one system. There is a force of gravity of $2mg$ pulling the system one direction, and a force of gravity of $mg$ pulling the other direction.\n\n- $F_{net}=2mg-mg=mg$\n\nThe total mass of the system is $3m$.\n\nTherefore the acceleration of the system is the total mass of the system divided by the net force.\n\n- $a=\\dfrac{F_{net}}{m}=\\dfrac{mg}{3m}=\\dfrac{g}{3}$\n\nWhen Newton's second law is applied to $m_1$;\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\nma&=F_{net}\\\\\n\\hspace{2.3cm}\\\\\nm \\left( \\dfrac{g}{3} \\right) &=mg-T_1\\\\\n\\hspace{2.3cm}\\\\\nT_1&=\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)mg\\\\\n\\end{aligned}$\n\nWhen Newton's second law is applied to $m_3$\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\nT_2-mg&=m\\left(\\frac{g}{3}\\right)\\\\\n\\hspace{2.3cm}\\\\\nT_2&=\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)mg\\\\\n\\end{aligned}$\n\nTherefore\n\n$\\hspace{2cm} T_2=2T_1$"},{"ref":"PH0547","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0547\n\nA box is sliding down an incline at a constant speed of $2$ m s$^{-1}$. The angle of the incline is $\\theta$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-06-30%20at%205.28.45%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-30 at 5.28.45 PM\">\n\nThe magnitude of the total of the opposing forces is $16$ N. What is the force of gravity acting on the box?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}      \\dfrac{8}{\\sin\\theta}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}      \\dfrac{16}{\\sin\\theta}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}      \\dfrac{8}{\\cos\\theta}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}      \\dfrac{16}{\\cos\\theta}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe box is moving at a constant speed. According to Newton’s First Law, the resultant force acting on the box must be zero. The free body diagram of the forces acting on the box:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0547-1.png\" alt=\"PH0547-1\">\n\nWeight ($F_g$) has two components (shown in red): perpendicular to the slope and parallel to the slope.\n\nFrom the diagram we can use trigonometry to find these components, and set them equal to the forces that are acting opposite to them:\n\n- $F_g\\cos\\theta=F_N \\hspace{2em}$ and $\\hspace{2em}F_g\\sin\\theta=16$\n\nThe second equation is the important one here, because it shows the balances forces in the direction parallel to the slope.\n\nRearranging to solve for $F_g$ gives\n\n- $F_g=\\dfrac{16}{\\sin\\theta}$"},{"ref":"PH0548","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0548\n\nAn object starts to move in a straight line under the effect of two opposite forces of $12$ N and $16$ N.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0548.png\" alt=\"PH0548\">\n\nAfter $5$ s, the displacement of the object is $25$ m. What is the mass of the object?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  1$ kg\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  2$ kg\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  3$ kg\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  4$ kg","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFrom the equations of motion, the following expression can be written:\n\n- $s=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}at^2$\n\nThe object starts to move from rest, therefore u=0:\n\n- $25=\\dfrac{1}{2}(a{)(5)}^2$\n\n- $a=2$ m s$^{-2}$\n\nBy applying Newton’s Second Law to the object,\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\nF&=ma\\\\\n\\hspace{1.5em}16-12&=(m)(2)\\\\\nm&=2\\textrm{ kg}\\\\\n\\end{aligned}$"},{"ref":"PH0549","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0549\n\nA box of mass $3$ kg is placed on a box of mass $7$ kg. The coefficient of static friction between the surfaces of the boxes is 0.5. A horizontal force $F$ is applied to the $7$ kg box which sits on a frictionless surface.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0549.png\" alt=\"PH0549\">\n\nWhat is the maximum value of $F$ that can move the boxes without the $3$ kg box slipping?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$  1 N\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$  5 N\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$  10 N\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$  50 N","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe free-body diagrams of the boxes:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-07-08%20at%205.51.14%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-08 at 5.51.14 PM\">\n\nNote that it is friction from the bottom box that provides the net force that accelerates the $3$ kg box, and this same force of friction pulls backwards on the $7$ kg box.\n\nThe maximum value of the force of friction $F_f$ can be calculated as follows;\n\n- $F_f=\\mu_s F_N=\\mu_s mg=(0.5)(3)g=1.5g = 15$ N\n\nWhen Newton's Second Law is applied to the mass of $3$ kg;\n\n- $F_{net}=ma$\n\n- $1.5g=3a$\n\n- $0.5g=a$\n\nTo move without slipping, both of the masses must have the same acceleration. Treating both boxes as one system\n\n- $F_{net}=ma$\n\n- $F_{net}=(10)(0.5g)$\n\n- $F=5(10)=50$ N\n\nHence, the correct answer is D."},{"ref":"PH0550","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0550\n\nA ball of mass $m$ is falling vertically through the air. The drag force acting on the ball is given by $kv^2$ where $k$ is constant and $v$ is the speed of the ball. What is the maximum speed reached by the ball?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}      \\dfrac{k}{mg}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}      \\dfrac{mg}{k}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}      \\sqrt{\\dfrac{k}{mg}}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  \\sqrt{\\dfrac{mg}{k}}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nWhile the ball is falling, two opposite forces act on it. These are weight and drag. The speed of the ball increases until the drag force balances the weight. Therefore, the following expression can be written for maximum speed $v_{max}$:\n\n- $k(v_{max})^2=mg$\n\n- $(v_{max})^2=\\dfrac{mg}{k}$\n\n- $v_{max}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{mg}{k}}$\n\nHence, the correct answer is D."},{"ref":"PH0555","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0555\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n1. Define distance. [1 mark]\n\n2. An object moves in a straight line on a level road. The variation of the object's distance *d* with time *t* is shown on the graph below.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-07-02%20at%202.47.38%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-02 at 2.47.38 PM\">\n\n    1.     Describe the motion of the object between $t=0.5$ s and $t=1.0$ s. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Calculate the instantaneous speed of the object at $t=0.5$ s. [2 marks]\n\n    3.     On the axes below, sketch a possible graph of the variation of velocity $v$ of the object with time $t$. There is no need to add values to the axes. [2 marks]\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-07-02%20at%202.50.32%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-02 at 2.50.32 PM\">\n\n    4. Determine the direction of the change in momentum of the object during the motion. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Length of the path followed $\\checkmark$\n\n2.\n    1. Slows down/decelerates $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The slope of the graph gives the speed of the object. The slope decreases between $t=0.5$ s and  $t=1.0$ s. Therefore the object slows down.\n\n    2. Any evidence for drawing tangent at $t=0.5\\, s$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\ll\\dfrac{28-10}{1-0}=\\gg18\\pm2\\, m\\,s^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The gradient of the graph at the time of interest gives the instantaneous speed of the object. To find the gradient, the tangent line should be drawn.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshoot%202025-07-02%20at%202.52.52%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshoot 2025-07-02 at 2.52.52 PM\">\n\n        The gradient of the tangent line =$\\dfrac{28-10}{1-0}=18$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n    3. A straight or a curved line with a negative gradient $\\checkmark$\n\n        Starts from the y-intercept and ends up at the x-axis $\\checkmark$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-06-02%20at%2011.30.12%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-02 at 11.30.12 AM\">\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The gradient of the distance-time graph indicates the speed of the object. In one-dimensional motion, speed and velocity have the same magnitude. Since the gradient gets a smaller value with time and ends up with a zero value, velocity gets smaller and be zero at the end.\n\n        The exact mathematical shape of the distance-time curve is not known, therefore it is not possible to determine the exact shape of the velocity-time graph. A linear graph has been included here, but curvature in the graph is permitted.\n\n    4. The final momentum is less than the initial momentum $\\checkmark$\n\n        Opposite to the motion/backwards/negative $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The velocity of the object decreases. Therefore, the final momentum $p_f$ is less than the initial $p_i$ momentum. The change in momentum $\\Delta p$;\n\n        - $\\Delta p=p_f-p_i$\n\n        since $p_i$ > $p_f$, the result should be negative which indicates the direction of the change in momentum is opposite to the motion of the object."},{"ref":"PH0557","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0557\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nThe motion of an object released from a high place in a vacuum is observed. The variation of its velocity $v$ with time $t$ is recorded and the following graph is obtained.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-30%20at%209.27.44%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-30 at 9.27.44 AM\">\n\n1. Discuss the role that observations play in the development of scientific understanding. [1 mark]\n\n2. Other than velocity and time, list two quantities that can be obtained from the graph. [2 marks]\n\n3.\n    1.     The height of the release point is $70$ m. Show that the speed $v_1$ of the object just before the contact with the ground is $37$ m s$^{-1}$. [2 marks]\n\n    2. The object rebounds from the ground with a speed $v_2$ of $17$ m s$^{-1}$. The mass of the object is $8.0$ kg and the contact time during the rebound is $0.50$ s.\n\n       Calculate the average force acting on the object from the ground. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. They lead to the development of theories $\\checkmark$\n\n    - **OR**\n\n    They are used to construct or test a hypothesis $\\checkmark$\n\n    - **OR**\n\n    They are used to construct a mathematical relationship $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Observations are fundamental for many parts of physics. They help to understand the behaviour of nature. According to observations, mathematical relationships can be obtained, a hypothesis can be constructed or tested and new theories can be developed.\n\n2. acceleration $\\checkmark$\n\n    displacement/distance $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The slope of the velocity-time graph is equal to acceleration and the area under the graph gives the displacement. Since the motion happens in one dimension and one direction, the magnitude of displacement and distance are the same. The distance can be also obtained.\n\n3.\n    1. ${v_1}^2=0+(2)(-9.81)(-70)$ $\\checkmark$\\\n    *Accept using other uniform acceleration equations*\n\n        ${\\ll v}_1=\\gg$:answer[37.06]$\\ \\ll\\textrm{m s}^{-1}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The object falls with an acceleration of $-9.81$ m s$^{-2}$ and it is released from rest. The speed of the object can be found from\n\n        - $v^2=u^2+2as$\n\n        where $u=0$ and $s=-70$ m\n\n        - ${v_1}^2=0+(2)(-9.81)(-70)$\n\n        - ${v_1}^2=0+(2)(-9.81)(-70)$\n\n        - $v_1=37.06$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n    2. $p_{before} = 296$ kg m$s^{-1}$ and $p_{after}=136$ kg m s$^{-1}$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\Delta p =mv_2-\\ mv_1=136-\\left(-296\\right)=432$ kg m s$^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $F_{net} = \\dfrac{\\Delta p}{t}\\ =\\ \\dfrac{432}{0.50}\\ =\\ 864$ N $\\checkmark$\n\n        $F_N \\ll = F_{net}+mg=864+\\left(8\\right)\\left(9.81\\right)\\gg=$ :answer[942 N] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The momentum of the object before the collision with the ground\n\n        - $p_{before}=mv_1=\\left(8\\right)\\left(-37\\right)=\\ -296$ kg m s$^{-1}$\n\n        The momentum of the object after the collision with the ground\n\n        - $p_{after}=mv_2=\\left(8\\right)\\left(17\\right)=136$ kg m s$^{-1}$\n\n        Change in momentum $\\Delta p$ of the object\n\n        - $\\Delta p=mv_2-\\ mv_1=136-\\left(-296\\right)=432$ kg m s$^{-1}$\n\n        The resultant force $F_{net}$ acting on the object during the  rebound\n\n        - $F_{net}=\\dfrac{\\Delta p}{t}\\ =\\ \\dfrac{432}{0.50}\\ =\\ 864$ N\n\n        During the collision, two forces act on the object: contact and weight. They are in opposite directions. Therefore, the resultant force $F_{net}$;\n\n        $\\begin{aligned}\n        F_{net} &= 864\\textrm{ N} \\\\\n        F_N-W &= 864\\textrm{ N} \\\\\n        F_N-mg &= 864\\textrm{ N} \\\\\n        F_N-\\left(8\\right)\\left(9.81\\right) &=864\\textrm{ N}\\\\\n        F_N\\ &=864+\\left(8\\right)\\left(9.81\\right)\\\\\n        F_N\\ &=942\\textrm{ N}\\\\\n        \\end{aligned}$\n\n        Note that the force from the ground accomplishes two things here: it exerts a net force on the ball that causes its change in momentum and it also counteracts the force of gravity that acts downwards on the ball."},{"ref":"PH0560","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0560\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThree identical cylinders sit inside a cup. Their radius of each is 5 cm and their centres of mass are marked in the diagram. The lines connecting the centres of the masses pass through the contact points between the cylinders.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-30%20at%209.33.38%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-30 at 9.33.38 AM\">\n\n1. Draw and label the free-body diagram for the cylinder $M_3$ [2 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0560-A.png\" alt=\"PH0560-A\">\n\n2. 1. For analysis purposes, the three cylinders can at times be considered as a single object consisting of the mass of all of the cylinders. Explain why this simplification may be useful.  [1 mark]\n\n    2. The reaction force of the ground on $M_3$ is $30$ N. Show that the mass of each cylinder is around 1.0 kg. [2 marks]\n\n3. Determine the force that mass $M_3$ exerts on $M_1$ [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  $\\hspace{1em}$\n\n     <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-30%20at%209.34.36%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-30 at 9.34.36 AM\">\n\n    Correct direction and labeling of the forces $\\checkmark$\n\n    The reaction force of the ground is greater than the other forces $\\checkmark$\n\n2. 1. Interactions  between the cylinders can be ignored/it allows only external forces to be analyzed  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Every cylinder experiences forces due to their weights and forces from the other cylinders and the container walls. To decrease complexity in representing forces on the objects, the cylinders are simplified as combined to form one object so that forces between the cylinders can be ignored and only external forces can be considered.\n\n    2. Total weight of the cylinder = reaction of the ground on $M_3$\n\n        Weight of each cylinder = $\\dfrac{30}{3}=10\\ \\ll\\textrm{N}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $m=\\ll\\dfrac{10}{9.81}=\\gg$ :answer[$1.02\\ \\ll\\textrm{kg}\\gg$ ] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        When the three cylinders are considered as a single mass, the forces between the cylinders can be ignored. The vertical forces acting on the combined  masses are the weight $W_t$ of all discs and the *R* reaction force of the ground on the $M_3$.\n\n        - $W_t=R$\n\n        - $W_t=30$ N\n\n        Therefore, the weight of one disc $W$\n\n        - $W=\\dfrac{30}{3}=10$ N\n\n        So, the mass $m$ of one disc\n\n        - $m=\\dfrac{10}{9.81}=1.02$ kg\n\n3. The weight of the $M_1$ is balanced by the vertical component of the force from $M_3$ on $M_1$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    The reaction of $M_3$ on $M_1=\\ll\\dfrac{W}{\\cos\\theta}=\\dfrac{10}{0.87}=\\gg$ :answer[11.5 N] $\\checkmark$\n\n    **OR**\n\n    Reaction of the $M_3$ on the $M_1$ = Reaction of the $M_1$ on the $M_3$\n\n    **AND**\n\n    Reaction of the $M_2$ on the $M_3$ = Reaction of the $M_1$ on the $M_3$\n\n    Reaction=$\\ll\\dfrac{N-W}{2(\\cos\\theta)}=\\dfrac{30-10\\ }{\\left(2\\right)\\left(0.87\\right)}=\\gg11.5\\ \\ll\\textrm{N}\\gg$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The weight of $M_1$ is supported by the force of reaction $R_{31}$ of the $M_3$ on $M_1$. Therefore\n\n    $W(\\cos\\theta)=R_{31}$  where $\\theta$ is the angle shown on the diagram below.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-30%20at%209.48.31%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-30 at 9.48.31 AM\">\n\n    The length *L* can be found with the Pythagoras theorem\n\n    - $L=\\sqrt{{10}^2-5^2}=8.7$ cm\n\n    Finding $\\cos\\theta$\n\n    - $\\cos\\theta=\\dfrac{8.67}{10}=0.87$\n\n    - $R_{31}=\\dfrac{W}{\\cos\\theta}$\n\n    - $R_{31}=\\dfrac{10}{0.87}=11.5$ N\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Alternative solution}}$: \\\n    According to Newton\\'s Third Law, the force of reaction $R_{31}$ of the $M_3$ on $M_1$ equals the force of the reaction  $R_{13}$ of the $M_1$ on $M_3$. The vertical component of the $R_{13}$ can be found with the help of geometry\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-30%20at%209.48.35%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-30 at 9.48.35 AM\">\n\n    The length *L* can be found with the Pythagorean theorem\n\n    - $L=\\sqrt{{10}^2-5^2}=8.7$ cm\n\n    Finding $\\cos\\theta$\n\n    - $\\cos\\theta=\\dfrac{8.67}{10}=0.87$\n\n    According to Newton\\'s Third Law, the force of reaction $R_{31}$ of the $M_3$ on $M_1$ equals the force of the reaction  $R_{13}$ of the $M_1$ on $M_3$. The vertical component of the $R_{13}$ can be found with the help of geometry\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-30%20at%209.34.36%E2%80%AF0AM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-30 at 9.34.36 0AM\">\n\n    The reaction of $M_2$ on the $M_3$ and the reaction of $M_1$ on the $M_3$ must be equal to each other to cancel each other\\'s horizontal components. $M_3$ is in equilibrium and vertical forces acting on cancel each other\n\n    - $2(R_{13})(cos\\theta)+W=N$\n\n    - $R_{13}=\\dfrac{N-W}{2(cos\\theta)}=\\dfrac{30-10\\ }{(2)(0.87)}=11.5\\ N$"},{"ref":"PH0579","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0579\n\nThe graph below shows the variation of the resultant force $F$ acting on a box with time $t$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0579q.png\" alt=\"PH0579q\">\n\nThe box starts to move from rest. Which of the following graphs shows the variation of the momentum $p$ of the box with time $t$?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n|| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0579a.png\" alt=\"PH0579a\"> |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0579b.png\" alt=\"PH0579b\">|\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0579c.png\" alt=\"PH0579c\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0579d.png\" alt=\"PH0579d\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe area underneath the force-time graph gives the change in momentum of the box. Since the area increases with time at a constant rate, the momentum should increase at a constant rate. The only graph that satisfies this condition is the graph in A.\n\nTherefore, the answer is A.\n\nAlternatively, the impulse equation gives $\\Delta p = F \\Delta t$ .\n\nAs $F$ is a constant,  $\\Delta p$ is directly proportional to $t$ and a straight line through the origin is expected."},{"ref":"PH0580","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0580\n\nTwo blocks of masses $m$ and $2m$ approach each other and collide on a horizontal smooth surface. After the collision, they move together. The speeds of the blocks before the collision are $5v$ and $2v$ as shown.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-06-30%20at%205.36.05%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-30 at 5.36.05 PM\">\n\nWhich of the following correctly gives the speed of the blocks after the collision and the nature of the collision?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Speed after collision | Nature of collision |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $3v$ |Elastic |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{v}{3}$ | Elastic|\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $3v$ | Inelastic |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{v}{3}$ | Inelastic |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAccording to the principle of conservation of momentum, the total momentum before the collision equals the total momentum after the collision.\n\nAs the blocks are moving in opposite directions, a sign convention is introduced where motion to the right is positive.\n\n- $p_{initial} = p_{final}$\n\n- $m(5v)+2m\\left(-2v\\right)=3mv_f$  where $v_f$ is the speed of the blocks after the collision\n\n- $mv=3mv_f$\n\n- $\\dfrac{v}{3}=v_f$\n\nThe initial total kinetic energy of the blocks before the collision $E_{ki}$:\n\n- $E_{ki}=\\dfrac{1}{2}m{(5v)}^2+\\dfrac{1}{2}2m{(2v)}^2$\n\n- $E_{ki}=\\dfrac{1}{2}(33mv^2)$\n\nThe total kinetic energy of the blocks after the collision $E_{kf}$:\n\n- $E_{kf}=\\dfrac{1}{2}(3m){\\bigg(\\dfrac{v}{3}\\bigg)}^2$\n\n- $E_{kf}=\\dfrac{1}{6}mv^2$\n\nThe total kinetic energy is conserved in elastic collisions while it is not conserved in inelastic collisions.  Since the total kinetic energy after the collision is less than the total kinetic energy of the blocks before the collision, the collision is inelastic.\n\n(In any collision where objects “stick together” the situation is an inelastic collision.)"},{"ref":"PH0581","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0581\n\nAn egg hits a wall covered with a soft material and sticks to it. The graph shows the variation of horizontal force $F$ acting on the egg with time $t$ during the collision.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-30%20at%2012.31.57%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-30 at 12.31.57 PM\">\n\nThe soft material on the wall is removed and an identical egg with the same initial speed collides with and sticks to the wall. Which graph shows the variation of horizontal force $F$ acting on the second egg with time $t$ during the collision?  The graph for the first egg is shown as a dotted line.\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n||  <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-30%20at%2012.32.39o%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-30 at 12.32.39o PM\"> |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-30%20at%2012.32.44%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-30 at 12.32.44 PM\">|\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-30%20at%2012.32.48%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-30 at 12.32.48 PM\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-30%20at%2012.32.52%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-30 at 12.32.52 PM\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nDue to colliding with a hard surface instead of a soft surface, the time for the collision will be much less. Since the egg hits the wall with the same velocity and stops in both situations, the change in momentum of the egg is the same for both collisions. The area under a force-time graph gives the change in momentum for an object. Thus the areas under the graphs should be equal.\n\nBy using Newton’s second law\n\n- $F_{avg}=\\dfrac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t}$\n\n- $\\Delta t = \\dfrac{\\Delta p}{F_{avg}}$\n\nAccording to this relationship, a shorter time of collision will result in a greater average force and also greater peak force. This is shown in graph A."},{"ref":"PH0582","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0582\n\nA trolley is pushed in a level parking area of a market on a very rainy day. The rain fills the trolley at a rate of $R$ kg s$^{-1}$. Ignoring frictional forces, what is the force needed to move the trolley at a constant speed of $v$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}      \\ R v$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  \\dfrac{R}{v}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}      \\dfrac{v}{R}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}      R+v$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAs water enters the trolley, a force is needed to increase the water’s horizontal momentum so that it is moving at the same speed as the trolley.\n\nWe are given that the rate at which the water enters the trolley is $R$, so\n\n- $R=\\dfrac{\\Delta m}{\\Delta t}$\n\nThe force F acting on the trolley:\n\n- $F=\\dfrac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t}$\n\n- $F=\\dfrac{\\Delta (mv)}{\\Delta t}$\n\nSince $v$ is constant,\n\n- $F=v \\dfrac{\\Delta m}{\\Delta t} \\hspace{2em}$  where $\\hspace{2em}\\dfrac{\\Delta m}{\\Delta t}=R$\n\n- $F=R v$"},{"ref":"PH0583","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0583\n\nA rocket is moving in the absence of a gravitational field with constant speed. It starts to accelerate by burning fuel. At the start, the fuel contributes to most of the mass of the rocket. The burned fuel is ejected backwards from the rocket with a constant speed of $v$  relative to the rocket and a constant rate of $\\sigma\\ {kg\\,s}^{-1}$. Which graph shows the variation of acceleration $a$ of the rocket with time $t$ as most of the fuel is used?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n|| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0583a.png\" alt=\"PH0583a\"> |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0583b.png\" alt=\"PH0583b\">|\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0583c.png\" alt=\"PH0583c\"> |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0583d.png\" alt=\"PH0583d\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe resultant force $F_{net}$ acting on the space rocket:\n\n- $F_{net}=ma \\hspace{2em}$\n\nWhere $m$ is the mass of the rocket at any given time.\n\nWhen fuel is ejected from the rocket, it experiences a change in momentum in the frame of reference of the rocket.\n\nA given mass $m$ of fuel has its velocity change from 0 relative to the rocket to its ejected speed of $v$.\n\nTherefore the change of momentum of this mass of fuel is $p_2 - p_1 = mv - 0 = mv$\n\nThe force on the fuel causing this change in momentum is given by the equation\n\n$\\hspace{2em}F_{net}=\\dfrac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t}=\\dfrac{mv}{\\Delta t}$\n\nAnd we know that the rate of fuel ejected is\n$\\sigma$ which equal to $\\dfrac{m}{\\Delta t}$\n\nTherefore the net force on the ejected fuel is\n\n- $F_{net}=v\\sigma$\n\nTherefore\n\n- $a=\\dfrac{F_{net}}{M}=\\dfrac{v\\sigma}{M}$\n\nwhere $M$ is the total initial mass of the rocket and $v$ is the velocity of the ejected fuel relative to the rocket.\n\nThe mass $m_f$ of the burned fuel after the time $t$:\n\n- $m_f=\\sigma t$\n\nThe total mass of the rocket decreases over time due to burned fuel ejection, the acceleration of the rocket after the time $t$:\n\n- $a=\\dfrac{v\\sigma}{\\left(M-\\sigma t\\right)}$\n\nAccording to this relationship, acceleration increases at an increasing rate. So the answer is D.\n\nQualitatively, the total mass of the rocket is constantly decreasing due to the ejected fuel. As the fuel is ejected at a constant rate, there is a constant net force on the rocket. With a constant net force and decreasing mass, there will be an increasing acceleration. At the start, the change in mass will have little effect on the acceleration, but over time the effect of the decrease in mass will become more and more significant and result in greater and greater acceleration."},{"ref":"PH0584","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0584\n\nA jet of water with a cross sectional area of $A$ strikes perpendicular to a wall at a speed of $v$. After the collision, the water loses all of its horizontal speed and falls to the ground. The magnitude of the force created on the wall due to the water is $F$.\n\nWhich expression give the density of the water?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}      \\dfrac{F}{Av^2}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}      \\dfrac{F}{Av}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}     FAv$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}      FAv^2$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nConsidering the mass of water that strikes the wall in 1 second:\n\n- $m=\\rho V \\hspace{2em}$\n\nwhere $\\rho$ is the density of the water and $V$ is the volume of the water.\n\nAssigning the length of the column of water to be $l$ and cross sectional area $A$\n\n- $m=\\rho V=\\rho Al$\n\nBut $l = vt$ and $t=1$, therefore $l\\ =v$\n\nGiving\n\n- $m=\\rho Av$\n\nAfter the impact, the horizontal velocity of the jet is zero.\n\n- $F=\\dfrac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t}\\hspace{2em}$ and   $\\hspace{2em}\\Delta t = 1$\n\n- $F=\\dfrac{\\Delta (mv)}{1}=m\\Delta v$\n$ = (\\rho Av)v\\ =\\ \\rho Av^2$\n\nRearranging gives\n\n- $\\rho=\\dfrac{F}{Av^2}$"},{"ref":"PH0586","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0586\n\nA car of mass $m$ and a truck of mass $3m$ travelling in opposite directions collide and stick together. The speeds of the car and the truck just before the crash are $2v$ and $v$ respectively. What is their speed after the collision?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}      \\dfrac{1}{4}v$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}      \\dfrac{4}{5}v$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}      \\dfrac{5}{4}v$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}      4v $","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe vehicles move in opposite directions, therefore the total momentum before the collision is\n\n- $m(2v)+(-3m)v=-mv$\n\nBy the principle of conservation of momentum, the momentum of the vehicles after the collision is equal to the total momentum before the collision.\n\n- $-mv=\\left(m+3m\\right)v_f$\n\nwhere $v_f$ is the velocity of the vehicles after the collision and the total mass is $4m$.\n\n- $v_f=\\dfrac{-mv}{4m}$\n\n- $v_f=\\dfrac{-v}{4}$\n\nTherefore the speed is $\\dfrac{1}{4}v$."},{"ref":"PH0614","marks":"12","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0614\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\n1. 1. A spacecraft is moving in a zero gravity zone in space with constant speed. Its pilot decided to accelerate by burning fuel. The burned fuel is ejected backwards from the rocket with a constant speed of $2.5\\times{10}^2$ m s$^{-1}$ relative to the rocket at a constant rate of $2.0\\times{10}^2$ kg s$^{-1}$.\n\n        Show that the net force on the rocket due to the ejection of burnt fuel is $5 \\times 10^4$ N. [1 mark]\n\n    2. The spacecraft has a mass of $4.2\\times{10}^3$ kg at the instant that it starts to burn the fuel. Calculate the acceleration of the spacecraft after $1.0$ s has passed. [2 marks]\n\n2.  The spacecraft enters the atmosphere of Mars and slows down due to friction. To decelerate its speed more to reach a safe landing speed, the operator runs a propeller that pushes against the gas in the atmosphere.\n\n    1. By referring to Newton's Third Law, outline why there is a force acting on the spacecraft due to the propeller. [2 marks]\n\n    2.     The average density of the Mars atmosphere is $0.020$ kg m$^{-3}$. The radius of the propeller blades is $1.2$ m.  At a given instant, the average force from the propeller to slow down the spacecraft is $1.4$ kN.\n\n        Assuming that the propeller causes all gas that it passes through to come to rest relative to the falling spaceship, estimate the speed of the spaceship at this instant. [4 marks]\n\n    3.  The landing legs of the spacecraft have springs that can compress as the spacecraft makes contact with the ground. Explain how this decreases the average force acting on the spacecraft as it lands. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. $F=\\dfrac{\\Delta(mv)}{\\Delta t} = \\dfrac{\\Delta m}{\\Delta t} v = (2.0\\times{10}^2\\ \\ )(2.5\\times{10}^2) = 5.0 \\times 10^4$ << N >>$\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    From Newton's 3rd Law, the force of the rocket on the gases is equal to the force of the gases on the rocket.\n\n        For the gases, the net force is equal to the rate of change of momentum.\n\n        Substituting in the values will lead the net force\n\n        $\\begin{aligned}\n    F&=\\dfrac{\\Delta(mv)}{\\Delta t}\\\\\n     &= \\dfrac{\\Delta m}{\\Delta t} v \\\\\n     &= (2.0\\times{10}^2\\ \\ )(2.5\\times{10}^2) \\\\\n     &= 5.0 \\times 10^4\\textrm{ N}\\\\\n     \\end{aligned}$\n\n    2. Mass of the rocket at $t=1$ s is\n\n        - $4200-\\left(2.0\\times{10}^2\\right)\\left(1.0\\right)=4.0\\times{10}^3$ kg $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $a=\\dfrac{F_{net}}{m}=\\dfrac{5\\times{10}^4}{4.0\\times{10}^3} = $ :answer[$12.5$ m s$^{-2}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The total mass $m_t$ of the rocket after $1$ s:\n\n        - $m_t=4200-\\left(2.0\\times{10}^2\\right)\\times\\left(1.0\\right)=4.0\\times{10}^3$ kg\n\n        To find the acceleration of the rocket:\n\n        - $a=\\dfrac{F_{net}}{m}=\\dfrac{5\\times{10}^4}{4.0\\times{10}^3}=12.5$ m s$^{-2}$\n\n2. 1. Propeller exerts a force on the gas $\\checkmark$\n\n        There is an equal and opposite force from the gas on the propeller $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The gas in the atmosphere experiences a force due to the propeller blades. According to Newton's Third Law, a force with the same magnitude and opposite direction acts on the spacecraft.\n\n    2. $F_{av}=\\dfrac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t}{} = m\\dfrac{\\Delta v}{\\Delta t}\\, \\checkmark$\n\n        $m=\\rho V=\\rho Al=\\rho\\pi r^2vt \\, \\checkmark$\n\n        $v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{F_{av}}{\\rho\\pi r^2}}\\ \\, \\checkmark$\n\n        = :answer[$120$ m s$^{-1}$] $\\, \\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The distance $l$ covered by the spacecraft in a short time of $t$:\n\n        $l=vt$   where $v$ is the average speed of the spacecraft\n\n        The mass $m$ of the gas pushed during the time of $t$:\n\n        $m=\\rho V=\\rho Al=\\rho\\pi r^2vt$ where $r$ is the radius of the blades\n\n        The mass experiences a change of velocity of $v$, as relative to the aircraft it is initially moving at speed $v$ and comes to rest due to the propellers.\n\n        The average force $F_{av}$ needed from the propeller;\n\n        $\\begin{aligned}\n        F_{av}&=\\dfrac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t}\\\\\n        \\hspace{1.3cm}\\\\\n        &=\\dfrac{m\\Delta v}{\\Delta t}\\\\\n        \\hspace{1.3cm}\\\\\n        &= \\dfrac{\\rhoπr^2vt\\Delta v}{\\Delta t}\\\\\n        \\hspace{1.3cm}\\\\\n        &=\\rhoπr^2v^2\\\\\n        \\end{aligned}$\n\n        Rearranging gives\n\n        $\\hspace{1.12cm}v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{F_{av}}{\\rho\\pi r^2}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1.4\\times{10}^3}{(0.020)\\pi{(1.2)}^2}}=120$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n    3. $\\Delta p=F_{av}\\Delta t$   $\\, \\checkmark$\n\n        duration of the collision is increased $\\, \\checkmark$\n\n        Change in momentum is the same so the average force is less $\\, \\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The time of collision is increased with using soft material.  By using Newton’s second law;\n\n        - $F_{av}=\\dfrac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t}$\n\n        For the same magnitude of the change in momentum, the average force acting on the object is less when the time of collision is increased."},{"ref":"PH0629","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0629\n\nA car makes a U-turn on a flat horizontal road at a speed of 9 m s$^{-1}$ as shown in the diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-05-30%20at%205.29.58%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-30 at 5.29.58 PM\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Images by OpenClipart-Vectors from Pixabay - https ://pixabay.com]}}$\n\nThe inner radius of the U-turn, $r_1$, is $4$ m and its outer radius, $r_2$  is $10$ m.\n\nWhat is the best estimate for minimum acceleration of the car when making the U-turn?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $0.9$ m s$^{-2}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2.2$ m s$^{-2}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $8.1$ m s$^{-2}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $20.2$ m s$^{-2}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nCentripetal acceration is given by\n\n- $a=\\dfrac{v^2}{r}$\n\nThe minimum acceleration is at the largest radius.\n\nThe centripetal acceleration:\n\n- $a=\\dfrac{9^2}{10}=8.1$ m s$^{-2}$"},{"ref":"PH0630","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0630\n\nAn airplane in straight level flight experiences a lift force $L$ that is proportional to the speed of the plane and is perpendicular to the axis of the wings and as shown in diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0630zza.png\" alt=\"PH0630zza\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Image created using Images by Alvin Hikmawan from Pixabay - https ://pixabay.com]}}$\n\nOn a banked turn at the same speed, the wings make a small angle $\\theta$ with the horizontal.\n\nWhich of the following is the best estimate of the centripetal force on the airplane?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $L$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $L\\cos\\theta$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $L\\sin\\theta\\ $\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $W\\cos\\theta\\ $","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nNote that the angle that the wings make with the horizontal is equivalent to the angle that the tail of the plane makes with the vertical.\n\nOn the turn, the lift force makes an angle of $\\theta$ with the vertical.\n\nThe centripetal force will act towards the centre of the circular path. In this case, it is the horizontal component of the lifting force.\n\nThis is given by $L\\sin\\theta$\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-04-07%20at%2011.23.51%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-07 at 11.23.51 AM\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Image created using Images by Alvin Hikmawan from Pixabay - https ://pixabay.com]}}$"},{"ref":"PH0631","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0631\n\nA jet aircraft is performing a “loop-the-loop” stunt in which it moves in a vertical circular path as shown in the diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH00631zz.png\" alt=\"PH00631zz\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Image created using Images by nnaakk from Pixabay - https ://pixabay.com]}}$\n\nWhich of the following represents the free body diagram of the pilot at the top of the loop?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n|| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0631az.png\" alt=\"PH0631az\"> |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0631bz.png\" alt=\"PH0631bz\"> |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0631cz.png\" alt=\"PH0631cz\"> |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0631dz.png\" alt=\"PH0631dz\">|\n\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Image created using Images by OpenClipart-Vectors from Pixabay - https ://pixabay.com]}}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThere are two forces acting on the pilot which are the gravitational force (weight) and the normal force of the pilot\\'s chair.\n\nThe normal force always acts from the chair onto the person. At the top of the loop, this will act vertically downwards.\n\nThe weight is always vertically downwards."},{"ref":"PH0632","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0632\n\nAn object moves in a circular path of radius $2$ m with angular displacement of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ rad in $5$ s.\n\nWhat is the average linear speed of the object?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{\\pi}{20}$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{\\pi}{10}$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}$ m s$^{-1}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe linear speed is given by\n\n- $v=r\\omega$\n\nand\n\n- $\\omega=\\dfrac{\\Delta \\theta }{\\Delta t}$\n\nThen:\n\n- $v = (r) \\dfrac{\\Delta \\theta}{\\Delta t} = (2) \\dfrac{(\\frac{\\pi}{2})}{5} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{5}$ m s$^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH0633","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0633\n\nA CD stores data on its surface on a number of concentric circular tracks of different radii. The radius of the first track is 25 mm and that of the last track is 60 mm as shown in the diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0633.png\" alt=\"PH0633\">\n\nThe data from the CD is read by rotating the disk under a laser system where the tangential speed is always 1.5 m s$^{-1}$ for the track being read.\n\nWhich of the following is correct about the angular speed of the first track $\\omega_1$ and the angular speed of the last track $\\omega_2$?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\omega_1$ | $\\omega_2$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ $60$ rad s$^{-1}$ $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Same as $\\omega_1$ |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $60$ rad s$^{-1}$ | Less than $\\omega_1$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $25$ rad s$^{-1}$ | Same as $\\omega_1$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $25$ rad s$^{-1}$ |$\\hspace{1.5em}$ Greater than $\\omega_1$ $\\hspace{1.5em}$|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nSince the tangential speed is constant for all tracks, then the angular speed is different for each track.\n\nThe angular speed of the first track:\n\n- $\\omega_1=\\dfrac{v}{r_1}$\n\n- $\\omega_1=\\dfrac{1.5}{25\\times{10}^{-3}}$\n\n$\\hspace{2.9em}$ $=60$ rad s$^{-1}$\n\nThe angular speed of the last track will be less than that of the first track since the angular speed is inversely proportional to the radius of rotation."},{"ref":"PH0635","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0635\n\nA ball of mass $0.4$ kg is attached to a string of length $0.2$ m and is moving in a vertical circular path with tangential speed of $\\sqrt3$ m s$^{-1}$ at the instant shown in the diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0635.png\" alt=\"PH0635\">\n\nGiven that $\\sin(60\\degree)=\\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}$ and $\\cos(60\\degree)=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, what is the tension in the string at the shown position?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $(6+2\\sqrt3)$ N\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 8 N\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 4 N\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $(6-2\\sqrt3)$ N","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\\\nThe ball is rotating in a vertical circular path, then the forces acting on it are the tension of the string and the weight.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0635a.png\" alt=\"PH0635a\">\n\nThe centripetal force acting on the ball is the vector sum of the tension of the string and the component of the weight that is the same direction as the string\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $F_c=T-mg\\cos(60\\degree)$\n\nThe centripetal force\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $F_c=\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}$\n\nThen\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $T=\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}+mg\\cos(60\\degree)$\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $T=\\dfrac{(0.4){(\\sqrt3)}^2}{0.2}+\\left(0.4\\right)\\left(10\\right)\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$\n\n$\\hspace{2.9em}$ = $8$ N"},{"ref":"PH0637","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0637\n\nA roller coaster car of mass $m$ approximates circular motion at point $A$ as shown in the diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0637.png\" alt=\"PH0637\">\n\nThe circular radius of the track curvature at point $A$ is $r$ and the track exerts a normal reaction force $F_N$ on the car.\n\nWhich expression gives the linear speed of the car at point $A$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\sqrt{2rg}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{r(F_N-mg)}{m}}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{r(mg-F_N)}{m}}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe centripetal force at point $A$ is the vector sum of the weight of the car and the normal reaction force of the track\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $F_{centripetal}=mg-F_N$\n\nAnd\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $F_{centripetal}=\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}$\n\nThen\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}=mg-F_N$\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{r(mg-F_N)}{m}}$"},{"ref":"PH0638","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0638\n\nA ball is tied to a $10\\,$cm rope and rotates in a horizontal circular path starting from point X with a linear speed of 20 cm s$^{-1}$. The rope is cut after 2 s, at which time the object is at point Y as shown in the diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0638oo.png\" alt=\"PH0638oo\">\n\nWhich of the following is correct about the angular displacement of the ball between $X$ and $Y$ and the direction of motion of the ball after the rope is cut?\n\n| | Angular Displacement | Direction of Motion |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| 2 rad | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-07-03%20at%204.53.36%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-03 at 4.53.36 PM\">\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 2 rad |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-07-03%20at%204.53.47%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-03 at 4.53.47 PM\"> |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 4 rad |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-07-03%20at%204.53.55%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-03 at 4.53.55 PM\">  |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 4 rad |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-07-03%20at%204.54.07%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-03 at 4.54.07 PM\"> |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nNote that although the direction is not stated in the question, from determining the angular displacement, the direction it is traveling can be determined.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\omega=\\dfrac{v}{r}$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\omega=\\dfrac{20}{10}=$ 2 rad s$^{-1}$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\omega =\\dfrac{\\Delta \\theta}{\\Delta t}$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\Delta \\theta = \\omega \\Delta t = (2)(2) = 4$ rad\n\nBecause one full circle is $2 \\pi$ radians, we can see that $\\Delta \\theta$ is approximately $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ of a full circle. Therefore, it must be moving clockwise around the circle. This allows us to determine that the tangential velocity at that point in time must be up and to the right."},{"ref":"PH0641","marks":"12","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0641\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nA washing machine contains a cylinder that carries clothes and rotates at constant angular speed about a horizontal axis as shown in the diagram. Frictional forces act between the clothes and the inner surface of the cylinder.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202026-11-04%20at%208.24.54%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-11-04 at 8.24.54 AM\">\n\nThe radius of the cylinder is 0.27 m and the linear speed of its inner surface is 1.60 m s$^{-1}$.\n\n1.  Draw the free body diagram for the piece of clothing at the position shown. [2 marks]\n\n2.  The cylinder continues turning until the piece of clothing is at a position that makes an angle of $\\phi$ to the horizontal as shown.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202026-11-04%20at%208.25.25%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-11-04 at 8.25.25 AM\">\n\n    $\\\\$\n    1.  With use of a free body diagram, show that the centripetal force on the piece of clothing at this instant is given by the expression $F_c = F_N + mg\\sin\\phi$. [2 marks]\n\n    2.  Calculate the angular speed, expressing your answer to the correct number of significant figures. [2 marks]\n\n    3.  Show that the angle $\\phi$ at which the piece of clothing loses contact with the cylinder is $75\\degree$. Assume the clothing does not slip relative to the cylinder. [3 marks]\n\n    4.  Determine the time taken for a piece of clothing to move from the lowest point of the cylinder (point A) to the point where it loses contact. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  :::center\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202026-11-04%20at%208.26.55%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-11-04 at 8.26.55 AM\">\n\n    Weight vertically down **and** normal force towards the centre $\\checkmark$\\\n    Friction vertically upwards, roughly the same length as the weight $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Weight always acts downwards and the normal force acts perpendicular to the surface. The speed is constant, so the only acceleration is the centripetal acceleration, which is horizontal at this position. There is therefore no vertical resultant force, so friction must act upwards and be equal in size to the weight.\n\n2.  1.  :::center\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202026-11-04%20at%208.26.59%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-11-04 at 8.26.59 AM\">\n\n        Force diagram showing weight vertically down, normal force towards the centre and friction along the tangent $\\checkmark$\\\n        Component of weight towards the centre $= mg\\sin\\phi$, **and** $F_N + mg\\sin\\phi = F_c$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The normal force always acts perpendicular to the surface and towards the centre, friction parallel to the surface and gravitational force down. Because friction has no component towards the centre, it does not contribute to the centripetal force. The component of the gravitational force that points towards the centre does contribute to the centripetal force. This can be found from $mg\\sin\\phi$. Adding this to the normal force gives the centripetal force.\n\n    2.  ::indent\n        - $\\omega = \\ll\\dfrac{v}{r}\\gg$\n\n        - $\\omega = \\dfrac{1.60}{0.27} =$ :answer[$5.9$ $\\ll$rad s$^{-1}$$\\gg$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\\\$\n\n        2 significant figures $\\checkmark$\n\n    3.  Normal force is zero $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\sin\\phi = \\ll\\dfrac{v^2}{rg}\\gg = \\dfrac{(1.60)^2}{(0.27)(9.8)}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\phi =$ :answer[$75.4\\degree$] $\\approx 75\\degree$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\\\$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        When the piece of clothing loses contact, the normal force of the cylinder on the clothing is zero.\n\n        The centripetal force in this case is only the component of the gravitational force towards the centre of the circular path.\n\n        - $mg\\sin\\phi = \\dfrac{mv^2}{r}$\n\n        - $\\sin\\phi = \\dfrac{mv^2}{mrg}$\n\n        Then\n\n        - $\\phi = \\sin^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{v^2}{rg}\\right) = \\sin^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{(1.60)^2}{(0.27)(9.8)}\\right) = 75.4\\degree$\n\n        $\\\\$\n\n    4.  The angular displacement is 2.9 rad $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $t = \\ll\\dfrac{\\theta}{\\omega}\\gg = \\dfrac{2.9}{5.9}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $t =$ :answer[$0.49$ $\\ll$s$\\gg$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\\\$\n\n        Allow ECF for use of $\\omega$ value from (b)(ii)\n\n        $\\\\$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Angular displacement is $90\\degree + 75\\degree = 165\\degree$\n\n        In radians, this gives\n\n        - $\\dfrac{(165)(\\pi)}{180} = 2.9$ rad\n\n        - $t = \\dfrac{\\theta}{\\omega} = \\dfrac{2.9}{5.9}$\n\n        - $t = 0.49$ s\n\n        $\\\\$"},{"ref":"PH0642","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0642\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nA conical pendulum consists of a mass $m$ tied to a $22$ cm string that makes an angle $\\theta$ with the vertical as shown in the diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH06421a.png\" alt=\"PH06421a\">\n\n- Mass of the bob = $8.0$ g\n\n- Angle with the vertical $\\theta = 20\\degree$\n\n1. Draw the free body diagram of the bob in the given position.            [2 marks]\n\n2. 1. Show that angular velocity $\\omega$ is given by\n$\\omega=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g\\tan\\theta\\ }{r}}$\n[2 marks]\n\n    2. Calculate the linear velocity of the bob. [2 marks]\n\n    3. Determine the tension in the string.                    [2 marks]\n\n3.  1. Explain the effect of increasing the angular speed ω on the angle θ.     [3 marks]\n\n    2. The maximum tension the string can provide is $1.5$ N.\n\n        Determine the maximum angular speed. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.    $\\hspace{2.3cm}\\\\$\n\n      <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0642-2a.png\" alt=\"PH0642-2a\">\n\n        Gravitational force vertically downwards. $\\checkmark$\n\n        Tension force in the direction of the string $\\checkmark$\n\n2. 1. $\\underline{\\textrm{Alternative 1}}$\n\n        - $m\\omega^2r=T\\sin\\theta$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $T=\\dfrac{mg}{\\cos\\theta\\ }$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Substitution of second equation into first shown to obtain the correct expression $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Alternative 2}}$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}=T\\sin\\theta$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $T=\\dfrac{mg}{\\cos\\theta }$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Substitution of second equation into first shown to obtain the correct expression $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\omega=\\ll\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g\\tan\\theta\\ }{r}}\\gg$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The centripetal force is the horizontal component of the tension.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-06-02%20at%208.23.37%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-02 at 8.23.37 AM\">\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Alternative 1}}$\n\n        - $F_c=m\\omega^2r=T\\sin\\theta\\ $\n\n        and\n\n        - $T=\\dfrac{mg}{\\cos\\theta\\ }$\n\n        Then\n\n        - $m\\omega^2r=\\dfrac{mg}{\\cos\\theta\\ } {\\sin}\\theta\\ $\n\n        - $\\omega^2=\\dfrac{g\\,{\\tan}\\ \\theta\\ }{r}$\n\n        - $\\omega=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g\\,{\\tan}\\ \\theta\\ }{r}}$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Alternative 2}}$\n\n        - $F_c=\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}=T\\sin\\theta$\n\n        and\n\n        - $T\\cos\\theta\\ =mg$\n\n        - $T=\\dfrac{mg}{\\cos\\theta}$\n\n        Then\n\n        - $\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}=\\dfrac{mg}{{\\cos} \\,\\theta}\\sin\\theta\\ $\n\n        - $v^2=rg\\tan\\theta\\ $\n\n        - $v=\\sqrt{rg\\tan\\theta\\ }$\n\n        The angular speed is given by\n\n        - $\\omega=\\dfrac{v}{r}$\n\n        Then\n\n        - $\\omega=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{rg\\tan\\theta }}{r}$\n\n        - $\\omega=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g\\tan\\theta }{r}}$\n\n        2.    $r = 22 \\,sin (20\\degree)=7.5$ cm $\\checkmark$\n\n                $v=\\ll\\sqrt{rg\\tan\\theta\\ }\\gg$\n\n                $=\\sqrt{(7.5\\times{10}^{-2})(9.81)(\\tan20 })  $\n\n                or\n\n                $\\omega=\\ll\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g\\tan\\theta}{r}}\\gg$\n\n                $=\\ll\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\left(9.81\\right)\\left(\\tan20 \\right)}{\\left(7.5\\times{10}^{-2}\\right)}}\\gg$\n\n                $=\\ll6.9\\textrm{ rad s}^{-1}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n                $v=\\ll\\omega r\\gg=(6.9\\ )(7.5\\times{10}^{-2})$\n\n                $v=$ :answer[0.52] $ \\ll$ m s$^{-1}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n                $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n                - $r = 22 \\sin (20\\degree)=7.5$ cm\n\n                - $F_c=\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}=T\\sin\\theta$\n\n                and\n\n                - $T\\cos\\theta=mg$\n\n                - $T=\\dfrac{mg}{\\cos\\theta}$\n\n                Then\n\n                - $\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}=\\dfrac{mg}{\\cos\\theta}\\sin\\theta$\n\n                - $v^2=rg\\tan\\theta$\n\n                - $v=\\sqrt{rg\\tan\\theta}$\n\n                - $=\\sqrt{(7.5\\times{10}^{-2})(9.81)(\\tan20 })  $\n\n                - $=0.52$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n                or\n\n                - $\\omega=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g\\tan\\theta}{r}}$\n\n                - $=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\left(9.81\\right)\\left(\\tan20 \\right)}{\\left(7.5\\times{10}^{-2}\\right)}}$\n\n                - $=6.9$ rad s$^{-1}$\n\n                - $v=\\omega r=(6.9\\ )(7.5\\times{10}^{-2})$\n\n                - $v= 0.52$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n        3.    $\\underline{\\textrm{Alternative 1}}$\n\n                - $T=\\ll\\dfrac{mv^2}{r\\sin\\theta}\\gg=\\dfrac{(8.0\\times{10}^{-3}){(0.52)}^2}{(7.5\\times{10}^{-2})(\\sin20)\\ }$ $\\checkmark$\n\n                - $T=$ :answer[0.08] $\\ll$ N $\\gg $ $\\checkmark$\n\n                $\\underline{\\textrm{Alternative 2}}$\n\n                - $T=\\ \\ll\\dfrac{mg}{\\cos\\theta}\\gg$\n                $=\\dfrac{(8.0\\times{10}^{-3})(9.81)}{\\cos20}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n                - $T=$ :answer[0.08] $\\ll$ N $\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n                $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n                $\\underline{\\textrm{Alternative 1}}$\n\n                Since\n\n                - $\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}=T\\sin\\theta$\n\n                Then\n\n                - $T=\\dfrac{mv^2}{r\\sin\\theta}$\n\n                - $T=\\dfrac{(8\\times{10}^{-3}){(0.52)}^2}{(10\\times{10}^{-2})(\\sin20)}=0.08$ N\n\n                $\\underline{\\textrm{Alternative 2}}$\n\n                - $T\\cos\\theta=mg$\n\n                - $T=\\dfrac{mg}{\\cos\\theta}$\n\n                - $T=\\dfrac{(8\\times{10}^{-3})(9.81)}{\\cos20}=0.08$ N\n\n3. 1. The vertical component of the tension force remains constant, as it is equal to the weight of the bob. $\\checkmark$\n\n        The horizontal component of the tension provides the centripetal force, which increases as $\\omega$ increases. $\\checkmark$\n\n        Because the horizontal component increases while the vertical component stays constant, the angle $\\theta$ will increase with $\\omega$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-06-02%20at%208.21.52%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-02 at 8.21.52 AM\">\n\n        - $T\\cos\\theta=mg$\n\n        and\n\n        - $T\\sin\\theta=F_c$\n\n        Therefore,\n\n        - $tan\\theta=\\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}=\\dfrac{F_c}{mg}$\n\n        Because $mg$ is constant and $F_c$ is increasing as ω increases,\n\n        $\\tan\\theta$ and therefore $\\theta$ increase as $\\omega$ increases.\n\n   2. $mg = (0.008)(9.8) = 0.08$ N\n\n        0.08 N $\\ll$ 1.5 N\n\n        Therefore centripetal force is equal to the tension force/rope is almost horizontal/θ very close to $90\\degree$. $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\omega=\\ll\\sqrt{\\dfrac{T}{mr}}\\gg$\n\n        - $\\omega=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1.5}{\\left(8\\times{10}^{-3}\\right)\\left(22\\times{10}^{-2}\\right)}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\omega=$ :answer[29.2] $\\ll\\textrm{ rad s}^{-1}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Gravitational force is\n\n        - $mg = (0.008)(9.8) = 0.08$ N\n\n        The maximum tension is 1.5 N, which is the resultant of the vertical component of tension (mg) and the horizontal component of the tension, $F_c$.\n\n        Therefore,\n\n        - ${1.5}^2={0.08}^2+\\ {F_c}^2$\n\n        Solving gives\n\n        - $F_c=\\ 1.49787\\ =\\ 1.5$ N\n\n        This shows that the centripetal force is effectively equal to the tension force and the string is very close to horizontal.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-07-08%20at%203.58.49%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-08 at 3.58.49 PM\">\n\n        - $m\\omega^2r=T$\n\n        - $\\omega=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{T}{mr}}$\n\n        - $\\omega=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1.5}{(8\\times{10}^{-3})(22\\times{10}^{-2})}}=29.2$ rad s$^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH0651","marks":"13","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0651\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\nThe rotor ride in the amusement park consists of a vertical cylinder that rotates around its axis. Its floor drops down when it rotates fast enough, so that a person inside it is held against its walls. The walls are made of rubberized material such that the coefficient of static friction $\\mu <1$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-06-02%20at%208.40.27%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-02 at 8.40.27 AM\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Image created using Images by  Clker-Free-Vector-Images from Pixabay - https://pixabay.com/]}}$\n\n1. Explain in terms of forces how a person can remain suspended against the wall without falling when the floor is dropped. [3 marks]\n\n2.\n    1. Show that the maximum period of rotation for the person to remain at rest relative to the wall is given by\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $T=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{4\\pi^2\\mu r}{g}}$\n\n        Where\\\n        $r$ = radius of the cylinder\\\n        $\\mu$ = coefficient of static friction between the wall and the person [3 marks]\n\n    2. The radius of the rotor is 4.5 m and the coefficient of static friction of the walls is 0.40.\n\n        Calculate the angular speed of the cylinder necessary for this situation. [3 marks]\n\n3.\n    1. To make the ride more exciting, an idea of tilting the axis of the rotor by an angle $\\theta$ is discussed.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-06-02%20at%208.38.35%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-02 at 8.38.35 AM\">\n\n        Draw and label the free body diagram of a person at point **A** when the ride is in operation. [1 mark]\n\n    2. The cylinder is tilted a full 90$\\degree$ so that its axis of rotation is now horizontal. Deduce whether the minimum speed of rotation for this orientation is greater than or less than the minimum speed for the original situation with a vertical axis of rotation. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. The normal force provides the centripetal force, $\\checkmark$\n\n    Minimum speed is required / relation between normal force and linear speed. $\\checkmark$\n\n    Friction balances the weight. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The friction force of the wall on the person is affected by the normal force\n\n    - $\\hspace{1em}$ $F_f=\\mu\\ F_N$\n\n    The normal force of the walls in this situation is the centripetal force, which means it is affected by the linear velocity\n\n     - $\\hspace{1em}$ $F_N=F_c=\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}$\n\n    This means that, there\\'s a minimum speed at which the magnitude of the normal force will be enough so that the friction force of the walls will balance the weight of the person and keep it at rest without falling.\n\n    The person\\'s weight is in equilibrium with the friction force of the wall.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0651aaaa.png\" alt=\"PH0651aaaa\">\n\n2.\n    1. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ $F_f=\\mu\\ F_N=\\mu\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $F_f\\ = mg$ therefore $v^2=\\dfrac{rg}{\\mu}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $T=\\dfrac{2\\pi r}{v} \\dfrac{v^2}{r} = \\dfrac{4 \\pi^{2r}}{T^2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        substitute and solve to get answer\n\n        - $\\ll\\ T=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{4\\pi^2\\mu r}{g}}\\gg $\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The normal force is the centripetal force\n\n        - $F_N=\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}$\n\n        And the friction force is given by\n\n        - $F_f=\\mu\\ F_N$\n\n        Then\n\n        - $F_f=\\mu\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}$\n\n        Since the friction force is in equilibrium with the weight\n\n        - $F_f=mg$\n\n        Then\n\n       $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\begin{aligned}\n\\mu\\frac{mv^2}{r}\\ &=mg\\\\\n\\mu\\frac{v^2}{r}\\ &=g\\\\\nv^2\\ &=\\frac{rg}{\\mu}\\\\\nv\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{rg}{\\mu}}\\\\\n\\end{aligned}$\n\n        Since the linear speed is given by\n\n        - $v=\\dfrac{2\\pi r}{T}$\n\n        Then\n\n        - $T=\\dfrac{2\\pi r}{v}=2\\pi\\ r\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\mu}{rg}}$\n\n        - $T=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{4\\pi^2\\mu r}{g}}$\n\n        ***OR***\n\n        Since\n\n        - $\\dfrac{v^2}{r}=\\dfrac{4\\pi^2r}{T^2}$\n\n        Then\n\n        - $T^2=\\dfrac{4\\pi^2r^2}{v^2}=\\dfrac{4\\pi^2r^2\\mu}{rg}$\n\n        - $T=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{4\\pi^2\\mu r}{g}}$\n\n    2. ::indent\n        - $T=\\ll\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\left(4{)(\\pi}^2\\right)\\left(0.40\\right)\\left(4.5\\right)}{\\left(9.8\\right)}}\\gg=2.69\\ \\ll\\textrm{ s}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\omega=\\ll\\dfrac{2\\pi}{T}\\gg$\n\n        - $\\omega=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{2.69}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - :answer[$\\omega=2.33\\ \\ll\\textrm{ rad s}^{-1}\\gg$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The angular speed is related to the linear speed by\n\n        - $v=\\omega\\ r$\n\n        And\n\n        - $v=\\dfrac{2\\pi r}{T}$\n\n        Then\n\n        - $\\omega=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{T}$\n\n        Since\n\n        - $T=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{4\\pi^2\\mu r}{g}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\left(4{)(\\pi}^2\\right)\\left(0.40\\right)\\left(4.5\\right)}{\\left(9.8\\right)}}=2.69$ s\n\n        Then\n\n        - $\\omega=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{2.69}=2.33$ rad s$^{-1}$\n\n3.\n    1. Correct orientation **AND** labelling of vectors. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0651c_MS.png\" alt=\"PH0651c_MS\">\n\n    2. Horizontal axis of rotation: $v^2  = rg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Vertical axis of rotation: $v^2  = \\dfrac{(rg)}{\\mu}$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\mu < 1$\\,\n        therefore vertical minimum speed is greater than the horizontal minimum speed of rotation. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        For the horizontal axis of rotation, the minimum speed occurs when the centripetal acceleration at the top of the motion is equal to the acceleration due to gravity, resulting in a normal force of zero.\n\n        - $\\dfrac{v^2}{r} = g$\n\n        - $v^2  = rg$\n\n        For the vertical axis,\n\n        - $\\dfrac{mv^2}{r} = F_N$\n\n        - $F_F = \\mu F_N = mg$ therefore $F_N= \\dfrac{mg}{\\mu}$\n\n        Which gives\n\n        - $\\dfrac{mv^2}{r} = \\dfrac{mg}{\\mu}$\n\n        - $v^2  = \\dfrac{(rg)}{\\mu}$\n\n        Because the question states that $\\mu < 1$\n\n        The minimum speed for the vertical rotational axis is greater than the minimum speed for the horizontal axis of rotation."},{"ref":"PH0658","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0658\n\nA mass of $400$ g is connected to a spring on a frictionless surface.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0658-3.png\" alt=\"PH0658-3\">\n\nThe spring constant is $0.06\\ $N cm$^{-1}$. What is the magnitude of the acceleration when the mass is 20 cm from equilibrium?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $0.03\\ $m s$^{-2}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $0.06\\ $m s$^{-2}$\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $0.6\\ $m s$^{-2}$\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $3\\ $m s$^{-2}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nNote that because the length units on the spring constant (cm) match the units on the extension of the spring (cm), the force from the spring in N can be found by simply multiplying the two terms.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $F_{net}=F_H = kx = (.06\\,$N cm$^{-1})(20\\,\\ce{cm})= 1.2\\, $N\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $a = \\dfrac{F_{net}}{m}=\\dfrac{1.2\\,\\ce{N}}{0.40\\,\\ce{kg}}=3\\, $m s$^{-2}$"},{"ref":"PH0819","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0819\n\nA ball is falling vertically in a liquid. The overall density of the ball and the density of the liquid are the same. Which of the following is correct for the change in speed of the ball and the direction of the acceleration?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1.5em}$The motion of the ball$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Acceleration |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| Speeding up | Upwards |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Slowing down| Upwards |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Speeding up | Downwards |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Slowing down | $\\hspace{1.5em}$ Downwards $\\hspace{1.5em}$|","markScheme":"**答案：B**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThree forces are acting on the ball the weight of the ball, buoyancy, and drag. While buoyancy and drag are upwards, weight is downwards.\n\nThe buoyancy :\n\n- $F_B = {\\rho}_{liquid}Vg$\n\nWeight:\n\n- $W=mg={\\rho}_{ball}Vg$\n\nSince the density of the ball and the liquid are the same, weight and buoyancy cancel each other and the net force acting on the ball is the drag force. Due to having upward net force while moving downwards, the ball must be slowing down. Moreover, if the initial speed was zero at the time of contact with the water, the ball would experience zero net force. The drag force provides a net upwards force, so the acceleration of the ball is upwards."},{"ref":"PH0820","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0820\n\nSmall marbles with equal mass but different diameters are released in liquids with different densities and reach terminal velocity. Which of the following combinations of marble and the density of the liquid will result in the shortest time for the marble to fall a given height?  Assume that in all cases the marbles sink in the liquid and the viscosity of the liquid is constant.\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Diameter of the marble$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$The density of the liquid$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| Smaller diameter | Lower density |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Greater diameter| Lower density |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Smaller diameter | Higher density |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Greater diameter | Higher density |","markScheme":"**答案：A**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFor this question we can consider the terminal velocities of the two marbles. The marble with the greatest terminal velocity will reach the bottom in the shortest amount of time. It can also be shown that the marble with the greatest terminal velocity experiences the greatest acceleration in the situation where terminal velocity is not reached.\n\nTerminal velocity takes place when the net force is zero.\n\nTherefore\n\n- $F_g = F_d+F_b$\n\n- $mg = \\rho V g+6\\pi \\eta rv$\n\nSolving for terminal velocity gives\n\n- $v = \\dfrac{mg-\\rho V g}{6\\pi \\eta r}$\n\nSubstituting the formula for the volume of a sphere\n\n- $v = \\dfrac{mg-\\rho (\\frac{4}{3} \\pi r^3) g}{6\\pi \\eta r}$\n\nFirstly, we see that if the density of the liquid $\\rho$ decreases, the numerator increases and so the speed will increase.\n\nAdditionally, we see that as $r$ increases, the numerator will at some point reach zero. This will be the point at which the density of the marble equals the density of the fluid. We are told that the marble always sinks, and so the numerator must remain positive.\n\nLooking at the expression, decreasing $r$ will have the effect of both increasing the numerator (thus increasing the velocity) and decreasing the denominator (also increasing the velocity)."},{"ref":"PH0852","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0852\n\nA box of weight $400$ N is placed on a scale in an elevator. The scale reads $300$ N.\n\nWhich of the following is correct about the magnitude and direction of the acceleration of the elevator?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |$\\hspace{1.5em}$ Magnitude $\\hspace{1.5em}$|$\\hspace{1.5em}$ Direction $\\hspace{1.5em}$|\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $0.25$ m s$^{-2}$ | Upwards |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $0.25$ m s$^{-2}$| Downwards |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $2.5$ m s$^{-2}$ | Upwards |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $2.5$ m s$^{-2}$ | Downwards |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe scale's reading represents the upward force on the box from the scale. It is less than the weight, which means that the elevator is accelerating downwards.\n\nThe weight of the box is given as $400$ N\n\nMass can be found through\n\n- $W=mg$\n- $400 = m(10)$\n- $m=40$ kg\n\nResolving the forces and using Newton's Second Law gives\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\Sigma F = ma$\\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $400-300=(40)a$\\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $a=2.5$ m s$^{-2}$"},{"ref":"PH0853","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0853\n\nAn inclined plane makes an angle $\\theta$ with the horizontal. An object with mass $m$ is just about to slide on the inclined plane.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-06-02%20at%2010.28.12%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-02 at 10.28.12 AM\">\n\nWhich of the following is correct about the coefficient of static friction $\\mu_s$ and the free body diagram of the mass?\n\n| |$\\hspace{1.5em}$ $\\mu_s$ $\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\hspace{1.5em}$Free body diagram $\\hspace{1.5em}$|\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\sin \\theta$ | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-07-04%20at%201.30.37%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-04 at 1.30.37 PM\"> |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\sin \\theta$| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-07-04%20at%201.30.19%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-04 at 1.30.19 PM\"> |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\tan \\theta$ | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-07-04%20at%201.30.37%E2%80%AF1PM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-04 at 1.30.37 1PM\"> |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\tan \\theta$ | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-07-04%20at%201.30.191%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-04 at 1.30.191 PM\"> |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe free body diagram of the object. Note that there is no force pointing down the slope. It is a component of weight ($mg$) that acts down the slope.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-06-02%20at%2010.28.53%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-02 at 10.28.53 AM\">\n\nSince the object is just about to slide, then\n\n- $N=mg\\cos\\theta$\n\nAnd\n\n- $F_f=mg\\sin\\theta$\n\nThe static friction force is given by\n\n- $F_f=\\mu_sF_N=\\mu_smg\\cos\\theta$\n\nThen\n\n- $mg\\sin\\theta = \\mu_smg\\cos\\theta$\n\n- $\\mu_s=\\dfrac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos \\theta} $\n\n- $\\mu_s=\\tan\\theta $"},{"ref":"PH0932","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0932\n\nThe diagram below shows a person of mass 80 kg standing in an elevator of mass 800 kg. The elevator accelerates upwards at a rate of 1.2 m s$^{-2}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-06-02%20at%2010.46.12%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-02 at 10.46.12 AM\">\n\nWhat is the normal contact force exerted on the person by the elevator?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em} $ 96 N\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em} $ 690 N\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em} $ 780 N\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em} $ 880 N","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThere are two forces acting on the person - Weight and normal contact force. The lift is accelerating upwards so there must be a net force upwards.  Below is a free body diagram showing these forces.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0932Ans.png\" alt=\"PH0932Ans\">\n\nNet force = $ma$\n\nNormal contact force $-$ Weight = $ma$\n\nNormal contact force = $mg + ma = m(g + a) = 80(9.8 + 1.2) = 80(11) = 880$ N\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Further intuition}}$:\n\nThe normal contact force is the upward force felt by the person in the elevator. The person will feel heavier when the elevator is accelerating upwards.\n\nNormal contact force = $m(g+a)$ will be true for any $a$ if we take upwards acceleration as positive.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Examples:}}$\n\nWhen $a = 0$ (the elevator is stationary), normal contact force = $mg$\n\nWhen $a$ is negative (the elevator is accelerating downwards) normal contact force = $m(g - a)$\n\nWhen $a = -g$ (elevator is in freefall) normal contact force = 0"},{"ref":"PH0935","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0935\n\nA car is travelling along a straight line for a total time of 100 s. Throughout this time the driving force provided by the car is 1.2 kN and the total resistive force acting on the car is 500 N.\n\nWhich row correctly shows the rate of change of momentum and the impulse of the car during the 100 s?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Rate of change of momentum | Impulse |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 7 $\\times 10{^2}$ kg m s${^{-2}}$ | 7 $\\times 10{^0}$ kg m s${^{-1}}$ |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 7 $\\times 10{^2}$ kg m s${^{-2}}$ | 7 $\\times 10{^4}$ kg m s${^{-1}}$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 1.7 $\\times 10{^3}$ kg m s${^{-2}}$  | 1.7 $\\times 10{^1}$ kg m s${^{-1}}$  |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 1.7 $\\times 10{^3}$ kg m s${^{-2}}$ | 1.7 $\\times 10{^5}$ kg m s${^{-1}}$ |","markScheme":"**答案：B**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nNewton's second law states that net force = rate of change of momentum.\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of momentum is simply the net force acting on the car\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t}=1200$ N - 500 N = 700 N = 700 kg m s$^{-2}=7 \\times 10^2$ kg m s$^{-2}$\n\nImpulse\n\n- $J$ = $F_{net}\\Delta t$ = (700)(100) = 7 $\\times$ $10{^4}$ kg m s$^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH0946","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0946\n\nAn object is swung at the end of a rigid rod in a vertical circle at a constant speed $v$ such that there is an outward tension on the rod at all times. The radius of the circle is $r$. This is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH0946.png\" alt=\"PH0946\">\n\nThe tension in the rod when the object is at its lowest point in the circle is 50% greater than the tension in the rod when the object is at its highest point.\nWhich of the following expressions is equivalent to the acceleration of freefall on Earth, $g$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em} $ $\\dfrac{v{^2}}{5r}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em} $ $\\dfrac{2v{^2}}{3r}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em} $ $\\dfrac{v{^2}}{r}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em} $ $\\dfrac{3v{^2}}{2r}$","markScheme":"**答案：A**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nBelow shows a free body diagram for the object at its highest and lowest points.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-06-02%20at%2010.54.38%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-02 at 10.54.38 AM\">\n\nIn both scenarios the object is moving in a circle therefore the net force is a centripetal force. Because the speed and radius are constant, the centripetal acceleration is the same at both locations.\n\n- $F_{net}=ma = \\dfrac{mv{^2}}{r}$\n\nWhere $m$ is the mass of the object.\n\n(Acceleration is always towards the centre of the circle so at highest point acceleration points downwards and at lowest point acceleration points upwards)\n\nLet the tension in the rod when the object is at its highest point be $T$. We are told that the tension at the lowest point is 50% higher, therefore at the lowest point the tension is $1.5T$.\n\nHighest point:\n\n- $T + mg = \\dfrac{mv{^2}}{r}$\n\nLowest point:\n\n- $1.5T - mg = \\dfrac{mv{^2}}{r}$\n\nNow solving these to eliminate $T$:\n\n- $3T + 3mg = \\dfrac{3mv{^2}}{r}$\n\n- $3T - 2mg = \\dfrac{2mv{^2}}{r}$\n\nSubtracting the equations gives\n\n- $5mg = \\dfrac{mv{^2}}{r}$\n\n- $g = \\dfrac{v{^2}}{5r}$"},{"ref":"PH0968","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0968\n\nThe diagram shows the total upward force and the total downward force acting on a hot air balloon at one instant. The arrows are drawn to scale.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202026-10-20%20at%208.34.50%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-10-20 at 8.34.50 AM\">\n\nConsider the following statements about the possible motion of the balloon.\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | The balloon is accelerating   |\n  | II. | The balloon is moving upwards |\n  | III. | The balloon is moving downwards |\n\nWhich statements, considered independently, **could** be true?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nI is correct - The diagram shows that the upward force is greater than the downward force. So, there is a net upward force acting on the balloon. $F=ma$ so there is an upward acceleration. This statement is definitely true here.\n\nII could be correct – Balloon could be moving upwards - acceleration is also upwards so speed of the balloon would be increasing.\n\nIII could be correct – Balloon could be moving downwards - acceleration is upwards so speed of the balloon would be decreasing (deceleration).\n\nFor linear motion, acceleration tells us nothing about the speed, just how the speed is changing. The balloon could be moving upwards, moving downwards or stationary."},{"ref":"PH0969","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0969\n\nA person pulls a rope with a force $F$ at an angle of $60\\degree$ to the horizontal. The rope is connected to a load over a frictionless pulley as shown in the diagram below. The load is stationary.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-06-02%20at%2011.20.21%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-02 at 11.20.21 AM\">\n\nWhich of the following is correct about the weight of the load and the net force exerted on the pulley by the rope?\n\n| | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Weight of the load$\\hspace{1.5em}$  | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Net force exerted on the pulley by the rope$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| Less than $F$ |Less than  2$F$ |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Less than $F$ | Equal to 2$F$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Equal to $F$ | Less than  2$F$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Equal to $F$ | Equal to 2$F$ |","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\nThe tension in the rope is $F$. Therefore, the box experiences an upward force of $F$. The downward force acting on the box is its weight. The box is travelling at a constant speed - Newton's first law tells us that the net force must be zero. Therefore: weight $= F$.\n\nThe rope exerted a force $F$ downwards and a force $F$ at angle $60\\degree$ below the horizontal on the pulley. This is illustrated below\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\nThe vector sum of these forces is the total force exerted by the rope on the pulley. This is illustrated below:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\nThis vector sum must be less than 2$F$. The greatest possible vector sum of two vectors magnitude $F$ will be when they are pointing in the same direction."},{"ref":"PH0981","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0981\n\nThe diagram below shows an object attached to a light inextensible string. The object is travelling in a horizontal circle at a constant speed.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\nWhat is the direction of the resultant force acting on the object? Select from the labeled arrows on the diagram.","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe object is moving in a circle at a constant speed so the resultant force = centripetal force. The centripetal force is directed towards the center of the circular path that the object is following. The answer is D.\n\nThere are two forces acting on the object. Weight (acting in the direction C) and tension (acting in direction A). The resultant of these two forces in the vertical plane is zero. The resultant force is the horizontal component of the tension (acting in the direction D)."},{"ref":"PH1005","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1005\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA roller coaster car with a mass of 80 kg moves on the frictionless track shown in the diagram starting from point A.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH1005_a.png\" alt=\"PH1005_a\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Image created using Images by OpenClipart-Vectors from Pixabay - https:// pixabay.com]}}$\n\nAt points B and C, the track approximates a circular path so that the radii at B and C are 12 m and 18 m respectively.\n\n1. Compare the normal force exerted by the track on the car at points B and C. [2 marks]\n\n2. Draw and label the free body diagram of the car at point C. [2 marks]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-07-04%20at%201.08.24%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-04 at 1.08.24 PM\">\n\n3. Calculate the maximum speed of the car at point C so that it can remain on the track. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. $N_B=\\dfrac{mv^2}{r_B}+mg$ **AND** $N_C=mg-\\dfrac{mv^2}{r_C}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    The normal force at B is greater than the normal force at C. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n    Resolving the forces at B\n\n    - $\\dfrac{mv^2}{r_B}=N_B-mg$\n\n    - $N_B=mg +\\dfrac{mv^2}{r_B}$\n\n    While at C,\n\n    - $\\dfrac{mv^2}{r_C}=mg-N_C$\n\n    - $N_C=mg-\\dfrac{mv^2}{r_C}$\n\n    The normal force at B is greater than the normal force at C.\n\n2.  :::center\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-07-04%20at%201.11.27%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-04 at 1.11.27 PM\">\n\n    Upward normal force **AND** downward gravitational force. $\\checkmark$\n\n    The downward arrow is bigger than the upward arrow. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    Because the car is at the top of a curve, the centripetal acceleration must be towards the centre which is in the downwards direction. The acceleration must also therefore be downwards, so the gravitational force must be greater than the upwards normal force.\n\n3. The maximum speed is when the normal force is zero. $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $v=\\ll\\sqrt{r_cg}\\gg=\\sqrt{\\left(18\\right)(9.81)}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $v=13\\  \\textrm{m s}^{-1}\\ \\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n    The maximum speed at point C is when the normal force of the track on the car is zero.\n\n    The normal force at C\n\n    - $F_N=mg-\\dfrac{mv^2}{r_c}$\n\n    When $F_N = 0$\n\n    - $mg=\\dfrac{mv^2}{r_c}$\n\n    Using the given value of $r=18$ m\n\n    - $v=\\sqrt{r_cg}=\\sqrt{(18)(9.81)}=13.29\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH1023","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1023\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\n1. A small solid sphere is released from rest at the surface of a container of water. The initial position of the sphere is illustrated below.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-06-03%20at%201.50.24%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-03 at 1.50.24 PM\">\n\n    The graph below shows how the velocity of the sphere $v$ varies with time $t$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-06-03%20at%201.57.14%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-03 at 1.57.14 PM\">\n\n    Explain the shape of the graph. [3 marks]\n\n2. 1. The radius of the sphere is 0.61 mm and the viscosity of the water is $1.0\\times 10^{-3}$ Pa s. Show that the maximum drag force acting on the sphere is around $5\\times 10^{-6}$ N. [2 marks]\n\n    2. It is given that the total density of the sphere is 50% greater than that of water. Calculate the weight of the sphere. [3 marks]\n\n3. After 0.8 seconds the sphere reaches the bottom of the container and comes to rest instantaneously. Use the graph to estimate the depth of water in the container. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Initially there is a net force downwards / initial forces are weight (downwards) and buoyancy (upwards) and weight is greater than buoyancy, so the sphere accelerates downwards.  $\\checkmark$\n\n    Downwards acceleration decreases due to drag/friction/viscous drag.  $\\checkmark$\n\n    Terminal speed is reached / sphere reaches a constant speed when $W=F_b+F_d$ (Weight = Drag + Buoyancy)  $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    Initially (when $v=0$) the only forces acting on the sphere are weight $W$ and buoyancy $F_b$. The free body diagram is as follows:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n    $W$ is greater than $F_b$ causing the sphere to accelerate downwards.\n\n    As the velocity $v$ increases, drag force $F_d$ increases opposing the direction of the motion. When $F_b$ has increased to the point where the forces are balanced $W=F_b+F_d$. The free body diagram is as follows:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n    Now the sphere is in equilibrium so will remain at a constant velocity (terminal speed).\n\n2. 1. ::indent\n        - $F_d=6\\pi \\eta rv$ and $v=0.4$ m s$^{-1}$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $F_d=6\\pi (1.0\\times 10^{-3})(0.61\\times10^{-3})(0.4)= $ :answer[$4.6\\times 10^{-6}\\textrm{ N}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        Drag force is maximum when the sphere reaches its terminal speed. We can see form the graph that velocity plateaus at $0.4$ m s$^{-1}$. Hence terminal speed is $0.4$ m s$^{-1}$.\n\n        The equation for viscous drag for small spherical objects is $F_d=6\\pi \\eta rv$\n\n        Substituting our values:\n\n        - Viscosity $\\eta =1.0\\times 10^{-3}$ Pa s\n\n        - Radius $r=0.61\\times 10^{-3}$ m\n\n        - Terminal speed $v=0.4$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n        - $F_d=6\\pi(1.0\\times 10^{-3})(0.61\\times 10^{-3})(0.4)=4.6\\times 10^{-6}$ N\n\n    2. Weight is 50% greater than buoyancy / $W=1.5F_b$ / $F_b=\\dfrac{2}{3}\\,W$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        Deducing that\n        $\\dfrac{W}{3}=F_d$ / $W=3F_d$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $W=(3)(4.6\\times 10^{-6})=14\\times 10^{-6}\\textrm{ N}=1.4\\times 10^{-5}\\textrm{ N}$    $\\checkmark$\n\n        (Note: Use of $F_d=5\\times 10^{-6}$ gives $1.5\\times10^{-5}\\textrm{ N})$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Alternative solution}}$:\n\n        Density of water $= 1.0\\times 10^3$ kg m$^{-1}$\n\n        Density if sphere $= 1.5\\times 10^3$ kg m$^{-1}$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $W=\\rho Vg $\n\n        And\n\n        - $V=\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $W=(\\rho)\\Bigl(\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3\\Bigr)g=(1.5\\times 10^3)\\Bigl(\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi (0.61\\times 10^3)^3\\Bigr)(9.81)=1.4\\times 10^{-5}\\textrm{ N}$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        Award full marks for correct answer.\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        Let ${\\rho}_{sphere}$ and ${\\rho}_{water}$ be the respective densities of the sphere and the water and $V$ be the volume of the sphere.\n\n        We know that ${\\rho}_{sphere}=1.5{\\rho}_{water}$\n\n        Weight: $W=m_{sphere}g={\\rho}_{sphere}Vg=1.5{\\rho}_{water}Vg$\n\n        Buoyancy: $F_b={\\rho}_{water} Vg$\n\n        So, we may write:\n\n        - $\\dfrac{W}{F_b} =\\dfrac{1.5{\\rho}_{water}Vg}{{\\rho}_{water} Vg}=1.5  $\n\n        - $F_b=\\dfrac{2}{3} W $\n\n        At terminal speed $W=F_b+F_d$\n\n        Substituting $F_b=\\dfrac{2}{3} W$ gives:\n\n        - $W=\\dfrac{2}{3} W+F_d$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{W}{3}=F_d $\n\n        - $W=3F_d $\n\n        - $W=(3)(4.6\\times 10^{-6})=14\\times 10^{-5}\\,N=1.4\\times 10^{-3}\\textrm{ N}$\n\n3. Evidence of using area under graph\n\n    e.g., “Depth = Area under graph” or “large square $=0.01$ m”  $\\checkmark$\n\n    :answer[Depth 0.27 m] $\\checkmark$\n\n    Allow answers in range 0.25 m - 0.29 m\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    Displacement is the area under the velocity-time graph.\n\n    The displacement covered by the sphere in $0.8$ s is the depth of water in the container.\n\n    Each large square has an area of (0.1 s)(0.1 m s$^{-1})=$0.01 m.\n\n    There are around 27 large squares between the curve and the lines $t=0$ and $t=0.8$.\n\n    So, area $=(27)(0.01)=0.27$ m = depth."},{"ref":"PH1024","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1024\n\nA ball of mass 100 g hits the ground at an angle of $45\\degree$ and a speed of 10 m s$^{-1}$. The collision is elastic, and the ball makes contact with the ground for a total time of 100 ms. This is illustrated below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-06-03%20at%202.03.13%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-03 at 2.03.13 PM\">\n\nWhat is the magnitude of the average net force on the ball during the collision?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $7.1$ N\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $10$ N\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $14$ N\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $20$ N","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n- $F_{net}=\\dfrac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t}=\\dfrac{m\\Delta v}{\\Delta t}$\n\nThe horizontal component of the speed/momentum of the object does not change - the force exerted by ground is purely vertical.\n\nInitial and final vertical velocities are $-10\\sin{(45\\degree)}$ and +$10\\sin{(45\\degree)}$ if we take upwards as the positive direction (this will give a positive force as the direction of the force exerted by the ground on the ball is upwards) see diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-06-03%20at%202.07.40%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-03 at 2.07.40 PM\">\n\nFor the vertical direction $\\Delta v=$ final velocity $-$ initial velocity $=10\\sin{45\\degree}-(-10\\sin{45\\degree})=10\\sin{45\\degree}+10\\sin{45\\degree}=20\\sin{45\\degree}$\n\n- $F_{net}=\\dfrac{m\\Delta v}{\\Delta t}=\\dfrac{(0.1)(20\\sin{45\\degree})}{0.1}=20\\sin{45\\degree}$ = 14 N\n\n(Here we have converted to SI units 100 g = 0.1 kg and 100 ms = 0.1 s)"},{"ref":"PH1068","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1068\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA solid sphere of mass $m$ is released from rest below the surface of oil and moves downwards.\n\n1. Draw the free body diagram of the sphere at the instant it was released. [2 marks]\n\n  <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-07-03%20at%204.33.43%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-03 at 4.33.43 PM\">\n\n2. 1. Show that the terminal speed of the sphere is given by:\n\n        $v=\\dfrac{2r^2 g(\\rho_s-\\rho_o)}{9\\eta}$\n\n        where $\\rho_s$ is the density of the sphere and $\\rho_o$ is the density of oil. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Another sphere from the same material but with mass $8m$ is released in the oil. Determine the terminal velocity of the new sphere in terms of the terminal velocity of the smaller sphere, $v$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Weight downwards and buoyant force upwards  $\\checkmark$\\\n    Weight arrow is longer than the buoyant force arrow.  $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$\\\n    The sphere is accelerating downwards. This is because the gravitational force on the sphere is bigger than the buoyant force. Note that at the instant of release, there is no velocity and therefore no drag force.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-06-03%20at%204.44.43%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-03 at 4.44.43 PM\">\n\n2. 1.  $mg=\\rho_o Vg+6\\pi \\eta rv$  $\\checkmark$ \\\n        Correct steps to reach $v=\\dfrac{2r^2 g(\\rho_s-\\rho_o)}{9\\eta}$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$\\\n        When the sphere reaches terminal speed, its weight is equal to the sum of the buoyant and drag forces.\n\n        - $F_g=F_b+F_d$\n\n        - $mg=\\rho_o Vg+6\\pi \\eta rv$\n\n        - $\\rho_s Vg=\\rho_o Vg+6\\pi \\eta rv$\n\n        - $v=\\dfrac{\\rho_s Vg-\\rho_o Vg}{6\\pi \\eta r}$\n\n        - $v=\\dfrac{Vg(\\rho_s-\\rho_o)}{6\\pi \\eta r}$\n\n        - $v=\\dfrac{\\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^3 g(\\rho_s-\\rho_o)}{6\\pi \\eta r}$\n\n        - $v=\\dfrac{2r^2 g(\\rho_s-\\rho_o)}{9\\eta}$\n\n    2. $r_2=2r$  $\\checkmark$\\\n        $v \\propto r^2$  $\\checkmark$\\\n        $v_2=4v$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$\\\n        Since the new sphere is from the same material as that in part (b) (i), then:\n\n        - $\\dfrac{m_2}{V_2} = \\dfrac{m}{V}$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{8m}{V_2} = \\dfrac{m}{V}$\n\n        - $V_2=8V$\n\n        - $(r_2)^3=8r^3$\n\n        - $r_2=2r$\n\n        From the relation in (b)(i)\n\n        - $v \\propto r^2$\n\n        Then\n\n        - $v_2=4v$"},{"ref":"PH1078","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1078\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n1. A suitcase of mass $12$ kg is placed on the top of a car. Both the car and the suitcase are initially at rest.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-06-03%20at%204.51.37%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-03 at 4.51.37 PM\">\n\n    The car begins to move with increasing acceleration. The suitcase accelerates along with the car until its acceleration reaches $2.5$ m s$^{-2}$, at which point the suitcase just starts to slide towards the back of the car.\n\n    1. Calculate the coefficient of static friction between the car and the suitcase. [2 marks]\n\n    2. If the car were to continue accelerating at $2.5$ m s$^{-2}$, explain why the suitcase's acceleration relative to the ground would be less than $2.5$ m s$^{-2}$. [2 marks]\n\n2. The car stops suddenly, and the suitcase continues moving forwards. When it loses contact with the car, the velocity of the suitcase is $4.5$ m s$^{-1}$ relative to the ground in the horizontal direction.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-06-03%20at%204.49.00%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-03 at 4.49.00 PM\">\n\n    The height of the car is $1.5$ m. The suitcase does not rotate as it falls, and air resistance is negligible.\n\n    $\\\\$\n\n    1. Show that the time taken for the suitcase to hit the ground is around $0.6$ s. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Calculate the speed at which the suitcase hits the ground. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. Frictional force / $\\mu_s F_N$ / $\\mu_s mg\\ =(12)(2.5)$ $\\checkmark$\\\nEquates frictional force to net force to get $\\mu_s\\ =\\ \\dfrac{2.5}{9.8}\\ =$ :answer[$0.26$] $ \\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The only force acting on the suitcase in the horizontal direction is friction. This friction causes the suitcase to accelerate to the right (relative to the ground).\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-06-03%20at%204.54.57%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-03 at 4.54.57 PM\">\n\n        When the suitcase is at rest relative to the car its acceleration is the same as that of the car so using\n\n        - $F_{net} = ma$\n\n        We can say that\n\n        - $F_f = (8) (\\ce{acceleration\\ of\\ the\\ car})$\n\n        Frictional force between two objects when there is no relative motion between the objects is given by $F_f \\leq \\mu_s F_N$\n\n        When the object is on the point of motion, the static friction force is at a maximum and we can say that $F_f = \\mu_s F_N$\n\n        The suitcase just starts to slide when the acceleration of the car is $2.5$ m s$^{-2}$ so we can say that\n\n        - $\\mu_s F_N = ma = (12) (2.5)$\n\n        Vertical forces are balanced so reaction force is equal to weight.\n\n        - $\\mu_s mg = (12) (2.5)$\n\n        - $\\mu_s (12) (9.8) = (12) (2.5)$\n\n        Cancelling a factor of 12 and rearranging for $\\mu_s$ gives\n\n        - $\\mu_s (9.8) = (2.5)$\n\n        - $\\mu_s = \\dfrac{2.5}{9.8}\\ =\\ 0.26$\n\n    2. Maximum static coefficient of friction > dynamic / kinetic coefficient of friction / $\\mu_s\\ >\\ \\mu_d$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Force of dynamic / kinetic friction will be less than the force of static friction $\\checkmark$\\\n\n        **OR**\n\n        Once in motion frictional force will be less than $(mass\\ of\\ suitcase)\\ \\times\\ (acceleration\\ of\\ car)$ so suitcase accelerates forwards at a slower rate than the car $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Frictional force between two objects when there is no relative motion between the objects is given by $F_f\\ \\leq\\ \\mu_s F_N$\n\n        Frictional force between two objects when there is relative motion between the objects is given by $F_f\\ =\\ \\mu_d F_N$\n\n        $\\mu_s\\ >\\ \\mu_d$ so $\\mu_s R\\ >\\ \\mu_d R$ when the object begins to move the frictional force becomes smaller.\n\n        To move with the car the net force on the suitcase must be $= (7) (2.5)$\n\n        But $\\mu_d F_N < \\mu_s F_N = (7)(2.5)$\n\n        So, the suitcase accelerates forwards at a slower rate than the car (So relative to the car the suitcase accelerates backwards).\n\n2. 1. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ $s\\ =\\ ut\\ +\\ \\dfrac{1}{2}at^2\\ =\\dfrac{1}{2}at^2$\n\n        - $t\\ =\\ \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2s}{a}}\\ =\\ \\sqrt{\\dfrac{(2)(1.5)}{9.8}}\\ =$ :answer[$0.55$ s] $\\checkmark$\n\n        Must see extra significant figure for mark.\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        We can treat the horizontal and vertical dimensions independently.\n\n        In the vertical dimension we know the following:\n\n        - (displacement) $s\\ =\\ 1.5$ m\n        - (initial velocity) $u\\ =\\ 0$\n        - (acceleration) $a\\ =\\ 9.8$ m s$^{-2}$\n\n        We can therefore use the following equation to find time taken\n\n        - $s = ut + \\dfrac{1}{2}at^2$\n\n        Substituting in our values gives:\n\n        - $1.5 = (0)(t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ (9.8)t^2$\n\n        - $1.5 =\\dfrac{1}{2} (9.8)t^2$\n\n        Rearranging for $t$:\n\n        - $(2)(1.5) = (9.8)t^2$\n\n        - $t^2 = \\dfrac{(2)(1.5)}{9.8}$\n\n        - $t = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{(2)(1.5)}{9.8}}\\ =\\ 0.55$ s (2sf)\n\n    2. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ $(v_y)^2\\ =\\ u^2\\ +\\ \\dfrac{2as}{v_y}\\ =\\ u\\ +\\ at$\n\n        - $(v_y)^2\\ =\\ (0)^2\\ +\\ (2)\\ (9.8)\\ (1.5)\\ =29.4$\\\n\n        **OR**\n\n        - $v_y\\ =\\ (9.8)\\ (0.55)\\ =5.4$ m s$^{-1}\\ $ seen $\\checkmark$\n\n        - speed $= \\sqrt{(v_y)^2 + (v_x)^2}\\ = \\sqrt{29.4 + 4.5^2}\\ =$ :answer[$7.0$ m s$^{-1}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        Allow full marks for use of $t = 0.6\\,s$ giving a final answer of $speed = 7.4$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n        *Alternative:*\n\n        Energy is conserved\n\n        - $\\dfrac{1}{2}m(v_{final})^2 = \\dfrac{1}{2}m(v_{initial})^2 + mgh$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{1}{2}(v_{final})^2\\ = \\dfrac{1}{2}(4.5)^2 + (9.8) (1.5)$\n\n        - $v_{final}\\ =\\ \\sqrt{4.5^2 + (2) (9.8) (1.5)} =$ :answer[$7.0$ m s$^{-1}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Speed is the magnitude of velocity. The velocity of the suitcase will have a horizontal and a vertical component when it hits the ground.\n\n        The horizontal velocity of the suitcase remains constant as there are no horizontal forces acting on the suitcase as it falls so $v_x\\ =\\ 4.5$ m s$^{-1}$.\n\n        In the vertical dimension we know the following:\n\n        - (displacement) $s = 1.5$ m\n        - (initial velocity) $u = 0$\n        - (acceleration) $a = 9.8$ m s$^{-2}$\n        - (time) $t = 0.55$ s\n\n        It's best to use information given in the question so selecting the equation $v^2 = u^2 + 2as$\n\n        - $(v_y)^2 = (0)^2 + (2) (9.8) (1.5) = 29.4$\n\n        - $v_y = \\sqrt{29.4} = 5.42\\ (3sf)$\n\n        Now speed $v_{total}$ is the magnitude of the vector sum of $v_x$ and $v_y$ ($v_x$ and $v_y$ are perpendicular so we can use Pythagoras' theorem)\n\n        - $(v_{total})^2\\ =\\ (v_y)^2\\ +\\ (v_x)^2$\n\n        - $v_{total} = \\sqrt{(v_y)^2 + (v_x)^2} = \\sqrt{(5.42 …)^2 + 4.5^2} = \\sqrt{29.4 + 4.5^2} = 7.0$ m s$^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH1100","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH1100\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nIn an experiment to investigate Newton's 2nd Law, a student attaches a trolley of mass $M$ to a string that passes over a pulley and is attached to a hanging mass $m$ as shown. The student measures the time taken for the cart to travel between two marks on the table when accelerating from rest.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/PH1100aaaa.png\" alt=\"PH1100aaaa\">\n\nFor one of the trials, the student measured the time taken to be $1.2 ± 0.2\\, $s. The distance between the markings was $1.50 ± 0.03\\, $m. The acceleration can be found from the equation $a=\\dfrac{2s}{t^2}$ .\n\n1. 1. Determine the experimental value for the acceleration, with uncertainty.  [4 marks]\n\n   2. The student increases the distance between the two marks on the table and repeats the experiment. Explain the effect of this change on the uncertainty on the calculated acceleration.    [2 marks]\n\n2.  The student uses the equation\n\n    - $a=\\dfrac{mg}{(M+m)}$\n\n    to predict the acceleration of the trolley. Give two reasons why the experimental value will likely be lower than the result from the calculation based on the masses.  [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1. ::indent\n        - $a=\\dfrac{2(1.5)}{(1.2)^2} =2.08\\, \\ce{m\\, s^{-2}}$    $\\checkmark$\n\n       Relative uncertainty = $\\dfrac{.03}{1.5}+2 \\dfrac{0.2}{1.2}=0.35=35\\% $  $\\checkmark$\n\n       Uncertainty = $(2.08)(0.35)= 0.72$  $\\checkmark$\n\n       $a= $:answer[$(2.1± 0.7) \\, $m s$^{-2}$] $\\hspace{2em}$    OR   $\\hspace{2em} $:answer[$(2.08 ±  0.72) \\, $m s$^{-2}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n       $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n       Substituting the measured values into the given equation\n\n       - $a=\\dfrac{2s}{t^2} =2\\dfrac{1.5}{(1.2)^2}\\ =2.08\\, $m s$^{-2}$\n\n       To find the uncertainty, we will need to add the relative/percentage uncertainties. Because the time is squared, this percentage uncertainty is added twice.\n\n       - Relative uncertainty = $\\dfrac{.03}{1.5}+2 \\left(\\dfrac{0.2}{1.2}\\right)=0.35=35\\% $\n\n       The uncertainty on acceleration is found by multiplying the experimental value by the calculated relative uncertainty.\n\n       Uncertainty = $(2.08)(0.35)= 0.72  $\n\n        For the final statement, the number of decimal places on the acceleration and that on the uncertainty should match.\n\n       - $a=(2.1\\pm 0.7) \\,$m s$^{-2}\\ \\ \\ \\ OR\\ \\ \\ (2.08 \\pm 0.72)\\,$m s$^{-2} $\n\n    2.  Uncertainty on the time and distance measurements are unchanged but the distance and time values increase.   $\\checkmark$\n\n        Relative uncertainties decrease, therefore the uncertainty on acceleration will decrease.   $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nAlthough the marks are moved further apart, we assume that the same measuring technique is used and so the uncertainty on the distance measurement will remain $± 0.03$ m. Similarly, the uncertainty on the time will likely remain $± 0.2$ s. The fractional uncertainties for both will therefore decrease, leading to a decrease in the fractional uncertainty on the acceleration. These changes should not affect the acceleration value itself, therefore the absolute uncertainty on the acceleration will also decrease.\n\n2.  friction/air resistance. $\\checkmark$\n\n    energy goes into the rotation of the pulley/trolley wheels. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Friction will oppose the motion of the cart, reducing the net force and therefore the acceleration. Friction will also exist in the pulley\n\n    The pulley has mass and rotates at an increasing rate as the cart accelerates. This is also true for the wheels on the cart. This takes some energy from the system that would normally go into the kinetic energy of the cart."},{"ref":"PH1334","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1334\n\nTwo balls $A$ and $B$ are moving towards each other along the same line. Ball A is moving rightwards with a speed of 9 m s$^{-1}$ and ball B is moving leftwards with a speed of 2 m s$^{-1}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-06-04%20at%207.11.49%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-04 at 7.11.49 AM\">\n\nThey collide elastically and after the collision ball $A$ rebounds to the left with a speed of 12 m s$^{-1}$.\n\nWhat is the speed and direction of ball $B$ after the collision?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $5$ m s$^{-1}$ to the left\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $1$ m s$^{-1}$ to the left\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $1$ m s$^{-1}$ to the right\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $5$ m s$^{-1}$ to the right","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nDuring a perfectly elastic collision the relative speed between the colliding objects remains constant.\n\nBefore the collision, A was moving to the right at $9$ m s$^{-1}$ and B was moving to the left at $2$ m s$^{-1}$ which means that both objects were moving towards each other at a relative speed of $11$ m s$^{-1}$.\n\nAfter the collision they must then be moving away from each other at the same relative speed.\nGiven that A is moving leftwards at a speed of $12$ m s$^{-1}$, B must be moving leftwards at a speed of $1$ m s$^{-1}$ and so the relative speed between them would still be $11$ m s$^{-1}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-06-04%20at%207.10.38%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-04 at 7.10.38 AM\">"},{"ref":"PH1798","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1798\n\nA spherical object of radius $r$ is released from rest inside a fluid, after some time it reaches a terminal velocity $v$. The density of the fluid is $\\rho_f$ and the density of the object is negligible compared to $\\rho_f$. Which of the following is a correct expression for the viscosity of the fluid?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{2\\rho_fr^2g}{9v}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{4\\rho_fr^3g}{9v}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{2\\rho_fr^2gv}{9}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{4\\rho_fr^3gv}{9}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nBecause of the very low density of the object, in this situation it will be rising upwards through the fluid and the drag force will be directed downwards. The forces acting on the object are represented in the diagram below:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-06-04%20at%209.35.15%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-04 at 9.35.15 AM\">\n\nWeight is negligible due to the negligible object density.\n\n- $\\rho_{\\textrm{object}}\\ll\\rho_f$\n\n- $(\\textrm{weight}=\\rho_{\\textrm{object}}Vg\\ll\\rho_{\\textrm{object}}Vg=\\textrm{buoyancy})$\n\nWhen the object is at terminal velocity the net force acting on it is zero so we have:\n\n- $\\textrm{weight}=\\textrm{drag force}$\n\nWhere:\n\n- Buoyancy $=\\rho_fVg$\n- Drag $=6\\pi\\eta rv$\n\nWhere $g$ is acceleration due to gravity, $V$ is the volume of the ball, and $\\eta$ is the viscosity of the liquid.\n\n- $\\rho_fVg=6\\pi\\eta rv$\n\nNow substituting the volume of a sphere $V=\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3$ and rearranging for $\\eta$ we have:\n\n- $\\rho_f\\dfrac{4}{3}r^3g=6\\pi\\eta rv$\n\n- $\\rho_f\\dfrac{4}{3}r^2g=6\\eta v$\n\n- $\\eta=\\dfrac{2\\rho_fr^2g}{9v}$"},{"ref":"PH1800","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1800\n\nAn object of mass $m$ and initial velocity $v$ collides with a stationary object of mass $2m$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-03-25%20at%207.24.54%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-03-25 at 7.24.54 PM\">\n\nAfter the collision the object of mass $2m$ moves with velocity $\\dfrac{2v}{3}$.  What is the final velocity of the object of mass $m$ and the magnitude of impulse of the collision?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\textrm{Final velocity of mass}$ $m$ |$\\hspace{1.5em}$ $\\textrm{Impulse}$ $\\hspace{1.5em}$|\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\dfrac{-v}{3}$ | $\\dfrac{2mv}{3}$ |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{-v}{3}$ | $\\dfrac{4mv}{3}$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{v}{3}$ | $\\dfrac{2mv}{3}$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{v}{3}$ | $\\dfrac{4mv}{3}$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nUsing conservation of momentum, we set the initial momentum equal of the system equal to the final momentum. We can represent the final velocity of the object of mass $m$ as $v_{fm}$. Applying the law of conservation of momentum gives\n\n- $mv=mv_{fm}+2m\\left(\\dfrac{2v}{3}\\right)$\n\n- $v=v_{fm}+2\\left(\\dfrac{2v}{3}\\right)$\n\n- $v_{fm}=v-\\dfrac{4v}{3}=\\dfrac{-v}{3}$\n\nThe negative sign represents the fact that the smaller mass will bounce backwards in the collision.\n\nImpulse $J$ is equal to change in momentum.\n\n- $J=m\\Delta v$\n\nThe simplest way to find the impulse of this collision is to consider the change in momentum of the ball that is initially stationary.\n\n- $J=m\\Delta v=2m\\left(\\dfrac{2v}{3}-0\\right)=\\dfrac{4mv}{3}$"},{"ref":"PH1845-1","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1845-1\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nA tennis ball of mass 58 g is moving at an initial speed of 15 m s$^{-1}$ towards a racket. The plane of the racket remains perpendicular to the ball's motion. After the collision, the ball's final speed is 25 m s$^{-1}$ in the opposite direction. The graph shows the variation of the net force $F$ with time $t$ for the period of time that the ball is in contact with the racket, with the peak force being $F_{max}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screensh0ot%202025-08-28%20at%208.20.12%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screensh0ot 2025-08-28 at 8.20.12 AM\">\n\n1.    The gravitational effects on the ball are negligible compared to the force of the racket. Determine the value of $F_{max}$. [3 marks]\n\n2. 1. When the tennis ball loses contact with the racket, its center of mass is at a height of 55 cm above the ground and travels at a speed of 25 m s$^{-1}$ at an angle of 7.0$\\degree$ above the horizontal.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/ph1845-1qbi.png\" alt=\"ph1845-1qbi\">\n\n    A net of height 93 cm above the ground is located 9.9 m horizontally in front of the point where the ball loses contact with the racket. The ball passes over the net if its center of mass is above the height of the net. Assuming air resistance is negligible, deduce whether the ball will pass over the net. [3 marks]\n\n    2. Discuss how air resistance may affect your answer to part (b)(i). [1 mark]\n\n3. For practice, the tennis ball is attached to a light, inextensible string and is swung in a horizontal circle around a vertical pole as shown. The string makes an angle $\\theta$ with the vertical.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/ph1845-1qci1.png\" alt=\"ph1845-1qci1\">\n\n    1.    On the diagram below draw and label all of the forces acting on the ball and indicate the direction of the acceleration. [2 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/ph1845-1qci2.png\" alt=\"ph1845-1qci2\">\n\n    2. The speed of the ball is 4.1 m s$^{-1}$ and the radius of the circular path is 1.3 m. Determine the angle $\\theta$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. $\\hspace{1.8em}$ Area $=\\Delta p =\\dfrac{1}{2}(0.04)F_{max}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $\\Delta p=2.32$ kg m s$^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $F_{max}=\\dfrac{2.32}{0.02}=$ :answer[$116$ N] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The area underneath the force time graph is equal to the impulse of the collision. Impulse is also equal to change in momentum $\\Delta p$.\n\n    The area under the graph is given by the area of the triangle:\n\n    - Area $=\\dfrac{1}{2}bh=\\dfrac{1}{2}(0.04)(F_{max})=0.02F_{max}$\n\n    The mass of the ball remains constant and the change in velocity is given by:\n\n    - $\\Delta v=v-u=25-(-15)=40$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n    Here we have assigned a negative sign to the initial velocity as the directions of the velocities are opposite. The change is momentum given by:\n\n    - $\\Delta p=m\\Delta v=(0.058)(40)=2.32$ kg m s$^{-1}$\n\n    Therefore, we have:\n\n    - $2.32=0.02F_{max}$\n\n    - $F_{max}=\\dfrac{2.32}{0.02}=116$ N\n\n2. 1. $\\hspace{1.8em}$ $t=\\dfrac{9.9}{25\\cos(7.0)}=0.399$ s $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $h=25\\sin(7.0)(0.399)-\\dfrac{1}{2}(9.8)(0.399)^2+0.55$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $h=0.99\\textrm{ m}\\gt0.93\\textrm{ m}$ (allow 2sf)\n\n        Or\n\n        - $\\Delta h=0.44\\textrm{ m}\\gt0.38\\textrm{ m}$\n\n        Therefore, the ball will pass over the net. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Resolving the velocity into horizontal and vertical components we have:\n\n        - $v_h=25\\cos(7.0)$\n\n        - $v_v=25\\sin(7.0)$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/ph1845-1abi.png\" alt=\"ph1845-1abi\">\n\n        In the horizontal direction, acceleration is zero, so the time taken for the ball to travel a horizontal distance of 9.9 m is given by:\n\n        - $t=\\dfrac{9.9}{25\\cos(7.0)}=0.399$ s\n\n        In the vertical direction we have a constant acceleration of $g=-9.8$ m s$^{-2}$ (taking upwards as the positive direction).\n\n        We can therefore use the following suvat equation to determine the vertical distance travelled by the ball during the 0.399 s.\n\n        - $s=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}at^2$\n\n        - $\\Delta h=25\\sin(7.0)(0.399)+\\dfrac{1}{2}(-9.8)(0.399)^2=0.436$ m\n\n        The initial height of the ball from the ground was 0.55 m so the total height of the ball is given by:\n\n        - $h=0.55+0.436=0.986$ m\n\n        - $0.986\\gt0.93$\n\n        The height reached by the center of mass of the ball is greater than the height of the net, therefore the net will be cleared.\n\n    2. Height of the ball will be lower due to air resistance\n\n        Ball may not / will likely not clear the net OWTTE $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Air resistance causes a resistive force on the ball in both the horizontal and vertical directions. Therefore, the vertical kinetic energy of the ball is now being converted into work done against drag as well as the gravitational force. As a result, the ball will reach a lower maximum height and will not travel as far horizontally.\n\n3. 1. Two arrows drawn $\\checkmark$\n\n        All three arrows correctly drawn $\\checkmark$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-09-05%20at%209.15.16%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-09-05 at 9.15.16 AM\">\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The only forces acting on the ball are its weight (acting downwards) tension (which acts parallel to the string away from the ball).\n\n        The acceleration vector points towards the center of the circle because ball moves in a circle at a constant speed.\n\n    2. $\\hspace{1.8em}$ $T\\cos\\theta=mg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $T\\sin\\theta=\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\tan\\theta=\\dfrac{v^2}{gr}$ (note this alone scores 2 marks)\n\n        - $\\theta=\\left(\\dfrac{4.1^2}{(9.8)(1.3)}\\right)=$ :answer[$53\\degree$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Resolving the tension $T$ into its horizontal and vertical components we have:\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/ph1845-1acii.png\" alt=\"ph1845-1acii\">\n\n        There is no acceleration in the vertical plane, so the net vertical force is zero. Therefore, we have:\n\n        - $T\\cos\\theta=$ weight\n\n        - $T\\cos\\theta=mg$ [1]\n\n        The acceleration and hence the net force is directed towards the center of the circle. The net force is therefore $T\\sin\\theta$.\n\n        - $T\\sin\\theta=ma$\n\n        The ball is moving in a circle as acceleration is given by $a=\\dfrac{v^2}{r}$. Therefore:\n\n        - $T\\sin\\theta=\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}$ [2]\n\n        Dividing equation [2] by equation [1] we have:\n\n        - $\\dfrac{T\\sin\\theta}{T\\cos\\theta}=\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}\\right)}{mg}$\n\n        - $\\tan\\theta=\\dfrac{v^2}{gr}$\n\n        Substituting in our values we have:\n\n        - $\\theta=\\left(\\dfrac{4.1^2}{(9.8)(1.3)}\\right)=52.8\\degree$"},{"ref":"PH1853","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1853\n\nTwo objects with masses $3m$ and $2m$ are attached by an inextensible string with negligible mass. Both objects are placed on a frictonless incline at an angle $\\theta$ above the horizontal.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/ph1853q.png\" alt=\"ph1853q\">\n\nA force parallel to the incline is pulling the system at constant speed up the incline.\n\nWhich of the following correctly describes the magnitude of the tension in the string connecting the objects?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $mg\\sin\\theta$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2mg\\sin\\theta$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $3mg\\sin\\theta$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $5mg\\sin\\theta$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nTo find the magnitude of the tension we must write Newton's 2$^{\\textrm{nd}}$ Law for the object with a mass $3m$ along with an axis that is parallel to the incline.\n\nAlong this direction, the object is acted upon by the tension force and the component of the weight which is parallel to the incline.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-09-06%20at%205.27.37%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-09-06 at 5.27.37 PM\">\n\nGiven that the system moves at constant speed, the total force must be zero. Resolving the forces in the direction parallel to the incline gives\n\n- $T-3mg\\sin\\theta=0$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the tension on the string is\n\n- $T=3mg\\sin\\theta$"},{"ref":"PH1888","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1888\n\nSpheres $A$ and $B$ are made of the same material that sinks in water. Both spheres are small, and $A$ has twice the radius of $B$. Both are dropped into water and reach terminal velocity.\n\nWhat is the ratio $\\dfrac{\\textrm{terminal velocity of object }A}{\\textrm{terminal velocity of object }B}$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{1}{2}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 4\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 8","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nWhen the objects enter the water, the weight downwards is opposed by the upwards forces of buoyancy and drag:\n\n- $F_{net}=ma$\n\n- weight $-$ buoyancy $-$ drag $=ma$\n\nAt terminal velocity the net force is zero, so we have:\n\n- weight $-$ buoyancy = drag\n\n- $mg-\\rho_wVg=6\\pi\\eta rv$\n\n- $\\rho_sVg-\\rho_wVg=6\\pi\\eta rv$\n\nWhere $\\rho_s$ is the density of the sphere, $\\rho_w$ is the density of water, $V$ is the volume of the sphere, $\\eta$ is the viscosity of water, $r$ is the radius of the sphere, and $v$ is the velocity of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting the volume of the sphere as $V=\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3$ and factorizing, we have:\n\n- $\\rho_s\\left(\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3\\right)g-\\rho_w\\left(\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3\\right)g=6\\pi\\eta rv$\n\n- $\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3g(\\rho_s-\\rho_w)=6\\pi\\eta rv$\n\nFinally cancelling a factor of $r$:\n\n- $\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^2g(\\rho_s-\\rho_w)=6\\pi\\eta v$\n\nComparing spheres $A$ and $B$ we have:\n\n- $\\begin{aligned}\n\\ r^2&\\propto v\\\\\n\\\\\\\n\\ \\dfrac{r^2}{v}&=\\textrm{ constant}\\\\\n\\\\\\\n\\ \\dfrac{(r_A)^2}{v_A}&=\\dfrac{(r_B)^2}{v_B}\\\\\n\\\\\\\n\\ \\dfrac{v_A}{v_B}&=\\dfrac{(r_A)^2}{(r_B)^2}\\\\\n\\\\\\\n\\ \\dfrac{v_A}{v_B}&=\\left(\\dfrac{r_A}{r_B}\\right)^2\\\\\n\\\\\\\n\\ \\dfrac{v_A}{v_B}&=\\left(\\dfrac{2}{1}\\right)^2\\\\\n\\\\\\\n\\ \\dfrac{v_A}{v_B}&=4\\\\\n\\end{aligned}$"},{"ref":"PH1889","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1889\n\nTwo objects of mass $m$ and $2m$ are stacked vertically as shown. A horizontal force $F$ is applied to the lower object, causing the system to accelerate. When the acceleration reaches 1 m s$^{-2}$ the top object just begins to slide.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/ph1889q.png\" alt=\"ph1889q\">\n\nWhat is the coefficient of static friction between the objects?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.05\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.1\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.2\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.4","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe maximum frictional force between the two objects is what keeps the top object from sliding. This friction provides the only horizontal force and therefore the net force acting on the top object.\n\n- $F_f=(2m)a$\n\nWhen the top object just begins to slide, static friction is at its maximum value:\n\n- $F_f=\\mu_sF_N=\\mu_s(2m)g$\n\n- $\\mu_s(2m)g=2ma$\n\n- $\\mu_sg=a$\n\n- $\\mu_s=\\dfrac{a}{g}=\\dfrac{1}{10}=0.1$"},{"ref":"PH1896","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1896\n\nTwo objects $A$ and $B$ of the same mass undergo an elastic collision. Which of the following shows the correct velocity-time graph for $A$ and $B$?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    | $\\hspace{2cm}$  |B.   ||\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-08-26%20at%204.01.26%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-08-26 at 4.01.26 PM\">   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-08-26%20at%204.01.32%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-08-26 at 4.01.32 PM\">|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-08-26%20at%204.01.39%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-08-26 at 4.01.39 PM\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Forces%20%26%20Momentum/images/Screenshot%202025-08-26%20at%204.01.43%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-08-26 at 4.01.43 PM\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nDuring any collision between two objects, the rate of change of momentum of the objects $\\dfrac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t}$ must be equal and opposite (Newton's third law).\n\n$\\Delta p=\\Delta(mv)$\nand $A$ and $B$ have the same mass, so in this case the rate of change of velocity of the objects must be equal and opposite. We can therefore rule out options $C$ and $D$ as the gradients of the graphs during the collision are not equal and opposite.\n\nKinetic energy is conserved in an elastic collision. In option $B$ both of the objects have less kinetic energy after the collision than before the collision, so the correct answer is option $A$."}]},{"name":"Kinematics","questions":[{"ref":"PH0033","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0033\n\nA cargo container is released from an airplane. A parachute is opened remotely a few seconds later to land it safely. Which graph shows the variation of the vertical acceleration $a$ with time $t$ for the cargo container from when it leaves the plane until landing?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |   |  B. ||\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-27%20at%202.15.24%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-27 at 2.15.24 PM\"> |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-27%20at%202.15.44%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-27 at 2.15.44 PM\">|\n|C. |   |   |  D.| |\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-27%20at%202.15.50%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-27 at 2.15.50 PM\">|  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-27%20at%202.15.56%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-27 at 2.15.56 PM\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe cargo starts with large negative (downward) acceleration because the instant that it is released from the plane its acceleration is equal to the acceleration due to gravity.\n\n As the cargo container falls, it is speeding up; as it is speeding up, drag force increases, so the acceleration decreases until the cargo container reaches a terminal speed where acceleration is zero. After the parachute is opened, drag force will increase, which is greater than the weight of the cargo container, causing an opposite, upwards, acceleration (deceleration).\n\n As the cargo container slows down the drag force decreases, so the net force decreases, and acceleration decreases again until the cargo container reaches a lower terminal speed where acceleration is zero and the forces are balanced."},{"ref":"PH0080","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0080\n\nAn object moves at constant velocity for a period of time, after which it undergoes uniform deceleration until it comes to rest. On a velocity-time graph, which of the following would represent the displacement of the object?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Gradient of the line connecting the initial and final velocities.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ x-intercept\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ y-intercept\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Area under the graph","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFor option A, the gradient of the line connecting the starting and ending velocities gives the average acceleration.\n\nFor option B, x-intercept represents the time of when velocity is zero, therefore is incorrect.\n\nFor option C, y-intercept represents the velocity when the time is zero, therefore is incorrect.\n\nFor option D, area under the graph is the displacement, which is the correct answer."},{"ref":"PH0081","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0081\n\nThe graph shows the variation with time $t$ of the velocity $v$ of a car traveling along a straight and level road.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-06-05%20at%208.45.48%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-05 at 8.45.48 AM\">\n\nWhat time does it take for the car to travel a distance of 31.5 m from $t$ = 0?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 2 s\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 7 s\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 10 s\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 12 s","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nDisplacement is the area under the graph of velocity-time. Breaking the shape into several areas up to time 7 s:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-06-05%20at%208.59.10%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-05 at 8.59.10 AM\">\n\n- Area = $\\dfrac{1}{2}(2)(4) + (5)(4) + \\dfrac{1}{2}(5)(3) = 31.5$ m"},{"ref":"PH0082","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0082\n\nAn object is thrown vertically upwards at the edge of a cliff at height $h$ above the ground. When the object passes the edge of the cliff while falling downwards, a second, identical object is released from rest. Air resistance is negligible. What increases for the second object as the two objects fall?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Acceleration\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Distance from the ground\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Velocity relative to the object released earlier\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Distance from the object released earlier","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFor options A & C, the accelerations of both objects are gravitational acceleration and constant, therefore the velocity difference is constant and both options are incorrect.\n\nFor option B, the object released later was released from rest and so the distance from the ground decreases. Therefore, B is incorrect.\n\nFor option D, the relative velocity of the two objects is constant because the acceleration for both objects is the same, therefore the distance between the two objects will increase and it is the correct option. In other words, the first object will always be moving faster than the second object, so the distance between them will increase."},{"ref":"PH0123","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0123\n\nThe diagram below shows a car of mass $m$ descending a slope. The car, initially at rest, covers a distance of 27 m in 3.0 s.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH0123.png\" alt=\"PH0123\">\n\nThe magnitude of the acceleration is given by\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 3.0 m s$^{-2}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 6.0 m s$^{-2}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 9.0 m s$^{-2}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 81 m s$^{-2}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFrom the equation of motion\n\n- $ s = ut + \\dfrac{1}{2} at^2$\n\n initially the velocity, $u$ is zero giving\n\n- $a = \\dfrac{\\textrm{2 s}}{t^2} \\hspace{1em}$\n\nSubstituting $t = 3$ s:\n\n- $a=\\dfrac{\\textrm{(2)(27.0 m)}}{(3.0\\ \\ce{s})^2} = 6.0$ m s$^{-2}$"},{"ref":"PH0132","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0132\n\nA projectile is fired with a launch angle $\\alpha$ above the horizontal and an initial velocity of 40 m s$^{-1}$ towards a cliff that rises 300 m above the plane of projection. The cliff is 120 m from the launch point.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH0132yyy.png\" alt=\"PH0132yyy\">\n\nAssuming that air resistance has no effect on the motion, then the time of flight taken by the projectile to reach the face of the cliff, in seconds, is given by\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em} t = 3$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}t = 7.5$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}t = \\dfrac{4}{\\cos\\alpha} $\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}t = \\dfrac{3}{\\cos\\alpha}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nTime is determined from the horizontal motion\n\n- $u_x=40\\cos\\alpha$\n\n- $x = u_xt=(40 \\cos\\alpha)t$\n\n- $120=(40\\cos\\alpha)t$\n\n- $t = \\dfrac{3}{\\cos\\alpha}$"},{"ref":"PH0145","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0145\n\nA small object is fired horizontally at a speed of 300 m s$^{-1}$ from the top of a building. Air resistance is negligible. The object lands 900 m from the base of the building. What is the height of the building?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 15 m\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 34 m\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 44 m\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 88 m","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nCalculate the time taken for the small object to land\n\n- Speed = $\\dfrac{\\textrm{distance}}{\\textrm{time}} \\Rightarrow \\textrm{time} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{distance}}{\\textrm{speed}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{900}{300}$ = 3 s\n\nFor the vertical motion, u = 0 m s$^{-1}$, a = g = 9.81 m s$^{-2}$ , t = 3 s\n\nUsing the uniform acceleration equation:\n\n- $s = ut + \\dfrac{1}{2}at^2$\n\n- $s = 0 + \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg)(9.81)(3)^2$\n\n- $s = 44$ m\n\nNote that using $g=10$ m s$^{-2}$  here gives an answer of 45 m, with the closest answer being $44$ m."},{"ref":"PH0160","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0160\n\nThe take off speed of a passenger aircraft is 75.0 m s$^{-1}$. A plane starts from rest and accelerates at a steady rate of 1.25 m s$^{-2}$. Which of the following correctly states the minimum duration of the acceleration, and the minimum length of the runway needed for the take off?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Duration of acceleration | Length of the runway |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{75.0\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}}{1.25\\textrm{ m s}^{-2}}$ | $\\dfrac{(75.0\\textrm{ m s}^{-1})^2}{(2)(1.25\\textrm{ m s}^{-2})}$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\dfrac{75.0\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}}{1.25\\textrm{ m s}^{-2}}$ | $\\dfrac{(75.0\\textrm{ m s}^{-1})^2}{1.25\\textrm{ m s}^{-2}}$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{(75.0\\textrm{ m s}^{-1})^2}{(2)(1.25\\textrm{ m s}^{-2})}$ | $\\dfrac{75.0\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}}{1.25\\textrm{ m s}^{-2}}$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{(75.0\\textrm{ m s}^{-1})^2}{(2)(1.25\\textrm{ m s}^{-2})}$ | $\\dfrac{(75.0\\textrm{ m s}^{-1})^2}{(2)(1.25\\textrm{ m s}^{-2})}$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n- $a = \\dfrac{v-u}{t}$\n\n- $\\hspace{2em} \\Rightarrow t = \\dfrac{v-u}{a} = \\dfrac{75.0\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}-0}{1.25\\textrm{ m s}^{-2}} = \\dfrac{75.0\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}}{1.25\\textrm{ m s}^{-2}}$\n\n- $v^2 = u^2 + 2as $\n\n- $\\hspace{2em} \\Rightarrow s = \\dfrac{v^2-u^2}{2a} = \\dfrac{(75.0\\textrm{ m s}^{-1})^2-0^2}{2(1.25\\textrm{ m s}^{-2})} = \\dfrac{(75.0\\textrm{ m s}^{-1})^2}{2(1.25\\textrm{ m s}^{-2})}$"},{"ref":"PH0161","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0161\n\nA projectile is fired at an angle above the horizontal on the surface of the Moon. Which of the following statements is correct about its motion:\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The magnitude of the horizontal component of its velocity remains unchanged until it falls on the surface of the Moon.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The magnitude of the vertical component of its velocity remains unchanged until it falls on the surface of the Moon.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ The magnitude of the horizontal component of its velocity decreases steadily until it falls on the surface of the Moon.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The magnitude of the vertical component of its velocity decreases steadily until it falls on the surface of the Moon.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe Moon has no atmosphere - it is a reasonable expectation that students know this information (and if you didn't know it, take note). As there is no air drag, there's no change in the horizontal component of velocity. It does not decrease (making option C incorrect, and option A correct).\n\nThe magnitude of vertical component decreases only while the projectile moves upwards. At its maximum height, the vertical component becomes zero and then starts increasing in the downward direction until it hits ground (making D incorrect).\n\nOption B is obviously incorrect as the Moon's gravitation keeps changing the vertical component."},{"ref":"PH0204","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0204\n\nThe graph shows the variation of velocity $v$ with time $t$ of a car.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-04-07%20at%208.41.51%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-07 at 8.41.51 AM\">\n\nConsider the following quantities:\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | Displacement |\n  | II. | Acceleration |\n  | III. | Change in velocity |\n\nAssuming there were numerical values on the axes, which of the following could be deduced from the graph?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nChange in velocity can be read from the vertical axis.\n\nThe area under the line gives the displacement.\n\nAcceleration can be deduced from the gradient of the graph.\n\nTherefore, all statements are correct and the answer is D."},{"ref":"PH0242","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0242\n\nA ball is released from rest and falls vertically for 3 s on Earth. Air resistance is negligible. What is the average speed of the ball during the motion?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 3 m s$^{-1}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 10 m s$^{-1}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 15 m s$^{-1}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 30 m s$^{-1}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nSolution 1:\n\n- $v = u + at = 0 + (10)(3) = 30$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n- $v_{av} = \\dfrac{v+u}{2} = \\dfrac{30+0}{2} = 15$ m s$^{-1}$\n\nSolution 2: \\\nDistance covered by the ball;\n\n- $s = \\dfrac{1}{2} at^2$\n\nDue to absence of air resistance;\n\n- $a = g$\n\n- $s = \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg)(10)(3)^2$\n\n- $s = 45$ m\n\n$\\textrm{average speed} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{total distance}}{\\textrm{total\\ time}}$\n\n$\\textrm{average speed} = \\dfrac{45}{3} = 15$ m s$^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH0259","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0259\n\nA pilot intends to fly a plane from point $A$ to point $B$. Due to strong winds, she points the nose of the plane in the direction shown by the vector $P$. Which of the choices below best represents the direction of the wind encountered by the plane?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH0259-1.png\" alt=\"PH0259-1\">\n\n| | | | | |\n\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---| \n\n| A.  |    |  |  B. ||\n\n|<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH0259A.png\" alt=\"PH0259A\"> | $\\hspace{2cm}$   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH0259B.png\" alt=\"PH0259B\">|\n\n| $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n|C. |   |   | D. | |\n\n|<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH0259C.png\" alt=\"PH0259C\"> |   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH0259D.png\" alt=\"PH0259D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nIn order for the plane to travel from $A$ to $B$, the resultant vector of the sum of the wind vector and the vector of the orientation of the plane must point in the same direction as the vector from $A$ to $B$, as shown in the diagram below. For any of the other choices, the resultant will not be in the direction $A$ to $B$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH0259aaa.png\" alt=\"PH0259aaa\">"},{"ref":"PH0260","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0260\n\nA coin is tossed vertically upwards from an original height of $1.5$ m above the ground. What is the maximum height reached **above the ground** if the coin was projected with an initial velocity of 3.0 m s$^{-1}$?  (Take $g=10$ m s$^{-2}$)\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em} 0.45$ m\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em} 1.50$ m\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em} 1.95$ m\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em} 6.00$ m","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nSolution 1: \\\nMaximum height occurs when the final speed $v$ is zero. The height reached above the hand\n\n- $\\begin{aligned}\n\\ s &= \\dfrac{v^2 - u^2}{2a}\\\\\n\\ s&=\\dfrac{0-9}{-20} \\\\\n\\ &= 0.45\\textrm{ m}\\\\\n\\end{aligned}$\n\nTo find maximum height above the ground, $0.45$ m $+\\ 1.5$ m = 1.95 m\n\nSolution 2: \\\nUsing conservation of energy, kinetic energy is converted completely into gravitational potential energy at the top of the motion\n\n- $\\dfrac{1}{2} mv^2 = mgh$\n\nCancelling mass $m$ from both sides gives\n\n- $\\dfrac{1}{2} (3)^2 = 10h$\n\n- $h=0.45$ m\n\nTo find maximum height above the ground, $0.45$ m $+\\ 1.5$ m = 1.95 m"},{"ref":"PH0265","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0265\n\nA train, initially at rest, accelerates steadily at $1.0$ m s$^{-2}$. The distance $s$ travelled during each successive second, for the first five seconds of its motion, was recorded in meters. Which of the following correctly represents the distance covered in each successive second?\n\n(Diagram is not to scale)\n\n| |  |\n| :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{3.5em}$| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202026-03-12%20at%207.14.51%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-03-12 at 7.14.51 AM\">\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{3.5em}$ | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202026-03-12%20at%207.15.04%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-03-12 at 7.15.04 AM\">|\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{3.5em}$ |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202026-03-12%20at%207.15.21%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-03-12 at 7.15.21 AM\"> |\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{3.5em}$ | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202026-03-12%20at%207.15.23%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-03-12 at 7.15.23 AM\"> |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nSince the question requires values of distance and provides values of initial velocity, acceleration and time, use a suitable kinematics equation to determine the distance travelled from 0 - 1 s, 0 - 2 s, 0 - 3 s, 0 - 4 s, and 0 - 5 s.\n\nThen find the distance travelled during each second by subtraction (Subtract the distance traveled in the first second from the distance traveled in the first two seconds, etc.)\n\nCompare your values of distance for each second with the choices given to determine the most suitable choice.\n\n1$^{\\textrm{st}}$ second: $s = \\dfrac{1}{2}at^2 = \\dfrac{1}{2}(1)(1)^2 = 0.5$ m\n\nTime 0 to 2: $s = \\dfrac{1}{2}at^2 = \\dfrac{1}{2}(1)(2)^2 = 2$ m (distance traveled 1.5 m)"},{"ref":"PH0280","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0280\n\nA basketball is launched at an angle and undergoes projectile motion. Air resistance is negligible. Which of the following is correct for the direction of its velocity vector and its acceleration vector at the highest point of its trajectory?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Direction of velocity vector | Direction of acceleration vector |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| horizontal | no acceleration |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | no velocity | no acceleration |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | no velocity | downward |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | horizontal | downward |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAt the top of its trajectory, vertical velocity will be zero, but the horizontal component of the velocity will maintain the same value throughout the motion, therefore velocity will be horizontal.\n\nAcceleration due to gravity is always acting and acts downward."},{"ref":"PH0281","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0281\n\nA football player kicks a ball initially at rest. The ball reaches a speed of 36 km h$^{-1}$ in a time of 0.5 s. Which of the following is the acceleration of the ball in terms of the gravitational acceleration, $g$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.2$g$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.5$g$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2$g$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 7$g$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nConverting 36 km h$^{-1}$ to m s$^{-1}$:\n\n- 36 km h$^{-1}=\\dfrac{36000\\ \\ce{m}}{3600\\ \\ce{s}} = 10\\ \\ce{ m s}^{-1}$\n\nAcceleration is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{change\\ in\\ velocity}}{\\textrm{change\\ in\\ time}}$\n\n therefore\n\n- $a=\\dfrac{10}{0.5} = 20$  m s$^{-2}$\n\nApproximating $g$ to be 10 m s$^{-2}$, the correct answer is  $2g$."},{"ref":"PH0282","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0282\n\nA student throws a ball with an initial velocity in the horizontal direction. Air resistance is negligible. At $t = 2.0$ s, the ball has travelled a distance $x$ in the horizontal direction and a distance $y$ in the vertical direction.\n\nWhat are the horizontal and vertical distances covered at $t = 1.0$ s, in terms of $x$ and $y$?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Horizontal distance | Vertical distance |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{x}{2}$ | $\\dfrac{y}{4}$ |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{x}{2}$ | $\\dfrac{y}{2}$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\dfrac{x}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{y}{2}$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{x}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{y}{4}$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nHorizontal velocity is constant, therefore, the distance travelled will be half in half the time.\n\nVertical height is proportional to $t^2$ ($s=\\dfrac{1}{2}at^2$) therefore if time is divided by 2, height will be divided by $2^2$ = 4."},{"ref":"PH0328","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0328\n\nA ball rolls horizontally off the edge of the top of a cliff. It hits the ground at a distance 2.0 m horizontally away from the base of the cliff with a vertical speed of 20 m s$^{-1}$. What is the height of the cliff?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 20 m\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 40 m\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 60 m\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 80 m","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nOnly the vertical motion needs to be considered here - the 2.0 m is not relevant to the calculation.\n\n- $v^2=u^2+2as$\n\nThere is initially no vertical velocity so $u=0$\n\n- $v^2=2gs$\n\n- $20^2 = (2)(10)(s)$\n\n- $s = \\dfrac{400}{(2)(10)}$\n\n- $s = 20$ m\n\nNote that for multiple choice questions it is normally appropriate to use 10 m s$^{-2}$ as the acceleration of freefall."},{"ref":"PH0331","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0331\n\nA ball is released from rest at a height $h$ above the ground. At each bounce 75% of its kinetic energy is lost. Which graph represents the variation of the ball's velocity $v$ with time $t$ from the beginning of motion until the moment just before the third bounce? Assume the acceleration of the ball is in the negative direction and that air resistance is negligible.\n\n| | | | | |\n\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n\n| A.  |    |  |   |B.|\n\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-27%20at%205.31.14%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-27 at 5.31.14 PM\"> |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-27%20at%205.31.26%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-27 at 5.31.26 PM\">|\n\n|C. |   |   |  |D. |\n\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-27%20at%205.31.34%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-27 at 5.31.34 PM\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-27%20at%205.31.41%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-27 at 5.31.41 PM\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nKinetic energy $E_k$ is directly proportional to the square of the magnitude of velocity $v$\n\n- $E_k = \\dfrac{1}{2} mv^2$\n\nThe velocity will decrease to half of its value at each bounce. \n\nThe value of the acceleration is constant, free fall acceleration $g$. The velocity time graph should be linear and all sections should have a negative gradient to represent downwards acceleration. \n\nThe direction of velocity is positive on the way up, and negative on the way down."},{"ref":"PH0424","marks":"11","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0424\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\n1. State the difference between distance and displacement. [2 marks]\n\n2. A dart player throws a dart horizontally with the speed of $15$ m s$^{-1}$ to hit a dartboard. The vertical displacement of the dart is $0.35$ m. Air resistance is negligible.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%2010.49.06%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 10.49.06 AM\">\n\n    1. Show that the time taken for the dart to reach the centre of the dartboard is approximately 0.3 s. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Calculate the angle below the horizontal, in degrees, of the velocity of the dart as it hits the dartboard. [3 marks]\n\n    3. Determine the displacement of the dart. [2 marks]\n\n    4. On the axes below, sketch how the square of the velocity $v^2$ of the dart varies with the square of time $t^2$. [2 marks]\n\n        (no need to add values on the axes)\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%2010.49.33%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 10.49.33 AM\">","markScheme":"1. Distance is the length of the path followed while displacement is the change in position $\\checkmark$\n\n    Distance is a scalar quantity while displacement is a vector quantity $\\checkmark$\n\n2. 1. Use of $t = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2s}{a}}$\n\n        - **OR**\n\n        Use of $v = \\sqrt{2as}$ and $t = \\dfrac{v}{a}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $t =  \\sqrt{\\dfrac{(2)(0.35)}{9.81}} $ = :answer[ 0.27 s] $\\checkmark$ (extra significant figures must be shown for MP2)\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$ \\\n        The initial velocity of the dart has no vertical component and accelerates due to gravity on the vertical axis. Therefore, the time of motion in the vertical axis can be found by following the suvat equation;\n\n        - $s = ut + \\dfrac{1}{2} at^2$ where $u=0$, $a=9.81$ m s$^{-2}$ and $s=0.35$ m\n\n        After substituting the values;\n\n        - $0.35 = \\dfrac{1}{2} (9.81 )t^2$\n\n        By rearranging;\n\n        - $t = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{(2)(0.35)}{9.81}} = 0.27$ s\n\n    2. vertical velocity $= 0 + (9.81)(0.27) =2.6$ m s$^{-1}$\n    $\\checkmark$\n\n        horizontal velocity $= 15$ m s$^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\tan\\, \\theta = \\dfrac{2.6}{15}$  giving   :answer[$\\theta$ = 10°] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$ \\\n        The horizontal component of the velocity of the dart is constant. Therefore, the horizontal component of velocity at the instant of reaching the dartboard is $15$ m s$^{-1}$.\n\n        The vertical component of velocity can be found by the following equation;\n\n        - $v=u+at$ where $u=0$, $a=9.81$ m s$^{-2}$ and $t=0.27$ s\n\n        By substituting known values;\n\n        - $v=0+(9.81)(0.27)=2.6$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n        Hence, the relationship between horizontal, vertical, and resultant velocity in terms of their directions is given as shown\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%2010.56.02%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 10.56.02 AM\">\n\n        By using geometry;\n\n        - $\\tan\\theta=\\dfrac{2.6}{15}$  giving\n         $\\theta=10\\degree$\n\n    3. Horizontal displacement$=(15)(0.27)=4.05$ m $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $s = \\sqrt{(s_h)^2 + (s_v)^2} = \\sqrt{4.05^2 + 0.35^2}=4.1$ m $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$ \\\n        The horizontal displacement $s_h$;\n\n        - $s_h=u_ht$ where $u_h=15$ m s$^{-1}$ and $t = 0.27$ s\n\n        - $s_h=(15)(0.27)=4.05$ m\n\n        The vertical displacement $s_v$;\n\n        - $s_v = 0.35$ m\n\n        The resultant displacement *s* can be found by geometry;\n\n        - $s = \\sqrt{(s_h)^2 + (s_v)^2} = \\sqrt{4.05^2 + 0.35^2} = 4.1$ m\n\n    4.  Straight line $\\checkmark$\n\n        Non-zero y-intercept $\\checkmark$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%2010.51.44%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 10.51.44 AM\">\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$ \\\n        The velocity of the dart has two components horizontal $v_x$ and vertical $v_y$. Therefore, it is;\n\n        - $v = \\sqrt{(v_x)^2 + (v_y)^2}$\n\n        Only $v_y$ changes by the following equation;\n\n        - $v_y = at$ where $a = g$\n\n        By substituting $a = g$ in the equation;\n\n        - $v_y = gt$\n\n        Replace $v_y$ with $gt$ in the previous equation;\n\n        - $v = \\sqrt{(v_x)^2 + (g^2t^2)}$\n\n        By taking the square of both sides to find a relationship between $v^2$ and $t^2$;\n\n        - $v^2 = (v_x)^2 + g^2t^2$\n\n        Since $v^2_x$ and $g^2$ are constant, this relationship can be summarized as;\n\n        - $y=mx+b$ where $(v_x)^2=b=\\textrm{y-intercept}$ and $g^2=m=\\textrm{slope}$\n\n        Both of the values are positive. Therefore; the graph should be a straight line with a positive gradient and positive y-intercept."},{"ref":"PH0425","marks":"14","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0425\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\n1.\n    1. Define acceleration. [1 mark]\n\n    2. A car is moving on a straight road. The variation of the car's velocity $v$ with time $t$ is seen in the graph below.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%2011.18.30%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 11.18.30 AM\">\n\n        Describe, in terms of acceleration, the motion of the car. [2 marks]\n\n2. A truck with a speed of 20 m s$^{-1}$ passes by a stationary car. The car starts to move with an acceleration of 4 m s$^{-2}$ just as the truck goes past. Both of the vehicles move in the same direction.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%2011.20.52%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 11.20.52 AM\">\n\n    1. Show that the time needed for the car to overtake the truck is 10 s. [3 marks]\n\n    2. On the graph below, draw the variation in speed $v$ of both vehicles with time $t$ during the first 10 seconds of the motion and comment about the areas under the graphs without calculation. [4 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-07-03%20at%204.43.13%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-03 at 4.43.13 PM\">\n\n3. In an accident scene, skid marks of a car stretching 100 m are found. According to the manufacturer of the car's tires, the deceleration that the car experiences with skidding tires is 5 m s$^{-2}$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%2011.35.58%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 11.35.58 AM\">\n\n    Determine whether the car exceeded the speed limit of 72 km h$^{-1}$. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. Rate of change of velocity $\\checkmark$\n\n    2. Accelerates at an increasing rate/increasing acceleration $\\checkmark$\n\n        Accelerates at a constant rate/constant acceleration $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$ \\\n        The gradient of the velocity-time graph shows the acceleration of the car. Initially, the slope increases. Then the steepness of the graph is constant. Therefore, acceleration of the car increases initially then it is constant.\n\n2. 1. Use of $s = ut + \\dfrac{1}{2} at^2$ for the car $\\checkmark$\n\n        Equating distances covered by both vehicles $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\ll s = 2t^2 = 20t \\gg$\n\n        - $\\ll t^2 = 10t \\gg$\n\n        - $t =$ :answer[10 s] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$ \\\n        Since they are at the same position initially, the car has to cover the same distance as the truck in the same time interval. The car accelerates uniformly which enables us to employ suvat equations. The distance travelled by car for a time of $t$;\n\n        - $s = ut + \\dfrac{1}{2} at^2$ where $u = 0$ and $a = 4$ m s$^{-2}$\n\n        Therefore;\n\n        - $s = \\dfrac{1}{2} (4)t^2 = 2t^2$\n\n        The truck moves at a constant speed. Hence, its distance for the time of $t$;\n\n        - $s = vt$\n\n        - $s = 20t$\n\n        When both of the distances are equated;\n\n        - $s = 2t^2 = 20t$\n\n        - $t^2 = 10t$\n\n        - $t = 10$ $s$\n\n    2. Straight line $\\checkmark$\n\n        Starts from (0,0) and ends at (10,40) $\\checkmark$\n\n        Horizontal line starts from (0,20) and ends at (10,20) $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\ll$ area under the graph of speed time gives distance travelled by the vehicles. Since $\\gg$ they cover the same distance in 10 s according to 2 b (i)   **and** areas are equal/same $\\checkmark$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-07-03%20at%204.45.25%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-03 at 4.45.25 PM\">\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$ \\\n        The slope of a speed-time graph of an object moving in one direction is acceleration. Since the car travels with a constant acceleration, the slope of the graph should be constant. Therefore, the graph should be a straight line. The car starts from rest and its speed at $t=10$ s can be found by;\n\n        - $v=u+at$ where $u=0$, $a=4$ m s$^{-1}$ and $t=10$ s\n\n        - $v=0+(4)(10)=40$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n        The speed of the truck does not change and it moves at 20 m s$^{-1}$. Its graph should be a horizontal line which has a y-intercept of 20.\n\n        The areas under the graphs of speed-time equal to the distance covered by the vehicles. Since both of the vehicles travelled the same distance in 10 seconds according to the previous part of the question, the areas should be the same.\n\n3. Use of $2as=u^2$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $u=31.6$ m s$^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    Conversion of $72$ km h$^{-1}$ to $20$ m s$^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $31.6$ m s$^{-1} > 20$ m s$^{-1}$ and yes $\\ll$ it exceeds $\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$ \\\n    The speed before pushing the brakes can be found by;\n\n    - $v^2=u^2+2as$ where $v=0$, $a=-5$  m s$^{-2}$ and $s=100$ m\n\n    Substitute given values in the equation;\n\n    - $0 = u^2 + (2)(-5)(100)$\n\n    - $0 = u^2 - 1000$\n\n    - $1000 = u^2$\n\n    - $u = 31.62$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n    To compare with the speed limit, the unit of speed limit should be converted to m s$^{-1}$ or the initial speed should be converted to km h$^{-1}$\n\n    - $\\dfrac{\\textrm{72 km}}{\\textrm{1 h}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{72000 m}}{\\textrm{3600 s}} = 20$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n    Since 31.62 m s$^{-1}$ > 20 m s$^{-1}$ the car exceeds the speed limit."},{"ref":"PH0492","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0492\n\nA stream flows east with a speed $v_s$. A boat points due south and moves with a speed $v_o$ relative to the water.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screensho0t%202024-10-26%20at%203.57.21%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screensho0t 2024-10-26 at 3.57.21 PM\">\n\nThe ratio  $\\dfrac{v_o}{v_s} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$\n\nWhich of the following is correct about the magnitude in m s$^{-1}$ and the angle $\\theta$ between the velocity of the boat and the side of the stream?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$ Magnitude $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{2.5em}$ $\\theta$ $\\hspace{2.5em}$ |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ A. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\sqrt{3}\\,v_o$ | $\\sin^{-1}\\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg)$ |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ B. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\sqrt{3}\\,v_o$ | $\\cos^{-1}\\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg)$ |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ C. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\sqrt{5}\\,v_o$ | $\\tan^{-1}(2)$ |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ D. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\sqrt{5}\\,v_o$ | $\\tan^{-1}\\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg)$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nSince $v_s = 2\\,v_o$\n\nThe magnitude of the net velocity \n\n- $v_{net}=\\sqrt{v_o\\textrm{}^2 +(2v_o)^2} = \\sqrt{5} v_o$\n\nThe direction is determined by the angle it makes with the side of the river\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH0492zzz2.png\" alt=\"PH0492zzz2\">\n\n- $\\tan \\theta = \\dfrac{v_o}{2v_o} =\\dfrac{1}{2}$  \n\n- $\\theta = \\tan^{-1}(\\dfrac{1}{2})$\n\nThus the previous answer of $\\sqrt{5} v_o$ combined with $\\theta = \\tan^{-1}(\\dfrac{1}{2})$ corresponds to answer D."},{"ref":"PH0494","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0494\n\nAn object moves 9 m east, then 12 m north. \n\nWhat is the magnitude of the total displacement of this object?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 3 m\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 15 m\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 21 m\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 36 m","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThrough vector addition, \n\nDisplacement = $\\sqrt{12^2+9^2}=$ 15 m\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-04-07%20at%2011.15.26%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-07 at 11.15.26 AM\">"},{"ref":"PH0495","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0495\n\nA ball is projected with an initial speed of 2 m s$^{-1}$ making an angle of $35\\degree$ with the horizontal as shown.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-04-07%20at%2011.06.31%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-07 at 11.06.31 AM\">\n\nWhich expression gives the vertical component of the velocity vector?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2\\cos(35\\degree)$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2\\sin(35\\degree)$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2\\tan(35\\degree$) m s$^{-1}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-04-07%20at%2011.06.37%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-07 at 11.06.37 AM\">\n\n- $\\sin(35\\degree) = \\dfrac{v_y}{2}$\n\nTherefore \n\n- $v_y = 2\\sin(35\\degree)$"},{"ref":"PH0496","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0496\n\nA car moves clockwise in a circular path as shown. The direction of its instantaneous velocity at two different times is shown in the diagram. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH0496.png\" alt=\"PH0496\">\n\nWhich of the following vectors shows the direction of the change in velocity?\n\n| | | | | |\n\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---| \n\n| A.  |   |  $\\hspace{3cm}$  | B.  ||\n\n|<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH0496A.png\" alt=\"PH0496A\"> |   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH0496B.png\" alt=\"PH0496B\">|\n\n|C. |   |   |  D.| |\n\n|<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH0496C.png\" alt=\"PH0496C\">|   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH0496D.png\" alt=\"PH0496D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe change in velocity = final velocity - initial velocity = $v_2 + (-v_1)$. In a vector diagram, we represent $-v_1$ as in the opposite direction to the $v_1$ direction, and then add the vectors $v_2$ and  $(-v_1)$ as shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH0496-1.png\" alt=\"PH0496-1\">"},{"ref":"PH0511","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH0511\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nIn an experiment, the free-fall of a ball bearing is used to measure the acceleration due to gravity. The ball bearing is suspended with an electromagnet. Then it is released from a height of $h$ above a table.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH0511_A.png\" alt=\"PH0511_A\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with chemix - https:// chemix.org/]}}$\n\nThe time *t* needed for the ball bearing to reach the ground after release is measured. The experiment is repeated for different values of $h$. The theoretically predicted relationship between time *t* and *h* is\n\n$t^2=\\dfrac{2h}{g}$\n\n1.   State why there is a need to collect data for a range of values of $h$ to verify this relationship. [1 mark]\n\n2.  The experimental data for $t^2$ and *h* are plotted on the graph below.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-04-16%20at%209.01.00%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-16 at 9.01.00 AM\">\n\n    1. Draw the line of best fit for the plotted data on the graph. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Suggest whether the data is consistent with the theoretical prediction. [2 marks]\n\n    3.     By using the line of best fit, determine a value for *g* and state the units. [4 marks]\n\n3. 1.     Compare the experimental result to the accepted value of *g* and suggest a reason for the discrepancy. [2 marks]\n\n    2. State and explain whether the source of error in (c)(i) is a systematic or random error. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. To prove the relationship between $t^2$ and *h* different values are needed to find a trend\n\n    **OR**\n\n    to reduce random error $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    To prove a relationship, one piece of data is not enough. The relationship can be generalized by using a graph where multiple points can reveal a trend. More data allows for drawing a graph that can support the relationship. Additionally, using more data and taking the average of them reduces the amount of random error.\n\n2.\n    1. A straight line with roughly equal numbers of points above and below the line $\\checkmark$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-04-16%20at%209.01.10%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-16 at 9.01.10 AM\">\n\n    2. $\\ll$ According to the theory $\\gg$  $t^2$ is proportional to $h$ **OR** ${t^2}\\propto\\ h$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        A best-fit line that is a straight line that passes through the origin can be drawn $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\ll$  Yes $\\gg$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        According to the predicted relationship;\n\n        - $t^2\\propto h$\n\n        This relationship suggests that the graph should be a straight line which passes through the origin. According to plotted data, that kind of straight line can be drawn.\n\n    3. Rearrangement of the formula to solve for $g$\n\n        - $g=2\\dfrac{h}{t^2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Using a correct  data point on the line\n\n        **OR**\n\n        Correct calculation of  the gradient $\\checkmark$\n\n        *g* is between :answer[9.0 and 9.5] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\ce{m s}^{-2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The given formula is\n\n        - $t^2=\\dfrac{2h}{g}$\n\n        The gradient is therefore equal to $\\dfrac{2}{g}$.\n\n        Finding the gradient gives\n\n        - gradient = $\\dfrac{0.19}{0.9}=0.21$\n\n        Solving for $g$:\n\n        - $0.21 = \\dfrac{2}{g}$\n\n        - $g=\\dfrac{2}{0.21}=9.5\\ \\ce{m s}^{-2}$\n\n3.\n    1. Air resistance acts on the object  $\\checkmark$\n\n        This increases the t value / increases the gradient $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The time *t* should be smaller than the measured value under the frictionless environment. According to the equation,\n\n        - $g=2\\bigg(\\dfrac{h}{t^2}\\bigg)$\n\n        Therefore in the absence of air resistance, the experimental value for $\\dfrac{h}{t^2}$ would be greater than the values obtained in this experiment. Therefore, the calculated value is lower than the accepted value.\n\n    2. Systematic $\\checkmark$\n\n        The result of the experiment is always less than the actual value/results are biased in one direction only $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The error is the result of the design of the experiment and leads to the results always being less than the actual value. Therefore, it is systematic."},{"ref":"PH0532","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0532\n\nThe graph shows the variation of speed $v$ of a ball with time $t$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-29%20at%201.10.05%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-29 at 1.10.05 PM\">\n\nThe initial speed of the ball is very high. Which of the following statements could be true for the ball?\n\n- A. It is thrown vertically upward in the presence of air resistance.\n\n- B. It is thrown vertically upward in the absence of air resistance.\n\n- C. It is thrown vertically downward in the presence of air resistance.\n\n- D. It is thrown vertically downward in the absence of air resistance.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAccording to the graph, the ball has a decreasing deceleration and reaches terminal velocity. Since the acceleration of the ball is not constant, air resistance must be acting on the ball. Air resistance always acts in the direction opposite to the velocity.\n\nIn an upward motion, air friction and gravity are in the same direction. Therefore, it is not possible for them to cancel each other so that the ball reaches terminal velocity. It should be moving in the downward direction. Hence, the answer is C."},{"ref":"PH0533","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0533\n\nA stone is thrown vertically upward in the presence of air resistance. Which of the following graphs best shows the variation of velocity $v$ of the stone with time $t$ until it reaches the maximum height above the ground?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n||  <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH0533aa.png\" alt=\"PH0533aa\"> |  ||<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH0533bb.png\" alt=\"PH0533bb\">\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH0533cc.png\" alt=\"PH0533cc\"> |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH0533dd.png\" alt=\"PH0533dd\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nWhile the stone is ascending, its speed decreases due to gravitational attraction and air resistance. Although gravitational attraction is constant, air resistance decreases as the speed decreases. Therefore, the total force opposing the motion decreases. This results in a decrease in the magnitude of the deceleration.\n\nWhen the acceleration decreases, the change in velocity gets lower for a constant change in time. Thus the gradient should decrease. Additionally, the velocity of the stone must be zero when it reaches maximum height. These two conditions are satisfied in choice C."},{"ref":"PH0535","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0535\n\nThe position of a body is located with a motion detector at constant time intervals.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-29%20at%2012.46.34%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-29 at 12.46.34 PM\">\n\nThe distance between two successive positions of the body is given in terms of a constant $d$. Which of the following graphs could be the variation of velocity $v$ of the body with time t?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n||<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-29%20at%2012.48.31%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-29 at 12.48.31 PM\">  | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-29%20at%2012.48.36%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-29 at 12.48.36 PM\">|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-30%20at%2010.36.48%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-30 at 10.36.48 AM\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-29%20at%2012.48.47%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-29 at 12.48.47 PM\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nEven though the instantaneous speed of the body is unknown, average speeds for each interval can be compared by using the distances covered for the same time interval. Initially, its average speed increases, then stays constant and finally decreases. Also, the object does not change direction, therefore it does not have negative velocity.  The only graph that satisfies these conditions is C."},{"ref":"PH0536","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0536\n\nAn object is launched with an angle of $\\theta$ from the ground and an initial velocity of $v$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-07-08%20at%203.52.21%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-08 at 3.52.21 PM\">\n\nWhich graph shows the variation of the horizontal component $v_h$ of the velocity of the object with time $t$? (Air resistance is not ignored)\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n|| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-29%20at%2011.45.57%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-29 at 11.45.57 AM\"> |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-29%20at%2011.44.36%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-29 at 11.44.36 AM\">|\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-29%20at%2011.44.41%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-29 at 11.44.41 AM\"> |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-29%20at%2011.44.46%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-29 at 11.44.46 AM\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe object starts its motion with non-zero horizontal velocity. Due to air resistance, It experiences negative acceleration (opposite to the direction of motion). The force from air resistance is proportional to the speed of the object. Since a decrease in speed results in a decrease in air resistance, the magnitude of negative acceleration should decrease over time. The gradient of the $v-t$ graphs indicates acceleration. The only graph that has a decreasing negative gradient is B.\nTherefore, the answer is B."},{"ref":"PH0538","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0538\n\nThe variation of displacement of a trolley with time $t$ is shown on the graph below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%201.57.43%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 1.57.43 PM\">\n\nThe magnitudes of the instantaneous velocities of the trolley at times $t_1$  and $t_2$ are $v_1$  and $v_2$ respectively. Which of the following correctly compares the instantaneous velocities with the magnitude of the average velocity of the trolley for the whole of the motion shown?\n\n| | $v_1$ | $v_2$ |\n| :- | :-: | :-: |\n| A. | Greater than the average velocity | Greater than the average velocity |\n| B. | Greater than the average velocity | Less than the average velocity |\n| C. | Less than the average velocity | Greater than the average velocity |\n| D. | Less than the average velocity | Less than the average velocity |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe magnitudes of instantaneous velocities can be found from the gradient of the graph at the corresponding points. The average velocity is the total displacement over time and it is mathematically equal to the slope of a straight line that connects points at the start and end of the motion on the graph.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%202.02.29%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 2.02.29 PM\">\n\nWhen the slopes of the lines are compared, it can be seen that\n\n$v_2>$ average velocity $>v_1$. Therefore the answer is C."},{"ref":"PH0539","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0539\n\nAn object is moving in a straight line. The graph shows the variation of the velocity $v$ of the object with time $t$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-10-24%20at%207.55.44%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-10-24 at 7.55.44 AM\">\n\nConsider the following statements about the graph:\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | At point A, the object changes its direction. |\n  | II. | At point C the object is at rest. |\n  | III. | The magnitude of the acceleration at point D is greater than at point B. |\n\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?\n\n- A. I only\n\n- B. II only\n\n- C. I and II only\n\n- D. II and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe slope of the velocity time graph gives the acceleration of the object. Therefore, at points A and D, there is no acceleration due to zero gradient. At point B, there is a negative acceleration.\n\nAdditionally, the velocity of the object at point C is zero as it aligns with the horizontal axis where $v=0$.\n\nHence, the answer is B."},{"ref":"PH0540","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0540\n\nA body is moving in a straight line with a constant acceleration of $a$. Its speed increases from $u$ to $v$ in a time of $t$. Consider the following expressions:\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. |     $ut+\\dfrac{(v-u)t}{2}$ |\n  |$\\hspace{1em}$||\n  | II. | $vt-\\dfrac{1}{2} at^2$ |\n  |$\\hspace{1em}$||\n  | III. | $\\dfrac{(v-u)t}{2}$ |\n\nWhich expression(s) can be used to determine the displacement of the body during the time interval $t$?\n\n- A. I only\n\n- B. II only\n\n- C. I and II only\n\n- D. II and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nWhen the expression in I is simplified, it is equal to;\n\n- $\\dfrac{(v+u)t}{2}$\n\nThis is a uniform acceleration formula for displacement, where the average velocity is multiplied by the time taken.\n\nThis is included in the data booklet as the formula of displacement.\n\nComparing expression III to expression I, it can be seen that expression III will give a lower value than expression I. Therefore, it does not give displacement.\n\n For expression II:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH0540.png\" alt=\"PH0540\">\n\nUsing the above graph to analyze expression II, the area under the sloping line will give the displacement. The area of this trapezoid can be found by subtracting the area of triangle $vXu$ from the area of rectangle $OvXt$.\n\nThe area of $OvXt$ is $vt$.\n\nThe area of $vXu$ is $\\frac{1}{2}(v-u)t$\n\nWe know that $v - u = at$, therefore after substitution, the area $vXu$ is equal to $\\frac{1}{2}at^2$.\n\nSubtracting the two areas gives the expression $vt - \\frac{1}{2}at^2$, which is the same as expression II.\n\nTherefore the correct answer is C"},{"ref":"PH0552","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0552\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nA ball of mass $1.6$ kg is dropped from a very high building. The only forces acting on the ball are the force of gravity and air resistance.  The graph shows the variation of the vertical velocity $v$ of the ball with time $t$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-07-02%20at%201.41.16%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-02 at 1.41.16 PM\">\n\n1. 1. Define terminal speed. [1 mark]\n\n    2. State the terminal speed of the ball.  [1 mark]\n\n2.  Estimate the vertical displacement of the ball during the $10.0$ s of motion. [2 marks]\n\n3.  1. Calculate the instantaneous acceleration of the ball at $t=2.0$ s. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Determine the magnitude of the vertical component of the force of air resistance at $t=2.0$ s [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  The maximum speed/velocity  obtained by an object during  motion in a fluid/gas/air/liquid $\\checkmark$\n\n    2. :answer[18] $\\ll\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        According to the graph, the velocity of the ball increases and then stays constant at 18 m s$^{-1}$.\n\n2. Any reference to the area under the graph being equal to the displacement. $\\checkmark$\n\n    :answer[$125\\pm10$] $\\ll\\textrm{m, downwards}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The area under the velocity-time graph is equal to the ball's displacement. The area can be calculated by using the small square approximation. The area $A_s$ of each small square:\n\n    - $A_s=(0.2)(1)=0.2$ m\n\n    There are approximately 625 small   squares. Therefore, the total area A or displacement:\n\n    - $A=(625)(0.2)=125$ m\n\n    Note that displacement is a vector quantity and so a direction should be stated. In this case, the ball is falling so the direction of the displacement is downwards.\n\n3. 1. Reference to use of the gradient of the graph to find instantaneous acceleration. $\\checkmark$\n\n        :answer[$2.8\\ \\pm\\ 0.5$] $\\ll\\textrm{m s}^{-2}\\gg $ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The gradient of the graph at a given time gives the instantaneous acceleration of the ball. To find the gradient, the tangent line should be drawn.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-07-02%20at%201.42.12%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-02 at 1.42.12 PM\">\n\n    2.  $mg-ma=$ air resistance $\\checkmark$\n\n        $(1.6)(9.81)-(1.6)(2.8)=$ air  resistance $\\checkmark$\n\n        :answer[11] $\\ll\\textrm{N}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        ECF for the use of the answer of (c) (i) to determine the acceleration\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The vertical resultant force $F_{net}$ acting on the mass:\n\n        - $F_{net}$ = Weight - Air Resistance\n\n        or\n\n        - $F_{net} = F_g - F_d$\n\n        and\n\n        - $F_{net}$ = $ma$\n\n        Therefore\n\n        - $ma = F_g - F_d$\n\n        - $F_d = mg - ma$\n\n        - $F_d=(1.6)(9.81)-(1.6)(2.8)$\n\n        - $F_d = 11.3$ N"},{"ref":"PH0553","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0553\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nThe graph shows how the speed *v* of a car varies with time *t*.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-07-03%20at%205.18.15%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-03 at 5.18.15 PM\">\n\n1. State the difference between average and instantaneous accelerations. [2 marks]\n\n2. Calculate the instantaneous acceleration of the car at $t=1.0$ s. [2 marks]\n\n3. Determine, with explanation, whether the car covers more distance before or after the car reaches a constant speed. [5 marks]","markScheme":"1. Average acceleration is the change in velocity over a period of time $\\checkmark$\n\n    Instantaneous acceleration is the acceleration at a particular instant/point in time $\\checkmark$\n\n2. Reference to the gradient of the graph = instantaneous acceleration $\\checkmark$\n\n    :answer[$7.9\\pm0.5\\ll\\textrm{m s}^{-2}\\gg$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The gradient of the graph at the interested time gives the instantaneous acceleration of the car. To find the gradient, the tangent line should be drawn. To do this, we draw a line that represents the gradient of the curve at the exact moment when $t=1$ s. Note that this line needs to be estimated by eye, and the mark scheme will normally have an allowed range of values to account for different answers.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-07-03%20at%205.21.16%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-03 at 5.21.16 PM\">\n\n    The gradient of the tangent line $= \\dfrac{\\textrm{rise}}{\\textrm{run}}=\\dfrac{26.0-11.0}{1.9-0}=7.9\\textrm{ m s}^{-2}$\n\n3. Time of reaching constant speed = 2.7 or 2.8 s $\\checkmark$\n\n    Any evidence of distance covered = area under the line $\\checkmark$\n\n    The distance covered at constant speed  $= \\ \\ll(25.0)(5.0-2.8)=\\ \\gg$ :answer[55]$\\ \\ll\\textrm{m}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    *Accept 58 m*\n\n    Any sensible estimate of the area to determine displacement before reaching a constant speed $\\checkmark$\n\n    After $\\checkmark$\n\n    Note that if plausible calculations are performed so tha the results are equal or that the distance moved before is larger, full marks will be awarded for a conclusion that matches the calculated result.\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The car reaches the constant speed of $25.0$ m s$^{-1}$ at $t=2.8$ s. This is the point where the graph becomes horizontal. The area under the speed-time graph equals the car\\'s distance travelled. The distance $d_1$ while moving at non-constant speed can be found by the method of small square approximation.\n\n    The area of one small square =$(1.0\\ \\ce{m s}^{-1})(0.1\\ \\ce{s}) = 0.1\\textrm{ m}$  and the number of small squares $= 524$\n\n    Alternatively, a larger square has an area = $(5\\ \\ce{m s}^{-1})(0.5\\ \\ce{s})=$ 2.5 m. Counting roughly, there are about 22 larger squares\n\n    $d_1=\\left(524\\right)\\left(0.1\\right)=52.4\\ $m $\\hspace{3em}$ or $d_1=\\left(22\\right)\\left(2.5\\right)=$ 55 m\n\n    The distance $d_2$ at a constant speed \\\n$d_2=(25)(5.0-2.8)=55$ m\n\n    $d_2$ > $d_1$  therefore, the car covers more distance after reaching the constant speed.\n\n    Or the distances are equal. (suitable conclusion based on the calculation performed)"},{"ref":"PH0554","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0554\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n1. Define displacement. [1 mark]\n\n2. A table tennis ball is thrown horizontally from the top of a building.  The variation of the ball's horizontal displacement $s$ with time $t$ is shown on the graph below.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-29%20at%205.40.560%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-29 at 5.40.560 PM\">\n\n    1. State which quantity is given by the gradient of the tangent to the graph at any time during the motion. [1 mark]\n\n    2. On the graph below, sketch the expected variation of horizontal displacement with time when air resistance is ignored. The original curve is given as a dashed line. [2 marks]\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-29%20at%205.46.15%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-29 at 5.46.15 PM\">\n\n3. A stone is then launched with a horizontal speed of $20$ m s$^{-1}$ from a height of $12$ m above the ground.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-29%20at%205.49.06%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-29 at 5.49.06 PM\">\n\n    Determine the magnitude of the displacement of the stone when it hits the ground if air resistance is considered to be negligible. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. Change in position **OR** straight line distance and direction between the starting and finishing points of journey $\\checkmark$\n\n2. 1. $\\underline{\\textrm{Horizontal}}$ velocity $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The gradient of a displacement-time graph at any point gives the velocity of the object at that instant.\n\n    2. $\\hspace{1em}$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-05-29%20at%205.51.01%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-29 at 5.51.01 PM\">\n\n        A straight line passing through the origin $\\checkmark$\n\n        Gradient matches starting gradient of curve $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Due to the absence of air resistance, the horizontal resultant force on the tennis ball will be zero. This results in zero acceleration. Therefore, the horizontal velocity will be constant. It can be represented by a straight line where the gradient matches the initial horizontal velocity of the object.\n\n3. The time needed to reach ground = 1.$6$ s $\\checkmark$\n\n    Horizontal displacement = $\\ll(20)(1.6)= \\gg 31\\ \\ll\\textrm{m}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    Displacement of the stone= $\\sqrt{{12}^2+{31}^2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $s=$ :answer[ 34 m] $\\checkmark$ (33 m also accepted here)\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Due to the absence of air resistance, the stone accelerates vertically due to gravity and the horizontal velocity of the stone is constant. The time $t$ taken to reach the ground can be found by\n\n    $\\begin{aligned}\n    s &= ut+\\frac{1}{2}at^2\\\\\n    -12 &= 0(t)+(\\frac{1}{2})(-9.81)t^2\\\\\n    t^2 &= 2.4\\\\\n    t &= 1.56\\textrm{ s} \\\\\n    \\end{aligned}$\n\n    Therefore, the horizontal displacement $s_h$ of the stone:\n\n    - $s_h=vt=\\left(20\\right)\\left(1.56\\right)=31$ m\n\n    The magnitude of the vertical displacement $s_v$ is $12$ m. Finding the magnitude of the displacement s of the stone:\n\n    $\\begin{aligned}\n    \\hspace{1.8em} s^2 &= (s_v)^2+(s_h)^2\\\\\n    s^2 &= {12}^2+{31}^2\\\\\n    s &= 33\\ or\\ 34\\, \\ce{m}\n    \\end{aligned}$"},{"ref":"PH0850","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0850\n\nA car decelerates with a constant rate until it stops.\n\nWhich graph shows the variation of speed of the car  $v$ with the distance travelled $s$?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    | $\\hspace{2cm}$  |   B.||\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH0850aa.png\" alt=\"PH0850aa\">  |  |  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH0850bb.png\" alt=\"PH0850bb\">|\n|C. |   |   | D. | |\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH0850cc.png\" alt=\"PH0850cc\">   |  |  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH0850dd.png\" alt=\"PH0850dd\">|\n\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Image created using https://www.geogebra.org/m/CtfF7SBc]}}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nNote that this is a velocity-displacement graph, and so needs to be considered carefully.\n\nSince\n\n- $v^2=u^2+2as$\n\nThe car decelerates to stop\n\n- $a$ is negative\n\nThen\n\n- $v^2=u^2-2as$\n\nAs the car moves slower, it takes more time to cover the same distance. The velocity changes at a constant rate. Therefore, each increment of distance has a larger and larger change in $velocity$."},{"ref":"PH0851","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0851\n\nA projectile is launched horizontally on Earth with an initial speed of $3.0$ m s$^{-1}$ from a height of $5.0$ m above the ground.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH0851aaaa.png\" alt=\"PH0851aaaa\">\n\nWhat is the magnitude of the displacement of the stone when it reaches the ground?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $5.0$ m\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $8.0$ m\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\sqrt{34}$ m\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\sqrt{51}$ m","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFor the time, analyzing the vertical direction\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $s = \\dfrac{1}{2}at^2$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $5 = \\dfrac{1}{2}(10)t^2$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $t = 1$ s\n\nThe horizontal displacement is\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\Delta x = u_x t = (3)(1) = 3$ m\n\nThe vertical displacement from the diagram is $5$ m.\n\nThe net displacement is\n\n- Displacement $=\\sqrt{5^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{34}$ m"},{"ref":"PH0930","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0930\n\nThe graph below shows the variation of velocity $v$ of an object with time $t$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-06-02%20at%2010.40.53%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-02 at 10.40.53 AM\">\n\nWhich of the following graphs shows how the displacement\n$s$ of the object, varies with $t$?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    | $\\hspace{2cm}$  |B.   ||\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-06-02%20at%2010.41.03%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-02 at 10.41.03 AM\"> |  | | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-06-02%20at%2010.41.09%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-02 at 10.41.09 AM\">|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-06-02%20at%2010.41.14%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-02 at 10.41.14 AM\"> | |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-06-02%20at%2010.41.21%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-02 at 10.41.21 AM\">|","markScheme":"**答案：B**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe area under a velocity-time graph is displacement. Both velocity and displacement are vectors so can be negative.\n\nThe graph in the question shows an object changing direction – (from the symmetry of the graph in the question we can see that) the negative and positive areas under the graph are equal in magnitude. This tells us the object must have returned to its initial position so we can disregard A and D.\n\nGraph C represents an object travelling at a constant positive velocity and switching to an equal and opposite negative velocity halfway through its journey.\n\nTherefore, the answer is B.\n\n(Note for those familiar with calculus: The graph in the question shows $\\cos (t)$ between 0 and $\\pi$. The correct answer shows $\\int \\cos(t)$ $dx$ = $\\sin (t)$ between 0 and $\\pi$.)"},{"ref":"PH0931","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0931\n\nThe graph below shows how the acceleration of an object $a$ varies with time $t$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-06-30%20at%205.16.39%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-30 at 5.16.39 PM\">\n\nWhat is the change in speed of the object?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $7.0$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $28.0$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $42.0$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $56.0$ m s$^{-1}$","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nChange in speed is the magnitude of the area under an acceleration-time graph.\n\nTo find the area under the graph we can split it into two sections.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-06-30%20at%205.17.41%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-30 at 5.17.41 PM\">\n\nSection 1 is a rectangle. Area $=(4)(7)=28$ m s$^{-1}$\n\nSection 2 is a triangle. Area $= \\dfrac{1}{2}(4)(7)=14$ m s$^{-1}$\n\nTotal area $= 28+14=42$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Alternative solution}}$\n\nThis can also be done in one step using the area of a trapezium $A=\\dfrac{1}{2}(8+4)(7)=42$ m s$^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH0967","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0967\n\nAt time $T=0$ Object A is launched from the top of a cliff of height $h$ at a velocity $v$ and an angle of $30\\text{\\textdegree}$ below the horizontal. At the same time Object B is launched from the bottom of the cliff at an angle of 30$\\text{\\textdegree}$ above the horizontal with the same velocity $v$. The objects collide at time $T=t$.\n\nThe trajectories of the objects are shown in the diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-06-02%20at%2010.56.33%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-02 at 10.56.33 AM\">\n\nAir resistance is negligible. What is $t$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em} $ $t=\\dfrac{h}{2v\\,\\cos(30\\text{\\textdegree})}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em} $ $t=\\dfrac{h}{2v\\,\\sin(30\\text{\\textdegree})}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em} $ $t=\\dfrac{h}{v\\cos(30\\text{\\textdegree})}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em} $ $t=\\dfrac{h}{v\\sin(30\\text{\\textdegree})}$","markScheme":"**答案：B**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nBoth have the same horizontal velocity ($v_H=v \\cos(30\\text{\\textdegree})$) so we may ignore the horizontal direction\n\n(A person travelling in a car with the same horizontal velocity as the objects would only see vertical motion)\n\nSo, we must consider only the vertical equations of motion of the objects.\n\n- $s=u\\,t+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,a\\,t^2+initial\\ position$\n\nLet us treat the upward direction as positive and the ground to be at position $s=0$. (These choices are arbitrary – but we must be consistent)\n\nThe initial vertical velocity of object A is $-v\\,\\sin(\\,30\\text{\\textdegree})$ and the initial velocity of object B is $v\\,\\sin( 30\\text{\\textdegree})$. This is illustrated below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-06-02%20at%2011.00.26%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-02 at 11.00.26 AM\">\n\nThe position object A $s_A$ is given by\n\n$s_A=-v\\,\\sin(30\\text{\\textdegree})t-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,g\\,t^2+h$ (acceleration vector points downwards so $a=-g$)\n\nThe position of object B $s_B$ is given by\n\n- $s=v\\,\\sin(30\\text{\\textdegree})t-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,g\\,t^2$\n\nWe want to know when the objects collide (where the vertical positions are the same) so equating $s_A$ and $s_B$ gives.\n\n- $-v\\,\\sin(30\\text{\\textdegree})t-\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2+h=v\\,\\sin(30\\text{\\textdegree})t-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,g\\,t^2$\n\nNotice we have $-\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2$ on both sides so these cancel. There is a frame of reference in which the objects are not accelerating so acceleration is irrelevant to time of collision! (A person in freefall beside the objects would see no acceleration - they would measure $s_A=-v\\,\\sin(30\\text{\\textdegree})+h$ and $s_B=v\\,\\sin(30\\text{\\textdegree})$\n\n- $-v\\,\\sin(30\\text{\\textdegree})t+h=v\\,\\sin(30\\text{\\textdegree})t$\n\n- $h=2v\\,\\sin(30\\text{\\textdegree})t$\n\n- $t=\\dfrac{h}{2v\\,\\sin(30\\text{\\textdegree})}$"},{"ref":"PH1331","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1331\n\nA solid block accelerates uniformly down an inclined plane passing through points\nA, B and C. It takes $2.00\\,s$ to move from A to B and also from B to C.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/PH1331.png\" alt=\"PH1331\">\n\nThe distance between A and B is $2.00$ m and between B and C is $3.00$ m.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the acceleration of the block?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $0.125$ m s$^{-2}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $0.250$ m s$^{-2}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $0.375$ m s$^{-2}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $0.625$ m s$^{-2}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe average speed between points A and B is given by:\n\n- $v_\\textrm{average}\\textrm{(A to B)} = \\dfrac{2.00\\textrm{ m}}{2.00\\textrm{ s}}=1.00$ m s$^{-1}$\n\nThis corresponds to the instantaneous speed of the block $1.00\\,s$ after passing through A which occurs before passing through the midpoint between A and B.\n\nSimilarly, the average speed between points B and C is given by:\n\n- $v_\\textrm{average}\\textrm{(B to C)} = \\dfrac{3.00\\textrm{ m}}{2.00\\textrm{ s}}=1.50$ m s$^{-1}$\n\nAgain, this corresponds to the instantaneous speed of the block $1.00$ s after passing through B (before passing through the midpoint between B and C).\n\nTherefore, it takes $2.00$ s for the speed of the block to change from $1.00$ m s$^{-1}$ to $1.50$ m s$^{-1}$.\n\nThe acceleration is then:\n\n- $a=\\dfrac{\\Delta v}{\\Delta t}=\\dfrac{1.50\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}-1.00\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}}{2.00\\textrm{ s}}=0.250$ m s$^{-2}$"},{"ref":"PH1797","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1797\n\nThe diagram shows the path of a football kicked from the ground.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/Screenshot%202025-03-24%20at%205.29.42%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-03-24 at 5.29.42 PM\">\n\nAir resistance is negligible. Which of the arrows $A$, $B$, $C$, or $D$ represent the direction of the acceleration of the ball at the instant in time shown?","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nIn the absence of air resistance, gravity is the only force acting on the object so its acceleration acts vertically downwards.\n\nAt the point of time shown in the diagram, the vertical velocity of the ball is decreasing whilst its horizontal velocity remains the same."},{"ref":"PH1852","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1852\n\nThe graph below represents the velocity $v$ against time $t$ of an object which is initially at rest and moves in a straight line. It is given that the areas $A_1$ and $A_2$ are 10 m and 30 m respectively.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Kinematics/images/ph1852q.png\" alt=\"ph1852q\">\n\nAt $t=10$ s the object is located at a distance $D$ from its starting point.\n\nWhich of the following correctly describes the value of $D$ and the average speed of the object from $t=0$ s to $t=10$ s?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\bf D$ / m | Average speed / m s$^{-1}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| 20 | 2  |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 40 | 2  |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{2.5em}$ 20 $\\hspace{2.5em}$ | 4 |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 40 | 4  |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe area $A_1$ represents a distance travelled in a positive direction whereas area $A_2$ represents a distance travelled in a negative direction.\n\nThe total displacement of the object is then given by:\n\n- $A_1-A_2=10\\textrm{ m}-30\\textrm{ m}=-20\\textrm{ m}$\n\nThis tells us that at $t=10$ s the object is located at a distance of 20 m from the origin, therefore $D=20$ m.\n\nTo determine the average speed of the object we must find the ratio between the distance travelled and the time taken:\n\n- Average speed $=\\dfrac{\\textrm{distance travelled}}{\\textrm{time taken}}=\\dfrac{10\\textrm{ m}+30\\textrm{ m}}{10\\textrm{ s}}=\\dfrac{40\\textrm{ m}}{10\\textrm{ s}}=4$ m s$^{-1}$"}]},{"name":"Unassigned","questions":[{"ref":"PH0154","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0154\n\nA point $P$ is subjected to three simultaneous forces of magnitudes $F_A > F_B > F_C$. Point $P$ is in equilibrium. Consider the following statements.\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | $F_A = F_B + F_C$ |\n  | II. | $F_A \\leq F_B + F_C$ |\n  | III. | $F_A > F_B - F_C$ |\n\nWhich of the statements is **not always true** about the magnitudes of the forces?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nVectors of forces in equilibrium form a triangle or a straight line.\n\nThe sum of two sides of a triangle is always greater than the third (II is always true).\n\nThe difference between two sides of a triangle is always less than the third (III is also always true).\n\nI is true **only when** the three forces are in a straight line and $F_B$ and $F_C$ are both opposing $F_A$(not always true, as required by the question)."},{"ref":"PH0490","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0490\n\nTwo trucks X and Y are pulling a car out of thick mud along a straight path. The only other horizontal force acting on the car is a drag force opposite to the direction of motion. The diagram shows a top view of the direction of the force applied by truck $X$ and the direction of motion of the car.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-06-30%20at%205.26.09%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-30 at 5.26.09 PM\">\n\nWhich of the following vectors shows the direction of the force applied by truck $Y$? \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-06-30%20at%205.26.19%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-30 at 5.26.19 PM\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe motion of the car here is in the direction of the sum of the two forces vectors from $X$ and $Y$. \\\n\nThe only vector that will add to vector $X$ to produce a net force vector to the right is one in the direction of C.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-06-30%20at%205.26.14%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-30 at 5.26.14 PM\">"},{"ref":"PH0551","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0551\n\nAn object is moving at constant velocity. Which of the following could be the free-body diagram representing the forces acting on the object?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n|| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Unassigned/images/PH0551A.png\" alt=\"PH0551A\">|$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Unassigned/images/PH0551B.png\" alt=\"PH0551B\">|\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  |  <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Unassigned/images/PH0551C.png\" alt=\"PH0551C\">|  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Unassigned/images/PH0551D.png\" alt=\"PH0551D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAccording to Newton’s First Law, when the resultant force acting on the object is zero, acceleration will be zero and the velocity of the object is constant.  When head to tail method is applied to add the three vectors of option C, the following is obtained.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Unassigned/images/PH0551MS.png\" alt=\"PH0551MS\">\n\nThe closed triangle represents a resultant force of zero.\n\nHence, the correct answer is C."},{"ref":"PH0821","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0821\n\nBelow are three suggested units for viscosity\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | kg m$^{-1}$ s$^{-1}$   |\n  | II. | Pa s |\n  | III. | N s m$^{-1}$ |\n\nWhich of the unit combinations is/are correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I and II\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ II and III","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nDrag:\n\n- $F_d=6\\pi \\eta rv$\n\nBy making viscosity the subject of the formula:\n\n- $\\eta =\\dfrac{F_d}{6\\pi rv}$\n\nUnits are substituted:\n\n- $\\eta = \\dfrac{\\textrm{N}}{(\\textrm{m})(\\textrm{m s}^{-1})}=\\dfrac{\\textrm{N}}{(\\textrm{m}^2\\textrm{ s}^{-1})}=\\textrm{ N s m}^{-2}$\n\nBy replacing N m$^{-2}$ = Pa:\n\n- N s m$^{-2}$ = Pa s\n\nBy replacing N = kg m s$^{-2}$:\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{N}}{\\textrm{m}^2\\textrm{ s}^{-1}}=\\dfrac{\\textrm{kg m s}^{-2}}{\\textrm{m}^2\\textrm{ s}^{-1}}=\\textrm{kg m}^{-1}\\textrm{ s}^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH1837","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH1837\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\n1. A student wants to measure the bending of a long plastic ruler. The ruler is fixed at both ends and a mass is added at its midpoint. The student varies the mass and measures the vertical deflection.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-08-27%20at%2012.42.28%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-08-27 at 12.42.28 PM\">\n\n    The following data are different trials to measure deflection of the same mass.\n\n    $\\\\$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Unassigned/images/ph1837q1b.JPEG\" alt=\"ph1837q1b\">\n\n    $\\\\$\n\n    1. State why performing several trials can lead to a more accurate experimental result.   [1 mark]\n\n    2. Calculate the average value for the deflection value $d$, making use of proper experimental techniques. [2 marks]\n\n2. The deflection of the ruler is predicted to be directly proportional with mass.\n\n    The graph shows the variation of deflection with mass.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-08-27%20at%2012.47.24%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-08-27 at 12.47.24 PM\">\n\n    1. Referring to  the graph, state the classification of experimental error that has occurred and suggest a reason for this error.   [2 marks]\n\n    2. The relation between deflection and mass is given by\n\n        $d=\\dfrac{mgL^3}{48EI}$\n\n        Where $L$ is the length of the ruler and the unit of $I$ is m$^4$.\n\n        Determine the units of $E$ in SI fundamental units. [2 marks]\n\n    3. The following set of data is given\n\n        $m=(35\\pm0.1)$ kg\n\n        $L=(2.0\\pm0.0005)$ m\n\n        $d=(5.0\\pm0.05)$ cm\n\n        $I=(2.5\\pm0.4)\\times10^{-9}$ m$^4$\n\n        The uncertainty of $g$ is negligible.\n\n        Determine the absolute uncertainty of $E$. [3 marks]\n\n    4. The student suggests that the value of $I$ depends on the cross-sectional area of the ruler.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Unassigned/images/ph1837qd.JPEG\" alt=\"ph1837qd\">\n\n        $I$ is directly proportional to $h^3$.\n\n        A student uses a measuring tape to find the value of $h$ to be $3.0\\pm0.5$ mm.\n\n        Suggest an instrument that could be used to obtain an improved value for $h$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. Random error can be eliminated through averaging. $\\checkmark$\n\n        **Or**\n\n        Anomalies can be avoided $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        If just one trial is taken, that value could have a random fluctuation from the actual value and there is no way of knowing this. By taking a number of readings and averaging the results, random error will cause the readings to be above and below the actual value, and averaging the readings will give a result closer to the actual value. Also, if there is a significant anomaly, this can be seen when compared to the other readings.\n\n    2. Removes the $1.7$ cm reading as an outlier. $\\checkmark$\n\n        - Average$=\\dfrac{2.4+2.3+2.2+2.3+2.1}{5}= 2.3\\  \\ce{cm}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The outlier is at trial 3\n\n        If we exclude the outlier, then\n\n        - Average$=\\dfrac{2.4+2.3+2.2+2.3+2.1}{5}=2.3$ cm\n\n2. 1. Systematic error $\\checkmark$\n\n        Zero error in measuring devices.\n\n        **Or**\n\n        Beam deflects under its own weight $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The graph is not passing through the origin and yet the points form a straight line. This suggests that all measurements differ from the real value by a fixed amount.\n\n        There is a systematic error.\n\n        This systematic error can be due to zero error in measuring devices.\n\n        It also can be because the beam deflects under its own weight, and so would have non-zero deflection for no mass added.\n\n    2. Substitution of units for quantities $\\dfrac{(\\textrm{kg})(\\textrm{m s}^{-2})(\\textrm{m}^3)}{(\\textrm{m})(\\textrm{m}^4)}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        kg m$^{-1}$ s$^{-2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n        - $E=\\dfrac{mgL^3}{48dI}$\n\n        Unit of $E$ is found by substituting the units for all quantities in the expression. Note that $g$ is an acceleration and the units on $I$ have been given in the question.\n\n        - $\\dfrac{(\\textrm{kg})(\\textrm{m s}^{-2})(\\textrm{m}^3)}{(\\textrm{m})(\\textrm{m}^4)}=\\textrm{kg m}^{-1}\\textrm{ s}^{-2}$\n\n    3. At least one fractional uncertainty is correct $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta E}{E}=\\dfrac{0.1}{35}+(3)\\left(\\dfrac{0.0005}{2}\\right)+\\dfrac{0.05}{5}+\\dfrac{0.4}{2.5}=0.174$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\Delta E=\\dfrac{\\Delta E}{E}\\times E=(0.174)(4.6\\times10^{11})= 8\\times10^{10}\\ll \\ce{kg m}^{-1} \\ce{s}^{-2}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Since\n\n        - $E=\\dfrac{mgL^3}{48dI}$\n\n        Then\n\n        - $E=\\dfrac{(35)(9.8)(2^3)}{(48)(5\\times10^{-2})(2.5\\times10^{-9})}=4.6\\times10^{11}$ kg m$^{-1}$ s$^{-2}$\n\n        The fractional uncertainty in $E$ is given by\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta E}{E}=\\dfrac{\\Delta m}{m}+(3)\\left(\\dfrac{\\Delta L}{L}\\right)+\\dfrac{\\Delta d}{d}+\\dfrac{\\Delta I}{I}$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta E}{E}=\\dfrac{0.1}{35}+(3)\\left(\\dfrac{0.0005}{2}\\right)+\\dfrac{0.05}{5}+\\dfrac{0.4}{2.5}=0.174$\n\n        The absolute uncertainty is given by\n\n        - $\\Delta E=\\dfrac{\\Delta E}{E}\\times E=(0.174)(4.6\\times10^{11})=8\\times10^{10}$ kg m$^{-1}$ s$^{-2}$\n\n    4. Any instrument with lower uncertainty (micrometer/vernier caliper) $\\checkmark$\n\n        Lower fractional uncertainty/more precise $I$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        A micrometer or a vernier caliper has a lower uncertainty than a meter tape, specifically around 0.01 mm for a micrometer and 0.1 mm for a vernier caliper.\n\n        This increases the precision of measuring $h$ and decreases its fractional uncertainty, leading to a more precise calculation of $I$."}]},{"name":"Work, Energy, and Power","questions":[{"ref":"PH0035","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0035\n\nA motor uses a cable to pull a trolley up an inclined plane at a constant velocity.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/ph0035q.JPEG\" alt=\"ph0035q\">\n\nConsider the following statements about the energy changes of the trolley during its motion.\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | The work done by the engine has greater magnitude than the work done by friction. |\n  | II. |    The total of kinetic and potential energy of the trolley increases. |\n  | III. | The gain in gravitational potential energy of the trolley has the same magnitude as the work done by the engine. |\n\nOf the statements above, which is/are correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nI.    Correct: as the trolley moves up gravitational potential energy increases so work done by the trolley is done against both friction and gravity.\n\nII.    Correct: kinetic energy is constant since trolley is moving at constant velocity but gravitational potential energy increases as height of it from ground increases.\n\nIII.    Incorrect: some amount of work done by the engine is used to increase gravitational potential energy, but some of it is wasted as work done by friction."},{"ref":"PH0037","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0037\n\nA ball with mass $m$ is released from a height of $h$ and undergoes free fall. The ball hits the ground and rebounds vertically. On rebound, the ball reaches a maximum height of $h'$. The time taken for the collision is $T$. During the collision, what are the change in kinetic energy of the ball and the magnitude of the average net force on the ball?\n\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$ Change in kinetic energy $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Average force $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $mg(h-h')$ | $\\dfrac{m(\\sqrt{2gh} + \\sqrt{2gh'})}{T}$ |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $mg(h'-h)$ | $\\dfrac{m(\\sqrt{2gh} - \\sqrt{2gh'})}{T}$ |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $mg(h'-h)$ | $\\dfrac{m(\\sqrt{2gh} + \\sqrt{2gh'})}{T}$ |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $mg(h-h')$ | $\\dfrac{m(\\sqrt{2gh} - \\sqrt{2gh'})}{T}$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nChange in kinetic energy  = Loss of mechanical energy = change in potential energy \n\n- = $mgh' - mgh = mg(h'-h)$\n\nWe can find the velocity before hitting the ground using conservation of energy\n\n-  $\\dfrac{1}{2}mu^2=mgh$\n\ngiving\n\n-   $u = \\sqrt{2gh}$ \n\nSimilarly, velocity after bouncing from the ground \n\n- $v = - \\sqrt{2gh'}$\n\nChange in momentum\n\n- $\\Delta p = m (v-u)$ = $m (-\\sqrt{2gh'} - \\sqrt{2gh})$ \n\nForce is rate of change of momentum\n\n- $F_{av} = \\dfrac{-m(\\sqrt{2gh} + \\sqrt{2gh'})}{T}$\n\nBecause the question asks for the magnitude of the average force, we can take the positive value.\n\n- $F_{av} = \\dfrac{m(\\sqrt{2gh} + \\sqrt{2gh'})}{T}$"},{"ref":"PH0038","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0038\n\nA race car starts to race on a straight horizontal race track from rest. The car's engine has a constant power output and the car experiences a resistive force that increases proportionally with its speed.\n\nWhich graph represents the variation with distance $s$ of the power $P$ required by the engine to overcome the resistive force?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n|| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-05-14%20at%201.43.13%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-14 at 1.43.13 PM\">|$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-05-14%20at%201.43.20%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-14 at 1.43.20 PM\">|\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-05-14%20at%201.43.42%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-14 at 1.43.42 PM\"> |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-05-14%20at%201.43.27%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-14 at 1.43.27 PM\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe power from the engine to overcome the resistive force $F_{res}$ at any given instant is found from the formula\n\n- $P_{res} = F_{res}v$\n\nWhere $v$ is the speed of the car.\n\nAt the start of the motion, both the resistive force and speed are zero, and both of these increase with time. Because of the increasing resistive force, however, eventually a terminal speed will be reached where $F_{res}$ and $v$, and therefore $P_{res}$, are all constant."},{"ref":"PH0039","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0039\n\nA 74 kg boy starts to ride a 6.0 kg bicycle from rest. The graph shows how the acceleration $a$ of the bicycle varies with the distance travelled $x$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-06-05%20at%208.49.04%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-05 at 8.49.04 AM\">\n\nWhat is the total work done by the boy?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.3 kJ\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 3.7 kJ\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 4.0 kJ\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 6.0 kJ","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nWork Done = (Force $\\times$ Distance) = (Mass $\\times$ Acceleration $\\times$ Distance)\n\n(Acceleration $\\times$ Distance) is the area under the graph.\n\nWork done is (Mass $\\times$ Graph Area) = (74+6)(50) = 4000 J = 4 kJ"},{"ref":"PH0084","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0084\n\nA system that consists of two light identical springs connected in series stores a total elastic potential energy $E_H$ when a load is added to the springs. One of the springs is then removed and the same load is added to the remaining spring.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-05-14%20at%202.13.46%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-14 at 2.13.46 PM\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Created with Chemix (https://chemix.org)]}}$\n\nIn terms of $E_H$, what is the total elastic potential energy stored in the single spring situation?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2$E_H$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $E_H$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.5$E_H$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.25$E_H$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nUsing Hooke's law,\n\n- $F=k\\Delta x$\n\nthe elastic potential energy is\n\n- $E_p=\\dfrac{1}{2} k\\Delta x^2=\\dfrac{1}{2} F\\Delta x$.\n\nIn the two-spring situation, both springs experience the same downward force and so they have the same extension given by the equation $F=k\\Delta x$.\n\n- $F=k\\Delta x$.\n\nWhen one of the springs is removed, the load remains unchanged and the remaining spring experiences the same extension. Therefore the elastic potential energy stored is also halved."},{"ref":"PH0118","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0118\n\nA block of mass 16 kg is pushed and released along a rough horizontal surface at an initial speed of 2 m s$^{-1}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-05-14%20at%202.30.42%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-14 at 2.30.42 PM\">\n\nThe block travels through a distance of 16 m and is brought to rest. What is the magnitude of the frictional force that brings the block to rest?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2 N\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 4 N\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 8 N\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 16 N","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe net work done on the block \\\n= Change in kinetic energy of the block\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\n W_{net}&=\\Delta E_k \\\\\n \\hspace{1em}\\\\\n Fs \\cos(\\theta)& = \\frac{1}{2}m(v_f)^2 - \\frac{1}{2}m(v_i)^2 \\\\\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\Fs\\cos(\\theta) &= 0 -\\frac{1}{2}m(v_i)^2 \\\\\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n(F)(16)(\\cos\\,180^o)&=-\\frac{1}{2}(16)(2)^2 \\\\\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n(F)(16)(-1)&=-32 \\\\\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\nF&=2\\textrm{ N}\\\n\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlternative:\n\n- $v^2 = u^2 + 2as \\Rightarrow a = \\dfrac{v^2 - u^2}{2s} = \\dfrac{0^2 - 2^2}{2(16)} = -0.125$ m s$^{-2}$\n\n- $|F| = m|a| = 16|-0.125| = 2$ N"},{"ref":"PH0125","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0125\n\nA builder uses a block and tackle pulley system to lift a bundle of materials of mass 15 kg to the top of a building of height 6 m in 9 s. What is the power delivered to the bundle? Assume $g=10$ N kg$^{-1}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/PH0125.png\" alt=\"PH0125\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Image Source: Block-and-tackle-in-use.svg by Themightyquill licensed under CC BY-SA 4.0}]}$\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 10 W\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 17 W\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 100 W\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 900 W","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\n\\textrm{Power} &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{Work done}}{\\textrm{Time}}\\\\\n\\hspace{2.3cm}\\\\\n\\end{aligned}$\n\nIn this case the work done is giving gravitational potential energy to the object. Change in gravitational potential energy is given by $\\Delta E_p=mg\\Delta h$\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\n\\textrm{Power} &= \\dfrac{mgh}{t}\\\\\n\\hspace{2.3cm}\\\\\n&= \\dfrac{(15\\textrm{ kg})(6\\textrm{ m})(10\\textrm{ N kg}^{-1})}{9\\textrm{ s}}\\\\\n\\hspace{2.3cm}\\\\\n &= 100\\textrm{ W}\\\n \\end{aligned}$"},{"ref":"PH0131","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0131\n\nA motorised toy car starts from rest, with the motor delivering increasing power to the car over time as shown in the graph below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-06-05%20at%209.08.04%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-05 at 9.08.04 AM\">\n\nAssuming no energy losses, which of the following gives the kinetic energy of the car after 8.0 seconds?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.20 J\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 5.0 J\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 160 J\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 320 J","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe energy can be found by calculating the area under the graph.\n\n- Work done = $\\dfrac{(Power)(time)}{2}$\n\n- $W = \\dfrac{(40\\textrm{ W})(8\\textrm{ s})}{2} = 160$ J\n\nAlternative solution:\n\nBecause Power increases at a constant rate, the average Power ($P_{av}$) can be found from the average of the starting and ending powers.\n\n- $P_{av} = \\dfrac{0+40}{2} = 20$ W\n\n- $E=P_{av}t = (20)(8) = 160$ J"},{"ref":"PH0138","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0138\n\nA natural gas plant burns fuel with a energy density of 80 MJ kg$^{-1}$. \n\nThe rate of energy input of the natural gas is 1.6 GW. What is the rate at which the fuel burns? \n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.0005 kg s$^{-1}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 10 kg s$^{-1}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1500 kg s$^{-1}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 20 kg s$^{-1}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nThe energy delivered per second (power) will be equal to the product of the mass burning rate and the energy density. \n\nTherefore,\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\Delta m}{\\Delta t}(80\\textrm{ MJ kg}^{-1})=1.6$ GW\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\Delta m}{\\Delta t}=\\dfrac{1.6\\times10^9\\textrm{ J s}^{-1}}{8.0\\times10^7\\textrm{ J kg}^{-1}}=20$ kg s$^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH0139","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0139\n\nThe Sankey diagram below shows the energy degraded at different stages of a nuclear power plant. \n\nWhich of the following sections corresponds to the stage with the highest energy degradation? \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/PH0139.png\" alt=\"PH0139\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe arrows that turn downwards represent energy degradation. The widths are proportional to the amount of energy in that stage. The widest of these is B."},{"ref":"PH0142","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0142\n\nThe figure below shows a Sankey diagram for a small thermal plant. What is the efficiency of the system?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-04-03%20at%201.58.29%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-03 at 1.58.29 PM\">\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 10%\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 20%\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 40%\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 60%","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nIn Sankey diagrams for energy, useful energy is shown as continuing straight while energy losses point downwards.\n\nFrom the Sankey diagram, subtracting the value of the heat losses gives\n\nTo find the useful power:\n\n- $1600 - (320 + 640) = 640$ kW\n\nEfficiency can now be calculated:\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\hspace{2em}\\eta &= \\frac{\\textrm{Useful power out}}{\\textrm{Total power in}} \\times 100\\\\\n\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n&= \\frac{640\\textrm{ kW}}{1600\\textrm{ kW}} \\times 100\\% \\\\\n\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n&= 40\\%\n\n\\end{aligned}$"},{"ref":"PH0206","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0206\n\nA box of mass $2$ kg is pushed with an initial speed of 10 m s$^{-1}$ on a rough horizontal floor. A uniform frictional force of $10$ N acts on the box. What is the distance travelled by the box at the instant when the kinetic energy of the box is halved?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 10 m\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 5 m\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2 m\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1 m","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe loss of kinetic energy is equal to half of the original kinetic energy:\n\n- $E_{k(Lost)}= \\dfrac{E_{k(initial)}}{2}$\n\nWork done by friction is equal to the loss of kinetic energy.\n\n- $E_{k(Lost)} = W = F_fs$\n\n- $\\dfrac{E_{k(initial)}}{2} = F_fs$\n\nBy substituting given values in the equation using $E_k=\\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2$\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2em} \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg) \\dfrac{(2)(10^2)}{2}  &= 10\\,s\\\\\n\\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\bigg)(100) &= 10\\,s\\\\\n50 &= 10\\,s\\\\\ns &= 5\n\\end{aligned}$\n\nTherefore, the answer is B."},{"ref":"PH0217","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0217\n\nWhich of the following units is not a unit of power?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ W\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ J\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ N m s$^{-1}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ J s$^{-1}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe SI unit of power is W and it is defined as J s$^{-1}$.\n\nAdditionally, power can be calculated by\n\n- $P = Fv$\n\nWhen units of force and speed are substituted in the equation, the units become\n\n$\\hspace{1.5em}$ N m s$^{-1}$\n\nBut J is the SI unit of energy.\n\nTherefore, the answer is B."},{"ref":"PH0237","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0237\n\nA car engine has an input power of $P_{in}$ and efficiency of $\\eta$.  Which of the following gives the wasted energy from the engine in time of $t$? \n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ P$_{in}$ $\\eta$t\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ P$_{in}$ $\\dfrac{\\eta}{t}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ P$_{in}$t - P$_{in}$ $\\eta$t\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ P$_{in}$t - $\\dfrac{\\eta}{t}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nUseful power output is given by $P_{in}\\eta$, so the useful energy is $P_{in}\\eta t$. \n\nInput energy is given by $P_{in}t$,\n\ntherefore, wasted energy is given by $P_{in} t$ - $P_{in}\\eta t$ \n\nTherefore the answer is C.\n\nAlternative 2: \\\n\n$E_{Waste} = E_T(100\\% - \\eta) = P_{in}t(1-\\eta) = P_{in}t - P_{in}\\eta t$"},{"ref":"PH0244","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0244\n\nAn object is released from rest from a very high building. Air resistance is not negligible. Which of the following graphs shows the variation of the gravitational potential energy $E_p$ of the object with time $t$? \n\n| | | | | |\n\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-05-26%20at%2010.55.50%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-26 at 10.55.50 AM\"> |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-05-26%20at%2010.55.55%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-26 at 10.55.55 AM\">|\n\n|$\\hspace{1cm}$ \n\n|C. |   |   |D.  | |\n\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-05-26%20at%2010.56.02%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-26 at 10.56.02 AM\"> |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-05-26%20at%2010.56.07%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-26 at 10.56.07 AM\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\n- $\\Delta E_p = mg\\Delta h$\n\nDue to downward acceleration due to gravity, the rate of decrease of gravitational potential energy with time must increase – slope must become steeper as it speeds up. For slow speeds and low air resistance, the height follows the function\n\n- $\\Delta h=\\dfrac{1}{2}at^2$ \n\nAfter the object reaches terminal velocity, the rate of decrease of gravitational potential energy is constant with time."},{"ref":"PH0246","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0246\n\nA box is pulled by a child with a horizontal force of 5 N. The box moves a horizontal distance of 4 m in a time of 2 s. What is the average power delivered by the child to the box?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2.5 W\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 5.0 W\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 10 W\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 40 W","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nPower = $\\dfrac{\\Delta W}{t}$ and $W = Fs$\n\n- $W = (5\\ \\ce{ N})(4\\ \\ce{m}) = 20$ J\n\n- $P=\\dfrac{20\\textrm{ J}}{2\\textrm{ s}} = 10$ W\n\nAlternative:\n\n- $P_{av} = Fv_{av} = F\\bigg(\\dfrac{\\textrm{s}}{\\textrm{t}}\\bigg) = 5\\bigg(\\dfrac{4}{2}\\bigg) = 10$ W"},{"ref":"PH0264","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0264\n\nThe graph shows the variation of length $l$ of a spring of original length $L_0$ as it is stretched to length $L$ by a variable force $F$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-05-27%20at%204.11.33%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-27 at 4.11.33 PM\">\n\nThe work done against the spring is represented by which of the following?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The area marked X.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The area marked Y.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ The area marked Z.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The sum of the areas marked Y and Z","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nSince $W = F s$, work can be found from areas on the graph. Normally, the graph of a spring being stretched has force on the vertical axis and extension or length on the horizontal axis and the work done is the area beneath the graph. Because the axes are switched, and the area must still be found between using the length axis as the base, we use the area to the left of the graph. Only the triangular area contained between the graph and the length axis from $L$ to $L_0$ represents the work done."},{"ref":"PH0273","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0273\n\nWhich of the following is not appropriate for measuring energy?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ J\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ N m\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ kg m s$^{-2}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ kg m$^{2}$ s$^{-2}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nA. J (joule) is an accepted unit of energy\n\nB. Can be shown to represent energy using the work formula:  $W=Fs$ ; in terms of units, 1 J = 1  N m\n\nC. Can be analysed using Newton's Second Law $F=ma$; in terms of units, 1 N = 1 kg m s$^{-2}$. This is a unit of force not a unit of energy and therefore the $\\underline{\\textrm{correct}}$ answer. \n\nD. Can be shown to represent energy using work and Newton's Second Law: $W = Fs = mas$; In terms of units, \n\nJ = N m = kg m s$^{-2}$ m = kg m$^{2}$ s$^{-2}$"},{"ref":"PH0283","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0283\n\nA box of mass $m$ is pulled across a horizontal surface with a force $F$ as shown in the diagram. The coefficient of dynamic friction between the box and the surface is $\\mu$. What is the energy transferred to thermal energy while a box is moved by a distance of $d$?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-04-07%20at%2011.10.31%E2%80%AFAM2.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-07 at 11.10.31 AM2\">\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $Fd$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $(F-\\mu mg)d$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\mu mgd$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $mgd$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nEnergy transferred to thermal energy will be the work done by the frictional force.\n\n- $F_F=\\mu F_N = \\mu mg$  (where $F_N=mg$)\n\n- $W = Fs = \\mu mgd$"},{"ref":"PH0358","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0358\n\nA small ball of mass $m$ is attached to a string and moves in a vertical circular path of radius $r$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/PH0358-1.png\" alt=\"PH0358-1\">\n\nWhat is the minimum velocity $v$ at the lowest point of the path for the ball to continue moving in a circular path at top of the circle?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2mgr$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{mgr}{2}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\sqrt{2gr}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\sqrt{5gr}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/PH0358_2.png\" alt=\"PH0358_2\">\n\nTo calculate the minimum value of velocity at the lowest point, we need to calculate the minimum kinetic energy.\n\nAt the lowest point, the total energy is only kinetic energy and the potential energy is zero. \\\nFrom the law of conservation of energy,\\\nTotal energy at the top = Total energy at the lowest point\n\nAt the top the minimum speed for a circular path to be maintained occurs when the centripetal force = weight of the ball\n\n- $\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}=mg \\hspace{2em}$ giving $\\hspace{2em} v^2=gr$\n\nTotal energy at the top = kinetic energy + gravitational potential energy\n\n- $E_T=\\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2+mgh$\n\nWhere $h=2r$ because it is equal to the diameter of the circular path.\n\n- $E_T=\\dfrac{1}{2}mgr+mg\\left(2r\\right)=\\dfrac{5}{2}mgr$\n\nAt the lowest point,\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{1.5em}E_T=E_K&=\\frac{1}{2}mv^2\\\\\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\\frac{5}{2}mgr&=\\frac{1}{2}mv^2\\\\\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\nv^2&=5gr\\\\\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\nv&=\\sqrt{5gr}\n\\end{aligned}$"},{"ref":"PH0397","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0397\n\nA mass $m$ attached to a string is moving in a horizontal circular motion as shown. The tension on the string is $T$, and the angle with the vertical is $\\theta$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%2010.29.13%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 10.29.13 AM\">\n\nBelow are three statements about this motion:\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$ Centripetal force = $mg$ $\\tan(\\theta)$.\n\n- II.$\\hspace{0.8em}$ Total gravitational and kinetic energy of the mass is constant.\n\n- III. $\\hspace{0.4em}$ If the rope is cut, the mass will fall downwards with a straight line trajectory.\n\nWhich of the statements are correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Only I\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nStatement I is correct: \\\n$\\hspace{3em}$ $T$ $\\cos(\\theta)$ = $mg$,  which gives $T = \\dfrac{mg}{\\cos(\\theta)}$\n\n$\\hspace{3em}$ centripetal force ($F_c$) = $T$ $\\sin(\\theta)$\n\nSubstituting $\\dfrac{mg}{\\cos(\\theta)}$ for $T$ in the second equation:\n\n$\\hspace{3em}$ $F_c=mg$ $\\dfrac{\\sin(\\theta)}{\\cos(\\theta)}$ = $mg$ $\\tan(\\theta)$.\n\nStatement II is correct: \\\nThe path is a horizontal plane, therefore gravitational potential energy is constant. No work is done, therefore kinetic energy is constant. Total mechanical energy is constant.\n\nStatement III is false: \\\nThe mass has a horizontal velocity during this motion. When the rope is cut, the only force will be the weight, therefore it will move in a projectile motion starting with a horizontal velocity, following a vertical parabolic path (not fall directly downwards)."},{"ref":"PH0459","marks":"11","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0459\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\n1. Define potential energy. [1 mark]\n\n2. A body of mass 2.0 kg is pushed with an initial speed of 1.2 m s$^{-1}$  down the slope of a frictionless 30$\\degree$ incline from a height of $h$. As soon as it reaches the bottom of the incline, it begins to move on a rough horizontal table where it comes to rest momentarily after colliding with a spring of spring constant 32 N m$^{-1}$. (Air friction is ignored)\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%201.38.19%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 1.38.19 PM\">\n\n    $\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Created with Chemix (https://chemix.org)]}}$\n\n    1. The speed of the body is 3.06 m s$^{-1}$ when it reaches the bottom of the incline. Calculate $h$. [2 marks]\n\n    2.     The distance between the bottom of the slope and the body's position is $x$. On the graph, sketch the variation of the square of the speed $v^2$ of the body with distance $x$ from the bottom of the slope. [2 marks]\n\n        (no need to add values to the axes)\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%201.21.26%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 1.21.26 PM\">\n\n    3. At the instant that the body stops momentarily, the spring is compressed as 0.43 m. The coefficient of friction between the body and the rough surface is 0.40. Determine the distance that the body covered on the horizontal rough surface between the point of leaving the ramp and stopping momentarily. [4 marks]\n\n3. The incline is replaced with one of a steeper gradient. The body is pushed at the same speed from the same height. Explain the effect of the increase in gradient on the maximum compression of the spring in comparison to the maximum compression in the original situation.          [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. The energy possessed by the system due to the relative positions of its components or parts **OR** energy possessed by an object due to its position in a field $\\checkmark$\n\n2. 1. Use of conservation of energy $\\checkmark$\n\n        $h = \\ll \\dfrac{1}{2g}(v^2-u^2) = \\gg$ :answer[$0.40$ m] $\\checkmark$\n\n        (also allow use of forces and acceleration to achieve the same result)\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The energy of the body $E_i$ at the beginning is the sum of the kinetic energy $E_{ki}$ and the difference in the potential energy $\\Delta E_p$  between the starting point and the bottom of the incline;\n\n        - $E_i = E_{ki} + \\Delta E_p = \\dfrac{1}{2}mu^2 + mgh \\hspace{1em}$ where $u = 1.2$ m s$^{-1}$ and $m = 2.0$ kg\n\n        The energy $E_f$ when the body reaches the rough surface equals the kinetic energy $E_{kf}$ due to the final speed;\n\n        - $E_f = E_{kf} = \\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2$ where $v=3.06$ m s$^{-1}$ and $m=2$ kg\n\n        The incline is frictionless and there is no air friction. Therefore, the total energy must be conserved throughout the motion. \n\n        $\\begin{aligned}\n\n        E_i &= E_f\\\\\n\n        \\hspace{2.3cm}\\\\\n\n        \\frac{1}{2}mu^2 + mgh &= \\frac{1}{2}mv^2\\\\      \n\n        \\hspace{2.3cm}\\\\\n\n        \\frac{1}{2}u^2 + gh &= \\frac{1}{2}v^2\\\\      \n\n        \\hspace{2.3cm}\\\\\n\n        gh &= \\frac{1}{2}v^2 - \\frac{1}{2}u^2\\\\      \n\n        \\hspace{2.3cm}\\\\\n\n        h &= \\frac{1}{2g}(v^2-u^2)\\\\\n\n        \\end{aligned}$\n\n        when the given values are substituted in the equation;\n\n        - $h = \\dfrac{1}{(2)(9.81)}(3.06^2 - 1.2^2) = 0.40$ m\n\n    2. $\\textrm{ }$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-05-28%20at%201.22.42%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-28 at 1.22.42 PM\">\n\n        A straight line with a non-zero minimum value of $v^2$ at $x=$max $\\checkmark$\n\n        negative gradient $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The height of the body at any position can be expressed as;\n\n        - $h=x\\sin\\theta$\n\n        The sum of gravitational potential energy and kinetic energy of the body is constant for every position of the body on the incline;\n\n        - $E_{tot} = \\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2 + mgh = \\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2 + mgx\\sin\\theta$ where $c$ is a constant.\n\n        It can be rearranged as:\n\n        - $E_{tot} - mgx\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2$\n\n        - $v^2=\\dfrac{2E_{tot}}{m} - 2gx\\sin\\theta$ where $\\dfrac{2E_{tot}}{m}$ and $2g\\sin\\theta$ are constant\n\n        This relationship is similar to the following expression:\n\n        - $y=mx + C$ where $C=\\dfrac{2E_{tot}}{m}$, $m=-2g\\sin\\theta$, $x = x$ and $y = v^2$\n\n        Therefore, the relationship between $x$ and $v^2$ can be expressed with a straight line that starts from a non-zero value and has a negative gradient.\n\n    3. Change in energy in the spring = $\\ll \\dfrac{1}{2}(32)(0.43)^2 = \\gg 3.0$ J $\\checkmark$\n\n        Energy difference/lost = $\\ll \\dfrac{1}{2}(2.0)(3.06)^2 - 3.0 = \\gg - 6.4$ J $\\checkmark$\n\n        Work done = $\\ll$ Friction = $\\mu mg\\cos\\theta d = (0.40)(2.0)(9.81)d(-1) \\gg - 7.8\\,d\\,\\checkmark$\n\n        $d=$ :answer[$0.82$ m] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The change in energy between the time at which the body starts to move on the rough surface and stops momentarily is the difference between the kinetic energy $E_{Ki}$ of the body and the elastic potential energy of the spring $E_{pi}$; \n\n        - $\\Delta E = E_{Ki} - E_{Pi}$\n\n        \n\n        - $\\Delta E = \\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2 - \\dfrac{1}{2}kx^2$ where $v = 3.06$ m s$^{-1}$, $m=2.0$ kg, $k=32$ N m$^{-1}$ and $x = 0.43$\n\n        - $\\Delta E = \\dfrac{1}{2}(2.0)(3.06)^2 - \\dfrac{1}{2}(32)(0.43)^2=6.4$ J\n\n        Work is done by the friction force $F_f$ on the body for a distance $d$;\n\n        - $W = F_fd\\cos\\theta = \\mu Nd\\cos\\theta = \\mu mgd\\cos\\theta$ \n\n        \n\n        where \n\n        \n\n        - $\\mu = 0.40,\\,\\theta = 180°$\n\n        - $W = (0.40)(2.0)(9.81)d (-1) = -7.8d$\n\n        The work done $W$ acting on the system equals the change in energy $\\Delta E$\n\n        - $W = \\Delta E$\n\n        - $-7.8\\,d = -6.4$ J\n\n        - $d = \\dfrac{-6.4}{-7.8} = 0.82$ m\n\n3. No change/same $\\checkmark$\n\n    The initial total energy of the body is same with previous alignment $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    The initial total energy of the body $E$\n\n    - $E = \\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2 + mgh$\n\n    None of the quantities above is changed. So the initial total energy is the same as the previous situation.\n\n    The initial total energy of the body equals the sum of elastic potential energy $E_p$ and the magnitude of work done by friction $W$;\n\n    - $E = E_p + W$\n\n    - $E = \\dfrac{1}{2}kx^2 + \\mu mgd$\n\n    None of the quantities above is changed with the angle of the incline. Hence, compression does not change."},{"ref":"PH0460","marks":"11","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0460\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\n1. Define power. [2 marks]\n\n2. A mass of 2.0 kg is pulled by a variable horizontal force $F$ on a smooth surface. A thread attached to the mass passes over an ideal pulley and supports another mass of 3.0 kg. \n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-05-29%20at%202.02.26%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-29 at 2.02.26 PM\">\n\n    The variation of $F$ with the distance moved $s$ by the mass of 2.0 kg is shown in the graph.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-05-29%20at%201.54.070%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-29 at 1.54.070 PM\">\n\n    1. Calculate work done, in J, on the 2.0 kg mass by the force $F$ when the mass travelled 0.90 m. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Calculate the final speed of the 2.0 kg mass. [3 marks] \n\n3. On another occasion, the horizontal force is no longer applied and the 2.0 kg mass is replaced with a motor which pulls the 3.0 kg mass with a constant speed of 0.50 m s$^{-1}$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-05-29%20at%202.04.50%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-29 at 2.04.50 PM\">\n\n    1.     On the graph, show the variation of the change in gravitational potential energy $\\Delta E_p$ of the mass with time $t$. [2 marks]\n\n        (no need to add values to the axes)\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-05-29%20at%204.44.21%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-29 at 4.44.21 PM\">\n\n    2. The power supplied to the motor is 20 W. Calculate the efficiency of the motor. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Rate of $\\checkmark$\n\n    Energy transfer/work done $\\checkmark$  \n\n    (accept work/change in energy $\\checkmark$ divided by time taken for the change $\\checkmark$)\n\n2. 1. Area=work done $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\ll (40)(0.9) + 0.5(120)(0.9) = \\gg$ :answer[$90$ J]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The area of the force-distance graph gives work done by the force when the force is parallel to the direction of motion. The area can be calculated by dividing it into two subsections; a rectangle and a triangle. The area of the rectangle $A_r$;\n\n        - $A_r=(0.9)(40)=36$\n\n \n\n        The area of the triangle $A_t$;\n\n        - $A_t=(0.5)(0.9)(120)=54$\n\n        When they are added,\n\n        - $36+54=90$ J\n\n    2. Increase in gravitational potential energy of the system/$\\Delta E_p = (3.0)(9.81)(0.9) = 26$ J $\\checkmark$\n\n        $90-26=64\\,\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\ll 64 = \\frac{1}{2}(2.0)v^2 + \\frac{1}{2}(3.0)v^2 = 2.5v^2 \\gg$\n\n        $\\ll v = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{63.5}{2.5}} = \\gg$ :answer[$5.0$ m s$^{-1}$]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        When force is applied to the mass, it accelerates. This increases its and the other mass's speed. Additionally, the other mass is pulled upward. Therefore, the energy supplied to the system by work done should be used as an increase in the kinetic energy and gravitational potential energy of the system. \n\n        The kinetic energy increase in the system $\\Delta E_K$; \n\n        - $\\Delta E_K = \\Delta E_{Kmass\\,of\\,2\\,kg} + \\Delta E_{Kmass\\,of\\,3\\,kg}$\n\n        - $\\Delta E_K = \\frac{1}{2}(2.0)v^2 + \\frac{1}{2}(3.0)v^2 = 2.5v^2$  where $v$ is the common speed of the masses\n\n        The mass of 2.0 kg does not change its vertical position. Therefore, an increase in gravitational potential energy happens only for the mass of 3.0 kg. The increase in gravitational potential energy $\\Delta E_p$;\n\n        - $\\Delta E_p = mgh$ where $m = 3.0$ kg and $h = 0.9$ m\n\n        - $\\Delta E_p = (3.0)(9.81)(0.9) = 26.5$ J\n\n        The energy is used for the increase in kinetic energy;\n\n        - $\\Delta E_K = W - \\Delta E_p = 90 - 26.5 = 63.5$ J\n\n        Therefore\n\n        - $2.5v^2 = 63.5$\n\n        - $v^2 = 25.4$\n\n        - $v=5.0$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n3. 1. $\\textrm{ }$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/PHG0460c.png\" alt=\"PHG0460c\">\n\n        $\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra by Tom Walsh- https://www.geogebra.org/m/jrWMapS7]}}$\n\n        A straight line $\\checkmark$\n\n        passing through the origin $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The change in gravitational potential energy $\\Delta E_p$ of the mass;\n\n        - $\\Delta E_p = mgh$  \n\n        Since the mass is moving with constant speed, $h$ can be expressed as \n\n        - $h = vt$ where $v$ is the speed of the mass\n\n        When it is substituted into the previous expression;\n\n        - $\\Delta E_p = mgvt$ where $m$, $g$ and $v$ are constant\n\n        Therefore, the change in gravitational potential energy and time should be directly proportional. This relationship can be shown on the graph with a straight line passing through the origin. \n\n    2. Power is delivered to the mass = $(29)(0.50) = 15$ W $\\checkmark$\n\n  \n\n        $\\ll$ Efficiency = $\\dfrac{Power\\,out}{Power\\,in} \\times 100\\% = \\dfrac{14.72}{20} \\times 100\\% = \\gg$ :answer[$74\\%$]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The power delivered $P_D$ by the motor is used for pulling the mass of 3 kg. It can be calculated as;\n\n        $P_D = Fv$ where $F$ is the force on the mass due to tension and $v$ is the speed of the mass\n\n        Since the mass is moving with constant speed, tension $F$ must be balancing the weight of the object $W$. \n\n        - $F = W = mg = (3.0)(9.81) = 29$ N\n\n        So, the power is delivered to the mass; \n\n        - $P_D = Fv = (29)(0.50) = 15$ W\n\n        - Efficiency = $\\dfrac{Power\\,out}{Power\\,in} \\times 100\\% = \\dfrac{14.72}{20} \\times 100\\% = 74\\%$"},{"ref":"PH0462","marks":"13","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0462\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\n1. Define momentum. [1 mark]\n\n2. A golf ball of mass 45 g moving with a constant velocity of 2.0 m s$^{-1}$ undergoes a head-on collision with a stationary ball of mass 2.0 kg. The line that joins the centres of the balls is along the direction of the velocity of the golf ball. \n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-06-30%20at%205.41.16%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-30 at 5.41.16 PM\">\n\n    1. Use Newton's Third Law and Second Law to deduce why the change in momentum of the golf ball is equal and opposite to the change in momentum of the stationary ball during the collision. [3 marks]\n\n    2.     The variation of force acting on the golf ball $F$  with the time $t$ during the collision is shown in the graph. \n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-06-30%20at%205.05.14%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-30 at 5.05.14 PM\">\n\n        Show that the magnitude of the change in momentum of the golf ball is approximately 0.09 kg m s$^{-1}$. [2 marks]\n\n    3. Calculate the speed of the golf ball just after the collision. [3 marks]\n\n    4.    Discuss whether the collision is elastic or inelastic. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1. The product of mass and velocity. $\\checkmark$\n\n2. \n\n    1. According to Newton's third Law, the force acting on the golf ball equals and is opposite to the force acting on the stationary ball/$F_g=-F_s$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        According to Newton's Second Law, the net force equals the rate of change in momentum/$\\dfrac{\\Delta p_g}{\\Delta t}=-\\dfrac{\\Delta p_s}{\\Delta t}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Since the time of collision is the same for both of the objects, changes in momenta are equal but in opposite directions/ since $\\Delta t$ for each ball is equal, $\\Delta p_g=-\\Delta p_s$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        According to Newton's third law, the force acting on the golf ball $F_g$ equals and is opposite to the force acting on the stationary ball $F_s$ during the collision. \n\n        - $F_g=-F_s$ \n\n        According to Newton's Second Law, the net force equals the rate of change in momentum. Therefore, the rate of change in momentum of the ball $\\dfrac{\\Delta p_g}{\\Delta t}$   equals and is opposite to the rate of change of momentum in the stationary ball $\\dfrac{\\Delta p_s}{\\Delta t}$. \n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta p_g}{\\Delta t}=-\\dfrac{\\Delta p_s}{\\Delta t}$\n\n        Since the time of collision is the same for both of the objects, changes in momenta are equal but in opposite directions. \n\n    2. Area under the graph = impulse / change in momentum $\\checkmark$\n\n        change in momentum = $\\dfrac{1}{2}(2.4)(72 \\times 10^{-3}) = 0.086\\ \\textrm{kg m s}^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$ (extra significant figures must be shown to receive full marks)\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The area under the “Force acting on the object” “Time of acting force” graph equals the impulse and also the change in momentum. Therefore, the area of the triangle $A_t$ should be calculated to get the change in momentum $\\Delta p$;\n\n        $\\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\Delta p &= A_t\\\\\n\n         &= \\dfrac{1}{2}bh\\\\ \n\n         &= \\dfrac{1}{2}(2.4)(72 \\times 10^{-3})\\\\\n\n         &=0.086\\textrm{ kg m s}^{-1}\n\n         \\end{aligned}$\n\n    3. Use of $\\Delta p=mv-mu$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $-0.086=(45 \\times 10^{-3})v-(45 \\times 10^{-3})(2.0)$ $\\checkmark$ (negative sign on change in momentum must be seen)\n\n        $v=\\ll \\dfrac{4 \\times 10^{-3}}{45 \\times 10^{-3}}\\gg= 0.089\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Note: Use of 0.09 kg m s$^{-1}$ gives a final speed of 0 m s$^{-1}$. This is accepted if full working is shown.\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The force acting on the golf ball during the collision is towards the left. Hence, the change in momentum of the golf ball must be towards the left. When left is chosen as negative and right is chosen as positive for the vectors, the following relationship can be written for change in momentum;\n\n        $\\Delta p=mv-mu$ where $\\Delta p=-0.086$ kg m s$^{-1}$, $m=45\\times10^{-3}$ kg, $u=2.0$ m s$^{-1}$, and $v=$ final velocity after the collision. \n\n        $\\begin{aligned}\n\n        -0.086&=(45 \\times 10^{-3})v-(45 \\times 10^{-3})(2.0)\\\\\n\n        \\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n        (90 \\times 10^{-3})-0.086&=(45 \\times 10^{-3})v\\\\\n\n        \\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n        4 \\times 10^{-3}&=(45 \\times 10^{-3})v\\\\\n\n        \\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n        \\frac{4 \\times 10^{-3}}{45 \\times 10^{-3}}&=v\\\\\n\n        \\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n        0.089\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}&=v\n\n        \\end{aligned}$\n\n    4. Kinetic energy before the collision = $\\dfrac{1}{2}m_g u_g^2=\\dfrac{1}{2}(45 \\times 10^{-3})(2.0)^2=0.090$ J $\\checkmark$\n\n        Change in momentum of larger ball = 0.086 N s\n\n        - $p = mv$\n\n        \n\n        - $0.086=2.0v$\n\n        Speed of the stationary ball after the collision $=0.043\\,\\ce{m s}^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Correct calculation of final total kinetic energy according to the result of (b)(iii)\n\n        - $E_{total}=\\dfrac{1}{2}(45 \\times 10^{-3})(0.089)^2+ \\dfrac{1}{2}(2.0)(0.043)^2=0.0020$ J\n\n        - $0.0020$ J $\\checkmark$\n\n        Kinetic energy after is less so the collision is inelastic. $\\checkmark$\n\n        \n\n        Note: A calculation leading to the kinetic energy after being equal to the kinetic energy before and a statement that the collision is elastic would score only one mark total.\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The total kinetic energy of the balls before the collision $E_{tkb}$ equals the kinetic energy of the golf ball before the collision $E_{kgb}$;\n\n        - $E_{tkb}=E_{kgb}=\\dfrac{1}{2} m_g u_g^2=\\dfrac{1}{2}(45 \\times 10^{-3})(2.0)^2=0.090$ J\n\n        The speed of the stationary ball after the collision can be found by\n\n        $\\Delta p=mv-mu$ where $\\Delta p=0.086$ kg m s$^{-1}$, $m=2.0$ kg, $u=0$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n        - $\\begin{aligned}\n\n        \\ 0.086 &=2.0v\\\\\n\n        \\ \\frac{0.086}{2.0}&= v\\\\\n\n        \\ 0.043\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}&=v\\\\\n\n        \\end{aligned}$\n\n        The total kinetic energy of the balls after the collision $E_{tka}$ equals the sum of the kinetic energy of the golf $E_{kga}$ and stationary balls $E_{ksa}$ after the collision;  \n\n        - $\\begin{aligned}\n\n        E_{tka}&=E_{kga}+E_{ksa}\\\\\n\n        &=\\frac{1}{2} m_g v_g^2+\\frac{1}{2} m_s v_s^2\\\\\n\n        &=\\frac{1}{2}(45 \\times 10^{-3})(0.089)^2+\\frac{1}{2}(2.0)(0.043)^2\\\\\n\n        &=0.0020\\textrm{ J}\n\n        \\end{aligned}$\n\n        $E_{tkb}>E_{tka}$, the kinetic energy of the systems is not conserved. Therefore, collision is inelastic."},{"ref":"PH0463","marks":"4","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0463\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\n1. State the law of conservation of momentum. [2 marks]\n\n2. A stationary body explodes into two pieces of mass as $m_1$ and $m_2$ in opposite directions. \n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/PH0463.png\" alt=\"PH0463\">\n\n    Just after the explosion, the ratio of the kinetic energy of $m_1$ to the kinetic energy of $m_2$ is found to be 9. \n\n    \n\n   $\\dfrac{E_{k(m1)}}{E_{k(m2)}} = 9$\n\n    \n\n    Calculate the ratio of masses $\\dfrac{m_1}{m_2}$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. When the net force on a system is zero / the system is isolated $\\checkmark$\n\n    The momentum of the system does not change / the total momentum is constant $\\checkmark$\n\n2. The magnitude of the momentum of the pieces is equal $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $\\ll \\dfrac{\\frac{p^2}{2m_1}}{\\frac{p^2}{2m_2}} = 9 \\gg$\n\n    \n\n    - $\\dfrac{m_1}{m_2} = \\dfrac{1}{9}\\,\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    Since the body is at rest, the total momentum before the explosion is zero. Due to the conservation of momentum, total momentum after the collision must be zero. The two pieces move in the opposite directions. Therefore, the magnitude of momentum they possess must be the same to cancel each other. \n\n    \n\n    From the data booklet\n\n    \n\n    - $E_k = \\dfrac{p^2}{2m}$\n\n    \n\n    The ratio of kinetic energies; \n\n    Therefore \n\n    - $E_k \\propto \\dfrac{1}{m}$\n\n    \n\n    We are told that\n\n    - $\\dfrac{E_{k(m1)}}{E_{k(m2)}} = 9$\n\n    Therefore,\n\n    \n\n    - $\\dfrac{m_1}{m_2} = \\dfrac{1}{9}$"},{"ref":"PH0464","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0464\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nA box half filled with sand is pulled by a motor at a constant speed of $2.0$ m s$^{-1}$ on a frictionless surface. During the motion, sand is added to the box from above at a constant rate of $\\sigma$ kg s$^{-1}$. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-05-29%20at%201.45.240%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-29 at 1.45.240 PM\">\n\n1. State the condition related to the mass of an object in order for the equation $F_{net}=ma$ to be equivalent to the equation $F_{net}=\\dfrac{\\Delta p}{t}$ . [1 mark]\n\n2. The force applied by the motor on the box is $0.11$ N. Determine $\\sigma$. [2 marks]\n\n3. The motor has an efficiency of 75%. Calculate the total power provided to the motor. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Mass of the object should be constant $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    Net force is equal to the rate of change of momentum\n\n    - $F_{net}=\\dfrac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t}=\\dfrac{\\Delta (mv)}{\\Delta t}$    \n\n    Therefore $F_{net}$ can depend on the rate of change of mass or the rate of change of velocity or both.\n\n    The equation \n\n    - $F=ma$    \n\n    is equivalent to \n\n    \n\n    - $F=m\\dfrac{\\Delta v}{t}$\n\n    and therefore assumes that mass is constant and that only velocity is changing with time.\n\n2. ::indent\n\n    - $F=\\dfrac{\\Delta p}{t}=\\dfrac{\\Delta(mv)}{t}$\n\n    - $\\dfrac{F}{v}=\\dfrac{\\Delta m}{t}$\n\n    - $\\dfrac{F}{v}=\\sigma$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $\\sigma=\\dfrac{0.11}{2.0}= $ :answer[$0.055\\textrm{ kg s}^{-1}$]  $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n     The force $F$ applied to the box;\n\n    - $F=\\dfrac{\\Delta p}{t}=\\dfrac{\\Delta(mv)}{t}$\n\n    Since $v$ is constant;\n\n    - $F=\\dfrac{v\\Delta m}{t}$\n\n    When $\\dfrac{\\Delta m}{t}$ is replaced with $\\sigma$;\n\n    - $F=v\\sigma$\n\n    - $\\dfrac{F}{v}=\\sigma$ where $F=0.11$ N and $v=2.0$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n    - $\\sigma=\\dfrac{0.11}{2.0}=0.055$ kg s$^{-1}$\n\n3. Useful power output of the motor $=\\ll Fv=(0.11)(2.0)=\\gg 0.22$ W $\\checkmark$\n\n    The power provided to the motor $=\\ll \\dfrac{0.22}{75\\%} \\times 100\\% = \\gg $ :answer[ 0.29 W] $ \\checkmark$\n\n    \n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    The useful power output $P_o$ of the motor;\n\n    - $P_o=Fv=(0.11)(2.0)=0.22$ W\n\n    The efficiency of the motor;\n\n    - Efficiency = $\\dfrac{\\textrm{Useful power output}}{\\textrm{Input power}} \\times 100\\%$\n\n    So the input power;\n\n    - Input power = $\\dfrac{\\textrm{Useful power output}}{\\textrm{Efficiency}} \\times 100\\%$ \n\n    Substitute the given and calculated values;\n\n    - Input power = $\\dfrac{0.22}{75\\%} \\times 100\\% = 0.29\\,W$"},{"ref":"PH0497","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0497\n\nA spring with spring constant $k$ is extended by a distance $x$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-04-07%20at%208.19.25%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-07 at 8.19.25 AM\">\n\n$\\small{\\textrm{[Created using Geogebra https://www.geogebra.org/m/vUK7gGkN]}}$\n\nThe uncertainty of $k$ is 2% and that of $x$ is 3%. \n\nWhat is the uncertainty of the potential energy stored in the spring? \n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 8%\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 11%\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 12%\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 18%","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe formula for potential energy stored is \n\n- $E_H=\\dfrac{1}{2}kx^2$\n\nSince $E_H$ is calculated from multiplying $k$ and $x^2$ (ignoring the constant $\\frac{1}{2}$ as there is no uncertainty for it), then the uncertainty of $E_H$ is calculated by adding the fractional uncertainties of $x^2$ and $k$.\n\nThe fractional uncertainty of $x^2$ is double the fractional uncertainty on $x$, which gives \n\n- $2\\times3\\% = 6\\%$\n\nThe uncertainty of $E_P$ therefore \n\n-  2% + 6% = 8%"},{"ref":"PH0559","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0559\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nTwo blocks of masses 3.0 kg and 4.0 kg are held stationary with a thread connecting the blocks. The mass of 3.0 kg is on a rough incline that makes an angle of 30° with the ground and it starts to slip up when the other mass is slightly more than 4.0 kg. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/PH0559MS.png\" alt=\"PH0559MS\">\n\n1. State two differences between static friction and dynamic friction [2 marks]\n\n2. Calculate the coefficient of static friction between the mass of 3.0 kg and the rough surface. [3 marks]\n\n3. The thread connecting masses breaks and the mass of 3.0 kg slides down. Show that the acceleration of the mass of 3.0 kg is around 1.5 m s$^{-2}$. The coefficient of dynamic friction between 3.0 kg mass and the rough surface is 0.40. [2 marks]\n\n4. Determine the speed of the mass of 3.0 kg after it has moved through a vertical height of 1.0 m. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Static friction occurs when the object is not moving (relative to a surface) and Dynamic friction occurs when there is relative motion between the object and a surface $\\checkmark$\n\n    The magnitude of static friction is dependent on external forces and the magnitude of dynamic friction is not dependent on external forces **OR** static friction force can vary up to a maximum while dynamic friction is a constant value  $\\checkmark$\n\n2. Component of weight parallel to the incline = $\\ll mg\\sin(30°)=(3.0)(9.81)(0.5)=\\gg 15 \\ll\\textrm{N}\\gg$ and tension in thread $T=mg=(4.0)(9.81)=39$ N $\\checkmark$\n\n    Static friction = $\\ll 39-15=\\gg 24 \\ll\\textrm{N}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    coefficient of static friction = $\\ll \\dfrac{24}{(3.0)(9.81)\\cos(30°)}= \\gg$ :answer[0.96] $\\checkmark$ (0.94 to 0.96 accepted)\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    According to the diagram, the mass of 3 kg is balanced with weight $W$, tension $T$ static friction $F_f$ and normal force $N$;\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/PH0559Q.png\" alt=\"PH0559Q\">\n\n    Weight has two components parallel to the incline $W_h$ and perpendicular to the incline $W_v$; \n\n    - $W_h=mg\\sin(30°)=(3.0)(9.81)(0.5)=15$ N down to the slope\n\n    The tension balances the weight of the mass of 4.0 kg; \n\n    - $T=mg=(4.0)(9.81)=39$ N\n\n    The forces parallel to the incline must cancel each other; \n\n    - $\\begin{aligned}\n\n    W_h+F_f&=T\\\\\n\n    \\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n    15+F_f&=39\\\\\n\n    \\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n    F_f&=24\\textrm{ N}\\\\\n\n    \\end{aligned}$\n\n    By using the equation of static friction;\n\n    $\\begin{aligned}\n\n    F_f&=\\mu R\\\\\n\n    \\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n    \\mu(3.0)(9.81)\\cos(30°)&=24\\\\\n\n    \\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n    \\mu&=0.96\\\\\n\n    \\end{aligned}$\n\n3. The dynamic friction = $\\ll\\mu R=(0.40)(3.0)(9.81)\\cos30°=\\gg 10 \\ll N\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $a$ = $\\ll\\dfrac{14.7-10.2}{3}=\\gg $ :answer[1.5 m s$^{-2}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    There are only two forces that act on the mass parallel to the incline. They are components of the weight $W_h$ down to the slope and dynamic friction $F_f$ up the slope. The dynamic friction; \n\n    - $F_f=\\mu R=(0.40)(3.0)(9.81)\\cos(30°)=10$ N\n\n    - $F_{net}=ma$\n\n    - $14.7-10.2=3.0a$\n\n    - $a=1.5$ m s$^{-2}$\n\n4. displacement of the mass = $\\ll \\dfrac{1.0}{\\sin(30^o)} = \\gg 2.0 \\ll\\textrm{m}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $v = \\ll\\sqrt{(2)(1.5)(2.0)}=\\gg 2.4 \\ll\\textrm{m s}^{-1}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    **OR**\n\n    Heat lost due to friction = $\\ll F_f d=(10)(2.0)=\\gg 20\\ll\\textrm{J}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $v$ = $\\ll \\sqrt{\\dfrac{(2)(9.0)}{(3.0)}}=\\gg$ :answer[2.4 m s$^{-1}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    The overall displacement s of the mass when it displaced 1.0 m vertically;\n\n    - $s=\\dfrac{1.0}{\\sin(30^o)}=2.0$ m\n\n    By using a suvat equation;\n\n    - $v^2=u^2+2as$ while $u=0$, $a=1.5$ m s$^{-2}$, and $s=2.0$ m\n\n    - $v^2=(2)(1.5)(2.0)=6.0$\n\n    - $v=2.4$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n    This question can be also done with the conservation of energy approach; \n\n    The energy dissipated due to friction is\n\n    - $Q=F_f d=(10)(2)=20$ J\n\n    and the change in gravitational potential energy can be found by\n\n    - $\\Delta E_p=mg\\Delta h=(3.0)(9.81)(1.0)=29$ J\n\n    The gravitational potential energy was converted to thermal energy from friction and kinetic energy, therefore\n\n    - $29=\\Delta E_k+20$\n\n    - $\\Delta E_k=9.0$ J\n\n    Solving for speed gives\n\n    - $\\Delta E_k=\\frac{1}{2} mv^2$\n\n    - $v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2\\Delta E_k}{m}}$\n\n    - $v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{(2)(9.0)}{(3.0)}}=2.4$ m s$^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH0562","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0562\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nA cannonball is fired horizontally from a cannon positioned at the edge of a cliff.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/PH0562zzz.png\" alt=\"PH0562zzz\">\n\n1. In the context of Newton\\'s Laws, explain why the cannon recoils to the left when the cannonball is fired. [2 marks]\n\n2. The initial recoil speed of the cannon is $1.0$ m s$^{-1}$ and its mass is 1200 kg.\n\n    1. After the ball is fired, the cannon rolls to the left and experiences a resistive force of $8.0$ kN as it rolls. Determine the distance covered by the cannon. [2 marks]\n\n    2. The mass of the cannonball is $9.0$ kg. Show that the magnitude of the velocity of the ball after being fired is approximately $133$ m s$^{-1}$. [2 marks]\n\n    3. Calculate the magnitude of the displacement of the cannonball after $5.0$ s.  Air resistance can be ignored. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. By Newton\\'s 3rd law, there is a force to the right acting on the cannonball $\\checkmark$\n\n    There is an equal and opposite force (to the left) on the cannon $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    There is a force acting on the cannonball during the firing. According to Newton\\'s third law, there must be an equal and opposite force on the cannon.\n\n2.\n    1. $a=\\dfrac{F}{m}=\\dfrac{8000}{1200}=6.7$ m s$^{-2}$\n\n        $s=\\dfrac{v^2}{2a}=\\dfrac{1}{(2)(6.7)}= $ :answer[0.074 m]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The deceleration *a* of the cannon is:\n\n        - $a=\\dfrac{F}{m}=\\dfrac{8000}{1200}=6.7$ m s$^{-2}$\n\n        The distance *s* can be calculated using the uniform acceleration equations\n\n        - $v^2=u^2+2as$\n\n        where\n\n        -  $v=0$\n        - $u=1$ m s$^{-1}$\n        - $a=-6.7$ m s$^{-2}$\n        - $0=1^2+(2)(-6.7)s$\n        - $s=\\dfrac{1}{(2)(6.7)}=0.074$ m\n\n    2. Any reference to the conservation of momentum $\\checkmark$\n\n        $v_2 = \\dfrac{(-m_1)(v_1)}{m_2} = \\dfrac{-(1200)(1.0)}{9.0} = \\ll$ -133 m s$^{-1} \\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Due to the conservation of momentum, the total momentum of the objects before the firing should be equal to the total momentum of the objects after the firing.\n\n        The total momentum of the objects before the firing is zero due to the absence of motion. Therefore the total momentum of the objects after firing is also zero\n\n        - $0=m_1v_1+m_2v_2$\n\n        where\n\n        - $m_1=1200$ kg\n        - $v_1=1$ m s$^{-1}$\n        - $m_2=9.0$ kg\n        - $v_2=\\dfrac{{-m}_1v_1}{m_2}=\\dfrac{-(1200)(1.0)}{9.0}=-133$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n        The negative sign represents the direction of the velocity. Therefore it is opposite to the direction of the motion of the cannon.\n\n    3. Horizontal displacement $=vt=\\left(133\\right)\\left(5.0\\right)=666$ m $\\checkmark$\n\n        Vertical displacement=$ut+\\fdrac{1}{2}at^2=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(9.81\\right)\\left(5^2\\right)=122$ m $\\checkmark$\n\n        Displacement of the cannonball$=\\sqrt{{666}^2+{122}^2}= $ :answer[677 m]  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Due to the absence of air resistance, the horizontal velocity of the ball is constant and vertical velocity increases with the acceleration of free fall. The horizontal displacement *x* of the cannonball\n\n        - $x=vt$  where $v=133$ m s$^{-1}$ and $t=3.0$ s\n\n        - $x=\\left(133\\right)\\left(5.0\\right)=666$ m\n\n        The vertical displacement *y* of the cannonball\n\n        - $y=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}at^2$\n\n        where\n\n        - $u=0$\n        - $a=9.81$ m s$^{-1}$\n        - $t=5.0\\, s$\n        - $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(9.81\\right)\\left(5^2\\right)=122\\, $m\n\n        The magnitude of the displacement *s* can be found by using Pythagoras\\'s theorem;\n\n        - $s=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$\n        - $s=\\sqrt{{666}^2+{122}^2}=677$ m"},{"ref":"PH0564","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0564\n\nA varying force acts on a spring. The variation of the extension $x$ of the spring with force $F$ acting on it is shown in the graph below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-05-30%20at%2011.51.09%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-30 at 11.51.09 AM\">\n\nThe elastic potential energy stored in the spring is $E$ when the extension of the spring is $e$. What is the work done by force on the spring to increase the extension from $e$ to $3e$ ?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  2E$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}      4E$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}      8E$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}      9E$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe elastic potential energy stored in the spring is $E$ when it is stretched by $e$;\n\n- $E_H=\\dfrac{1}{2}ke^2$  where $k$ is the spring constant of the spring\n\nThe elastic potential energy stored in the spring when it is stretched by $3e$ can be found  as;\n\n- $E_{Hfinal}=\\dfrac{1}{2}k{(3e)}^2=\\dfrac{1}{2}k{9e}^2=9E$\n\nThe work done on the spring can be found by calculating the change in the elastic potential energy $\\Delta E_H$ of the spring.\n\n- $\\mathrm{\\Delta}E_H=\\mathrm{\\Delta}E_{H(final)}-\\mathrm{\\Delta}E_{H\\left(initial\\right)}$\n\n- $\\mathrm{\\Delta}E_H=9E-E=8E$\n\nTherefore, the answer is C."},{"ref":"PH0565","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0565\n\nA metal ball of mass $m$ is released from a height of $h$ above a massless platform attached to a vertical spring.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/PH0565.png\" alt=\"PH0565\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with chemix - https://chemix.org/]}}$\n\nThe spring constant of the spring is given by $\\dfrac{mg}{h}$. The ball hits the spring and compresses it. When the spring is compressed by a distance $h$, what is the speed of the ball?\n\n(Energy transfer only happens between spring and ball and the kinetic energy of the spring can be ignored)\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}      \\sqrt{2h}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}      \\sqrt{2gh}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}      \\sqrt{3h}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  \\sqrt{3gh}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nWhen the initial position and final position of the ball are compared in terms of energy, it can be realized that the decrease in gravitational potential energy $E_p$ of the ball equals the increase in kinetic energy $E_{k}$ of the ball and the increase in elastic potential energy $E_H$ of the spring. Additionally, the total decrease in height of the ball is $2h$.\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta E_p&=E_k +E_H\\\\\n\\hspace{2.3cm}\\\\\nmg(2h)&=\\frac{1}{2}mv^2+\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{mg}{h}h^2 \\\\\n\\hspace{2.3cm}\\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}mv^2&=mg(2h)-\\frac{1}{2}mgh\\\\\n\\hspace{2.3cm}\\\\\nmv^2&=4mgh-mgh\\\\\n\\hspace{2.3cm}\\\\\nmv^2&=3mgh\\\\\n\\hspace{2.3cm}\\\\\nv&=\\sqrt{3gh}\\\\\n\\end{aligned}$\n\nTherefore, the answer is D."},{"ref":"PH0566","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0566\n\nA box of mass 2.0 kg starts to move on a level surface under the effect of a varying net force $F$. The variation of $F$ with the displacement $s$ of the object is shown in the graph below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-06-30%20at%205.12.14%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-30 at 5.12.14 PM\">\n\n What is the speed of the box at s = 5 m?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $5 m s$^{-1}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  \\sqrt{5}$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}      1$5 m s$^{-1}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}      \\sqrt{15}$ m s$^{-1}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe work done $W$ by the $F$ on the box equals the change in kinetic energy $\\Delta E_k$ of the box and work done can be found by calculating the area under the graph, the area between the line and the displacement axis.\n\n- $W=\\Delta E_k \\hspace{2em}$     and   $\\hspace{2em} W=$ Area of the graph\n\n- $\\Delta E_k=$ Area of the graph (using trapezoid formula)\n\n- $\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(2\\right)v^2=\\dfrac{1}{2}(8+2)(5)$\n\n- $v^2=25$\n\n- $v=5\\, $m$\\,$s$^{-1}$\n\nAlternatively, we can treat the area as a rectangle and a triangle.\n\n- $\\Delta E_k=$ Area of the graph (using rectangle and triangle)\n\n- $\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(2\\right)v^2=(2)(5)+\\dfrac{1}{2}(6)(5)$\n\n- $v^2=25$\n\n- $v=5\\, $m$\\,$s$^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH0568","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0568\n\nTwo masses of $2$ kg and $4$ kg are connected with a string that passes over a pulley. They are released from rest. Energy is lost in the pulley due to friction.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-07-08%20at%205.43.14%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-08 at 5.43.14 PM\">\n\nWhen the $2$ kg mass has gained a gravitational potential energy of $E$, the $4$ kg mass has a kinetic energy of $\\dfrac{1}{2}E$. How much energy has been lost to friction up to that point in time?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}      \\dfrac{E}{4}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}      \\dfrac{E}{2}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}      E$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}      2E$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nSince both of the masses are connected by the string, they should have the same speed and vertical displacement.\n\nBecause both $E_p$ and $E_k$ are proportional to the mass of the object,\n\nGravitational potential energy loss of the mass of $4$ kg will be twice the gain of the small mass, which would be $2E$\n\nSimilarly, the kinetic energy of the mass of $2\\, kg$ will be half of that of the $4\\,kg$ which gives $\\dfrac{E}{4}$\n\nDue to the principle of energy conservation;\n\nThe potential energy lost by the mass of  $4$ kg is equal to the sum of\n\n- The potential energy gained by the mass of $2$ kg $(2E)$\n- energy lost due to friction $(U)$\n- kinetic energy of the mass of $2$ kg $\\bigg(\\dfrac{E}{4}\\bigg)$ and $4$ kg $\\bigg(\\dfrac{E}{2}\\bigg)$\n\n- $2E\\ =E+U+\\dfrac{E}{4}+\\dfrac{E}{2}$\n\n- $U=\\dfrac{E}{4}$"},{"ref":"PH0569","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0569\n\nThe engine of a racing car provides a forward thrust of 800 N. The average of the total resistive forces acting on the car is 300 N and the car covers a distance of 1.0 km in 10 s.\n\nWhat is the average power input of the engine?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}      5.0\\times{10}^3$ W\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}      8.0\\times{10}^3$ W\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}      5.0\\times{10}^4$ W\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  8.0\\times{10}^4$ W","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe distance covered by the car in meters;\n\n- $1\\ \\ce{km}=1000$ m\n\nThe average speed $v_{av}$  of the car;\n\n- $v_{av}=\\dfrac{s}{t}=\\dfrac{1000\\textrm{ m}}{10\\textrm{ s}}=100$ m s$^{-1}$\n\nThe average output power $P_{av}$ of the engine;\n\n- $P_{av}=Fv_{av} \\hspace{2em}$ where  $\\hspace{2em}F=800$ N since it is the force of the engine\n\n- $P_{av}=\\left(800\\right)\\left(100\\right)=8.0\\times{10}^4$ W\n\nNote that this power from the engine does not all contribute to the kinetic energy of the car, as some of it is used to overcome the resistive forces."},{"ref":"PH0570","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0570\n\nAn object is thrown vertically upwards on the surface of the Moon. The maximum height that the object can reach is $h$. When the object reaches the height of  $\\dfrac{h}{3}$ above the surface, what is the ratio\n\n$\\hspace{3cm} \\dfrac{\\textrm{Kinetic\\ energy\\ of\\ the\\ object\\ at}\\ \\dfrac{h}{3}\\ }{\\textrm{Gravitational\\ potential\\ energy\\ of\\ the\\ object\\ at}\\ \\dfrac{h}{3}\\ }$\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em} 1 $\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}      \\dfrac{2}{3}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}      \\dfrac{3}{2}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  2$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nSince there is no atmosphere on the moon, energy is not lost to the surroundings due to air resistance. At the highest position of the object, all of the kinetic energy will have been converted to gravitational potential energy $E_t$.\n\nThus the total energy of the object:\n\n- $E_t=mgh$\n\nwhere $mg$ is the weight of the object.\n\nThe total energy at $\\dfrac{h}{3}$ should be the sum of the kinetic $E_{k\\ at\\ \\frac{h}{3}}$ and the gravitational potential energy of the object $E_{p\\ at\\ \\frac{h}{3}}$.\n\n- $E_t=E_{k\\ at\\ \\frac{h}{3}}+E_{p\\ at\\ \\frac{h}{3}}$\n\n- $mgh=E_{k\\ at\\ \\frac{h}{3}}+mg\\dfrac{h}{3}$\n\n- $mg\\dfrac{2h}{3}=E_{k\\ at\\ \\frac{h}{3}}$\n\nTherefore\n\n- $\\dfrac{E_{k\\ at\\ \\frac{h}{3}}}{E_{p\\ at\\ \\frac{h}{3}}}=\\dfrac{mg\\frac{2h}{3}}{mg\\frac{h}{3}}=2$"},{"ref":"PH0571","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0571\n\nA box is pulled up a slope inclined at 30° to the horizontal by a force of $F$. The angle between the direction of $F$ and the plane of the incline is also 30°.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/PH0571.png\" alt=\"PH0571\">\n\nThe box is pulled a distance of d up along the slope. What is the work done by F on the box?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}      Fd\\cos(30°) $\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}      Fd\\cos(60°) $\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}      Fd\\sin(30°) $\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}      Fd\\cos^2(30°) $","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe component of the force parallel to the slope can be expressed as\n\n- $F_p=F\\cos(30°)$\n\nThe work done $W$ is the product of the force $F_p$ in the direction of motion and distance $d$.\n\n- $W=F_pd$\n\n- $W=F\\cos(30°) d=Fd\\cos(30°)$"},{"ref":"PH0572","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0572\n\nAn object of mass $1.0$ kg rests on a rough surface. Another object of mass $2.0$ kg is connected to it with a string.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-07-08%20at%205.53.49%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-08 at 5.53.49 PM\">\n\nWhen the objects are released, the masses accelerate at $\\dfrac{g}{2}$. The work done by the tension of the string on the $1.0$ kg mass is $40$ J. Which of the following is correct for the $1.0$ kg mass during this period?\n\n| | Magnitude of the force of friction / N | Distance travelled / m |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| 10 |$4.0$ |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 5 | $4.0$  |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 10 | $2.0$  |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 5 | $2.0$  |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe acceleration is given as $\\dfrac{g}{2}$ which is equal to $\\dfrac{10}{2} = 5$ m s$^{-2}$\n\nThe free body diagram of the forces on the mass of $2.0$ kg:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-05-30%20at%2012.18.45%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-30 at 12.18.45 PM\">\n\nAccording to Newton's second law, the relationship between tension $T$, the weight of the object $W$ and the acceleration of the object:\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\nF_{net}&=ma\\\\\n\\hspace{2.3cm}\\\\\nW_2-T&=(2.0)(5)\\\\\n\\hspace{2.3cm}\\\\\n(2)(10)-T&=(2.0)(5)\\\\\n\\hspace{2.3cm}\\\\\nT&=10\\textrm{ N}\\\\\n\\end{aligned}$\n\nThe free body diagram of the forces on the mass of $1.0$ kg:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-05-30%20at%2012.18.51%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-30 at 12.18.51 PM\">\n\nThe normal reaction force $F_N$ of the ground on the box balances the weight $W$ of the object. According to Newton's second law, the relationship between Tension $T$, the force of friction on the object $F_f$ and the acceleration of the object\n\n- $T-F_f=(1.0)(5)$\n\nWhen the magnitude of $T$ is substituted in the equation;\n\n- $10-F_f=(1.0)(5)$\n\n- $\\hspace{2em}F_f=5$ N\n\nThe work done $W$ by $T$ can be found by the product of $T$ and the distance $d$ travelled by the object.\n\n- $W_1=Td$\n\n- $40=10d$\n\n- $d=4.0$ m"},{"ref":"PH0585","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0585\n\nA 1000 kg car is moving with a kinetic energy of ${10}^5$ J.\n\nEstimate the order of magnitude of its momentum.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}      {10}^3$ kg m s$^{-1}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}      {10}^4$ kg m s$^{-1}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}      {10}^5$ kg m s$^{-1}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}      {10}^6$ kg m s$^{-1}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe relationship between kinetic energy $E_k$ and momentum $p$ of the car is given by the following formula:\n\n- $E_k=\\dfrac{p^2}{2m}$\n\n- $p^2=2mE_k$\n\nUsing the mass of the car as ${10}^3\\, $kg.\n\n- $p^2\\approx 2\\left({10}^3\\right)\\left({10}^5\\right)$\n\nBecause we are only looking for the order of magnitude, we can disregard the 2\n\n- $p^2\\approx{10}^8 $\n\n- $p\\approx{10}^4$"},{"ref":"PH0588","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0588\n\nAn object of mass $m$ with a speed of $3.0$ m s$^{-1}$ collides horizontally with a stationary box of mass $2m$. After the collision, the object and the box move together.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-05-30%20at%2012.46.16%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-30 at 12.46.16 PM\">\n\nThe friction acting on the box and the object is $6.0$ N.\n\nWhich expression gives total distance travelled by the combined objects before coming to rest after the collision?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}      \\dfrac{m}{16}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}          \\dfrac{m}{12}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}      \\dfrac{m}{8}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}      \\dfrac{m}{4}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nBy using the principle of conservation of momentum, the combined velocity $v$ of the object and the box can be found.\n\nThe total momentum of the object and the box before the collision = $3m$\n\nThe total momentum of the object and the box after the collision = $(2m+m)v=3mv$\n\n- $3m=3mv$\n\n- $1.0\\ \\ce{m s}^{-1}=v$\n\nUsing conservation of energy\n\nInitial kinetic energy of the system = $\\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2 = \\dfrac{1}{2}(3m)1^2 = 1.5m$\n\nFinal kinetic energy of the system = 0\n\nTherefore work done by friction is\n\n- $W =\\dfrac{3m}{2}$\n\n- $W = Fs $\n\n- $\\dfrac{3m}{2} = 6 s$\n\n- $s = \\dfrac{m}{4}$"},{"ref":"PH0617","marks":"11","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0617\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\n1.\n    The diagram below shows a water slide. As a test, an object is released from point A, 6.0 m above the ground level. Point B at the end of the slide is 2.0 m above the ground level.  Location C is the point of contact of the slide with the ground. The slide approximates a circular shape at this point.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/PH0617.png\" alt=\"PH0617\">\n\n    Air resistance and friction on the object are negligible.\n\n    1. Draw the free-body diagram of the forces acting on the object as it passes point D. [2 marks]\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/PH0617q2.png\" alt=\"PH0617q2\">\n\n    2. At the instant the object passes point C, explain why there is acceleration and yet no work is done. [2 marks]\n\n2. The object slides from point A to point B and is launched from point B with an angle.\n\n    1. Show that the speed of the object at point $B$ is approximately $8.9$ m s$^{-1}$ [2 marks]\n\n    2. At point B, the object is launched into the air and lands in a pool of water that is 2.2 m below point B.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-06-30%20at%205.52.08%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-30 at 5.52.08 PM\">\n\n        After 1.1 s, it reaches the surface of the water. Show that the angle $\\theta$ of the launch above the horizontal is approximately 22$\\degree$. [3 marks]\n    3. Calculate the horizontal distance travelled by the object between losing contact with the slide and reaching the water. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.  $\\hspace{1em}$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-05-30%20at%204.36.32%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-30 at 4.36.32 PM\">\n\n        Correct directions of forces with correct labels $\\checkmark$\n\n        The weight vector is longer than the reaction force $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Two forces are acting on the box. Since the box is accelerating down to the slope, the resultant force should be down the slope. The component of the weight that is perpendicular to the incline must be balanced by the normal force. The other component of the weight is the force that creates acceleration. Therefore, the weight should be greater than the normal force. It can be represented with a longer arrow for weight.\n\n    2. Acceleration is only a change in direction $\\checkmark$\n\n        The resultant force is perpendicular to the direction of the motion $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        There is a change in direction of the box, therefore there is acceleration and there must be a non-zero resultant force.\n\n        In uniform circular motion, the resultant force is perpendicular to the direction of the motion.\n\n        The work done is:\n\n        - $W=Fd\\cos(\\theta)$,\n\n        where $\\theta$ is the angle between the direction of the motion and the force\n\n        Since $\\theta=90\\degree$, $\\cos(90\\degree)=0$.\n\n        Therefore the work done is zero.\n\n2.\n    1.  ::indent\n        - $mg\\Delta h=\\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2 \\ \\checkmark$\n\n        - $v=$ :answer[$8.86$ m s$^{-1}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        By using energy conservation between points $A$ and $B$:\n\n        $\\begin{aligned}\n        E_{P\\ at\\ 6\\ m}&=E_{P\\ at\\ 2\\ m}+E_K\\\\\n        mgh_6 &= mgh_2 +\\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2\\\\\n        m\\left(9.81\\right)\\left(6.0\\right)&=m\\left(9.81\\right)\\left(2.0\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}mv^2\\\\\n        \\left(9.81\\right)\\left(6.0\\right)&=\\left(9.81\\right)\\left(2.0\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}v^2\\\\\n        \\sqrt{\\left(39.2\\right)\\left(2\\right)}&=v\\\\\n        v&=8.86\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}\n        \\end{aligned}$\n\n    2. Use of $u=8.9\\sin\\theta$ and $s=-2.2\\textrm{ m}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $-2.2=\\left(8.9\\right)\\left(\\sin\\theta\\right)\\left(1.1\\right)+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(-9.81\\right)\\left(1.1\\right)^2$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\theta=22.3\\degree$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The launch speed of the box is $8.9$ m s$^{-1}$. The vertical displacement $s$ of the box to reach the surface of the water is -2.2 m. The vertical component of the launch velocity $u$:\n\n        - $u=8.9\\sin\\theta$\n\n        By using uniform acceleration  equations, the following relationship can be written:\n\n        $\\begin{aligned}\n        s\\ &=ut+\\frac{1}{2}at^2\\\\\n        -2.2\\ &=\\left(8.9\\sin\\theta\\right)\\left(1.1\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\left(-9.81\\right)\\left(1.1\\right)^2\\\\\n        5.9-2.2\\ &=\\left(8.9\\sin\\theta\\right)\\left(1.1\\right)\\\\\n        \\frac{3.7}{\\left(8.9\\right)\\left(1.1\\right)}&=\\sin\\theta \\\\\n        0.38\\ &=\\sin\\theta\\\\\n        22.3\\degree &=\\theta\n        \\end{aligned}$\n\n    3. Horizontal component of the launch velocity = $8.9 \\cos22=8.3$ m s$^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $d=\\left(8.3\\right)\\left(1.1\\right)$ = :answer[$9.1$ m] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The horizontal component of the launch velocity $u_h$;\n\n        - $u_h=8.9 \\cos22$\n\n        - $u_h=8.3$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n        Since the horizontal speed is constant, the distance travelled $d$;\n\n        - $d=ut$\n\n        - $d=\\left(8.3\\right)\\left(1.1\\right)=9.1$ m"},{"ref":"PH0634","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0634\n\nA trapeze artist is swinging from position **A** to position **C** as shown in the diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/PH0634a.png\" alt=\"PH0634a\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Image created using Images by OpenClipart-Vectors from Pixabay - https ://pixabay.com]}}$\n\nThe combined mass of the artist and the swing is 70 kg, the length of the rope of the swing is $L$ and the tension in the rope at position **B** is $T$.\n\nWhich expression gives the kinetic energy at position **B**?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em} \\ \\dfrac{L\\left(T-700\\right)}{2}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em} \\ \\dfrac{LT}{2}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em} \\ LT$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em} \\ \\dfrac{L\\left(T+700\\right)}{2}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAt position **B**, at the bottom of the circular path, the net force is\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $\\sum F=T-mg=\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}$\n\nThen, the tension of the rope\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $T=\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}+mg$\n\nand\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $mg=700 \\ N$\n\nThe kinetic energy at position **B**\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $E_k=\\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2$\n\nRearranging the formula giving the tension we can find an expression for $v^2$:\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $v^2=\\dfrac{r\\left(T-mg\\right)}{m}$\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $v^2=\\dfrac{L\\left(T-700\\right)}{m}$\n\nThen\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $E_k=\\dfrac{1}{2}m\\left(\\dfrac{L\\left(T-700\\right)}{m}\\right)=\\dfrac{L\\left(T-700\\right)}{2}$"},{"ref":"PH0639","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0639\n\nA roller coaster goes around the inside of a loop of radius $R$, shown in the diagram. The combined mass of a car and the passengers is $M$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-07-08%20at%205.33.30%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-08 at 5.33.30 PM\">\n\nWhen the car is at its highest point, the normal reaction force of the track on the car is equal to half of the weight of the car and passengers.\n\nWhich expression gives the kinetic energy at the top of the loop?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{RMg}{4}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{3RMg}{4}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $RMg$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\\\nAt the top of the loop, the centripetal force is the vector sum of the combined weight of the car and the passengers, and the normal force of the track on the car.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-07-08%20at%205.35.25%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-08 at 5.35.25 PM\">\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $\\dfrac{Mv^2}{R}=Mg+0.5Mg=\\dfrac{3}{2}Mg$\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $Mv^2=\\dfrac{3}{2}RMg$\n\nSince the kinetic energy\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $E_k=\\dfrac{1}{2}Mv^2$\n\nThen\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $E_k=\\dfrac{\\frac{3}{2}RMg}{2}=\\dfrac{3RMg}{4}$"},{"ref":"PH0643","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0643\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\n1.\n    A ball of mass $0.20$ kg moving on a frictionless horizontal plane collides elastically with a vertical wall.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-06-30%20at%205.32.51%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-30 at 5.32.51 PM\">\n\n    The variation of the force $F$ acting on the ball during the collision with time $t$ is shown on the graph below.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-06-30%20at%205.13.31%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-30 at 5.13.31 PM\">\n\n    The speed of the ball just before the impact is $5.0$ m s$^{-1}$. Calculate the maximum force exerted on the wall by the ball. [3 marks]\n\n2. The wall is covered with a coating that causes an inelastic collision.  The ball hits the wall with the same initial speed and bounces back with a speed of $3.0$ m s$^{-1}$.\n\n    1. Explain, by considering the moments just before and after the collision, how the principle of conservation of energy applies to the collision. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Calculate the loss of the energy of the ball during the collision. [2 marks]\n\n    3. During this collision, the ball undergoes a change in momentum. Discuss whether the the law of conservation of momentum applies in this situation. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Change in momentum = $\\left(0.20\\right)\\left(5.0\\right)-\\left(0.20\\right)\\left(-5.0\\right)=2.0$ kg m s$^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    The area under the graph=change in momentum  $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\hspace{2em}$ $F_{max}=\\dfrac{(2.0)(2)}{(10\\times{10}^{-3})}$ = :answer[$400$ N] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The area under the graph gives the change in momentum $\\Delta p$;\n\n    $\\hspace{2em}$ Area = $\\dfrac{F_{max}(10\\times{10}^{-3})}{2}=\\Delta p$\n\n    $\\hspace{2em}$ $\\Delta p=mv-mu$\n\n    The ball moves in the opposite direction after the collision\n\n    $\\hspace{2em}$ $\\Delta p=mv-mu$\n\n    $\\hspace{2em}$ $\\dfrac{F_{max}(10\\times{10}^{-3})}{2}=(0.20)(5.0)-(0.20)(-5.0)$\n\n    $\\hspace{2em}$ $\\dfrac{F_{max}(10\\times{10}^{-3})}{2}=2.0$\n\n    $\\hspace{2em}$ $F_{max}=\\dfrac{(2.0)(2)}{(10\\times{10}^{-3})}=400$ N\n\n2.\n    1. Loss of kinetic energy of the ball $\\checkmark$\\\nEquals to the increase in internal energy of the objects and surroundings $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The law of conservation of energy states that in any interaction, energy is not created or destroyed. In this situation, although the ball loses kinetic energy overall, this energy is transferred to thermal energy in the surroundings during the collision.\n\n    2. $\\hspace{2em}$ $E_{Ki}=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(0.2\\right)\\left(5.0\\right)^2=2.5$ J\n\n        and\n\n        $\\hspace{2em}$ $E_{Kf}=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(0.2\\right)\\left(3.0\\right)^2=0.9$ J $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\hspace{2em}$ $\\Delta E_K=2.5-0.9$ = :answer[$1.6$ J] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The kinetic energy of the ball $E_{K_i}$ before the collision:\n\n        $\\hspace{2em}$ $E_{K_i}=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(0.2\\right)\\left(5.0\\right)^2=2.5$ J\n\n        The kinetic energy of the ball $E_{K_f}$ after the collision:\n\n        $\\hspace{2em}$ $E_{K_f}=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(0.2\\right)\\left(3.0\\right)^2=0.9$ J\n\n        The kinetic energy lost $\\Delta E_{K}$:\n\n        $\\hspace{2em}$ $\\Delta E_{K}=2.5-0.9=1.6$ J\n\n    3. Law states that momentum of a closed system is constant in the absence of external forces. $\\checkmark$\n\n        Considering the ball only, the wall exerts an external force therefore its momentum changes.\n\n        **OR**\n\n        System incorporates the ball and the wall. The change of momentum of the ball is equal and opposite to the change in momentum of the wall/earth $\\checkmark$"},{"ref":"PH0644","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0644\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nAn elastic bungee cord of natural length $20$ m is being tested. A spherical object, originally at rest, is released from a platform. Air resistance and the mass of the cord are insignificant.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/PH0644-000.png\" alt=\"PH0644-000\">\n\n1. State the principle of conservation of energy. [2 marks]\n\n2. Describe the energy changes that take place in the spherical object from the time it is released until it comes to rest for the first time.    [2 marks]\n\n3. Show that the speed of the object just before the cord starts to extend is approximately $20$ m s$^{-1}$. [2 marks]\n\n4. The magnitude of the average $\\underline{\\textrm{net}}$ force acting on the object between the point where the cord starts to extend and the point where it reaches maximum extension is $36$ N. The time taken during the fall of the object between these points is $2.8$ s.\n\n    1. Show that the mass of the object is approximately $5.0$ kg. [2 marks]\n\n    2.     On the graph below, sketch how the the elastic potential energy $E_{H}$ of the spring changes with the vertical displacement $s$ as the cord stretches from the point $s=0$. There is no need to add values to the axes.  [2 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-06-02%20at%208.28.03%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-02 at 8.28.03 AM\">\n\n    3. The extension of the cord at the point in time when the object has zero acceleration is $11$ m. Determine the spring constant of the cord. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. (in any process) energy is neither created nor destroyed $\\checkmark$\n\n    It can only be transformed from one form into another. $\\checkmark$\n\n2. The gravitational potential energy of the object is converted to the kinetic energy of the object. $\\checkmark$\n\n    The kinetic and gravitational potential energy of the object are converted into elastic potential energy within the cord. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    When it is released, its gravitational potential energy is converted to kinetic energy due to the decrease in height above the ground. As soon as the centre of mass of the object has fallen by $20$ m, the cord starts to extend and store elastic potential energy. As it comes to rest, the object's kinetic and gravitational potential energy are converted to the elastic potential energy of the cord.\n\n3.  ::indent\n    - $mg \\Delta h=\\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $v=19.8\\ \\ce{ m s}^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The decrease in height of the object is $20$ m. Its decrease in gravitational potential energy equals the increase in kinetic energy.\n\n    - $mg\\Delta h= \\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2$\n\n    - $v=\\sqrt{2g\\Delta h}$\n\n    - $v=\\sqrt{(2)(9.8)(20)}$\n\n    - $v=19.8$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n    Alternatively,\n\n    - $v^2=u^2+2as$\n\n    - $v^2=0^2+2(-9.8)(-20)$\n\n    - $v=19.8$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n4. 1. ::indent\n        - $F_{avg}\\Delta t=\\Delta p$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $m = 5.04\\ \\ce{kg} \\ \\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The object is stationary and has zero momentum when the cord has maximum extension. By using impulse = change in momentum;\n\n        $\\begin{aligned}\n        F_{avg}\\Delta t&=\\Delta p\\\\\n        (36)(2.8)&=m(19.8)\\\\\n        100.8&=m(19.8)\\\\\n        m&=\\dfrac{100.8}{19.8}\\\\\n        m&=5.09\\textrm{ kg}\\\\\n        \\end{aligned}$\n\n    2.  $\\hspace{2em}$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-06-02%20at%208.32.17%E2%80%AF0AM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-02 at 8.32.17 0AM\">\n\n        Parabolic graph with positive gradient. $\\checkmark$\n\n        Passes through origin. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The elastic potential energy stored in the cord is proportional to the square of the extension. Since the force acting on the cord is downwards due to the weight of the object, the vertical displacement of the object s as the cord starts to extend equals the extension x of the cord.\n\n        - $s^2\\propto x^2\\propto E_{H}$\n\n        This relationship can be represented by a graph in the shape of\n\n        - $y=x^2$\n\n    3. Weight of the object = force of the spring on the object/$W=kx$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $k=\\dfrac{W}{x} = \\dfrac{49}{11}$\n\n        - $k=4.5\\ \\ce{ N m}^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The forces acting on the object are the weight of the object and the force of spring on the object. When the object has no acceleration, these two forces are balanced by each other. The weight of the object;\n\n        - $W=mg=\\left(5.0\\right)\\left(9.8\\right)=49$ N\n\n        By using Hooke's Law:\n\n        - $F=kx$\n\n        - $k=\\dfrac{F}{x}$\n\n        - $k=\\dfrac{49}{11}$\n\n        - $k=4.5$ N m$^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH0845","marks":"13","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0845\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\n1. In a game, players try to hit a moving cart by projecting a ball at an angle from a spring-loaded launcher.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/PH0845aaaa.png\" alt=\"PH0845aaaa\">\n\n    When the spring is fully uncompressed, it extends to the open end of the barrel. The frictional forces acting on the ball are negligible.\n\n    1. State the main energy changes taking place when the ball is moving inside the barrel. [2 marks]\n\n    2.     The following data is available:\n\n        Mass of the ball: $0.065$ kg\n\n        The compression of the spring before release: $0.22$ m\n\n        The spring constant: $10$ N m$^{-1}$\n\n        Show that the launch speed of the ball at the end of the barrel is approximately $2.3$ m s$^{-1}$. [3 marks]\n\n2. When launching the ball, the spring applies a force for $0.022$ s.  Determine the average net force acting on the ball during this time interval. [2 marks]\n\n3. One competitor aligned the launcher at $30\\degree$ above the horizontal. At the instant the ball left the launcher, it was $1.25$ m directly above the front of a moving cart which was moving at a steady speed of $2.1$ m s$^{-1}$ directly away from the launcher.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/PH0845aaa1.png\" alt=\"PH0845aaa1\">\n\n    1. Show that the time taken for the ball to displace $1.25$ m vertically downward after leaving the barrel is around $0.64$ s. [3 marks]\n\n    2. The length of the cart is $35$ cm\n\n        Deduce whether or not the ball will hit the top of the cart. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. Elastic potential energy decreases $\\checkmark$\\\n    Gravitation potential energy and kinetic energy increase $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        The spring-ball system stores elastic potential energy. Upon releasing the ball, both its speed and altitude experience an increase. Consequently, this energy is converted into the ball's gravitational potential energy and kinetic energy.\n\n    2. $\\hspace{1.8em}$ $\\Delta h = \\left ( \\Delta x \\right ) \\left(sin30 \\right) = \\dfrac{0.22}{2} =$ :0.11 m $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{(10)(0.22^2)}{2} = \\dfrac{0.065(v)^2}{2} + (0.065)(9.8)(0.11)$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $v = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{(10)(0.22^2) - (2)(0.065)(9.8)(0.11)}{0.065}} = 2.3 \\ll \\ce{m s}^{-1} \\gg $\n\n        *Allow two marks if gravitational potential energy is not considered and the answer 2.7 is obtained.*\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        The increase in vertical position $\\Delta h$ of the ball during the launch:\n\n        - $\\Delta h = (\\Delta x)(\\sin30) = \\dfrac{0.22}{2} = 0.11$ m\n\n        During the launch, the elastic potential energy lost is equal to the gains in gravitational potential energy and kinetic energy:\n\n        - $\\Delta E_{H} = \\Delta E_k + \\Delta E_p$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{k \\Delta x^2}{2} = \\dfrac{mv^2}{2} + mg \\dfrac{\\Delta x}{2}$\n\n        By substituting given values:\n\n        - $\\dfrac{(10)(0.22^2)}{2} = \\dfrac{0.065(v)^2}{2} + (0.065)(9.8)(0.11)$\n\n        - $v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{(10)(0.22^2) - (2)(0.065)(9.8)(0.11)}{0.065}}=2.3$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n2. The magnitude of the change in momentum of the ball $= m \\Delta v= 0.150$ kg m s$^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $\\dfrac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t}=\\dfrac{0.15}{0.022}=$ :answer[$6.8$ N] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    The change in momentum $\\Delta p$ of the ball:\n\n    - $\\Delta p = mv = (0.065)(2.3)=0.150$ kg m s$^{-1}$\n\n    Using impulse, we can find the average force $F$ acting on the ball during the interval:\n\n    - $F = \\dfrac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t} = \\dfrac{0.150}{0.022} = 6.79$ N\n\n3.\n    1. The magnitude of the vertical component of the initial velocity $= 1.15$ m s$^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Vertical velocity at the level of the cart $= 5.1$ m s$^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $t=\\dfrac{v-u}{a}=\\dfrac{(-5.1-1.15)}{-9.81}=$ :answer[$0.64$ s] $\\checkmark$\n\n        *OR*\n\n        - $-1.25=1.15t + \\dfrac{1}{2}(-9.81)t^2=$ :answer[$0.64$ s] $\\checkmark$\n\n        *Accept alternative usage of uniform acceleration equations*\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        The magnitude of the vertical component of the initial velocity $v_{0v}$:\n\n        - $v_{0v} = 2.3\\ \\sin30 = 1.15$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n        Using the uniform acceleration equations in multiple steps:\n\n        The velocity $v_f$ of the ball when it is displaced at $1.25$ m downwards.\n\n        - $v_f ^2 = v_i ^2 + 2as$\n\n        - $v_f ^2 = 1.15^2 + (2)(-9.81)(-1.25) = -5.1$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n        - $v_f = -5.1$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n        The time $t$ of the motion:\n\n        - $v = u + at$\n\n        - $t = \\dfrac{v - u}{a} = \\dfrac{-5.1 - 1.15}{-9.81} = 0.64$ s\n\n        Alternative method:\n\n        - $s = v_{0v}t + \\dfrac{1}{2}at^2$\n\n        where $s$ is $-1.25$ m, $a$ is $-9.81$ m s$^{-2}$ and $v_{0v}$ is $1.15$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n        - $-1.25 = 1.15t + \\dfrac{1}{2}(-9.81)t^2$\n\n        Solving using the quadratic formula gives the positive root\n\n        - $t = 0.64$ s\n\n    2. Horizontal displacement of the ball = $v_h t=(2.3\\cos(30\\degree))(0.64)=(1.99)(0.64) =1.27$ m $\\checkmark$\n\n        Front of the cart moves $s = vt = (2.1)(0.64) =1.35$ m $\\checkmark$\n\n        The front of the cart moves $0.07$ m further than the ball and the cart is $35$ cm long, so the ball hits the cart $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        The magnitude of the horizontal component of the initial velocity $v_{0_h}$:\n\n        - $v_{0_h}=2.3\\cos30=1.99$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n        The horizontal displacement $s_h$ of the ball as it displaced $1.25$ m vertically downward:\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $s_h = v_{0_h}t = (1.99)(0.64) = 1.28$ m\n\n        The horizontal displacement $s_{hc}$ of any part of the cart:\n\n        - $s_{h_c} = vt =(2.1)(0.64) = 1.35$ m\n\n        Comparing the numbers and knowing that the cart is $0.35$ m long, the ball will hit the cart, $1.35 - 1.28 = 0.07$ m behind the front of the cart."},{"ref":"PH0854","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0854\n\nA spring that obeys Hooke's Law is stretched a distance $x$ and stores energy $E$.\n\nWhat is the work done by the spring when it returns to the point where $x$ is reduced to half of its original value?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{E}{4}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{E}{2}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{3E}{4}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2E$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe energy of a stretched spring is given by\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $E=\\dfrac{1}{2}kx^2$\n\nWhen the stretched length is halved, the final energy is\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $E_f=\\dfrac{1}{2}k\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)^2=\\dfrac{1}{4} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} kx^2 \\right) = \\dfrac{1}{4}E$\n\nThe work done is given by\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $W=\\Delta E$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $W=E - \\dfrac{1}{4}E=\\dfrac{3E}{4}$"},{"ref":"PH0855","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0855\n\nA tennis ball with mass $60$ g travels horizontally with constant speed $u$. The ball hits a stationary racket and rebounds in the opposite direction with a horizontal speed $v$. The time for the collision is $5.0$ ms.\n\nWhat is the magnitude of the average force on the ball?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $0.06(u-v)$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $0.06(u+v)$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $12(u-v)$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $12(u+v)$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $F\\Delta t=\\Delta p$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $F=\\dfrac{m(\\Delta v)}{\\Delta t} = \\dfrac{(60 \\times 10^{-3})\\left ((-v)-u \\right )}{5 \\times 10^{-3}}=-12(u+v)$\n\nThe magnitude of the force is\n$F=12(u+v)$\n\nNote that because there is a change in direction and the velocities are treated as vectors, we add the magnitudes of the starting and finishing velocities to find the change in velocity."},{"ref":"PH0892","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0892\n\nBelow shows a Sankey diagram for an LED.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-07-02%20at%202.58.46%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-02 at 2.58.46 PM\">\n\nWhich of the following shows the percentage efficiency of the LED and the total power input?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Percentage efficiency | Total power input |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $40\\%$ | $4$ W |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $40\\%$ | $20$ W |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $60\\%$ | $4$ W |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $60\\%$ | $20$ W |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nTotal power input = total power output = $12\\ +\\ 8\\ =\\ 20$ W\n\nFor an LED useful energy is visible light energy:\n\nPercentage efficiency = $\\dfrac{\\textrm{ Useful energy output}}{\\textrm{ Total energy input}}\\ (100\\%)\\ =\\ \\dfrac{8}{20}\\ (100\\%)\\ =\\ 40\\%$"},{"ref":"PH0933","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0933\n\nA mass of $m$ kg is hung on the end of the spring. When the system is in equilibrium, the elastic potential energy stored in the spring is $E$.\n\nA downward force is then applied to the system causing the total elastic potential energy stored to double. This is shown in the diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-06-02%20at%2010.50.30%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-02 at 10.50.30 AM\">\n\nThe downward force is then removed, and the system oscillates about the equilibrium position.\n\nWhat is the magnitude of the downward force and the kinetic energy of the mass as it passes the equilibrium position?\n\nYou may assume friction and air resistance are negligible.\n\n| | Magnitude of downward force | Kinetic energy of mass as it passes equilibrium |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| ($\\sqrt {2}$ - 1)$mg$ | $E$ |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | ($\\sqrt {2}$ - 1)$mg$ | (3 $-$ 2 $\\sqrt {2}) E$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\sqrt {2}\\,mg$ | $E$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\sqrt {2}\\,mg$ | (3 $-$ 2 $\\sqrt {2}) E$ |","markScheme":"**答案：B**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nLet\n\n- $E = \\dfrac {1}{2} kx{^2}$\n\nWhere $k$ is the spring constant and $x$ is that extension of the spring when it is in the equilibrium position. (This is the length of the spring with mass $m$ attached - natural length of the spring).\n\nLet $W = mg$ be the (weight) force of the mass hanging on the spring.\n\n- $W = kx$\n\nSubstituting $x = \\dfrac {W}{k}$ into $E = \\dfrac {1}{2} kx{^2}$ gives\n\n- $E = \\dfrac {W{^2}}{2k}$\n\n$k$ is constant so we may say that $E$ $\\alpha$ $W{^2}$\n\n- $\\dfrac {E}{W{^2}}$ = constant\n\nSo, we may write\n\n- $\\dfrac {W{^2}}{E} = \\dfrac{(F_{t})^2}{2E}$ \n\nwhere $F_{t}$ is the total force acting on the spring when it is being pulled down.\n\n- $W{^2} = \\dfrac{(F_{t})^2}{2}$\n\n- $2W{^2} = {(F_{t})^2}$ \n\n- $\\sqrt {2} W = F_{t}$\n\nFinding the extra force gives\n\n- $F_{extra}=F_{t} - W = \\sqrt {2} W - W = (\\sqrt {2} - 1) W = (\\sqrt {2} - 1)mg$\n\nFor kinetic energy:\n\n$E_{k}$ is not equal to $2E - E = E$ because $GPE$ is involved as well as $E_{k}$ and $EPE$.\n\nThe simplest way to treat this scenario is to consider the ‘extra force’, $F_{extra}$ and ‘extra extension from equilibrium’, $x_{extra}$.\n\n- $F_{extra}$ = $kx_{extra}$\n\nAnd $k = \\dfrac{mg}{x}$\n\nSo\n\n- $(\\sqrt {2} - 1)mg = \\dfrac{mg}{x}(x_{extra})$\n\n- $(\\sqrt {2} - 1) = \\dfrac{1}{x} (x_{extra})$\n\n- $(x_{extra})= (\\sqrt {2} - 1)x$\n\n- $E_{k} = \\dfrac{1}{2}k((\\sqrt {2} - 1)x){^2}$\n\n- $E_{k} = \\dfrac{1}{2}kx{^2}((\\sqrt {2} - 1)){^2}$\n\n- $E_{k} = E ((\\sqrt {2}){^2} - \\sqrt {2} - \\sqrt {2} + 1)$\n\n- $E_{k} = (3 - 2 \\sqrt {2})E$\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Further intuition}}$:\n\nIt may seem strange that $E_{k} = \\dfrac{1}{2}k(x_{extra})^2$ because:\n\n- $1)$ $EPE$ $\\not= E_{k}$ because $GPE$ is also involved\n\n- $2)$ $A$ $\\propto$ $B{^2}$ does not imply that (_Extra A_) $\\propto$ (_Extra B_)${^2}$\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$For example: If $A = B{^2}$, $1 = 1{^2}$ and $4 = 2{^2}$ but $(4 - 1) \\not= (2 - 1){^2}$\n\nBut it turns out that these facts taken together produce the result that $E_{k} = \\dfrac{1}{2}k(x_{extra})^2$ . Here is proof:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-06-02%20at%2010.49.31%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-02 at 10.49.31 AM\">\n\nIf the spring is in position 3 and the extra force is released, the mass will move upwards (past position 2) and oscillate in simple harmonic motion with position 2 as the equilibrium.\n\nLet us take $GPE$ to be zero in position 3.\n\nTotal energy in position $3 = \\dfrac{1}{2}k(x + y){^2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}k(x{^2} + 2xy + y{^2})$\n\nTotal energy in position $2 = E_{k} + mgy + \\dfrac{1}{2}kx{^2}$\n\nEnergy is conserved so $E_{k} + mgy + \\dfrac{1}{2}kx{^2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}k(x{^2} + 2xy + y{^2})$\n\n- $E_{k} + mgy + \\dfrac{1}{2}kx{^2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}kx{^2} + kxy+ \\dfrac{1}{2}ky{^2}$\n\n- $E_{k} + mgy = kxy + \\dfrac{1}{2}ky{^2}$\n\nNow $k = mgx$ so\n\n- $E_{k} + mgy = (\\dfrac{mg}{x})xy + \\dfrac{1}{2}ky{^2}$\n\n- $E_{k} + mgy = mgy + \\dfrac{1}{2}ky{^2}$\n\n- $E_{k} = \\dfrac{1}{2}ky{^2}$\n\n- $E_{k} = \\dfrac{1}{2}k(x_{extra})^2$"},{"ref":"PH0934","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0934\n\nA boat is traveling along a straight line for a total time of one hour. The boat travels at a constant speed and covers a total distance of 2.4 km. The thrust of the boat's engine is 1.8 kN.\n\nWhat is the energy exerted by the boat's engine in one second?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em} $ 0.67 kJ\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em} $ 1.2 kJ\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em} $ 4.3 kJ\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em} $ 78 kJ","markScheme":"**答案：B**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n- $P = \\dfrac{E}{t}$\n\nso energy exerted in one second is the same as power.\n\n- $P = Fv$\n\n- $v = 2.4$ km h$^{-1} = 2.4(1000$ m)(3600 s)$^{-1}$ = $\\dfrac{2400\\textrm{ m}}{3600\\textrm{ s}} = \\dfrac{2}{3}$  m s$^{-1}$\n\n- $P = (1.8$ kN)$\\bigg(\\dfrac{2}{3}$ m s$^{-1}\\bigg)$ = 1.2 kW\n\nTherefore the energy exerted in one second is 1.2 kJ."},{"ref":"PH0970","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0970\n\nThe engine of a car exerts a constant driving force as the car accelerates from rest along a straight horizontal road. Friction and air resistance are negligible. Which of the following graphs shows how the power of the engine $P$ varies with distance traveled by the car $s$?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n|| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-06-02%20at%2011.25.18%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-02 at 11.25.18 AM\">  |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-06-02%20at%2011.25.23%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-02 at 11.25.23 AM\">|\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-06-02%20at%2011.25.28%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-02 at 11.25.28 AM\"> |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-06-02%20at%2011.25.32%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-02 at 11.25.32 AM\">|","markScheme":"**答案：A**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n- $P=Fv$\n\nForce is constant so $P\\propto v$.\n\nConstant force implies constant acceleration. So, we may use the equations for constant acceleration (suvat).\n\n- $v^2=u^2+2as$\n\nThe car starts from rest so\n\n- $v^2=2as$\n\nGiven that $a$ is constant we can say\n\n- $v^2 \\propto s$\n\n- $v \\propto \\sqrt{s}$\n\nSo as $s$ increases $v$ increases at a decreasing rate.\n\n- $P \\propto v \\propto \\sqrt{s}$\n\nTherefore\n\n- $P \\propto \\sqrt{s}$\n\nSo as $s$ increases $P$ increases at a decreasing rate and the graph will take the shape of a square-root curve."},{"ref":"PH0971","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0971\n\nAn object of mass $2$ kg with initial kinetic energy $10$ J comes to rest as it travels a distance of $3$ m along a flat horizontal rough surface as shown in the diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-04-07%20at%2011.38.35%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-07 at 11.38.35 AM\">\n\nWhat is the coefficient of dynamic friction between the object and the surface?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{1}{6}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{1}{3}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{1}{2}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{2}{3}$","markScheme":"**答案：A**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\n\nKinetic energy is transferred to work done (by friction)\n\n- $\\Delta E_K = F_f s = (\\mu F_N)s =(\\mu_dmg)s$\n\nSubstituting the given values leads to\n\n- $10 = \\mu_d(2)(10)(3)$\n\n- $1 = \\mu_d(2)(3)$\n\n- $\\mu_d = \\dfrac{1}{6}$"},{"ref":"PH0972","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0972\n\nA bullet of mass $m$ is fired horizontally at a speed $v$ into a block of mass $M$. The bullet remains inside the block upon collision. The block is connected to a light inextensible string so that the collision of the bullet causes the block to move from position 1 to a maximum height at position 2 as shown in the diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\nIf air resistance is negligible, which of the following expressions gives the value of $h$, the vertical distance moved by the center of mass?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{mv^2}{2gM}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{m^2v^2}{2gM^2}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{mv^2}{2g(m+M)}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{m^2v^2}{2g(m+M)^2}$","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nWork is done as the bullet travels through the block and comes to a stop. So, this is not an elastic collision - (translational) kinetic energy is not conserved.\n\nWe must use conservation of momentum to determine the velocity and from that the kinetic energy of the block and bullet after the collision.\n\n- $mv=(M+m)v'$\n\nWhere $v'$ is the velocity of the block and bullet immediately after the collision.\n\n- $v'=\\dfrac{mv}{m+M}$\n\n- $E_{K_{after}} = \\dfrac{1}{2} (m+M) v'^2 = \\dfrac{1}{2} (m+M) \\Bigl(\\dfrac{mv}{m+M}\\Bigr)^2$\n\nKinetic energy of block and bullet is transferred to gravitational potential energy. $h$ is the maximum height reached by the block + bullet so we may say\n\n- $\\dfrac{1}{2} (m+M) \\Bigl( \\dfrac{mv}{m+M} \\Bigr)^2=(m+M)gh $\n\nCanceling out a factor of $(m+M)$ on both sides\n\n- $\\dfrac{1}{2} \\Bigl(\\dfrac{mv}{m+M}\\Bigr)^2 = gh$\n\n- $\\dfrac{1}{2g} \\Bigl(\\dfrac{mv}{m+M}\\Bigr)^2 = h$\n\n- $h=\\dfrac{m^2v^2}{2g(m+M)^2}$"},{"ref":"PH1004","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1004\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nWhile practising, a table tennis player hits a ball of mass 2.8 g that collides with the floor and then bounces back from the wall. The speed of the ball just before reaching the floor is 8.0 m s$^{-1}$. The ball leaves the floor with an angle of 65$\\degree$ to the floor as shown.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-06-30%20at%205.39.45%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-30 at 5.39.45 PM\">\n\n1. Due to the collision with the floor, the ball loses\n30$\\%$ of its initial kinetic energy. Show that the leaving speed of the ball from the floor is around 7 m s$^{-1}$. [2 marks]\n\n2. The ball strikes the wall just as it reaches the highest point of its motion. Determine the horizontal distance between the bounce point and the wall. [3 marks]\n\n3. The ball collides elastically with the wall. The time of contact during the collision is 0.040 s. Calculate the average horizontal force exerted by the wall on the ball during the collision.     [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. $\\textrm{Initial\\ Kinetic\\ Energy}=\\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2 =\\dfrac{1}{2}(2.8\\times{10}^{-3})({8)}^2=0.090$ J $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $v_f= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{(0.7)(E_k)(2)}{m}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{(0.7)(.090\\ )(2)}{(2.8\\times{10}^{-3})}}=6.7\\ \\ce{m s}^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    The kinetic energy $E_{ki}$ just before the collision; $E_{ki}=\\dfrac{1}{2}mu^2$  where $u$ is the speed of the ball just before the collision\n\n    - $E_{ki}=\\dfrac{1}{2}(2.8\\times{10}^{-3})({8)}^2=0.090$ J\n\n    Since 30$\\%$ of the $E_{ki}$ is lost, the final kinetic energy $E_{Kf}$ is\n\n    - $E_{kf}=\\left(0.7\\right)\\left(0.090\\right)=0.063$ J\n\n    - $E_{kf}=\\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2$,  where $v$ is the speed of the ball just after the collision\n\n    - $0.063=\\dfrac{1}{2}(2.8\\times{10}^{-3})({v)}^2$\n\n    - $v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{(0.063)(2)}{(2.8\\times{10}^{-3})}}=6.7$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n2. Vertically,\n\n    - $u_y = 6.7\\sin(65\\degree)$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $t\\ =\\ \\dfrac{v-u}{a}\\ =\\ \\dfrac{0\\ -\\ 6.7\\sin({65\\degree})}{9.8}\\ = \\ 0.62$ s $\\checkmark$\n\n    Horizontally,\n\n    - $s=vt=6.7\\cos({65\\degree})\\left(0.62\\right)=1.8\\ \\ce{m} \\ \\checkmark$ (note that use of 7.0 m s$^{-1}$ gives an answer of 1.9 m)\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    The ball undergoes projectile motion. The collision with the wall takes place when the ball is at the highest part of its motion, so the vertical component of the motion can be used to find the time of flight.\n\n    - $u_y = 6.7\\sin(65\\degree)$ and $y = 0$\n\n    - $t\\ =\\ \\dfrac{v-u}{a}\\ =\\ \\dfrac{0-6.7\\sin\\left({65\\degree}\\right)}{9.8}\\  =\\ 0.62$ s\n\n    Using this time in the horizontal direction, where $u_x = 6.7\\cos(65\\degree)$\n\n    - $s=vt=6.7\\cos\\left({65\\degree}\\right)\\left(0.62\\right)=1.8$ m\n\n3. $\\Delta p = 2mv = 2(0.0028)(6.7\\cos(65\\degree)) =0.016$ N s $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $F\\ =\\dfrac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t}= \\dfrac{0.016}{0.040}= 0.40\\ \\ce{N} \\ \\checkmark$ (note that use of 7.0 m s$^{-1}$ gives an answer of 0.41 N)\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    As the ball rebounds elastically, the final speed is equal to the initial speed.\n\n    These speeds are equal to the horizontal component of the velocity\n\n    - $v_x=6.7\\cos(65\\degree) =2.83$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n    To find change in momentum\n\n    $\\begin{aligned}\n    \\Delta p&=p_2-p_1 \\\\\n    &=mv_2-mv_1 \\\\\n    &=0.0028\\left(2.83-\\left(-2.83\\right)\\right)\n    =0.016\\textrm{ N s}\n    \\end{aligned}$\n\n    Note that because of the change in direction, the change in momentum is equal to twice the initial momentum.\n\n    To find force,\n\n    - $F\\ =\\ \\dfrac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t}=\\dfrac{0.016}{0.040}= 0.40$ N"},{"ref":"PH1168","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1168\n\nA mass, $m$, is attached to a spring (of negligible mass) of spring constant $k$ on a smooth horizontal surface. The mass is initially pulled a distance, $x$, from its equilibrium position on the surface and released. At the point where the mass has moved a distance $\\dfrac{x}{3}$ from its starting point, the speed of the mass is $v$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-06-03%20at%205.22.20%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-03 at 5.22.20 PM\">\n\nWhich expression is equal to $k$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{9mv^2}{5x^2}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{5x^2}{9mv^2}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{9mv^2}{8x^2}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{8x^2}{9mv^2}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nSince there is no energy loss due to friction or external forces, the mechanical energy of the system remains constant. After the release of the mass, some of the elastic potential energy of the system is transferred to the kinetic energy of the mass. The extension of the spring at the moment the mass covers the distance of $\\dfrac{x}{3}$:\n\n- $x-\\dfrac{x}{3}=\\dfrac{2x}{3}$\n\nThe energy elastic potential energy decreased $\\Delta E_H$ after release to the point where the mass covers the distance of $\\dfrac{x}{3}$:\n\n- $\\Delta E_H=\\dfrac{1}{2}kx^2-\\dfrac{1}{2}k\\Bigl(\\dfrac{2x}{3}\\Bigr)^2=\\dfrac{1}{2}k\\dfrac{5x^2}{9}$\n\nThe energy is transferred to kinetic energy $E_K$:\n\n- $E_K=\\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2=\\dfrac{1}{2}k\\dfrac{5x^2}{9}$\n\n- $k=\\dfrac{9mv^2}{5x^2}$"},{"ref":"PH1169","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1169\n\nEnergy density is defined as the amount of energy stored in a substance per unit volume. Amounts of substances A and B are employed to supply the same average power to a city over an identical time period. The densities of these substances are $\\rho$ and $2\\rho$, respectively, with their respective volumes being $2V$ and $V$. Consider the following statements about substance A:\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | A greater mass was used than substance B   |\n  | II. | It has a lower energy density than substance B |\n  | III. | It provided the same amount of energy as substance B |\n\n Which statements are correct for substance A and the amount of it used in the time period?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ III only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I and II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe mass of substances can be determined by multiplying their density and volume, ensuring their masses are equal. Since both substances provide the same average of power over the same time, they provide the same amount of energy. ($E=Pt$)\n\nEnergy density can be calculated by\n\n- Energy density = $\\dfrac{\\textrm{energy provided}}{\\textrm{volume}}$\n\nWhile both provide the same amount of energy, Substance B has a smaller volume compared to Substance A. Consequently, Substance A possesses a lower energy density."},{"ref":"PH1170","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1170\n\nA camping stove uses fuel for cooking food in a pot. The fuel has an energy density of $u\\,\\ce{J m}^{-3}$, and the volume of fuel consumed is $V$ m$^3$. The energy transferred from the fuel to the cooking pot is $E\\,$J.\n\nWhat is the efficiency of the cooking stove?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{EV}{u}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{u}{EV}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{E}{uV}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{uV}{E}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n- Energy density = $\\dfrac{\\textrm{energy provided}}{\\textrm{volume}}$\n\nThe energy $E_F$ provided by the fuel (total energy in):\n\n- $E_F = \\textrm{Energy density}\\times\\textrm{Volume}$\n\n- $E_F=uV$\n\nThe efficiency $\\eta$ of the stove:\n\n- $\\eta = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Useful energy out}}{\\textrm{Total energy in}}=\\dfrac{E}{uV}$"},{"ref":"PH1791","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1791\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nA spring of mass m is compressed against a block of mass $2m$ on a frictionless surface. The system is held stationary and the elastic potential energy stored in the spring is $E_H$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshot%202025-06-04%20at%209.31.08%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-04 at 9.31.08 AM\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with chemix - https://chemix.org/]}}$\n\nThe system is released and the block and spring move horizontally. All of the elastic potential energy of the spring is converted into kinetic energy of the system.\n\n1. 1.   Show that after the system is released, the speed of the block $v_b$ is given by the equation:\n\n        - $v_b=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{E_H}{3m}}$ [3 marks]\n\n    2. The following data are given:\n\n        - Mass of spring $m=120$ g\n\n        - Spring constant of spring $k=85$ N m$^{-1}$\n\n        - Initial compression of the spring $x=8.0$ cm\n\n        Calculate $v_b$. [2 marks]\n\n2. 1. The block moves onto a surface where the coefficient of dynamic friction between the block and the ground is $\\mu_d=0.07$. When the block is at point $A$ its velocity is $0.71$ m s$^{-1}$. The block comes at a complete stop at point $B$.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Space%2C%20Time%2C%20and%20Motion/Work%2C%20Energy%2C%20and%20Power/images/Screenshoot%202025-06-04%20at%209.29.42%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshoot 2025-06-04 at 9.29.42 AM\">\n\n        Determine the distance between $A$ and $B$. [3 marks]\n\n    2. It is observed that the force required to start the block moving again is greater than the force required to keep it moving at a constant speed. Explain this observation. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. 1. Conservation of energy:\n\n        - $E_H=\\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2_s+\\dfrac{1}{2}(2m)(v_b)^2$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Conservation of momentum:\n\n        - $2mv_b=mv_s$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Correct substitution and rearranging for $v_b$:\n\n        - $E_H=\\dfrac{1}{2}m(2v_b)^2+\\dfrac{1}{2}(2m)(v_b)^2$\n\n        - $E_H=\\dfrac{1}{2}m(6v_b^2)$\n\n        - $v_b=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{E_H}{3m}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Due to the conservation of energy, we have:\n\n        - $E_H=\\dfrac{1}{2}mv_s^2+\\dfrac{1}{2}(2m)(v_b)^2$\n\n        - $E_H=\\dfrac{1}{2}m(v_s^2+2v_b^2)$\n\n        The initial momentum of the system is zero. Due to the conservation of momentum, the magnitudes of the two momenta will be equal:\n\n        - $2mv_b=mv_s$\n\n        - $2v_b=v_s$\n\n        (working with (scalar) speeds $v_s$ and $v_b$ rather than (vector) velocities)\n\n        Combining these equations we have:\n\n        - $E_H=\\dfrac{1}{2}m((2v_b)^2+2v_b^2)$\n\n        - $E_H=\\dfrac{1}{2}m(6v_b^2)$\n\n        - $E_H=3m(v_b)^2$\n\n        - $(v_b)^2=\\dfrac{E_H}{3m}$\n\n        - $v_b=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{E_H}{3m}}$\n\n    2. ::indent\n        - $E_H=\\dfrac{1}{2}k(\\Delta x)^2=\\dfrac{1}{2}(85)(0.080)^2\\ll=0.272\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $v_b=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.272}{3(0.12)}}=$ :answer[0.87 m s$^{-1}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Elastic potential energy $E_H$ is given by:\n\n        - $E_H=\\dfrac{1}{2}k(\\Delta x)^2$\n\n        Where $k$ is the spring constant and $x$ is the extension or compression of the spring.\n\n        Substituting in our values gives:\n\n        - $E_H=\\dfrac{1}{2}k(\\Delta x)^2=\\dfrac{1}{2}(85)(0.08)^2=0.272$ J\n\n        Now substituting these values into the equation given in part (a)(i) we have:\n\n        - $v_b=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.272}{3(0.12)}}=0.87$ m s$^{-1}$\n\n2. 1. ::indent\n        - $a=\\ll-\\gg\\dfrac{\\mu_dmg}{m}=\\ll-\\gg(0.07)(9.81)=\\ll-\\gg0.687$ m s$^{-2}$ (3sf) $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $s=\\dfrac{v^2-u^2}{2a}=\\dfrac{0^2-0.71^2}{2(-0.687)}=0.37$ m $\\checkmark$\n\n        - **OR**\n\n        - $\\mu_dmgs=\\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $s=\\dfrac{0.71^2}{2(0.687)}=$ :answer[$0.37$ m] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The net force acting on the block is equal to the frictional force given by:\n\n        - $F_f=\\mu_dF_N$\n\n        Where $\\mu_d$ is the coefficient of dynamic friction and $F_N$ is the normal reaction force.\n\n        The net vertical force is zero so the normal reaction force acting on the block is equal to its weight:\n\n        - $F_N=mg$\n\n        - $F_f=\\mu_dmg$\n\n        Net force:\n\n        - $F_{net}=ma$\n\n        Where $m$ is the mass of the block and $a$ is its acceleration. The net force on the block is negative as it acts in the opposite direction to the block's velocity:\n\n        - $ma=-\\mu_dmg$\n\n        - $a=-\\mu_dg$\n\n        - $a=-(0.07)(9.81)=-0.6867$ m s$^{-2}$\n\n        Now for constant acceleration we have:\n\n        - $v^2=u^2+2as$\n\n        Where $v$ is final velocity, $u$ is initial velocity, $a$ is acceleration and $s$ is displacement. Rearranging for $s$ and substituting our values we have:\n\n        - $v^2-u^2=2as$\n\n        - $s=\\dfrac{v^2-u^2}{2a}=\\dfrac{0^2-0.71^2}{2(-0.687)}=0.37$ m\n\n        Alternatively, we can use conservation of energy. The block loses all its kinetic energy due to work done by friction.\n\n        Work done by friction is given by:\n\n        - $W = Fs$\n\n        And the force of friction can be found from:\n\n        - $F_f=\\mu_dF_N$\n\n        - $F_N=mg$\n\n        - $F_f=\\mu_dmg$\n\n        Equating the change in kinetic energy to the work done by friction gives:\n\n        - $\\mu_dmgs=\\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2$\n\n        Solving for $s$ leads to the same equation as the other solution:\n\n        - $s=\\dfrac{v^2-u^2}{2a}=\\dfrac{0^2-0.71^2}{2(-0.687)}=0.37$ m\n\n    2. coefficient of static friction/ maximum static frictional force is greater than the coefficient of dynamic friction/dynamic frictional force. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        When an object is already in motion, the dynamic frictional force opposes its motion and remains constant. However, when the object is at rest, the static frictional force prevents it from moving.\n\n        Static friction can take any value up to its maximum limit, which is given by:\n\n        - $F_f=\\mu_sF_N$\n\n        where $\\mu_s$ is the coefficient of static friction and $F_N$ is the normal reaction force.\n\n        The coefficient of static friction is greater than the coefficient of dynamic friction, so the maximum static frictional force is also greater than the dynamic frictional force. This means that more force is required to start an object moving from rest than is required to keep it moving at a constant speed against a dynamic frictional force."},{"ref":"PH1910","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1910\n\nA motor with a power output of 6.0 W is used to lift a 2.0 kg object vertically at constant speed for 10 seconds. What is the best estimate, in metres, for the maximum vertical height the object can be raised in this time?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.3\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 3\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 10","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe power output is equal to work done per unit time:\n\n- $P=\\dfrac{\\Delta W}{\\Delta t}$\n\nThe work done lifting an object of mass\n$m$ by a height $\\Delta h$ is given by:\n\n- $E_p=mg\\Delta h$\n\nWhere $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity $\\approx10$ m s$^{-2}$\n\nCombining these equations and rearranging for height we have:\n\n- $P=\\dfrac{mg\\Delta h}{\\Delta t}$\n\n- $\\Delta h=\\dfrac{P\\Delta t}{mg}$\n\nSubstituting in our values we have:\n\n- $\\Delta h=\\dfrac{(6)(10)}{(2)(10)}=3$ m"}]}]},{"name":"The Particulate Nature of Matter","subtopics":[{"name":"Electric Circuits","questions":[{"ref":"PH0049","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0049\n\nA cell of emf $\\epsilon$ and internal resistance $r$ is connected to a variable external resistance of $R$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0049Q.png\" alt=\"PH0049Q\">\n\nWhich graph shows how $V$, the potential difference between $A$ and $B$, varies with external resistance $R$?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |   |B.   ||\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0049A.png\" alt=\"PH0049A\">  $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0049B.png\" alt=\"PH0049B\">|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0049C.png\" alt=\"PH0049C\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0049D.png\" alt=\"PH0049D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe voltage across $AB$ is also equal to the terminal voltage. For very low values of $R$, the terminal voltage will be zero. As $R$ increases and the current drops, the terminal voltage will approach the emf of the cell. Thus the correct graph will show voltage starting from zero and then leveling off to a fixed value.\n\nMathematically:\n\nTerminal voltage is given by the equation $V_T = \\varepsilon-Ir$, where $r$ is the internal resistance of the cell.\n\nThe current at any time is given by the formula $I=\\dfrac{\\varepsilon}{R+r}$\n\nCombining the equations gives $V_T = \\varepsilon-\\dfrac{\\varepsilon r}{R+r}$\n\nWhich factors to give $V_T = \\varepsilon \\left(1-\\dfrac{r}{R+r}\\right)$"},{"ref":"PH0050","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0050\n\nA circuit of a cell of electromotive force (emf) 18.0 V and negligible internal resistance is connected to three resistors each of resistance $6.0\\,\\Omega$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0050.png\" alt=\"PH0050\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra, - https://www.geogebra.org/m/JbAKa82Y}]}$\n\nThe ammeter is ideal. What is the reading of the ammeter?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 1.0 A\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 2.0 A\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 3.0 A\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 6.0 A","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n$R_{eq}=6+\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\dfrac{1}{6}+\\dfrac{1}{6}}\\right)=9\\,\\Omega$\n\nCurrent in the circuit, $I=\\dfrac{V}{R_{eq}}=\\dfrac{18}{9}=2$ A\n\nThe current will split evenly between the two resistors as their resistances are equal.\n\nTherefore the ammeter reads 1 A"},{"ref":"PH0104","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0104\n\nIn the circuit shown, a resistor of $5\\,\\Omega$ is connected to a long resistive wire with resistance of $15\\,\\Omega$ through a sliding contact that can move along the entire length of the wire. Both are connected to a power supply with 15 V  of emf and negligible internal resistance.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/Screenshot%202025-04-18%20at%2010.12.23%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-18 at 10.12.23 AM\">\n$\\small{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra, - https://www.geogebra.org/m/JbAKa82Y}]}$\n\nWhat is the maximum and minimum values of current through the power supply?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Maximum current / A | Minimum current / A |\n| :--   | :-: | :-: |\n| A.$\\hspace{1em}$ | $3.0$ | $0.75$ |\n| B. | $4.0$ | $0.75$ |\n| C. | $3.0$ | $1.0$ |\n| D. | $4.0$ | $1.0$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nWhen the sliding contact is placed at the end of the resistive wire, it is a parallel circuit.\n\n$\\dfrac{1}{R_T}=\\dfrac{1}{15}+\\dfrac{1}{5}= \\dfrac{4}{15}\\longrightarrow R_T = \\dfrac{15}{4} \\, \\Omega$\n\nHence by $V=IR$, the current through the power supply is 4.0 A.\n\nWhen the sliding contact is placed at the start of the resistive wire, the potential difference across the $5\\,\\Omega$ resistor is 0 V. Hence there is only current through $15\\,\\Omega$ resistive wire. Hence the current through the power supply is 1.0 A."},{"ref":"PH0106","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0106\n\nA cell is connected to a variable resistor. The terminal potential difference (p.d.) and current through the cell are measured and plotted on the graph shown. What is the internal resistance of the cell?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0106.png\" alt=\"PH0106\">\n\n$\\small{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra, - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ $5.0\\, \\Omega$\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ $4.0\\, \\Omega$\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ $3.0\\, \\Omega$\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ $2.0\\, \\Omega$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\nThe terminal voltage formula $\\varepsilon =I(R+r)$ can be rearranged by noticing that $IR$ is the terminal p.d. of a cell $V$.\n\nResulting in the equation $V=\\varepsilon -Ir$. Therefore, the negative of the gradient of the graph is the answer.\n\nGradient $\\,= \\dfrac{\\text{rise}}{\\text{run}}=\\dfrac{1.0-5.0}{2.0-0}=-2.0$\n\nBecause the internal resistance is the negative of the gradient, it must be equal to 2.0 $ \\Omega$."},{"ref":"PH0209","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0209\n\nA 6 V battery with an internal resistance of $2\\,\\Omega$ is connected to a $1\\,\\Omega$ external resistor. Which statement is correct when the $1\\,\\Omega$ resistor is replaced with a $2\\,\\Omega$ resistor?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The potential difference across the terminals of the battery is increased.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The current in the battery is increased.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The power dissipated in the battery is increased.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The total resistance of the circuit is decreased.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe total resistance of the circuit is increased from $3\\,\\Omega$ to $4\\,\\Omega$ due to the series connection of internal and external resistors. The current of the circuit is decreased with the increase of total resistance.\n\nThe power dissipated inside the battery;\n\n$P=i^2 r$\n\nThe dissipated power is decreased due to a decrease in current and constant internal resistance.\n\nOn the other hand, the potential difference across the terminal of the battery;\n\n$V=\\varepsilon - ir$\n\nThe emf of the battery ($\\varepsilon$) and internal resistance ($r$) is constant while the current is decreased. Therefore, potential difference ($V$) is increased. The answer is A."},{"ref":"PH0229","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0229\n\nIn the circuits below, all resistors have the same resistance, and the cells do not have internal resistance. The emf of the cell in circuit 1 is $E_1$ while it is $E_2$ in circuit 2.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0229.png\" alt=\"PH0229\">\n$\\small{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra, - https://www.geogebra.org/m/JbAKa82Y}]}$\n\nAll resistors dissipate the same power. Which of the following gives the ratio $\\dfrac{E_1}{E_2}$?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ $2$\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ $1$\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{1}{2}$\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{1}{4}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\\\nThe potential difference across each resistor in circuit 1 is $\\dfrac{E_1}{2}$ due to series connection while it is $E_2$ in circuit 2 due to parallel connection. The power dissipated in the resistors is equal, and for each resistor the dissipated power is given by\n\n- $P = \\dfrac{V^2}{R}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{(\\dfrac{E_1}{2})^2}{R} = \\dfrac{(E_2)^2}{R}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{(E_1)^2}{2^2} = \\dfrac{(E_2)^2}{1}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{(E_1)^2}{(E_2)^2} = \\dfrac{4}{1}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{E_1}{E_2} = \\dfrac{2}{1} = 2$"},{"ref":"PH0270","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0270\n\nThe following graph shows the relationship between current and potential difference across an unknown component in a series circuit.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0270.png\" alt=\"PH0270\">\n\nWhich of the following is true about the component?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ The component is not a resistor.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ The component is made of non-conducting material.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ The component is a non-ohmic resistor.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The component is an ohmic resistor.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ All electrical components are resistors to some extent. Even the best conductors will take up some amount of energy as electricity flows through them.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ While all materials are conducting given the appropriate voltage, at voltages as low as those shown on the graph, no current would flow if the material was not a good conductor of electricity.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ According to the graph, the ratio $V$/$I$ is not constant. By definition, that indicates a non-ohmic resistor. C is the correct answer.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ The voltage-current graph of an ohmic resistor is a straight line whose gradient represents a constant resistance."},{"ref":"PH0272","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0272\n\nThe diagram below shows a circuit connected to a 12 V battery with negligible internal resistance.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0272-3a.png\" alt=\"PH0272-3a\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra, - https://www.geogebra.org/m/JbAKa82Y}]}$\n\nWhat is the value of the current $I$ through the $2\\,\\mathit{\\ce{\\Omega}}$ resistor?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 1 A\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 2 A\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 3 A\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 4 A","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\\\nStart by calculating the combined resistance of the circuit. Since the $6\\,\\Omega$ and $2\\,\\Omega$ resistors are in parallel and the $1.5\\,\\Omega$ resistor is in series with both, we have $\\dfrac{1}{\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2}}+1.5\\,\\Omega = 3\\,\\Omega.$\n\n Now, using the definition of resistance, the current in the circuit is $V=(I)(R)$; $I=\\dfrac{12}{3}=4$ A. Current through parallel resistors distributes in the inverse ratio of the resistances, $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ of the current will flow through the $2\\,\\Omega$ resistor and $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ through the $6\\,\\Omega$ resistor. Therefore the correct answer is $3$ A (C)\n\nAlternatively, after calculating the resistance of the parallel combination as $1.5\\,\\Omega$, it can be seen that this is in series with the other $1.5\\,\\Omega$ resistor, which means they will share the total voltage equally, giving 6 V for the parallel combination.\n\nThen using Ohm's Law, the current $I=\\dfrac{6\\,V}{2\\,\\Omega} = 3$ A."},{"ref":"PH0289","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0289\n\nA battery of emf $\\varepsilon$ and an internal resistance $r$ is connected across a resistance $R$. The total power dissipated in the circuit is $P$.\n\nAn identical resistor and an identical battery are added in series to the circuit. What is the new total power dissipated in this circuit?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ $P$\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ $2P$\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ $4P$\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ $8P$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nUsing the equation\n\n- $P=\\dfrac{V^2}{R}$\n\nthe total power in the first circuit is\n\n- $P=\\dfrac{V^2}{R+r}$.\n\nWhen the new battery is added, the total emf becomes 2 V and the internal resistance becomes $2r,$ so the new power calculation is\n\n- $P_{\\text{new}}=\\dfrac{(2\\ce{V})^2}{2R+2r}=\\dfrac{4V^2}{2(R+r)}=2P_\\text{old}$."},{"ref":"PH0415","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0415\n\nAn electric circuit is connected as shown. An electric current of 2 A flows through the electric cell with an internal resistance $r$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0415.png\" alt=\"PH0415\">\n$\\small{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra, - https://www.geogebra.org/m/JbAKa82Y}]}$\n\nWhat is the value for $r$ ?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ $1\\, \\Omega$\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ $2\\, \\Omega$\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ $0.5\\, \\Omega$\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ $0.25\\, \\Omega$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe $26\\, \\Omega$ resistors are connected in parallel, their total resistance is,\n\n$\\dfrac{1}{R_{total}}=\\dfrac{1}{6}+\\dfrac{1}{6}=\\dfrac{2}{6}=\\dfrac{1}{3}$\n\n$R_{total}=3\\,\\Omega$\n\nThese 2 resistors are connected in series with the $2\\, \\Omega$ resistor,\n\n$R=3+2=5\\, \\Omega$\n\nThe emf $\\varepsilon$,\n\n$\\varepsilon=I(R+r)$\n\n$12=2(5+r)$\n\n$r=\\dfrac{12}{2}-5=6-5=1\\, \\Omega$"},{"ref":"PH0416","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0416\n\nAn electric circuit with emf $\\varepsilon$ and internal resistance $r$ is connected to a variable resistor $R$ which varies from $0\\, \\Omega$ to $6\\, \\Omega$.\n\nThe variation of the power dissipated in the resistor $R$ with the value of the resistance $R$ is shown in the graph.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0416.png\" alt=\"PH0416\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra, - https://www.geogebra.org/m/JbAKa82Y}]}$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\varepsilon$, if the maximum dissipated power is when $R$ is equal to the internal resistance of the battery $r$ ?\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ 9 V\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ 10 V\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ 12 V\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ 18 V","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe power dissipated in $R$,\n\n- $P=I^2R$\n\nThe emf $\\varepsilon$ of a cell with internal resistance $r$,\n\n- $\\varepsilon=I(R+r)$\n\n- $I=\\dfrac{\\varepsilon}{(R+r)}$\n\nSo,\n\n- $P=\\left(\\dfrac{\\varepsilon}{R+r}\\right)^2R$\n\n- $P=\\dfrac{\\varepsilon^2}{(R+r)^2}R$\n\nFrom the graph, the maximum power is 18 W at $R=2\\, \\Omega$.\n\nSince the maximum power is at $R=r$, then $r=2\\, \\Omega$\n\n- $18=\\left(\\dfrac{\\varepsilon^2}{(2+2)^2}\\right)(2)=\\left(\\dfrac{\\varepsilon^2}{8}\\right)$\n\n- $\\varepsilon=\\sqrt{(18)(8)}=\\sqrt{144}=12$ V"},{"ref":"PH0434","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0434\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nA cell with internal resistance $r$ and emf $\\varepsilon$ is connected to a variable resistor $R$, an ideal ammeter and an ideal voltmeter.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0434.png\" alt=\"PH0437\">\n[Source: Created with GeoGebra, by Tom Walsh - https://www.geogebra.org/m/JbAKa82Y]\n\n1. State what is meant by terminal voltage.[1 mark]\n\n2. Explain why the reading of the voltmeter is less than $\\varepsilon$. [2 marks]\n\nThe variation of the reading of the voltmeter with the current $I$ is shown in the graph.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0434-1.png\" alt=\"PH0437\">\n[Source: Created with GeoGebra,-  https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]\n\n3. Determine the emf $\\varepsilon$ and the internal resistance $r$ of the cell.     [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. The potential difference measured between the terminals of a battery (which has internal resistance).$\\checkmark$\n\n2. The cell has an internal resistance. $\\checkmark$\n\n    There is a potential difference across the internal resistance / in a real-life cell, energy is lost to the internal resistance and therefore the potential difference across the cell is not equal to the emf  $\\,\\checkmark$\n\n    OWTTE.\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$ \\\n    Current passes through the internal resistance of the cell\n\n    $I=\\dfrac{\\varepsilon}{R+r}$\n\n    And the potential difference across the internal resistance will be\n\n    $V=Ir$\n\n    So the emf of the battery $\\varepsilon$,\n\n    $ε=Ir+IR$\n\n    voltmeter reading = $IR=\\varepsilon-Ir$\n\n3.  $ \\varepsilon =3\\, \\ll \\ce{V} \\gg \\, \\checkmark$\n\n    $ r =\\, \\ll \\dfrac{3-0}{3-0} \\gg \\,=  1\\, \\ll \\Omega \\gg \\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$ \\\n    The emf $\\varepsilon$ of the cell is when there's no current in the circuit. At $I = 0$, the potential difference is 3 V which is the emf $\\varepsilon$.\n\n    The internal resistance is the negative gradient of the graph,\n\n    $r = -\\dfrac{0-3}{3-0} = 1 \\, \\Omega$"},{"ref":"PH0436","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0436\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nA cell with emf $\\varepsilon$ and internal resistance $r$ is connected to a variable resistor $R$, an ammeter and a voltmeter as shown.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0436.png\" alt=\"PH0436\">\n\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra by Tom Walsh- https://www.geogebra.org/m/JbAKa82Y]}}$\n\nBoth meters are ideal, and the variable resistor can vary from $0$ to $9\\,\\Omega$.\n\n1. Derive a relation for the power $P$ dissipated in the variable resistor in terms of the internal resistance $r$, emf $\\varepsilon$ and the value of the variable resistor $R$.  [2 marks]\n\nThe graph shows the variation of the power dissipated in the resistor $R$ with the value of the resistance $R$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0436-1.png\" alt=\"PH0436-1\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\nThe maximum dissipated power is when $R$ equals the internal resistance of the battery $r$.\n\n2. Determine the values of the internal resistance $r$ and the emf $\\varepsilon$ of the cell.  [3 marks]\n\n3.  1. Calculate the smallest possible reading on the ammeter.   [2 marks]\n\n    2. On the axes given, sketch the variation of the reading of the voltmeter with the reading of the ammeter for all values of the variable resistor $R$ and state the quantity represented by the gradient.   [2 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0436-2.png\" alt=\"PH0436-2\">\n    [Source: Created with GeoGebra, - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]","markScheme":"1. $P=I^2R$\n\n    $\\bold{OR}$\n\n    $P=\\dfrac{V^2}{R}$\n\n    $\\bold{OR}$\n\n    $P=VI \\,\\checkmark$\n\n    $P=\\dfrac{\\varepsilon^2}{(R+r)^2} R \\,\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$ \\\n    The power dissipated in $R$,\n\n    $P=I^2R$\n\n    The emf $\\varepsilon$ of a cell with internal resistance $r$,\n\n    $\\varepsilon = I(R+r)$\n\n    $I = \\dfrac{\\varepsilon}{R+r}$\n\n    So,\n\n    $P=\\left(\\dfrac{\\varepsilon}{R+r}\\right)^2 R$\n\n    $P = \\dfrac{\\varepsilon^2}{(R+r)^2}R$\n\n    $\\bold{OR}$\n\n    If we start with\n\n    $\\begin{aligned}\n    \\\\[-10pt]\nP &= \\dfrac{V^2}{R} \\\\[12pt]\nV &= \\varepsilon - Ir, \\text{ and } I = \\dfrac{\\varepsilon}{R+r} \\\\[12pt]\nV &= \\varepsilon - \\dfrac{\\varepsilon r}{(R+r)} = \\dfrac{\\varepsilon(R+r)-\\varepsilon r}{R+r} = \\dfrac{\\varepsilon R}{R+r} \\\\[14pt]\nV^2 &= \\dfrac{\\varepsilon^2 R^2}{(R+r)^2} \\\\[12pt]\nP &= \\dfrac{\\varepsilon^2}{(R+r)^2}R     \\\\[-10pt]\n\\end{aligned}$\n\n    $\\bold{OR}$\n\n    If we start with\n\n    $\\begin{aligned}\n    \\\\[-6pt]\n    P &= VI \\\\[10pt]\n    V &= \\varepsilon - \\dfrac{\\varepsilon r}{R+r}  = \\dfrac{\\varepsilon(R+r)-\\varepsilon r}{R+r} = \\dfrac{\\varepsilon R}{R+r} \\\\[14pt]\n    P&= \\left(\\dfrac{\\varepsilon R}{R+r}\\right)\\left(\\dfrac{\\varepsilon }{R+r}\\right) \\\\[12pt]\n    P &= \\dfrac{\\varepsilon^2}{(R+r)^2}R    \\\\[10pt]\n    \\end{aligned}$\n\n2. $r = 1\\,\\ll \\Omega\\gg \\, \\checkmark$\n\n    $\\varepsilon = (2)(1)\\left(\\sqrt{\\dfrac{25}{1}}\\right)\\,\\checkmark $\n\n    $\\varepsilon = 10 \\,\\ll \\ce{V}\\gg\\, \\checkmark$. (allow full marks for alternative solution leading to 10 V)\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$ \\\n    The maximum power is when the variable resistor $R$ is equal to the internal resistance of the cell $r$.\n\n    From the graph, the maximum power is 25 W at $R = 1\\,\\Omega$\n\n    The internal resistance $r$,\n\n    $r= 1\\,\\Omega$\n\n    Using the relation derived in the previous part,\n\n    $\\begin{aligned}\n    P &= \\dfrac{\\varepsilon^2}{(R+r)^2} R \\\\\n    25 &= \\left(\\dfrac{\\varepsilon^2}{(1+1)^2}\\right)(1) \\\\\n    \\varepsilon &= (2)(1)\\left(\\sqrt{\\dfrac{25}{1}}\\right) = 10\\ \\ce{V}\n    \\end{aligned}$\n\n3.  1. $I=\\dfrac{10}{1+9}\\,\\checkmark$\n\n        $I =  1 \\,\\ll \\ce{A}\\gg\\,\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$ \\\n        The smallest reading of the ammeter is at the maximum value of the variable resistor.\n\n        $\\begin{aligned}\n        \\varepsilon &= I(R+r)\\\\I &= \\dfrac{\\varepsilon}{(R+r)} \\\\\n        I &= \\dfrac{10}{9+1} = 1\\,\\ce{A}\n        \\end{aligned}$\n\n        Note that ECF marks will be awarded for the use of incorrect values for\nε and r obtained in part (b). A student with no response in part (b) could use self-declared values here and receive full marks for this question part.\n\n    2. A straight line with negative slope. $\\checkmark$\n\n        The gradient represents the (negative of the) internal resistance of the cell. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0436-3.png\" alt=\"PH0436-3\">\n        [Source: Created with GeoGebra, - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]\n\n        $V = \\varepsilon - I r$"},{"ref":"PH0438","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0438\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe graph shows the variation of current with voltage of a filament.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0438.png\" alt=\"PH0438\">\n[Source: Created with GeoGebra, - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]\n\nThis filament is used in a light bulb, and it is made of a long tungsten wire that is wound several times. The filament is heated up when current flows and produces light.\n\nThe following data are available for the filament.\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{The diameter of tungsten wire} &= 6.0\\,\\mu \\ce{m} \\\\\n\\text{The resistivity of tungsten when it produces light} &= 2.0\\,\\mu\\Omega \\,\\ce{cm}\n\\end{aligned}$\n\n1. State Ohm's law.  [1 mark]\n\n2. The filament's normal operation in a light bulb is at a potential difference of 6.0 V.\n\n    1. Show that the resistance of the filament at a potential difference of 6.0 V is $120\\,\\Omega$.  [1 mark]\n\n    2. Calculate the length of the filament.  [1 mark]\n\nThe light bulb is connected to a $100.0\\,\\Omega$ resistor and a cell with emf $\\varepsilon$ and negligible internal resistance.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/Ph0438-2.png\" alt=\"Ph0438-2\">\n[Source: Created with GeoGebra, by Tom Walsh - https://www.geogebra.org/m/JbAKa82Y]\n\n3. Calculate the required emf of the cell $\\varepsilon$ to operate the bulb at the normal conditions of the filament.   [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. The resistance of a conductor is constant / current proportional to potential difference if its temperature is constant. $\\checkmark$\n\n    (No mark is awarded for just “V = IR” or equivalent)\n\n2.  1. $R = $:answer[$\\dfrac{6}{50\\times 10^{-3}}\\,$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\ll R =120\\,\\Omega \\gg\\,$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$ \\\n        From the graph, when the potential difference is 6 V, the current is $50\\,\\ce{m} \\ce{A}$. So,\n\n        $R=\\dfrac{V}{I} \\rightarrow R=\\dfrac{6}{50\\times 10^{-3}}=120\\,\\Omega$\n\n    2. $L =  \\dfrac{(120)(\\pi)(3\\times 10^{-6})^2}{2\\times 10^{-8}} $\n\n        L = :answer[0.17 m ] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$\n\n        $\\begin{aligned}\n        R &= \\rho \\dfrac{L}{A} \\\\[9pt]\n        L &= \\dfrac{RA}{\\rho} = \\dfrac{(120)(\\pi)(3\\times 10^{-6})^2}{2\\times 10^{-8}}=0.17\\, \\ce{m}\n        \\end{aligned}$\n\n3. $\\varepsilon = (100)(50\\times10^{-3})+6\\,\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\varepsilon = $:answer[11 V] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$ \\\n    The emf of the cell is the sum of the voltages of the resistors and the light bulb.\n    For normal operation conditions of the filament mentioned in (b)(i), we can read off the graph that at 6V, the current is 50 mA.\n\n    The bulb and the resistor are connected in series, which means they have the same current.\n    So,\n\n    $\\varepsilon = (I)(R)+6 = (100)(50\\times10^{-3})+6 = 11$ V"},{"ref":"PH0439","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0439\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nA potentiometer with maximum resistance $R$ is connected to a $12\\,$V cell with negligible internal resistance, a $10\\,\\Omega$ resistor, an ideal voltmeter and an ideal ammeter as shown.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0439.png\" alt=\"PH0439\">\n[Source: Created with GeoGebra, by Tom Walsh - https://www.geogebra.org/m/JbAKa82Y ]\n\nThe potentiometer value can vary from $0$ to $12\\,\\Omega$, and $d$ represents the position of the slider.\n\n1. Show that the ammeter reads a current of $0.5\\,$A.  [2 marks]\n\n2.\n    1. Sketch the variation of the reading of the voltmeter with $d$.   [1 mark]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/Screenshot%202025-11-17%20at%208.16.26%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-11-17 at 8.16.26 AM\">\n\n    The potentiometer is now connected into the circuit as shown.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0439-2.png\" alt=\"PH0439-2\">\n    [Source: Created with GeoGebra, by Tom Walsh - https://www.geogebra.org/m/JbAKa82Y]\n\n    2. Describe how the readings on the ammeter and voltmeter will change as the distance d is increased.          [1 mark]\n\n    3. Compare between the minimum and maximum readings of the voltmeter for both circuits.     [1 mark]\n\n    4. Explain how the graph drawn in part b(i) would change with this new configuration.  [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. $I = \\dfrac{12}{12+10}\\, \\checkmark$\n\n    $I =  0.545\\, \\ll \\ce{A} \\gg\\, \\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$ \\\n    The potentiometer resistance $R$ and the $10\\,\\Omega$ resistor are connected in series.\n\n    $I = \\dfrac{V}{R} \\rightarrow I = \\dfrac{12}{12+10} = 0.545\\,$A\n\n2.  1. Linear relation from the origin. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$ \\\n        In this connection the current is constant, and according to the definition of resistance;\n\n        $V= IR$\n\n        The relation between potential difference and resistance in this case is a direct relation.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0439bi.png\" alt=\"PH0439bi\">\n        $\\small{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra, - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\n    2. The reading on the voltmeter will increase and the reading on the ammeter will decrease. $\\checkmark$\n\n       $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$ \\\nAs the distance $d$ increases, so does the resistance in the potentiometer.\n\n        Because the potentiometer is in series with the 10 Ω resistor, the overall resistance of the circuit increases and the current in the circuit will decrease as a result.\n\n       The 12 V potential difference from the cell is shared between the potentiometer and the 10 Ω resistor, with the individual potential differences proportional to their resistances. As the resistance in the potentiometer increases, its proportion of the total resistance increases. Due to this, the potential difference across it will also increase which leads to an increase in the reading on the voltmeter.\n\n    3. Same$\\,\\checkmark$\n\n       $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$ \\\n    The minimum reading when $R$ is zero, will be $0$.\n\n        The maximum is when $R$ is set at the maximum value, so the current will be the same as the case in (a)\n\n    4. Non-linear relation because the current is not constant. $\\checkmark$\n\n        Curve such that gradient is decreasing (or sketch) $\\checkmark$\n\n         $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation:}}$ \\\n         When $d$ is at a maximum, the resistance contributed by the potentiometer to the circuit will be $R$, just as in part b(i). Therefore the current at that point must be the same. At any other choice of $d$, the total resistance contributed by the potentiometer will be less than $R$, and so the current through the potentiometer will be higher than in part b(i). As a result, the potential difference across the potentiometer will also be higher.\n\n        Quantitatively:\n\n        The current in this connection is not constant, as it depends on the amount of resistance in the potentiometer\n\n        Using R to represent the resistance of the section determined by $d$.\n\n        $I=\\dfrac{12}{10+R}$\n\n        From the definition of resistance,\n\n        $\\begin{aligned}\n        V &= IR \\\\[9pt]\n        V &= \\dfrac{12}{10+R}\\, R\n        \\end{aligned}$\n\n        This relation is not linear.\n\n        Because $d$ is proportional to $R$, the shape of a $V-d$ graph will be the same as in the $V-R$ relationship shown below,\n\n        The variation will be as shown\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0439bIII.png\" alt=\"PH0439bIII\">\n        $\\small{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra, - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$"},{"ref":"PH0452","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0452\n\nTwo identical lamps $L_1$ and $L_2$ are connected to a cell with no internal resistance as shown below. Resistors $R_1$ and $R_2$ are also identical. Switch $S$ controls part of the circuit.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0452.png\" alt=\"PH0452\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra, - https://www.geogebra.org/m/JbAKa82Y}]}$\n\nWhich of the following statements about the brightness of the lamp $L_2$ are correct?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | If the resistance of $R_2$ increases, the brightness of $L_2$ increases. |\n  | II. | If the resistance of $R_1$ increases, the brightness of $L_2$ decreases. |\n  | III. | If switch $S$ is opened, the brightness of $L_2$ does not change. |\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$ Only I\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n- I. $\\quad$ Incorrect: The potential difference between $L_2$ plus $R_1$ is equal to the potential difference of the battery. As the battery does not change the current passing through / the power of / brightness of the lamp stays constant.\n\n- II. $\\enskip$ Correct: The potential difference between $L_2$ plus $R_1$ is equal to the potential difference of the battery. As $R_1$ increases the current passing through / power of / brightness of the lamp decreases.\n\n- III. $\\,$ Correct: The potential difference between $L_2$ plus $R_1$ is equal to the potential difference of the battery. As the battery does not change the current passing through / the power of / brightness of the lamp stays constant."},{"ref":"PH0519","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH0519\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\n1. The circuit shown is used to measure the variation of the resistance *R* of a wire with the length *L* of the wire. The wire is connected across points $X$ and $Y$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%204.41.11%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 4.41.11 PM\">\n\n    A voltmeter and an ammeter are connected to the circuit in order to investigate the relationship.\n\n    1. Draw the ammeter and voltmeter on the given circuit with their correct connections. [2 marks]\n\n    2. State the purpose of connecting a resistor in series with the wire as shown in the diagram. [1 mark]\n\n2. The diameter *d* of the wire is kept constant at $d=0.58\\pm0.01 \\, $mm. Determine the fractional uncertainty of the cross-sectional area *A* of the wire. [2 marks]\n\n3. The graph shows the variation of resistance of the wire *R* with the wire\\'s length *L*.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%204.28.53%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 4.28.53 PM\">\n\n    1. Draw the line of best fit. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Use the graph to estimate the resistivity of the wire. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. Correct symbol of ammeter connected in series with the wire. $\\checkmark$\n\n        Correct symbol of voltmeter connected parallel to the wire. $\\checkmark$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%204.41.15%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 4.41.15 PM\">\n\n    2. To decrease power dissipated in the wires **OR** to prevent heating of the wire **OR** to reduce current in the wire. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    When a resistor is connected in series with the wire, it increases the overall resistance of the circuit and thus decreases the current. A lower current decreases the power dissipated in the wire as seen in the relationship $P=I^2R$, thus preventing the wire from heating up, which may affect the value of its resistance.\n\n2. Percentage uncertainty of $A=2\\bigg(\\dfrac{0.01}{0.58}\\bigg)(100 \\%)$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\hspace{4.4cm}=$:answer[$0.0344$] OR :answer[$3.44\\%$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n    - $A=\\pi\\ (r^2)=\\pi{(\\dfrac{d}{2})}^2$\n\n    When values with absolute uncertainty are multiplied, the sum of the relative uncertainties is used to find the new uncertainty. In this case, the relative uncertainty of the area is equal to twice the relative uncertainty on the diameter (which is also equal to twice the relative uncertainty of the radius)\n\n    - $A=2(\\dfrac{0.01}{0.58})(100)=0.0344 \\ll=3.44\\% \\gg$\n\n3.\n    1. Straight line passing within all uncertainty bars $\\checkmark$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%204.29.06%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 4.29.06 PM\">\n\n    2.  Evidence of calculating the gradient. $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\rho=\\dfrac{\\left(8.8-1.4\\right)(\\pi)\\left(\\frac{0.58\\times{10}^{-3}}{2}\\right)^2}{\\left(60-10\\right)\\times{10}^{-2}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\rho=$ :answer[$3.9\\times{10}^{-6}\\ll \\Omega\\, $m$ \\gg $] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Since $\\rho=\\dfrac{RA}{L}$\n\n        Then; the gradient of the graph is\ngradient $=\\ \\dfrac{\\rho}{A}$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\rho}{A}=\\dfrac{8.8-1.4}{\\left(60-10\\right)\\times{10}^{-2}}=14.8$\n\n        Solving for $\\rho$ while converting diameter to radius by dividing by two gives\n\n        - $\\rho=14.8(\\pi r^2)=14.8\\pi{\\bigg(\\dfrac{0.58\\times{10}^{-3}}{2}\\bigg)}^2=3.9\\times{10}^{-6}\\, \\Omega \\,$m"},{"ref":"PH0520","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH0520\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nThe circuit shown is used to measure the internal resistance of a cell. The voltmeter and the ammeter used are ideal.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/Screenshot%202026-08-22%20at%208.12.45%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-08-22 at 8.12.45 AM\">\n\n1.\n    1. Differentiate between precise and accurate measurements. [2 marks]\n\n    2. The voltmeter used has a zero error. State the type of uncertainty this will introduce into the voltmeter\\'s measurements. [1 mark]\n\n2.\n    1. The graph shows the variation of the voltmeter\\'s reading $V$ with current $I$.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/Screenshot%202025-06-04%20at%204.31.31%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-04 at 4.31.31 PM\">\n\n        Determine the emf of the cell. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Estimate the internal resistance of the cell including its uncertainty. [3 marks]\n\n    3. Determine whether the zero error in the voltmeter will affect the calculated value of the internal resistance. Justify your answer. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1.\n        In precise measurements the random uncertainty is small/the measurements are close to each other/there are many significant figures on the measurements. $\\checkmark$\n\n        Accurate measurements are close to the actual values. $\\checkmark$\n\n    2. Systematic uncertainty. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nA systematic uncertainty affects all recorded points in the same way. The zero error will cause all points to be either too high or too low by the same amount.\n\n2.\n    1. Evidence that emf is the y-intercept. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\varepsilon= $ :answer[$8.9\\ to\\ 9.1$] $\\ll\\ \\ce{V}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/Screenshot%202025-06-04%20at%204.35.14%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-04 at 4.35.14 PM\">\n\n        The formula for the terminal voltage of a cell is given by\n\n        - $V_T = \\varepsilon - ir$\n\n        Thus the line of best fit intercepts the y-axis at the emf.\n\n    2. Gradient=$-r$/evidence of calculating the gradient $\\checkmark$\n\n        $r= $ :answer[$1.0\\,\\Omega$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        uncertainty = :answer[$\\pm\\,0.1\\ll \\Omega\\gg$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        The gradient of this graph is $-r$\n\n        gradient$=\\ \\dfrac{6.75-9}{2.25-0}=-1$\n\n        Then\n\n        - $r=1.0\\, \\Omega$\n\n        To determine the uncertainty in the gradient, we can the line of best fit with the maximum gradient possible with use of the uncertainty bars.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/Screenshot%202025-04-16%20at%209.08.42%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-16 at 9.08.42 AM\">\n\n        The uncertainty in gradient is:\n\n        - $\\Delta$ gradient  =best  fit  gradient - worst  fit   gradient\n\n        - worst fit gradient=$\\dfrac{6.65-9.2}{2.25-0}=-1.13$\n\n        - $\\Delta$ gradient  $= -1 -(-1.13) = 0.1 \\Omega$\n\n    3. Not affected $\\checkmark$\n\n        Error will cancel out in calculations/gradient remains the same $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        The zero error will raise or lower all the measured values by the same amount, but will not affect the gradient.\n\n        The internal resistance is calculated from the gradient of the graph of voltage variation with current.\n\n        In these calculations, the raised values will cancel out, which means that the value of the internal resistance of the cell will not be affected.\n\n        Alternatively, the systematic error will shift the plotted points upwards or downwards, but will not affect the gradient."},{"ref":"PH0567","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0567\n\nElectrons are accelerated between two conducting plates that have a potential difference of 100 V. Estimate the total energy that would be delivered by the conducting plates to ${10}^{21}$ electrons.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}      {10}^5\\ $J\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}      {10}^4\\ $J\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}      {10}^3\\ $J\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}      {10}^2\\ $J","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe energy $E$ is given to each electron in J:\n\n- $E=q\\Delta V=(1.6 \\times 10^{-19})(100)=1.6 \\times10^{-17}\\,$J\n\nThe total energy $E_T$ is given to the electrons in J:\n\n- $E_T=({10}^{21})(1.6\\times{10}^{-17})$\n\n- $E_T=1.6\\times{10}^4\\,$ J"},{"ref":"PH0593","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0593\n\nA wire carries electrical current. If $9.0 \\times 10^{12}$ electrons pass a given point in the wire every 4.5 seconds, what is the current in the wire?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em} 2.0 \\times 10^{-7}$ A\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em} 3.2 \\times 10^{-7}$ A\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em} 3.2 \\times 10^{-4}$ A\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em} 2.0 \\times 10^{12}$ A","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFrom the data booklet, current $I = \\dfrac{\\Delta q}{\\Delta t}$.\n\nThe number of electrons passing the given point in the wire every second,\n\n- $N_e=\\dfrac{9\\times{10}^{12}}{4.5}=2\\times{10}^{12}$.\n\nEach electron carries a charge of $-1.6\\times{10}^{-19}$ C.\n\nTherefore the magnitude of current\n\n- $I=\\left(2\\times{10}^{12}\\right)\\left(\\left|-1.6\\times{10}^{-19}\\ \\ce{C}\\right|\\right)=3.2\\times{10}^{-7}$ A."},{"ref":"PH0599","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0599\n\nA light bulb is lit by 1 kJ of energy being transferred to the bulb by a potential difference of 200 V.  This transfer happens over a time of 2 seconds.\n\nWhat is the current passing through the bulb?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em} 0.0025$ A\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}0.$ A\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}2.5$ A\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}5$ A","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nUsing $V=\\dfrac{W}{q}$ from the data booklet, we find that the charge *q* passing through the bulb in 2 seconds equals\n\n- $\\dfrac{W}{V}=\\dfrac{1000\\ \\ce{J}}{200\\ \\ce{V}}=5$ C.\n\nSince $I = \\dfrac{\\Delta q}{\\Delta t}$, the current in the bulb is $\\dfrac{5\\ \\ce{C}}{2\\ \\ce{s}}=2.5$ A."},{"ref":"PH0600","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0600\n\nIf the radius of a segment of wire is doubled, and its length decreases by half, by what factor does the wire\\'s resistance change?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 4\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 8","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n- $\\rho=\\dfrac{RA}{L}$, so $R=\\dfrac{\\rho L}{A}$.\n\nFor a wire with circular cross-section, $A=\\pi\\ r^2$.\n\nIf $r\\rightarrow2r$, then $A\\rightarrow\\left(2^2\\right)A=4A$.\n\nAlso, $L \\rightarrow \\dfrac{L}{2}$\n\nIf the initial resistance of the wire is $R_1$, and the final resistance is $R_2$, then $\\dfrac{R_1}{R_2}=\\dfrac{\\frac{\\rho L}{A}}{\\frac{\\rho(\\frac{L}{2})}{(4A)}}=8$."},{"ref":"PH0601","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0601\n\nA student constructed the series circuit below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0601.png\" alt=\"PH0601\">\n\nSome time after closing the switch, which of the following is true about how the temperature of component *A* and current in the circuit will change with time?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Temperature of *A* | Current in the circuit |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| Increases | Increases |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Increases | Decreases |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Decreases | Increases |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Decreases | Decreases |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFrom the circuit symbols in the data booklet, component *A* is a heating element.\n\nWhen current flows in the circuit, the heating element will increase in temperature and the resistance will increase. This will increase the total resistance of the circuit and result in a decrease in the current due to the relationship $I = \\dfrac{V}{R}$."},{"ref":"PH0602","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0602\n\nThe circuit below consists of a cell and two identical resistors in parallel, with an ideal ammeter in one of the branches. The current in this branch is $I_1$ and the current in the other branch is $I_2$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0602.png\" alt=\"PH0602\">\n\nIf the ideal ammeter is replaced by a real ammeter, what will be the effect on currents $I_1$ and $I_2$?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $I_1$ | $I_2$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\hspace{1.5em}$Decreases $\\hspace{1.5em}$| Decreases |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Increases | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Stays the same $\\hspace{1.5em}$|\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Decreases | Increases |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Decreases | Stays the same |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nWhen the ammeter is ideal, it has zero resistance.\n\nKirchhoff\\'s loop rule indicates that $I_1=\\dfrac{V}{R}$ and $I_2=\\dfrac{V}{R}$.\n\nWhen the ideal ammeter is replaced with a real one, the ammeter has a non-zero resistance $R_a$.\n\nBecause the loop including $I_2$ is unchanged, it is still true that $I_2=\\dfrac{V}{R}$.\n\nNow, however, $I_1=\\dfrac{V}{R+R_a}$, which is less than before."},{"ref":"PH0603","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0603\n\nThe following circuit serves as a potential divider, where $V_{in}=7  V_{out}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0603.png\" alt=\"PH0603\">\n\nIf $R_1=18\\ \\mathrm{\\Omega}$, what is the value of $R_2$?\n\nA. $\\hspace{1em} 0.1 \\ \\Omega$\n\nB. $\\hspace{1em} 1 \\ \\Omega$\n\nC. $\\hspace{1em} 3 \\ \\Omega$\n\nD. $\\hspace{1em} 5 \\ \\Omega$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFrom $R=\\dfrac{V}{I}$ and the fact that the resistors are in series,\n\n- ${\\ V}_{in}=\\left(R_1+R_2\\right)I$.\n\nAlso $V_{out}=R_2I$, therefore\n\n- $I=\\dfrac{V_{in}}{R_1+R_2}=\\dfrac{V_{out}}{R_2}$.\n\nSince $V_{in}=7V_{out}$ and $R_1=18\\ \\mathrm{\\Omega}$\n\n-  $\\dfrac{{7V}_{out}}{18+R_2}=\\dfrac{V_{out}}{R_2}$\n\ncross-multiplying\n\n- $7R_2=18+R_2$.\n\nTherefore\n\n- $R_2=3\\, \\mathrm{\\Omega}$."},{"ref":"PH0604","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0604\n\nIn the following graph, the relationship between voltage $V$ and current $I$ for two different resistors $R_A$ and $R_B$ is displayed.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0604aa.png\" alt=\"PH0604aa\">\n\nWhich of the following statements is correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ For both curves, the resistance can be found from the inverse of the gradient of the tangent at a point.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The resistance of *A* is never equal to the resistance of *B*\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ The gradient of the line for *B* gives the resistance of $R_B$\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The resistance of *A* is always greater than or equal to the resistance of *B*.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nResistance at any measured current value equals $\\dfrac{V}{I}$. Therefore to find the resistance for any given current, the actual values of $V$ and $I$ for that current must be used in the equation.\n\nBecause for any given value of *V* the current in *A* is greater than the current in *B*, the ratio of $\\dfrac{V}{I}$ will never be equal (and the resistance of *A* is always less than that of *B*). Thus the correct answer is B.\n\nLine *B* is a straight line through the origin, therefore the inverse of the gradient would give the correct resistance from the ratio $\\dfrac{V}{I}$.\n\nNote, firstly, that the gradient of the tangent would give units of $\\dfrac{Amperes}{Volts}$, which are not equivalent to the unit of resistance. Also, if the axes were switched, the gradient of the graph would only be equivalent to the resistance if the resistance was constant (the graph was a straight line through the origin)."},{"ref":"PH0606","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0606\n\nConsider the circuits below that are located side by side as shown. One of these circuits includes a thermistor, which decreases in resistance as it increases in temperature.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0606.png\" alt=\"PH0606\">\n\nIf the switch in the circuit on the left is closed, what will happen to the brightness of the lamp over time, and why?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Lamp brightness | Reason |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| Increases | Current in lamp circuit increases |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Decreases | Current in lamp circuit decreases |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Increases | Current in lamp circuit decreases |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Decreases | Current in lamp circuit increases |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nOnce the switch is closed, the heating element in the circuit on the left <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0606-i.png\" alt=\"PH0606-i\"> increases in temperature.\n\nThis increase affects the thermistor <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0606-ii.png\" alt=\"PH0606-ii\">by **decreasing** its resistance. As a result the current in the circuit on the right increases.\n\nThe power in the lamp is given by the equation\n\n- $P=I^2R$\n\nTherefore the increase in current causes the lamp\\'s power and brightness to increase."},{"ref":"PH0608","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0608\n\nAn electric motor lifts containers through a vertical distance of 30 m. Each container weighs 200 N and takes 60 seconds to lift.\n\nIf the motor operates at 100 V and has an efficiency of 40\\%, what current must be delivered to the motor?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.25 A\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.4 A\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1.0 A\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2.5 A","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nA motor efficiency of $40\\%$ means that $0.4$ =$\\dfrac{Useful\\ power\\ out}{Total\\ power\\ in}$.\n\nThe useful power out $P_{out}=\\dfrac{E}{t}=\\dfrac{\\left(F\\right)\\left(d\\right)}{t}=\\dfrac{\\left(200\\ \\ce{N}\\right)\\left(30\\ \\ce{m}\\right)}{60\\ \\ce{s}}=100\\ \\ce{W}$.\n\nTotal power in $P_{in}=\\ \\dfrac{100\\ \\ce{W}}{0.4}=250$ W.\n\nSince this is electrical power, $P_{in}=250\\ \\ce{W}=VI=\\left(100\\ \\ce{V}\\right)\\left(I\\right)$.\n\nTherefore $I=\\dfrac{250\\ \\ce{W}}{100\\ \\ce{V}}=2.5$ A."},{"ref":"PH0609","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0609\n\nTwo resistors, $P$ and $Q$, demonstrate the following relationships between current $I$ and potential difference $V$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0609aa.png\" alt=\"PH0609aa\">\n\nThe resistors are arranged as follows.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0609b.png\" alt=\"PH0609b\">\n\nIf the ammeter reads a current of 3.0 A, what is the potential difference across the cell?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.75 V\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$    3.0 V\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$    5.0 V\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$    7.0 V","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nBecause the components are in parallel, the current read by the ammeter is the sum of the currents through the resistors *P* and *Q*.\n\nFrom the graph we see their combined current is 3.0 A at a potential difference of 5.0 V.\n\nThis must be the potential difference that the cell supplies to the circuit."},{"ref":"PH0612-3","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0612-3\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n1. The diagram below shows a small section of wire which is a part of a closed electric circuit.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0612aaa%20copy.png\" alt=\"PH0612aaa copy\">\n\n   Due to a potential difference across the circuit, an electric field exists in the wire, as shown. Explain, in terms of the charge carriers, why current exists in the wire. [2 marks]\n\n2. The electric field is due to a potential difference of 120 V across the circuit. If the field performs 600 J of work moving negative charges over a time period of 2 seconds, show that approximately $3 \\times 10^{19}$ electrons move through a cross-section of wire in this time.              [2 marks]\n\n3. Calculate the current in the wire. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Charge carriers in the wire are electrons which are free to move. $\\checkmark$\n\n    The electrons move to the left due to force from the field. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    The electric field exerts a force on any charged particles in the wire. Charged particles that are free to move will be accelerated in the direction of the force.\n\n    Electric field lines point from higher to lower electric potential. Negatively charged particles are attracted to more positive regions of potential and experience a force in the opposite direction to electric field lines.\n\n2. $V=\\dfrac{W}{q} \\implies q=\\dfrac{W}{V}=\\dfrac{600\\ \\ce{J}}{120\\ \\ce{V}}=5$   $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\dfrac{5\\ \\ce{C}}{\\left|-1.6 \\times 10^{-19}\\ \\ce{C}\\right|}$  = :answer[$3.1 \\times {10}^{19}$]   $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    Using $V=\\dfrac{W}{q}$, we solve for $q=\\dfrac{W}{V}=\\dfrac{600\\ \\ce{J}}{120\\ \\ce{V}}=5$ C.\n\n    The number of electrons represented by this charge is $\\dfrac{5\\ \\ce{C}}{\\left|-1.6 \\times 10^{-19}\\ \\ce{C}\\right|}=3.1 \\times {10}^{19}$.\n\n3. $I = \\dfrac{\\Delta q}{\\Delta t} = \\dfrac{5 \\ \\ce{C}}{2 \\ \\ce{s}} =  $ :answer[$2.5\\ce{A}$] $\\checkmark$"},{"ref":"PH0648","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0648\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nMany conducting wires are fastened together in a bundle to make a cable. Each wire is made of copper, with density 9.0 g cm$^{-3}$, has a diameter of 1.5 mm, and a mass of 400 kg.\n\n1. Show that the length of the cable is approximately 25 km. [2 marks]\n\n2.\n    1. Each meter of the cable has a mass of $1.7\\, $kg. Determine the number of wires that make up the cable. [2 marks]\n\n    2. State a reason why the cable is made of many smaller wires instead of one big one. [1 mark]\n\n    3. Each wire in the cable has a resistance of $55\\, \\Omega$. If the cable carries $750\\,$A, calculate the electrical power lost in each kilometer of cable. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. $\\hspace{1em}$ $L=\\dfrac{m}{\\pi r^2\\rho}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $L=\\dfrac{400\\ \\ce{kg}}{\\pi(0.75 \\times  {10}^{-3}\\ \\ce{m})^2(9.0 \\times {10}^3\\, \\dfrac{\\ce{kg}}{\\ce{m}^3})}$ = :answer[$25.1\\ \\ce{km}$]\n$\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n    Density $\\rho=\\dfrac{m}{V}$ and for each cylindrical wire, $V=\\pi\\ r^2L$.\n\n    Therefore $L=\\dfrac{m}{\\pi r^2\\rho}$.\n\n    Substituting in quantities in base SI units,\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $L=\\dfrac{400\\ \\ce{kg}}{\\pi(0.75\\ \\times \\ {10}^{-3}\\ \\ce{m})^2(9.0\\ \\times {10}^3\\dfrac{\\ce{kg}}{\\ce{m}^3})}$\n\n    $\\hspace{1.7em}$ $=25163\\ $m\n\n    $\\hspace{1.7em}$ $=\\ 25.1\\ $km\n\n2.\n    1. $\\dfrac{m}{L}=\\dfrac{400\\ \\ce{kg}}{25000\\ \\ce{m}} = $ $0.016\\ \\dfrac{\\ce{kg}}{\\ce{m}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n         $\\hspace{1em}$ $N=\\dfrac{1.7\\ \\frac{\\ce{kg}}{\\ce{m}}}{0.016\\ \\frac{\\ce{kg}}{\\ce{m}}}=$ :answer[$106$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    2. *Any one of the following:*\n\n        - - Many wires make the cable **more flexible** $\\checkmark$\n        - - Many wires make the cable **more durable/stronger**\n        - - If one small wire fails, others can continue to carry the current\n\n    3. $R_{1km}=\\dfrac{55\\ \\Omega}{(\\ce{25} \\,\\ce{km})(106)}=$ $0.021\\ \\Omega$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $P_{1km}=I^2R_{1km}=$ :answer[$11.7\\ \\ce{k}\\ce{W}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nThe resistance of 1 km of the one wire\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $R_{1km}=\\dfrac{55\\ \\Omega}{(\\ce{25}\\ \\ce{km})}=2.2\\ \\Omega$\n\n        There are 106 wires in parallel. To find the resistance of the parallel wires, we divide the resistance of one wire by the number of wires, giving the resistance of 1 km of cable to be\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{2.2\\ \\Omega}{106}=0.0208\\ \\Omega$\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $P_{1km}=I^2R_{1km}=(750\\, \\ce{A})^2\\left(0.0208\\, \\Omega\\right)=11.7\\, $kW"},{"ref":"PH0670-2","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0670-2\n\n\\[Maximum marks: 9\\]\n\n1. A student investigates the magnetic field produced by a coil by constructing a circuit with a cell of emf 12 V with non-negligible internal resistance, a variable resistor, a coil, and connecting wires with negligible resistance.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0670aa.png\" alt=\"PH0670aa\">\n\n    The length of the copper wire used for constructing the coil is 9.4 m.\n\n    1.     Explain why the potential difference across the cell is less than 12 V when the cell is connected to the circuit. [2 marks]\n\n    2. The resistance of the variable resistor $R$ and the internal resistance of the battery $r$ are $2.5\\, \\mathrm{\\Omega}$ and $0.025\\, \\mathrm{\\Omega}$, respectively. The potential difference across the coil is 8.2 V. Calculate the resistance per metre of the coil. [3 marks]\n\n    3. A compass is placed on top of the wire at point $P$, as shown in the following diagram. Deduce the direction of the magnetic field in the location of the compass.  [2 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0670bb.png\" alt=\"PH0670bb\">\n\n2. The resistance of the variable resistor is gradually decreased to zero. On the axes below, sketch the variation of potential difference across the battery $V$ with the current passing through the coil $I$ as the variable resistor is moved through its full range.\n\n    (*There is no need to add values to the axes*) [2 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0670bbbb.png\" alt=\"PH0670bbbb\">","markScheme":"1.\n    1. When current flows through the battery $\\checkmark$\n\n        There is a potential difference across the internal resistance $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nWhen there is a current flow through the cell, there is a potential difference across the internal resistance, and this results in a reduction in the potential difference across the terminals of the cell.\n\n    2. Total V across variable resistance and internal resistance $=12-8.2=$ $3.8$ V $\\checkmark$\n\n        The current flow through the circuit = $\\dfrac{3.8}{2.5+0.025} =$ $1.5$ A $\\checkmark$\n\n        *OR*\n\n        $R=\\dfrac{(8.2)(2.5+0.025)}{3.8}=5.4\\ \\mathrm{\\Omega}$\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{5.4}{9.4} =$ :answer[$0.58\\ \\mathrm{\\Omega}\\,\\ce{m^{-1}}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nWithin the circuit, the sum of potential differences across the resistors will be equal to the emf of the cell:\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $12=8.2+V_{r+R}$\n\n        Where $V_{r+R}$ is the sum of the potential difference across variable resistance and the internal resistance.\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $V_{r+R}=12-8.2=3.8$ V\n\n        The current flow through the circuit:\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $I=\\dfrac{V_{r+R}}{R+r}=\\dfrac{3.8}{2.5+0.025}=1.5$ A\n\n        By using $R=\\dfrac{V}{I}$ across the coil:\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $R=\\dfrac{V}{I}=\\dfrac{8.2}{1.5}=5.4\\ \\mathrm{\\Omega}$\n\n        *OR*\n\n        The potential difference is shared among the resistors in proportion to their resistance values:\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{3.8}{2.5+0.025}=\\dfrac{8.2}{R}$\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $R=\\dfrac{(8.2)(2.5+0.025)}{3.8}=5.4\\ \\mathrm{\\Omega}$\n\n        The resistance per meter = $\\dfrac{5.4}{9.4}=0.58\\ \\mathrm{\\Omega}\\,\\ce{m^{-1}}$\n\n    3. The current flows clockwise in the circuit $\\checkmark$\n\n        The direction of magnetic field at point $P$ is downwards\nor towards the bottom of the page $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nThe current flows clockwise in the circuit. When the right-hand grip rule is applied to the current passing through the coil, the magnetic field direction at point $P$ can be found towards the bottom of the page. Since the compass needle aligns with the direction of the magnetic field, the compass needle should be pointing to the bottom of the page.\n\n2. Straight line with negative gradient $\\checkmark$\n\n    No y or x intercepts $\\checkmark$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0670xxxx.png\" alt=\"PH0670xxxx\">\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nThe potential difference across the battery $V$:\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $V=\\varepsilon-Ir$\n\n    Since the emf $\\varepsilon$ and internal resistance $r$ do not change, this relationship can be modelled as the following:\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $y=c-mx$\n\n    where $x$ is $I$, $m$ is $r$, $c$ is $\\varepsilon$ and $y$ is $V$\n\n    This relationship indicates a straight line with a negative gradient. It is a closed circuit with emf. Therefore, the current will never be zero and the potential difference cannot be zero accordingly. Hence, the line does not have y and x-intercepts."},{"ref":"PH0847-2","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0847-2\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n1. A student investigates the potential difference-current (V/I) characteristics of a resistor.\n\n    The diagram shows the circuit used to measure potential difference and current of the  resistor.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH2847a.png\" alt=\"PH2847a\">\n\n    Complete the circuit diagram above by showing the correct position of the voltmeter and ammeter with their circuit symbols. [2 marks]\n\n2. The student took measurements of the potential difference and current in the unknown resistor for two different positions of the variable resistor:\n\n    - $I_1 =3.5 \\times 10^{-2}$ A ,  $V_1=3.0$ V\n\n    - $I_2 =7.5 \\times 10^{-2}$ A ,  $V_2=7.0$ V\n\n    Deduce whether the resistor under study is ohmic or non-ohmic [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Correct position of ammeter $\\checkmark$\\\n    Correct position of voltmeter $\\checkmark$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH2847MSa.png\" alt=\"PH2847MSa\">\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        To determine the I-V characteristics of the resistor, the current flowing through it and the potential difference across it must be measured. Therefore, the ammeter should be connected in series to the non-ohmic resistor while the voltmeter should be parallel.\n\n2. $R_1 = \\dfrac{3}{3.5 \\times 10^{-2}}=86\\, \\Omega$\n\n    $R_2 = \\dfrac{7.0}{7.5 \\times 10^{-2}}=93\\, \\Omega$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    Ratio of $\\dfrac{V}{I}$ is not constant $\\checkmark$\n\n    Therefore non-ohmic $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Ohmic behaviour results in the ratio $\\dfrac{V}{I}$ remaining constant\n\n    Calculate the ratio for each position of the variable resistor\n\n    $R_1 = \\dfrac{V_1}{I_1} =\\dfrac{3}{3.5 \\times 10^{-2}}=86\\, \\Omega$\n\n    $R_2 = \\dfrac{V_2}{I_2}=\\dfrac{7.0}{7.5 \\times 10^{-2}}=93\\, \\Omega$\n\n    Because this ratio does not remain constant, non-ohmic behaviour is exhibited"},{"ref":"PH0881","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0881\n\nThe diagram below shows part of a circuit connected to a 12 V battery. The circuit contains two resistors of resistance $R$ and $3R$ with currents $3I$ and $2I$ flowing through them respectively. Terminals **X** and **Y** are connected via an external circuit.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0881.png\" alt=\"PH0881\">\n\nWhat is the potential difference between **X** and **Y**?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 3 V\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 4 V\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 8 V\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 9 V","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nPotential difference between **X** and **Y** is the same as the potential difference across the $3R$ resistor.\n\nPotential difference across $R$ resistor = $(3I)(R)=3IR$\n\nPotential difference across $3R$ resistor = $(3R)(2I)=6IR$\n\nRatio of resistances is $3IR:6IR = 1:2 $\n\nVoltage must be split in the same ratio (current is the same in series so $V\\ \\alpha\\ R$)\n\nRatio of voltages is $1:2=4:8$\n\nTherefore the voltage between **X** and **Y** is 8 V\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Alternatively}}$\\\nUsing Kirchhoff's first law: $\\sum I = 0$ (junction), there must be a current of $3I-2I=I$ flowing between **X** and **Y**.\n\nPotential difference is the same for parallel combination so $I\\ \\alpha\\ \\dfrac{1}{R}$.\n\nRatio of the currents is $2I:I=2:1$\\\nRatio of resistances is $1:2$ so resistance between **X** and **Y** is $2 \\times 3R=6R$.\n\nTotal resistance of parallel combination $R_{total}$ is given by\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{1}{R_{total}} = \\dfrac{1}{3R} + \\dfrac{1}{6R} = \\dfrac{2}{6R} + \\dfrac{1}{6R} = \\dfrac{3}{6R}$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $R_{total} = \\dfrac{6R}{3} = 2R$\n\nRatio of resistances in whole circuit is $R:2R=1:2$. Current is the same in series so $V\\ \\alpha\\ R$. So ratio of potential differences is $1:2$. Potential difference between **X** and **Y** is given by\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{2}{1+2} \\times 12 = 8$ V"},{"ref":"PH0882","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0882\n\nTwo cylindrical resistors **A** and **B** are made from the same material and have the same length. Resistor **A** has twice the cross-sectional area of resistor **B**.\n\nWhat is the ratio $\\dfrac{Power\\ dissipated\\ across\\ resistor\\ A}{Power\\ dissipated\\ across\\ resistor\\ B}$ when they are connected in series and parallel?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Series $\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\hspace{1.5em}$Parallel$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$| 2 |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 2 | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 2 | 2 |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\rho = \\dfrac{RA}{L}$\n\nSame material implies same resistivity. $\\rho$ and $l$ are constant so $R\\ \\alpha\\ \\dfrac{1}{A}$. **A** has twice the cross-sectional area of **B**. Therefore, **A** has half the resistance of **B**.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{Resistance\\ of\\ A}{Resistance\\ of\\ B} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$\n\nIn series current is the same in both resistors so $P=I^2R$ implies that $P\\ \\alpha\\ R$.\n\nTherefore\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{Power\\ dissipated\\ across\\ resistor\\ A}{Power\\ dissipated\\ across\\ resistor\\ B} = \\dfrac{Resistance\\ of\\ A}{Resistance\\ of\\ B} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$\n\nIn parallel potential difference is the same across both resistors so\n$P=\\dfrac{V^2}{R}$ implies that $P\\ \\alpha\\ \\dfrac{1}{R}$. Therefore\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{Power\\ dissipated\\ across\\ resistor\\ A}{Power\\ dissipated\\ across\\ resistor\\ B} = \\dfrac{1}{\\left( \\dfrac{Resistance\\ of\\ A}{Resistance\\ of\\ B}\\right)} = \\dfrac{1}{\\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)} = 2$"},{"ref":"PH0891","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0891\n\nThe diagram below shows a variable resistor connected in series with a cell of internal resistance $r$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0891AAAA.png\" alt=\"PH0891AAAA\">\n\nThe resistance of the variable resistor is initially much higher than the internal resistance of the cell. The resistance of the variable resistor is then decreased.\n\nWhich of following graphs shows how the current measured in the ammeter $I$ varies with the potential difference measured in the voltmeter $V$?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n|| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0891A.png\" alt=\"PH0891A\">  |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0891B.png\" alt=\"PH0891B\">|\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  |  <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0891C.png\" alt=\"PH0891C\"> |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0891D.png\" alt=\"PH0891D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\varepsilon\\ =\\ I(r\\ +\\ R)\\ =\\ Ir\\ +\\ IR\\ =\\ V\\ +\\ Ir$\n\nWhere $V$ is the potential difference between the terminals (what the voltmeter is measuring in this question) and $I$ is the current in the circuit.\n\n$\\varepsilon$ is the emf of the cell and $𝑟$ is the internal resistance of the cell – both of which are constant.\n\nRearranging $\\varepsilon\\ =\\ V\\ +\\ Ir$ allows us to write:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $V\\ =\\ -Ir\\ +\\ \\varepsilon$\n\n(Because $\\varepsilon$ and $r$ are constant) we can compare this to $y\\ =\\ mx\\ +\\ c$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $V\\ =\\ -Ir\\ +\\ \\varepsilon$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $y\\ =\\ mx\\ +\\ c$\n\nGraph relating $V$ and $I$ is linear with a negative gradient (of magnitude $r$ and y-intercept of $\\varepsilon$).\n\nA more intuitive alternative:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\varepsilon\\ =\\ I(r\\ +\\ R)$\n\nAs resistance of variable resistor $R$ increases, $R\\ +\\ r$ increases so $I$ decreases.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$  Potential difference across internal resistance $=\\ Ir$\n\nAs $I$ deceases, the potential difference across the internal resistance decreases (linearly as Potential difference across internal resistance $α\\ I$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$  $V\\ =\\ \\varepsilon\\ fr -$ Potential difference across internal resistance\n\nAs potential difference across internal resistance decreases $V$ increases linearly\n\nSo as $I$ decreases $V$ increases linearly."},{"ref":"PH0944","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0944\n\nThe diagram below shows part of an electric circuit.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH0944.png\" alt=\"PH0944\">\n\nAll three resistors have the same area and length. $R_2$ has twice the resistance of $R_1$. The number of electrons flowing through $R_1$ per unit time is $N$. What is the number of electrons flowing through $R_3$ per unit time?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  N$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  \\dfrac{3N}{2}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  2N$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  3N$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n- $I=\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{ \\Delta t}$\n\nThe number of electrons is proportional to charge. Therefore\n\n- $\\dfrac{N}{t} \\propto I$\n\nThe area and length of the resistors are the same therefore the resistances are the same.\n\nBecause they are in parallel, the voltage is the same across $R_1$ and $R_2$ and $V=IR$ so $I \\propto \\dfrac{1}{R}$.\n\nRatio of resistances $R_1:R_2$ is 1:2 so ratio of currents $I_1:I_2$ is 2:1.\n\n$R_2$ has half the current of $R_1 $\n\nTherefore the number of electrons per unit time flowing through $R_2$ is $\\dfrac{N}{2}$\n\nThe number of charges per unit time exiting parallel combination is\n\n- $N + \\dfrac{N}{2}=\\dfrac{3N}{2}$\n\nGiving the charge per unit time passing through $R_3$ to be $\\dfrac{3N}{2}$"},{"ref":"PH0979","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0979\n\nThe diagram shows a circuit containing eight resistors connected to a cell of emf 12 V and negligible internal resistance.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\nA voltmeter is connected between two points in the circuit. Which of the following connections will result in a potential difference reading of the largest magnitude?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ P to Q\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Q to R\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Q to S\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ R to S","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe potential difference across each of the four sections of the parallel combination will be 12 V.\n\nResistors in series have the same current, so for each pair of resistors $V\\propto R$\n\nRatio of resistances = Ratio of potential difference across those resistors\n\nFor section containing $P$: Ratio of potential difference across the resistors $= 1:1=6:6$\n\nFor section containing $Q$: Ratio of potential difference across the resistors $= 1:5=2:10$\n\nFor section containing $R$: Ratio of potential difference across the resistors $= 2:1=8:4$\n\nFor section containing $S$: Ratio of potential difference across the resistors $= 1:11$\n\nFor each final ratio above, the first term gives the potential difference across the first resistor.\n\nIf we let the potential either side of the cell be 0 V and 12 V we may write the potentials at $P$, $Q$, $R$ and $S$ as follows\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n(Note: We can choose any arbitrary values for the potentials either side of the cell - as long as they have a difference of 12 V and the positive side of the cell has a larger potential)\n\nThe largest difference here is $(11-4)$ between points $R$ and $S$."},{"ref":"PH0980","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0980\n\nThe diagram below shows a circuit containing a cell, a voltmeter, a $6.0\\, \\Omega$ resistor and an open switch.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\nThe reading on the voltmeter decreases by 20% when the switch is closed. What is the internal resistance of the cell?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $1.2\\,\\Omega$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $1.5\\,\\Omega$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $4.5\\,\\Omega$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $4.8\\,\\Omega$","markScheme":"**答案：B**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n- $\\varepsilon=V_T+Ir$\n\nWhere $V_T$ is the terminal potential difference, also the potential difference across the external circuit, and $Ir$ is the potential difference across the internal resistor.\n\nWhen the switch is open the current in the circuit is zero (or so small that it is effectively zero - some current must be flowing for the voltmeter to give a reading). Therefore,\n\n- $\\varepsilon= V_T$\n\nThe reading on the voltmeter will be equal to the emf of the cell.\n\nWhen the switch is closed, the terminal potential difference is 20% less than the emf of the cell.\n\n- $V_T=\\varepsilon-0.2\\varepsilon=0.8\\varepsilon$\n\nNow\n\n- $\\varepsilon=V_T + Ir$\n\n- $\\varepsilon=0.8\\varepsilon+Ir$\n\n- $Ir=0.2\\varepsilon$\n\nPotential will drop across internal and external resistors in proportion to their resistances. So\n\n- Internal resistance : External resistance $= 0.2\\varepsilon:0.8\\varepsilon=0.2:0.8=1:4=1.5:6$\n\n- Internal resistance $= 1.5\\,\\Omega$"},{"ref":"PH1077","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1077\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n1. The graph below shows how current varies with potential difference across filament lamp L.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH1077aaaa.png\" alt=\"PH1077aaaa\">\n\n    1. Outline why the graph shows that the filament lamp is non-ohmic.[1 mark]\n\n    Filament lamp L is connected to a $2.0\\, \\Omega$ resistor and a 6.0 V cell in parallel. The internal resistance of the cell is negligible.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/1077bbb.png\" alt=\"1077bbb\">\n\n    2. Calculate the power dissipated across L [2 marks]\n\n    3. Calculate the current flowing through the cell. [2 marks]\n\n2. The filament lamp is removed and a 10 V cell with an internal resistance of $1\\,\\Omega$  is placed in the circuit in series shown in the diagram below.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/1077cccc.png\" alt=\"1077cccc\">\n\n    Determine the potential difference across the $2\\, \\Omega$ resistor. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. current is not <directly> proportional to potential difference / *V* over *I* ratio is not constant $\\checkmark$\\\n**OR**\nresistance changes with current/potential difference $\\checkmark$\\\n**OR**\nresistance is not constant $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    An ohmic conductor is defined as having a constant current to potential difference ratio (a constant resistance).\n\n    The graph here is curved meaning that the resistance changes. Hence *L* is not ohmic.\n\n    Filament lamps undergo a significant increase in temperature when current is increased. A higher temperature means faster atomic vibrations leading to increased resistance.\n\n    2. Potential difference across $L\\ =$ $6\\ \\ce{V}$\\\n    **AND**\\\n    Current in $L\\ =$ $5\\ \\ce{A}$ $\\checkmark$\\\n    **OR**\\\n    Resistance of $L\\ =$ $1.2\\ \\Omega$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Use of $P\\ =IV / I^2R / \\dfrac{V^2}{R}$ to obtain $P\\ =$ :answer[$30\\ \\ce{W}$ ] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The potential difference across *L* is 6 V because it is connected in parallel\n\n        Reading from the graph in part (a)(i) we can see that the current is 5 A when the potential difference is 6 V\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $P = IV = (5)(6) = 30$ W\n\n        3. Current in $L\\ =$ $5\\ \\ce{A}$\\\n        **AND**\\\n        Current in resistor $= \\dfrac{V}{R} = \\dfrac{6}{2}\\ =$ 3 A $\\checkmark$\n\n        < junction rule implies that> current through cell $=\\ 3\\ce{A}\\ + 5\\ce{A}\\ =$ :answer[8 A ] $\\checkmark$\n\n        *Alternative:*\n\n        Total resistance $R_{total}$ is given by $\\dfrac{1}{R_{total}}\\ =\\ \\dfrac{1}{1.2}\\ +\\ \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ =\\ \\dfrac{8}{6}$\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $R_{total}\\ =\\ \\dfrac{6}{8}\\ =$ $0.75\\ \\Omega$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $I\\ =\\ \\dfrac{V}{R}\\ =\\ \\dfrac{6}{0.75}\\ =$ :answer[$8 \\ce{A}$ ] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The potential difference across *L* and the resistor is $6$ V\n\n        From the graph in (a)(i) we can see that the current in $L\\ =\\ 5$ A\n\n        Using $V\\ =\\ IR$ we can calculate the current in the $2\\ \\Omega$ resistor.\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $I\\ =\\ \\dfrac{V}{R}\\ =\\ \\dfrac{6}{2}\\ =\\ 3$ A\n\n        We know that at a junction, the sum of all currents entering a junction equals the sum of all currents leaving the junction (due to conservation of charge)\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/1077dddd.png\" alt=\"1077dddd\">\n\n        Therefore, the relation between the current in the cell $I_C$, the current in the resistor $I_R$ and the current in the filament lamp $I_L$ is $I_C\\ =\\ I_R\\ +\\ I_L\\ =\\ 3\\ce{A}\\ +\\ 5\\ce{A}\\ =\\ 8 \\ce{A}$\n\n2. Loop equation showing correct voltages, i.e., $10\\ -\\ 6$ on one side $\\checkmark$\\\n**OR**\\\nboth emfs positive on different sides of the equation $\\checkmark$\\\n**OR**\\\ncorrect resistances, i.e. $I\\ (1\\ +\\ 2)$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $10\\ -\\ 6\\ =I\\ (1\\ +\\ 2)$\\\n    **OR**\\\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $I\\ =\\dfrac{4}{3}$ A seen $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $V\\ =\\ \\left( \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\\ (2)\\ =$ :answer[$2.7\\ \\ce{V}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    Allow any valid method.\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    For a series circuit, we know that the sum of all voltage rise and drop around any closed loop is equal to zero (due to conservation of energy).\n\n    Total voltage rise $=\\ 10\\ -\\ 6\\ =\\ 4\\,\\ce{V}$ because the cells are working in opposite directions (The 10 V cell alone would cause a clockwise current and the 6 V cell alone would cause an anticlockwise current. Together the cells cause a clockwise current because the 10 V cell is larger)\n\n    Total voltage drop $=\\ IR_{total}\\ =\\ I(1\\ +\\ 2)\\ =\\ 3I$ (both resistances are positive because current is flowing clockwise through them both)\n\n    Therefore $4\\ =\\ 3I$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $I\\ =\\ \\dfrac{4}{3}$\n\n    Voltage across $2\\ \\Omega$ resistor $=\\ IR\\ =\\ \\left( \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\\ (2)\\ =\\ \\dfrac{8}{3}\\ =\\ 2.7$ V"},{"ref":"PH1790","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1790\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\nThe graph below shows how resistance $R$ varies with temperature $T$ for a thermistor.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/Screenshot%202025-03-21%20at%201.43.21%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-03-21 at 1.43.21 PM\">\n\nThe diagram below shows a circuit containing the thermistor, a cell of e.m.f $\\varepsilon$ and a resistor of resistance $R$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/Screenshoot%202025-03-21%20at%201.41.21%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshoot 2025-03-21 at 1.41.21 PM\">\n\n1.    Explain how the voltmeter reading changes as the temperature of the thermistor increases. [2 marks]\n\n2. The thermistor's resistance is set equal to that of the fixed resistor. However, the voltmeter reading is observed to be **less than** $\\dfrac{\\varepsilon}{2}$. Suggest two possible reasons for this. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Resistance of the thermistor decreases so voltage across it decreases/ the thermistor shares a greater proportion of the total voltage.\n\n    **OR**\n\n    Total resistance of the circuit decreases so the current increases. $\\checkmark$\n\n    Voltage across the resistor increases/voltmeter reading increases. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    From the graph, we can see that an increase in temperature will lead to a decrease in resistance.\n\n    The total voltage of the circuit $\\varepsilon$ is divided between the thermistor and the fixed resistor. As the thermistor's resistance decreases, it takes a smaller share of the total voltage. This means the voltage across the fixed resistor increases, and since the voltmeter is measuring this voltage, its reading increases.\n\n    Alternatively, since the total resistance of the circuit is lower, from Ohm's Law $\\varepsilon=IR_{total}$, we see that the current in the circuit increases. Therefore, the voltage across the fixed resistor $V=IR$ must increase.\n\n2. $\\underline{\\textrm{Any two from}}$\n\n    The cell has an internal resistance\n\n    The voltmeter is not ideal/Voltmeter has a finite resistance OWTTE\n\n    The connecting wires have a resistance $\\checkmark$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    When the thermistor's resistance equals the fixed resistor's resistance, we would normally expect the voltmeter to read $\\dfrac{\\varepsilon}{2}$ because a **potential divider** splits the total voltage evenly when both resistances are equal. However, the actual reading is **less than** $\\dfrac{\\varepsilon}{2}$. because of the following reasons:\n\n    **1. Internal Resistance of the Cell**\n    - Real batteries have some internal resistance, which reduces the actual voltage available in the circuit.\n\n    - Some of the voltage is \"lost\" inside the cell, meaning the voltage across the external components (thermistor and resistor) is less than $\\epsilon$\n\n    **2. Non-Ideal Voltmeter**\n\n    - An ideal voltmeter has infinite resistance, meaning it does not draw any current.\n\n    - In reality, voltmeters have finite resistance, meaning they draw a small current, which slightly alters the potential difference they are measuring.\n\n    - The resistance of the parallel combination of the resistor R and the voltmeter will be less than R which will result in a lower voltage reading\n\n    **3. Resistance of Connecting Wires**\n\n    - The wires in the circuit also have small resistance.\n\n    - This means some energy is dissipated in the wires, causing a further reduction in the voltage across the fixed resistor, making the voltmeter reading slightly lower than expected."},{"ref":"PH1812","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1812\n\nThe diagram shows a combination of resistors:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/Screenshot%202025-03-25%20at%201.39.12%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-03-25 at 1.39.12 PM\">\n\nWhat is the effective resistance between points $A$ and $B$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{12}{5}\\ \\Omega$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{5}{2}\\ \\Omega$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{50}{11}\\ \\Omega$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $10\\ \\Omega$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFor resistors $R_1$, $R_2$, $R_3$ in series we have:\n\n- $R_{total}=R_1+R_2+R_3+...$\n\nFor resistors $R_1$, $R_2$ in parallel we have:\n\n- $\\dfrac{1}{R_{total}}=\\dfrac{1}{R_1}+\\dfrac{1}{R_2}$\n\nWith respect to points $A$ and $B$, the $1\\,\\Omega$, $2\\,\\Omega$ and $3\\,\\Omega$ are in series so their combined resistance is given by:\n\n- $R=1+2+3=6\\,\\Omega$\n\nBetween $A$ and $B$, we then have a parallel combination with $6\\,\\Omega$ and $4\\,\\Omega$, therefore:\n\n- $\\dfrac{1}{R_{total}}=\\dfrac{1}{4}+\\dfrac{1}{6}=\\dfrac{3}{12}+\\dfrac{2}{12}=\\dfrac{5}{12}$\n\n- $R_{total}=\\dfrac{12}{5}\\Omega$"},{"ref":"PH1813","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1813\n\nThe circuit shown below contains three identical resistors $X$, $Y$, and $Z$ of resistance $R$ and a battery of emf $\\varepsilon$ and negligible internal resistance.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH1813q.JPEG\" alt=\"PH1813q\">\n\nWhat is the ratio $\\dfrac{\\textrm{power dissipated by resistor X}}{\\textrm{power dissipated by resistor Y}}$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{1}{4}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{1}{2}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $4$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe power $P$ dissipated across a component is given by:\n\n- $P=I^2R$\n\nWhere $I$ is the current through the component and $R$ is its resistance.\n\nBecause all resistors are identical, we know that the current will split evenly in the parallel branches, so\n\n- $I_X=2I_Y$\n\nBecause $R$ is the same for all components,\n\n- $P\\propto I^2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{power dissipated by resistor X}}{\\textrm{power dissipated by resistor Y}}=\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{current through resistor X}}{\\textrm{current through resistor Y}}\\right)^2=\\left(\\dfrac{2I_Y}{I_Y}\\right)^2=4$\n\nAlternatively,\n\nThe power $P$ dissipated across a component is given by:\n\n- $P=\\dfrac{V^2}{R}$\n\nWhere $V$ is the voltage across the component and $R$ is its resistance.\n\n$X$ and $Y$ have the same resistance, so we have $P\\propto V^2$. We therefore need to find the ratio of the voltages across the resistors.\n\nThe resistance of the parallel combination of $Y$ and $Z$ is given by:\n\n- $\\dfrac{1}{R_{total}}=\\dfrac{1}{R}+\\dfrac{1}{R}=\\dfrac{2}{R}$\n\n- $R_{total}=\\dfrac{R}{2}$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the voltages across $X$ and $Y$ is $1:\\dfrac{1}{2}=2:1$.\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{power dissipated by resistor X}}{\\textrm{power dissipated by resistor Y}}=\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{voltage across resistor X}}{\\textrm{voltage across resistor Y}}\\right)^2=\\left(\\dfrac{2}{1}\\right)^2=4$"},{"ref":"PH1815","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1815\n\nThe diagram shows two identical cells, each with an emf of $3\\,$V and internal resistance $r$, are connected in a circuit with a $3.0\\,\\Omega$ resistor.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH1815q.JPEG\" alt=\"PH1815q\">\n\nThe ammeter reads $1.5$ A. What is $r$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $0.25\\,\\Omega$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $0.50\\,\\Omega$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $0.75\\,\\Omega$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $1.0\\,\\Omega$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe total emf of the circuit is $3+3=6$ V as both cells work to push the current in the same direction around the circuit.\n\nThe total resistance of the circuit is $3+2r$.\n\nUsing $V_{total}=IR_{total}$ we have:\n\n- $6=1.5(3+2r)$\n\n- $4=3+2r$\n\n- $1=2r$\n\n- $r=0.5\\,\\Omega$"},{"ref":"PH1844-1","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1844-1\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\n1. The resistance of a metal electric heating element increases as the current flowing through it increases. With reference to the atoms inside the heating element, explain why this occurs. [2 marks]\n\n2. An electric immersion heater is connected to a fixed resistor $R$ in the circuit shown below.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/ph1844-1qb1.png\" alt=\"ph1844-1qb1\">\n\n    The graph below shows how the current $I$ in the circuit varies with time $t$ after the switch is closed.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/Screenshot%202026-04-15%20at%201.08.20%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-04-15 at 1.08.20 PM\">\n\n    Explain the shape of the graph. [2 marks]\n\n3. When the current reaches a steady value, the power dissipated by the heater is 50 W. Calculate the resistance of the fixed resistor $R$. [3 marks]\n\n4. The average diameter and total length of metal heating element inside the immersion heater are 0.8 mm and 7.8 m respectively. Determine the resistivity of the metal at the temperature at which the heater is operating in part (c). [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Electrons collide with metal atoms $\\checkmark$\n\n    Electrons transfer heat energy to metal atoms which increases the resistance of the metal $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    As the current increases, the electrons flowing through the heating element move faster and collide more frequently with the metal ions (positive ions). These collisions transfer energy to the ions, increasing the vibrations of the metal lattice. This increase in ion vibration causes more frequent collisions and greater resistance to the flow of electrons.\n\n2. Resistance / temperature of the heater increases initially and then reaches a steady / constant value $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\ll$Total resistance of the circuit increases and then reaches a constant / steady value so$\\gg$  $I=\\dfrac{V}{R_{total}}$ therefore current in the circuit decreases until it reaches a steady / constant value $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    When the switch is closed the current very quickly (effectively instantaneously) reaches a value given by:\n\n    - $I=\\dfrac{V}{R_{total}}$\n\n    Where $V$ is the voltage of the cell and $R_{total}$ is the total resistance of the circuit.\n\n    This current causes the temperature of the immersion heater to increase. Total resistance in the circuit increases, so the current decreases as total voltage is constant and $V_{total}=IR_{total}$.\n\n    After some time the temperature (and therefore resistance) of the heater reaches a constant value when all of the heat energy is being conducted away from it to the surroundings. Current therefore reaches a constant value.\n\n3. Alternative 1\n\n    - $R_{heater}=\\dfrac{50}{4.2^2}=2.83$ $\\Omega$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $R_{total}=\\dfrac{15}{4.2}=3.57$ $\\Omega$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $R=3.57-2.83=$ :answer[0.74 $\\Omega$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    Alternative 2\n\n    - $P_{total}=(4.2)(15)=63$ W $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $P_R=63-50=13$ W $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $R=\\dfrac{13}{4.2^2}=0.74$ $\\Omega$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    Alternative 3\n\n    - $V_{heater}=\\dfrac{50}{4.2}=11.9$ V $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $V_R=15-11.9=3.1$ V $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $R=\\dfrac{3.1}{4.2}=0.74$ $\\Omega$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Using $P=I^2R$, the resistance of the heater is given by:\n\n    - $R_{heater}=\\dfrac{P_{heater}}{I^2_{heater}}=\\dfrac{50}{4.2^2}=2.83$ $\\Omega$\n\n    Using $V=IR$ the total resistance of the circuit is given by:\n\n    - $R_{total}=\\dfrac{V_{total}}{I_{total}}=\\dfrac{15}{4.2}=3.57$ $\\Omega$\n\n    The resistors are in series, so we have:\n\n    - $R_{total}=R+R_{heater}$\n\n    Rearranging for $R$ and substituting our values we have:\n\n    - $R=R_{total}-R_{heater}=3.57-2.83=0.74$ $\\Omega$\n\n4. $\\hspace{1.7em}$ $A=\\dfrac{\\pi(0.8\\times10^{-3})^2}{4}$ OR $A=5.0\\times10^{-7}$ seen $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $\\rho=\\dfrac{RA}{L}=\\dfrac{(2.83)\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi(0.8\\times10^{-3})^2}{4}\\right)}{7.8}=$ :answer[$1.8\\times10^{-7}$ $\\Omega$ m] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The cross-sectional area of the wire is given by:\n\n    - $A=\\dfrac{\\pi d^2}{4}=\\dfrac{\\pi(0.8\\times10^{-3})^2}{4}=5.0\\times10^{-7}$\n\n    Note that $r = \\dfrac{d}{2}$ and so $r^2 = \\dfrac{d^2}{4}$.\n\n    The resistance of the heater is given by:\n\n    - $R_{heater}=\\dfrac{P_{heater}}{I^2_{heater}}=\\dfrac{50}{4.2^2}=2.83$ $\\Omega$\n\n    Resistivity is given by:\n\n    - $\\rho=\\dfrac{RA}{L}=\\dfrac{(2.83)(5.0\\times10^{-7})}{7.8}=1.8\\times10^{-7}$ $\\Omega$ m"},{"ref":"PH1898","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1898\n\nA cell has an internal resistance of 1.0 $\\Omega$ and an emf of 15 V.\n\nWhen a resistor of resistance $R$ is connected across the terminals of the cell, the terminal potential difference is 10 V.\n\nDetermine the resistance $R$.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1.0 $\\Omega$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2.0 $\\Omega$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 3.0 $\\Omega$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 4.0 $\\Omega$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe electromotive force of the cell is given by:\n\n- $\\varepsilon=I(R+r)$\n\nThe equation can also be written as:\n\n- $\\varepsilon=IR+Ir$\n\nThe term $IR$ on the right-hand side corresponds to the potential difference across the resistance $R$, and given that it is the only resistance connected to the cell, it is also equal to the terminal potential difference which is 10 V.\n\nWe can now calculate the output current by substituting values as follows:\n\n- $15\\textrm{ V}=10\\textrm{ V}+I(1.0\\,\\Omega)$\n\n- $I=\\dfrac{15\\textrm{ V}-10\\textrm{ V}}{1.0\\,\\Omega}=5.0$ A\n\nThe external resistance can now be calculated using Ohm's law:\n\n- $V=IR$\n\n- $R=\\dfrac{10}{5}=2.0\\,\\Omega$"},{"ref":"PH2649","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH2649\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n1. A 24 V source is connected to a series of $N$ identical bulbs in parallel, shown below. The maximum circuit current is 2.0 A. If each bulb has a resistance of 5.0 k $\\Omega$, determine the maximum number of bulbs in the circuit. [3 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH2649b.png\" alt=\"PH2649b\">\n\n2. A second circuit is constructed as shown below. The resistance characteristics of components **Y** and **Z** are shown in the graph.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH2649ci.png\" alt=\"PH2649ci\">\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Electric%20Circuits/images/PH2649cii.png\" alt=\"PH2649cii\">\n\n    Determine which resistor has greater resistance.  [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. $R_{total}= \\dfrac{5000\\ \\Omega}{N}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n     $\\hspace{1em}$ $I = \\dfrac{V}{R_{total}}=\\dfrac{24\\  (N)}{5000\\ }\\le2.0$ A $\\checkmark$\n\n     $\\hspace{1em}$ $N\\le\\dfrac{10000}{24}\\ \\Rightarrow$ :answer[$N=416$]  $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nUsing the equation $\\dfrac{1}{R_{total}}=\\dfrac{1}{R_1}+\\dfrac{1}{R_2}+\\ldots$ for the total circuit resistance of identical resistors in parallel can be used to find $R_{total}$.\n\n      $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{1}{R_{total}}=N\\lparen \\dfrac{1}{R_{total}})$\n\n      $\\hspace{1em}$ $R_{total}=\\dfrac{R}{N}=\\dfrac{5000}{N}$\n\n      Using this value in the resistance equation indicates that the maximum circuit current\n\n     $\\hspace{1em}$ $I_{max}=\\dfrac{V}{R}=\\dfrac{24(N)}{5000}$\n\n     $\\hspace{1em}$ $2\\ =\\dfrac{24(N)}{5000}$\n\n     Rearranging to solve for $N$ gives a value of 416 (Note that the number is rounded down. To round up would make the current over the allowed limit).\n\n2.  At $6 \\ \\ce{V}, R_Z\\approx\\dfrac{6\\ \\ce{V}}{1.8\\ \\ce{A}}=3.3$  while $R_{Y\\ }\\approx\\ \\dfrac{6\\ \\ce{V}}{4.6\\ \\ce{A}}=1.3\\ \\Omega$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    Component **Z** has greater resistance $\\checkmark$\n\n    **OR**\n\n    From the graph, $I_Y > I_Z$ at all potential differences and $R=\\dfrac{V}{I}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    Component Z has greater resistance    $\\checkmark$\n\n     $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nUsing the resistance formula $R = \\dfrac{V}{I}$ to find the resistance of each resistor when $V = 6$ V.\n\n     Taking approximate values from the graph, at 6 $V$ the current in **Y** is roughly 4.6 A, while the current through **Z** is about 1.8 A.\n\n     $R=\\dfrac{V}{I}$, therefore the resistor with the least current at 6 V will have the greatest resistance. At this potential difference, resistor **Z** has less current than resistor **Y**, and therefore a greater resistance.\n\n     Alternatively,\n\n     It can be seen from the graph that $I_Y > I_Z$ at all potential differences (although 6 V is the potential difference it is operating at in this situation). Through the definition of resistance\n$R = \\dfrac{V}{I}$\n\n    We can see that if $V$ is the same (which it is in the parallel arrangement here), larger currents reflect smaller resistances.\n\n    Because **Y** has a larger current than **Z**, it must have a smaller resistance. **Z** has greater resistance than **Y**."}]},{"name":"Greenhouse Effect","questions":[{"ref":"PH0059","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0059\n\nThe orders of energy transformations in three power stations are given;\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n\n  | | |\n\n  | :-- | :-- |\n\n  | I. | chemical $\\rightarrow$ thermal $\\rightarrow$ kinetic $\\rightarrow$ electrical |\n\n  | II. | kinetic $\\rightarrow$ kinetic $\\rightarrow$ electrical |\n\n  | III. | gravitational potential $\\rightarrow$ kinetic $\\rightarrow$ electrical |\n\nWhich row correctly matches the type of power station with the energy transformations?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  $\\hspace{6em}$I$\\hspace{6em}$  |  $\\hspace{5em}$ II $\\hspace{5em}$ |  $\\hspace{4em}$III $\\hspace{4em}$ |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ A. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Coal power station | Hydroelectric power station | Wind power station |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ B. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Coal power station | Wind power station | Hydroelectric power station |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ C. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Solar power station | Hydroelectric power station | Coal power station |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ D. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Nuclear power station | Wind power station | Coal power station |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nI. $\\hspace{1em}$ Chemical energy in fossil fuel is transformed into thermal energy by burning and thermal energy is used to move (kinetic energy) steam that rotates the turbine and generator (also kinetic energy). Finally, electric energy is produced in generators.\n\nII.    $\\hspace{1em}$ The kinetic energy of wind rotates the blades (kinetic energy) and rotates the generator. Finally, electric energy is produced in generators.\n\nIII. $\\hspace{1em}$ The gravitational potential energy of water behind a dam converts into kinetic energy as water falls down and rotates the turbine and generator. Finally, electric energy is produced in generators."},{"ref":"PH0060","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0060\n\nAn object  has a surface area of $2.0$ m$^2$ and emissivity $e=0.5$. The object emits electromagnetic radiation of peak wavelength $2.90 \\times 10^{-5}$ m. What is the best estimate for the energy radiated by the object in one second?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $1.4$ J\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2.8$ J\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $5.7$ J\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $11.3$ J","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nFrom the data booklet, $\\lambda T=2.9\\times 10^{-3}$\n\nWhich gives $T=\\dfrac{2.9\\,\\times\\,10^{-3}}{2.9\\,\\times\\,10^{-5}}=100\\,K$\n\nAlso from the data booklet $P=e\\sigma AT^4$\n\n$P=(0.5)(5.67\\times 10^{-8})(2)(100)^4=5.67\\,W\\cong 5.7\\,$J in one second"},{"ref":"PH0093","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0093\n\nWhich of the following energy resources is non-renewable?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Biomass\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Solar\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Wind\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Uranium-235","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nUranium-235 is a rare radioactive isotope on Earth, extracted from uranium ores. The available uranium is limited and will be depleted in time. Biomass will replenish in a human lifetime, so is considered renewable."},{"ref":"PH0096","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0096\n\nThe following three statements give possible reasons why the average intensity of sunlight incident on a solar panel during a day on Earth is only a fraction of the solar constant. \n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n\n  | | |\n\n  | :-- | :-- |\n\n  | I. | Scattering of sunlight in the atmosphere |\n\n  | II. | Rotation of the Earth |\n\n  | III. | The surface of the solar panel is not perpendicular to incident sunlight |\n\nWhich of the statements are correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only \n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III \n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nStatement I is correct. The scattering of sunlight returns part of the sunlight back to space, reducing intensity on the ground.\n\nStatement II is correct. The rotation of the Earth causes day and night, reducing the time the solar panel is exposed to sunlight and also changing the incident angle of sunlight on the panel.\n\nStatement III is correct. The intensity on the solar panel is maximum when its surface is perpendicular to incident sunlight, but due to the seasons, the time of day and the installation angle of the panel the sunlight will not be normally incident on the panel.\n\nThus, D is the answer."},{"ref":"PH0134","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0134\n\nSolar radiation is incident on a planet, whose surface absorbs 65% of incident intensity. What is the albedo of the planet? \n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.25\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.35\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.65\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nAlbedo = $\\dfrac{\\textrm{reflected power}}{\\textrm{incident power}}$\n\nIf the planet absorbs 65%, it reflects 35%\n\nGiving albedo = $\\dfrac{35}{100}=0.35$"},{"ref":"PH0140","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0140\n\nOn a certain day, the solar intensity on the Earth's surface is 300 W m$^{-2}$. Photovoltaic cells which have an efficiency of 20% are used to convert solar energy to electrical energy. What should be the minimum area of the photovoltaic cells to produce an output of 6.0 kW assuming that the incident light is normal to the surface of the panel?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 20 m$^2$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 40 m$^2$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 100 m$^2$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 3000 m$^2$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nEfficiency is the ratio of power output to power input expressed as a percentage. \n\n- Efficiency = $\\dfrac{\\textrm{Power output}}{\\textrm{Power input}} \\times 100$\n\n- Power output = 6000 W\n\n- Power input = (300)(Area)\n\n- $0.2=\\dfrac{6000}{300(A)}$\n\n    \n\n- $A=\\dfrac{6000}{(0.2)(300)}$\n\n   \n\n- $A=100$ m$^2$"},{"ref":"PH0141","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0141\n\nAn area on the Earth's surface receives an average solar intensity of 250 W m$^{-2}$. What is the minimum area of photovoltaic cells needed to generate 2 kW of electrical power if the photocells operate at 40% efficiency? \n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.5 m$^2$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1.0 m$^2$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2.0 m$^2$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 20 m$^2$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nFor the solar cells functioning at 40% efficiency, the product of efficiency, power input per square meter and the area of the solar cells the output energy will be 2 kW. \n\nHence, $P=(0.40)(250\\textrm{ W m}^{-2})(A)$\n\n- $A=\\dfrac{2000\\textrm{ W}}{100\\textrm{ W m}^{-2}}=20$ m$^2$"},{"ref":"PH0238","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0238\n\nWhich of the following is a non-renewable energy resource?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Nuclear\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Wind\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Solar\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Geothermal","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nNon-renewable sources are sources where the rate of replenishment is much longer than a human lifespan. Examples include nuclear, coal, oil, and natural gas. Therefore, the answer is A."},{"ref":"PH0239","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0239\n\nSeveral energy conversions occur in power stations. Examples of electricity generation that both involve energy transfer from thermal to electrical are \n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Coal-fired and hydroelectric\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Coal-fired and nuclear\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Solar and natural gas\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Solar and wind","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nBoth in coal-fired and nuclear, thermal energy is converted to electrical energy. Therefore, the answer is B."},{"ref":"PH0556","marks":"11","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0556\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\n1. \n\n    1. Define the albedo of a body [1 mark]\n\n    2. It was suggested that painting rocky mountain tops with a white chalk-based paint could help reduce the impact of global warming. In terms of albedo, explain why this could work. [2 marks]\n\n2. \n\n    1. Given that the radius of the Earth is $6.4 \\times 10^6$ m, show that at any given time, the total amount of energy incident on the Earth's surface is approximately $1.8 \\times 10^{17}$ J per second. [2 marks]\n\n    2. The average albedo of the Earth is 0.30. What is the rate at which the Earth absorbs energy from the Sun? [1 mark]\n\n    3. Using your answer from (b)(ii) and ignoring atmospheric effects, estimate the average temperature of the Earth for which equilibrium would be established. Assume the Earth's average emissivity is 0.80 [2 marks]\n\n    4. Greenhouse gases in the atmosphere elevate the average temperature of the Earth such that it is higher than the answer found in (b)(iii). Explain the mechanism by which this occurs. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. \n\n    1. Albedo is the fraction of the incident light that is reflected by a surface. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        Normally expressed as a decimal, it is found from $\\dfrac{\\textrm{Power}_{\\textrm{reflected}}}{\\textrm{Power}_{\\textrm{incident}}}$\n\n    2. White is more reflective therefore has a higher albedo $\\checkmark$\n\n        Less radiation absorbed will result in less warming $\\checkmark$\n\n2. \n\n    1. $\\hspace{1.8em}$ $A = \\pi r^2 = \\pi(6.4 \\times 10^6)2 = 1.29 \\times 10^{14}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $P=AS$\n\n        - $\\hspace{0.8em}$ $=(1.29\\times10^{14})(1.36\\times10^3)$\n\n        - $\\hspace{0.8em}$ $=$ :answer[$1.75\\times10^{17}$ W]\n\n        \n\n        (must show 3 or more significant figures to receive the second mark)\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The shape that blocks the sunlight is a circle with the radius of the Earth. \n\n        \n\n        - $A = \\pi r^2 = \\pi(6.4 \\times 10^6)2 = 1.29 \\times 10^{14}$\n\n        That area multiplied by the solar constant will give the total incident energy.\n\n        - $P=AS$\n\n        - $\\hspace{0.8em}$ $=(1.29\\times10^{14})(1.36\\times10^3)$\n\n        - $\\hspace{0.8em}$ $=1.75\\times10^{17}$ W\n\n        \n\n    2. Power absorbed = $(1-0.3)(1.75 \\times 10^{17})$ = :answer[$1.2 \\times 10^{17}\\,W$] (or $1.3\\times10^{17}$  if $1.8 \\times 10^{17}$ is used)\n\n    3. $P = e \\sigma A T^4$ , where $A = 4\\pi(6.4 \\times 10^6)^2$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $T = \\sqrt[4]{\\dfrac{1.2 \\,\\times\\, 10^{17}}{(0.80)(5.67\\, \\times\\, 10^{-8})(4)(\\pi)(6.4 \\,\\times \\,10^6)^2}}$ = :answer[$268$ K] $\\checkmark$\n\n    4. Greenhouse gases absorb infrared radiation released by the Earth. $\\checkmark$\n\n        This radiation is re-radiated in all directions by the greenhouse gases. $\\checkmark$\n\n        With about 50% of this radiation returning back to the Earth's surface $\\checkmark$"},{"ref":"PH0558","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0558\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\n1.    An object $X$ of surface area $24.3$ cm$^2$ emits $300$ W of radiation when its temperature is $1215$ K.\n\n    1. State what is meant by emissivity. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Show that this object is a blackbody radiator. [3 marks]\n\n2. Calculate the peak wavelength of the emitted radiation. [2 marks]\n\n3. The object $X$ is left to cool down until it reaches $800$ K. On the axes given, sketch the variation of intensity with wavelength of the radiation of object $X$ at both the original $1215$ K and the cooler temperature of $800$ K. Label the graphs. [3 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Greenhouse%20Effect/images/PH0558-1.png\" alt=\"PH0558-1\">\n\n    $\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra, - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$","markScheme":"1. \n\n    1. The ratio of the power emitted by a surface to the power emitted by a black body of the same dimensions at the same temperature. $\\checkmark$\n\n    2. $\\hspace{1.8em}$ $\\ll P = e\\sigma AT^4\\gg$\n\n        - $e=\\dfrac{300}{(5.67\\times 10^{-8})(24.3\\times 10^{-4})(1215)^4}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $e\\approx 1$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        The emissivity of a blackbody is 1 / the object is a blackbody since emissivity is **OWTTE** $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The power of the radiation is given by\n\n        - $P=e\\sigma AT^4$\n\n        Solving for emissivity ($e$) gives\n\n        - $e=\\dfrac{300}{(5.67\\,\\times \\,10^{-8})(24.3\\,\\times\\, 10^{-4})(1215)^4}\\approx 1$\n\n        The emissivity of a blackbody is 1\n\n        This means that this object is a blackbody. \n\n2. $\\hspace{1.8em}$ $\\ll \\lambda_{max}=\\dfrac{2.90\\times 10^{-3}}{T}\\gg$\n\n    - $\\lambda_{max}=\\dfrac{2.90 \\times 10^{-3}}{1215}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $\\lambda_{max}$ = :answer[$2.39\\times 10^{-6}\\ll\\textrm{ m}\\gg$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    The peak wavelength can be found by Wein's displacement law, where $T$ is the kelvin temperature.\n\n    - $\\lambda_{max}=\\dfrac{2.90 \\times 10^{-3}}{T}$\n\n    - $\\lambda_{max}=\\dfrac{2.90 \\times 10^{-3}}{1215}=2.39 \\times 10^{-6}$ m\n\n3. $\\hspace{1em}$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Greenhouse%20Effect/images/PH0558-2.png\" alt=\"PH0558-2\">\n\n    $\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra, - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\n    Correct curve for variation of intensity with wavelength. $\\checkmark$\n\n    Maximum radiation intensity at $800$ K is lower than the maximum at 1215. $\\checkmark$\n\n    Peak wavelength for $800$ K is longer/shifted to the right compared with $1215$ K $\\checkmark$ \n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    The intensity graph is the distribution curve shown.\n\n    At a higher temperature, an object will radiate energy at a higher rate, so the area under the curve is larger. \n\n    The peak wavelength is also shifted to higher frequencies/shorter wavelengths for the hotter object."},{"ref":"PH0561","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0561\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nThe Sun radiates energy to the surface of the Earth. Some of this energy is scattered and the rest reaches the Earth's surface.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Greenhouse%20Effect/images/PH0561b.png\" alt=\"PH0561b\">\n\n1. Differentiate between heat transfer methods: conduction, convection and thermal radiation. [3 marks]\n\n2. At any given time, the power of the radiation of the Sun absorbed by the Earth's surface is $P$. $P$ is given by\n\n   $\\hspace{1em}P=(1-\\alpha)S\\pi R^2$\n\n   Where $\\alpha$ is the albedo of the Earth’s surface.\n\n    \n\n    1. Show that the average intensity of radiation $I_{avg}$ that is absorbed by the Earth's surface is given by $I_{avg}=\\frac{(1-\\alpha)S}{4}$ [2 marks]\n\n    2. Calculate the average intensity of the radiation that is absorbed by the Earth's surface. The Earth's albedo is 0.3. [1 mark]\n\nSome of the infrared radiation from the Earth is absorbed and reradiated by the greenhouse gases.\n\n3. Explain this absorption and emission of radiation in terms of molecular energy levels. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Conduction is the transfer of thermal energy through atomic vibrations $\\checkmark$\n\n    Convection is the transfer of thermal energy resulting from the mass movement of groups of atoms or molecules in fluids $\\ll $often arising  from differences in density in a gravitational field. $\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    Thermal radiation is the transfer of thermal energy by electromagnetic radiation. $\\checkmark$\n\n2. \n\n    1. $\\hspace{1.8em}$ $I=\\dfrac{Power}{Area}$ / intensity is power divided by area $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $I_{avg}=\\dfrac{(1-\\alpha)S\\pi R^2}{4\\pi R^2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The intensity of radiation can be found from\n\n        - $I=\\dfrac{Power}{Area}$\n\n        Considering the Earth's surface to be spherical, then the average intensity of radiation absorbed is\n\n        - $I_{avg}=\\dfrac{(1-\\alpha)S\\pi R^2}{4\\pi R^2}$\n\n        \n\n        - $I_{avg}=\\dfrac{(1-\\alpha)S}{4}$\n\n    2. $\\hspace{1.8em}$ $\\ll I_{avg}=\\dfrac{(1-\\alpha)S}{4}\\gg$\n\n        - $\\ll I_{avg}=\\dfrac{(1-0.3)(1.36\\times 10^3)}{4}\\gg$ \n\n        - $I_{avg}$ = :answer[$238\\ll\\textrm{ W m}^{-2}\\gg$] $\\checkmark$\n\n3. Energy difference between molecular energy levels matches the energy of an infrared photon. $\\checkmark$\n\n    Infrared photons are absorbed. $\\checkmark$\n\n    Excited gas molecules return to their lower energy states and emit the absorbed photons. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    The different energy states of the greenhouse gas molecules also correspond to the resonant frequencies of the molecules. The emitted infrared photons match these resonant frequencies and are absorbed by the greenhouse gases. The photons are then re-emitted when the greenhouse gas molecules exit their resonant state."},{"ref":"PH0952","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0952\n\nLight is incident on a patch of fresh snow. It is found that for every photon absorbed by the snow, four photons are reflected. What is the albedo value of the fresh snow?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.2\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.25\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.75\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.8","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n- Albedo $= \\dfrac{\\textrm{Total scattered power}}{\\textrm{Total incident power}}$\n\nWe are told that for every absorbed photon, four are scattered. This means that out of five incident photons, four will be scattered and one absorbed so the ratio is\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{ Number of scattered photons}}{\\textrm{Total number of incident photons}} = \\dfrac{4}{5} = 0.8$\n\nThe scattering rate of  the photons is equivalent to the scattering rate of the incident light and therefore the albedo.\n\n- Albedo $= 0.8$"},{"ref":"PH1007","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1007\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nThe government of a medium sized city plans to replace coal power plants with photovoltaic cells to power the city.\n\nThe following data are available:\n\n- $\\bullet \\hspace{1em}$ Total area available for photovoltaic cells    = 62.6 km$^2$\n- $\\bullet \\hspace{1em}$ Maximum intensity of light incident on photovoltaic cells = $1.36 \\times 10^3$ W m$^{-2}$\n- $\\bullet \\hspace{1em}$ Average efficiency of energy conversion in photovoltaic cells = 20 $\\%$\n- $\\bullet \\hspace{1em}$ Hours of sunshine in one month    = 150 hours\n\n1. Explain why the government\\'s choice to reduce its reliance on coal would be beneficial to the Earth\\'s climate. [2 marks]\n\n2. Outline, in terms of energy changes, how electrical energy is generated from coal.  [3 marks]\n\n3. Using the data given, calculate in GJ the maximum expected energy generation for one month. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Burning coal emits carbon dioxide (CO$_2$) $\\checkmark$\n\n    Which is a greenhouse gas/traps heat radiated by the Earth $\\checkmark$\n\n2. The chemical energy of coal is transferred to the internal energy of the water/steam $\\checkmark$\n\n    The internal energy of the water gives kinetic energy to rotate a turbine  $\\checkmark$\n\n    The kinetic energy of the turbine is transferred to electrical energy in the generator/dynamo $\\checkmark$\n\n3. $(1.36 \\times 10^3 )(62.6 \\times 10^6 )(150)(3600)(0.20)$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    :answer[$9.2 \\times 10^6  \\ll\\textrm{GJ} \\gg$]  $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    150 hours = $(150)(3600) = 540 \\ 000 \\textrm{ s}$\n\n    $62.6 \\textrm{ km}^2 = 62.6 \\times 10^6 \\textrm{ m}^2$\n\n    $\\begin{aligned}\n    \\textrm{Total Incident Energy} &= \\textrm{Energy Intensity} \\times \\textrm{Area} \\times \\textrm{Time} \\\\\n    &= (1.36 \\times 10^3 )(62.6 \\times 10^6 )( 540 \\ 000 ) \\\\\n    &= 4.6 \\times 10^{16}\\textrm{ J}\n    \\end{aligned}$\n\n    $\\begin{aligned}\n    \\textrm{Electrical energy obtained} &= \\textrm{Total energy } \\times \\textrm{Efficiency} \\\\\n    &= (4.6 \\times 10^{16})(0.20)\\\\\n    &= 9.2 \\times 10^{15}\\textrm{ J}\\\\\n    &= 9.2 \\times 10^6\\textrm{ GJ}\n    \\end{aligned}$"},{"ref":"PH1012","marks":"11","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1012\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\n1. A house uses a solar heating panel with an area of $1.7$ m$^2$. The panel receives an average solar intensity of $240$ W m$^{-2}$. The albedo of the solar panel is 0.30.\n\n    1. State the name of the type of solar panel that generates electricity. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Show that the absorbed power by the solar heating panel is around $290$ W. [1 mark]\n\n    3.     The solar panel only transfers $73$ J of heat to the water that circulates just below it every second. Determine the efficiency of the panel. [1 mark]\n\n2. The water has a specific heat capacity of $4200$ J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$ and its temperature rises by $32\\degree$ C.\n\n    1. Calculate the mass of the water that circulates in one minute. [3 marks]\n\n    2. Many solar panels use a mixture of propylene glycol and water to prevent water from freezing. Propylene glycol has a higher molar mass than water. Compare the mean square speed of the molecules of propylene glycol to the speed of the molecules of water. [2 marks]\n\n3. Outline one advantage and disadvantage of using solar photovoltaic cells  to generate electricity compared to nuclear power stations. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. Photovoltaic cells $\\checkmark$\n\n    2. Incident power on the solar panel $=(240)(1.7)=410$ W $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $(0.7)(410 )=286$ W $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The power $P$ of incident light on the solar heating panel:\n\n        - $P=IA=(240)(1.7)=410$ W\n\n        The power absorbed $P_0$ by the solar heating panel:\n\n        - $P_0=(0.7)(410 )=286$ W\n\n    3. $\\hspace{1.8em}$ $\\dfrac{73}{290}= $ :answer[0.25] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        The efficiency $\\eta$:\n\n        - $\\eta=\\dfrac{\\textrm{useful  power out}}{\\textrm{total power in}} =\\dfrac{73}{290}=0.25$\n\n2. 1. Heat supplied in one minute $=(73)(60)=4400$ J $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $m=\\dfrac{4400}{(4200)(32)}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\hspace{0.9em}$ = :answer[$3.3\\times10^{-2}$ kg] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        The heat $Q$ supplied to the water in one minute:\n\n        - $Q= P\\,t=(73)(60)=4400$ J\n\n        - $Q=mc\\Delta T$\n\n        - $m=\\dfrac{Q}{c\\Delta T}=\\dfrac{4380}{(4200)(32)}=3.3\\times 10^{-2}$ kg\n\n    2. Both of the liquids have the same temperature at any time so the average kinetic energy of the molecules is the same\n\n        Greater mass results in lower mean square speed therefore propylene glycol molecules are slower/water molecules are faster $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        Both of the liquids have the same temperature at any time. By using the following formula:\n\n        - $E_k=\\dfrac{3}{2}k_bT$\n\n        They should have the same average kinetic energy for their molecules. By using the formula of kinetic energy:\n\n        - $E_k=\\dfrac{1}{2}m<c^2>$\n\n        Greater mass results in a smaller mean square speed. Therefore, propylene glycol molecules will be slower than water molecules.\n\n3. Advantage\\\n    Renewable/free/small sizes are possible/low risk $\\checkmark$\n\n    Disadvantage\\\n    Low energy density/not reliable $\\checkmark$\n\n    Accept any other suitable advantage or disadvantage"},{"ref":"PH1090","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1090\n\nA surface with an area $A_S$ emits electromagnetic radiation at a rate of $P_S$. An ideal blackbody with the same temperature and area $A_B$ emits radiation at a rate of $P_B$. What is the emissivity of the surface?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{P_SA_B}{P_BA_S}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{P_SA_S}{P_BA_B}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{P_BA_B}{P_SA_S}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{P_BA_S}{P_SA_B}$","markScheme":"**答案：A**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\n\nBy using Stefan Boltzmann's Law for both bodies:\n\n- $P_S=\\epsilon \\sigma A_S T^4$\n\nSince the emissivity of a black body is 1:\n\n- $P_B=\\sigma A_B T^4$\n\nDividing the two equations:\n\n- $\\dfrac{P_S}{P_B}=\\dfrac{\\epsilon \\sigma A_S T^4}{\\sigma A_B T^4}=\\epsilon \\dfrac{A_S}{A_B}$\n\n- $\\epsilon=\\dfrac{P_S A_B}{P_B A_S}$"},{"ref":"PH1091","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1091\n\nA system with an albedo of 0.3 scatters radiation that is incident on it. The power of scattered radiation is $1200$ W. Determine the power absorbed by the system.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $840$ W\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $1200$ W\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $2800$ W\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $4000$ W","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\n\n- Albedo $=\\dfrac{\\textrm{Total scattered power}}{\\textrm{Total incident power}}$\n\n- Total incident power $=\\dfrac{\\textrm{Total scattered power}}{\\textrm{Albedo}} = \\dfrac{1200}{0.3} = 4000$ W\n\n- Absorbed power $=4000$ W$-1200$ W $=2800$ W"},{"ref":"PH1092","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1092\n\nWhich of the following statements is correct for the albedo of a particular area of the Earth's surface?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | The albedo remains constant for the same area throughout the year. |\n  | II. | The albedo tends to increase when the area is covered by snow. |\n  | III. | The albedo is generally lower for areas covered with trees compared to those covered with concrete.  |\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Only I\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Only II\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Only III\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Only II and III","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\n\nThe albedo of land varies due to several factors. Cloud formations, which change daily, significantly influence albedo by affecting the amount of incoming solar radiation reflected into space. Additionally, the presence of snow tends to increase albedo, as snow is a highly reflective surface. Conversely, areas covered with trees tend to have lower albedo because trees absorb a significant amount of sunlight for photosynthesis, reducing the overall reflectivity of the land."},{"ref":"PH1093","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1093\n\nA hypothetical planet, with a radius twice that of Earth, orbits the Sun at a distance twice that of the Earth's distance from the Sun. Determine the average intensity of solar radiation on the surface of the hypothetical planet in terms of the solar constant of the Earth, $S$.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $S$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{S}{2}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{S}{4}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{S}{16}$","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\n\nThe intensity of the Sun on the Earth is solar constant.\n\n- $S=\\dfrac{P}{4\\pi d^2}$\n\nSo the power $P$ of the Sun in terms of solar constant:\n\n- $P=S4\\pi d^2$\n\nWhere the $d$ is the distance between the Sun and the Earth.\n\nThe intensity $I$ of the Sun at the distance of the planet's orbit:\n\n- $I=\\dfrac{S4\\pi d^2}{4\\pi (2d)^2}=\\dfrac{S4\\pi d^2}{4\\pi 4d^2}=\\dfrac{S}{4}$\n\nThe power $P_p$ received by the planet:\n\n- $P_p=IA=\\dfrac{S}{4} \\pi(2R)^2=\\pi R^2 S$\n\nWhere the $R$ is the radius of the Earth.\n\nThe average intensity $I_{av}$ received by the planet:\n\n- $I_{av}=\\dfrac{P_p}{A_S}=\\dfrac{\\pi R^2 S}{4\\pi(2R)^2}=\\dfrac{S}{16}$\n\nThis last calculation is the common \"solar constant divided by 4\" calculation that is used to average the incident solar energy over the entire surface of the Earth."},{"ref":"PH1094","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1094\n\nMethane $CH_4$, carbon dioxide $CO_2$ and water vapor $H_2 O$ are three gases present in the atmosphere. Which of these gases are naturally occurring greenhouse gases?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Methane and carbon dioxide\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Carbon dioxide and water vapor\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Methane and water vapor\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Methane, carbon dioxide, and water vapor","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\n\nMethane is a naturally occurring greenhouse gas. It can be emitted from natural sources such as wetlands, as well as human activities like agriculture and the production of fossil fuels. Carbon dioxide is another naturally occurring greenhouse gas. It is emitted from natural sources like volcanic eruptions, respiration, and decay of organic matter. Water vapor is the gas form of water and is the most abundant greenhouse gas in the Earth's atmosphere. It is naturally present in the atmosphere and plays a crucial role in the Earth's climate system."},{"ref":"PH1095","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1095\n\nGreenhouse gases absorb particular wavelengths of radiation and re-radiate the radiation as they transition back to lower energy states. Which is most correct for the type of radiation absorbed and the direction in which the radiation is emitted?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Type of radiation$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Direction of emission$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| Ultraviolet | All |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Ultraviolet | Towards the earth |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Infrared | All |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Infrared | Towards the earth |","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\n\nGreenhouse gases in the Earth's atmosphere interact with infrared radiation. When these gases absorb infrared radiation from the Earth's surface and atmosphere, their molecules undergo energy changes, increasing their internal energy. As a result of the relaxation process, the molecules release the absorbed energy in the form of radiation. This emitted radiation is primarily in the form of infrared and can be scattered in any direction, not just towards the Earth. Therefore, the correct answer is that greenhouse gases emit infrared radiation in all directions during the relaxation process."},{"ref":"PH1096","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1096\n\nConsider the following statements about the behaviour of greenhouse gases.\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | They absorb radiation that matches their natural frequency.   |\n  | II. | They absorb radiation with frequencies matching the energy difference between molecular energy levels. |\n  | III. | They reach high temperatures after absorbing radiation, warming the Earth. |\n\nWhich statements are correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Only I\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Only II\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Only I and II\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Only II and III","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\n\nThe absorption of radiation emitted by the Earth and the atmosphere by greenhouse gases can be explained by both resonance and energy differences between molecular energy levels.\n\nBecause the photon energies match the differences in the energy levels, the photons are absorbed and the molecules enter excited energy states.\n\nThe resonance model states that the frequency of the radiation matches the resonant frequencies of the molecules, causing them to oscillate in a resonant state.\n\nOn absorption of the radiation, the molecule energies increase slightly, but it is the re-radiation of the photons back to Earth that causes the Earth’s temperature to increase."},{"ref":"PH1098","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1098\n\nA grey body of emissivity $e$ and albedo $1-e$ has a total surface area of $A$ cm$^2$. The absolute temperatures of the body and the surroundings are $T$ and $\\dfrac{T}{2}$ respectively. The surroundings can be considered as a black body. The net power, in watts, transferred from the body to the surroundings is\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{1}{16}eA\\sigma T^4$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{1}{16}e(A\\times10^{-4})\\sigma T^4$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{15}{16}eA\\sigma T^4$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{15}{16}e(A\\times10^{-4})\\sigma T^4$","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\n\nThe power emitted $P_e$ by the body:\n\n- $P_e=e(A\\times10^{-4})\\sigma T^4$\n\nThe albedo is given as $1 - e$, so the fraction of incident power absorbed will be $e$.\n\nThe power received $P_r$ by the body from surroundings:\n\n- $P_r=e(A\\times10^{-4})\\sigma\\Bigl(\\dfrac{T}{2}\\Bigr)^4=e(A\\times10^{-4})\\sigma \\dfrac{T^4}{16}$\n\nThe net power $P$ transferred:\n\n- $P=P_e-P_r=e(A\\times10^{-4})\\sigma T^4-e(A\\times10^{-4})\\sigma \\dfrac{T^4}{16} = \\dfrac{15}{16} e(A\\times10^{-4})\\sigma T^4$"},{"ref":"PH1099","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1099\n\nWhich of the following is **not** a reason why the solar constant is regarded as an average value rather than a fixed value?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Elliptical orbit of the Earth\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Solar flares on the Sun\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Variations in atmospheric composition\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Solar cycles","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\n\nVariations in atmospheric composition do not directly influence the calculation of the solar constant. The solar constant primarily represents the average amount of solar radiation received per unit area at a distance of one astronomical unit (AU) from the Sun. While changes in atmospheric composition can affect how much solar radiation is absorbed or reflected by Earth's atmosphere, they are not a direct factor in determining the solar constant itself. Therefore, option C is the correct choice as it is not one of the reasons why the solar constant is regarded as an average value rather than an absolute value."},{"ref":"PH1103","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1103\n\nA city launches a “carbon capture” project to remove carbon dioxide from the atmosphere. The graph shows the relative amounts of carbon dioxide emitted by natural and human sources, plotted alongside the amount of carbon dioxide absorbed before and after the start of the project.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Greenhouse%20Effect/images/PH1103.png\" alt=\"PH1103\">\n\nDuring the project, the city's mayor announces that the city has achieved “net zero”: the rate of carbon capture equals the city's contributions to the enhanced greenhouse effect.\n\n1. Estimate the year in which this achievement takes place. [3 marks]\n\nFollowing the announcement, scientists complain that the mayor's claim does not account for emissions of Gas $A$, which is a primary greenhouse gas.\n\n2. 1. State which portion of the electromagnetic spectrum must be absorbed by Gas $A$ for it to behave as a greenhouse gas. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Describe how electromagnetic absorption by Gas $A$ molecules takes place, with reference to both bond resonance and molecular energy levels. [2 marks]\n\n3. In terms of the energy entering and leaving the Earth's atmosphere, explain how increasing the concentration of greenhouse gases raises the surface temperature. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Carbon capture at net zero = Domestic $CO_2$ + Industrial $CO_2$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    Carbon capture (2028) > Domestic $CO_2$ + Industrial $CO_2$\n\n    Carbon capture (2027) < Domestic $CO_2$ + Industrial $CO_2$\n\n    Year will be :answer[2027] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    The enhanced greenhouse effect refers to the warming effects of greenhouse gases released by human activities, meaning that $CO_2$ resulting from natural processes may be excluded from consideration.\n\n    Alternatively, one may determine the year in which net zero is achieved by simply noting that whilst net zero has already been achieved at the start of 2028 (as $A$ exceeds $R$), $R$(domestic) and $R$(industrial) are between two and three at the beginning of 2027, meaning that their sum is greater than $A$, which has a value of four. It therefore follows that equality between $R$ and $A$ must occur in the year 2027.\n\n2. 1. Infra-red absorption $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        Greenhouse gases contribute to the greenhouse effect by absorbing infra-red radiation emitted by the Earth’s surface, preventing it from escaping the atmosphere.\n\n    2. Radiation frequency matches molecular resonant frequencies, causing excited state  $\\checkmark$\n\n        Radiation excites molecules from one energy state to a higher energy excited state  across a quantised energy gap $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        In the resonance model of absorption, molecules of Gas $A$ absorb electromagnetic radiation when the resonance frequencies of their bond vibrations match the frequencies of incident waves.\n\n        Alternatively, an energy level model may be used to describe absorption: electromagnetic radiation is absorbed by a molecule of Gas $A$ when its energy matches the energy gap between the ground-state energy level of the molecule, and a higher “excited” energy state which corresponds to excited bond vibrations within the molecule.\n\n3. Increased greenhouse gas concentration decreases energy radiated into space/increases incident radiation on the Earth's surface $\\checkmark$\n\n    Consequently, energy from radiation absorbed by the Earth’s surface exceeds radiation emitted $\\checkmark$\n\n    Surface temperature increases until energy radiated from the Earth’s surface equals energy entering it $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    In thermal equilibrium, the radiative energy entering the Earth’s atmosphere is equal to the radiative energy leaving it. Greenhouse gases suppress the rate of radiative loss by absorbing infra-red radiation emitted from the surface and radiating half of it back to the Earth’s surface. Therefore, increasing the concentration of these gases leads to a reduction in the rate of energy loss from the atmosphere, and in turn the radiation incident on the Earth’s surface. When the Earth’s surface has heated to an extent that the radiative energy emitted once again equals the radiative power incident on the surface, thermal equilibrium is reestablished."},{"ref":"PH1113","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1113\n\nWhich of the following effects would result in an increase in the average temperature of the Earth?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | Increased surface albedo   |\n  | II. | Increased atmospheric transmission of visible light |\n  | III. | Increased atmospheric absorption of infra-red radiation |\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I and II\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I and III\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ II and III\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nSurface albedo refers to the proportion of incident radiation reflected by the Earth’s surface. Increasing the albedo decreases the amount of energy absorbed by the surface and subsequently re-emitted as infra-red radiation that can be trapped by the atmosphere; thus, increasing albedo reduces the average temperature of the Earth.\n\nIncreasing the atmospheric transmission of visible light enables more energy to reach the Earth’s surface, contributing to an increase in the Earth’s temperature. Subsequent thermal emissions from the surface are predominantly in the infra-red region of the electromagnetic spectrum, meaning that visible light transmittivity has negligible impact on the trapping of radiation by greenhouse gases.\n\nFinally, increasing atmospheric absorption of infra-red radiation enhances the greenhouse effect, as this change increases the proportion of thermal emissions from the Earth’s surface that become trapped due to absorption and subsequent re-emission by greenhouse gases."},{"ref":"PH1114","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1114\n\nThe production rates of greenhouse gases from various sources within a country are measured over a period of thirty years. The graph shows how the measured production rates vary with time.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Greenhouse%20Effect/images/PH1114.png\" alt=\"PH1114\">\n\nWhich of the following statements are correct?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | Contributions to the enhanced greenhouse effect decrease after 15 years |\n  | II. | Total contributions to the enhanced greenhouse effect exceed $8$ Mt per year at 15 years |\n  | III. | The enhanced greenhouse effect accounts for a lower proportion of total global warming at the end of the measurement period than at the start |\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I and II\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I and III\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ II and III\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe enhanced greenhouse effect refers to warming due to human sources of greenhouse gases. Consequently, it is true that contributions to the enhanced greenhouse effect decrease after 15 years, even though contributions from natural sources continue to increase during this period; hence, Statement I is correct.\n\nThe sum of all contributions to the greenhouse effect surpasses $8$ Mt per year after 15 years, but human sources do not; therefore, Statement II is incorrect.\n\nAt the start of the measurement period, emissions from human sources occur at an approximately five times higher rate than those from natural sources. By comparison, the emission rate from human sources after 30 years is around double the rate of natural emissions. Hence, human sources account for a greater proportion of emissions at the start of the measurement period than at the end, so Statement III is correct."},{"ref":"PH1115","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1115\n\nAn experiment is carried out to investigate the absorption of electromagnetic radiation by four gases of equal molar concentration. The proportion A of energy absorbed is plotted versus spectral frequency $f$ in each case. Which of the four gases would make the greatest contribution to the greenhouse effect?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  ||  | B.  ||\n|| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Greenhouse%20Effect/images/PH1115A.png\" alt=\"PH1115A\"> |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Greenhouse%20Effect/images/PH1115B.png\" alt=\"PH1115B\"> |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Greenhouse%20Effect/images/PH1115C.png\" alt=\"PH1115C\"> |  |  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Greenhouse%20Effect/images/PH1115D.png\" alt=\"PH1115D\"> |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nGreenhouse gases contribute to the greenhouse effect by absorbing and then re-emitting thermal radiation from the Earth’s surface, which is predominantly in the infra-red portion of the electromagnetic spectrum. Consequently, the gases that produce the greatest greenhouse effect are those that absorb most strongly at infra-red frequencies. Whilst Gas B and Gas C both absorb a large total amount of radiation over the measured range of frequencies, very little absorption occurs in the infra-red range. Similarly, despite Gas D absorbing nearly all UV radiation, it absorbs only a small quantity of infra-red waves. By contrast, Gas A absorbs the highest quantity of infra-red radiation, even though it produces the lowest total absorption over the entire electromagnetic spectrum."},{"ref":"PH1788","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1788\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nThe diagram below shows a simple energy balance model for the Earth.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Greenhouse%20Effect/images/Screenshot%202025-03-21%20at%2010.17.17%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-03-21 at 10.17.17 AM\">\n\n1.    Determine the intensity of the incoming radiation absorbed by the atmosphere. [1 mark]\n\n2. Use the diagram to deduce the albedo of the Earth. [1 mark]\n\n3. Explain in terms of energy balance how a decrease in the average albedo value of the Earth would cause the Earth to reach a new equilibrium temperature. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. ::indent\n    - $340-170-85-15=$ :answer[70 W m$^{-2}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Conservation of energy tells us that all of the incoming solar radiation is either reflected or absorbed by the Earth's atmosphere and surface. We therefore have:\n\n    - $85+15+170+X=340$\n\n    - $340-170-85-15=70$ W m$^{-2}$\n\n2. ::indent\n    - $\\dfrac{15+85}{340}=$ :answer[0.29] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Albedo is given by:\n\n    - $\\textrm{albedo}=\\dfrac{\\textrm{total scattered power}}{\\textrm{total incident power}}$\n\n    In the diagram, 15 W m$^{-2}$ is reflected by the atmosphere, and 85 W m$^{-2}$  is reflected by the surface. The total scattered power is $15+85=100$ W m$^{-2}$. The total incident radiation is 340 W m$^{-2}$.\n\n    Substituting these values into our equation we have:\n\n    - $\\textrm{albedo}=\\dfrac{\\textrm{total scattered power}}{\\textrm{total incident power}}=\\dfrac{100}{340}=0.29$\n\n3. $\\ll$A decrease in albedo would result in$\\gg$ more solar radiation being absorbed by the Earth, $\\checkmark$\n\n    Earth's temperature will increase until emitted radiation = absorbed radiation $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Albedo represents the reflectivity of the Earth. A lower albedo means less solar radiation is reflected and more is absorbed by the Earth's surface and atmosphere.\n\n    To achieve energy balance, the radiated energy must equal the absorbed energy. Radiated energy is given by the equation\n\n    - $L=\\sigma AT^4$\n\n    And so the temperature $T$ must increase so that $L$ can match the radiation absorbed."},{"ref":"PH1893","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1893\n\nThe Earth receives solar radiation at a solar constant $S$. The Earth-atmosphere system has an average albedo $a$.\n\nWhat is the average power absorbed by the Earth and its atmosphere per unit area?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{Sa}{4}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{S(1-a)}{4}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $Sa$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $S(1-a)$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe solar constant $S$ is the power per unit area received at the top of Earth's atmosphere, the incoming radiative power is dependent on the projected surface of a planet along the direction of the path of the rays, resulting in a mean value of the incoming intensity being $\\dfrac{S}{4}$.\n\nAlbedo $a$ is the fraction of the incident radiation that is reflected, so the fraction of the incident radiation that is absorbed is $1-a$.\n\nTherefore, the average intensity (power per unit area) absorbed is given by:\n\n- $\\dfrac{S(1-a)}{4}$\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Alternative approach}}: $\n\nThe solar constant $S$ is the power per unit area received at the top of Earth's atmosphere, but only the circular cross-sectional area of the Earth $\\pi r^2$ (where $r$ is the Earth's radius) directly receives sunlight.\n\nSo, the total incoming power $P$ is given by:\n\n- $P=S\\pi r^2$\n\nHowever, not all of this is absorbed. Albedo a is the fraction of the incident radiation that is reflected, so the fraction of the incident radiation that is absorbed is $1-a$.\n\n- Absorbed power $=S\\pi r^2(1-a)$\n\nTo find the average absorbed power per unit area over the entire surface of the Earth (which has an area of $4\\pi r^2$) divide by that:\n\n- Average absorbed power per unit area $=\\dfrac{S\\pi r^2(1-a)}{4\\pi r^2}=\\dfrac{S(1-a)}{4}$"},{"ref":"PH1908","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1908\n\nThe diagram below shows a simple energy balance model for the Earth.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Greenhouse%20Effect/images/Screenshot%202025-09-24%20at%2010.18.04%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-09-24 at 10.18.04 AM\">\n\nThe intensity of radiation reflected by the clouds is half the intensity of the radiation absorbed by the clouds. What is the intensity of the radiation absorbed by the clouds?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 10 W m$^{-2}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 15 W m$^{-2}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 20 W m$^{-2}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 30 W m$^{-2}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nIn the energy balance model, the total energy absorbed and reflected is equal to the incoming energy.\n\nLet the intensity reflected by clouds be $X$ and the intensity absorbed by the clouds be $Y$.\n\n- $340=20+50+X+Y+70+170$\n\n- $X+Y=30$\n\nThe intensity of radiation reflected by the clouds is half the intensity of the radiation absorbed by the clouds so $X=\\dfrac{Y}{2}$.\n\n- $\\dfrac{Y}{2}+Y=30$\n\n- $\\dfrac{3}{2}Y=30$\n\n- $Y=20$ W m$^{-2}$"}]},{"name":"Ideal Gas Model","questions":[{"ref":"PH0032","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0032\n\nA quantity of 2000 kg Carbon Dioxide ($\\ce{CO2}$) is maintained in a container tank. The molar mass of Carbon Dioxide ($\\ce{CO2}$) is 44 g mol$^{-1}$. Determine the correct estimate of the number of Carbon Dioxide ($\\ce{CO2}$) molecules in the container tank.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $10^{22}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $10^{25}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $10^{28}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $10^{31}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\n$N=N_A \\times \\dfrac{m}{M}$\n\n$N=(6.02 \\times 10^{23}) (\\dfrac{2000 \\times 10^3}{44}) \\approx 300 \\times 10^{26} \\approx 10^{28}$"},{"ref":"PH0041","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0041\n\nIdeal gases obey the assumptions in the kinetic model. The behaviour of real gases is different from ideal gases. Which of the following statements about an ideal gas is **not** correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Forces between ideal gas molecules are negligible when not undergoing a collision.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ All molecules in an ideal gas have negligible volume.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Ideal gases can be liquefied.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Collisions of ideal gas molecules with each other are elastic.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nA gas can be liquefied if there are forces of attraction acting on gas molecules. According to the kinetic model, forces between gas molecules are negligible. Therefore, ideal gases can not be liquefied."},{"ref":"PH0042","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0042\n\nThe following graph shows the relationship between temperature and volume for a real gas at constant pressure. What must be the conditions for pressure and density of the gas?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/PH0042.png\" alt=\"PH0042\">\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Low pressure and low density\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Low pressure and high density\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ High pressure and low density\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ High pressure and high density","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe graph shows Charles' Law for an ideal gas. A real gas may be approximated by an ideal gas under low pressure and when the density is low. At higher pressures and larger densities, the effects of intermolecular forces become significant. \n\nFor example, when the particles are under high pressure and pushed close together (high density), the intermolecular forces between the gas particles become significant and a real gas will no longer approximate an ideal gas."},{"ref":"PH0086","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0086\n\nAn enclosed container with an internal volume V contains $3.0 \\times 10^{23}$ helium atoms at a Kelvin temperature $T$. What is the pressure of the helium gas?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $(3.0 \\times 10^{23}) \\dfrac{RT}{V}$  \n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $3.0 \\dfrac{k_B T}{V}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{3.0}{6.02}\\dfrac{RT}{V}$  \n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{3.0}{6.02}\\dfrac{k_B T}{V}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nApply $PV=nRT$, in which $n$ is the number of moles of gas molecules. Using \n\n$n=\\dfrac{N}{N_A} = \\dfrac{3.0}{6.02}$ arrives at the solution."},{"ref":"PH0098","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0098\n\nWhich of the following real gases at atmospheric pressure would be least likely to approximate to an ideal gas? \n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Helium gas at temperature of 56 K\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Oxygen gas at temperature of 500 K\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Nitrogen at temperature of 374 K\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Hydrogen gas at temperature of 22 K","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nIdeal gas approximation is only applicable for low pressure and high temperature. This is because when the particles are close together (high pressure) or interact with slower speeds (low temperatures), intermolecular forces start to play a role in their interactions. One of the assumptions of ideal gases is that there are no intermolecular forces.\n\nFor the gases listed, the assumption is least applicable for hydrogen gas at 22 K."},{"ref":"PH0126","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0126\n\nA vessel of volume $1.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ m$^3$ contains neon gas at a pressure of $2.0 \\times 10^5$ Pa, when the temperature is 200 K. The molar gas constant is approximately $8.0$ J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$ and the molar mass of neon is $20$ g mol$^{-1}$. \n\nWhat is the approximate mass of the neon in the vessel?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.1 g\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.4 g\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2.5 g\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 5.0 g","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe number of moles can be found by dividing the mass ($m$) by the molar mass ($M$), \n\n- $n=\\dfrac{m}{M}$\n\nUsing $PV=nRT$, $\\hspace{1em} PV=\\frac{m}{M} RT$   \n\n- $\\begin{aligned}\n\nm&=Mn\\\\\n\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n&= \\frac{PVM}{RT}\\\\\n\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n&= \\frac{(2.0 \\times 10^5\\textrm{ Pa})(1.0 \\times 10^{-3}\\textrm{ m}^3)(20\\textrm{ g mol}^{-1})}{(8.0\\textrm{ J mol}^{-1}\\textrm{ K}^{-1})(200\\textrm{ K})}\\\\\n\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n&= \\frac{4000}{1600}\\\\\n\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n&= 2.5\\textrm{ g}\n\n\\end{aligned}$"},{"ref":"PH0149","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0149\n\nAn ideal gas is enclosed in a container of fixed volume. When the temperature of the gas is $27\\degree$C, its pressure is $p$. Calculate the pressure in the gas when it is heated to $54\\degree$C.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $p$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $1.09p$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $1.5p$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2p$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nTemperature appears to be doubled; but that is using the celsius scale. Absolute temperature is not doubled. So, $D$ is incorrect. \n\nVolume cannot change, since the container's volume is fixed. \n\nThe number of moles cannot change since the gas is enclosed in the container. \n\nHence, pressure is proportional to absolute temperature:\n\n $\\dfrac{p_2}{p}=\\dfrac{273+54}{273+27}\\Rightarrow p_2=1.09p$."},{"ref":"PH0220","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0220\n\nA fixed mass of ideal gas is trapped in a closed container. The graph shows how two variables related to the gas vary.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/PH0220.png\" alt=\"PH0220\">\n\nThe gradient of the graph is $kV$ while $k$ is constant and $V$ is the volume of the container. \n\nIn the table below, which row correctly describes quantities X and Y? \n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{6em}$ X $\\hspace{6em}$ | $\\hspace{6em}$ Y $\\hspace{6em}$ |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Temperature in Celsius | Pressure |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Temperature in Kelvin | Pressure |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Pressure | Temperature in Celsius |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Pressure | Temperature in Kelvin |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nFrom the ideal gas law, \n\n$pV = nRT$ therefore, \n\n- $T = \\dfrac{V}{nR}P$\n\nThis equation would lead to a graph of $T$ vs $P$ that passes through the origin, however the given graph does not. This can be accounted for by a conversion from absolute temperature to Celsius temperature through a shift of 273.\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Alternatively}}$ \\\n\n A graph of pressure against temperature will give a straight line.\n\nThe vertical (X) axis has negative values. This means it must be temperature in Celsius, as neither pressure nor Kelvin temperature can have a negative value. Thus, the horizontal (Y) axis is pressure and the correct answer is A."},{"ref":"PH0221","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0221\n\nAn ideal gas is in a fixed volume container which sits on an electronic balance. More of the same gas is added to the container. Which of the following will not change?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The separation between the gas molecules\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The collision frequency of the gas molecules with each other\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ The average size of the molecules\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The reading on the electronic balance","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nDue to the fixed volume, the separation between the gas molecules decreases. According to the ideal gas law, the pressure of the gas will increase and so does the collision frequency. The mass of the container will also be greater with the addition of more gas in the container. The only constant quantity is the average size of the molecules. It is dependent on molecular structure only. Therefore the answer is C."},{"ref":"PH0267","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0267\n\nA cylinder of fixed volume contains $n$ moles of an ideal gas at constant temperature. An additional $n$ moles of the same gas is forced into the cylinder via a one-way valve without changing the temperature. Which of the following best explains the final pressure in the cylinder?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Pressure decreases because more particles collide with each other allowing fewer particles to reach the walls of the cylinder.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Pressure decreases because the total kinetic energy of the particles is now distributed over a larger number of particles.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Pressure increases because more particles are now available to collide with the walls of the cylinder.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Pressure increases because the average speed of the particles increases.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nGas pressure is caused by the particles colliding with the walls, applying a force to them. It is related to the rate of particle-wall collisions per unit surface area of the container per unit time.\n\nA is incorrect because with an increased number of particles more particle-wall collisions will occur and that will lead to a greater rate of collision per unit surface area of cylinder per unit time since there will be no change in the speed of the particles.\n\nB is incorrect because actually the additional particles bring in more kinetic energy so the existing kinetic energy is not being distributed over a larger number of particles. Additionally, since the temperature is being kept constant, the average kinetic energy is constant.\n\nC is correct because there are now more particles in the same volume, the rate of collision per unit surface area of the cylinder per unit time will increase.\n\nD is incorrect because there is no change in the average speed of the particles due to the temperature remaining the same. Temperature is related to the average kinetic energy of the particles, and the particle kinetic energy depends on the speed of the particles."},{"ref":"PH0274","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0274\n\nAn inflated balloon containing an ideal gas at temperature $T$ in degrees Celsius floats in a room where the atmospheric pressure is $P$. The volume remains constant at $V$. If the balloon contains $M$ moles of the gas, which of the following is true?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $M = \\dfrac{PV}{R(T+273)}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $M = \\dfrac{R(T+273)}{PV}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $M = \\dfrac{k_b P}{RV}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $M = \\dfrac{PV}{k_b (T-273)}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nThe question gives $T$ in celsius. In kelvin, the temperature would be $T + 273$. \n\nUsing the equation $PV=nRT$, we get number of moles = $\\dfrac{PV}{RT}$. Since $M$ represents the number of moles, it will substitute into the equation for $n$ and $(T + 273)$ will substitute as temperature in the equation giving\n\n$M=\\dfrac{PV}{R(T+273)}$ which is answer A"},{"ref":"PH0278","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0278\n\nA student is using the equation $pV = nRT$ to solve for $n$ with the following percentage uncertainties in the measurements. \n\n| $\\hspace{1em}$ Quantity $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Uncertainty $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n| :-: | :-: |\n\n| P | $\\pm\\,2\\,\\%$ |\n\n| V | $\\pm\\,6\\,\\%$ |\n\n| T | $\\pm\\,1\\,\\%$ |\n\nWhat is the percentage uncertainty in the calculated value of n?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 3 %\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 7 %\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 9 %\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 12 %","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nTo calculate $n$, the student would rearrange the formula to get\n\n- $n=\\dfrac{PV}{RT}$\n\nIn both operations, the percentage uncertainty must be added, therefore the correct answer is C (2 \\% + 6 \\% + 1 \\% = 9 \\%)"},{"ref":"PH0287","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0287\n\nAn ideal gas is held in a container of volume $V$ and at pressure $P$. Gas is pumped into the container so that the number of molecules doubles and heat is added so that the average kinetic energy of the gas also doubles. The volume is adjusted so that the pressure remains constant. What is the new volume of the container?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em} 8 V$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em} 4 V$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em} 2 V$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $V$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nThe average kinetic energy doubles which means that the temperature doubles. \n\nTo maintain constant pressure, volume would have to double. \n\nSince the number of molecules also doubles, volume would have to double again to maintain constant pressure. \n\nThus the volume must get 4 times larger from $pV=nRT$"},{"ref":"PH0478","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0478\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\n1. Define pressure. [1 mark]\n\n2. An ideal gas is in a container of volume $2.5 \\times 10^4\\,\\ce{cm^3}$. The pressure of the gas is $3.0 \\times 10^4$ Pa at a temperature of $127\\degree$ C.\n\n    1. Determine how many molecules there are in the gas. [3 marks]\n\n    2. Estimate the total kinetic energy of the gas molecules. [2 marks]\n\n3. The gas is heated at a constant rate  to reach a temperature of 800 K. The change in the volume of the container is negligible. \n\n    1. Determine the pressure of the gas at 800 K. [2 marks]\n\n    2.     On the graph below, sketch how the mean square speed $\\overline{c^2}$ of the gas molecules varies with temperature $T$ during the heating process. [2 marks]\n\n        (there is no need to add values on the axis)\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/PH0478.png\" alt=\"PH0478\">","markScheme":"1. $\\ll$ Perpendicular $\\gg$ force per unit area $\\checkmark$\n\n2. \n\n    1. The temperature of the ideal gas in Kelvin / $T=273+127=400$ K $\\checkmark$\n\n        Usage of $n=\\dfrac{pV}{RT}=\\ll \\dfrac{(3.0\\, \\times\\, 10^4)(2.5\\, \\times\\, 10^{-2})}{(8.31)(400)}=\\gg 0.23$ mol $\\checkmark$\n\n        Number of molecules / $N=\\ll nN_A=(0.226)(6.02\\times10^{23})=\\gg$ :answer[$1.4\\times 10^{23}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The temperature of the ideal gas in Kelvin;\n\n        - $T=273+127=400$ K\n\n        The volume of the gas must be converted to m$^3$;\n\n        - $V=(2.5 \\times 10^4)(10^{-6})=2.5 \\times 10^{-2}$\n\n        By using the ideal gas equation, the number of mole n of the ideal gas ; \n\n        - $n=\\dfrac{pV}{RT}= \\dfrac{(3.0\\, \\times \\,10^4)(2.5\\, \\times\\, 10^{-2})}{(8.31)(400)}= 0.226$ mol\n\n        Number of molecules $N$;\n\n        - $N=nN_A=(0.226)(6.02 \\times 10^{23})=1.36 \\times 10^{23}$\n\n    2. ::indent\n\n        - $E_{k(total)}=U$\n\n        - $U= \\dfrac{3}{2}Nk_bT=\\dfrac{3}{2}(1.36 \\times 10^{23})(1.38 \\times 10^{-23})(400) \\checkmark $\n\n        - $\\hspace{1em} =1100\\ \\ce{J}\\ \\checkmark$\n\n        \n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The total kinetic energy $\\overline{E}_k$ per of the molecules of an ideal gas is equal to the internal energy $U$;\n\n        - $U= \\dfrac{3}{2}Nk_bT$\n\n        \n\n        Substituting all of the known values gives\n\n        \n\n        - $U=\\dfrac{3}{2}(1.36 \\times 10^{23})(1.38 \\times 10^{-23})(400)  $\n\n        - $\\hspace{1em} =1100\\ \\ce{J}\\ $\n\n3. \n\n    1. Usage of $P_f=\\dfrac{(p_i)(T_f)}{T_i}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\ll\\dfrac{(3.0\\, \\times \\,10^4)(800)}{400}=\\gg$ :answer[$6.0 \\times 10^4\\ll \\ce{Pa}\\gg$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The pressure and the temperature of an ideal gas are directly proportional to each other;\n\n        $\\dfrac{p}{T}$ = constant\n\n        So the following relationship can be used for initial and final pressures;\n\n        $\\dfrac{p_i}{T_i} = \\dfrac{p_f}{T_f}$\n\n        By rearranging the equation;\n\n        $P_f=\\dfrac{(p_i)(T_f)}{T_i} = \\dfrac{(3.0 \\,\\times \\,10^4)(800)}{400}=6.0 \\times 10^4$ Pa\n\n    2. $\\hspace{1em}$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/PH0478-1.png\" alt=\"PH0478-1\">\n\n        A straight line whose extension should pass through the origin $\\checkmark$\n\n        The line should be between 400-800 $K$ on the x-axis $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The average kinetic energy $\\overline{E}_k$ per molecule of a gas;\n\n        $\\overline{E}_k=\\frac{1}{2} m\\overline{c^2} =\\frac{3}{2} k_b T \\hspace{2em}$ where $\\frac{3}{2} k_b$ = constant and $\\frac{1}{2} m$ = constant\n\n        So ;\n\n        $\\overline{c^2} \\propto T$\n\n        This relationship can be represented with a straight line whose extension should pass through the origin. Additionally, the temperature is changed from 400 K to 800 K. Therefore, the line should be between these values on the x-axis."},{"ref":"PH0479","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0479\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n1. Distinguish between an ideal gas and a non-ideal gas with reference to the internal energy of the molecules. [2 marks]\n\n2. An ideal gas of mass 20 g is heated in a sealed container. The temperature of the gas is increased from $127\\degree$ C to $327\\degree$ C. The specific heat capacity of the gas at constant volume is $2.5 \\times 10^4\\,\\ce{J\\,kg^{-1}\\,K^{-1}}$.\n\n    1. Calculate the increase in total kinetic energy of the molecules of the ideal gas. [3 marks]\n\n    2. The average speeds of the molecules at $127\\degree$ C and $327\\degree$ C  are $v_i$ and $v_f$ respectively. Calculate $\\dfrac{v_i}{v_f}$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Internal energy of the ideal gas = total kinetic energy of the molecules $\\checkmark$\n\n    Internal energy of the non-ideal gas = total kinetic energy of the molecules + total potential energy of the molecules $\\checkmark$\n\n2. \n\n    1. Mention of heat supplied = increase in internal energy = increase in total kinetic energy of the molecules of the ideal gas $\\checkmark$\n\n        Usage of $Q=mc\\Delta T$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\ll 20 \\times 10^{-3}\\times 2.5\\times 10^4\\times 200=\\gg$ :answer[$1.0\\times 10^5\\, \\ce{J}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The change in temperature ∆T;\n\n        $\\Delta T=327-127=200\\degree$ C\n\n        The heat supplied to the gas;\n\n        $Q=mc\\Delta T=(20\\times 10^{-3})(2.5\\times 10^4)(200)=1.0\\times 10^5$ J\n\n        Since internal energy increase will increase only the kinetic energy of the ideal gas. \n\n        The increase in total kinetic energy of the ideal gas is $1.0\\times 10^5$ J\n\n    \n\n    2. $127°C=\\ll 127+273=\\gg 400$ K \n\n        $327°C=\\ll 327+273=\\gg 600$ K $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\overline{\\ll E}_k=\\dfrac{1}{2} m\\overline{v^2}=\\dfrac{3}{2} k_b T\\gg$    \n\n        $\\overline{v} \\propto \\sqrt{T}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\dfrac{v_i}{v_f} =\\ll \\dfrac{\\sqrt{400}}{\\sqrt{600}} = \\gg $ :answer[0.82] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The temperatures are in Kelvin;\n\n        $127\\degree \\ce{C}=127+273=400$ K\n\n        $327\\degree \\ce{C}=327+273=600$ K\n\n        The average kinetic energy $\\overline{E}_k$ per molecule of a gas;\n\n        $\\overline{E}_k=\\dfrac{1}{2} m\\overline{v^2}=\\dfrac{3}{2} k_b T \\hspace{2em}$ where $\\dfrac{3}{2} k_b$ = constant and $\\frac{1}{2} m$ = constant\n\n        So;\n\n        $\\overline{v^2} \\propto T = \\overline{v}\\propto \\sqrt{T}$\n\n        \n\n        According to this relationship;\n\n        $\\dfrac{\\overline{v}}{\\sqrt{T}}$ = constant\n\n        When the given values are substituted;\n\n        $\\dfrac{v_i}{\\sqrt{400}}$ = constant = $\\dfrac{v_f}{\\sqrt{600}}$\n\n        $\\dfrac{v_i}{v_f} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{400}}{\\sqrt{600}}=0.82$"},{"ref":"PH0480","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0480\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n1. Define one mole. [2 marks]\n\n2. An ideal gas is heated in a closed container of volume 2.5 m$^3$. The variation of the pressure of the gas $p$ with temperature $T$ is shown on the graph below. \n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/PH0480.png\" alt=\"PH0480\">\n\n    1. Determine the amount of gas, in mol, in the closed container. [2 marks]\n\n    2. State and explain how the gradient of the graph would change if the amount of gas in the container was increased. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Amount of a substance $\\checkmark$\n\n    That contains a number of particles equal to Avogadro's number/the same number of particles as 12 g of Carbon-12 $\\checkmark$\n\n2. \n\n    1. Usage of $n=\\dfrac{pV}{RT}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $n=\\ll\\dfrac{(3.0\\,\\times \\,10^3)(2.5)}{(8.31)(360)}=\\gg$ :answer[$2.5$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        By using the ideal gas law, the number of moles n can be found as;\n\n        $n=\\dfrac{pV}{RT}$\n\n        Any point on the graph can be employed to substitute for p and V.\n\n        $n=\\dfrac{(3.0\\,\\times\\, 10^3)(2.5)}{(8.31)(360)}= 2.5$\n\n    \n\n    2. Greater/steeper $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\dfrac{p}{T}=\\dfrac{nR}{V}$\n\n        $\\dfrac{p}{T}$ = gradient of the graph and $\\dfrac{R}{V}$ = constant\n\n        $\\ll$ Therefore; $\\gg$\n\n        Gradient $\\propto n$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The ideal gas law;\n\n        - $pV=nRT$\n\n        When it is rearranged;\n\n        - $\\dfrac{p}{T}=\\dfrac{nR}{V}$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{p}{T}$ = gradient of the graph and $\\dfrac{R}{V}$ = constant\n\n        Therefore;\n\n        - Gradient $\\propto n$\n\n        An increase in the number of moles increases the gradient of the graph."},{"ref":"PH0514","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH0514\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\n1. A student investigates the variation of gas pressure with volume at a constant temperature. Air is trapped inside a gas syringe with a constant cross-sectional area of $A$. The plunger of the gas syringe is tied to a mass holder. The student measures the volume $V$ of the trapped gas for different hanging masses with the external constant air pressure, $P_A$, remaining constant.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%201.17.05%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 1.17.05 PM\">\n    $\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with chemix - https:// chemix.org/]}}$\n\n    1.     After each increase in mass, the student waits for some time to measure the volume of the gas. Outline why this is necessary.  [1 mark]\n\n        The recorded data is plotted on a graph:\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/Screenshot%202026-02-23%20at%207.16.06%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-02-23 at 7.16.06 AM\">\n\n    2. Draw the line of best fit for the plotted data. [1 mark]\n\n    3.     Determine the gradient of the graph. [2 marks]\n\n2. Theory suggests that\n\n    - $(P_A-\\dfrac{mg}{A})V=k$;\n\n    where *m* is the total mass of the plunger and hanging masses, *g* is the acceleration of free fall and *k* is a constant.\n\n    1.     Determine the units of *k* in fundamental SI units. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Calculate the percentage uncertainty of the volume *V* when the mass *m* is 700 g. [2 marks]\n\n3. In a particular experiment, the researcher measures\n\n    - $m=0.700\\pm0.005\\ \\ce{kg}$, \\\n $A=0.00010\\pm0.00001{\\ \\ce{m}}^2$ and\\\n $g=9.81\\pm0.01\\ \\ce{m s}^{-2}$.\n\n Calculate the absolute uncertainty of $\\dfrac{mg}{A}$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. To keep the temperature constant\n\n        **OR**\n\n        To allow the system to return to thermal equilibrium $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Temperature is one of the control variables in the experiment. It must be kept constant. During the motion of the plunger, the gas temperature will decrease when the gas expands. Therefore, time is needed for the gas to reach room temperature again.\n\n    2. A straight line passes through within the error bars and has roughly equal numbers of data points on each side $\\checkmark$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/Screenshot%202026-02-23%20at%207.14.31%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-02-23 at 7.14.31 AM\">\n\n    3. Calculation of gradient using a minimum mass range of 400 g. $\\checkmark$\n\n        Calculated gradient between $1.8\\times{10}^{-3}{\\ \\ce{cm}}^3\\,\\ce{g}^{-1}$ and $0.7{\\times10}^{-3}{\\ \\ce{cm}}^3\\ce{g}^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$ (units must be included for MP2)\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Gradient of the graph = $\\dfrac{2.9-2.0}{700-0}=1.29\\times{10}^{-3}{\\, \\ce{cm}}^3\\,\\ce{g}^{-1}$\n\n2.\n    1. The unit of $P_A=\\ll\\dfrac{\\ce{kg m s}^{-2}}{\\ce{m}^2}=\\gg\\ \\ce{kg}\\,\\ce{m}^{-1}\\ce{s}^{-2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        The fundamental SI units of $k=  \\ll\\ (\\ce{kg}\\,\\ce{m}^{-1}\\ce{s}^{-2})(\\ce{m}^3) \\gg =\\ \\ce{kg}\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}\\ce{m}^2$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The unit of atmospheric pressure $P_A$ is Pascals. The formula for pressure is\n\n        - $P = \\dfrac{F}{A}$ and also $F=ma$.\n\n        Pascals can then be expressed in SI fundamental units as follows;\n\n        - The unit of $P_A=\\dfrac{\\ce{kg}\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}}{\\ce{m}^2}=\\ce{kg}\\,\\ce{m}^{-1}\\ce{s}^{-2}$\n\n        - The unit of $\\dfrac{mg}{A}$  is the same as the unit of $P_A$\n\n        SI fundamental units of $k$ is therefore pressure units multiplied by volume units:\n\n        - $=\\ (\\ce{kg}\\,\\ce{m}^{-1}\\ce{s}^{-2})(\\ce{m}^3)  =\\ \\ce{kg}\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}\\ce{m}^2$\n\n    2. ::indent\n        - $V=2.9\\pm0.2\\ {\\ce{cm}}^3$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta V}{V} \\times 100 \\% = \\dfrac{0.2}{2.9} \\times 100\\% = $ :answer[$6.9\\%$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nThe volume of the trapped gas  *V* when $m=700$ g;\n\n        - $V=2.9\\pm0.2\\ \\ce{cm}^3$\n\n        Therefore, percentage uncertainty;\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta V}{V} \\times 100 \\% = \\dfrac{0.2}{2.9} \\times 100\\% = 6.9\\%$\n\n3. Correct calculation of individual percentage uncertainties $\\checkmark$\n\n    - for mass = $\\dfrac{\\Delta m}{m} \\times 100 \\% = \\dfrac{0.005}{0.700} \\times 100 \\% = 0.7\\%$\n\n    - for area = $\\dfrac{\\Delta A}{A} \\times 100 \\% = \\dfrac{0.00001}{0.00010} \\times 100 \\% = 10\\%$\n\n    - for gravity = $\\dfrac{\\Delta g}{g} \\times 100 \\% = \\dfrac{0.01}{9.81} \\times 100\\% = 0.1\\%$\n\n    The percentage uncertainty of $\\dfrac{mg}{A} = \\ \\ll \\dfrac{\\Delta m}{m}  + \\dfrac{\\Delta g}{g}  + \\dfrac{\\Delta A}{A}  = 0.7\\% + 0.1\\% + 10\\% \\gg \\ = 10.8\\%$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $\\dfrac{mg}{A}=\\dfrac{(0.700)(9.81)}{0.00010}=68670\\ $Pa\n\n    - $\\Delta (\\dfrac{mg}{A}) = \\dfrac{(0.700)(9.81)}{0.00010} \\times \\dfrac{10.8}{100} = 7416 = $ :answer[$7000 \\ \\ce{Pa}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n    - For $\\dfrac{mg}{A}$ with its absolute uncertainty:\n\n    - $\\dfrac{mg}{A} \\pm \\Delta(\\dfrac{mg}{A}) = \\dfrac{(0.700)(9.81)}{0.00010} \\pm \\dfrac{(0.700)(9.81)}{0.00010} \\times \\dfrac{10.8}{100} = 68670 \\pm 7416$\n\n    With correct rounding of the uncertainty to 1 significant figure and matching precision in the calculated value, it would be $69000 \\pm 7000$ Pa"},{"ref":"PH0521","marks":"11","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH0521\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nA student investigates gas behaviour. A volume of gas is trapped in a capillary tube by a thin layer of oil. The tube, a thermometer and a ruler are immersed in water.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/Screenshot%202026-08-20%20at%2010.06.30%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-08-20 at 10.06.30 AM\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with chemix - https:// chemix.org]}}$\n\nThe student slowly added boiling water and recorded the values of the temperature *T* of the water and the height *h* of the gas trapped inside the capillary tube. The recorded data is plotted on a graph as shown.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%204.03.23%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 4.03.23 PM\">\n\n1. Draw the best fit line for the plotted data. [1 mark]\n\n2. The theory suggests that\n\n    - $h=mT+C$\n\n    where *m* and *C* are constant. Temperature *T* is in $\\degree$C.\n\n    1. Suggest whether the data are consistent with the theory. [2 marks]\n\n    2. By using the graph drawn in (a), determine the constants *m* and *C*. [4 marks]\n\n    3. State the units of the gradient in fundamental SI units. [1 mark]\n\n3. Following the experiment, the student tested their thermometer in an ice bath and in boiling water. The temperatures were 2$\\degree$C and 102$\\degree$C respectively.\n\n    1. Explain how this finding will affect how the student interprets their results. [2 marks]\n\n    2. The student also goes on to find the intercept on the temperature axis. State whether this error causes their intercept value to be higher or lower than expected. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. A straight line passes through the error bars $\\checkmark$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%204.03.38%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 4.03.38 PM\">\\\n\n2.\n    1. The relationship $h = mT + C$ suggests a straight line on the graph $\\checkmark$\n\n        A straight line can be drawn that passes through all of the uncertainty bars (therefore consistent) $\\checkmark$\n\n    2. Any evidence of calculation of gradient $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $m=\\ll\\dfrac{11.80\\ -10.64\\ }{95\\ -60}=\\gg 0.033 \\ll$ cm C$^{-1}$  $\\gg \\checkmark$\\\n        (answers between 0.030 and 0.036 accepted)\n\n        Any evidence of substitution of correct data in the equation $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $C=8.65 \\ll $C $\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\\\n        (answers between 8.60 and 8.70 accepted)\n\n         $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The equation suggests that *m* is the gradient of the graph and *C* is the y-intercept.\n\n        $m=gradient\\ of\\ the\\ graph=\\dfrac{11.80\\ -10.64\\ }{95\\ -60}=0.033$\n\n        When any data is substituted in the equation with the value of *m*;\n\n        - For $h = 10.8$ and $T = 65$;\n\n        - $10.8=0.033\\times65+C$\n\n        - $C=10.8-0.033\\times65=8.65$\n\n    3. SI unit of  $\\ce{m} \\ = \\ \\ll \\dfrac{\\ce{m}}{\\degree \\ce{C}}\\ \\gg = \\ce{m}{ \\ \\ce{K}^{-1}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    When the equation is rearranged;\n\n        $m=\\dfrac{h-C}{T}$\n\n        Substitute SI units of the quantities;\n\n        SI unit of $m=\\dfrac{m}{\\degree C}=\\ce{mK}^{-1}$\n\n3.\n    1. There is a systematic error ($\\checkmark$) where all readings are 2$\\degree$C too high ($\\checkmark$).\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The ice water reading should be 0$\\degree$C and the boiling water 100$\\degree$C. This indicated that the thermometer has a zero error of 2$\\degree$C. As all data points are likely affected by this fixed amount, it is a systematic error.\n\n    2. As all points have been shifted to the right, the temperature intercept reading will be higher than expected. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The graph could be corrected by shifting data points to the left. This will shift the line of best fit to the left, decreasing the value for the temperature intercept."},{"ref":"PH0620","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0620\n\nSystems A and B both consist of an ideal gas at initial temperature $T$. In System A, the temperature of the gas is increased. In System B, gas molecules are added with the temperature of the system remaining constant.\n\nWhich of these statements about the total intermolecular potential energy of the two systems is correct?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | System A | System B |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\hspace{1.5em}$Potential energy increases$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |$\\hspace{1.5em}$Potential energy increases $\\hspace{1.5em}$|\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Potential energy is constant | Potential energy increases |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Potential energy is constant |Potential energy is constant |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Potential energy increases | Potential energy is constant |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nIdeal gases have no intermolecular forces between the particles, and therefore all of their internal energy consists of kinetic energy. There is no intermolecular potential energy. In both cases, the described changes will not affect the potential energy, as the potential energy is always equal to zero."},{"ref":"PH0645","marks":"11","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0645\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\n1. Outline how a gas exerts pressure on a surface.  [3 marks]\n\n2.  1. A balloon containing helium gas is released from the ground. The pressure exerted by the helium is approximately equal to the pressure exerted by surrounding air. The balloon expands at constant temperature as it rises.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/PH0645.png\" alt=\"PH0645\">\n        $\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with chemix - https://chemix.org/]}}$\n\n        The balloon contains 16 g of helium (molar mass $4.0\\, \\ce{g\\, mol^{-1}}$), has a temperature $T$ of 300 K and a pressure $P_0$ of 100 kPa at ground level.\n\n        Calculate the volume $V_0$ of the balloon at ground level. [2 marks]\n\n    2. At heights much less than 10 km, the pressure $P$ inside the balloon is      related to its height $h$ (in m) by the approximation\n\n        $P\\approx P_0\\left(1-\\dfrac{h}{10\\ \\ce{km}}\\right)$\n\n        The balloon ascends until its volume is 5% higher than the volume at ground level.\n\n        Estimate the new height of the balloon. [3 marks]\n\n    3.  The same balloon at 100 kPa is placed inside a sealed, rigid container of volume $3V_0$ and a temperature of 300 K. A pump is used to remove air from the container. When the air remaining in the container consists of half as many molecules as the number of molecules inside the balloon, the balloon bursts.\n\n        Determine the pressure inside the container at the moment the balloon bursts. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  Gas molecules colliding with a surface experience a change in momentum. $\\checkmark$\n\n    The change in momentum is caused by a force from the walls of the balloon. $\\checkmark$\n\n    By Newton's 3rd law, the particles also exert a force on the walls of the balloon, resulting in pressure. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The kinetic theory of gases models gas molecules as hard spheres of negligible volume that undergo elastic collisions, resulting in a pressure on any surface that is in contact with the gas.\n\n2.  1.  $n=\\dfrac{Mass}{Molar\\ mass}=\\dfrac{16\\ \\ce{g}}{4\\ \\ce{g}\\ \\ce{{mol}}^{-1}}=4\\ \\ce{mol}\\ $ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $V=\\dfrac{nRT}{P}=\\dfrac{(4\\ \\ce{mol})(8.31\\, \\ce{J}\\, \\ce{K^{-1}}\\ \\ce{{mol}^{-1}})(300\\ \\ce{K})}{{10}^5\\, \\ce{Pa}}=$ :answer[$0.10\\ \\ce{m}^3$]   $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The molar quantity $n$ of helium is given by\n\n        -  $n=\\dfrac{Mass}{Molar\\ mass}=\\dfrac{16\\ \\ce{g}}{4\\ \\ce{g}\\ \\ce{{mol}}^{-1}}=4$ mol\n\n        Substituting this value into the ideal gas law yields\n\n        $V_0=\\dfrac{nRT}{P_0}=\\dfrac{(4\\ \\ce{mol})(8.31\\ \\ce{J}\\ \\ce{K^{-1}}\\ \\ce{{mol}}^{-1})(300\\ \\ce{K})}{{10}^5\\ \\ce{Pa}}=0.10\\ \\ce{m}^3$\n\n    2.  ::indent\n        - $\\dfrac{V}{V_0}=\\dfrac{P_0}{P}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $1-\\dfrac{h}{10000\\ \\ce{m}}=\\dfrac{1}{1.05}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $h=$ :answer[$476\\, \\ce{m}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n         From the ideal gas law it follows that when the number of moles $n$ and temperature remain constant,\n\n        - $PV$ = constant\n\n        - $PV=P_0V_0$\n        - $\\dfrac{V}{V_0}=\\dfrac{P_0}{P}$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{P_0}{P}=\\dfrac{V}{V_0}=\\ \\dfrac{1}{\\left(1-\\dfrac{h}{10000}\\right)}$\n\n        Therefore\n\n        - $V_0=V\\left(1-\\dfrac{h}{10000}\\right)$\n\n        And from the given information\n\n        - $\\dfrac{V_0}{V}=\\dfrac{1}{1.05}$\n\n        - $1-\\dfrac{h}{10000\\ \\ce{m}}=\\dfrac{1}{1.05}\\ $\n\n        - $h=(10000)\\dfrac{0.05}{1.05}=\\dfrac{500}{1.05}=476$ m\n\n3.  ::indent\n    - $P_{final}=\\dfrac{n_{total}RT}{3V_0}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $n_{total}=\\dfrac{1}{RT}\\left(P_0V_0+\\dfrac{P_0V_0}{2}\\right)=\\dfrac{3P_0V_0}{2RT}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $P_{final}=\\dfrac{P_0}{2}=$ :answer[$50\\, \\ce{\\ce{k}Pa}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Calculate the final pressure by first determining the moles of gas present in the container.\n\n    The number of moles of gas in the balloon at the start is given by\n\n    - $n_b=\\dfrac{p_{0\\ }V_0}{RT}$\n\n    It is also known that the number of moles of gas that surround the balloon when it bursts is half the number of moles of gas inside the balloon\n\n    - $n_{c\\ }=\\dfrac{1}{2}n_b=\\dfrac{p_{0}V_0}{2RT}$\n\n    Giving\n\n    - $n_{total}=n_b+n_{c\\ }=\\dfrac{3P_0V_0}{2RT}$\n\n    It is known that the total volume of the gas in the container is $3V_0$, therefore\n\n    - $P_{final}=\\dfrac{n_{total}RT}{3V_0}$\n\n    - $=(\\dfrac{3P_0V_0}{2RT})(\\dfrac{RT}{3V_0})$\n\n    - $=\\dfrac{P_0}{2}=\\ 50$ kPa"},{"ref":"PH0647","marks":"12","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0647\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\nWater in a boiling flask is heated from 20$\\degree$C by a constant input power $P_{in}$. The resulting steam exits the flask via a tube with an internal cross-sectional area of $30\\,\\ce{cm}^2$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/PH0647a.png\" alt=\"PH0647a\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with chemix - https://chemix.org/]}}$\n\n1. Discuss the changes that take place in the kinetic and potential energies of the water as it boils. [2 marks]\n\n2. Estimate the mean velocity of water molecules (mass $18.0u$) in the steam. Assume that steam behaves as an ideal gas. [2 marks]\n\n3.\n    1. The burner is adjusted so that $P_{in}$ equals 5.0 kW. The steam pressure is approximately 100 kPa.\n\n        Determine the average density $\\rho$ of the steam. [3 marks]\n\n    2. Show that the velocity $v$ of the steam through the outlet tube can be expressed as\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $v=\\dfrac{1}{A \\rho}\\ \\dfrac{\\Delta m}{\\Delta t}$\n\n        where $A$ is the cross-sectional area of the outlet tube and $\\dfrac{\\Delta m}{\\Delta t}$ is the mass flow rate of the steam. [2 marks]\n\n    3. The specific latent heat of vaporization is $2.3 \\ \\ce{MJ \\ kg^{-1}}$.\n\n        Calculate the average flow speed of the steam as it exits the outlet tube. [4 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    Kinetic energy of the water molecules stays constant during boiling $\\checkmark$\n\n    Potential energy increases as water changes from liquid to gas $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The kinetic energy of water molecules is directly proportional to the water temperature, which remains constant during boiling as all the energy supplied to the water is converted to an increase in intermolecular potential energy.\n\n    The intermolecular potential energy increases during boiling due to the increased mean distance between water molecules, where the energy is being used to overcome intermolecular forces.\n\n2.  $\\hspace{1em} v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3RT}{m}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $v = $ :answer[$719\\ \\ce{m\\, s^{-1}}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nSetting the molar translational kinetic energy of the water equal to the molar thermal energy of translational modes yields\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2=\\dfrac{3}{2}k_BT$\n\n    where $m$ is the mass, $v$ is velocity, $T$ is temperature (373 K) and $k_B$ is Boltzmann’s constant. Rearranging this equation gives\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3k_BT}{m}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3\\left(1.38 \\times {10}^{-23}\\ \\ce{J\\, K^{-1}}\\right)\\left(373\\ \\ce{K}\\right)}{18.0\\left(1.661 \\times {10}^{-27}\\, \\ce{kg}\\right)}}=719\\,\\ce{m\\, s^{-1}}$\n\n3.\n    1.  $\\hspace{1em} \\rho=\\dfrac{m}{V}$ and $n=1$ $m = 18$  g $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $V\\ =\\dfrac{nRT}{p}$\n\n        **OR**\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\rho=\\dfrac{p}{nRT}(m)\\ =\\ \\dfrac{(100\\ 000)(18)}{(1)(8.314)(373)}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Giving\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\rho=$ :answer[$581\\ \\ce{g\\ m^{-3}}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$\n\n        Density = $\\dfrac{mass}{volume}$\n\n        We know from the fact that one molecule has a mass of 18 $u$ that the mass of one mole of steam is 18 $g$\n\n        Using the ideal gas law\n$pV\\ =\\ nRT$\n\n        We can solve for volume to get\n$V=\\dfrac{nRT}{p}$\n\n        Solving for density we get\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\rho=\\dfrac{m}{V}=\\dfrac{m}{\\dfrac{nRT}{p}}=\\dfrac{mp}{nRT}=\\dfrac{(18)(100000)}{(1)(8.314)(373)}=581\\ \\ce{g\\ m^{-3}}$\n\n        *Alternative solution*\n\n        The density $\\rho$ of the steam can be calculated using the ideal gas law:\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\rho=M_r\\dfrac{n}{V}=M_r\\dfrac{p}{RT}$\n\n        where $M_r$ is the molar mass of water, $R$ is the molar gas constant, $p$ is pressure and $T$ is temperature.\n\n        Substituting the given values into this expression for density yields\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\rho=\\dfrac{\\left(18.0\\ \\ce{g\\ {mol}}^{-1}\\right)\\left({10}^5\\ \\ce{Pa}\\right)\\ }{\\left(8.31\\ \\ce{J\\ {K^{-1}}\\ mol}^{-1}\\right)\\left(373\\ \\ce{K}\\right)}=581\\ \\ce{g\\ m^{-3}}$\n\n    2. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{\\Delta V}{\\Delta t} = \\dfrac{1}{\\rho} \\dfrac{\\Delta m}{\\Delta t}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{\\Delta V}{\\Delta t} = A \\ v$\n$\\checkmark \\hspace{1em} $\n giving$\\hspace{1em}$ $v=\\dfrac{1}{A\\rho}\\dfrac{\\Delta m}{\\Delta t}$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        In a given time interval $\\Delta t$, the volume $\\Delta V$ of steam that flows through the outlet tube is equal to the cross-sectional area $A$ of the tube and the distance $x$ covered by the steam in that time. As $x$ is given by:\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $x = v \\Delta t$\n\n        and\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\Delta V = Ax$\n\n        it follows that\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{\\Delta V}{\\Delta t} = Av$\n\n        Density $\\rho$ is defined as the mass per unit volume; hence, volume flow rate can also be expressed as\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{\\Delta V}{\\Delta t} = \\dfrac{1}{\\rho} \\dfrac{\\Delta m}{\\Delta t}$\n\n        Combining these expressions yields\n$v=\\dfrac{1}{A\\rho}\\dfrac{\\Delta m}{\\Delta t}$\n\n    3. ::indent\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta m}{\\Delta t}=\\dfrac{P_{\\text{in}}}{L}=2.17 \\times 10^{-3}\\ \\text{kg s}^{-1}\\quad \\checkmark$\n\n        - $v=\\dfrac{1}{A\\rho}\\dfrac{\\Delta m}{\\Delta t}=\\dfrac{1}{(30 \\times 10^{-4}\\ \\text{m}^2)(581 \\times 10^{-3}\\ \\text{kg m}^{-3})}(2.17 \\times 10^{-3}\\ \\text{kg s}^{-1})\\quad \\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\hspace{1em}$ $v=$ :answer[$1.2\\, \\ce{m\\, s^{-1}}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n        Since the energy $E$ required to boil a mass $m$ of water with specific latent heat capacity $L$ is given by\n\n        - $E=mL$\n\n        And energy input can also be determined from\n\n        - $E=P t$\n\n        it follows that\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta m}{\\Delta t}=\\dfrac{P_{\\text{in}}}{L}$\n\n        Recalling the derived expression for $v$ where all variables are known\n\n        - $v=\\dfrac{1}{A\\rho}\\dfrac{\\Delta m}{\\Delta t}$\n\n        Substituting gives\n\n        - $v=\\dfrac{1}{A\\rho}\\dfrac{\\Delta m}{\\Delta t}=\\dfrac{1}{(30\\times10^{-4}\\ \\text{m}^2)(581\\times10^{-3}\\ \\text{kg m}^{-3})}(2.17\\times10^{-3}\\ \\text{kg s}^{-1})$\n\n        - $v=1.2\\ \\text{m s}^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH0729","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0729\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\n1. An astronaut is stranded in space 2.0 km from their space shuttle. Fortunately, the astronaut's oxygen tank is fitted with a valve that allows them to propel themselves forward by ejecting oxygen gas (molar mass $32.0\\, \\ce{g\\, mol^{-1}}$) at a velocity of $340\\, \\ce{m\\, s^{-1}}$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/729aaa.png\" alt=\"729aaa\">\n\n    The total initial mass of the astronaut and their equipment, including their oxygen tank, is 160 kg. The $0.040\\, \\ce{m^3}$ tank contains oxygen gas at an initial pressure of 500 kPa and a constant temperature of 298  K. The astronaut consumes oxygen at a constant rate of $0.60\\, \\ce{g\\, min^{-1}}$.\n\n    Show that the astronaut's breathing consumes approximately $14\\%$ of the initial oxygen supply every hour. [3 marks]\n\n2.\n    1. State how Newton's third law of motion results in pressure on the wall of the oxygen tank. [3 marks]\n\n    2. Based on the answer to part b(i), explain how the oxygen gas pressure causes the astronaut to accelerate as oxygen is ejected from the tank. [3 marks]\n\n3.\n    1. To return to the shuttle, the astronaut decides to eject $70\\%$ of their oxygen supply in a very short time interval.\n\n        Estimate the time required for the astronaut to reach the shuttle. Assume that there are no forces to oppose the astronaut's movement. [3 marks]\n\n    2. Determine whether the astronaut reaches the shuttle before running out of oxygen. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. $\\hspace{1em}$ $n_{breath}=60\\ \\ce{min}\\left(\\dfrac{0.60\\ \\ce{g\\ {min}}^{-1}}{32.0\\, \\ce{g\\, {mol}^{-1}}}\\right)=1.125\\ \\ce{mol}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $n_{tank}=\\dfrac{\\left(5\\times{10}^5\\ \\ce{Pa}\\right)\\left(0.040\\ \\ce{m^3}\\right)}{\\left(8.31\\ \\ce{J\\ {mol}}^{-1}{\\ \\ce{K}}^{-1}\\right)\\left(298\\ \\ce{K}\\right)}=8.076$ mol $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\%\\ consumed\\ per\\ hour=\\dfrac{n_{breath}}{n_{tank}}\\ \\times \\ 100\\%=\\dfrac{1.125}{8.076}\\ \\times \\ 100\\%=13.9\\%$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nIn one hour, the astronaut consumes $n_{breath}$ moles of gas, which can be expressed as\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $n_{breath}=60\\ \\ce{min}\\left(\\dfrac{0.60\\ \\ce{g\\ {min}}^{-1}}{32.0\\ \\ce{g\\ {mol}}^{-1}}\\right)=1.125$ mol\n\n    The number of moles in the tank, $n_{tank}$, is given by\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $n_{tank}=\\dfrac{\\left(5\\times{10}^5\\ \\ce{Pa}\\right)\\left(0.040\\ \\ce{m^3}\\right)}{\\left(8.31\\ \\ce{J\\ {mol}}^{-1}\\ce{{\\ K}}^{-1}\\right)\\left(298\\ \\ce{K}\\right)}=8.076$ mol\n\n    Finally, one may calculate the percentage of oxygen consumed in one hour:\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $P=100\\ \\times \\dfrac{n_{breath}}{n_{tank}}\\%\\ \\approx\\ 14\\%$\n\n2.\n    1. Gas molecules colliding with a surface experience a change in momentum $\\checkmark$\n\n        The change in momentum is caused by a force from the container wall $\\checkmark$\n\n        By Newton's 3$^{\\mathrm{rd}}$ law, the molecules also exert a force on the wall of the oxygen tank, resulting in pressure $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nThe kinetic theory of gases models gas molecules as hard spheres of negligible volume that undergo elastic collisions, resulting in a pressure on any surface that is in contact with the gas.\n\n    2. The momentum of escaping oxygen is caused by the force exerted by colliding oxygen molecules inside the tank $\\checkmark$\n\n        By Newton's 3$^{\\mathrm{rd}}$ law, an equal and opposite force acts on the astronaut $\\checkmark$\n\n        By Newton's 2$^{\\mathrm{nd}}$ law, the force acting on the astronaut causes forward acceleration $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nAs in part b(i), the elastic collisions between gas molecules yield a resultant force on the gas surroundings. When the valve is opened, molecules closest to the outlet of the tank experience a net force applied by colliding molecules approaching the outlet, causing them to accelerate out of the tank. This change in momentum yields an equal and opposite change in momentum of the tank and the astronaut (principle of conservation of momentum), which is a consequence of Newton's third law (the force on ejected oxygen produces an equal and opposite force on the stored oxygen) and Newton's second law (the acceleration of an object is given by the force applied to it divided by its mass).\n\n3.\n    1. $\\hspace{1em}$ $v=\\dfrac{m_{ejected}}{M-m_{ejected}}v_{O_2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ **OR**\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $v\\approx\\dfrac{m_{ejected}}{M}v_{O_2}$\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $t_{travel}=\\dfrac{x}{v}=x\\dfrac{M-m_{ejected}}{m_{ejected}v_{O_2}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ **OR**\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $t_{travel}=\\dfrac{x}{v}\\approx\\ x\\dfrac{M}{m_{ejected}v_{O_2}}$\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $t_{travel}=1.4$ hours $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nThrough conservation of momentum, the momentum of the astronaut and tank should be equal to the momentum of the ejected gas.\n\n        Therefore:\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\left(M-m_{ejected}\\right)v=m_{ejected}v_{O_2}$\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $v=\\dfrac{m_{ejected}}{M-m_{ejected}}v_{O_2}\\approx\\dfrac{m_{ejected}}{M}v_{O_2}$\n\n        where the final approximation follows from the fact that $m_{ejected}$ is much less than the initial mass of the astronaut (including the oxygen tank), $M$.\n\n        The oxygen tank initially contains a mass of oxygen gas given by\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $m_{O_2}=\\left(32.0\\ \\ce{g\\ {mol}}^{-1}\\right)n_{tank}=\\left(32.0\\ \\ce{g\\ {mol}}^{-1}\\right)\\left(8.076\\ \\ce{mol}\\right)=0.258$ kg\n\n        If the astronaut ejects $70\\%$ of the oxygen, their velocity becomes\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $v\\approx \\ 0.70\\ \\dfrac{m_{O_2}}{M}v_{O2}=0.70\\dfrac{0.258}{160}\\left(340\\ \\ce{m\\ s^{-1}}\\right)=0.384\\ \\ce{m\\ s^{-1}}$\n\n        Hence, the time required to reach the shuttle is given by\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $t_{travel}=\\dfrac{x}{v}=\\dfrac{2,000\\ \\ce{m}}{0.384\\ \\ce{m\\ s^{-1}}}\\approx5,200\\ \\ce{s}=1.4$ hours\n\n    2. $\\hspace{1em}$\n\n        $Moles consumed = (0.30)(8.076) = 2.42\\ \\checkmark$\n\n        $t_{breathe}= \\dfrac{8.076\\, \\ce{mol}}{1.125\\, \\ce{mol}\\, \\ce{h^{-1}}} ≈2.2$ hours\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $t_{breathe}>t_{travel}\\ \\left(astronaut\\ survives\\right)$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nAfter releasing $70\\%$ of the oxygen supply, the breathing time available to the astronaut is given by\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $t_{breath}=\\left(\\dfrac{100-70}{100}\\right)\\dfrac{n_{tank}}{n_{breath}}=0.30\\dfrac{8.076\\ \\ce{mol}}{1.125\\ \\ce{mol\\ {hour}}^{-1}}\\approx2.2$ hours\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\therefore\\ t_{breath}>t_{travel}$\n\n        As the travel time is less than the available breathing time, the astronaut survives.\n\n        *Allow full marks for correct calculation and conclusion based on the answer found in part c(i)*"},{"ref":"PH0750","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0750\n\nAn ideal gas piston of total volume $900\\ $cm$^3$ contains $600\\ $cm$^3$ of air and 300 cm$^3$ of water at a temperature of $27\\degree$ C. The system is cooled and the piston contracts at constant pressure.\n\nWhat is the approximate total piston volume at the point where the water first starts to freeze?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/PH0750aaaa.png\" alt=\"PH0750aaaa\">\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $273\\ $cm$^3$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $546\\ $cm$^3$\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $819\\ $cm$^3$\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $846\\ $cm$^3$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe initial temperature is $27\\degree $C = 300 K\n\nFrom the ideal gas law, the volume of gas at constant pressure obeys the relation\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{V}{T}=constant$\n\nHence, the final volume of the gas is given by\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $V_{final}=\\dfrac{T_{final}}{T_{initial}} \\times V_{initial}=\\dfrac{273}{300}(600\\ {\\ce{cm}}^3)=546\\ {\\ce{cm}}^3$\n\nAdding in the volume of the water yields a total volume of\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $V_{total}=V_{water}+V_{final}=846\\ {\\ce{cm}}^3$\n\nAnswer **A** is obtained by switching the initial water and gas volumes, then forgetting to add the water volume to the final gas volume.\n\nAnswer **B** results from calculating the final gas volume correctly but forgetting to add the water volume.\n\nAnswer **C** is yielded by inserting the total piston volume into the ideal gas equation rather than the gas volume only."},{"ref":"PH0751","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0751\n\nTwo cylindrical ideal gas pistons of equal initial volume and pressure are compressed at constant temperature by a force $F$. The cross-sectional area of Piston **A** is two times the cross-sectional area of Piston **B**.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/PH0751aaa.png\" alt=\"PH0751aaa\">\n\nWhich graph correctly shows how the inverse of the length $\\dfrac{1}{L}$ of each piston changes with increasing $F$?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    | $\\hspace{2cm}$  |B.   ||\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/PH0751-aaaa.png\" alt=\"PH0751-aaaa\"> |  |  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/PH0751-bb.png\" alt=\"PH0751-bb\">|\n|C. |   |   |D.  | |\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/PH0751-cc.png\" alt=\"PH0751-cc\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/PH0751-dd.png\" alt=\"PH0751-dd\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\\\nAs temperature is constant, the pressure $P$ for a given volume $V$ in each piston is given by\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $P=\\dfrac{P_0V_0}{V}=\\dfrac{P_0V_0}{AL}$\n\nwhere $A$ is the cross-sectional area of the piston, and $P_0$ and $V_0$ are the initial pressure and volume. The volume $V$ can be found from the product of the cross-sectional area $A$ and its length $L$.\n\nThe pressure is related to applied force by the equation:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $P=P_0+\\Delta P=P_0+\\ \\dfrac{F}{A}$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\therefore\\ P_0+\\dfrac{F}{A}=\\dfrac{P_0V_0}{AL}$\n\nRearranging to make $\\dfrac{1}{L}$ the subject yields:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{1}{L}=\\dfrac{A}{P_0V_0}\\left(\\dfrac{F}{A}+P_0\\right)$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{1}{L}=\\dfrac{F}{P_0V_0}+\\dfrac{A}{V_0}$\n\nThus, the gradient (given by the coefficient of $F$) is independent of $A$, whilst the y-intercept is directly proportional to $A$.\n\nHence, the graph for Piston A intercepts the y-axis at two times the value of the Piston B graph.\n\nAs the gradient is independent of $A$, the two scenarios exhibit the same rate of increase in $\\dfrac{1}{L}$ with respect to $F$."},{"ref":"PH0752","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0752\n\nGas System **A** contains non-interacting molecules of negligible size at pressure $P$, whilst Gas Systems **B** and **C** contain large, strongly interacting molecules at pressures $10P$ and $\\dfrac{P}{2}$, respectively.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/PH0752.png\" alt=\"PH0752\">\n\nWhich line of the table correctly describes how closely the three systems resemble an ideal gas?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Most like ideal gas | Second most like ideal gas | Least like ideal gas |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| System A | System B | System C\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | System A | System C | System B\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | System B | System A | System C\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | System C | System A | System B","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nIn an ideal gas system, it is assumed that molecules have negligible size and that there are no intermolecular forces. System A most resembles an ideal gas because it satisfies these criteria.\n\nWhilst Systems B and C both contain molecules of significant size with strong intermolecular forces, System B is most likely to exhibit non-ideal gas characteristics due to the relatively high gas pressure, which corresponds to greater proximity between the molecules: a decreased intermolecular distance yields increased intermolecular forces, as well as enhancing the influence of non-negligible molecular size (the total molecular volume constitutes a greater percentage of the container volume)."},{"ref":"PH0753","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0753\n\nContainer 1 contains $128$ g of oxygen ($32$ g mol$^{-1}$) and Container 2 contains $56$ g of nitrogen ($28$ g mol$^{-1}$). The graph shows how the pressure $P$ inside each container varies with volume $V$ at constant temperature ($R$ is the molar gas constant).\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/PH0753xyx.png\" alt=\"PH0753xyx\">\n\nWhich line of the table gives the temperature inside the two containers?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Temperature of Container 1 / K | Temperature of Container 2 / K  |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| 100 | 100 |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 200 | 50 |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 200 | 200 |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 400 | 200 |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFrom the ideal gas law, one obtains the equation\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $P=nT\\dfrac{R}{V}$\n\nHence, the gradient of the graph can be expressed as\\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $gradient=nT$\n\nFor Container 1,\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $gradient=\\dfrac{20000}{50}\\ \\ce{K}\\ \\ce{mol}=400$ K mol\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $n=\\dfrac{mass}{molar\\ mass}=\\dfrac{128\\ \\ce{g}}{32\\ \\ce{g\\ {mol}}^{-1}}=4$ mol\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\therefore\\ T=\\dfrac{gradient}{n}=100$ K\n\nFor Container 2,\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $gradient=\\dfrac{12000}{60}\\ \\ce{K}\\ \\ce{mol}=200$ K mol\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $n=\\dfrac{mass}{molar\\ mass}=\\dfrac{56\\ \\ce{g}}{28\\ \\ce{g}\\ \\ce{{mol}}^{-1}}=2\\ \\ce{mol}$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\therefore\\ T=\\dfrac{gradient}{n}=100$ K\n\n(If one mistakenly switches the $n$ values of the two containers, answer **B** is obtained.\n\nAnswer **C** results from miscalculating both gradients by a factor of two.\n\nFinally, wrongly assuming that both containers contain one mole of gas yields answer **D**.)"},{"ref":"PH0754","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0754\n\nSystem **A** contains $10^{23}$ ideal gas molecules in a volume of $0.5$ m$^3$. System **B** contains one mole of ideal gas molecules in a volume of $5.0$ m$^3$. The systems are at equal temperatures.\n\nWhich system contains the most molecules, and which is at the highest pressure?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Most molecules | Highest pressure |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| System A | System A |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | System A | System B |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | System B | System A |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | System B | System B |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nOne mole of gas is defined as approximately $6.02 \\times 10^{23}$ molecules, meaning that System B contains the most gas molecules.\n\nThus System B has approximately 6 times the number of molecules as System A.\n\nSystem B also has $10\\times$ the volume of System A.\n\nWith $T$ constant, we know that\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $P\\ \\propto\\dfrac{n}{V}$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\therefore P_{B\\ }\\approx\\ \\dfrac{6}{10}P_A\\\\ $\n\nAnd so System A has the highest pressure.\n\n**OR**\n\nFrom the ideal gas law, the pressure in System A is given by\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $P_A=\\dfrac{Nk_BT}{V_A}$\n\nwhereas the pressure in System B can be expressed as\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $P_B=\\dfrac{nRT}{V_B}$\n\nSince the temperatures are equal and the volume in System A is one-tenth of the volume in System B,\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{P_B}{P_A}=\\dfrac{nR}{10Nk_B}=\\dfrac{8.31}{10(10^{23})\\left(1.38\\times 10^{-23}\\right)}=\\dfrac{8.31}{13.8}<1$\n\nThus, System A has a greater pressure than System B."},{"ref":"PH0757","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0757\n\nA piston contains ideal gas in a volume of $100$ cm$^3$ at a pressure of $100$ kPa. The piston is compressed until it has a volume of $40$ cm$^3$ and a pressure of $125$ kPa.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/PH0757aaaa.png\" alt=\"PH0757aaaa\">\n\nWhat is the ratio of the final temperature to the initial temperature?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.32\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.50\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1.00\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2.00","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\\\nThe temperature of the ideal gas is given by\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $T=\\dfrac{PV}{nR}$\n\nBecause $nR$ is constant for both gases, the ratio of the final temperature to the initial temperature can be expressed as\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{T_f}{T_i}=\\dfrac{\\left(125\\textrm{ kPa}\\right)\\left(40\\textrm{ cm}^3\\right)}{\\left(100\\textrm{ kPa}\\right)\\left(100\\textrm{ cm}^3\\right)}=\\dfrac{5000}{10000}=0.50$\n\n(Answer **A** is obtained by mistakenly switching the initial and final pressure values.\n\nOne arrives at answer **C** by invoking an incorrect assumption that no temperature change can occur during ideal gas compression.\n\nAnswer **D** results from calculating the reciprocal ratio.)"},{"ref":"PH0759","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0759\n\nA 10 L cylinder containing nitrogen gas at a pressure of $20$ MPa is used to fill a $20$ L cylinder that initially contains nitrogen gas at a pressure of $2$ MPa. The two cylinders are at equal temperature.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/PH0759a.png\" alt=\"PH0759a\">\n\nWhat is the final pressure inside the two cylinders?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $8$ MPa\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $11$ MPa\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $14$ MPa\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $22$ MPa","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe number of molecules in Cylinders 1 and 2 are respectively given by:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $N_1=\\dfrac{P_1V_1}{k_BT}$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $N_2=\\dfrac{P_2V_2}{k_BT}$\n\nThe final number of molecules in the two cylinders must equal the sum of these two values; hence,\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $N_f=\\dfrac{P_f\\left(V_1+V_2\\right)}{k_BT}=N_1+N_2$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ ${\\therefore P}_f\\left(V_1+V_2\\right)=P_1V_1+P_2V_2$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ ${\\therefore P}_f=\\dfrac{P_1V_1+P_2V_2}{V_1+V_2}=\\dfrac{\\left(20\\textrm{ MPa}\\right)\\left(10\\textrm{ L}\\right)+\\left(2\\textrm{ MPa}\\right)\\left(20\\textrm{ L}\\right)}{\\left(10\\textrm{ L}+20\\textrm{ L}\\right)}=8$ MPa\n\n(Answer **B** results from averaging the two starting pressures without taking the cylinder volumes into account.\n\nAnswer **C** is obtained by mistakenly switching the volumes of the two cylinders.\n\nAnswer **D** is equal to the sum of the two initial pressures.)"},{"ref":"PH0808","marks":"11","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0808\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nThe inside face of an ideal gas piston is coated in an organic film (molar mass $240\\ \\ce{g\\ mol^{-1}}$) of negligible volume that sublimes at $200\\degree$ C with a specific latent heat of vaporization of $70.0\\ \\ce{\\ce{k}J\\ mol^{-1}}$. The piston has a cross-sectional area of $100\\ \\ce{cm^2}$ and a mass of 1.00 kg. The gas cylinder has an initial internal length $y$ of 1.0 m and it contains gas at an initial pressure of 101 kPa held at the sublimation temperature. The pressure of the surrounding air is 100 kPa. The forces on the piston are initially in equilibrium.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/PH0808aab.png\" alt=\"PH0808aab\">\n\n1. Calculate how many moles of gas are contained in the piston prior to sublimation. [2 marks]\n\n2.\n    1. Show that the initial mass of the organic film is approximately 19 g. [3 marks]\n\n    2. Estimate the number of moles initially contained within the organic film. [2 marks]\n\n3. Power is supplied to the film at a rate of 10 W.\n\n    Estimate the time required for the film to completely sublime. [2 marks]\n\n4. As a consequence of the sublimation, the pressure inside the cylinder remains 101 kPa and the temperature is 473 K.\n\n    Estimate the final equilibrium value of $y$. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. $\\hspace{1em}$ $n_{gas}=\\dfrac{PAy}{RT}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $n_{gas}=0.257$ mol $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\nAccording to the ideal gas law,\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $n_{gas}=\\dfrac{PV}{RT}=\\dfrac{PAy}{RT}$\n\n    Inserting the values given yields:\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $n_{gas}=\\dfrac{\\left({101\\times10}^3\\ \\ce{Pa}\\right)\\left({10}^{-2}\\ \\ce{m}^2\\right)\\left(1.0\\ \\ce{m}\\right)}{\\left(8.31\\ \\ce{J\\ K^{-1}}{\\ \\ce{mol}}^{-1}\\right)\\left(473\\ \\ce{K}\\right)}=0.257$ mol\n\n2.\n    1. $\\hspace{1em}$ $Gravitational\\ force=\\left(m_{film}+m_{piston}\\right)g$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        **OR**\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $Gravitational\\ force=\\left(m_{film}+1.00\\ \\ce{kg}\\right)\\left(9.81\\ \\ce{m\\ s}^{-2}\\right)$\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $Upward\\ force=\\left(P-P_0\\right)A$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        **OR**\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $Upward\\ force=10$ N\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $m_{film}=\\dfrac{\\left(P-P_0\\right)A}{g}-m_{piston}$\n\n        $\\hspace{3.8em}$ $=\\dfrac{10\\ N}{9.81\\ \\ce{m\\ s^{-2}}}-1.00\\ \\ce{kg}\\approx$ :answer[$19\\ \\ce{g}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\nAs the forces are in equilibrium, the weight of the film plus the force exerted by surrounding air pressure must equal the upward force applied by the ideal gas. Hence,\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\left(m_{film}+m_{piston}\\right)g+P_0A=PA$\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\therefore\\ m_{film}=\\dfrac{\\left(P-P_0\\right)A}{g}-m_{piston}$\n\n        Inserting the values given yields:\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $m_{film}=\\dfrac{\\left({10}^3\\ \\ce{Pa}\\right)\\left({10}^{-2}\\ \\ce{m}^2\\right)}{9.81\\ \\ce{m\\ s^}{-2}}-1.00\\ \\ce{kg}=0.01937\\ \\ce{kg}\\approx19$ g\n\n    2. $\\hspace{1em}$ $moles=\\dfrac{mass}{molar\\ mass}$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $moles=\\dfrac{19.37\\ \\ce{g}}{240\\ \\ce{g\\ {mol}^{-1}}}=$ :answer[$80.7\\ \\ce{mmol}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        **OR**\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $moles\\approx\\dfrac{19\\ \\ce{g}}{240\\ \\ce{g}\\ \\ce{{mol}}^{-1}}\\approx80$ mmol\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\nThe number of moles in the film is defined as\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $n_{film}=\\dfrac{m_{film}}{molar\\ mass}$\n\n        Inserting the mass value obtained from part b(i) yields\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $n_{film}=\\dfrac{19.37\\ \\ce{g}}{240\\ \\ce{g\\ {mol}}^{-1}}=80.71$ mmol\n\n3. $\\hspace{1em}$ $(Power)(t)\\ =n_{film}L$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    **OR**\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\left(10\\right)t=\\left(0.0801\\ \\ce{mol}\\right)(70\\times{10}^3\\ \\ce{Jmol}^{-1})$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $t=$ :answer[$565$ s] $\\checkmark$\n\n    **OR**\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $t\\approx560$ s\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\nFrom the definition of molar latent heat of vaporization, it follows that the energy involved can be expressed as\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $E=Pt$\n\n    And also by\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $E=nL$\n\n    Where in this case the latent heat is expressed in terms of moles instead of mass. Setting the equations equal to one another gives\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $Pt=nL$\n\n    Substituting the known values then gives\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\left(10\\right)t=\\left(0.0801\\ \\ce{mol}\\right)(70\\times{10}^3\\ \\ce{Jmol^{-1}})$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $t=565$ s\n\n    A more approximate value of 560 s (to two significant figures) is obtained if *the values* are expressed to three significant figures in the calculation rather than four.\n\n4. $\\hspace{1em}$ $P=\\dfrac{\\left(n_{gas}+n_{film}\\right)RT}{Ay}$\n\n    **OR**\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $101\\ \\times{10}^3=\\dfrac{\\left(0.338\\ \\ce{mol}\\right)\\left(8.31\\ \\ce{J\\ K^{-1}}\\ \\ce{{mol}^{-1}}\\right)\\left(473\\ \\ce{K}\\right)}{\\left(0.01\\ \\ce{m}^2\\right)y}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $y=1.32=$ :answer[$1.3$ m] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\nFrom the ideal gas law\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $PV=nRT$\n\n    The total number of moles of gas\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $n_{total}=n_{gas}+n_{organic}$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $n_{total}=\\left(0.257+0.081\\right)=0.338$ mol\n\n    The volume is given by the expression\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $V=Ay$\n\n    Therefore\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $PAy=nRT$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $y=\\dfrac{nRT}{PA}$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $y=\\dfrac{\\left(0.338\\right)\\left(8.314\\right)\\left(473\\right)}{\\left(101000\\right)\\left(0.01\\right)}$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $y=1.32=1.3$ m"},{"ref":"PH0809","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0809\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\n1. In a game of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$tug of war'', opposing teams pull on each end of a rope. Team **A** applies a force of $1.3\\ $kN to the left. Team **B** applies a force of $1.5\\ $kN to the right, but a cylindrical ideal gas piston pushes against Team **B** with a force $F$. The piston is initially at the same pressure as the surrounding atmosphere (100 kPa) with a cross-sectional area of $30\\ \\ce{cm}^2$ and length $1.0\\ $m. The piston remains at constant temperature during compression.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/PH0809a.png\" alt=\"PH0809a\">\n\n    Show that the pressure change in the piston, $\\Delta P$, is related to the rightwards displacement of the teams, $x$ (in meters), by the equation,\n\n    $\\Delta P = 100\\ \\textrm{kPa} \\left( \\dfrac{x}{1-x} \\right)$ [3 marks]\n\n2. Once the tug of war starts, calculate the equilibrium value of $x$. [4 marks]\n\n3. After the game, the teams are reset and the gas inside the piston is heated, but the piston is prevented from expanding. When the game commences, the piston is released, but no displacement takes place. The forces applied by the two teams are the same as in the first game.\n\n    If the gas temperature was $27\\degree$C during the first game, estimate the temperature increase inside the piston between the two games. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. $P_0 + \\Delta P = P_0\\dfrac{V_0}{V}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $V = A\\left(1-x\\right)$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    Substitution of $V$ and $V_0$, and rearrangement to obtain final equation $ P_0 \\left( \\dfrac{x}{1-x} \\right)$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nFrom the ideal gas law applied at constant temperature it follows that\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $PV=constant$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $PV=P_0 V_0 $\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\therefore P = P_0 + \\Delta P = \\dfrac{P_0V_0}{V}$\n\n    where the pressure $P$ inside the piston at volume $V$ is an amount $\\Delta P$ above atmospheric pressure $P_0$; in the scenario given, $P$ is equal to $P_0$ when $V$ equals the initial volume $V_0$.\n\n    Denoting the initial length as $L_0$ ($1.0\\ m$) and the displacement of Team **A** as $x$,\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $V = A\\left(L_0 - x \\right)$\n\n    Hence,\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\Delta P = \\dfrac{P_0V_0}{V} - P_0$\n\n    $\\hspace{2.8em}$ $= P_0 \\left ( \\dfrac{V_0 - V}{V} \\right)$\n\n    $\\hspace{2.8em}$ $= P_0 \\left ( \\dfrac{AL_0 - A(L_0 - x)}{A(L_0 - x)} \\right)$\n\n    $\\hspace{2.8em}$ $ = P_0\\left ( \\dfrac{x}{L_0 - x} \\right )$\n\n    $\\hspace{2.8em}$ $ = P_0 \\left( \\dfrac{x}{1-x} \\right)$\n\n2. ::indent\n\n    - $F_{piston} = (1.5 - 1.3)kN = 200\\ $N $\\checkmark$\n\n    -  $\\Delta P = \\dfrac{200}{30 \\times 10^{-4}} =$ $67 \\times 10^3\\ $Pa $\\checkmark$\n\n    - 67 kPa = 100k kPa $ \\left( \\dfrac{x}{1-x} \\right)$\n\n    giving\n\n    - x = :answer[$0.40\\ $m] $\\checkmark$\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nThe force from the piston must cause Team **B** to be in equilibrium. To balance the forces of the pulling ropes, the piston must push:\n\n- $F_{piston} = (1.5 - 1.3)\\textrm{kN} = 200\\ $N\n\nThe pressure in the cylinder must be atmospheric pressure plus the extra pressure, exerted to create $200\\ N$ of force on the piston. The extra pressure can be found from:\n\n- $\\Delta P = \\dfrac{200}{A} =  \\dfrac{200}{30 \\times 10^{-4}} = 67 \\times 10^3\\ $Pa\n\nWe can now substitute this into the given equation\n\n- 67 kPa = (100 kPa)$\\bigg( \\dfrac{x}{1-x} \\bigg)$\n\n- $0.67(1-x)=x$\n- $0.67=1.67x$\n- $x=0.40$\n\n3. From part b, $P=100 + 0.67=167\\ $kPa\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{P_1}{T_1} = \\dfrac{P_2}{T_2}$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{100}{300} = \\dfrac{167}{T_2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $T_2 = \\dfrac{(167)(300)}{100} =$ :answer[500 K] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\Delta T = 500 - 300 =$ :answer[200 K] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nFrom the ideal gas law, it follows that at constant volume,\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{P}{T} = \\textrm{constant}$\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\therefore T_2 = T_1 \\bigg(\\dfrac{P_2}{P_1}\\bigg)$\n\n    where $T_2$ and $P_2$ are the gas temperature and pressure, respectively, in the new game, and $T_1$ and $P_1$ are the gas temperature and pressure, respectively, in the first game.\n\n    Substituting gives\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $T_2 = \\dfrac{(167)(300)}{100} = 500\\ $K\n\n    Solving for the change in temperature:\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\Delta T = 500 - 300 = 200\\ $K $\\checkmark$"},{"ref":"PH0858","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0858\n\nTwo containers **A** and **B** contain the same ideal gas at the same temperature. The volumes of **A** and **B** are $3$ cm$^3$ and $1.5$ cm$^3$ respectively.\n\nThe pressures in **A** and **B** are $\\dfrac{p}{2}$ and $p$ respectively.\n\nThe two containers are connected together and the gases are mixed together at constant temperature.\n\nWhat is the change in pressure of container **B**?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{-2}{3}p$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{-1}{3}p$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{1}{3}p$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{2}{3}p$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFrom the ideal gas law\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $pv=nRT$\n\nIn container **A**,\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $n_A = \\dfrac{p_AV_A}{RT}$\n\nIn container **B**,\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $n_B = \\dfrac{p_BV_B}{RT}$\n\nAnd after the gases have combined,\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $n_A + n_B = \\dfrac{P\\left(V_A + V_B \\right)}{RT}$\n\nTherefore\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{p_AV_A}{RT} + \\dfrac{p_BV_B}{RT} = \\dfrac{P\\left(V_A + V_B \\right)}{RT}$\n\nSubstituting in the given values gives\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{\\dfrac{p}{2} (3)}{RT} + \\dfrac{p(1.5)}{RT} = \\dfrac{P(3 + 1.5)}{RT}$\n\nSimplifying gives\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{3p}{2} + \\dfrac{3p}{2} = 4.5 P$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $3p = 4.5P$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $P = \\dfrac{3}{4.5}p = \\dfrac{2}{3}p$\n\nTherefore the pressure in **B** has changed by\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $ \\dfrac{2}{3}p -p=-\\dfrac{1}{3}p$"},{"ref":"PH0859","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0859\n\nThe mass of a nucleus of $\\ce{^{12}_{6}C}$ in g is $m_c$. $N_A$ is Avogadro's constant in $\\ce{mol^{-1}}$.\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to 1 g?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{N_A m_c}{12}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $N_A m_c$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{N_A}{12m_c}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{N_A}{m_c}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\\\nThe unified atomic mass unit $u$ is defined as $\\dfrac{1}{12}$ of the mass of a carbon-12 atom. So $u=\\dfrac{m_c}{12}$\n\nAvogadro's constant, $N_A$ is the number of molecules in one mole of a substance (which is also the number of unified atomic mass units in 1 g)\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{N_Am_c}{12}=N_Au=1$ g"},{"ref":"PH0872","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0872\n\nA cylindrical piston of internal volume $V$ is filled with $N$ identical gas molecules at temperature $T$. Three changes are proposed to **increase** the pressure inside the piston:\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | Increase $V$ without changing $N$ or $T$      |\n  | II. | Increase $N$ without changing $V$ or $T$ |\n  | III. | Increase $T$ without changing $N$ or $V$|\n\nWhich of the proposed changes would work?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I and II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I and III only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nOption I causes pressure $P$ to decrease as $V$ is increased without changing temperature $T$ or molecular quantity $N$:\n\n- $P=\\dfrac{Nk_B T}{V}    ∝ \\dfrac{1}{V} \\hspace{2em}$ (constant $N$ and $T$)\n\nConversely, Option II results in increased $P$ because $N$ is increased\n\n- $P=\\dfrac{Nk_B T}{V}  ∝ N \\hspace{2em}$(constant $T$ and $V$)\n\nFinally, from the same expression for $P$, it follows that Option III also yields an increase in $P$ when $T$ is increased:\n\n- $P=\\dfrac{Nk_B T}{V} ∝T\\hspace{2em}$ (constant N and V )\n\nTherefore options II and III will result in an increase in pressure."},{"ref":"PH0873","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0873\n\nA vertical piston of cross-sectional area $10\\,\\ce{cm^2}$ contains $10^{-2}$ moles of ideal gas at a pressure of $100\\, \\ce{kPa}$. The piston has an equilibrium internal length of 10 cm.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\nWhat is the average kinetic energy per mole of gas?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $0.5\\, \\ce{\\ce{k}J\\,\\text{mol}^{-1}}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $1.0\\, \\ce{\\ce{k}J\\,\\text{mol}^{-1}}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $1.5\\, \\ce{\\ce{k}J\\,\\text{mol}^{-1}}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $3.0\\,\\ce{\\ce{k}J\\,\\text{mol}^{-1}}$","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe kinetic energy of a gas is found from the equation for internal energy\n\n- $U=\\dfrac{3}{2}nRT$\n\nThe ideal gas law gives us\n\n- $PV=nRT$\n\nAnd so the internal energy equation becomes\n\n- $U=\\dfrac{3}{2}PV$\n\nVolume can be calculated to becomes\n\n- $V = (10\\ \\ce{cm}^2)(10\\ \\ce{cm})=100\\ \\ce{cm}^3 = 0.0001\\ \\ce{m}^3$\n\nGiving\n\n- $U=\\dfrac{3}{2}(100000)(0.0001) =15$ J\n\nThe kinetic energy per mole of gas is therefore\n\n- $\\dfrac{E_k}{n^{-1}}=\\dfrac{15}{0.01}=1500\\ $J mol$^{-1}$\n\n**OR**\n\nThe pressure $P$ inside the piston is related to gas density $\\rho$ and average molecular velocity v via the equation\n\n- $P=\\dfrac{\\rho v^2}{3}$\n\nExpressing $\\rho$ in terms of the molar quantity $n$ of gas, molar mass $M$, cross-sectional area $A$ and internal length $L$ yields\n\n- $P=\\dfrac{nMv^2}{3AL}$\n\nThe average kinetic energy per mole of gas is given by\n\n- $E_k=\\dfrac{mv^2}{2}$\n\n- $\\therefore P = \\dfrac{2nE_k}{3AL}$\n\nRearranging for $E_k$ gives\n\n- $E_k=\\dfrac{3ALP}{2n}$\n\nInserting the values provided yields\n\n- $\\therefore E_k = \\dfrac{3ALP}{2n} = \\dfrac{3(0.001\\,\\ce{m}^2)(0.1\\,\\ce{m})(100000\\,\\ce{Pa})}{2(0.01\\,\\text{mol})}=1500\\,\\ce{J}\\,\\text{mol}^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH0874","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0874\n\nAn expanding balloon contains $10^{-3}$ moles of ideal gas molecules at a constant pressure of 100 kPa. The graph shows how the average molecular velocity $v$ changes with the internal volume $V$ of the balloon.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/Screenshot%202026-08-10%20at%207.57.38%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-08-10 at 7.57.38 AM\">\n\nWhat is the molar mass of the gas ?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $15\\,\\ce{g}\\,\\text{mol}^{-1}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $30\\,\\ce{g}\\,\\text{mol}^{-1}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $45\\,\\ce{g}\\,\\text{mol}^{-1}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $60\\,\\ce{g}\\,\\text{mol}^{-1}$","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe pressure $P$ inside the balloon is given by\n\n- $P=\\dfrac{\\rho v^2}{3}$\n\nExpressing the density $\\rho$ in terms of the gas volume $V$, the molar quantity $n$ of gas, and the molar mass $M$, one obtains\n\n- $P=\\dfrac{nMv^2}{3V}$\n\nRearrangement yields\n\n- $v^2=\\dfrac{3P}{nM} V$\n\nThus, the gradient $k$ of a plot of $v^2$ vs $V$ can be expressed as\n\n- $k=\\dfrac{3P}{nM}$\n\n- $\\therefore M = \\dfrac{3P}{nk}$\n\nThe gradient of the graph is\n\n- $k= \\dfrac{\\text{rise}}{\\text{run}} = \\dfrac{200\\times 10^3\\,\\ce{m^2\\,s^{-2}}}{40\\times 10^{-6}\\,\\ce{m^3}}=5\\times 10^9\\,\\ce{m^{-1}\\,s^{-2}}$\n\nInserting the values given and the measured gradient of the plot gives\n\n- $M = \\dfrac{3(10^5\\,\\ce{Pa})}{(10^{-3}\\,\\text{mol})(5\\times 10^9\\,\\ce{m^{-1}\\,s^{-2}})}=0.06\\,\\ce{kg}\\,\\text{mol}^{-1}=60\\,\\ce{g}\\,\\text{mol}^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH0875","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0875\n\nTwo pistons of equal initial volume are connected so that a displacement $x$ of piston A causes an equal displacement of Piston B. The pistons contain the same number of gas molecules with the same average molecular velocity, but the molecules in Piston A are half as massive as those in Piston B. The cross-sectional area of Piston A is three times greater than the cross-sectional area of Piston B. No external forces act on either piston.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/Screenshot%202024-12-17%20at%202.05.19%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2024-12-17 at 2.05.19 PM\">\n\nHow do the pistons move relative to their initial positions?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Piston A expands and Piston B compresses.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Piston A compresses and Piston B expands.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Neither piston moves.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Insufficient information is provided.","markScheme":"**答案：A**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe initial pressure in Piston A can be expressed as\n\n- $P_A = \\dfrac{{\\rho}_A v_A^2}{3}$\n\nBecause the masses of the particles are related by a factor of 2, the densities are also related by this factor and it follows that\n\n- $P_A = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\Bigl(\\dfrac{{\\rho}_B}{2}\\Bigr)v_B^2$\n\nHence, the initial pressure inside Piston A is half the initial pressure inside Piston B.\n\nThe total force applied by Piston A is given by\n\n- $F_A=P_A A_A$\n\nWe are told that $A_A=3A_B$\n\nExpressing the pressure and cross-sectional area of Piston A in terms of the pressure and cross-sectional area of Piston B gives\n\n- $F_A = P_A A_A = \\dfrac{P_B}{2} (3A_B) = \\dfrac{3}{2}F_B$\n\nThus, the force applied by Piston A is greater than the force applied by Piston B, meaning that Piston A expands and Piston B compresses."},{"ref":"PH0876","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0876\n\nThe internal energies $U$ of two ideal gases are measured with respect to temperature $T$. Gas A contains 0.25 moles of gas molecules, whilst Gas B comprises $10^{23}$ gas molecules.\n\nWhich graph shows how $U$ varies with $T$ for the two gases?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n|| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">  |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\"> |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">|","markScheme":"**答案：A**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe internal energy of Gas A is given by\n\n- $U_A=\\dfrac{3}{2} n_A RT$\n\nwhereas the internal energy of Gas B is best expressed as\n\n- $U_B=\\dfrac{3}{2} N_B k_B T$\n\nTaking the ratio of these quantities for the same temperature gives\n\n- $\\dfrac{U_A}{U_B} =\\dfrac{n_A R}{N_B k_B}\\approx \\dfrac{(0.25\\, \\text{mol})(8.3\\,\\ce{J\\,K^{-1}}\\text{mol}^{-1})}{(10^{23})(1.4\\times 10^{-23}\\,\\ce{J\\,K}^{-1})}\\approx 1.5$\n\nHence, the gradient of the $U$ vs $T$ graph for Gas A is 1.5 times higher than the gradient of the corresponding graph for Gas B."},{"ref":"PH0877","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0877\n\nTwo insulated containers of ideal monatomic gas are connected via an insulated tube of negligible volume. Container $A$ initially contains two times as many molecules as container $B$. Container $A$ has an initial temperature $T_A$ and the initial temperature of the gas in Container $B$ is $\\dfrac{2}{3} T_A$. The gases mix to reach equilibrium at temperature $T_F$.\n\nWhat is the ratio $\\dfrac{T_F}{T_A}$    ?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  \\dfrac{4}{9}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  \\dfrac{3}{4}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  \\dfrac{8}{9}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  \\dfrac{4}{3}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nPrior to connection, the internal energy in Container A can be expressed as\n\n- $U_A=\\dfrac{3}{2} N_A k_B T_A  $\n\nWe are told that\n\n- $N_B=\\dfrac{N_A}{2} \\hspace{2em}$and that$\\hspace{2em} T_B=\\dfrac{2T_A}{3}$\n\nCorrespondingly, the internal energy in Container B is given by\n\n- $U_B=\\dfrac{3}{2} N_B k_B T_B=\\dfrac{3}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{N_A}{2}\\right)\\left(\\dfrac{2T_A}{3}\\right)$\n\n- $∴U_B=\\dfrac{1}{2} N_A k_B T_A$\n\nSince no energy or matter is exchanged with the surroundings, the final internal energy must equal the sum of the initial internal energies and the total number of molecules must equal the sum of $N_A$ and $ N_B$ :\n\n- $U_{total}=U_A+U_B=U_A=\\dfrac{3}{2} N_A k_B T_A + \\dfrac{1}{2} N_A k_B T_A =\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) N_A k_B T_A=2N_A k_B T_A  $\n\nWhen combined, the total number of molecules is\n\n- $N_a+ \\dfrac{1}{2}N_A = \\dfrac{3}{2}N_A$\n\nAnd equating the two expressions for final internal energy gives\n\n- $\\dfrac{3}{2} \\left(\\dfrac{3}{2} N_A \\right) k_B T_F=2N_A k_B T_A$\n\n- $∴T_{final}=\\dfrac{8T_A}{9}$"},{"ref":"PH0937","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0937\n\nA three-dimensional $\\lq$L' shaped object lies on a flat surface and exerts a pressure $P$ on the surface. The diagrams below show the view from above and from an angle.\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| |    |  |   ||\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\"> |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">|\n\nWhich of the following is the correct expression for the mass of the object?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{Pd}{g}(x+y)$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{Pg}{d}(x+y)$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $Pdh(x+y)$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{P}{dh}(x+y)$","markScheme":"**答案：A**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n- $P=\\dfrac{F}{A}$\n\nForce is the weight of the object $=mg$\n\nArea of the shape is $xd+yd=d(x+y)$\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n- $P=\\dfrac{mg}{d(x+y)}$\n\n- $Pd(x+y)=mg $\n\n- $m=\\dfrac{Pd}{g}(x+y)$"},{"ref":"PH0938","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0938\n\nTwo cubic containers $A$ and $B$ each contain a mass $m$ of the same ideal gas. The same force per unit area is exerted on the walls inside the containers by the ideal gas molecules.\n\nContainer $A$ has side lengths $x$. The absolute temperature of the ideal gas inside container $A$ is $T_A$ and the average translational speed of the molecules is $v_A$.\n\nContainer $B$ has side lengths $2x$ Which row correctly identifies the absolute temperature of the gas and the average translational speed of the molecules in container $B$?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Temperature  | Average translational speed |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $2T_A$  |$\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}{2}v_A $ |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $2T_A$  | $2\\sqrt2v_A $   |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $8T_A$  | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}{2}v_A$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $8T_A$  | $2\\sqrt2v_A$  |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nVolume of container $A = x^3$\n\nVolume of container $B = \\left(2x\\right)^3=8x^3$\n\nSo, container $B$ has a volume eight times greater than container $A$.\n\n- $\\dfrac{PV}{T}=constant\\  $\n\nPressure is the same for each container so we may write:\n\n- $\\dfrac{V}{T}=constant\\  $\n\n- $\\dfrac{V_B}{T_B}=\\dfrac{V_A}{T_A} $\n\n- $\\dfrac{8V_A}{T_B}=\\dfrac{V_A}{T_A}  $\n\n- $\\dfrac{T_B}{8}=T_A $\n\n- $T_B=8T_A $\n\nNow average $E_k=\\dfrac{3}{2}k_BT \\hspace{1.5em}$ and $\\hspace{1.5em}E_k=\\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2$\n\n- $E_k\\ \\alpha\\ T\\ \\alpha\\ v^2 $\n\nWe may write:\n\n- $\\dfrac{T}{v^2}=constant\\  $\n\n- $\\dfrac{T_A}{(v_A)^2}=\\dfrac{T_B}{(v_B)^2}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{T_A}{(v_A)^2}=\\dfrac{8T_A}{v_B^2} $\n\n- $\\dfrac{v_B^2}{8}=(v_A)^2 $\n\n- $v_B=\\sqrt8v=2\\sqrt2v_A $"},{"ref":"PH0973","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0973\n\nA box of volume $V$ contains $n$ moles of an ideal gas. A student varies the temperature of the gas and measures how the pressure inside the box varies.\n\nThe student plots the results on a graph with pressure in Pa against temperature in $\\degree $C. The student then draws a line of best fit extrapolating the data until the line crosses the temperature axis.\n\nWhich row correctly shows the gradient and temperature axis intercept of the graph?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Gradient$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$ Temperature Axis Intercept / $\\degree $C $\\hspace{1.5em}$|\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\dfrac{nR}{V}$ |-273 |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{nR}{V}$ | 0 |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{V}{nR}$ | -273 |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{V}{nR}$ | 0 |","markScheme":"**答案：A**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nBecause temperature is the independent variable, it will be plotted on the horizontal axis. Pressure is the dependent variable and will be plotted on the vertical axis.\n\n- $PV=nRT$\n\nWhere the temperature will be the absolute temperature in Kelvin.\n\n- Gradient $= \\dfrac{P}{T} = \\dfrac{nR}{V}$\n\nThe temperature axis intercept will be absolute zero (0 kelvin) because when pressure = 0 temperature is as low as possible.\n\nAbsolute zero is $-273\\degree$C. So the temperature axis intercept for this question is $-273\\degree$C.\n\nThe graph is shown below\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">"},{"ref":"PH1022","marks":"10","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1022\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\n1. A closed glass vessel with 5.0 L internal volume initially contains 0.20 moles of a reactive, monatomic ideal gas of molar mass $50.0\\, \\ce{g\\,\\text{mol}^{-1}}$. Inside the vessel, a plate is suspended from a spring scale. A pressure gauge is used to continuously measure the pressure $P$ inside the vessel.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n    Calculate the initial gas density inside the vessel. Assume that the volume occupied by the catalyst and spring scale is negligible. [2 marks]\n\n2. Over time the gas reacts with a substance on the metal plate to form a solid. From the spring scale measurement, the density $\\rho$ of gas remaining in the vessel is calculated.\n\n    The graph shows how $\\dfrac{P}{\\rho}$ changes with time $t$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n    1. Show that the gradient of the graph is proportional to the rate of change of temperature $T$ inside the vessel, following the equation\n\n    $\\dfrac{P}{\\rho}=(0.166\\,\\ce{J\\,g^{-1}\\,K^{-1}})T$ [3 marks]\n\n    2. Determine whether the reaction causes the temperature of the gas to increase or decrease over time. [2 marks]\n\n    3. Estimate the final temperature inside the vessel. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. $m_0=nM_r=10$ g  $\\checkmark$\n\n    ${\\rho}_0=\\dfrac{m_0}{V}=0.002\\,\\ce{g\\,cm^{-3}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    The initial mass $m_0$ of gas is related to its molar mass $M_r$ and number of moles $n$ via the equation\n\n    - $m_0=nM_r=(0.20\\, \\text{mol})(50.0\\,\\ce{g}\\,\\text{mol}^{-1})=10.0$ g\n\n    The initial density ${\\rho}_0$ is in turn given by\n\n    - ${\\rho}_0=\\dfrac{m_0}{V}=\\dfrac{10.0\\,\\ce{g}}{5000\\,\\ce{cm^3}}=0.002\\,\\ce{g\\,cm^{-3}}$\n\n    The volume $V$ has been set equal to 5.0 L because the volume of the solid constituents of the vessel (the catalyst and spring scale) is stated to be negligible.\n\n2. 1. $\\dfrac{P}{\\rho}=\\dfrac{v^2}{3}$   $\\checkmark$\n\n        $v^2=\\dfrac{3RT}{M_r}$    $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\dfrac{P}{\\rho}=\\dfrac{RT}{M_r} =\\dfrac{8.31\\,\\ce{J}\\,\\text{mol}^{-1}\\,\\ce{K}^{-1}}{50.0\\,\\ce{g}\\,\\text{mol}^{-1}}T=(0.166\\,\\ce{J\\,g^{-1}\\,K^{-1}})T$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        OR\n\n        $PV=nRT$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $V=\\dfrac{nM_r}{\\rho}$   $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\dfrac{P}{\\rho}=\\dfrac{RT}{M_r} = \\dfrac{8.31\\,\\ce{J}\\,\\text{mol}^{-1}\\,\\ce{K}^{-1}}{50.0\\,\\ce{g}\\,\\text{mol}^{-1}}T=(0.166\\,\\ce{J g^{-1}\\,K}^{-1})T$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        From the ideal gas law, pressure $P$, gas density $\\rho$ and mean molecular velocity $v$ are related through the expression\n\n        - $P=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\rho v^2$\n\n        - $\\therefore \\dfrac{P}{\\rho}=\\dfrac{v^2}{3}$\n\n        In addition, the internal energy $U$ of a monatomic is given by\n\n        - $U=\\dfrac{3}{2}nRT$\n\n        The internal energy is also the total kinetic energy of the particles in the gas, giving\n\n        - $U=\\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2=\\dfrac{1}{2}nM_r v^2$\n\n        Where $nM_r$ is the mass of the particles in the gas. Combining the two equations gives\n\n        - $U=\\dfrac{3}{2}nRT=\\dfrac{1}{2}nM_r v^2$\n\n        - $\\therefore v^2=\\dfrac{3RT}{M_r}$\n\n        Thus, $v^2$ can be substituted in the first equation to obtain\n\n        - $\\dfrac{P}{\\rho}=\\dfrac{RT}{M_r}$\n\n        Alternatively, one may arrive to the same equation directly from the ideal gas law:\n\n        - $PV=nRT$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{PV}{nM_r}=\\dfrac{RT}{M_r}$\n\n        Here, the final step follows from the definition used in part a,\n\n        - $\\rho=\\dfrac{nM_r}{V}$\n\n        Giving\n\n        - $\\dfrac{P}{\\rho}=\\dfrac{RT}{M_r}$\n\n        And\n\n        - $\\dfrac{R}{M_r} = \\dfrac{8.314}{50}=0.166$\n\n    2. $\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{\\Delta t} \\propto \\dfrac{\\Delta (\\frac{P}{\\rho})}{\\Delta t} > 0$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        Reaction causes a temperature rise  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        In part b(i), an equation was derived relating $\\dfrac{P}{\\rho}$ to the gas temperature $T$:\n\n        - $\\dfrac{P}{\\rho}=\\dfrac{RT}{M_r}$\n\n        Since $\\dfrac{P}{\\rho}$ increases with time $t$, it follows that the reaction causes an increase in temperature, as $T$ is directly proportional to $\\dfrac{P}{\\rho}$.\n\n    3. $\\dfrac{P}{{\\rho}_{final}}=90\\,\\ce{J\\,g^{-1}}$   $\\checkmark$\n\n        $T_{final}=\\dfrac{M_r}{R}\\Bigl(\\dfrac{P}{{\\rho}}\\Bigr)_{final}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $T_{final}=$ :answer[542 K]  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        From the graph, the final value of $\\dfrac{P}{\\rho}$ is $90\\,\\ce{J\\,g^{-1}}$. Thus, after rearranging the relation between $\\dfrac{P}{\\rho}$ and $T$, one obtains\n\n        - $T_{final}=\\dfrac{M_r}{R}\\Bigl(\\dfrac{P}{\\rho}\\Bigr)_{final}=\\dfrac{(50.0\\,\\ce{g}\\,\\text{mol}^{-1})(90.0\\,\\ce{J\\,g}^{-1})}{8.31\\,\\ce{J\\,K^{-1}}\\,\\text{mol}^{-1}}=542$ K"},{"ref":"PH1031-1","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1031-1\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n1. Almost 100% of Venus' atmosphere is composed of carbon dioxide. The density of the atmosphere on the surface of Venus is around $65\\,\\ce{kg\\,m^{-3}}$ and the molar mass of carbon dioxide is $44\\,\\ce{g\\,\\text{mol}^{-1}}$. Show that the molar density of the atmosphere on the surface of Venus is around $1500\\,\\ce{{mol}\\,m^{-3}}$. [1 mark]\n\n2. The average temperature on the surface of Venus is around $470\\,\\degree$C. Assuming that Venus' atmosphere behaves like an ideal gas, estimate Venus' surface atmospheric pressure. [2 marks]\n\n3. State two of the conditions for which a real gas approximates an ideal gas. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. $\\dfrac{65}{44\\times 10^{-3}}=1480\\,\\text{mol}\\,\\ce{m}^{-3}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        First, we must convert mass per unit mol into SI units.\n\n    - $44\\,\\ce{g}\\,\\text{mol}^{-1}=44(10^{-3}\\,\\ce{kg})\\text{mol}^{-1}=0.044\\,\\ce{kg}\\,\\text{mol}^{-1}$\n\n    - $\\dfrac{\\text{mass}}{\\text{volume}}\\div \\dfrac{\\text{mass}}{\\text{mol}}=\\dfrac{\\text{mass}}{\\text{volume}}\\times \\dfrac{\\text{mol}}{\\text{mass}}=\\dfrac{\\text{mol}}{\\text{volume}}$\n\n    (We can also do a similar analysis with units to find that $\\dfrac{\\ce{kg\\,m^{-3}}}{\\ce{kg\\,\\text{mol}^{-1}}}$  gives us the correct units)\n\n    - $\\dfrac{65\\,\\ce{kg\\,m^{-3}}}{0.044\\,\\ce{kg\\,\\text{mol}^{-1}}}=1477\\, \\text{mol} \\,\\ce{m}^{-3} $\n\n2. ::indent\n    - $PV=nRT \\hspace{1em}$ and $\\hspace{1em}470\\degree \\ce{C} = 743$ K $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $\\dfrac{n}{V}=1480$\n\n    - $P=\\Bigl(\\dfrac{n}{V}\\Bigr)(R)(T)=(1480)(8.31)(743)=9.1\\times 10^6\\,\\ce{Pa}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    (Award full marks for use of $\\dfrac{n}{V}=1500$ giving $9.3\\times 10^6\\,\\ce{Pa}$)\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n    - $PV=nRT$\n\n    Rearranging for $P$ gives\n\n    - $P=\\dfrac{nRT}{V}=\\Bigl(\\dfrac{n}{V}\\Bigr)(R)(T)$\n\n    - $\\dfrac{n}{V}$ is moles per unit volume $= 1480\\,\\text{mol }\\,\\ce{m}^{-3}$ (calculated in part (a))\n\n    - $R$ is the molar gas constant $= 8.31\\, \\ce{J \\,K^{-1}}$ (given in your formula book)\n\n    - $T$ is absolute temperature $470\\degree$C= $470\\,\\ce{K}+273\\,\\ce{K} =743\\,\\ce{K}$\n\n    - $P=\\Bigl(\\dfrac{n}{V}\\Bigr)(R)(T)=(1480)(8.31)(743)=9.1\\times 10^6$ Pa\n\n3. Any two from:\n\n    Low pressure.   $\\checkmark$ \\\n    Low density.   $\\checkmark$ \\\n    High temperature. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    A real gas approximates to an ideal gas at conditions of low pressure, high temperature, and low density.\n\n    The data we are given allows us to accurately predict the pressure on Venus by assuming it is an ideal gas."},{"ref":"PH1101","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH1101\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nIn an experiment the volume occupied by a fixed mass of an ideal gas is varied. The temperature of the gas is kept constant at 293 K throughout the experiment. The pressure of the gas $P$ is measured for five different volumes $V$. Below shows a graph of $P$ against $V^{-1}$. The uncertainty in $V^{-1}$ is much less than the uncertainty in P for all of the data points.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/Screenshot%202025-06-04%20at%204.38.59%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-04 at 4.38.59 PM\">\n\n1.     Determine the relative uncertainty on pressure when $\\dfrac{1}{V} $ is equal to $5.0 \\times 10^2\\ \\ce{m}^{-3}$.   [2 marks]\n\n2.  1. Draw the line of best fit on the graph.    [1 mark]\n\n    2. Use your line of best fit to estimate the number of moles of gas used in the experiment                     [3 marks]\n\n    3.  It is suggested that $P$ and $\\dfrac{1}{V}$ are directly proportional. Explain why the data does not support this hypothesis.                                                [1 mark]\n\n    4.  With reference to the graph, suggest, with a reason, whether a systematic or random error is responsible for the hypothesis not being supported.         [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Absolute uncertainty +-0.2 x 105. $\\checkmark$\n\n    Relative uncertainty $=\\dfrac{0.2}{2.6}=0.08=8\\%.$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Reading from the graph, the uncertainty for the pressure is $0.2\\times{10}^5$ Pa\n\n    The pressure at this point is $2.6\\times{10}^5$ Pa\n\n    Relative uncertainty $=\\dfrac{\\Delta P}{P}=\\dfrac{0.2\\times{10}^5}{2.6\\times{10}^5}=0.077=7.8\\%$\n\n2. 1. Straight line that passes through all uncertainty bars $\\checkmark$\n\n      <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/Screenshot%202025-04-16%20at%209.35.59%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-16 at 9.35.59 AM\">\n\n    2. gradient $=nRT$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        Gradient of line of best fit $= 440\\ (\\ce{J})$ Allow values in range 420-460  (J). $\\checkmark$\n\n        Number of moles = $\\dfrac{gradient}{RT}=\\dfrac{440}{(8.31)(293)}=0.18\\, \\ce{mol}$ (Allow answers in range 0.17-0.19). $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n        - $PV=nRT$\n\n        Rearranging this equation, we can see\n\n        - $P=nRT\\left(\\dfrac{1}{V}\\right)$\n\n        Comparing this to $y=mx\\ (+c)$ tells us that gradient $=nRT $\n\n        Finding the gradient of the line of best fit.\n\n        - Gradient$=\\dfrac{difference\\ in\\ P}{difference\\ in\\ V^{-1} }=\\dfrac{1.1\\times{10}^5}{2.5\\times{10}^2}=440\\, (\\ce{J})\\ $\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/Screenshot%202025-04-16%20at%203.43.33%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-16 at 3.43.33 PM\">\n\n        - $n=\\dfrac{gradient}{RT}=\\dfrac{440}{\\left(8.31\\right)\\left(293\\right)}=0.18\\ $moles\n\n        (Note $R$ is the molar gas constant $=8.31\\, $J K$^{-1}\\, \\textrm{mol}^{-1}$ - this value is given in your data booklet)\n\n    3.   It is not possible to draw a straight line from the origin that passes within all uncertainty bars.  $\\checkmark$\n\n         $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n         The conditions for two variables to be directly proportional are that the graph shows a straight line that passes through the origin. For the given data and uncertainty bars, a straight line can be drawn; however, it crosses the pressure axis above the origin.\n\n    4.  Systematic error.   $\\checkmark$\n\n        All points have been shifted by the same amount.  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The points on the graph show a good amount of linearity, which implies that there is minimal random error.\n\n        Because the line of best fit passes above the origin, it suggests that all pressure measurements are a little higher than the actual amounts, or all $\\dfrac{1}{V}$ measurements are lower than they should be (or all V measurements are higher than the actual amount)"},{"ref":"PH1124","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1124\n\nA vessel contains a mixture of helium and methane gas. 40\\% of the mass of the gas is composed of helium, with the rest composed of methane.\n\nIf there is a total of 11 moles of gas in the vessel, what is the number of moles of methane in the gas?\n\nMolar mass of helium =  $4\\,\\ce{g}\\,\\textrm{mol}^{-1}$\\\nMolar mass of methane = $16\\,\\ce{g}\\,\\textrm{mol}^{-1}$\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 3\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 4\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 7\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ 8","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAdopting the notation He for helium and CH$_4$ for methane\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{Mass of CH}_4}{\\textrm{Mass of He}}=\\dfrac{60\\%}{40\\%}=\\dfrac{3}{2}$\n\n- Mass = Molar mass $\\times n$\n\nWhere $n$ is the number of moles\n\n- $\\dfrac{3}{2} = \\dfrac{(\\textrm{molar mass of CH}_4)(n \\textrm{ of CH}_4)}{(\\textrm{molar mass of He})(n \\textrm{ of He})}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{3}{2} = \\dfrac{(16)(n \\textrm{ of CH}_4)}{(4)(n \\textrm{ of He})}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{3}{2} = \\dfrac{4(n \\textrm{ of CH}_4)}{(n \\textrm{ of He})}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{3}{8}= \\dfrac{n \\textrm{ of CH}_4}{n \\textrm{ of He}}$\n\nSo we can say that that the ratio\n\n- $(n \\textrm{ of CH}_4):(n \\textrm{ of He}) = 3:8$\n\nSplitting 11 moles into the ratio $3:8$  we have 3  moles of methane and 8 moles of helium."},{"ref":"PH1523","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1523\n\nTwo containers each hold a different gas. The first gas has a density $\\rho$ and a pressure $2P$, with an average translational speed $v$. The second gas has a density $2\\rho$ and a pressure $P$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/Screenshot%202025-03-24%20at%205.37.42%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-03-24 at 5.37.42 PM\">\n\nWhat is the average translational speed of the gas molecules in the second container?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{v}{2}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{v}{\\sqrt{2}}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2v$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\sqrt{2}v$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe average translational speed v using the equation given in the data booklet:\n\n- $P=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\rho v^2$\n\n- $v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3P}{\\rho}}$\n\nFor the first container:\n\n- $v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3(2P)}{\\rho}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{6P}{\\rho}}$\n\nFor the second container:\n\n- $v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3P}{2\\rho}}=\\dfrac{v}{2}$"},{"ref":"PH1789","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1789\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n1. 1. Explain what is meant by the internal energy of an ideal gas. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Air contains a mixture of diatomic gases. A sample of air contains 480 moles and has an initial temperature of 112 °C. Explain why the internal energy of the air cannot be calculated using:\n\n    - $E_k=\\dfrac{3}{2}nRT$ [1 mark]\n\n2. 1.2 kW of power is supplied to the sample of air at a fixed volume. The graph below shows how its temperature $T$ varies with time $t$:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/Screenshot%202026-06-30%20at%207.27.24%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-06-30 at 7.27.24 AM\">\n\n    The average specific heat capacity of the air during this process is 720 J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$. Use the graph to determine the molar mass of air. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. The total random kinetic energy of the particles of the gas $\\checkmark$\n\n        Note—mark is not awarded if potential energy is included in the answer.\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        For an ideal gas, internal energy is equal to kinetic energy. The potential energy of an ideal gas is assumed to be zero because one of the assumptions of the ideal gas model is that there are no intermolecular forces between the particles.\n\n    2. This equation only applies to **monatomic** ideal gases. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        For an ideal gas, internal energy is equal to kinetic energy as potential energy is zero. However, the kinetic energy equation, $E_k=\\dfrac{3}{2}nRT$ only applies to monatomic ideal gases.\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Extra information above and beyond the syllabus}}$\n\n        The equation $E_k=\\dfrac{3}{2}nRT$ is derived from the ideal gas model for monatomic gases, where the internal energy is purely due to the kinetic energy of the gas particles. This equation assumes that the gas consists of particles that have only translational motion and no rotational or vibrational energy modes, which is true for monatomic gases like noble gases (e.g., helium or neon).\n\n        However, air is a mixture of diatomic gases, primarily nitrogen ($\\textrm{N}_2$) and oxygen ($\\textrm{O}_2$). Diatomic molecules have additional energy contributions beyond just translational motion. These molecules also have rotational and, at higher temperatures, vibrational energy modes. This increases the total internal energy of the gas.\n\n        For diatomic gases, the correct expression for the internal energy would account for these extra degrees of freedom. In general, the internal energy for a diatomic ideal gas can be expressed as:\n\n        - $E_k=\\dfrac{5}{2}nRT$\n\n        This includes translational, rotational, and, at sufficiently high temperatures, vibrational contributions to the energy.\n\n2. $\\underline{\\textrm{Alternative 1}}$\n\n    - $E=(1200)(6)(60)=432000$ J\n\n    **AND**\n\n    - $\\Delta T=44$ °C / K $\\checkmark$\n\n    (Allow any valid time and change in temperature)\n\n    - $m=\\dfrac{Q}{c\\Delta T}=\\dfrac{432000}{(720)(44)}=13.7$ kg $\\checkmark$\n\n    - molar mass $=\\dfrac{\\textrm{mass}}{\\textrm{number of moles}}=\\dfrac{13.7}{480}=0.029$ kg mol$^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    Note: Use of exact mass gives 0.028 kg mol$^{-1}$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Alternative 2}}$\n\n    gradient $=0.122$ K s$^{-1}$ / 0.122 °C s$^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $m=\\dfrac{P}{c(\\textrm{gradient})}=\\dfrac{1200}{(720)(0.122)}=13.7$ kg $\\checkmark$\n\n    molar mass $=\\dfrac{\\textrm{mass}}{\\textrm{number of moles}}=\\dfrac{13.7}{480}=$ :answer[0.029 kg mol$^{-1}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    From the graph we can see that the change in temperature across the 6 minutes is given by:\n\n    - $\\Delta T=156-112=44$ K\n\n    The heat energy supplied to the gas across the 6 minutes is given by:\n\n    - $E=Pt=(1.2\\times10^3)(60)(6)=432000$ J\n\n    (Note that we have converted power from kW to W and time from minutes to s)\n\n    Now we have:\n\n    - $Q=mc\\Delta T$\n\n    Where $Q$ is heat energy, $m$ is mass, $c$ is specific heat capacity and $\\Delta T$ is change in temperature.\n\n    Substituting our values and rearranging for $m$ gives:\n\n    - $m=\\dfrac{Q}{c\\Delta T}=\\dfrac{432000}{(720)(44)}=13.7$ kg\n\n    Molar mass is given by:\n\n    - molar mass $=\\dfrac{\\textrm{mass}}{\\textrm{number of moles}}=\\dfrac{13.7}{480}=0.029$ kg mol$^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH1811","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1811\n\nThe volume of an ideal gas decreases at constant temperature, with no change in the number of gas molecules.\n\nBelow are three statements about the gas:\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | The average speed of the particles increases. |\n  | II. | The frequency of collisions between particles and the container walls increases. |\n  | III. | The pressure of the gas increases. |\n\nWhich of the statements are correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n**I is incorrect:**\n\nAbsolute temperature is directly proportional to the kinetic energy of the molecules. The temperature of the gas remains constant, so $E_k$ and hence the speed remains constant.\n\n**II is correct:**\n\nThere is less space for particles to move inside the container so collisions with the walls occur more frequently.\n\n**III is correct:**\n\nFor an ideal gas we have:\n\n- $PV=Nk_BT$\n\nWhere $P$ is the pressure of the gas, $V$ is its volume, $T$ is its absolute temperature, $N$ is the number of molecules, and $k_B$ is the Boltzmann constant.\n\nFor constant $N$ and $T$ we have:\n\n- $P\\propto\\dfrac{1}{V}$\n\n therefore a decrease in volume implies an increase in pressure."},{"ref":"PH1858","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1858\n\nTwo sealed containers are connected by a closed valve. Both contain the same ideal gas and are kept at the same temperature.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Ideal%20Gas%20Model/images/Screenshot%202025-08-25%20at%205.49.53%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-08-25 at 5.49.53 PM\">\n\nOne of the containers has a volume $V$ and inside, gas is at a pressure $P$.\n\nThe second container has a volume $2V$ and inside, gas is at a pressure $\\dfrac{1}{2}P$.\n\nThe valve is opened so that gas can flow freely across containers. Eventually an equilibrium pressure $P_f$ is reached.\n\nAssuming that the temperature of both gases remains constant at all times, which of the following correctly describes the value of $P_f$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{2}{3}P$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{3}{4}P$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{4}{5}P$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{5}{6}P$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nInitially, the number of particles $N_1$ in the first container is given by:\n\n- $N_1=\\dfrac{PV}{k_BT}$\n\nSimilarly, the number of particles $N_2$ in the second container is given by:\n\n- $N_2=\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}P\\right)(2V)}{k_BT}=\\dfrac{PV}{k_BT}$\n\nThis means that there is an equal number of particles in each container.\n\nAfter the valve is opened, the system consists of a gas with a total number of particles\n\n- $N=N_1+N_2=2\\dfrac{PV}{k_BT}$, occupying a total volume $3V$.\n\nWe can now use the ideal gas law to find the final equilibrium pressure of the gas, substituting in the combined volume $3V$ and total particles $N$:\n\n- $P_f(3V)=Nk_BT$\n\nSubstituting $N$ leads to:\n\n- $P_f3V=2\\dfrac{PV}{k_BT}k_BT$\n\nThat can be simplified to:\n\n- $P_f=\\dfrac{2}{3}P$"},{"ref":"PH1895","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1895\n\nA sample of $\\ce{^{24}_{12}Mg}$ has the same mass as a sample of $\\ce{^{56}_{26}Fe}$.\n\nWhat is the ratio\n\n $\\hspace{8em}\\dfrac{\\textrm{number of moles in }\\ce{^{24}_{12}Mg}\\textrm{ sample}}{\\textrm{number of moles in }\\ce{^{56}_{26}Fe}\\textrm{ sample}}$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{3}{7}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{6}{13}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{13}{6}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{7}{3}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe number of moles in a substance $n$ is given by:\n\n- $n=\\dfrac{\\textrm{mass}}{\\textrm{mass number}}$\n\n- mass $=n\\times$ mass number\n\nThe mass number of $\\ce{^{56}_{26}Fe}$ is 56 and the mass number of $\\ce{^{24}_{12}Mg}$ is 24.\n\nThe mass of each sample is the same, so we have:\n\n- $\\begin{aligned}\n\\ 56n_{Fe}&=24n_{Mg}\\\\\n\\\\\\\n\\ 56&=24\\dfrac{n_{Mg}}{n_{Fe}}\\\\\n\\\\\\\n\\ \\dfrac{n_{Mg}}{n_{Fe}}&=\\dfrac{56}{24}=\\dfrac{7}{3}\\\\\n\\end{aligned}$"}]},{"name":"Thermal Energy Transfers","questions":[{"ref":"PH0040","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0040\n\nA temperature-time graph for the heating of 100 g of a substance is shown, from its state as a solid to its state as a gas. The material is being heated in a well-insulated container at a constant heating rate of 50 W. It is assumed that mass remains constant and no heat is lost to the surroundings. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/PH0040.png\" alt=\"PH0040\">\n\nWhat is the specific heat capacity of the material in the liquid state? \n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.08 kJ kg$^{-1} $K$^{-1}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2.0 kJ kg$^{-1} $K$^{-1}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 4.8 kJ kg$^{-1} $K$^{-1}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 60 kJ kg$^{-1} $K$^{-1}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nSubstance is in its liquid state between $t = 4$ min and $t = 12$ min (8 mins) \n\nEnergy provided by the heater \n\n- E = Pt = (50)(8)(60) = 24 kJ\n\nThis is equal to the heat provided to the liquid\n\n- $Q = m c_l \\Delta T$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\Delta T=80-30 = 50\\degree$ C $= 50$ K\n\n- $24000 = (0.1)(c_l)(80-30)$\n\n- $c_l = 4.8$ kJ kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH0087","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0087\n\nA substance, initially solid of mass 2.00 kg is heated by a 4.0 kW electric heater causing it to melt. During heating, the substance also exchanges heat with its surroundings. The substance starts melting immediately and has fully turned to liquid after 400 s. Given that the specific latent heat of fusion of the solid is 900 kJ kg$^{-1}$, what is the average rate of heat transfer between the substance and the surroundings in one second?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$ Direction of heat transfer $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Rate of heat transfer $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$A. $\\hspace{1.5em}$| from the surroundings | 500 W |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$B. $\\hspace{1.5em}$| to the surroundings | 500 W |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C. $\\hspace{1.5em}$| from the surroundings | 1750 W |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D. $\\hspace{1.5em}$| to the surroundings | 1750 W |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nHeat from electric heater: (4.0)(400) = 1600 kJ \n\nTotal heat gained to cause melting: $(2.0)(900)=1800$ kJ\n\nTherefore the heater does not provide enough energy, and extra energy must have entered from the surroundings.\n\nThe energy that entered from the surroundings is given by\n\n- 1800 kJ $ -$ 1600 kJ = 200 kJ\n\nHence power from the surrounding\n\n- $P = \\dfrac{E}{t}=\\dfrac{200}{400}$ = 0.5 kW = 500 W"},{"ref":"PH0120","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0120\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/PH0120zzz.png\" alt=\"PH0120zzz\">\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$\n\nA hot sphere of temperature $T$ and surface area $A_1$ radiates towards a metal plate of area $A_2$ that is a very large distance $D$ away. Which of the following expressions gives the rate at which radiant energy is received by the plate? \n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{\\sigma A_1 A_2 T^4}{4{\\pi}D}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{\\sigma A_1 T^4}{D^2}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{\\sigma A_1 A_2 T^4}{4{\\pi}D^2}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{\\sigma A_2 T^4}{D^2}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe power radiated by the sphere is $P_1=\\sigma A_1 T^4$\n\nThe intensity received by the plate is \n\n- $b = \\dfrac{L}{4\\pi r^2}= \\dfrac{P_1}{4\\pi D^2} =\\dfrac{\\sigma A_1 T^4}{4{\\pi}D^2}$ \n\nThe power received by the plate is \n\n- $P_2 = b A_2$\n\nTherefore combining the formulae, the power received by the plate is \n\n- $\\dfrac{\\sigma A_1 A_2 T^4}{4{\\pi}D^2}$"},{"ref":"PH0155","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0155\n\nAn immersion heater of constant power output is used to supply thermal energy to a cup of water at 373 K. The heater is turned on at time $t=0$. Which graph shows a possible variation of temperature $T$ of the water, with time $t$? \n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | | | |\n\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n\n||<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-10%20at%203.10.54%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-10 at 3.10.54 PM\">|$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-10%20at%203.10.59%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-10 at 3.10.59 PM\">|\n\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n\n|  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-10%20at%203.11.04%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-10 at 3.11.04 PM\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-10%20at%203.11.11%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-10 at 3.11.11 PM\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\n373 K is the boiling point of water. Temperature does not change until all water is boiled. B and C show temperature change at $t=0$. D is not compatible with the question. \n\nYou might have seen graphs that resemble options B and D in this context, they are there to distract you."},{"ref":"PH0157","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0157\n\nA thin metal plate of mass $m$ is held at an absolute temperature $T$, in an enclosure of temperature $T_s<T$ . One face of the plate has an area $A$. The temperature of the plate decreases by a small value $\\Delta T$ in a short interval $\\Delta t$, due to radiation. Assuming the metal plate to be a perfect black body, its specific heat capacity is given by: \n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{\\sigma AT^4 \\Delta t}{m(\\Delta T)}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{\\sigma A(T^4-T_s\\textrm{}^4 )\\Delta t}{m(\\Delta T)}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{2\\sigma AT^4 \\Delta t}{m(\\Delta T)}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{2\\sigma A(T^4-T_s\\textrm{}^4 )\\Delta t}{m(\\Delta T)}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nSpecific heat capacity is defined as the thermal energy $(\\Delta Q)$ gained or lost per kelvin change in temperature $(\\Delta T)$ per unit mass ($m$). \n\nIn this problem thermal energy is lost by radiation only. \n\n$\\begin{aligned}\n\n\\therefore \\Delta Q&=P_{net}(\\Delta t)\\\\\n\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n&=P_{radiated}-P_{absorbed}\\\\\n\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n&=\\sigma(2A)(T^4)(\\Delta t)-\\sigma(2A)(T_s\\textrm{}^4)(\\Delta t)\\\\\n\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n&=\\sigma(2A)(T^4-T_s\\textrm{}^4)(\\Delta t)\\\\\n\n\\end{aligned}$\n\n                                \n\n(considering both surfaces of the plate and using Stefan-Boltzmann equation). \n\nHence, specific heat capacity $c=\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{m(\\Delta T)}=\\dfrac{2\\sigma A(T^4-T_s\\textrm{}^4 )\\Delta t}{m(\\Delta T)}$"},{"ref":"PH0212","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0212\n\nA spherical object of surface area $A$ and absolute temperature $T$ can be regarded as a black body. The power of the radiation emitted from the object is $P$. What would be the power of the radiation emitted by an object with an absolute temperature of $2T$ and a surface area of $\\dfrac{A}{2}$? \n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $P$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2P$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $4P$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $8P$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nPower of the emission for the initial conditions; \n\n- $P= \\sigma AT^4$\n\nPower of the emission for the final conditions; \n\n- $P_x = \\sigma \\bigg(\\dfrac{A}{2}\\bigg) (2T)^4 $\n\n- $= \\sigma \\bigg(\\dfrac{A}{2}\\bigg) 16T^4 = 8\\sigma AT^4$\n\nTherefore \n\n- $P_x= 8P$"},{"ref":"PH0249","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0249\n\nA material with a mass of m is heated. The graph shows the variation of the temperature $T$ of the material with the energy supplied $E$. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-18%20at%204.36.39%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-18 at 4.36.39 PM\">\n\nWhich one of the following gives the specific heat capacity of the material?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{2E_0m}{T_2-T_1}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{E_0m}{T_1}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{2E_0}{m(T_2-T_1)}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{E_0}{m(T_2-T_1)}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nRearranging the equation for specific heat capacity gives\n\n- $c =\\dfrac{Q}{m\\Delta T}$ \n\nThe specific heat capacity equation applies to the sloped part of the graph, where temperature is changing. \n\n- $c = \\dfrac{2E_0-E_0}{m(T_2-T_1)}$ \n\n- $c = \\dfrac{E_0}{m(T_2-T_1)}$"},{"ref":"PH0285","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0285\n\nWater in a beaker is heated on a hot plate at a rate $P$. The graph shows the change in temperature of the water $T$ over time $t$. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-18%20at%204.38.15%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-18 at 4.38.15 PM\">\n\nWhich graph correctly shows the new change in temperature of the water when the rate of thermal energy transfer is increased? \n\nThe original graph is shown by the dotted line. \n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | | | |\n\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-18%20at%204.38.26%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-18 at 4.38.26 PM\"> | | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-18%20at%204.38.33%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-18 at 4.38.33 PM\">|\n\n|\n\n|C. |   |   |D.  | |\n\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-18%20at%204.38.39%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-18 at 4.38.39 PM\">  | | |  <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-18%20at%204.38.45%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-18 at 4.38.45 PM\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nWhen the power increases, the temperature will rise more quickly, giving a larger gradient for the first part. \n\nThe change of state will occur at the same temperature as the original graph but take less time to complete."},{"ref":"PH0286","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0286\n\nWater of mass $m$ at an initial temperature of $20\\degree$C is provided with thermal energy at a rate $P$. Its temperature rises to $30\\degree$C in time $t$. \n\nThe rate of energy transfer is doubled and the mass of the water is halved. What is the final temperature, in time $t$, if the initial temperature is again $20\\degree$C?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 30$\\degree$C\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 40$\\degree$C\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 60$\\degree$C\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 80$\\degree$C","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nUsing the formula for specific heat capacity:\n\n- $Q = mc\\Delta T \\hspace{2em} $\n\nWe can equate it to the energy as determined from the power formula:\n\n- $E = Pt$\n\n- $Pt = mc\\Delta T$\n\n- $\\Delta T = \\dfrac{Pt}{mc}$\n\nDoubling energy transfer rate will double the temperature rise. Halving the mass will also double the temperature rise. The change in temperature will be 4 times larger than in the initial case, which is $40\\degree$C. \n\nThis gives the final temperature as $60\\degree$C."},{"ref":"PH0346","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0346\n\nIn order to calculate the specific latent heat of fusion of ice, a mass $m$ of ice at a temperature of -2˚C was left inside a beaker until it changed into water at 20˚C.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-18%20at%204.43.52%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-18 at 4.43.52 PM\">\n\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with Chemix, https://chemix.org/]}}$\n\n The specific heat capacity of ice is $c_i$, the specific heat capacity of water is $c_w$, and the specific latent heat of fusion of ice is $L_F$.\n\nIf $q$ is the energy needed to make this change for 1 kilogram of ice, which expression gives the specific latent heat of fusion of ice $L_F$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $q-2c_i-20c_w$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $q-22c_i$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $q-22c_w$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $q-\\dfrac{2c_i-20c_w}{m}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe energy needed to change ice from -2$\\degree$C to 20$\\degree$C can be divided into:\n\n- The energy needed to increase the temperature of ice from -2$\\degree$C to 0$\\degree$C: \\\n\n$mc_i \\Delta T=mc_i (0-(-2))=2mc_i$\n\n- The energy needed to change ice to water, without change in temperature: \\\n\n$mL$\n\n- The energy needed to change water from 0$\\degree$C to 20$\\degree$C: \\\n\n$mc_w \\Delta T=mc_w (20-0)=20mc_w$\n\nThe total energy needed = $2mc_i+mL+20mc_w$\n\nSince $q$ is the energy for 1 kg, therefore the total energy needed = $mq$\n\nSo, to calculate L\n\n- $mq=2mc_i+mL+20mc_w$  \n\n- $L=\\dfrac{mq-2mc_i-20mc_w}{m}$  \n\n- $L=q-2c_i-20c_w$"},{"ref":"PH0347","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0347\n\nA sealed container contains a mixture of hydrogen and nitrogen gas. \n\nThe mass ratio $\\dfrac{\\textrm{mass of a hydrogen molecule}}{\\textrm{mass of a nitrogen molecule}}=\\dfrac{1}{7}$\n\nThe ratio $\\dfrac{\\textrm{average kinetic energy of hydrogen}}{\\textrm{average kinetic energy of nitrogen}}$ is\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 7\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{1}{7}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Dependent on the concentration of each gas.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe container is sealed.\n\nThe two gases are mixed together and therefore are in thermal equilibrium, which means they have the same temperature.\n\nThe temperature is a measure of the average kinetic energy, so both gases have the same average kinetic energy, which makes the ratio of their average kinetic energies to be 1."},{"ref":"PH0447","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0447\n\n100 mL of water at $20\\degree$C is poured into an aluminum container and heated at a constant rate until all of the water is boiled. After time $t_1$ water starts to boil, and at time $t_2$ all of the water has boiled away. \n\n \n\nConsider the following statements:\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n\n  | | |\n\n  | :-- | :-- |\n\n  | I. | If a glass container with the same mass is used instead of aluminum, $t_1$ increases. ($c_{\\textrm{glass}} < c_{\\textrm{aluminum}}$) |\n\n  | II. | If the container with water is thermally isolated, $t_2$ decreases. |\n\n  | III. | If more water is added to the container, $t_1$ increases. |\n\nWhich of the statements are correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Only I\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"I. Incorrect: As the heater heats both the water and the container, the container itself gains energy to increase temperature. Greater specific heat capacity means more energy is required to result in the same temperature change. So, for the glass container less energy is needed to reach $100\\degree$C which will take less time.\n\n \n\nII. Correct: When there is isolation for the container there is less heat lost to the surroundings, resulting in higher efficiency. So the boiling finishes earlier. \n\n \n\nIII. Correct: As the amount of water increases more energy is required to result in the same temperature change."},{"ref":"PH0448","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0448\n\nWhich of the graphs below best describes the change in average kinetic energy of $\\overline E_k$ the molecules of a beaker of water against time $t$ as it is heated at a constant rate until it completely boils away?\n\n| | | | | |\n\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n\n|| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-09-08%20at%207.43.35%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-09-08 at 7.43.35 AM\">  |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-09-08%20at%207.43.41%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-09-08 at 7.43.41 AM\">|\n\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-09-08%20at%207.43.47%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-09-08 at 7.43.47 AM\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-09-08%20at%207.43.51%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-09-08 at 7.43.51 AM\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nAverage kinetic energy increases as the temperature increases and stays constant during the vapourisation process. Moreover, average kinetic energy is zero at absolute zero ($-273\\degree$C) temperature, which is not the case for water ($0 - 100\\degree$C). Therefore we need to choose an answer that has a non-zero intercept on the temperature axis."},{"ref":"PH0468","marks":"12","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0468\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\n1. Define specific heat capacity. [2 marks]\n\n2. A sample of a substance initially in the solid state of mass of 200 g is heated by a heater of constant power. The variation of the temperature $T$ with time $t$ is shown on the graph below. \n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/PH0468zzz.png\" alt=\"PH0468zzz\">\n\n    1. The specific heat capacity of the sample in the liquid state is $1400$ J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$. Determine the power of the heater. [3 marks]\n\n    2. Determine the specific latent heat of vaporization of the sample. [2 marks]\n\n    3. Explain, by considering the molecular model, why the time needed to boil the sample is more than the time needed for melting the same sample. [3 marks]\n\n    4. It is realized that some of the sample in the liquid state evaporates during the heating process. State two differences between evaporation and boiling. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. The energy required to change the temperature by 1$\\degree$/K/unit degree $\\checkmark$\n\n    per unit mass/ of 1 kg mass $\\checkmark$\n\n2. \n\n    1. Usage of any time interval between 120 and 260 s $\\checkmark$\n\n        Usage of power = $\\dfrac{mc\\Delta T}{t}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $P = \\ll \\dfrac{(1400)(0.2)(60)}{140} = \\gg $ :answer[$120$ W] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:  \n\n        There are two time intervals in which temperature is kept constant. These intervals must be the time when melting and boiling takes place. The sample is in liquid form between these two time intervals. The time needed for increasing the sample's temperature from $30\\degree$C to $90\\degree$C is \n\n        - $260 - 120 = 140$ s\n\n        The energy supplied $Q$ by the heater in the mentioned time interval can be expressed in terms of the power $P$ of the heater; \n\n        - $Q=Pt=(P)(140)$\n\n        The specific heat capacity $c$;\n\n        - $c=\\dfrac{Q}{m\\Delta T}=\\dfrac{Pt}{m\\Delta T}=\\dfrac{(P)(140)}{m\\Delta T}$ where $m=0.2$ kg, $\\Delta T=60\\degree$C, and $c=1400$ J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$\n\n        - $\\begin{aligned}\n\n        1400&=\\frac{(P)(t)}{(0.2)(60)}\\\\\n\n        \\hspace{1em}\\\\  \n\n        (1400)(0.2)(60)&=140P\\\\\n\n        \\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n        \\frac{(1400)(0.2)(60)}{140}&=P\\\\\n\n        \\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n        120\\textrm{ W}&=P\\\\\n\n        \\end{aligned}$\n\n    2. ::indent \n\n        - $Q=Pt=(120)(280)=33600\\textrm{ J}$ $\\checkmark$ \n\n        \n\n        \n\n        (full credit given for usage of the previous answer for the power)\n\n        - $L=\\dfrac{33600}{0.20}=$ :answer[$168000$ J kg$^{-1}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        Specific latent heat L of any material;\n\n        - $L=\\frac{Q}{m}$\n\n        The time interval related to boiling is from 260 s to 540 s and heat $Q$ is required to change the state of the sample from liquid to gas; \n\n        - $Q=Pt=(120)(280)=33600$ J\n\n        So;\n\n        - $L=\\dfrac{33600}{0.20}=168000$ J kg$^{-1}$\n\n    3. In melting, energy is needed to weaken the bonds between molecules / separate them slightly $\\checkmark$\n\n        In boiling, the bonds are broken / molecules are separated widely $\\ll$ requiring more energy $\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Since the power of the heater is constant, more time is needed for boiling $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        In melting, energy is needed to weaken the bonds between molecules and separate them slightly. In boiling, the bonds are broken and molecules are separated with the energy. Therefore, more energy is needed for the boiling. Since the power of the heater is constant, more time is needed to supply more energy to the sample. \n\n    4. Any two from:\n\n        Evaporation takes place only at the surface of the liquid and Boiling happens through the liquid $\\checkmark$\n\n        Evaporation happens below boiling temperature and Boiling happens only at boiling temperature $\\checkmark$\n\n        Evaporation is slow/spontaneous and boiling is fast $\\checkmark$\n\n        Evaporation leads to cooling and boiling does not $\\checkmark$"},{"ref":"PH0469","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0469\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n1. Define the specific latent heat of fusion. [2 marks]\n\nAn ice cube of temperature $-20\\degree$C and mass $45$ g is placed in a bottle which is half filled with water of temperature $40\\degree$C and mass $1.2$ kg. Energy transfer only happens between the water and the ice.   \n\nThe following data are available:\n\nSpecific latent heat of fusion of ice $= 3.3 \\times 10^5$ J kg$^{-1}$\n\nSpecific heat capacity of ice $= 2.2 \\times 10^3$ J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$\n\nSpecific heat capacity of water $= 4.2 \\times 10^3$ J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$\n\n2. 1. Determine the final temperature $T$ of the water. [5 marks]\n\n    2. On the graph below, sketch how  the mass of **liquid** water in the bottle $m$ varies with the temperature $T$.   (no need to add values on the mass axis) [2]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202026-04-14%20at%207.49.17%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-04-14 at 7.49.17 AM\">","markScheme":"1. The energy required to change the state/phase of a unit mass of a substance at a constant temperature $\\checkmark$\n\n    between the liquid and solid states/phases$\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    \"Per kg\" or “per gram” would be acceptable rather than \"per unit mass\"\n\n    \n\n2. \n\n    1. Thermal energy lost of water / $Q=m_w c_w\\Delta T_w=(1.2)(4.2 \\times 10^3)(40-T)=201600-5040T$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        The energy needed to increase the temperature of the ice from $-20°C$ to $0°C$ / $Q_{ice}=m_ic_i\\Delta T_i=(45 \\times 10^{-3})(2.2 \\times 10^3)(20)=1980$ J\n\n        **and**\n\n        The energy needed to melt ice at $0°C$ / $Q_{melting}=m_iL=(45 \\times 10^{-3})(3.3 \\times 10^5)=14850$ J $\\checkmark$\n\n        The energy is needed to increase the temperature of melted ice (now it is water) from $0°C$ to $T$ / $Q_{iw}=m_ic_w\\Delta T_{iw}=(45 \\times 10^{-3})(4.2 \\times 10^3)T=189\\,T$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Recognition of energy lost by water is transferred to ice to reach a common temperature in the form of water / $Q=Q_{ice}+Q_{melting}+Q_{iw}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        \n\n        - $\\begin{aligned}\n\n        201600-5040T&=1980+14850+189T\\\\\n\n        \\ 184770&=5229T\\\\\n\n        \\end{aligned}$\n\n        \n\n        - $\\hspace{6.2em} T=$ :answer[$35\\degree$C]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        By assuming the final temperature of the water as $T$, the thermal energy lost $Q$ the water; \n\n        - $Q=m_w c_w\\Delta T_w=(1.2)(4.2 \\times 10^3)(40-T)=201600-5040T$\n\n        The energy $Q_{ice}$ is needed to increase the temperature of the ice from $-20\\degree$C to $0\\degree$C.  \n\n        - $Q_{ice}=m_ic_i\\Delta T_i=(45 \\times 10^{-3})(2.2 \\times10^3)(20)=1980$ J\n\n        The energy $Q_{melting}$ is needed to melt ice at $0\\degree$C;\n\n        - $Q_{melting}=m_iL=(45 \\times 10^{-3})(3.3 \\times 10^5)=14850$ J \n\n        The energy $Q_{iw}$ needed to increase the temperature of melted ice (now it is water) from $0\\degree$C to $T$\n\n        - $Q_{iw}=m_ic_w\\Delta T_{iw}=(45 \\times 10^{-3})(4.2 \\times 10^3)T=189T$\n\n        Since energy lost by water is taken by ice;\n\n        $\\begin{aligned}\n\n        Q&=Q_{ice}+Q_{melting}+Q_{iw}\\\\\n\n        201600-5040T&=1980+14850+189T\\\\\n\n        184770&=5229T\\\\\n\n        T&=35\\degree\\textrm{C}\n\n        \\end{aligned}$\n\n    2. $\\hspace{1em}$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202026-04-14%20at%207.53.42%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-04-14 at 7.53.42 AM\">\n\n        A vertical line for the increase in mass at $0\\degree$C $\\checkmark$\n\n        Horizontal lines apart from temperature = $0\\degree$C $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        Initially, the temperature of ice is increased with the energy provided by water. In this process, there is no change in the mass of the water. Then, the ice is melted at $0\\degree$C. During this event, the mass of the water in the system increases. After that, there is no ice left to be converted to water. Therefore, there is no change in mass for higher temperatures. This can be represented with the horizontal lines for the no change in mass and the vertical line for the increase in mass at $0\\degree$C."},{"ref":"PH0470","marks":"4","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0470\n\n**Maximum mark: 4**\n\n1. An electric heater with a power rating of 2.8 kW heats liquid that flows through it. The temperature of the liquid increases from $18\\degree$C to $38\\degree$C while passing through the heater. The flow rate of the liquid $\\sigma$ is $0.035$ kg s$^{-1}$. Calculate the specific heat capacity of the liquid. [2 marks]\n\n2. Suggest whether your answer for (a) is above or below the actual value of the specific heat capacity. Justify your answer. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Use of $c=\\ll \\dfrac{Pt}{m\\Delta T} \\gg= \\dfrac{P}{\\sigma \\Delta T}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $c=\\ll \\dfrac{2800}{(0.035)(20)}=\\gg$ :answer[$4000$ J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    The heat Q supplied to the liquid can be expressed as;\n\n    - $Q=Pt$\n\n    Specific heat capacity of the liquid;\n\n    - $c=\\dfrac{Q}{m\\Delta T}=\\dfrac{Pt}{m\\Delta T}$ \n\n    It can be rearranged as;\n\n    - $c=\\dfrac{P}{\\frac{m}{t}\\Delta T}=\\dfrac{P}{\\sigma\\Delta T}$\n\n    So\n\n    - $c=\\dfrac{2800}{(0.035)(20)}=4000$ J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$\n\n2. There is thermal energy lost to the surroundings during the heating process $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\ll$ Using $power \\times time$ in the calculations leads to $\\gg$ an overestimation of the specific heat capacity $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    There is thermal energy lost to the surroundings during the heating process. Therefore, the energy used up by the liquid to increase its temperature is less than $power \\times time$. \n\n    \n\n    - $c = \\dfrac{Pt}{m\\Delta T}$\n\n    \n\n    Because $power \\times time$ in the calculations is an overestimation of the energy given to the liquid, it results in an overestimation of the specific heat capacity value."},{"ref":"PH0510","marks":"13","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH0510\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\nIn an experiment, a water-filled beaker is positioned on an electronic balance. An immersion heater with a power rating of $P$ is introduced into the water. Once the water begins to boil, a researcher monitored the mass $m$ of the liquid in the beaker over time $t$ once the boiling commenced.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/PH0510-4.png\" alt=\"PH0510-4\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with chemix - https://chemix.org/]}}$\n\nThe theoretically predicted relationship between time $t$ and $m$ is\n\n- $L_v=\\dfrac{Pt}{C-m}$\n\n$C$ is the initial mass of the water when $t=0\\, $s and $L_v$ is the latent heat of vaporisation of water.\n\n$\\\\$\n\n1.   Give the units of $L_v$ in fundamental SI units. [2 marks]\n\n2. In a particular experiment, the researcher uses a heater of power $65.0\\pm0.1\\ $W and measures $m=0.120\\pm0.001\\, $kg, $C=0.125\\pm0.001\\ $kg, and $t=600\\pm1\\ $s.\n\n    1. Calculate the percentage uncertainty in the measured value of $L_v$. [3 marks]\n\n    2. For this experiment, calculate the value of $L_v$ and its absolute uncertainty. [2 marks]\n\n    3. The accepted value of $L_v$ is $2.3 \\times 10^6 \\, $J kg$^{-1}$. Explain why the experimental value does not match the accepted value. [2 marks]\n\n3. The researcher plots a graph to show how *m* varies with *t*.\n\n    1.     Outline why *P* must be kept constant during the experiment. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Assuming the relationship $L_v=\\dfrac{Pt}{C-m}$ is correct, sketch the expected mass-time relationship on the axes below. (There is no need to put numbers to the axes.) [2 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-04-07%20at%205.20.22%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-07 at 5.20.22 PM\">\n\n    3. Outline how to obtain the value of $L_v$ from the graph you have drawn  in c(ii). [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. $Unit\\ of\\ Power=\\ll\\dfrac{\\ce{kg} \\ \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}\\,\\ce{m}}{\\ce{s}}=\\gg\\ \\ce{kg} \\ \\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-3}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $Unit\\ of\\ L_v=\\dfrac{\\ce{kg} \\ \\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-3}\\,\\ce{s}}{\\ce{kg}}= \\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}$  $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n    - $Power=\\dfrac{W}{t}=\\dfrac{Fs}{t}=\\dfrac{mas}{t}$\n\n    Substituting in the fundamental units for each of the variables gives\n\n    - $\\textrm{Unit of Power}=\\dfrac{\\ce{kg} \\ \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}\\,\\ce{m}}{\\ce{s}}=\\ce{kg} \\ \\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-3}$\n\n    The unit of power is Watts. It is equal to kg m$^2$ s$^{-3}$.\n\n    Both *m* and *C* have fundamental units of kg, and we are multiplying but time which has units of s.\n\n    Therefore\n\n    - $Unit\\ of\\ L_v=\\dfrac{\\ce{kg} \\ \\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-3}\\,\\ce{s}}{\\ce{kg}}=\\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}$\n\n2.\n    1. The percentage uncertainty of $\\left(C-m\\right)= \\ll \\dfrac{\\Delta \\left(C-m \\right)}{\\left(C-m \\right)}\\times 100\\% = \\dfrac{0.002}{0.005} \\times 100\\%=≫40\\%$\n$\\checkmark$\n\n        Add percentage uncertainty $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\ll \\textrm{The percentage uncertainty of } L_v=0.154\\% + 0.167\\% + 40\\% =\\gg $ :answer[40%] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$\\\n        The $C-m$ with its absolute uncertainty;\n\n        $\\left(C-m\\right)\\pm \\Delta \\left(C-m\\right)= \\left(0.125-0.120\\right)\\pm \\left(0.001+0.001\n        \\right)=0.005\\pm0.002 \\ $kg\n\n        The percentage uncertainty of the given quantities:\n\n        - $The\\ percentage\\ uncertainty\\ of\\ Power=\\dfrac{\\Delta P}{P} \\times 100\\%= \\dfrac{0.1}{65.0} \\times 100\\%=0.154\\%$\n\n        - $The\\ percentage\\ uncertainty\\ of\\ time= \\dfrac{\\Delta t}{t} \\times 100\\%= \\dfrac{1}{600} \\times 100\\%=0.167\\%$\n\n        - $The\\ percentage\\ uncertainty\\ of\\ \\left(C-m\\right)=\\dfrac{\\Delta \\left(C-m\\right)}{\\left(C-m\\right)} \\times 100\\%= \\dfrac{0.002}{0.005} \\times 100\\%=40\\%$\n\n        Using the rules of propagation of uncertainties;\n\n        - $The\\ percentage\\ uncertainty\\ of\\ L_v=0.154\\%+0.167\\%+40\\%=40.3\\%$\n\n    2. $L_v \\ = \\ \\ll\\dfrac{65\\times600}{0.125-0.120}=\\dfrac{39000}{0.005}=\\ \\ \\gg$ :answer[$8\\times{10}^6$] $ \\ll$  J kg$^{-1}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\Delta L_v \\ = \\ \\ll \\dfrac{40.3 \\times 7.8 \\times 10^6}{100} = \\ \\gg $ :answer[$3 \\times 10^6$] $ \\ll $ J  kg$^{-1} \\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        *allow ECF for use of calculated $\\%$ uncertainty from (i)*\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$\\\n        When the given values are substituted in the relationship, the value of $L_v$\n\n        - $L_v=\\dfrac{65\\times600}{0.125-0.120}=\\dfrac{39000}{0.005}=7.8\\times{10}^6\\ $J kg$^{-1}$\n\n        The absolute uncertainty $\\Delta L_v$ of  $L_v$:\n\n        - $\\Delta L_v = \\dfrac{40.3 \\times 7.8 \\times 10^{6}}{100} = 3.14 \\times 10^{6}$ J kg$^{-1}$\n\n    3. Heat lost to the environment $\\checkmark$\n\n        Causes the m value in the equation to be too low/means not all P contributed to the vaporisation of water $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$\\\n        Due to environmental losses, a portion of the heat input is not effectively utilized for boiling, resulting in a lower quantity of water actually boiling compared to the ideal scenario. As a consequence, the observed mass ($m$) is higher and the corrected mass ($C-m$) is lower, leading to a calculated latent heat of vaporization ($L_v$) that exceeds the expected value.\n\n        Alternatively, the equation assumes that all the power supplied by the heater is utilized for boiling the water. However, in reality, there are losses, causing the actual power ($P$) to be lower than the assumed value. This leads to an overestimation of the latent heat of vaporization ($L_v$) in the equation, resulting in a higher value than the accepted value.\n\n3.\n    1. To eliminate variables other than *m* and *t*\\\n    **OR** \\\n    It is a control variable\\\n    **OR** \\\n    To isolate the relationship between *m* and *t* $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$\\\n        To investigate a relationship between two quantities, other variables must be kept constant. In this experiment, It is the power *P*.\n\n    2. Straight line $\\checkmark$ \\\n    With a negative gradient $\\checkmark$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-04-07%20at%205.21.42%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-07 at 5.21.42 PM\">\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$\\\n        When the relationship is rearranged;\n\n        $\\begin{aligned}\n        L_v&=\\dfrac{Pt}{C-m}\\\\\n        CL_v-mL_v&=Pt\\\\\n        CL_v-Pt&=mL_v\\\\\n    m&=-\\dfrac{P}{L_v}t+C\n\\end{aligned}$\n\n        This relationship is similar to\n\n        - $y=mx+c$\n\n        We can see that this should be a straight line with gradient $-\\dfrac{P}{L_v}$ and a vertical axis intercept of $C$.\n\n        Therefore, it represents a straight line with a negative gradient.\n\n    3. ::indent\n        - $L_v=\\dfrac{P}{\\textrm{the slope of the graph}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$\\\n        According to\n\n        - $y=mx+c$\n\n        and\n\n        - $m=-\\dfrac{P}{L_v}t+C$\n\n        Similarly, the slope of the line equals to $-\\dfrac{P}{L_v}$."},{"ref":"PH0512","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH0512\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\n1. In an experiment, a beaker containing liquid of mass $m$ is heated with a heater of power *P*. Then the variation of temperature *T* of the liquid with time *t* is recorded.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-04-16%20at%204.05.24%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-16 at 4.05.24 PM\">\n    $\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with chemix - https:// chemix.org/]}}$\n\n    The data from the experiment is shown on the graph below.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-06-04%20at%205.04.42%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-04 at 5.04.42 PM\">\n\n     1. On the graph, draw the line of best fit for the data. [1 mark]\n\n     2. Explain why it cannot be concluded that the relationship between temperature and time is directly proportional. [1 mark]\n\n2. Theory suggests that\n\n    - $\\dfrac{T-T_0}{t}=k$\n\n    where $T_0$ is the temperature when $t=0$ s.\n\n    1. Using the data given, calculate the percentage uncertainty of $(T-T_0)$ at time 25 s. [3 marks]\n\n    2.     Use the graph to determine constant *k*. [2 marks]\n\n    3. Theory also suggests that another expression for *k* is given by\n\n        - $k=\\dfrac{P}{mc}$,\nwhere *c* is another constant.\n\n        Determine the units of constant *c*, expressing your answer in fundamental SI units. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. A straight line that passes within all the uncertainty bars $\\checkmark$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-06-04%20at%205.05.56%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-04 at 5.05.56 PM\">\n\n    2. The graph does not pass through the origin.\n\n        **OR**\n\n        Celsius cannot be used for temperature proportionality/Kelvin temperature is proportional to average kinetic energy.  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    To have a proportional relationship between two quantities, the graph should be straight and pass through the origin. Even though a straight line can be drawn on the graph, it does not pass through the origin.\n\n        Furthermore, Celsius units for temperature are not directly related to a physical quantity of a substance so a proportional relationship would have little meaning. If the temperature units were in Kelvin, which is representative of the average kinetic energy of the particles of the substance, proportionality would have significant meaning. With conversion to Kelvin, the data in this experiment would not meet the criteria for proportionality.\n\n2.\n    1. $T_0=20\\pm0.5$ and $T=31 \\pm 0.5$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $T-T_o=11 \\pm 1 \\checkmark$\n\n        $\\ll \\dfrac{\\Delta (T-T_0)}{T-T_0} \\times 100\\%= \\dfrac{1}{11} \\times 100\\% = \\gg $ :answer[$9\\% $] $\\checkmark$\n\n    2. The gradient of the graph = $k$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Correct calculation of the gradient of the graph  reference to (a)(i). *Must use a time range of at least 15s in gradient calculation* $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\ll\\ k=\\dfrac{35-20}{34.5-0}=\\dfrac{15}{34.5}=0.43 \\gg$ accept answers in the range from 0.40 to 0.50\n\n    3. $c=\\ll\\dfrac{Pt}{m\\left(T-T_0\\right)}=\\gg \\dfrac{Pt}{m \\Delta T}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\ll\\dfrac{\\ce{kg}\\,\\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-3}\\,\\ce{s}}{\\ce{kg}\\ \\ce{K}}=\\dfrac{\\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}}{\\ce{K}}=\\gg\\ \\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}\\,\\ce{K}^{-1}$\n(*also accept if students find answer using knowledge of specific heat capacity*) $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The following can be written;\n\n        - $\\dfrac{T-T_0}{t}=k=\\dfrac{P}{mc}$\n\n        When the relationship is rearranged to find c;\n\n        - $c=\\dfrac{Pt}{m\\left(T-T_0\\right)}= \\dfrac{Pt}{m \\Delta T}$\n\n        When units are substituted;\n\n        - $Unit\\ of\\ c=\\dfrac{\\ce{W}\\,\\ce{s}}{\\ce{kg}\\,\\ce{K}}$\n\n        Since\n\n        - $Watts=\\ce{kg} \\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-2} \\ \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}=\\ce{kg} \\ \\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-3}$\n\n        - $SI\\ unit\\ of\\ c=\\dfrac{\\ce{kg}\\,\\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-3}\\ce{s}}{\\ce{kg}\\,\\ce{K}}=\\dfrac{\\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}}{\\ce{K}}=\\ce{m}^2\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}\\,\\ce{K}^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH0619","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0619\n\nA liquid of mass $M$, temperature $4T$ specific heat capacity $S$ is held in an insulated container of negligible mass. A second liquid of mass $2M$, temperature $T$ and specific heat capacity $3S$ is added to the container.\n\nWhat is the equilibrium temperature of the mixture?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  \\dfrac{10T}{7}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  \\dfrac{7T}{4}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  2T$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  \\dfrac{25T}{7}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nSince no energy is exchanged with the surroundings (the container is insulated), the overall internal energy change is zero. The internal energy change of the first liquid must therefore be equal in magnitude but opposite in sign to the internal energy change of the second.\n\nTherefore, substituting into the equation $E=mc \\Delta T$ for each liquid and using $T_f$ to represent the equilibrium temperature,\n\n- $Q=mc(T_f-T_i)$\n\nSubstituting the given information,\n\n- $Q_1=MS(T_f-4T)$\n\n- $\\Delta E_2=6MS(T_f-T)$\n\nUsing conservation of energy\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\nQ_1+Q_2&=0\\\\\nQ_1&= -Q_2\\\\\nMS(T_f-4T)&=-6MS(T_f-T)\\\\\n\\hspace{4em}MS(T_f-4T)&=6MS(T-{T_f})\\\\\nT_f-4T&=6T-6T_f\\\\\n7T_f&=10T\\\\\nT_f&=\\dfrac{10T}{7}\\\\\n\\end{aligned}$"},{"ref":"PH0621","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0621\n\nA pendulum consists of a mass $M$ with a specific heat capacity $S$ suspended from a point by a light string of length $L$. The pendulum is placed in an insulated container filled with a mass $2M$ of liquid with a specific heat capacity $2S$. The pendulum is released from rest with the string horizontal. Assume that acceleration due to gravity is $g$ and that both have the same starting temperature.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/PH0621aaaa.png\" alt=\"PH0621aaaa\">\n\nIf all of the energy from the pendulum is converted to thermal energy, what is the temperature change of the liquid?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  \\dfrac{gL}{2S}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  \\dfrac{gL}{5S}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  \\dfrac{2gL}{5S}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  \\dfrac{5gL}{2S}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nIn this situation, there is a conversion of gravitational potential energy ($mg\\Delta h$) to thermal energy ($mc\\Delta T$). The change in height of the pendulum is $L$.\n\nThe gravitational potential energy of the pendulum ($MgL$) is completely converted to thermal energy of the liquid mass 2M and pendulum mass M.\n\nTherefore,\n\n- $mg \\Delta h = (mc \\Delta T)_{pendulum}+(mc \\Delta T)_{liquid}$\n- $MgL=MS\\Delta T+(2M)(2S)\\Delta T$\n\n- $MgL=5MS\\Delta T$\n\n- $\\Delta T=\\dfrac{gL}{5S}$"},{"ref":"PH0622","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0622\n\nThe graph shows how the temperature of a solid wax block varies with time. The solid wax has a specific heat capacity of $2$ kJ kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$ and it absorbs heat at a rate of $20$ W.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-18%20at%204.49.13%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-18 at 4.49.13 PM\">\n\nWhat is the specific latent heat of fusion of wax?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  50$ J g$^{-1}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  100$ J g$^{-1}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  200$ J g$^{-1}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  400$ J g$^{-1}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe heating rate of the solid can be found from the gradient of the first section of the graph.\n\n- gradient = $\\dfrac{\\textrm{rise}}{\\textrm{run}}=\\dfrac{(310-280)}{60}=\\ 0.5$ K s$^{-1}= \\dfrac{\\Delta T}{t}$\n\nFor the heating of the solid,\n\n- $Pt = mc\\Delta T$\n\ntherefore the mass of the solid can now be determined from\n\n- $m=\\dfrac{P}{(\\frac{\\Delta T}{t})(c)}$\n\n- $=\\dfrac{20\\textrm{ J s}^{-1}}{(0.5\\textrm{ K s}^{-1})(2000\\textrm{ J kg}^{-1}\\textrm{ K}^{-1})}$\n\n- $=20$ g\n\nThe system remains at the melting temperature for 200 s;\n\n- $E = mL$\n\n- $Pt = mL$\n\nTherefore\n\n- $L=\\dfrac{(P)(t)}{m}=\\dfrac{(20\\textrm{ W})(200\\textrm{ s})}{20\\textrm{ g}}=200\\textrm{ J g}^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH0623","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0623\n\nA thermally isolated system consists of a coin of weight $mg$ and specific heat capacity $c$ suspended in a vacuum at a vertical distance *H* above a solid block. The block has mass 4*m*, specific heat capacity 2*c* and latent heat of fusion *L*. The melting temperature of the block is an amount $\\Delta T$ greater than the initial temperature of the block and the coin. The coin is dropped on to the block.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/PH0623.png\" alt=\"PH0623\">\n\nWhat is the minimum value of *H* required for the block to melt due to the collision?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{8c \\Delta T}{g}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{8c \\Delta T + 4L}{g}$\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{9c \\Delta T + 4L}{g}$\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{9c \\Delta T - 4L}{g}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nIn order to melt the block, the kinetic energy $E_k$ supplied by the coin must exceed the total latent heat of fusion for the block ($4mL$) plus the energy required to increase the temperature of the coin and the block by an amount $\\Delta T$ ($mc\\Delta T+(4m)(2c)\\Delta T$)=$9mc\\Delta T$.\n\nHence,\n\n- $E_k=9mc\\Delta T+4mL$\n\nThe kinetic energy on impact equals the initial gravitational potential energy of the coin, equal to $mgH$.\n\nConsequently,\n\n- $mgH=9mc\\Delta T+4mL$\n\nTherefore,\n\n- $H= \\dfrac{9c\\Delta T+4L}{g} $"},{"ref":"PH0624","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0624\n\nAn ice cube of mass $m$ at its melting temperature is placed in a mass $M$ of water at an initial temperature of $20\\degree$C. Assume that the specific heat capacity of liquid water is $4$ kJ kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$ and it has a specific latent heat of fusion of $320$ J g$^{-1}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/PH0624zzz.png\" alt=\"PH0624zzz\">\n\nAssuming that the system is thermally isolated, what is the minimum value of M required for the ice to completely melt?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  0.25m$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  4.0m$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  16m$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  80m$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nNote that the units of J g$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$ are equivalent to kJ kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$ for specific heat capacity. Also, the units of J g$^{-1}$ are equivalent to kJ kg$^{-1}$.\n\nThe minimum amount of energy required to completely melt the ice is equal to the product of $m$ and the specific latent heat of fusion $L$. The thermal energy available for this process is given by the product of water mass $M$, the specific heat capacity $c$, and the temperature change of the water (20 K). Therefore,\n\n- $mL=Mc\\Delta T$\n\n- $M=\\dfrac{mL}{c\\Delta T}$\n\n- $M=\\dfrac{m(320\\textrm{ kJ kg}^{-1})}{(4\\textrm{ kJ kg}^{-1}\\ K^{-1})(20\\textrm{ K})}$\n\n- $M=\\dfrac{320m}{80}$\n\n- $M=4m$"},{"ref":"PH0625","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0625\n\nA body of water loses energy at a constant rate. Assume that the specific heat capacity of ice is half the specific heat capacity of liquid water.\n\nWhich graph shows a **possible** variation of temperature with time?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n|| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/PH0625aa.png\" alt=\"PH0625aa\"> |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/PH0625bb.png\" alt=\"PH0625bb\">|\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  |  <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/PH0625cc.png\" alt=\"PH0625cc\"> |  |  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/PH0625dd.png\" alt=\"PH0625dd\"> |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nIce has half the specific heat capacity of liquid water and the rate of energy loss is constant. Therefore, after freezing the temperature of the system decreases at twice the rate compared to when cooling in the liquid state."},{"ref":"PH0627","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0627\n\nA chemical reaction occurs inside a solid of mass $m$ and specific heat capacity $c$. The initial temperature of the solid is $50$ K.\n\nWhat is the minimum energy that the reaction must release to increase the temperature of the solid to 100$\\degree$C?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $50 mc$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $100 mc$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $323 mc$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $373 mc$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe temperature of the solid is 373 K (100 + 273) after the reaction takes place, so a temperature change of $(373-50)=323$ K is required to achieve the target temperature.\n\nThe energy $Q$ needed for this change is related to the temperature change $\\Delta T$ by the expression\n\n- $Q =mc\\Delta T=323mc$."},{"ref":"PH0628","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0628\n\nA hollow, insulated ring is packed with a mass $M$ of ice pellets at an initial temperature of 0$\\degree$C. A mass 3*M* of steam is injected into the ring and circulates around it at an initial temperature of 100$\\degree$C. Assume that the specific latent heat of vaporization is seven times the specific latent heat of fusion *L*.\n\n*L* is also equal to the energy required to increase the temperature of a unit mass of liquid water by 80$\\degree$C.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-18%20at%204.58.41%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-18 at 4.58.41 PM\">\n\nWhat are the final states of matter in the ring?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Solid and liquid\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Liquid\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Gas\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Liquid and gas","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFirstly, consider the energy $E_{cond}$ released by total condensation of the steam and the energy $E_{melt}$ required for complete melting of the ice. Using the equations for latent heat $Q=mL$:\n\n- $\\hspace{2em}$ $Q_{cond}=3M\\times7L=21ML$\n\n- $\\hspace{2em}$ $Q_{melt}=ML$\n\nSince the energy provided by condensation of the steam is sufficient to completely melt the ice, answer **A** cannot be correct.\n\nTo convert the water from the melted ice to steam, the energy supplied by the circulating steam must be sufficient to heat the water to 100$\\degree$C and then overcome the latent heat of vaporization. However, if all the water in the system is to become steam, none of this energy can be provided by condensing steam. Since the circulating steam is initially at 100$\\degree$C, it follows that insufficient energy is available to convert all the water in the system to steam. Hence, answer **C** is incorrect.\n\nAfter all of the ice has melted, the remaining energy available to heat the resulting water from 0$\\degree$C is:\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $Q_{heat}=Q_{cond}-Q_{melt}=20ML$\n\nNow consider the heat energy that then raises the temperature of the water created by the melting ice.\n\nTo find the amount of energy needed to raise the water\\'s temperature to 100$\\degree$C , we use the equation:\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $Q = mc\\Delta T$\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $\\dfrac{Q}{m}=\\ c\\Delta T$\n\nFrom the given information, we know that *L* is the energy per unit mass  when $\\Delta T = 80$.\n\nThus\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $L=c\\left(80\\right)$\n\nGiving\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $c=\\dfrac{L}{80}$\n\nand to raise the temperature by 100$\\degree$C,\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $Q=mc\\Delta T$\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $Q=M(\\dfrac{L}{80})(100)$\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $= 1.25\\ ML$\n\nAs there is still $20 ML$ of heat energy in the non-condensed steam, some but not all of the steam will condense.\n\nThis will result in the final state being a mixture of liquid water at 100$\\degree$C and steam at 100$\\degree$C.\n\nTherefore the answer is **D**\n\nAs an alternative approach:\n\nThe sum of this energy and the energy removed from the condensed water as it cools must balance the energy used to heat the water from the melted ice. Assuming that the ring exclusively contains liquid water when the system equilibrates, and expressing the final temperature $T_f$ in units of $\\degree$C, one obtains the equation:\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $20mL+3mc\\left(100-T_f\\right)=mcT_f$\n\nRearranging for $T_f$ yields\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $T_f=\\dfrac{300c+20L}{4c}$\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $\\therefore \\ T_f=75\\degree C+5Lc$\n\nFinally, as it is stated that L equals 80c,\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ $T_f=475\\degree C$\n\nThis result is greater than 100$\\degree$C, which is not possible for liquid water. This contradiction means that some of the water must remain as steam in the final system, so answer **D** is correct."},{"ref":"PH0646","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0646\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nA $100$ g chocolate bar is placed onto a hot plate. The chocolate is initially at its melting temperature of $30\\degree$C.\n\nHeat is transferred from the plate to the chocolate at a constant rate $P_{in}$ of $12$ W, causing it to melt at a constant rate of $6$ g min$^{-1}$. After all the chocolate has melted, the liquid chocolate continues to heat to a temperature of $353$ K.\n\nSpecific heat capacity of melted chocolate = $1.0$ kJ kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$\n\nSpecific latent heat of fusion of chocolate = $50$ J g$^{-1}$\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/PH0646...png\" alt=\"PH0646..\">\n\n1. State how the kinetic and potential energy of the chocolate change as it melts. [2 marks]\n\n2.  1. Determine the amount of energy required to heat the **liquid** chocolate from the melting temperature to $353$ K. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Show that approximately 58% of the supplied energy is lost to the surroundings while the chocolate is melting. [3 marks]\n\n3. The graph shows how the temperature of the chocolate changes with time.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-18%20at%205.18.38%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-18 at 5.18.38 PM\">\n\n    Draw on the same graph a new line to show how temperature would change for a larger mass of chocolate with the same starting conditions.  [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.  The potential energy of the chocolate increases. $\\checkmark$\n\n    The kinetic energy of the chocolate stays constant. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The potential energy of the chocolate increases as the intermolecular distance is increased.\n\n    The kinetic energy stays constant as the temperature of the chocolate does not change during melting; all of the energy supplied by the hotplate is used to increase potential energy.\n\n2.  1.  ::indent\n        - $Q=(m_{choc})(c_{choc})\\Delta T$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $Q=(0.1\\textrm{ kg})(1\\textrm{ kJ kg}^{-1}\\textrm{ K}^{-1})(50\\textrm{ K})=$ :answer[$5$ kJ] or :answer[$5000$ J] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The total internal energy change of the chocolate over the $50$ K temperature change ($303$ K to $353$ K) is given by the product of the mass, specific heat capacity and temperature change.\n\n    2.  ::indent\n        - $Q_L=m_{choc}L_{choc}=5\\textrm{ kJ}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $Q_{in}=Power\\times\\dfrac{mass}{melting\\ rate}=(12\\textrm{ W})\\dfrac{100\\, g}{0.1\\textrm{ g s}^{-1}}=12\\textrm{ kJ}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{Q_{surroundings}}{Q_{in}}=1-\\dfrac{Q_L}{Q_{in}}=1-\\dfrac{5}{12}=0.583=58.3 \\%$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The melting rate given in the question is $6$ g min$^{-1}$\n\n        This corresponds to $6$ g every $60$ s, or $0.1$ g s$^{-1}$\n\n        The time required for the chocolate to completely melt can be found:\n\n        - $time=\\dfrac{mass}{melting\\ rate}=\\dfrac{100\\textrm{ g}}{0.1\\textrm{ g s}^{-1}}=1000$ s\n\n        The energy produced by the blowtorch in this time can be found from\n\n        - $E=Pt = (12)(1000)\\ =\\ 12000\\textrm{ J}\\ =\\ 12\\textrm{ kJ}$\n\n        The amount of latent heat required to melt the chocolate is given by\n\n        - $Q_L=mL =(100\\textrm{ g})(50\\textrm{ J g}^{-1})=5000\\textrm{ J}=5\\textrm{ kJ}$\n\n        Energy lost is\n\n        - $12$ kJ $-5$ kJ $=7$ kJ\n\n        As a percentage of the total energy, this gives\n\n        - $\\dfrac{7}{12} (100 \\%)  = 58.3 \\% $\n\n3.  $\\hspace{1em}$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-18%20at%205.19.49%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-18 at 5.19.49 PM\">\n\n    Plateau continues up to a higher $t$ value. $\\checkmark$\n\n    Temperature increases at a lower rate after plateau. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The total time required to melt the chocolate is given by\n\n    - $t_{melt}=\\dfrac{mass}{melting\\ rate}$\n\n    Consequently, increasing the mass of the chocolate causes the plateau to continue to a higher $t$ value.\n\n    After melting, temperature changes at a rate given by\n\n    - $\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{\\Delta t}=\\dfrac{P_{in}-P_{out}}{(m_{choc})(c_{choc})}$\n\n    Thus, increasing the mass of chocolate decreases the rate at which temperature changes."},{"ref":"PH0650","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0650\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nTwo identical buckets of water are suspended from spring scales so that their weights can be monitored. Each bucket initially contains 500 g of water at a temperature of 20$\\degree$C. The water in Bucket **A** is heated at a constant power of 1.0 kW. Bucket **B** is held at a constant temperature but water leaks from it at a rate of 6.0 g min$^{-1}$. Assume that the specific heat capacity of water is 4.0 J g$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$ and the specific latent heat of vaporization is 2.0 kJ g$^{-1}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-18%20at%205.21.11%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-18 at 5.21.11 PM\">\n    $\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with chemix - https://chemix.org/]}}$\n\n1. Calculate the rate of change of temperature in Bucket **A** before it reaches the boiling temperature of water. [2 marks]\n\n2. Estimate the total time required for the masses of the two buckets to equalize. [4 marks]\n\n3. The dashed line on the graph shows how the mass difference between Bucket **A** and Bucket **B** changes with time $t$ up to the point of mass equalization.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-18%20at%205.22.11%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-18 at 5.22.11 PM\">\n\n    When the two buckets are empty, each bucket is refilled with 500 g of water. The process of heating Bucket **A** and draining Bucket **B** is repeated with the input power to Bucket **A** now doubled to 2 kW. All other parameters remain the same as in the first experiment.\n\n    On the same graph, draw the variation of mass difference in the second experiment. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. ::indent\n    - $\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{\\Delta t} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{power}}{\\textrm{mass}  \\times  \\textrm{spec. \\ heat \\ capacity}} = \\dfrac{1000\\textrm{ J s}^{-1}}{(0.5\\textrm{ kg})(4000\\textrm{ J kg}^{-1}\\textrm{ K}^{-1})}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{\\Delta t} =$ :answer[$0.5\\textrm{ K s}^{-1}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The energy $E$ supplied to the water in Bucket **A** is related to the temperature change $\\Delta T$ by the expression\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $E = mc \\Delta T$\n\n    where $m$ is the mass and $c$ is the specific heat capacity.\n\n    Hence,\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $P = \\dfrac{\\Delta E}{\\Delta t} = mc \\dfrac{\\Delta T}{\\Delta t}$\n\n    Rearranging for $\\dfrac{\\Delta E}{\\Delta t}$ yields\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{\\Delta E}{\\Delta t} = \\dfrac{P}{mc} = \\dfrac{1000\\textrm{ J s}^{-1}}{(0.5\\textrm{ kg})(4000\\textrm{ J kg}^{-1}\\textrm{ K}^{-1})} = 0.5\\textrm{ K s}^{-1}$\n\n2. Boiling rate $=\\dfrac{P}{L}=0.5\\textrm{ g s}^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $t_{boil}=\\dfrac{373\\textrm{ K}-293\\textrm{ K}}{0.5\\textrm{ K s}^{-1}}=160\\textrm{ s}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $500\\textrm{ g}-\\left(0.1\\textrm{ g s}^{-1}\\right)t=500\\textrm{ g} -\\left(0.5\\textrm{ g s}^{-1}\\right)\\left(t-160\\textrm{ s}\\right)$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $t=$ :answer[$200$ s] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Using the rate value calculated in Part (a), the water reaches its boiling point after a time $t_{boil}$ given by\n\n    - $\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{\\frac{\\Delta T}{\\Delta t}} = \\dfrac{373\\textrm{ K} - 293\\textrm{ K}}{0.5\\textrm{ K s}^{-1}} = 160$ s\n\n    After this time, mass is lost as steam at a rate $\\dfrac{\\Delta m}{\\Delta t}$ that can be found from\n\n    - $E\\ =\\ Pt\\ =\\ mL$\n\n    where $L$ is the specific latent heat of fusion.\n\n    This gives\n\n    - $\\dfrac{\\Delta m}{\\Delta t} = \\dfrac{P}{L} = \\dfrac{1000\\textrm{ J s}^{-1}}{2000\\textrm{ J g}^{-1}} = 0.5\\textrm{ g s}^{-1}$\n\n    Finally, to calculate the time $t$ of equalization, measured from the beginning of the heating process, one must consider the equation\n\n    - $mass\\ of\\ right\\ bucket=mass\\ of\\ left\\ bucket\\ $\n\n    Recalling the rates of mass loss in each case, in addition to the time required to achieve boiling, gives\n\n    - $500\\textrm{ g}-\\left(0.1\\textrm{ g s}^{-1}\\right)t=500\\textrm{ g} -\\left(0.5\\textrm{ g s}^{-1}\\right)\\left(t-160\\textrm{ s}\\right)$\n\n    Rearranging for $t$ yields\n\n    - $0.4\\ \\ t=80$ s\n\n    -  $\\therefore\\ t=200$ s\n\n3. Mass difference increases at same rate as first experiment $\\checkmark$\n\n    Maximum mass difference halved with respect to first experiment $\\checkmark$\n\n    **OR**\n\n    Maximum mass difference occurs twice as quickly as in the first experiment\n\n    Mass difference decreases from maximum at approximately twice the rate of the first experiment $\\checkmark$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-18%20at%205.22.45%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-18 at 5.22.45 PM\">\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    As the temperature in Bucket **A** increases towards the boiling temperature, the rate of change of the mass difference depends exclusively on the rate of draining from Bucket **B**. This remains the same as in the first experiment, meaning that the gradient of the increase does not change.\n\n    As the temperature in Bucket **A** increases towards the boiling temperature, the rate of change of the mass difference depends exclusively on the rate of draining from Bucket **B**. This remains the same as in the first experiment, meaning that the gradient of the increase does not change.\n\n    As the power entering Bucket **A** has doubled and the rate of change of temperature is given by\n\n    - $\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{\\Delta t} = \\dfrac{P}{mc}$\n\n    Temperature increases twice as quickly as in the first experiment.\n\n    Consequently, the maximum mass difference is half the value of the first experiment, and peak mass difference occurs in half the time of the first experiment.\n\n    During boiling, the mass difference changes at a rate given by\n\n    - $\\dfrac{\\Delta (m_A - m_B)}{\\Delta t} = 0.1\\textrm{ g s}^{-1} - \\dfrac{P}{L}$\n\n    In the first experiment, this expression yields\n\n    - $\\dfrac{\\Delta (m_A - m_B)}{\\Delta t} = (0.1 - 0.5) \\textrm{ g s}^{-1} = -0.4 \\textrm{ g s}^{-1}$\n\n    Whilst in the second experiment one obtains\n\n    - $\\dfrac{\\Delta (m_A - m_B)}{\\Delta t} = (0.1 - 1.0)\\textrm{ g s}^{-1} = -0.9\\textrm{ g s}^{-1}$\n\n    Hence, the rate of mass change in the second experiment is 2.25 times the rate of change in the first experiment.\n\n    - $\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{\\Delta t} = \\dfrac{P}{mc}$\n\n    Temperature increases twice as quickly as in the first experiment.\n\n    Consequently, the maximum mass difference is half the value of the first experiment, and peak mass difference occurs in half the time of the first experiment.\n\n    During boiling, the mass difference changes at a rate given by\n\n    - $\\dfrac{\\Delta (m_A - m_B)}{\\Delta t} = 0.1\\textrm{ g s}^{-1} - \\dfrac{P}{L}$\n\n    In the first experiment, this expression yields\n\n    - $\\dfrac{\\Delta (m_A - m_B)}{\\Delta t} = (0.1 - 0.5)\\textrm{ g s}^{-1} = -0.4\\textrm{ g s}^{-1}$\n\n    Whilst in the second experiment one obtains\n\n    - $\\dfrac{\\Delta (m_A - m_B)}{\\Delta t} = (0.1 - 1.0)\\textrm{ g s}^{-1} = -0.9\\textrm{ g s}^{-1}$\n\nHence, the rate of mass change in the second experiment is 2.25 times the rate of change in the first experiment."},{"ref":"PH0694","marks":"11","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0694\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\n1. A \"Newton\\'s cradle\" contains a row of five identical metal balls, each of mass 1.0 g and specific heat capacity 400 J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$, suspended from light strings of length 30 cm. The balls hang in a straight line, touching each other.\n\n    Ball 1 is drawn back, with its string kept taut, until it makes an angle of 60$\\degree$ to the vertical, then released from rest. It swings down and collides with the remaining four stationary balls. The size of each ball may be treated as negligible.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202026-06-01%20at%201.25.59%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-06-01 at 1.25.59 PM\">\n\n    Over time, the system stops moving. Assuming that all initial energy is distributed equally as thermal energy in the balls, determine the temperature increase in temperature in each ball.   [3 marks]\n\n2. The cradle is placed inside a sealed, evacuated container. The strings and the container walls are perfect thermal insulators, so no energy is lost from the balls.\n\n    Explain why changing the mass of each ball, while keeping the material (and hence specific heat capacity) the same, does not affect the temperature rise calculated in part (a). [2 marks]\n\n3.  Ball 1 is removed from the cradle. It is thrown horizontally with a speed of 10.0 m s$^{-1}$ and embeds itself in a stationary ice cube of mass 9.0 g that rests on a smooth horizontal surface. The ball–ice system then slides freely along the surface.\n\n    1. Determine the speed of the ball–ice system immediately after the collision. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Show that the amount of energy converted to thermal energy during the collision is 45 mJ. [2 marks]\n\n4. The ice cube is initially at 0$\\degree$C. Assuming no energy is lost from the ball–ice system, calculate the percentage of the ice that melts as a result of the collision. [2 marks]\n\n    Specific latent heat of fusion of ice = 333 kJ kg$^{-1}$","markScheme":"1. ::indent\n    - $h = l(1-\\cos{60\\degree}) = 0.15\\textrm{ m}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $E_k = mgh = (1.0\\times10^{-3})(9.81)(0.15) = 1.47\\times10^{-3}\\textrm{ J}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $\\Delta T = \\dfrac{E_k}{5mc} = \\dfrac{1.47\\times10^{-3}}{(5\\times1.0\\times10^{-3})(400)} =$ :answer[$7.4\\times10^{-4}$ K] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The vertical height $h$ through which Ball 1 descends is\n\n    - $h=l\\left(1-\\cos{\\theta}\\right)=0.30\\left(1-\\cos{60\\degree}\\right)\\textrm{ m}=0.15\\textrm{ m}$\n\n    All gravitational potential energy converts to kinetic energy at the moment of collision:\n\n    - $E_k=E_p=mgh=\\left(1.0\\times10^{-3}\\textrm{ kg}\\right)\\left(9.81\\textrm{ m s}^{-2}\\right)\\left(0.15\\textrm{ m}\\right)=1.47\\times10^{-3}\\textrm{ J}$\n\n    This energy is shared equally among all five balls:\n\n    - $E_k=(5m)(c)(\\Delta T)$\n\n    - $\\Delta T = \\dfrac{E_k}{5mc} = \\dfrac{1.47\\times10^{-3}\\textrm{ J}}{(5\\times1.0\\times10^{-3}\\textrm{ kg})(400\\textrm{ J kg}^{-1}\\textrm{ K}^{-1})} = 7.4\\times10^{-4}\\textrm{ K}$\n\n2. ::indent\n    - $mgh=(5m)(c)(\\Delta T)  $ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $\\Delta T = \\dfrac{gh}{5c}\\ /\\ m$ cancels (Therefore $ \\Delta T$ is independent of $m$) $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The kinetic energy at impact is $E_k=mgh$. Setting this equal to the thermal energy gained by all five balls:\n\n    - $mgh=(5m)(c)(\\Delta T)$\n\n    Dividing both sides by $m$:\n\n    - $\\Delta T = \\dfrac{gh}{5c}$\n\n    Since $g$, $h$ (set by string length and release angle) and $c$ (set by material) are all unchanged, $\\Delta T$ does not depend on mass.\n\n3.\n    1. ::indent\n        - $m_1 u_1 = (m_1+m_2)v_f$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $v_f=\\dfrac{(1.0\\times10^{-3})(10.0)}{10.0\\times10^{-3}}=$ :answer[$1.0$ m s$^{-1}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        This is a perfectly inelastic collision; momentum is conserved.\n\n        - $p_{before}= p_{after}$\n\n        - $m_{\\textrm{ball}}v_{\\textrm{ball}}=(m_{\\textrm{ball}}+m_{\\textrm{ice}})v_f$\n\n        - $v_f=\\dfrac{m_{\\textrm{ball}}v_{\\textrm{ball}}}{m_{\\textrm{ball}}+m_{\\textrm{ice}}}=\\dfrac{\\left(1.0\\textrm{ g}\\right)\\left(10.0\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}\\right)}{10.0\\textrm{ g}}=1.0\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}$\n\n    2. ::indent\n        - $E_{heat}=\\dfrac{1}{2}m_1u_1^2-\\dfrac{1}{2}(m_1+m_2)v_f^2$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $E_{heat}=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(0.001\\textrm{ kg}\\right)\\left(10.0\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}\\right)^2-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(0.010\\textrm{ kg}\\right)\\left(1.0\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}\\right)^2=50\\textrm{ mJ}-5\\textrm{ mJ}=$ :answer[$45\\textrm{ mJ}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\langle$Full substitution must be shown; the answer is given.$\\rangle$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The energy converted to thermal energy equals the loss in kinetic energy:\n\n        - $E_{heat}=\\dfrac{1}{2}m_{\\textrm{ball}}v_{\\textrm{ball}}^2-\\dfrac{1}{2}(m_{\\textrm{ball}}+m_{\\textrm{ice}})v_f^2$\n\n        - $E_{heat}=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(0.001\\textrm{ kg}\\right)\\left(10.0\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}\\right)^2-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(0.010\\textrm{ kg}\\right)\\left(1.0\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}\\right)^2=0.050\\textrm{ J}-0.005\\textrm{ J}=0.045\\textrm{ J}$\n\n4. ::indent\n    - $m_{\\textrm{melted}}=\\dfrac{E_{heat}}{L}=\\dfrac{0.045\\textrm{ J}}{333\\times10^{3}\\textrm{ J kg}^{-1}}=1.35\\times10^{-7}\\textrm{ kg}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $\\%=\\dfrac{1.35\\times10^{-7}}{9.0\\times10^{-3}}\\times100\\%=$ :answer[$1.5\\times10^{-3}$%] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\langle$Allow ECF from (c)(ii).$\\rangle$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The thermal energy from the collision goes into melting a mass $m_{\\textrm{melted}}$ of ice:\n\n    - $E_{heat}=m_{\\textrm{melted}}\\times L$\n\n    - $m_{\\textrm{melted}}=\\dfrac{0.045\\textrm{ J}}{333\\times10^{3}\\textrm{ J kg}^{-1}}=1.35\\times10^{-7}\\textrm{ kg}$\n\n    Percentage of the total ice mass:\n\n    - $\\%=\\dfrac{1.35\\times10^{-7}}{9.0\\times10^{-3}}\\times100\\%\\approx1.5\\times10^{-3}\\%$\n\n1. ::indent\n    - $E_k=1.47\\textrm{ mJ}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $\\Delta T_{max} = \\dfrac{E_k}{5mc}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $\\Delta T_{max} =$ :answer[$0.74$ mK] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The initial vertical height $h$ of Ball 1 is given by\n\n    - $h=l\\left(1-\\cos{\\theta}\\right)=30\\left(1-\\cos{60\\degree}\\right)\\textrm{ cm}=15$ cm\n\n    The maximum kinetic energy on impact, $E_k$, is equal to the initial potential energy of Ball 1, $E_p$, which means that\n\n    - $E_k=E_p=mgh=\\left(0.001\\textrm{ kg}\\right)\\left(9.81\\textrm{ m s}^{-2}\\right)\\left(0.15\\textrm{ m}\\right)=1.47$ mJ\n\n    Finally, the maximum temperature change corresponds to complete conversion of the kinetic energy to thermal energy within the five balls:\n\n    - $E_k=(5m)(c)(\\Delta T)$\n\n    - $\\Delta T_{max} = \\dfrac{E_k}{5mc} = \\dfrac{0.00147\\textrm{ J}}{(0.005\\textrm{ kg})(400\\textrm{ J kg}^{-1}\\textrm{ K}^{-1})} = 0.74$ mK\n\n2. ::indent\n    - $\\Delta T = \\dfrac{gh}{5c}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    Therefore $\\Delta T$ is independent of the ball mass $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    As in part a, the kinetic energy converted to thermal energy is given by\\\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $E_k=E_p=mgh$\n\n    The temperature change obeys the equation\\\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\Delta T = \\dfrac{E_k}{5mc} = \\dfrac{gh}{5c}$\n\n    Hence, $\\Delta T$ is independent of mass.\n\n3.\n    1. ::indent\n        - $v_f=\\dfrac{m_{Ball\\ 1}v_{Ball\\ 1}}{m_{Ball\\ 1}+m_{ice}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $v_f=$ :answer[$1.0$ m s$^{-1}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The system must conserve momentum. Thus, after the Ball 1 has embedded itself in the ice, the velocity $v_f$ of the pair becomes\n\n        - $p_{before}=\\ p_{after}$\n\n        - $m_{Ball\\ 1}v_{Ball\\ 1}=(m_{Ball\\ 1}+m_{ice})v_f$\n\n        - $v_f=\\dfrac{m_{Ball\\ 1}v_{Ball\\ 1}}{m_{Ball\\ 1}+m_{ice}}=\\dfrac{\\left(1.0\\textrm{ g}\\right)\\left(10.0\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}\\right)}{10.0\\textrm{ g}}=1.0\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}$\n\n    2. ::indent\n        - $E_{heat}=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(m_{Ball\\ 1}v_{Ball\\ 1}^2-\\left(m_{Ball\\ 1}+m_{ice}\\right)v_f^2\\right)$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $E_{heat}=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\left(0.001\\textrm{ kg}\\right)\\left(10\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}\\right)^2-\\left(0.010\\textrm{ kg}\\right)\\left(1.0\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}\\right)^2\\right)$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $E_{heat}=$ :answer[$45$ mJ]\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The amount of energy converted to thermal energy is given by the difference between the initial and final kinetic energies:\n\n        - $E_{heat}=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(m_{Ball\\ 1}v_{Ball\\ 1}^2-\\left(m_{Ball\\ 1}+m_{ice}\\right)v_f^2\\right)$\n\n        - Therefore $E_{heat}=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\left(0.001\\textrm{ kg}\\right)\\left(10\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}\\right)^2-\\left(0.010\\textrm{ kg}\\right)\\left(1.0\\textrm{ m s}^{-1}\\right)^2\\right)=0.045$ J\n\n4. Percentage melted $= \\dfrac{E_{melt}}{Lm_{ice}} \\times \\ 100\\%$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    Percentage melted = :answer[0.0016\\%] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The mass melted is given by\n\n    - $m=\\dfrac{E_{melt}}{L_{ice}}$\n\n    where $L_{ice}$ is the specific latent heat of fusion. Note that $333\\textrm{ kJ kg}^{-1}= 333\\textrm{ J g}^{-1}$\n\n    - $m=\\dfrac{0.045\\textrm{ J}}{333\\textrm{ J g}^{-1}} = 1.4 \\times 10^{-4}$ g\n\n    Percentage melted is therefore\n\n    $\\% =\\ \\dfrac{1.4\\times{10}^{-4}}{9} \\times\\ 100\\%= 0.0016\\%$"},{"ref":"PH0755","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0755\n\nOne mole of $\\ce{N2}$ ($28$ g mol$^{-1}$) reacts with one mole of $\\ce{O2}$ ($32$ g  mol$^{-1}$) at constant temperature to form two moles of $\\ce{NO}$ ($30$ g mol$^{-1}$).\n\nWhich line of the table correctly describes the ratios between the mean velocities of the gas molecules?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1.5em} \\dfrac{\\textrm{Mean\\ N}_2\\ \\textrm{velocity}}{\\textrm{Mean\\ NO}\\ \\textrm{velocity}} \\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em} \\dfrac{\\textrm{Mean\\ O}_2\\ \\textrm{velocity}}{\\textrm{Mean\\ NO\\ velocity}} \\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| Less than 1| Less than 1|\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Less than 1| Greater than 1|\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Greater than 1| Less than 1|\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Greater than 1| Greater than 1|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe mean velocity of the $\\ce{N2}$ molecules is given by the equation:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{m_{\\left(N_2\\right)}v_{\\left(N_2\\right)}^2}{2}=\\dfrac{3k_BT}{2}$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\therefore\\ v_{\\left(N_2\\right)}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3k_BT}{m_{\\left(N_2\\right)}}}$\n\nwhere $m_{(N_2)}$ is the molar mass, $R$ is the molar gas constant and $T$ is temperature.\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $v_{\\left(O_2\\right)}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3k_BT}{m_{\\left(O_2\\right)}\\ }}$\n\nOnce converted to NO, the mean velocity of the molecules becomes\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $v_{\\left(NO\\right)}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3k_BT}{m_{\\left(NO\\right)}}}$\n\nThus, the velocity ratios can be expressed as\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{v_{\\left(N_2\\right)}}{v_{\\left(NO\\right)}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{m_{\\left(NO\\right)}}{m_{\\left(N_2\\right)}}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{30}{28}}>1$\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{v_{\\left(O_2\\right)}}{v_{\\left(NO\\right)}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{m_{\\left(NO\\right)}}{m_{\\left(O_2\\right)}}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{30}{32}}<1$\n\nNote that the number of moles of particles of each gas is not relevant here, as we are looking at individual particle masses and speeds."},{"ref":"PH0856","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0856\n\nFour insulated containers contain the same liquid with different masses and at different temperatures as shown in the diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-18%20at%204.59.39%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-18 at 4.59.39 PM\">\n\nThe four are then mixed in another insulated container.\n\nWhat is the final temperature of the mixture?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $45\\degree$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $55\\degree$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $60\\degree$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $180\\degree$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe four containers are filled with the same liquid then they have the same specific heat capacity\n\nThe net heat lost for the system is zero.\n\nUsing the formula\n$Q=mc \\Delta T$ for each container gives:\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\n\\hspace{2em} 0&=3mc(T-80)+2mc(T-60)+2mc(T-40)+mc(T-0)\\\\\n0&=(3T-240)+(2T-120)+(2T-80)+T\\\\\n8T&=440\\\\\nT&=55\\degree\n\\end{aligned}$"},{"ref":"PH0857","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0857\n\nA solid material is heated at a constant rate.\n\nThe graph shows the variation of its temperature $T$ with time $t$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-18%20at%205.01.15%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-18 at 5.01.15 PM\">\n\nThree statements are given about this material:\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$ The solid state has the highest specific heat capacity\n- II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ The specific latent heat of vaporisation $L_v$ is greater than the specific latent heat of fusion $L_f$\n- III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ The liquid state has the highest specific heat capacity\n\nWhich of these statements is correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe steepness of the line in the solid state shows that it takes the least time to rise in temperature; this means it has the lowest specific heat capacity. Statement **I** is incorrect.\n\nFrom the graph we can see that vaporisation takes more time than melting, therefore more energy is required for vaporisation and $L_v>L_f$.  Statement **II** is correct.\n\nThe liquid state is less steep than the solid state, indicating that it takes the longest time to rise in temperature; this means it has the highest specific heat capacity. Statement **III** is correct."},{"ref":"PH0868","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0868\n\nA steel ball is dropped into a body of water. The water is then cooled until it freezes. The thermal conductivity of ice is approximately four times higher than the thermal conductivity of water.\n\nWhich line of the table correctly describes how freezing affects the rates of heat transfer out of the ball by conduction and convection?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Conduction $\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\hspace{1.5em}$Convection$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| Decreases |Decreases |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Decreases | Increases |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Increases| Decreases|\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Increases | Increases |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nWhen the water freezes to ice, the rate of heat conduction increases because the thermal conductivity of ice is greater than that of water. However, heat transfer by convection ceases because the heat can no longer be transferred via motion of the water molecules."},{"ref":"PH0869","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0869\n\nTwo vertical cylinders are filled with water. The cylinders are identical and do not lose heat to their surroundings. Heat is supplied to the top face of Cylinder **A** at a rate $P$, and the temperature change of the bottom face is measured by Temperature Sensor **A**. The bottom face of Cylinder **B** is also heated at a rate $P$, and Temperature Sensor **B** measures the temperature change of the top face.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/PH0869aaa.png\" alt=\"PH0869aaa\">\n\nWhich temperature sensor measures the higher rate of temperature change?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Temperature Sensor **A**\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Temperature Sensor **B**\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ The rates are equal\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The relative rates depend on the value of $P$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nIn Cylinder **A**, heat is transferred from the top face to the bottom face by thermal conduction and radiation. However, convection to the bottom face does not occur because the heated water at the top of the tube is less dense than the cooler water below.\n\nIn Cylinder **B**, the rates of conduction and radiation are identical to those in Cylinder **A**, but heated water at the bottom of the tube is displaced upward by cooler, denser water from above. Hence, the upward transference of heat in Cylinder **B** is greater than the downward transference of heat in Cylinder **A**."},{"ref":"PH0870","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0870\n\nAn insulated metal rod has cross-sectional area $10$ cm$^2$ and length $80$ cm. A power of $25$ W is supplied to the top face of the rod, whilst the bottom face is held at a constant temperature of $20\\degree$C. Assume that the thermal conductivity of the metal is $400$ W m$^{-1}\\degree$C$^{-1}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-18%20at%205.05.09%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-18 at 5.05.09 PM\">\n\nWhat is the equilibrium temperature of the top face?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $22\\degree$C\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $40\\degree$C\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $50\\degree$C\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $70\\degree$C","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe rate of heat transfer from the top to bottom face is given by\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta t} = kA\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{\\Delta x}=kA\\dfrac{(T_{top}-T_{bottom})}{L}$\n\nwhere $A$ is the cross-sectional area, $k$ is the thermal conductivity, $L$ is the length, and $\\Delta T$ is the temperature difference between the top and bottom faces, which are at temperatures $T_{top}$ and $T_{bottom}$, respectively.\n\nIn equilibrium, the rate of heat transfer is equal to the power $P$ supplied to the top face:\n\n- $P=kA\\dfrac{T_{top}-T_{bottom}}{L}$\n\nRearranging to make $T_{top}$ the subject of the equation gives\n\n- $\\dfrac{LP}{kA}=T_{top}-T_{bottom}$\n\n- $\\therefore T_{top}=\\dfrac{LP}{kA}+T_{bottom}$\n\nInserting the values given (remembering to convert to SI units) yields\n\n$T_{top}=\\dfrac{(0.8\\,\\ce{m})(25\\,\\ce{W})}{(400\\,\\ce{W}\\,\\ce{m}^{-1}\\degree \\ce{C}^{-1})(0.001\\ \\ce{m}^2)}+20\\degree \\ce{C}= 70\\degree \\ce{C}$"},{"ref":"PH0871","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0871\n\nHeat is supplied at a power of $10$ W to one end of a horizontal metal rod of cross-sectional area $10$ cm$^2$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-18%20at%205.06.06%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-18 at 5.06.06 PM\">\n\nThe graph shows how temperature $T$ varies with horizontal distance $x$ measured from the hot end of the rod. It may be assumed that all of the supplied energy is conducted through the rod and dissipated at the other end.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-18%20at%205.06.53%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-18 at 5.06.53 PM\">\n\nWhat is the thermal conductivity of the rod?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $0.5$ W m$^{-1}\\,\\degree $C$^{-1}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $1.0$ W m$^{-1}\\,\\degree $C$^{-1}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $50$ W m$^{-1}\\,\\degree $C$^{-1}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $100$ W m$^{-1}\\,\\degree $C$^{-1}$","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe rate of heat transfer through the rod is given by\n\n- $P=\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta t}=kA \\dfrac{\\Delta T}{\\Delta x}$\n\nFrom the equation, $\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{\\Delta x}=\\dfrac{P}{kA}$\n\nAnd $\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{\\Delta x}$ is the gradient of the graph\n\nUsing two points from the graph\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{\\Delta x}=\\dfrac{60}{30}=2\\,\\degree$C cm$^{-1}$\n\nTherefore\n\n- $\\dfrac{P}{kA}=2$ K cm$^{-1}$\n\n- $k=\\dfrac{10\\textrm{ W}}{(2\\textrm{ K cm}^{-1})(10\\textrm{ cm}^2)}$\n\n- $=0.5\\textrm{ W}\\,\\degree$C$^{-1}$ cm$^{-1}$\n\n- $=50\\textrm{ W}$ m$^{-1}\\,\\degree$C$^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH0936","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0936\n\nA substance is initially in solid form. Heat energy is added at a constant rate until the substance just reaches its boiling point.\n\nWhich graph correctly shows how the temperature of the substance $T$ varies with heat energy added $Q$  ?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n|| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-18%20at%205.10.46%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-18 at 5.10.46 PM\"> |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-18%20at%205.10.52%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-18 at 5.10.52 PM\">|\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-18%20at%205.10.57%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-18 at 5.10.57 PM\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-18%20at%205.11.02%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-18 at 5.11.02 PM\">|","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe internal energy of a substance (total energy) is the sum of kinetic and potential energies of the particles.\n\nTemperature is directly proportional to the average kinetic energy of particles in a substance.\n\nDuring change of state, heat energy is transferred into potential energy, so temperature does not change. This corresponds to $\\lq$flat' regions on a graph of $T$ against $Q$.\n\nWhen heat energy is added and there is no change of state - energy is transferred to kinetic energy - so temperature increases in proportion to heat added. This corresponds to a sloped straight line on a graph of $T$ against $Q$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">"},{"ref":"PH0950","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0950\n\nA box containing an ideal gas is heated evenly from all sides. It is found that the temperature of the gas closest to the ground is the lowest. This is due to\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Conduction\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Convection\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Condensation\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Radiation","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nConvection is a movement of groups of atoms or molecules in fluids, that arises from change in density.\n\nConvection here occurs because hot gas molecules are less dense so will rise up against gravity. Cooler gas molecules are more dense so will fall to bottom of the box."},{"ref":"PH0951","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0951\n\nThe graph below shows wavelength against intensity for a black body.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-18%20at%205.16.47%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-18 at 5.16.47 PM\">\n\nHow would the shape of the graph change if the surface of the black body were at a higher temperature?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Peak becomes lower and shifts to the left\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Peak becomes lower and shifts to the right\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Peak becomes higher and shifts to the left\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Peak becomes higher and shifts to the right","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe peak wavelength is given by the equation\n\n- $\\lambda_{max}\\,T=2.9\\times{10}^{-3}$ K m\n\nTherefore, as temperature increases, the peak wavelength decreases and will shift to the left\n\nAnd the power is given by\n\n- $P=\\sigma AT^4$\n\nThe higher temperature of a black body, the higher the total energy of radiation given off. This will cause the area under the graph, which is representative of the amount of power emitted, to increase and the peak to go higher."},{"ref":"PH1021","marks":"15","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1021\n\n**Maximum mark: 15**\n\nA radially insulated metal rod of diameter 10 cm, length 150 cm, and thermal conductivity 200 W m$^{-1}$ $\\degree$C$^{-1}$ is embedded vertically in the top face of a large ice block and heated continuously at constant power supplied to the free end. At thermal equilibrium, the temperature of the heated end of the rod is 100$\\degree$C.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-18%20at%205.25.28%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-18 at 5.25.28 PM\">\n\n1. Explain, on a microscopic level, how heat is transferred to the ice from the hot end of the rod. [2 marks]\n\n2.  1. Show that a power of approximately 105 W enters the rod. Assume that the length of rod contained inside the ice block is negligible. [3 marks]\n\n    2. The graph shows how the mass of liquid water $m$ changes with time $t$. Ice has a specific latent heat of fusion of approximately 334 J g$^{-1}$.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-07-18%20at%205.26.28%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-07-18 at 5.26.28 PM\">\n\n        Estimate the percentage of the total supplied power that is lost to the surroundings. [3 marks]\n\n    3. The rod is replaced with a radially insulated rod of equal length, half the diameter and two times the thermal conductivity. The free end of the rod is heated until it reaches an equilibrium temperature of $100\\degree$C.\n\n        Determine the ratio $\\dfrac{\n        \\textrm{New\\,\\,rate\\,\\,of\\,\\,melting}}{\\textrm{Old\\,\\,rate\\,\\,of\\,\\,melting}}$. [2 marks]\n\n3. The metal rod is stripped of its insulation without altering the power supplied to the free end, and the melting rate of the ice is measured in thermal equilibrium.\n\n    1. Describe how the melting rate of the ice is affected by the change. [2 marks]\n\n    2. The uninsulated rod is next removed and then embedded vertically in the bottom face of the ice block. The same power is supplied to the free end of the rod and the equilibrium ice melting rate is again measured.\n\n        Discuss how the change of position alters the melting rate of the ice. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1. Kinetic/vibrational energy transferred through particle collisions $\\checkmark$\n\n        Heat is transferred from hot to cold end because particles at higher temperature collide with/pass energy to lower energy particles $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Heat transfer by conduction is caused by kinetic energy transfer through particle collisions. As heat is supplied to the free end of the rod, the particles vibrate more and the kinetic energy of particles at that end increases, causing them to collide with particles below and transfer energy to them. Considering particles at a given height up the rod, the kinetic energy of particles colliding from above is greater than the kinetic energy of particles colliding from below, meaning that the net direction of heat transfer is down the rod, from the hot end to the cold end.\n\n2.  1. Use of $\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta t} = \\dfrac{kA\\Delta T}{L}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\Delta T=100\\degree\\textrm{C}$ and $A=\\pi \\Bigl (\\dfrac{D}{2} \\Bigr )^2 \\approx 78.5\\textrm{ cm}^2$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta t} = \\dfrac{(200\\textrm{ W m}^{-1}\\degree\\textrm{C}^{-1})(0.00785\\textrm{ m}^2)(100\\degree\\textrm{C})}{1.5\\textrm{ m}} \\approx$ :answer[$104.6$ W] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Assuming that no heat is lost radially from the rod (the rod is radially insulated), the power transfer along the rod is given by\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta t} = \\dfrac{kA\\Delta T}{l}$\n\n        From the stated diameter $D$, the cross-sectional area $A$ is\n\n        - $A = \\pi \\Bigl (\\dfrac{D}{2} \\Bigr)^2 = 25\\pi \\textrm{ cm}^2 \\approx 78.5\\textrm{ cm}^2$\n\n        Finally, as the system is in thermal equilibrium, the cold end of the rod must be at a temperature of $0\\degree$C (the melting temperature of ice), meaning that the temperature difference $\\Delta T$ from the hot end to the cold end of the rod is $100\\degree$C.\n\n        Hence,\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta t} = \\dfrac{(200\\textrm{ W m}^{-1} \\degree\\textrm{C}^{-1})(0.00785\\textrm{ m}^2)(100\\degree\\textrm{C})}{1.5\\textrm{ m}} \\approx 105$ W\n\n    2.  ::indent\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta m}{\\Delta t} = 0.2\\textrm{ g s}^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta Q_{melt}}{\\Delta t} = L \\dfrac{\\Delta m}{\\Delta t} = (334)(0.2) = 66.8 \\ll \\textrm{W}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $P_{lost}=\\dfrac{105-66.8}{105}=0.364=$ :answer[$36.4\\,\\%$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The power used to melt the ice, $\\dfrac{\\Delta Q_{melt}}{\\Delta t}$, is related to the melting rate $\\dfrac{\\Delta m}{\\Delta t}$ and specific latent heat of fusion $L$ by the equation\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta Q_{melt}}{\\Delta t} = L \\dfrac{\\Delta m}{\\Delta t}$\n\n        From the gradient of the graph, it follows that\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta m}{\\Delta t} = 0.2\\textrm{ g s}^{-1}$\n\n        Hence,\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta Q_{melt}}{\\Delta t} = (334\\textrm{ J g}^{-1})(0.2\\textrm{ g s}^{-1})=66.8$ W\n\n        Since the power input to the rod is approximately $105$ W, the percentage of power lost to the surroundings is given by:\n\n        - $P_{lost}=100\\Bigl(1-\\dfrac{\\Delta Q_{melt}}{\\Delta Q_{total}} \\Bigr)\\% \\approx 100\\Bigl (1-\\dfrac{66.8}{105} \\Bigr)\\%=36.4\\%$\n\n    3. ::indent\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta t} \\propto kA, \\hspace{1em} k_{new}=2k_{old}, \\hspace{1em} A_{new}=\\dfrac{A_{old}}{4}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta Q_{new}}{\\Delta t} =$ :answer[$\\dfrac{1}{2}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The rate of heat transfer through thermal conduction is given by the equation\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta t} = \\dfrac{kA\\Delta T}{l}$\n\n        The new rod has the same equilibrium temperature distribution as the original, but it also has two times the thermal conductivity and half the diameter. The rate of heat transfer by conduction in the new scenario is therefore given by\n\n        - $\\dfrac{Q_{new}}{\\Delta t} = \\dfrac{k_{new}A_{new}\\Delta T}{L} = \\dfrac{\\Delta T}{L}(2k_{old})\\Bigl(\\dfrac{A_{old}}{4}\\Bigr) = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{\\Delta Q_{old}}{\\Delta t}$\n\n        Thus, the rate of melting of the ice is halved.\n\n3.  1.  Radiation/convection results in heat loss from the rod $\\checkmark$\n\n        The effect of increased radiation/convection is to decrease melting rate $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Removing the insulation causes heat to be lost radially from the rod through radiation and convection. Thus, since the power supplied to the rod remains constant, the rate of melting is decreased by the loss of insulation.\n\n    2.  Convection causes heat transfer in the direction away from ice when rod is embedded in top face of block $\\checkmark$\n\n        Convection causes heat transfer towards ice when the rod is embedded in bottom face of block $\\checkmark$\n\n        Embedding rod in bottom face causes higher melting rate than embedding in top face $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        In convection, heating of the surrounding air reduces its density, resulting in warmer air rising and cool air displacing the heated air to yield upward heat transfer.\n\n        In the first case, wherein the rod is embedded in the top face of the ice block, convection causes the heat lost by the rod to rise upwards from the heated free end. Consequently, heat lost from the rod through convection does not contribute to ice melting.\n\n        In the second case, the rod is instead embedded in the bottom face of the ice block. As convection causes upward heat transfer, some of the heat lost from the rod through convection reaches the ice block and contributes to melting. Therefore, the rate of melting in the second scenario is greater than in the first."},{"ref":"PH1801","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1801\n\nSolids $X$ and $Y$ are of equal volume and have densities of $\\rho$ and $2\\rho$, respectively. When heated starting from their melting points, $X$ requires an amount of energy $E$ to completely melt while $Y$ requires $3E$.\n\nThe specific latent heat of fusion of $X$ is $L$. What is the specific latent heat of fusion of $Y$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{L}{6}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{2L}{3}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{3L}{2}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $6L$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe substances have the same volume so using $\\rho=\\dfrac{m}{V}$ we have $\\rho\\propto m$. Therefore substance $Y$ has twice the mass of substance $X$.\n\nSpecific latent heat of fusion $L$, of a substance is given by:\n\n- $Q=mL$\n\nWhere $Q$ is equivalent to $E$, the energy required to completely melt a mass $m$ of the substance.\n\nWe therefore have:\n\n- $\\dfrac{L_Y}{L_X}=\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{E_Y}{m_Y}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{E_x}{m_x}\\right)}=\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{3E}{2m}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{E}{m}\\right)}=\\dfrac{3}{2}$"},{"ref":"PH1802","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1802\n\nWhich of the following best describes the phenomenon of convection?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Transfer of heat by electromagnetic waves.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Transfer of heat through direct contact between particles.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Transfer of heat through the vibration of particles in a solid.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Transfer of heat through the movement of fluid due to density differences.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nConvection is the transfer of heat through the movement of a fluid (liquid or gas) due to differences in density. When a fluid is heated, it expands, becomes less dense, and the hot fluid rises, while cooler, denser fluid sinks, creating a convection current.\n\nA is incorrect: This describes radiation, where heat energy is transferred by electromagnetic waves.\n\nB is incorrect: This describes conduction, where heat transfers through direct contact between particles.\n\nC is incorrect: This also describes conduction, particularly in solids, where heat spreads via particle vibrations without bulk movement."},{"ref":"PH1803","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1803\n\nOpposite sides of a solid metal cube of side length 0.2 m are held at temperatures 20°C and 100°C.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-03-25%20at%201.33.27%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-03-25 at 1.33.27 PM\">\n\nThe rate of heat energy transfer between the sides is $6.4$ kW. It can be assumed that there is no heat loss through the other sides of the cube. What is the thermal conductivity of the metal in W m$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $80$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $100$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $400$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2000$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe thermal conductivity equation states that the rate of energy transfer by conduction $\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta t}$ between two perpendicular surfaces is given by:\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta t}=kA\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{\\Delta x}$\n\nWhere $k$ is the thermal conductivity of the material between the surfaces, $A$ is the cross-sectional area of the surfaces, $\\Delta T$ is the difference between the temperature of the surfaces, and $\\Delta x$ is the displacement between the surfaces.\n\nHere we are given:\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta t}=6.4$ kW $=6400$ W\n\n- $\\Delta T=100-20=80$ K (Celsius and Kelvin are equivalent for differences in temperature).\n\n- $A=(0.2)(0.2)=0.04$ m$^2$\n\n- $\\Delta x=0.2$ m\n\nSubstituting these values into our equation and rearranging for $k$ gives:\n\n- $6400=k(0.04)\\dfrac{80}{0.2}$\n\n- $6400=16k$\n\n- $k=400$ W m$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH1844-2","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1844-2\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nAn immersion heater is fully submerged inside 0.35 kg of a liquid $X$. The graph shows how the temperature $T$ of the liquid varies with time $t$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/Screenshot%202025-09-23%20at%202.20.40%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-09-23 at 2.20.40 PM\">\n\n1.  Assuming the power delivered by the heater is 50 W, use the graph to determine the specific heat capacity of liquid $X$. [3 marks]\n\n2. The actual value of the specific heat capacity of liquid $X$ is 2800 J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$. The mass and temperature of the water were measured accurately.\n\n    Give one reason why this difference may have occurred and suggest an improvement to the experiment that will reduce this difference. [2 marks]\n\n3. The container of liquid at 40$\\degree $C is poured into a beaker with 500 g of water held at 10$\\degree $C. Mixture $Y$ is created. The specific heat capacity of water is 4200 J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$. Assuming no heat is transferred to the surroundings or the beaker, determine the equilibrium temperature of mixture $Y$ in Celsius to the nearest degree. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. $\\hspace{1.7em}$ $Pt=mc\\Delta T$\n\n    OR\n\n    - Gradient $=0.046$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - their gradient $=\\dfrac{P}{mc}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $c=\\dfrac{50}{(0.35)(0.046)}=$ :answer[$3100$ J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The change in thermal energy of a substance is given by:\n\n    - $Q=mc\\Delta T$\n\n    Where $m$ is the mass of the substance, $c$ is the specific heat capacity of the substance in a given state, and $\\Delta T$ is its change in temperature.\n\n    By dividing both sides of the equation by a period of time $\\Delta t$ we have:\n\n    - $\\dfrac{Q}{\\Delta t}=mc\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{\\Delta t}$\n\n    - $P=mc\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{\\Delta t}$\n\n    $\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{\\Delta t}$ is the gradient of the graph in the question therefore we have:\n\n    - $P=mc(\\textrm{gradient})$\n\n    The gradient of the graph is given by:\n\n    - $\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{\\Delta t}=\\dfrac{23}{500}=0.046$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Thermal%20Energy%20Transfers/images/ph1844-2aa1.png\" alt=\"ph1844-2aa1\">\n\n    Finally, substituting values into our equation and rearranging for $c$ we have:\n\n    - $50=(0.35)c(0.046)$\n\n    - $c=\\dfrac{50}{(0.35)(0.046)}=3100$ J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$\n\n2. One reason:\n\n    - Heat was lost to the surroundings / container\n\n    - The temperature of the liquid was not constant  / the energy was not evenly distributed $\\checkmark$\n\n    One corresponding improvement:\n\n    - Insulate container (e.g., wrap container in cotton wool)\n\n    - Use more thermally insulated container / container with low thermal conductivity / high specific heat capacity\n\n    - Stir liquid for the duration of the experiment $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    One reason for the discrepancy is that not all of the electrical power supplied by the heater was used to raise the temperature of the liquid. Some of this heat was lost to the surroundings and to the container holding the liquid. As a result, the energy input assumed in the calculation was higher than the actual energy transferred to the liquid, leading to a calculated specific heat capacity that is larger than the true value.\n\n    To reduce heat losses to the surroundings and improve the accuracy of the energy balance, the container could be insulated, for example by wrapping it in cotton wool or using a container made from a material with a low thermal conductivity. This would help to keep more of the supplied energy within the liquid.\n\n    Another reason could be that the temperature of the liquid was not uniform throughout. If the liquid was not stirred continuously during heating, there may have been areas of different temperatures within the container. The thermometer would only measure the temperature at a specific location rather than the true average temperature of the entire liquid. This non-uniformity can lead to an incorrect measurement of the change in temperature, and therefore specific heat capacity.\n\n    Stirring the liquid throughout the experiment would help ensure that the temperature is uniform throughout the liquid, giving a more accurate measurement of the average temperature change and improving the reliability of the calculated specific heat capacity.\n\n3. Use of heat lost = heat gained. $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $(0.35)(2800)(40-T)=(0.50)(4200)(T-10)$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $T=19.54=$ :answer[$20\\degree$C] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    From the conservation of energy and the assumption that there is no heat exchanged with the surroundings,\n\n    - heat lost by the liquid = heat gained by the water\n\n    Using the data booklet formula\n\n    - $Q=mc\\Delta T$\n\n    We can use $T$ to represent the equilibrium temperature and substitute in the values\n\n    - $(0.35)(2800)(40-T)=(0.50)(4200)(T-10)$\n\n    Giving\n\n    - $T=19.54=20\\degree$C"}]},{"name":"Unassigned","questions":[{"ref":"PH1830","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH1830\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nA student investigates the efficiency of a flask in maintaining the temperature $\\theta$  of 1 kg of water over time $t$. The temperature was recorded every 10 minutes for a total duration of 24 h.\n\nThe graph below shows the trend that was recorded during the experiment.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Unassigned/images/PH1830qa.JPEG\" alt=\"PH1830qa\">\n\nThe student wishes to investigate how the rate of decrease in temperature $(v_\\theta)$ is affected by the difference $\\Delta\\theta$ between the water temperature and the room temperature, which was recorded as 20°C.\n\n1. State the value of $\\Delta\\theta$ at $t=9$ h. [1 mark]\n\n2. By drawing a suitable tangent, determine the rate $v_\\theta$ at which temperature is decreasing at $t=9$ h. [3 marks]\n\nThe student calculates the value of $v_\\theta$ for different values of temperatures.\n\nThe graph below shows the **magnitude** of $v_\\theta$ for different values of $\\Delta\\theta$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-03-12%20at%2010.41.44%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-03-12 at 10.41.44 AM\">\n\n3. Use your answer to (b) to plot the data point at $\\Delta\\theta=25$°C. [1 mark]\n\nAfter plotting the graph, the student realises that the values of $|v_\\theta|$ were calculated with a relative uncertainty of $\\pm$10% and that the uncertainty in $\\Delta\\theta$ is negligible.\n\n4. On the graph above, draw error bars at the points where $\\Delta\\theta=10$°C and $\\Delta\\theta=70$°C. [2 marks]\n\nThe student suggests that the rate at which temperature of the water decreases is proportional to the temperature difference between the water and the environment.\n\n5. State and explain whether the data supports the student's suggestion. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. $\\Delta\\theta=$ :answer[25 $\\ll$°C$\\gg$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    From the graph, at $t=9$ h water temperature was about 45°C.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Unassigned/images/PH1830aa.JPEG\" alt=\"PH1830aa\">\n\n    The difference between this and room temperature (20°C) is then:\n\n    - $\\Delta\\theta=45$°C$ \\ - \\ 20$°C\n\n    - $\\Delta\\theta=25$°C\n\n2. Tangent at $t=9$ h traced. $\\checkmark$\n\n    Choosing (any) two points along the tangent (points at least 8 h apart). $\\checkmark$\n\n    Calculation of the gradient using $v_\\theta=\\dfrac{\\Delta\\theta}{\\Delta t}$\n\n    Calculated gradient must be within -3.5°C h$^{-1}$ and -2.5°C h$^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    To find the instantaneous rate at which temperature is decreasing, we must first trace a tangent to the graph at $t=9$ h.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Unassigned/images/PH1830ab1.JPEG\" alt=\"PH1830ab1\">\n\n    Now we must choose two points through which this tangent passes. It is recommended to choose points that are far apart from each other.\n\n    Different students might of course choose different points leading to a slightly different estimate of the rate of decrease in temperature.\n\n    We will use points (4, 60) and (22,5). These are points through which the tangent seems to be passing.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Unassigned/images/PH1830ab2.JPEG\" alt=\"PH1830ab2\">\n\n    We can now calculate the gradient of this line as:\n\n    - $v_\\theta=\\dfrac{\\Delta\\theta}{\\Delta t}=\\dfrac{5\\textrm{°C}-60\\textrm{°C}}{22\\textrm{ h}-4\\textrm{ h}}$\n\n    - $v_\\theta=-3.1$ °C h$^{-1}$\n\n3. Data point plotted correctly at (25, 3.1) $\\left(\\pm\\dfrac{1}{2}\\textrm{ square}\\right)$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-03-12%20at%2010.44.02%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-03-12 at 10.44.02 AM\">\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The data that corresponds $\\Delta\\theta=25$°C has a horizontal coordinate which is equal to 25°C and a vertical coordinate that corresponds to the magnitude of our answer to question (b): i.e. 3.1 °C h$^{-1}$.\n\n    Point should then be plotted at (25, 3.1)\n\n4. *Vertical* error bars plotted both points. $\\checkmark$\n\n    Error bars of the correct length for each point. $\\checkmark$\n\n    (Approximately $\\pm0.1$ for the first point and $\\pm0.8$ for the second one)\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-03-12%20at%2010.44.41%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-03-12 at 10.44.41 AM\">\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The relative uncertainty of $\\pm10$% affects only $|v_\\theta|$ . This means that the error bars must be represented vertically. No horizontal error bars are required given that the uncertainty in $\\Delta\\theta$ is negligible.\n\n    As seen on the graph, for the data point at $\\Delta\\theta=$10°C, the vertical coordinate is $|v_\\theta|=1$ °C h$^{-1}$. The relative uncertainty for this point is then:\n\n    - $\\Delta(|v_\\theta|)_{\\textrm{relative}}=0.1(1$ °C h$^{-1})=0.1$ °C h$^{-1}$\n\n    For the data point at $\\Delta\\theta=70$°C, the vertical coordinate is $|v_\\theta|=8$°C h$^{-1}$. The relative uncertainty for this point is then:\n\n    - $\\Delta(|v_\\theta|)_{\\textrm{relative}}=0.1(8$ °C h$^{-1})=0.8$ °C h$^{-1}$\n\n    We must then represent the corresponding error bars with these lengths.\n\n5. The data supports the student's suggestion. $\\checkmark$\n\n    A line that passes through the origin can be traced on the graph that passes through the error bars. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The student's suggestion is that the graph of $|v_\\theta|$ against $\\Delta\\theta$ should be linear and passing through the origin (proportionality).\n\n    The data supports this claim given that a line can be traced from the origin that passes (within the given uncertainties) through every point.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/The%20Particulate%20Nature%20of%20Matter/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202025-03-12%20at%2010.45.23%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-03-12 at 10.45.23 AM\">"}]}]},{"name":"Wave Behaviour","subtopics":[{"name":"Doppler Effect","questions":[{"ref":"PH0066","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0066\n\nA fire truck’s siren emits a frequency of $f_0$. Starting from rest, it moves with constant acceleration and passes a stationary observer at $t = t_o$. Which graph shows the variation of observed frequency $f$ by the stationary observer with time?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  |  B. ||\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/PH0066A.png\" alt=\"PH0066A\">  |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/PH0066B.png\" alt=\"PH0066B\">|\n|$\\hspace{1cm}$\n|C. |   |   | D. | |\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/PH0066C.png\" alt=\"PH0066C\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/PH0066D.png\" alt=\"PH0066D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nWhen the source is moving towards an observer, each new wavefront is created closer to the previously created wavefront than would be the case if the source were at rest. This results in a shortening of the wavelength and an increase in frequency in this direction.\n\nBecause the speed is increasing, the shortening of the wavelength and increase in frequency both increase over time, so we expect an increasing frequency for the first part of the motion.\n\nOnce the source passes the observer, the wavelength will become elongated and the perceived frequency will be lower than the emitted frequency. Because the acceleration continues, this difference in frequency will increase with time and so the graph should show decreasing observed frequency once the source has passed the observer.\n\nFor HL students:\n\n For a moving source, the received frequency is given by the equation\n\n- $f'=f\\bigg(\\dfrac{v}{v\\pm u_s}\\bigg)$\n\nAs the fire truck approaches the observer $f > f_o$  before $t_o$\n\nAs fire truck moves away from the observer $f < f_o$ after $t_o$\n\nand since it is speeding up, $\\Delta f$ is increasing with time, causing the observed frequency to increase as it approaches and decrease as it moves away."},{"ref":"PH0114","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0114\n\nA train is traveling at 30 m s$^{-1}$  towards a stationary observer. It sounds a horn of frequency 450 Hz.\n\nWhich of the following statements is true about the observed frequency compared with the emitted frequency as the train approaches?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Observed frequency compared to emitted frequency | Change in observed frequency over time |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Lower | No change |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Higher | Increases |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Lower | Decreases |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Higher | No change |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nWhen the source is moving towards an observer, each new wavefront is created closer to the previously created wavefront than would be the case if the source were at rest. This results in a shortening of the wavelength and an increase in frequency in this direction.\n\nBecause the source is moving at a constant speed, this change in wavelength and frequency is constant regardless of distance, as long as the source is approaching the observer.\n\nAlternative explanation for HL students:\n\nThe formula for this motion is:\n\n- $f^{'}=f\\left(\\dfrac{v}{v-u_s}\\right)$\n\n  Where $f^{'}$= frequency detected by observer\n\n  $f$ = source frequency\n\n  $v$ = speed of sound in still air\n\n  $u_s$ is the speed of the source.\n\nWe can see that the result inside the brackets will give a value larger than 1, and so the observed frequency will be higher than the emitted frequency. As the train speed does not change, the calculated value for the observed frequency will remain constant, although its intensity will increase."},{"ref":"PH0317","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0317\n\nA traveling source produces sound waves near a child. The wavefronts of the sound waves and the position of a child are seen in the figure below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/Screenshot%202026-11-05%20at%207.11.10%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-11-05 at 7.11.10 AM\">\n\nThe frequency of the generated sound waves is $f$. Which of the following is correct for both the direction of motion of the source and the observed frequency of the sound waves by the child?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | The direction of the motion | Observed frequency |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| Toward the child | Less than f|\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Toward the child | More than f |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Away from the child | Less than f |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Away from the child | More than f |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe distance between wavefronts is equal to the wavelength. Since the wavefronts towards the child are more separated than wavefronts in the other direction, the child will receive wavefronts less frequently. Therefore, the child hears the sound with less frequency than $f$. The observed frequency is less than the frequency of the wave created by the source when the source and observer are receding from each other."},{"ref":"PH0318","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0318\n\nA car and a bicycle move in the same direction with speeds of $v_0$ and $v$   respectively as shown in the diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/PH0318-1.png\" alt=\"PH0318-1\">\n\nThe car driver uses the horn to warn the cyclist. The frequencies of the sound heard by the car driver and cyclist are $f_o$ and $f $ respectively. Consider the following speeds.\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. |     $v_0=10 \\,\\ce{m}\\, \\ce{s}^{-1}$ and$\\ v=5\\, \\ce{m}\\, \\ce{s}^{-1}$   |\n  | II. | $    v_0 =5 \\, \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$ and $ v=10\\, \\ce{m}{\\, \\ce{s}}^{-1}$ |\n  | III. | $v_0=10\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$ and $ v=10\\,\\ce{m}\\, \\ce{s}^{-1} $|\n\nWhich of the speed combinations would result in  $f_0<f$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ III only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I and II only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nWhen the observer and source of the wave are approaching each other, the observed frequency is greater than the frequency of the produced wave. There is no relative motion between the car and the car driver. Therefore, $f_o$ is equal to the frequency of the sound of the horn.\n\nSince the observed frequency is greater than the original frequency of the sound of the horn, there should be a relative approach between the car and the cyclist.  This can only happen when the car has a greater speed than the cyclist according to the given figure."},{"ref":"PH0319","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0319\n\nA light source approaches a stationary receiver at an increasing speed. The frequency of the emitted light is constant. Which graph shows how the observed frequency $f$ by receiver varies with the speed $v$ of the light source for non-relativistic speeds?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/PH0319A.png\" alt=\"PH0319A\">   |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/PH0319B.png\" alt=\"PH0319B\">|\n|$\\hspace{1cm}$\n|C. |   |   | D. | |\n||<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/PH0319C.png\" alt=\"PH0319C\">    |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/PH0319D.png\" alt=\"PH0319D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nWhen there is a relative approach between the source of the wave and the receiver, the observed frequency ($f$) is higher than the emitted frequency ($f_0$) from the source due to the doppler effect. The produced wave is light. Therefore, the relationship between speed and the change in frequency;\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\Delta f}{f}≈\\dfrac{v}{c}$\n\nSince $f>f_0, \\hspace{2em} \\Delta f=f-f_0 $\n\n- $\\dfrac{f-f_0\\ }{f_0}\\approx\\dfrac{v}{c}$\n\nThis relationship suggests that an increase in $v$ will result in an increase in $f$ at a constant rate. So the graph should be a straight line. Additionally, when the speed is zero, the observed frequency must be equal to the emitted frequency of light. Therefore, the graph should start from a non-zero y-intercept. The only graph that satisfies these conditions is C."},{"ref":"PH0320","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0320\n\nA radio is placed on a turntable. The turntable rotates anticlockwise with constant angular frequency and the frequency of the generated sound waves is $f$. The frequency of sound waves is measured with a detector. The graph shows how the measured frequency $f^{\\prime}$ varies with time t.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/PH0320-1a.png\" alt=\"PH0320-1a\">\n\nThe diagram below shows four possible starting positions of the radio. Which letter gives the position of the radio at $t=0\\, s$?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/PH0320-2a.png\" alt=\"PH0320-2a\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAccording to the graph, The frequency at $t=0$ is $f$. It is the same with generated frequency.  Therefore, there must be no relative approach or receding between the source of the wave and the detector so that there is no Doppler effect.\n\nEven though the radio has non zero speed for all the points, there will be no component of velocity towards or away from the detector on point A or C.\n\nAccording to the graph, immediately following time 0, the detected frequency decreases, which corresponds to point A as it starts moving away from the detector. The directions of velocity for different positions are as follows;\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/PH0320-3a.png\" alt=\"PH0320-3a\">"},{"ref":"PH0322","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0322\n\nUltrasound incident on a moving blood cell is reflected and received by a detector.\n\n <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/PH0322.png\" alt=\"PH0322\">\n\nThe frequency of an incident wave is $f$ and the measured frequency by the detector is $f^{\\prime}$. The angle between the directions of the reflected wave and blood velocity $v$ is $\\theta$. Which of the following quantities is $f^{\\prime}$ dependent on?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}       v $ only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}       v $ and $f\\ $only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}       \\theta $ and $f$ only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}       v,\\ \\theta$ and $f $","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThis is the method used for measuring the flow rate of blood by using the Doppler effect. According to the Doppler effect, the observed frequency is dependent on emitted frequency $f$, the component of speed $v$ towards the observer. Since the change in angle $\\theta$ or $v$ may result in a change in the component of $v$ towards the observer, they both affect $f^{\\prime}$."},{"ref":"PH0324","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0324\n\nA geostationary satellite is used for TV broadcasting. It orbits so that its position remains fixed above a receiver on Earth. It undergoes circular motion with a speed of $v$ and the emitted frequency for a channel is $f$. Which of the following is correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $     The observed frequency by the receiver is less than $f$.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $     The observed frequency by the receiver is more than $f$.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $     The observed frequency by the receiver is equal to $f$.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ The observed frequency by the receiver is equal to $f(\\dfrac{v}{c}+1)$.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nDoppler effect only occurs when there is relative motion between the observer and the source. In this case, the satellite and receiver are motionless relative to each other. Therefore, there will be no change in frequency."},{"ref":"PH0325","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0325\n\nA car drives along a straight road. Radio waves of frequency $f_o$  emitted from an antenna located at one end of the road are received by the car. The graph shows how the observed frequency $f$ by the car varies with time $t$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/PH0325a.png\" alt=\"PH0325a\">\n\nWhat can be stated for the motion of the car?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Stationary then approaches the antenna at a constant speed.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Moving at constant speed towards the antenna then stationary\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Accelerates towards the antenna then moves with constant speed.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Accelerates towards the antenna then stationary.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe observed frequency and speed relationship for radio waves is given by\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\Delta f}{f_o}≈\\dfrac{v}{c}$\n\nSince the observed frequency is greater than emitted frequency according to the graph;\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\lvert f_o-f \\rvert}{f_o}\\approx\\dfrac{v}{c}$\n\nThe graph shows that the observed frequency initially increases. Then the observed frequency does not change. This indicates an increase in speed at the first part of the motion, then constant speed. Since the observed frequency is greater than the emitted frequency, the car can not be stationary."},{"ref":"PH0775","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0775\n\nHubble's law states that the speed at which galaxies recede from Earth is proportional to their distance from Earth. The velocities of galaxies can be determined by their Doppler redshift.\n\nA certain galaxy is moving away from Earth with a velocity of $10^6\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$. If a particular detected wavelength line is 600 nm, what is the magnitude of the Doppler shift in the wavelength?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ 0.2 nm\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ 2 nm\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ 10 nm\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ 20 nm","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation:}}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\Delta\\lambda}{\\lambda} \\approx \\dfrac{v}{c} $\n\n- $\\Delta\\lambda = \\lambda \\Bigl(\\dfrac{v}{c}\\Bigr) = (600 \\times10^{-9})\\Bigl(\\dfrac{10^6}{(3 \\times 10^8)}\\Bigr) $\n\n- $\\hspace{1.8em} = 2 \\times10^{-9}\\,\\ce{m} = 2$ nm"},{"ref":"PH0778","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0778\n\nA person is standing on the side of the road while a car races past them at velocity $v$, while emitting a frequency $f$. The person remains stationary some distance away from the road at all times.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\nWhich graph shows the variation of frequency with distance travelled by the ambulance? Assume that the person is standing at the origin.\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n|| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">  |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">|\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">|","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAt a large distance away from the person, the car is approaching in what can be perceived as a straight line. Hence there will be a Doppler shift, i.e. increase in frequency. As the car approaches the person, at the point where the car is passing the observer, the relative change in distance between the car and the observer is almost zero, hence there will be no Doppler shift and the frequency heard will be the same as emitted. The frequency must therefore decrease to equal $f$ at the origin.\n\nAs the car moves away from the person, again, the Doppler shift will come into effect as the car begins to move away from the person in an approximate straight line. Because the car is moving away from the observer, the observed frequency will be less than the emitted frequency."},{"ref":"PH0782","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0782\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n1. A space probe approaching the Earth communicates with the control center on Earth via radio waves. The speed of the space probe is 24 $\\ce{km}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$ and the frequency of the radio wave it emits is 150 MHz.\n\n    Calculate, in meters, the wavelength of the radio wave sent from the space probe. [2 marks]\n\n2. On the following diagram, the wavefronts of the emitted signal from the probe when it is stationary are shown.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/PH0782b.png\" alt=\"PH0782b\">\n\n    On the diagram above, sketch the wavefronts of the emitted signal by the probe when it is moving in the direction shown.      [2 marks]\n\n3. Determine the difference between the frequency observed at the command centre and the frequency radiated from the source.  [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. $\\dfrac {3.0 \\times 10{^8}}{150\\times 10{^6}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    :answer[$2.0\\,\\ce{m}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    By using the formula of wave speed:\n\n    - $v = \\lambda f$\n\n    After rearranging the formula:\n\n    - $\\lambda = \\dfrac {v}{f} = \\dfrac {3.0 \\times 10{^8}}{150 \\times 10{^6}}$ = 2.0 m\n\n2. Non concentric circular wavefronts $\\checkmark$\n\n    Wavefronts closer together on the right and further apart to the left $\\checkmark$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/PH0782Ansb.png\" alt=\"PH0782Ansb\">\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Due to the motion of the source towards the right, wavefronts will be closer on the right and smaller on the left. After each wavefront is created, the source moves a little to the right, so that next wavefront created is centered around a different location.\n\n3. $(150 \\times 10{^6})\\dfrac {24 \\times 10{^3}}{3.0 \\times 10{^8}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    :answer[$1.2 \\times 10{^4}\\,\\ce{Hz}$]  $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    By using the formula of the Doppler effect:\n\n    - $\\dfrac {\\Delta f}{f} = \\dfrac {v}{c}$\n\n    - $\\Delta f = f\\dfrac {v}{c} = (150 \\times 10{^6})\\dfrac {24 \\times 10{^3}}{3.0 \\times 10{^8}} = 1.2 \\times 10{^4}$ Hz"},{"ref":"PH0783","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0783\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n1. 1. An emission spectrum of a galaxy reveals a redshifted wavelength of hydrogen. Deduce the direction of motion of the galaxy relative to the Earth.   [2 marks]\n\n    2. The speed of a galaxy can change over time. Sketch a graph showing the variation of the change in wavelength of the emitted wave from the galaxy with the speed of the galaxy. [2 marks]\n    (There is no need to add values to the axes)\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/783bb.png\" alt=\"783bb\">\n\n2. A stationary observer measures the frequency and the speed of the wave radiated from a stationary loudspeaker as $f_{0}$ and $v_{0}$ respectively.\n\n    Complete the table below to compare how the observer's measured frequency and wave speed values change in the following situations of relative motion between the source and observer.  [2 marks]\n\n    | | Measured frequency | Measured wave speed |\n    | --- | :-: | :-: |\n    | Moving source towards the stationary observer |  | Same as $v_{0}$ |\n    | Moving observer towards the stationary source | Higher than $f_{0}$ |  |","markScheme":"1. 1. Explanation using the Doppler effect $\\checkmark$\n\n        moving away $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The term redshift means that the observed wavelength is longer than the wavelength originally emitted by the source. Due to the Doppler effect, when a wave source is moving away the spacing of wavefronts is increased because the movement of the source itself causes each new wave to be created at a new location that is in the opposite direction from where the wavefront is travelling.\n\n1. 2. Straight line $\\checkmark$\n\n        passing through origin\n\n        **OR**\n\n        extension of straight line passing through origin $\\checkmark$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/P0783Ansb.png\" alt=\"P0783Ansb\">\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        By using the formula of the Doppler effect:\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta \\lambda}{\\lambda} \\approx \\dfrac{v}{c}$\n\n        and the fact that the wavelength of the emission and speed of light is constant, it can be concluded:\n\n        - $\\Delta \\lambda \\propto v$\n\n        This relationship can be expressed as a straight line passing through the origin.\n\n2. :::tableoptions\n    | | Measured frequency | Measured wave speed |\n    | --- | :-: | :-: |\n    | Moving source towards the stationary observer | Higher than $f_{0} \\checkmark$   | Same as $v_{0}$ |\n    | Moving observer towards the stationary source | Higher than $f_{0}$ | Greater than  $v_{0} \\checkmark$ |\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    For the top left box:\n\n    Due to the loudspeaker moving towards the observer, the wavelength experiences a decrease in wavelength and an increase in frequency..\n\n    For the right bottom box:\n\n    When the loudspeaker remains stationary and the observer is moving towards the source, the observed wavelength remains equal to the emitted wavelength, while the observed frequency becomes higher than the source frequency. This situation implies an increase in speed which occurs due to the observer moving relative to the medium, in accordance with the wave speed formula:\n\n    - $v = \\lambda f$"},{"ref":"PH0999","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0999\n\nA theory to describe observed changes in the pitch of fast-moving sources of sound was first developed in 1842. This same theory later played an important role in interpreting evidence for which of the following?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ the equivalence of the electric and magnetic forces\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ the particulate nature of light\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ the wave-like nature of particles\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ the expansion of the universe","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\nThe Doppler effect was first analyzed in the context of sound waves. Later it was found that this same effect applies to light waves. Light from distant galaxies in all directions appears $\\lq$redshifted' (longer wavelength than it should) which tells us these galaxies are moving away from us. From this it can be concluded that either, we are at the center of the universe (very unlikely), or the universe is expanding!"},{"ref":"PH1063","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1063\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n1. As shown in the diagram below, a star in a distant galaxy (Star A) has an emission spectrum with a peak intensity at 690 nm.  Determine the approximate temperature of the star.  [2 marks]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\nThe spectrum of Star A is further observed to have dark absorption lines at specific wavelengths.  An absorption line of hydrogen is measured with a wavelength of 663 nm as compared to 656 nm in the laboratory.\n\n2. 1. Explain what information this change in the observed wavelength gives about the star.  [2 marks]\n\n    2. Determine the approximate speed of the Star A relative to the observer.  [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Use of Wien’s Law\n\n    ${\\lambda}_{max} T= 2.9 \\times 10^{-3}$\n\n    OR\n\n    $T = \\dfrac{2.9 \\times 10^{-3}}{{\\lambda}_{max}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    Substitute and solve,\n\n    $T=\\dfrac{2.9 \\times 10^{-3}}{6.90 \\times 10^{-7}}$\n\n    $T = 4200\\, \\ce{K}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    Stars are described as blackbody emitters that produce a continuous spectrum with a peak intensity at a specific wavelength, ${\\lambda}_{max}$.  Wien's Law states that this wavelength is inversely proportional to the temperature, $T$, of the star.\n\n    - ${\\lambda}_{max} = \\dfrac{b}{T}$\n\n    Rearranged,\n\n    - $T=\\dfrac{b}{{\\lambda}_{max}}$\n\n    The proportionality constant, $b$, is $2.9 \\times 10^{-3}\\, \\ce{m}\\, \\ce{K}$.\n\n    This gives,\n\n    - $T=\\dfrac{2.9 \\times 10^{-3}}{{\\lambda}_{max}}$\n\n    Substituting and solving (noting that $1\\, \\ce{nm} = 10^{-9}\\, \\ce{m}$),\n\n    - $T=\\dfrac{2.9 \\times 10^{-3}}{6.90\\times10^{-7}}$\n\n    - $T= 4200\\, \\ce{K}$\n\n2. 1. The star/Star A is moving away from the observer $\\checkmark$\n\n        The $\\underline{\\textrm{Doppler Effect}}$ results in the wavelength being longer/$\\ll$Doppler$\\gg$ shifted towards red end of spectrum $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        Relative motion between an observer and a source of waves results in the Doppler Effect where waves are observed to have a different frequency than those emitted.  When a source of waves is moving away from the observer, the distance between wavecrests increases - the wavelength increases and frequency decreases.  This difference is described as a Doppler “shift”. Note that the wave speed remains the same.\n\n        The emission spectrum from a star is said to be “red-shifted” when the star is moving away from an observer.  This is due to red wavelengths being at the longer end of the visible spectrum.  If a star were moving towards an observer the spectrum would be described as “blue-shifted” since the observed wavelengths would be shorter than those emitted.\n\n    2. Use of (accept equal sign),\n\n        $\\dfrac{\\Delta \\lambda}{\\lambda} \\approx \\dfrac{v}{c}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $v \\approx c \\dfrac{\\Delta \\lambda}{\\lambda}$\n\n        Substitution and solve,\n\n        $v \\approx (3.00\\times10^8)\\Bigl(\\dfrac{663-656}{656}\\Bigr)$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $v\\approx 3.2\\times10^6\\, \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        When a source is moving relative to an observer and emitting a wave of frequency, $f$, the observed frequency, $f'$, is given by,\n\n        - $f'=f+\\Delta f$\n\n        Similarly, the observed wavelength, $\\lambda '$, is given by,\n\n        - $\\lambda '=\\lambda+\\Delta \\lambda$\n\n        Both the frequency and wavelength are both shifted by small amounts in comparison to their original value, we can use the data booklet formula\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta f}{f}=\\dfrac{\\Delta \\lambda}{\\lambda}$\n\n        When the speed of the source is very small compared to the wave speed ($v\\ll c$), as is the case with a moving star emitting electromagnetic waves, it can be further said that,\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta f}{f}=\\dfrac{\\Delta \\lambda}{\\lambda}\\approx \\dfrac{v}{c}$\n\n        With reference to wavelength and rearranging,\n\n        - $v \\approx c \\dfrac{\\Delta \\lambda}{\\lambda}$\n\n        Substitution and solve,\n\n        - $v\\approx (3.00\\times10^8)\\Bigl(\\dfrac{663-656}{656}\\Bigr)$\n\n        - $v\\approx3.2\\times10^6\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH1064","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1064\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nA racing car is travelling along a very long straight section of a track at a constant speed.  A spectator is sitting next to the track halfway along the straight section.\n\n1. As the car passes, the spectator notices a change in pitch of the sound made by the car engine.  State the name given to this phenomena. [1 mark]\n\n2. 1. Use the table below to indicate (tick) how the sound waves heard by the spectator, as the car approaches, compare with those emitted by the car if it were at rest. [2 marks]\n\n        | | $\\hspace{1em}$Increased$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$Decreased$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$No Change$\\hspace{1em}$ |\n        | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n        | $\\hspace{1em}$Frequency$\\hspace{1em}$ |  |  |  |\n        | $\\hspace{1em}$Wavelength$\\hspace{1em}$ |  |  |  |\n        | $\\hspace{1em}$Wave Speed$\\hspace{1em}$ |  |  |  |\n\n    2. On the image below, sketch  the shape of the wavefronts as the car travels away from the spectator. [2 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n    3. Determine the change in frequency as heard by the driver of the racing car. [1 mark]\n\n3. The spectator moves away from the track. Outline how the  change in frequency in this new position compares with that in the original position next to the track. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. The Doppler Effect   $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    Relative motion between an observer and a source of waves results in the Doppler Effect where waves are observed to have a different frequency (and wavelength) than those emitted.\n\n2. 1. :::tableoptions{}\n        | | $\\hspace{1em}$Increased$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$Decreased$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$Constant$\\hspace{1em}$ |\n        | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n        | $\\hspace{1em}$Frequency$\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\checkmark$ |  |  |\n        | $\\hspace{1em}$Wavelength$\\hspace{1em}$ |  | $\\checkmark$ |  |\n        | $\\hspace{1em}$Wave Speed$\\hspace{1em}$ |  |  | $\\checkmark$ |\n\n        Frequency increases\\\n        AND\\\n        Wavelength decreases   $\\checkmark$\n\n        Wave speed remains constant   $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        When a source of waves is moving towards an observer, the distance between wavefronts in front of the source decreases - the wavelength decreases and frequency increases.  As the car approaches, the spectator hears the sound in front of the car with the higher frequency.\n\n        The opposite is true when a source of waves moves away from an observer.  In this case, the spectator will hear a sound with a lower frequency than is emitted by the car since the distance between wavefronts has increased.\n\n        The relative motion between the source and the observer does not change the speed at which the wave moves through the medium.  The sound wave will still travel at the speed of sound in air.\n\n    2. Circular wavefronts.    $\\checkmark$ \\\n        At least three clearly shown wavefronts that are closer together in front of the car AND further apart behind the car.   $\\checkmark$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        When a wave is emitted from a source, it will produce circular wavefronts, wavelength $\\lambda$, that travel at a constant speed from a fixed point of origin.  If a wave source is moving, then successive wavefronts have a different point of origin in the direction of travel of the source.  This means that the distance between wavefronts ahead of the source is less than $\\lambda$, and the distance between the wavefronts behind the source is greater than $\\lambda$.  Note that, since the source is moving at constant speed, the distance between wavefronts remains constant whether ahead or behind the source.\n\n    3. No change.   $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        Since the driver is in the car and moving at the same speed as the car, there is no relative motion between them.  The lack of relative motion means that the Doppler Effect will not be experienced by the driver and no change in frequency will be heard.\n\n3. Away from the track, the change in frequency will not be as sudden. OWTTE   $\\checkmark$ \\\n    The maximum and minimum frequencies of the car engine sound will be the same in each position.   $\\checkmark$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n    $\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    The Doppler Effect relates to the line-of-sight (radial) velocity of a moving source or observer.\n\n    When an observer is close to the path of a moving source, the radial velocity is approximately constant and changes direction suddenly when the observer is passed by the source.  Therefore the change in frequency is abrupt.\n\n    When an observer is further away from the path, the radial velocity gradually decreases, is zero when the source passes, and gradually increases as the source moves away.  This means that the frequency change is less abrupt.\n\n    The diagram below shows how the change in frequency depends on the distance from the path of the moving source (the solid line is closest)."},{"ref":"PH1900","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1900\n\nThe diagram below shows a source of sound $S$ moving to the right at constant speed, emitting wavefronts at regular intervals.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Doppler%20Effect/images/ph1900q.png\" alt=\"ph1900q\">\n$\\small{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra, - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\nTwo observers, $A$ and $B$, are at rest in the medium and placed behind and in front of the source respectively.\n\nConsider the following statements:\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | Observer $B$ hears a lower frequency than the one emitted by the source |\n  | II. | The speed at which wavefronts will reach $A$ is the same as the speed at which wavefronts will reach $B$. |\n  | III. | The source is moving at a speed which is lower than the speed of sound. |\n\nWhich of the following are correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nI. Incorrect. The source is moving towards $B$ which means that this observer will hear a higher frequency than the one emitted by the source.\n\nII. Correct. The speed at which wavefronts will reach both $A$ and $B$ is determined by the medium and in this case corresponds to the speed of sound.\n\nIII. Correct. Since wavefronts can form and exist ahead of the source, the source's speed must be less than the speed of sound in the medium. The Doppler Effect (compression in front, stretching behind) is visible, but not the shockwave pattern of supersonic motion."}]},{"name":"Refraction, Diffraction, Superposition, and Interference","questions":[{"ref":"PH0047","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0047\n\nWavefronts travel across a boundary from medium 1 to medium 2 as shown.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/refraction.png\" alt=\"refraction\">\n\nWhich of the following correctly describes the change, if any, in the speed, wavelength and frequency of the waves as they pass from across the boundary?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{2em}$ Speed $\\hspace{2em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Wavelength $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Frequency $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ A. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Increases | Increases | Unchanged |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ B. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Unchanged | Increases | Decreases |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ C. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Increases | Decreases | Increases |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ D. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Decreases | Decreases | Unchanged |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nNote that the question states that the waves travel from Medium 1 to Medium 2, so are travelling 'upwards' on the page.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/snell.png\" alt=\"snell\">\n\nIt can be seen on the diagram that when the waves cross the boundary, the wavelength decreases. At a boundary, the frequency of a wave is unchanged because the number of waves entering the boundary every second must equal the number of waves that leave the boundary every second.\n\nFrom the relationship $v = f\\lambda$, if $f$ remains constant and $\\lambda$ decreases, the $v$ must also decrease.\n\nAlso, if rays are added at $90^o$ to the wavefronts, it can be seen that the rays are bending towards the normal which indicates a decrease in speed as the waves cross the boundary."},{"ref":"PH0101","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0101\n\nTwo water waves with the same amplitude are traveling towards each other. The water level against position graph of one water wave is shown below. The equilibrium water level is 5 cm.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/PH0101.png\" alt=\"PH0101\">\n\nWhat are the maximum and minimum water levels when the two waves overlap?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$ Maximum water level $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Minimum water level $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ A. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 14 cm | 6 cm |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ B. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 9 cm | 6 cm |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ C. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 14 cm | 1 cm |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ D. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 9 cm | 1 cm |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe principle of superposition states that the resultant wave is the sum of displacement **from equilibrium** of each wave. \n\nThe amplitude of the wave is the maximum displacement from equilibrium, which can be read off the graph as 7 cm - 5 cm = 2 cm.\n\nSince each of the waves have an amplitude of 2 cm and the equilibrium water level is 5 cm, after superimposing the two waves we find that the amplitude of the resultant wave is 4 cm. \n\nTherefore, the maximum and minimum water levels can be determined as: 5 cm + 4 cm = 9 cm for the maximum level and 5 cm - 4 cm = 1 cm for the minimum level."},{"ref":"PH0102","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0102\n\nThe speed of light in medium $X$ is $\\dfrac{2}{3}$  of the speed in medium $Y$, while the speed of light in medium $Z$ is $\\dfrac{4}{5}$ of the speed in medium $Y$. When light travels from medium $X$ to medium $Z$ with an angle of incidence $i$, what is the sine of the angle of refraction, $\\sin (r)\\,$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{6}{5}\\, \\sin\\,(i)$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{5}{6}\\, \\sin\\,(i)$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{15}{8}\\, \\sin\\,(i)$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{8}{15}\\, \\sin\\,(i)$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nRearranging the data booklet equation in terms of medium $X$ and $Z$.\n\n- $\\dfrac{v_2}{v_1} = \\dfrac{\\sin\\,\\theta_2}{\\sin\\,\\theta_1}\\rightarrow \\dfrac{v_z}{v_x} = \\dfrac{\\sin\\,r}{\\sin\\,i}\\rightarrow \\sin\\,(r) = \\dfrac{v_z}{v_x}  \\sin\\,(i)$  \n\nThen consider the speed of light information given by the question\n\n- $v_x=\\dfrac{2}{3} v_y$, $v_z=\\dfrac{4}{5} v_y$\n\nThis gives \n\n- $\\dfrac{v_z}{v_x} = \\dfrac{\\dfrac{4}{5}}{\\dfrac{2}{3}}=\\dfrac{6}{5}$\n\nand\n\n-  $\\sin\\,(r) = \\dfrac{v_z}{v_x}  \\sin\\,(i) = \\dfrac{6}{5}\\sin\\,(i)$  \n\nHence A is the answer."},{"ref":"PH0122","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0122\n\nA monochromatic beam of light is incident on a double slit arrangement where the slit separation is $9.0 \\times 10^{-5}\\,\\ce{m}$ and the slit-screen distance is 1.0 m. The graph below shows the relative intensity of the light reaching the screen with respect to the distance from the central point of the pattern.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/PH0122.png\" alt=\"PH0122\">\n\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\nWhat is the wavelength of the light?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $9.0 \\times 10^{-7}\\,\\ce{m}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $3.0 \\times 10^{-7}\\,\\ce{m}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $4.5 \\times 10^{-5}\\,\\ce{m}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $4.5 \\times 10^{-7}\\,\\ce{m}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe fringe separation is the product of wavelength of the waves to the slit-screen distance divided by the slit width.  \n\n$s=\\dfrac{\\lambda D}{d}$\n\n$\\lambda=\\dfrac{sd}{D}$\n\nThe distance between bright fringes on the graph is 0.5 cm.\n\n$\\lambda =\\dfrac{(0.005)(9 \\times 10^{-5})}{1}$\n\n$\\lambda = 4.5 \\times 10^{-7}\\,\\ce{m}$"},{"ref":"PH0151","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0151\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\nTwo sources $A$ and $B$ produce waves of amplitude $a$, and period $T$ on the surface of a swimming pool as shown. The wave generator $A$ is turned on at time $t=0$, and $B$ at time $t=\\dfrac{T}{2}$. Both generators create a crest when first turned on. The wavefronts shown in the illustration are one wavelength apart each. Determine the amplitude of the oscillation of the medium at point $C$. \n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Zero.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $a$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2a$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Varies between 0 and $2a$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nWaves are produced in the same medium. This means they have the same speed. \n\nThey also have the same period, implying that their wavelengths are also equal.  \n\nSince the waves start half a period apart, the phase difference between the sources is half a cycle, and remains constant since they are produced at the same frequency.\n\nPoint $C$ is three wavelengths from $A$, and five wavelengths from $B$, making the path difference two wavelengths or two whole cycles.  But after every single cycle, the two waves maintain a constant phase difference of $\\pi$, leading to destructive interference."},{"ref":"PH0208","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0208\n\nA radio with a bright light is placed outside the doorway of a long, dark room as shown. A student in a corner near the door can hear the sound of the radio while standing in the dark region of the room. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/PH0208.png\" alt=\"PH0208\">\n\nWhich property of waves best explains why light does not reach her while the sound does?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Diffraction\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Interference\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Polarization\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Refraction","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nDiffraction is the spreading out of a wave while passing through an obstacle or aperture. The door of the room can be considered as the aperture. When the wavelength of the wave is comparable with the aperture size, diffraction is greater. The wavelength of the sound wave is much larger than the wavelength of the light wave and closer to the size of the door. Therefore, sound waves diffract more while passing through the door and reach the student while light waves do not. The answer is A."},{"ref":"PH0224","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0224\n\nA light ray is incident on a semicircular glass block from the air. The glass has a greater refractive index than air. Which diagram correctly represents a possible path of the light ray as it enters and leaves the block?   \n\n| | | | | |\n\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---| \n\n| A.  |    |   |   |B.|\n\n|<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/PH0224A.jpg\" alt=\"PH0224A\"> |   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/PH0224B.jpg\" alt=\"PH0224B\">|\n\n| $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n|C. |   |   |  |D. |\n\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/PH0224C.jpg\" alt=\"PH0224C\">|   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/PH0224D.jpg\" alt=\"PH0224D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nLight will refract away from the normal at a boundary where there is an increase in speed as in a glass to air boundary. Answers A and C show this. A light ray will not refract at a boundary if its angle of incidence is 0°. It must therefore enter the curved edge aligned with the center of the straight side as in A. Therefore the answer is A."},{"ref":"PH0225","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0225\n\nA light and a heavy spring are attached at point $Y$. A pulse was generated in the light spring and incident on the heavy spring. The positions of transmitted and reflected pulses after some time are as shown. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/ph0225c.png\" alt=\"ph0225c\">\n\nWhich of the following is true about the incident pulse?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ $\\textrm{A.}$ $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{Positive displacement}$ $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{Larger amplitude than reflected pulse}$ $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ B. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Positive displacement | Smaller amplitude than reflected pulse |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ C. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Negative displacement | Larger amplitude than reflected pulse |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ D. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Negative displacement | Smaller amplitude than reflected pulse |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe energy of the incident pulse is shared with reflected and transmitted pulses. Therefore, the amplitude of the incident pulse should be greater than the one of the reflected pulse. Pulses are inverted after reflection from the heavy boundaries. Hence, the incident pulse should have a negative displacement. Therefore the answer is C."},{"ref":"PH0241","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0241\n\nIncident light of wavelength $600\\,\\ce{nm}$ is emitted towards two slits separated by $10\\, \\ce{\\mu m}$. The interference pattern is observed on a wall. The distance between two adjacent maxima is 0.12 m. What is the distance of the wall from the slits?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2.0 mm\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2.0 cm\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2.0 dm\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2.0 m","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nFor the distance of the wall;\n\n- $D = \\dfrac{sd}{\\lambda}$\n\n- $D = \\dfrac{(10 \\,\\times \\,10^{-6}\\,\\ce{m})(0.12\\,\\ce{m})}{600 \\,\\times \\,10^{-9}\\,\\ce{m}}$\n\n- *D* = 2 m"},{"ref":"PH0295","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0295\n\nA wave is incident on a boundary between two media, $X$ and $Y$. The wave travels faster in medium $Y$ than in medium $X$\n\nWhich of the following diagrams shows a possible path for this wave?\n\n| | | | | |\n\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---| \n\n| A.  |    |   |   |B.|\n\n|<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/295.jpg\" alt=\"295\"> |   |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/295-2.jpg\" alt=\"295-2\">|\n\n| $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n|C. |   |   |  |D. |\n\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/295-3.jpg\" alt=\"295-3\">||||<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/295-4.jpg\" alt=\"295-4\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nThe wave is faster in medium $Y$ than in medium $X$. As a result, the ray representing the wave is expected to bend away from the normal, refracting into the original medium. \n\nIf the angle of incidence exceeds the critical angle, however, the angle of refraction will be undefined and the wave will undergo total internal reflection.\n\nThis is exactly what is shown by Option D, which is the correct response.\n\nOption A shows the ray bending towards the normal, Option C shows no bending at all, and Option B shows an impossible path for the wave to travel."},{"ref":"PH0303-3","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0303-3\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA parallel beam of red light passes through two parallel slits with negligible width and an interference pattern is formed on a distant screen.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/PH0303.png\" alt=\"PH0303\">\n\n$\\theta$ is the angle that shows the diffraction angle of the light waves reaching any point on the screen.\n\n1. Explain why an interference pattern is observed on the screen.  [3 marks]\n\n2. On the axes below, sketch a graph to show how the intensity I of light on the screen varies with angle $\\theta$.    [2 marks]\n    (There is no need to add values to the axes)\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/PH0303-1.png\" alt=\"PH0303-1\">\n\n3. In the arrangement, the red light source is replaced with a blue light source with the same intensity. How would the drawn graph in (a) (ii) differ?          [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. diffraction at slits $\\checkmark$\n\n    phase/path difference $\\checkmark$\n\n    waves superpose/interfere constructively or destructively $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        When light passes through a slit, it undergoes diffraction and is incident on the screen. Any point on the screen receives light from two slits and the waves superpose with each other.    There are some points in which maximum and minimum intensity are observed due to the path difference between two light waves emitted from two slits.\n\n    It is important to explain the answer in the following order;\n\n    - diffraction\n    - path difference\n    - constructive or destructive superposition/interference\n\n2.  $\\hspace{1em}$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/PH0303-3.png\" alt=\"PH0303-3\">\n\n    Same intensity for all maxima by eye $\\checkmark$\n\n    Same width for all maxima by eye $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The slits have negligible width. Therefore, the interference pattern is not modulated with a single slit diffraction pattern. Near the centre of a double slit interference pattern, the maxima are approximately all the same height.\n\n3.  Narrower maxima/maxima closer together $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The equation for interference;\n\n    - $n\\lambda=dsin\\theta$\n\n    By rearranging;\n\n    - $sin\\theta=\\dfrac{n\\lambda}{d}$\n\n    According to the equation, the angular separation of maxima is proportional to the wavelength of light. Since blue light has a shorter wavelength than red light, maxima will be narrower."},{"ref":"PH0451","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0451\n\nThe diagram shows a beam of light passing from medium $X$ to medium $Y$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/PH0451.png\" alt=\"PH0451\">\n\nIf the angle of incidence of the light increases, which of the following changes can be implemented to keep the angle of refraction the same?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n\n  | | |\n\n  | :-- | :-- |\n\n  | I. | Medium X can be replaced with another medium which has a lower refractive index. |\n\n  | II. | Medium X can be replaced with another medium which has a higher refractive index. |\n\n  | III. | Medium Y can be replaced with another medium which has a higher refractive index. |\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Only I\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nSnell's Law governs the angles of incidence and refraction as a wave passes between two media with different refractive indices:\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\ce {sin(θi)}} {\\ce{sin(θr)}} = \\dfrac{n_y}{n_x}$\n\n- $\\ce {sin(\\theta_r)} = (\\dfrac{n_x}{n_y})\\ \\ce {sin(\\theta_i)}$\n\nStatements I and III are correct: If $\\theta_i$ increases, $\\ce {sin(\\theta_i)}$ will also increase.\n\nAs a result, either $n_x$ must decrease (statement I) and/or $n_y$ must increase (statement III) to keep the angle of refraction $\\theta_r$ the same."},{"ref":"PH0513","marks":"9","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH0513\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nA student investigates the refraction of light. A light beam emitted from the ray box is incident on a transparent block.  The ray trace sheet and the position of the ray box and the transparent block are shown below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/raybox.png\" alt=\"raybox\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with chemix - https:// chemix.org/]}}$\n\n1. The distances *a* and *b* are measured. Then the measurement is repeated for different values for angle $\\theta$. The variation of *b* with *a* is plotted on the graph below according to obtained data.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%2010.53.50%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 10.53.50 AM\">\n\n    Draw the line of best fit for the plotted data on the graph. [1 mark]\n\n2. Theory suggests that\n\n    - $n=\\dfrac{a}{b}$\n\n    Where $n$ is the refractive index of the material and has a constant value.\n\n    1. Suggest whether the data is consistent with the theoretical prediction. [2 marks]\n\n    2. By using the graph calculate *n* with its absolute uncertainty for the data point when *a* is measured to be $3.0 \\,$cm. [4 marks]\n    3. The accepted value for the refractive index of the transparent block is 1.71. Comment on your result. [1 mark]\n\n3. State why using the line of best fit to find the value for $n$ is better than finding $n$ from an individual data point. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. A straight line passes through within the error bars $\\checkmark$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%2010.54.06%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 10.54.06 AM\">\n\n2.\n    1. $\\ll$ According to the theory $\\gg$, *a* is proportional to $b$ / $a\\propto b$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        A best-fit line that is a straight line that passes through the origin and pass through all uncertainty bars can be drawn $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\ll$ therefore, yes, the data agrees with the theory $\\gg$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        According to the predicted relationship;\n\n        $a\\propto\\ b$\n\n        This relationship suggests that the graph should be a straight line which passes through the origin. According to plotted data, that kind of straight line can be drawn.\n\n    2. Correct calculation of percentage uncertainties of *a* and *b* $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\ll \\dfrac{\\Delta a}{a} \\times 100\\% = \\dfrac{0.1}{3.0}\\times 100 \\% = 3.3\\% \\gg$\n\n        - $\\ll \\dfrac{\\Delta b}{b} \\times 100 \\% = \\dfrac{0.1}{1.8} \\times 100 \\% = 5.6 \\% \\gg$\n\n        The percentage error of n = $\\ll 3.3 \\% + 5.6 \\% \\gg = 8.9\\%$\n\n        - $n=\\ll\\dfrac{3.0}{1.8}=1.67=\\gg1.7$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\Delta n = \\ll \\dfrac{3.0}{1.8} \\times 0.089 = 0.148 = 0.15$ (0.1 and 0.2 also accepted here) $\\checkmark$\n\n        Giving\n\n        - $n=1.67\\pm 0.15$ **OR**  $   n =1.7\\pm 0.2 $  **OR**  $n=1.7 \\pm 0.1   $ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Note that precision (decimal places) on n and ∆n must match\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        According to the graph, values of *a* and *b* with their absolute uncertainties;\n\n        - $a=3.0\\pm0.1\\ $cm \\\n$b=1.8\\pm0.1\\ $cm\n\n        Solving for the percentage uncertainties of *a* and *b*:\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta a}{a} \\times 100\\% = \\dfrac{0.1}{3.0}\\times 100 \\% = 3.3\\%$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta b}{b} \\times 100 \\% = \\dfrac{0.1}{1.8} \\times 100 \\% = 5.6 \\%$\n\n        By using the rules of propagation of percentage uncertainties, the percentage uncertainties are added in multiplication or division operations.\n\n        - % $n=3.3\\%+5.6\\%=8.9\\%$\n\n        The value of *n*:\n\n        - $n=\\dfrac{3.0}{1.8} = 1.67$\n\n        - $n=1.67\\pm (1.67\\times 8.9\\%) =1.67\\pm0.148$\n\n        The uncertainty should be expressed to one significant figure, or two significant figures if the first digit is 1 which is the case here. So the best answer is\n\n        - $n=1.67±0.15$\n\n        But $n=1.7±0.1$  **OR** $n=1.7±0.2$ are also accepted here\n\n    3.\n        1.71 is between the upper and lower value of experimental result ($\\checkmark$) so it matches with the actual value/is accurate ($\\checkmark$)\n\n        **OR**\n\n        1.71 is not between the upper and lower value of experimental result so it does not match with the actual value/is inaccurate (ECF from previous parts)\n\n3. Using the gradient takes into account all data points collected $\\checkmark$\n\n    **OR**\n\n    reducing random error/and individual point may be an anomaly in the data. $\\checkmark$\n\n    reducing random error $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    To prove a relationship, one piece of data is not enough. The relationship can be generalized by using a graph. More data allows for drawing a graph that can support the relationship. Additionally, using more data and taking the average of them reduces the amount of random error."},{"ref":"PH0525","marks":"9","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0525\n\n**Maximum mark: 9**\n\nTwo speakers emit identical sound waves. A sound sensor moves along the line $XY$. As the sound sensor moves it detects locations where there is almost no sound. Some distances between the speakers and points $A$ and $B$ are given on the diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/PH0525.png\" alt=\"PH0525\">\n\n1. Explain why there are quiet locations. [3 marks]\n\n2. The speed of sound is $340\\ \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$. Point $O$ is where the highest intensity is received by the sound sensor. Point $A$ is the first quiet spot as the sensor moves from $O$ to $X$ and point $B$ is the second quiet spot as the sensor moves from $O$ to $Y$.\n\n    1. Show that the frequency of the sound is approximately 140 Hz. [3 marks]\n\n    2.     Calculate the distance $|S_1-B|$, distance between Speaker 1 and point $B$. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Sound waves from Speaker 1 and Speaker 2 superpose/interfere with each other. $\\checkmark$\n\n    At some points path difference is half integer of wavelength of sound wave/waves arrive out of phase $\\checkmark$\n\n    So destructive interference occurs $\\checkmark$\n\n2. \n\n    1. Path difference for point $A$ $\\hspace{1em} 3.4 - 2.2 = 1.2\\ \\ce{m}$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\Delta s=(n+\\dfrac{1}{2})\\lambda$, $\\hspace{1em} n=0$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\lambda=(2)(1.2)=2.4\\ \\ce{m}$\n\n        - $\\lambda f=v$\n\n        - $f=\\dfrac{340}{2.4}=141.6\\approx 140\\ \\ce{Hz}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        At the first minimum away from the central maximum, the path length difference between the two waves will be half a wavelength which will mean that the two waves arrive out of phase with one another at that point.\n\n        The path length difference can be found from $3.4 - 2.2 = 1.2\\ \\ce{m}$\n\n        The wavelength is therefore $2(1.2) = 2.4\\ \\ce{m}$\n\n        Then using the universal wave equation, \n\n        - $f=\\dfrac{v}{λ}=\\dfrac{340}{2.4}=141.6\\approx 140\\ \\ce{Hz}$ \n\n    2.  ::indent\n\n        - $\\Delta s=(n+\\dfrac{1}{2})\\lambda, \\hspace{1em} n=1$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\Delta s=(\\dfrac{3}{2})\\lambda = 3.6\\ \\ce{m}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\Delta s = |S_1-B| - |S_2-B|$\n\n        - $|S1-B| = 3.6 + 2.1$ = :answer[$5.7\\ \\ce{m}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        As point $B$ is the second location of destructive interference (on the second nodal line), the path length difference will be $(\\frac{3}{2})\\lambda = 3.6\\ \\ce{m}$\n\n        As the distance from $B$ to $S_2$ is known to be $2.1\\ \\ce{m}$, the distance from $B$ to $S_1$ must be $3.6 + 2.1 = 5.7\\ \\ce{m}$"},{"ref":"PH0530","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0530\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nA speaker emits sound waves toward a boundary between dry, cool air and moist, hot air. The speed of sound in the dry air is $340\\ \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$ and the speed of sound in the moist air is $375\\ \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$. Three wavefronts enter the boundary with an angle of 51$\\ce{\\degree}$ as shown.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/Screenshot%202026-04-22%20at%208.51.05%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-04-22 at 8.51.05 AM\">\n\n1. Draw lines to show wavefronts in the moist air. [3 marks]\n\n2. For the sound traveling in the moist air, calculate the angle between the wavefronts and the boundary. [2 marks]\n\n3. Now the moist air region has been replaced with another medium $X$. Determine the minimum speed of sound in the medium $X$ above which sound will not cross the boundary for this angle of incidence. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. \n\n    .\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/Screenshot%202026-04-22%20at%2010.45.42%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-04-22 at 10.45.42 AM\">\n\n    Correct orientation $\\checkmark$\n\n    Greater separation between wavefronts $\\checkmark$\n\n    Correct spacing in moist air (aligns with incident wavefronts) $\\checkmark$\n\n    (by eye) \n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    Moist air is less dense than dry air so sound moves faster. The wave direction will refract away from the normal.\n\n    Note that the angle between the wavefronts and the boundary is **equal to** the angle between the ray and the normal.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/Screenshot%202026-04-22%20at%209.16.11%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-04-22 at 9.16.11 AM\"> \n\n    Because frequency remains constant across the boundary, if the velocity increases the wavelength must also increase.The length of three wavefronts should be equally spaced and connect with the incident wavefronts at the boundary\n\n2. $\\dfrac{\\sin(51\\degree)}{\\sin\\theta}=\\dfrac{340}{375}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\theta$ = :answer[$59\\degree$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    Note that the angle between the wavefront and the boundary is equal to the angle between the ray and the normal. Thus Snell's Law can be used with the given angle to find the angle that the wavefront makes with the boundary after refraction.\n\n    Using Snell's law $\\dfrac{\\sin\\theta _1}{\\sin\\theta _2}=\\dfrac{v_1}{v_2}$. \n\n    $\\dfrac{\\sin(51\\degree)}{\\sin\\theta}=\\dfrac{340}{375}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\theta$ = :answer[$59\\degree$] \n\n3. $\\dfrac{\\sin(51\\degree)}{\\sin(90\\degree)}=\\dfrac{340}{v}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $v$ = :answer[$437\\ \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    For the waves not to be able to enter, the angle of incidence must be equal to the critical angle or higher. At the critical angle, the angle of refraction will be $90\\degree$.\n\n    Substituting into the Snell's Law equation\n\n    $\\dfrac{\\sin\\theta _1}{\\sin\\theta _2}=\\dfrac{v_1}{v_2}$. \n\n    $\\dfrac{\\sin(51\\degree)}{\\sin(90\\degree)}=\\dfrac{340}{v}$\n\n    $v = 437\\ \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH0573","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0573\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nTwo water waves $X$ and $Y$ travel on the same horizontal level as shown in the diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/PH0573.png\" alt=\"PH0573\">\n\n$\\small{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra, - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\nThe two waves have the same period of 3 s and different amplitudes.\n\n1. State what is meant by the principle of superposition. [2 marks]\n\n2. At $t = 0$ the two waves are just about to overlap.\n\n    1. Sketch the wave pattern at $t = 0.75\\ \\ce{s}$. [2 marks]\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/Screenshot%202025-12-29%20at%205.37.09%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-12-29 at 5.37.09 PM\">\n\n    2. Sketch the wave pattern when the two waves completely overlap and name the type of interference that is occurring. [2 marks]\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/Screenshoot%202025-12-29%20at%205.37.09%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshoot 2025-12-29 at 5.37.09 PM\">","markScheme":"1. Two or more waves overlap / **OWTTE** $\\checkmark$\n\n    The resultant displacement at any point is found by adding the displacements produced by each individual wave / **OWTTE** $\\checkmark$\n\n2. \n\n    1. The overlap is in the first half of the waves only $\\checkmark$\n\n        Maximum displacement of the overlapped part is positive and less than the amplitudes of both waves $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        At $t = 0.75$ s each wave will have moved one-quarter of their wavelength, which means that the front half of each wave will overlap.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/PH0573-1.png\" alt=\"PH0573-1\">\n\n        $\\small{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\n        The displacements of the two waves are added.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/PH0573-2.png\" alt=\"PH0573-2\">\n\n        $\\small{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\n    2. Correct wave pattern with higher amplitude than the two waves. $\\checkmark$\n\n        Constructive interference. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The two waves completely overlap and the displacements add to each other. When the overall result has a higher amplitude, it is constructive interference.\n\n        \n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/waves1.png\" alt=\"waves1\">\n\n        $\\small{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\n        Then the superposition will be\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/waves2.png\" alt=\"waves2\">\n\n        $\\small{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$"},{"ref":"PH0576","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0576\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n1. The diagram shows a light ray that travels from air into a glass block.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/PH0576.png\" alt=\"PH0576\">\n\n    1. State what is meant by the refractive index of a medium. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Knowing that\n\n   \n\n        The refractive index of glass = 1.5\\\n\n        The angle of incidence = $40\\degree$\n\n    \n\n        Draw on the diagram a ray to represent the path of light in the glass block. [1 mark]\n\n    \n\n    \n\n    3. Calculate the angle of refraction inside the glass block. [2 marks]\n\n2. Another light ray travels inside the glass block as shown in the diagram.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/PH0576-1.png\" alt=\"PH0576-1\">\n\n     Determine the minimum angle $\\theta$ for which this reflection would occur. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. \n\n    1. Ratio of the speed of waves in two different media. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        Normally, the refractive index gives the ratio of the speed of light in a vacuum to the speed of light in a medium ($n = \\dfrac{c}{v}$). More generally, however, refractive index can refer to the ratio of speeds at a boundary, ($n = \\dfrac{v_1}{v_2}$).\n\n    2. Any ray closer to the normal. $\\checkmark$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/PH0576-2.png\" alt=\"PH0576-2\">\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        Light has a higher speed in air than that in glass.\n\n        The refractive index of air is 1; while the refractive index of glass is greater than one since\n\n        $n=\\dfrac{c}{v}$\n\n        According to Snell's law\n\n        $\\dfrac{n_1}{n_2} =\\dfrac{\\sin(\\theta_2)}{\\sin(\\theta_1)}$\n\n        Then the angle of refraction is less than the angle of incidence and the refracted ray is closer to the normal.\n\n    3. $\\ll \\dfrac{n_1}{n_2} = \\dfrac{\\sin (\\theta_2)}{\\sin(\\theta_1)}\\gg$\n\n        $\\sin(\\theta_2)=\\dfrac{\\sin (40)}{1.5}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\theta_2$ = :answer[$25^o$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    Using Snell's law \n\n    - $\\dfrac{n_1}{n_2} = \\dfrac{\\sin (\\theta_2)}{\\sin(\\theta_1)}$\n\n    for air, n = 1\n\n    Substituting gives\n\n    - $\\sin(\\theta_2)=\\dfrac{\\sin (40)}{1.5}$ \n\n    - $\\theta_2$ = $25^o$\n\n    \n\n2. $\\ll \\dfrac{(n_1)}{(n_2)} = \\dfrac{\\sin\\,(\\theta_2)}{\\sin\\,(\\theta_1)}\\gg$\n\n    $\\sin \\ (\\theta_c)=\\dfrac{1}{1.5}$  $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\theta_c$ = :answer[$41.8\\degree$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    When light travels from a medium of higher reflective index; the angle of refraction is greater than the angle of incidence according to Snell's law\n\n    - $\\dfrac{n_1}{n_2} = \\dfrac{\\sin\\,\\theta_2}{\\sin\\,\\theta_1}$\n\n    The critical angle is the angle of incidence that corresponds to the angle of refraction of $90\\degree$.\n\n    Then\n\n    \n\n    - $\\dfrac{n_{glass}}{n_{air}} =\\dfrac{\\sin\\,(90)}{\\sin\\,\\theta_c}$\n\n    - $\\sin(\\theta_c)=(\\dfrac{1}{1.5})$\n\n    \n\n    - $\\theta_c=42\\degree$"},{"ref":"PH0687","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0687\n\nIn a double-slit experiment, monochromatic light with a wavelength of $600\\, $nm passes through two slits spaced $1.0\\, $mm apart. The resulting interference pattern is observed on a screen placed 2 meters away from the slits. What is the approximate value for the angle between the central peak and the 2$^{\\ce{nd}}$ order maximum?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  1.2 \\times 10^{-6}$ degrees.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  1.2 \\times 10^{-6}$ radians.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  1.2 \\times 10^{-3}$ degrees.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  1.2 \\times 10^{-3}$ radians.","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFor the distance from the centre of the pattern to the second order maximum\n\n- $2s=(2)\\dfrac{(\\lambda D)}{d}=(2)\\dfrac{(600 \\times 10{^{-9})(2)}}{1 \\times 10{^{-3}}}=2.4 \\times 10{^{-3}}\\,$m\n\nFor the angle\n\n- $\\tan \\theta= \\dfrac{2.4 \\times 10^{-3}}{2}=1.2 \\times 10{^{-3}}$\n\n- $\\tan \\theta \\approx \\theta = 1.2 \\times 10{^{-3}}$ radians\n\nAlternatively, we can use the HL equation from the data booklet:\n\n- $n \\lambda = d \\sin \\theta$\n\nGiving\n\n- $\\sin \\theta = \\dfrac{n \\lambda}{d} = \\dfrac{(2)(600 \\times 10{^{-9}})}{1 \\times 10{^{-3}}} = 1.2 \\times 10{^{-3}}$\n\n- $\\sin \\theta \\approx \\theta = 1.2 \\times 10{^{-3}}$ radians\n\nAs the small-angle approximation is valid here"},{"ref":"PH0879","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0879\n\nLight is incident on a double slit and a fringe pattern is observed on a screen. Point **A** is equidistant from both slits and point **B** lies in between a dark and light fringe as shown in the diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/PH0879aaaaaa.png\" alt=\"PH0879aaaaaa\">\n\nBelow are three statements about the fringe pattern.\n\n- I. $\\hspace{1em}$ The path difference of light arriving from each of the slits at B is between $0$ and $\\dfrac{\\lambda}{2}$.\n- II. $\\hspace{.7em}$ If only the distance between the double slit and the screen is increased, then the distance between adjacent bright fringes will increase\n- III. $\\hspace{.4em}$ If only the frequency of the light is increased, then the distance between adjacent bright fringes will decrease.\n\nWhich of the statements are correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n**I** is incorrect. At the bright fringe closest to $A$ the path difference is $\\lambda$. Between this bright fringe and the next dark fringe, the path difference varies between $\\lambda$ and $\\dfrac{3\\lambda}{2}$.\n\n**II** is correct. Fringe spacing is given by $s=\\dfrac{\\lambda D}{d}$. $D$ is the distance from the double slit to the screen. $\\lambda$ (wavelength) and $d$ (slit separation) are constant. $s\\ \\alpha\\ D$ so if $D$ increases $s$ increases.\n\n**III** is correct. $v=f\\lambda$ so if frequency increases then wavelength decreases. $s = \\dfrac{\\lambda D}{d}$. $\\lambda\\ \\alpha\\ s$ so if $\\lambda$  decreases then $s$ decreases."},{"ref":"PH0954","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0954\n\nTwo waves interfere at a point resulting in constant zero amplitude of vibration at that location.\n\nBelow are three statements about the waves\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | The two waves are coherent    |\n  | II. | The two waves have the same amplitude |\n  | III. | The two waves have a phase difference of $\\pi$  |\n\nWhich of the statements are correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em} $ I and II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em} $ I and III only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em} $ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em} $ I, II and III","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe principle of superposition states that when two waves meet the resultant displacement is the sum of the displacements of the original waves.\n\nTo have two waves completely cancel out (zero amplitude of vibration) they must\n\nI - have the same frequency and a constant phase difference (coherent) so that the waves are always out of phase at that location.\n\nII - have the same amplitude so that there is always full cancellation\n\nIII - Be $\\pi$ radians out of phase.\n\nThis is represented in the diagram below, which shows the displacements against time that the particles at that location would have due to the different waves. Because the displacements are always exactly out of phase and of the same amplitude at any given time, there will be full cancellation.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/PH0954oo.png\" alt=\"PH0954oo\">"},{"ref":"PH0976","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0976\n\nA microwave has a wavelength of 1 cm when it is traveling through a medium of refractive index 2.0. What is the nature of the wave and the number of oscillations per second?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Nature of the wave$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Number of oscillations per second$\\hspace{1.5em}$  |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| Longitudinal | $1.5\\times10^{10}$ |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Longitudinal | $6.0\\times10^{10}$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Transverse | $1.5\\times10^{10}$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Transverse | $6.0\\times10^{10}$ |","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nA microwave is an electromagnetic wave. All electromagnetic waves are transverse - oscillations are perpendicular to the direction of energy transfer. (Electromagnetic waves are transverse oscillations in the electromagnetic field).\n\nNumber of oscillations per second is frequency.\n\n- $v=f\\lambda$\n\n- $f=\\dfrac{v}{\\lambda}$\n\nSpeed of microwave in a vacuum is the speed of light $=3\\times 10^8\\ \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$.\n\n- $\\dfrac{n_{vacuum}}{n_{medium}}=\\dfrac{v_{medium}}{v_{vacuum}}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{1}{2}=\\dfrac{v_{medium}}{3\\times 10^8}$\n\n- $v_{medium}=\\dfrac{3\\times 10^8}{2}=1.5\\times 10^8$\n\n- $f=\\dfrac{v}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{1.5\\times 10^8}{1\\times 10^{-2}}=1.5\\times 10^{10}$"},{"ref":"PH1792-1","marks":"3","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1792-1\n\n**Maximum mark: 3**\n\nLight of wavelength $580$ nm is passed through two slits and projected onto a screen. The diagram below shows the location of points of maximum intensity.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/Screenshoot%202025-03-21%20at%207.02.25%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshoot 2025-03-21 at 7.02.25 PM\">\n\n1. Explain why these points of maximum intensity occur. [2 marks]\n\n2. Deduce how the distance between adjacent maxima would change if the distance between the slits and the screen were to increase. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. Constructive interference $\\checkmark$\n\n    Path difference$=n\\lambda$ where $n$ is an integer\n\n    **OR**\n\n    Phase difference 0\n\n    **OR**\n\n    Waves are in phase. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    When light passes through the two slits, it diffracts and overlaps, forming an interference pattern. At points of maximum intensity, the waves arriving from each slit are in phase, meaning their peaks and troughs align perfectly. This occurs when the path difference is $n\\lambda$, where $n$ is an integer (0, 1, 2, ...).\n\n2. $\\ll$The distance between adjacent maxima$\\gg$ increases as $s\\propto D$/from $s=\\dfrac{\\lambda D}{d}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    Fringe separation $s$ is given by:\n\n    - $s=\\dfrac{\\lambda D}{d}$\n\n    Where $\\lambda$ is wavelength, $D$ is the distance between the slits and the screen and $d$ is the separation between the slits.\n\n    $d$ and $\\lambda$ are constant so we have:\n\n    - $s\\propto D$\n\n    If $D$ increases $s$ will also increase."},{"ref":"PH1816","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1816\n\nThe diagram below shows the path followed by a light ray travelling between two media of refractive indices $n_1$ and $n_2$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/Screenshoot%202025-03-25%20at%201.58.06%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshoot 2025-03-25 at 1.58.06 PM\">\n\nWhat is the critical angle between the media?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\sin^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n_2}\\right)$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\sin^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n_1}\\right)$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\sin^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{n_1}{n_2}\\right)$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\sin^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{n_2}{n_1}\\right)$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFrom the diagram we see that light travelling from medium $n_1$ to medium $n_2$ bends towards from the normal. Therefore $n_1\\lt n_2$.\n\nThe critical angle occurs when the angle of refraction for light travelling from a slow medium into a faster medium, which in this case is medium $n_2$ to medium $n_1$, is $90\\degree$. Using Snell's law we have:\n\n- $\\dfrac{n_1}{n_2}=\\dfrac{\\sin\\theta_2}{\\sin\\theta_1}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{n_1}{n_2}=\\dfrac{\\sin\\theta_{critical}}{\\sin(90\\degree)}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{n_1}{n_2}=\\textrm{sin}(\\theta_{critical})$\n\n- $\\theta_{critical}=\\sin^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{n_1}{n_2}\\right)$"},{"ref":"PH1865","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1865\n\nMonochromatic light passes through two slits and forms an interference pattern on a screen.\n\nBelow are three changes that can be made to the experimental arrangement:\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | Increasing the distance between the slits and the screen |\n  | II. | Decreasing the frequency of the light |\n  | III. | Decreasing the distance between the slits |\n\nWhich of these changes, if made individually, would cause the fringe spacing to increase?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe fringe spacing $s$ in a double-slit interference pattern is given by:\n\n- $s=\\dfrac{\\lambda D}{d}$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ is the wavelength of the light, $D$ is the distance between the slits and the screen, and $d$ is the slit separation.\n\nNow let's consider each change:\n\n**I. Increasing the distance between the slits and the screen**\n\n$s\\propto D$ so increasing $D$ will cause $s$ to increase\n\n**II. Decreasing the frequency of the light**\n\nThe speed of light is constant, using $v=f\\lambda$ we see that $\\lambda\\propto\\dfrac{1}{f}$, so a decrease in frequency will lead to an increase in the wavelength of the light. Since $s\\propto\\lambda$ this will cause $s$ to increase.\n\n**III. Decreasing the distance between the slits**\n\n$s\\propto\\dfrac{1}{d}$ so a decrease in $d$ will lead to an increase in $s$.\n\nAll three changes I, II, and III will cause the fringe spacing to increase."},{"ref":"PH1866","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1866\n\nTwo loudspeakers emit sound waves with a wavelength of 20 cm. The diagram shows the positions of the two loudspeakers and a point $P$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/Screenshot%202025-09-06%20at%205.44.04%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-09-06 at 5.44.04 PM\">\n\nThe waves leaving both loudspeakers are in antiphase. What is the phase difference between the waves when they reach point $P$, and what type of interference occurs at $P$?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Phase difference / rad$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Type of interference$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| 0 | Constructive |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 0 | Destructive |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\pi$ | Constructive |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\pi$ | Destructive |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe path difference is given by:\n\n- $1.1\\textrm{ m}-1.0\\textrm{ m}=0.1\\textrm{ m}$\n\nExpressing path difference in terms of number of wavelengths, we have:\n\n- path difference $=\\dfrac{0.1}{0.2}=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\lambda$\n\nThe phase difference due to the path difference is given by:\n\n- phase difference $=\\dfrac{2\\pi(\\textrm{path difference})}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{2\\pi(0.1)}{0.2}=\\pi$ rad\n\nThe initial phase difference is $\\pi$ radians so the total phase difference is given by:\n\n- total phase difference $=\\pi+\\pi=2\\pi$\n\nA total phase difference of $2\\pi$ corresponds to waves arriving in phase with no phase difference, so they interfere constructively at point $P$."},{"ref":"PH1867","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1867\n\nBelow shows a wavefront diagram of a ray of monochromatic light travelling between 2 media, $A$ and $B$. Medium $A$ has a refractive index of 2. The spacing of the wavefronts of the wave in the two different media is shown on the diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Refraction%2C%20Diffraction%2C%20Superposition%2C%20and%20Interference/images/Screenshot%202025-09-06%20at%205.59.46%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-09-06 at 5.59.46 PM\">\n\nWhat is the speed of light in medium $B$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{c}{3}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{c}{2}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{2c}{3}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{3c}{4}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe refractive index $n$ of a medium is defined as:\n\n- $n=\\dfrac{c}{v}$\n\nWhere $c$ is the speed of light in a vacuum and $v$ is the speed of light in the medium.\n\nTherefore for medium $A$ we have:\n\n- $2=\\dfrac{c}{v_A}$\n\n- $2v_A=c$\n\n- $v_A=\\dfrac{c}{2}$\n\nWhen passing between media of different refractive indices, the speed and wavelength of light change while the frequency remains constant. The equation $v=f\\lambda$ tells us that $v\\propto\\lambda$. So, we have:\n\n- $\\dfrac{v_A}{v_B}=\\dfrac{\\lambda_A}{\\lambda_B}$\n\nIn a wavefront diagram, the distance between adjacent lines is equal to the wavelength of the light. Therefore, we have:\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\lambda_A}{\\lambda_B}=\\dfrac{5\\lambda_A}{5\\lambda_B}=\\dfrac{3\\,\\mu\\textrm{m}}{2\\,\\mu\\textrm{m}}=\\dfrac{3}{2}$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- $\\begin{aligned}\n\\ \\dfrac{v_A}{v_B}&=\\dfrac{3}{2}\\\\\n\\\\\\\n\\ v_A&=\\dfrac{3}{2}v_B\\\\\n\\\\\\\n\\ v_B&=\\dfrac{2}{3}v_A\\\\\n\\\\\\\n\\ v_B&=\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)\\left(\\dfrac{c}{2}\\right)\\\\\n\\\\\\\n\\ &=\\dfrac{c}{3}\\\\\n\\end{aligned}$"}]},{"name":"Simple Harmonic Motion","questions":[{"ref":"PH0044","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0044\n\nTwo identical objects $P$ and $Q$ are connected to identical springs. Both $P$ and $Q$ are performing simple harmonic motion (SHM) around equilibrium level $O$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0044.png\" alt=\"PH0044\">\n\nThe diagram shows positions of $P$ and $Q$ at time $t = 0$. Which graph shows how kinetic energy of $P$ and $Q$ vary with time $t$?\n\n| | | | | |\n\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---| \n\n| A.  |    |   |   |B.|\n\n| |  <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0044A.png\" alt=\"PH0044A\"> |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0044B.png\" alt=\"PH0044B\">|\n\n| $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n|C. |   |   |  |D. |\n\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0044C.png\" alt=\"PH0044C\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0044D.png\" alt=\"PH0044D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nPotential energy is in its maximum value at boundaries (extreme points), kinetic energy at a maximum value at equilibrium point. So at $t = 0$, for $P$ kinetic energy is a maximum, for $Q$ potential energy is a maximum but kinetic energy is zero. For both masses, the kinetic energy will maximise every half cycle.\n\nAt  $t = \\dfrac{T}{2}$ again for $P$ kinetic energy is a maximum, for $Q$ potential energy is a maximum."},{"ref":"PH0062","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0062\n\nAn object with a mass of $m$ is connected to a spring on a frictionless horizontal surface.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0062.png\" alt=\"PH0062\">\n\nThe object is released from point $A$ which is at a displacement $x$ from its equilibrium point $O$ and executes simple harmonic motion with a period of $T$. Consider the following statements about the situation if the object is released from displacement $2x$.\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n\n  | | |\n\n  | :-- | :-- |\n\n  | I. | The angular frequency of the oscillation will be less. |\n\n  | II. | Total energy of the object will be greater. |\n\n  | III. | The average speed of the object will not change. |\n\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nI. $\\hspace{1em}$ Incorrect. $\\omega = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{T}$, and period is found from $T=2\\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{m}{k}}$. This equation shows that period is independent of amplitude $x_0$\n\nII. $\\hspace{1em}$ Correct. $E_{Total} = E_{H{max}} = \\dfrac{1}{2} kx^2$. Therefore energy has increased due to the increase in amplitude.\n\nIII. $\\hspace{1em}$ Incorrect. Average speed = $\\dfrac{4x_0}{T}$. The period $T$ does not change but average speed increases due to the increase in the amplitude."},{"ref":"PH0099","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0099\n\nThe graph shows the variation with time $t$ of the displacement $s$ of an object suspended by a spring. At what time is the acceleration in the negative direction at a maximum?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0099aaaaa.png\" alt=\"PH0099aaaaa\">\n\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 4.0 s\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 3.0 s\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2.0 s\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1.0 s","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nRecall for simple harmonic motion, $a \\propto -x$, therefore the object experiences maximum negative acceleration at maximum positive displacement from equilibrium."},{"ref":"PH0113","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"?","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0113\n\nBelow are the graphs of the kinetic and potential energies against displacement $s$ for an object exhibiting simple harmonic motion.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0113.JPEG\" alt=\"PH0113\">\n\nWhich of the positions **marked on the axis** represents the location of maximum speed?","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe object is going fastest through the equilibrium position. At this location, all of the energy of the system is kinetic energy. There is zero elastic potential energy at this point as there is no displacement in the spring."},{"ref":"PH0115","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0115\n\nIn an experiment to determine the acceleration due to gravity, a student sets up a simple pendulum and varies the length of the string by a fixed amount and records the time for 15 oscillations. \n\nInitially, the pendulum starts with a period of 1.0 s. The length is then increased by 16%. What will be the approximate percentage increase in the period of the pendulum for the new length?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.06%\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 4%\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 8%\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 32%","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nFrom $T=2\\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{L}{g}}$\n\nThe rules for propagation of uncertainties can be applied in this situation.\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{T}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{\\Delta L}{L}$\n\nHence $\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{T}=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\times 16\\%=8\\%$"},{"ref":"PH0163","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0163\n\nA long pendulum executing simple harmonic motion is released from one end and reaches equilibrium position in 2 s.  Just after the equilibrium position, the length of the pendulum is reduced to one-fourth of its initial value because of wrapping around a nail. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/Screenshoot%202026-04-08%20at%207.54.05%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshoot 2026-04-08 at 7.54.05 AM\">\n\nWhat is the period of the pendulum in this situation?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 8 s\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 6 s\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 4 s\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2 s","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe period of a simple pendulum can be found by the following equation; \n\n- $T=2\\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{l}{g}}$\n\nTherefore the period is directly proportional to the square root of the length.\n\n- $T \\propto \\sqrt{l}$\n\nThe time needed to reach from one end of the oscillation to the equilibrium position is one-fourth of the period. Therefore, the period of the pendulum before its length is reduced is; \n\n- T = 8 s\n\nJust half of the motion will be completed with the initial length. So the time for reaching the equilibrium position and returning from the equilibrium position to starting position is 4 s. \n\nWhen the length is reduced to one-fourth of the initial value; \n\n- $T_{new} \\propto \\sqrt{\\dfrac{l}{4}} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{2} \\sqrt{l}= \\dfrac{1}{2} T$\n\nSo\n\n- $T_{new} = 4$ s\n\nBut just half of the motion will be covered with the new length. Therefore time needed to complete the other half of the oscillation is 2 s.\n\nSo, the total time to complete one oscillation-period of the oscillation- is 6 s."},{"ref":"PH0167","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0167\n\nAn object executes simple harmonic motion with an amplitude of $x_0$. Which graph shows the relationship between the kinetic energy $E_k$ and the displacement $x$ from the equilibrium position of the object?\n\n| | | | | |\n\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n\n| A.  |    |  $\\hspace{1cm}$|  B. ||\n\n| |  <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/Screenshot%202025-05-08%20at%203.35.27%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-08 at 3.35.27 PM\"> |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/Screenshot%202025-05-08%20at%203.35.33%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-08 at 3.35.33 PM\">\n\n|$\\hspace{1cm}$\n\n|C. |   |   | D. | |\n\n|  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/Screenshot%202025-05-08%20at%203.35.40%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-08 at 3.35.40 PM\"> |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/Screenshot%202025-05-08%20at%203.35.54%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-08 at 3.35.54 PM\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe kinetic energy of the object during simple harmonic motion; \n\n- $E_k=\\frac{1}{2} mv^2$         \n\nWe know that for a simple harmonic oscillator, the speed and therefore the kinetic energy is a maximum at the equilibrium position, and zero at the maximum displacement. This rules out option C.\n\nThe total energy of an oscillator is comprised of the sum of the elastic potential energy and the kinetic energy.\n\nUsing a spring for reference, we know that the potential energy stored in a spring is given by the equation\n\n- $E_H=\\dfrac{1}{2}kx^2$\n\nThis will be quadratic in shape. Subtracting this shape from constant total energy will give a graph that resembles half of a parabola opening downwards.  This corresponds to answer A.\n\nAlternatively, we can substitute \n\n- $v=\\omega \\sqrt{x_0^2-x^2}$ \n\ninto the kinetic energy equation to get \n\n- $E_k=\\frac{1}{2} m\\omega^2 (x_0^2-x^2)$  \n\nAccording to this expression, the kinetic energy is maximum at $x=0$ and zero at $x=x_0$. Additionally, it decreases at an increasing rate with the square of displacement. Therefore, the answer is A."},{"ref":"PH0168","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0168\n\nA particle undergoes simple harmonic motion. Which of the following can be an expression of acceleration $a$ in terms of displacement $x$ and a constant value $k$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $a = k^2 x$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $a = -k^2 x$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $a = -k x^2$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $a = k x^2$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nTwo conditions must be satisfied to define motion as simple harmonic motion; \n\n- The acceleration is proportional to the displacement.\n\n- The acceleration and displacement are in opposite directions. \n\nThe second condition indicates the existence of a negative sign in front of the displacement and the first condition demands to have $x$ instead of $x^2$ in the expression. These two conditions are satisfied in B."},{"ref":"PH0170","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0170\n\nA mass-spring system executes simple harmonic motion with a period $T$ in a vertical plane. What is the period when the oscillating mass is doubled and the system is brought to a planet where the gravitational field strength at the surface is halved?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$     $2T$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$     $\\sqrt2T$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$     $T$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$     $\\dfrac{T}{2}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nPeriod of a mass-spring system;\n\n$T=2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{m}{k}} $    where $m$ is mass and $k$ is the spring constant.\n\nFor the new condition;\n\nMass is $2m$ and the spring constant does not change. Additionally, the gravitational field strength does not affect the period.\n\nBy substituting new values in the equation;\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\nT_{new}&=2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{2m}{k}}\\\\\n&=(\\sqrt2)(2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{m}{k}})\\\\\n&=\\sqrt2T\\\\\n\\end{aligned}$"},{"ref":"PH0171","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0171\n\nA mass-spring system rests on a frictionless surface. The mass is pulled to the right and released from point $X$ at $t=0$ $\\textrm{s}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0171.png\" alt=\"PH0171\">\n\nThe mass executes simple harmonic motion between the points $X$ and $Y$. The mass reaches point Y for the first time at  $t=6$ $\\textrm{s}$. Which of the following correctly shows directions of velocity and acceleration of the mass at $t=10$ $\\textrm{s}$?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Velocity| Acceleration |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| → | → |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | → | ← |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | ← | → |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | ← | ← |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nIt is important to locate the mass when $t = 10$ $\\textrm{s}$ for identifying directions of velocity and acceleration. The time needed for mass to move from one end to the other end is $6$ $\\textrm{s}$. The motion of the mass is identical for both sides of the equilibrium position. Therefore, the time needed to cover from one end to equilibrium is $3$ $\\textrm{s}$. When mass moves for 10 seconds, it passes the equilibrium position for the second time towards point $X$ and is located between the equilibrium point and point $X$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0171-1.png\" alt=\"PH0171-1\">\n\nThe direction of velocity is towards $X$ (right). The retarding force is always towards equilibrium. Therefore, the acceleration is also towards equilibrium (left)."},{"ref":"PH0172","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0172\n\nThe graph below shows the variation with displacement $x$ of the acceleration $a$ from the equilibrium position of a particle that undergoes simple harmonic motion.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0172.png\" alt=\"PH0172\">\n\nThe magnitude of the slope of the graph is directly proportional to\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ The square of the oscillation frequency\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ The oscillation frequency\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ The square of the oscillation period\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ The oscillation period","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe slope of the graph is\n\nslope = $\\frac{a}{x}$\n\nBy using the relationship between acceleration and displacement;\n\n$a=ω^2x$ where $ω=2πf$\n\n$a=4\\pi^2f^2x$\n\n$\\dfrac{a}{x}=4\\pi^2f^2$    ,    where $4\\pi^2$ is constant\n\ntherefore $\\dfrac{a}{x} ∝ f^2$"},{"ref":"PH0173","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0173\n\nA simple pendulum undergoes simple harmonic motion. The graph shows the variation of a quantity $y$ with horizontal displacement $x$ from the equilibrium position.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/Screenshot%202025-04-02%20at%2011.00.45%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-02 at 11.00.45 AM\">\n\n Which of the following quantities could be represented by quantity $y$?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | The velocity of the pendulum |\n  | II. | The kinetic energy of the pendulum |\n  | III. | The potential energy of the pendulum |\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe y axis cannot represent the velocity of the pendulum, as a velocity-position graph would also show negative velocities to represent motion in both directions. In addition, the velocity-displacement graph for an object undergoing simple harmonic motion is elliptical in shape, and this graph is parabolic.\n\nUsing the formula for a spring (which is an example of a simple harmonic oscillator), we know that potential energy is equal to $\\dfrac{1}{2}kx^2$. A similar quadratic relationship between potential energy and displacement is true for a pendulum, so the potential energy will be a parabola, opening upwards.\n\nTotal energy must be constant, so the kinetic energy vs. position graph will be given by the total energy minus the potential energy, which will be a parabola opening downwards, which is what we see in the graph.\n\nUsing a Higher Level formula, the formula for kinetic energy is given by $\\frac{1}{2}m\\omega^2(x_0^2-x^2)$, which would be parabolic in shape, opening downwards as is shown in the graph.\n\nThe potential energy of the bob should be less at the equilibrium position than in the positions where displacement is maximum."},{"ref":"PH0222","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0222\n\nFor a body executing simple harmonic motion, which statement is **not** possible? \n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Acceleration and velocity are in the opposite direction \n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Acceleration and velocity are in the same direction\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ Resultant force and velocity are in the same direction\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ Resultant force and acceleration are in the opposite direction","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nFrom Newton's Second Law\n\n- $F_{net}=ma$\n\nAcceleration is proportional to the resultant force and will also be in the same direction. Therefore, it is not possible for the net force and the acceleration to be in opposite directions.\n\nIn simple harmonic motion, it is the acceleration and the displacement from the equilibrium position that are in opposite directions."},{"ref":"PH0223","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0223\n\nA body performs simple harmonic motion (shm).  Which of the following could be the speed against the time graph for one period of motion?\n\n$\\\\$ \n\n| | | | | |\n\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---| \n\n| A.  |    |   |   |B.|\n\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0223aaaa.png\" alt=\"PH0223aaaa\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0223bbb.png\" alt=\"PH0223bbb\">|\n\n|C. |   |   |  |D. |\n\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0223ccc.png\" alt=\"PH0223ccc\"> |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0223ddd.png\" alt=\"PH0223ddd\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nSpeed is always positive and it should have maximum and minimum values twice in one complete oscillation. Therefore the answer is C."},{"ref":"PH0268","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0268\n\nA mass $M$ hangs in equilibrium on a spring. The mass is pulled down 10 cm below the equilibrium position and released. It oscillates vertically about the equilibrium position performing simple harmonic motion. \n\nIf it takes 0.5 $\\ce{s}$ after release to return to its equilibrium position, which of the following is true for its first oscillation?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1.5em}$ Amplitude (cm) $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$ Frequency (Hz) $\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ A. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 10 | 2 |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ B. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 20 | 0.5 |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ C. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 10 | 0.5 |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ D. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 20 | 2 |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nSince it is pulled down by 10 cm, that is the maximum displacement and hence the amplitude.\n\nIf it takes 0.5 s to return to the equilibrium position, then the complete oscillation is $(0.5)(4) =2$ s. \n\nSince $f=\\dfrac{1}{T}$ and $T= 2$ s frequency is 0.5 Hz."},{"ref":"PH0294","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0294\n\nA particle performs simple harmonic motion with a frequency of 0.05 Hz. The distance traveled by the particle in one complete oscillation is 16 cm. Which of the following graphs shows a correct variation of its position?\n\n| | | | | |\n\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---| \n\n| A.  |    |   |  B. ||\n\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0294A.png\" alt=\"PH0294A\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0294B.png\" alt=\"PH0294B\">|\n\n|C. |   |   |  D.| |\n\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0294C.png\" alt=\"PH0294C\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0294D.png\" alt=\"PH0294D\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nSince frequency is 0.05 Hz, $T= \\dfrac{1}{f} = \\dfrac{1}{0.05} =$ 20 s \n\nThe amplitude is 4 cm on either side of the equilibrium position because in one full cycle it travels four amplitudes $\\bigg(\\dfrac{16}{4} = 4 \\bigg)$."},{"ref":"PH0482","marks":"11","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0482\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\nA  2.0 kg object is attached to a spring and vibrates vertically.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0482.png\" alt=\"PH0482\">\n\n[Figure 1]\n\nThe graph below shows the variation of the acceleration of the object with the displacement $x$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0482-1.png\" alt=\"PH0482-1\">\n\n$\\small{\\textrm{[created with Geogebra, https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\n1. \n\n    1. State and explain whether or not the oscillation of the mass is simple harmonic motion. [3 marks]\n\n    2. On Figure 1 above draw an arrow to show the direction of the acceleration of the mass which is displaced by $x$. [1 mark]\n\n2. \n\n    1. Show that the slope of the graph equals to $\\dfrac{-k}{m}$, where $k$ is the spring constant. [3 marks]\n\n    2. Use the graph to determine the spring constant, $k$, of the spring. [2 marks]\n\n3. For the same spring-mass system, show that the acceleration of the object is $-10\\,\\ce{cm}\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}$ when the mass is displaced by 0.12 cm [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. \n\n    1. It is doing SHM; $\\checkmark$\n\n        The graph shows the relationship $a \\propto -x$, acceleration is proportional to negative  displacement $\\checkmark$ \n\n        Graph is a straight line through the origin with a negative slope. $\\checkmark$\n\n    2. An arrow directing upward. $\\checkmark$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0482-2.png\" alt=\"PH0482-2\">\n\n2. \n\n    1.  $\\ll$Hooke's law $\\gg$ $F=-kx$ \n\n        **AND**\n\n        $\\ll$Newton's second law  $\\gg$ $F=ma$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $-kx=ma$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        slope of the graph = $\\dfrac{a}{x}=-\\dfrac{k}{m}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    2. Any data on the line chosen to calculate $k$ where,  \n\n        $k= -\\dfrac{ma}{x}$ where mass, $m = 2\\,\\ce{kg}$\n\n        For example \n\n        $a = 5\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}$ where $x = -0.06\\,\\ce{m}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $k$ = :answer[$167 \\ll N\\,m^{-1},\\,\\ce{kg}\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}\\gg$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        (accept from 165 to 170 if slope is calculated in an appropriate way)\n\n3. Using the spring constant from b(ii) and converting its units to N cm$^{-1}$\n\n    $\\begin{aligned}\n\n    -kx &= ma\\\\\n\n    -(1.66)(0.12) &= (2) a\\, \\checkmark\\\\\n\n    a&= -0.10\\ \\textrm{m s}^{-2} \\approx - 10\\,\\ce{cm}\\,\\ce{s}^{-2} \\,\\checkmark\\\\\n\n    \\end{aligned}$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanaation}}$\n\n    Our spring constant of 167 N m$^{-1}$ can be converted to 1.67 N cm$^{-1}$.\n\n    The force from the spring $kx$ is therefore\n\n    - $(1.66)(.12)=0.20\\ $N\n\n    We can now find acceleration\n\n    - $a=\\dfrac{F}{m}$ =$\\dfrac{0.20}{2}$= 0.10 m s$^{-2} =$ 10 cm s$^{-2}$\n\n    ."},{"ref":"PH0657","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0657\n\nA pendulum is released from rest from the position shown, where displacement to the right is considered to be the positive direction.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0657-2.png\" alt=\"PH0657-2\">\n\nWhich graph shows the variation of acceleration with respect to time?\\\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n|| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">  |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">|\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nIn the diagram shown, the release point is at the maximum displacement in the positive $x$ direction. The acceleration is proportional to but in the opposite direction to the displacement, therefore the acceleration-time graph should start with a maximum negative acceleration. Only graph D shows this."},{"ref":"PH0659","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0659\n\nA system experiences simple harmonic motion when a restoring force acts on an object displaced from an equilibrium position. Which of the following is $\\textbf{NOT}$ true for simple harmonic motion?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $The restoring force is proportional to the displacement from equilibrium.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em} $The kinetic energy of the system is maximum at maximum displacement.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em} $The maximum speed is proportional to the maximum displacement from equilibrium.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em} $The restoring force is always in the opposite direction to the displacement from equilibrium.","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n- A: $\\hspace{1em}  $This is the standard defining feature of SHM i.e.  $a=-\\omega^2x$.\n\n- C: $\\hspace{1em} $ maximum kinetic energy is equal to the maximum potential energy. Therefore\n\n- $\\hspace{6em} \\dfrac{1}{2}m(v_o)^2=\\dfrac{1}{2}k(x_o)^2$\n\n- $\\hspace{2.4em}$ and so $(v_o)^2 \\propto(x_o)^2$ and $v_o \\propto x_o$\n\n- $\\hspace{2.4em}$where $x_0$ is the maximum displacement, and $v_o$ is the maximum speed.\n\n- D: $\\hspace{1em} $This is one of the principal tenets of SHM. The restoring force always acts to bring an object back to the equilibrium position.\n\n- B: $\\hspace{1em} $The kinetic energy is always maximum at zero displacement from equilibrium, it is the POTENTIAL energy that is maximum at maximum displacement. This is incorrect, so B is the answer to select here."},{"ref":"PH0664","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"?","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0664\n\nConsider the following statements about the motion of a simple harmonic oscillator:\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | The amplitude of oscillation is always equal to the maximum displacement from equilibrium.   |\n  | II. | The period of oscillation always depends on the mass of the oscillator. |\n  | III. | The speed of the oscillator is maximum at the equilibrium position. |\n\nWhich of the statements are true?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nI: In principle, this is true.\n\nII: False, the period of oscillation can also depend on other factors, dependent on the nature of the oscillation (for example, for a pendulum the mass  is irrelevant).\n\nIII: This is true as per equation $v=\\pm \\omega(x_0^2-x^2)$, $v$ is greatest when the terms in the bracket produce the biggest difference, i.e. when $x$ is at a maximum.\n\nTherefore, B is the correct answer."},{"ref":"PH0730","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0730\n\nThe diagram shows a mass tied to a piece of string which is attached to the ceiling. The mass acts as a simple pendulum. It is released from position **A** and moves through its equilibrium position to position **B**.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0730.png\" alt=\"PH0730\">\n\nWhich graph shows a **possible** variation of acceleration $a$ with displacement $x$?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  |   |B.|\n||<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0730aaa.png\" alt=\"PH0730aaa\">   |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |  <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0730bb.png\" alt=\"PH0730bb\">|\n|$\\hspace{1cm}$\n|C. |   |   |  |D. |\n|  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0730ccc.png\" alt=\"PH0730ccc\">  |  |  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0730dd.png\" alt=\"PH0730dd\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFrom the databooklet:\\\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $a=\\ -\\omega^2x$\n\nThe acceleration is directly proportional to $x$, so it must be a straight-line graph passing through the origin. In addition, the gradient must be negative due to the acceleration and the displacement being in opposite directions, represented by the negative sign in the defining equation.\n\nHence the gradient is:\\\n $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{a}{x}=-\\omega^2$"},{"ref":"PH0732","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0732\n\nA mass ($M$) is attached to a spring of constant ($k$) and is oscillating in simple harmonic motion across a frictionless surface.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0732.jpg\" alt=\"PH0732\">\n\nWhich of the following, when done together, will reduce the time period ($T$) of oscillation?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Mass ($M$) | Spring constant ($k$) |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$|$\\hspace{1.5em}$ Decrease by $50\\%$ $\\hspace{1.5em}$| Decrease by $50\\%$|\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Increase by $50\\%$| Increase by $50\\%$|\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Increase by $50\\%$| Decrease by $50\\%$|\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Decrease by $50\\%$| Increase by $50\\%$|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFrom the databooklet:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $T=\\ 2\\pi\\ \\sqrt{\\dfrac{M}{k}}$\n\nIf BOTH $M$ and $k$ are reduced or increased by 50%, the time period remains the same, therefore answers A and B are incorrect.\n\nIncreasing the mass will increase $T$ and if $k$ is decreased, that will also have the effect of increasing $T$. Hence, answer C is incorrect.\n\nIf the mass is reduced by $50\\%$ and the spring constant ($k$) is also increased by $50\\%$ as in response **D**, then:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $T=\\ 2\\pi\\ \\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.5M}{1.5k}}$ will result in a reduction of the time period ($T$)"},{"ref":"PH0733","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0733\n\nJenny is standing on a rotating roundabout and the sun is casting her shadow onto a nearby wall. Jenny is in the position on the diagram when the roundabout starts rotating. The motion of her shadow approximates to simple harmonic.\n\nWhat is the most likely position of her shadow after the turntable has moved through an angle of $35.5 \\pi$?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0733a.png\" alt=\"PH0733a\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nFrom the databooklet:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $1\\  \\ce{radian} \\left(\\ce{rad}\\right)=\\dfrac{180\\degree}{pi}\\ $\n\nEvery $2\\ \\pi$ radians she returns to the same position, so $\\dfrac{35.5\\ \\pi}{2\\ \\pi}=17.75$ rotations\n\nJenny completes 17 full circles, then another 0.75 of a rotation.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ $0.75\\ \\times \\left(360\\degree\\right)=270\\degree$\n\nTherefore, Jenny moves an extra $270\\degree$ from her starting position after 17 rotations, so her shadow will be at position **B**, and her actual position will be $270\\degree$ counter-clockwise from her starting position."},{"ref":"PH0737","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0737\n\nAn object is oscillating in simple harmonic motion with a time period ($T$) of 1 second.\n\nWhich of the following graphs best represents the kinetic energy ($E_k$) and potential energy ($E_p$) of the motion?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  |   |B.|\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0737a.jpg\" alt=\"PH0737a\"> |   |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0737b.jpg\" alt=\"PH0737b\">|\n|$\\hspace{1cm}$\n|C. |   |   |  |D. |\n| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0737c.jpg\" alt=\"PH0737c\"> |   |  |  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH0737d.jpg\" alt=\"PH0737d\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe sum of the values of the potential energy ($E_p$) and the kinetic energy ($E_k$) is always equal to the maximum energy.\n\nIn answers **B** and **C** the sum of the values of the potential energy ($E_p$) and the kinetic energy ($E_k$) at any point in time does not always equal the maximum energy value.\n\nIn answers **A** and **D** the kinetic energy ($E_k$) starts at zero and the potential energy ($E_p$) starts at a maximum value, and throughout the motion the sum of the 2 values at any point in time equals the maximum, so only answer **A** or **D** can be correct.\n\nThe time period ($T$) is given to be 1 second. In that time period the object will pass through the equilibrium position twice, so the kinetic energy must reach a maximum value twice, and the potential energy will have 3 peak values.\n\nAnswer **A** shows the correct energy of motion for the potential energy ($E_p$) and the kinetic energy ($E_k$)."},{"ref":"PH0781","marks":"10","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0781\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\n1. A simple pendulum of length of $1.2\\,\\ce{m}$ oscillates at very small angles.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n    Air resistance can be ignored. The acceleration a of the pendulum is given as\n\n    $a=-\\dfrac{g}{l} x$\n\n    where $g$ is gravity, $l$ is the length of the pendulum and $x$ is the horizontal displacement of the bob from the mean position.\n\n    Deduce that the bob executes simple harmonic motion. [2 marks]\n\n2. Using the relationship between $a$ and $x$ given above, show that  the period $T$ of the oscillation of the simple pendulum can be found by  $T=2\\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{l}{g}}$. [2 marks]\n\n3. The graph below shows the variation of the horizontal displacement $s$ of the bob with time $t$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/Screenshot%202025-12-11%20at%2010.31.52%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-12-11 at 10.31.52 AM\">\n\n    On the graph, mark:\n\n    1. Point A where the acceleration is maximum and negative. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Point B where the velocity is maximum and positive. [1 mark]\n\n4. Determine the frequency of the simple pendulum under the conditions where its length is doubled, and it is relocated to a location with a gravitational acceleration of $9.7\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}$. [2 marks]\n\n5. Explain why the motion of a simple pendulum can not be regarded as simple harmonic motion when the air resistance is not negligible. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. $g$ and $l$ are constant so acceleration/$a$ is proportional to displacement/$x$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    negative sign indicates acceleration and displacement are in opposite directions $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    To classify a harmonic motion as a simple harmonic, the acceleration of the object must be directly proportional to the displacement of the object from the mean position but in opposite directions. Given that $\\dfrac{g}{l}$ is constant, the following relationship can be inferred:\n\n    - $a\\propto -x$\n\n    This relationship shows that acceleration is directly proportional to displacement but they are in opposite directions (due to the negative sign).\n\n2. Identification of $\\omega =\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g}{l}}$  or ${\\omega}^2=\\dfrac{g}{l}$       $\\checkmark$\n\n    Using $\\omega=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{T}$   $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    Acceleration a of the oscillation:\n\n    - $a=-{\\omega}^2 x=-\\dfrac{g}{l} x$\n\n    By cancelling out $x$ on both sides:\n\n    - ${\\omega}^2=\\dfrac{g}{l}$\n\n    - $\\omega = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{g}{l}}$\n\n    By substituting $\\omega=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{T}$ into the equation:\n\n    - $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{T}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g}{l}}$\n\n    - $T=2\\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{l}{g}}$\n\n3. 1. Correct position for point A $\\checkmark$\n\n      <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/Screenshot%202025-12-11%20at%2010.36.30%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-12-11 at 10.36.30 AM\">\n\n      $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    Since acceleration is directly proportional to displacement and in the opposite direction, the point of maximum negative acceleration should coincide with the position of maximum positive displacement.\n\n    2. Correct position for point B  $\\checkmark$\n\n       <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/Screenshot%202025-12-11%20at%2010.37.20%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-12-11 at 10.37.20 AM\">\n\n       $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n     The slope of the displacement-time graph indicates velocity. Hence, points B should correspond to locations with the steepest positive slopes.\n\n4. ::indent\n    - $\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{9.7}{2.4}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - = $0.32\\,\\ce{Hz}$  $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    The frequency $f$ of the simple pendulum:\n\n    - $f=\\dfrac{1}{T}=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{g}{l}}$\n\n    - $f=\\dfrac{1}{T}=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{9.7}{2.4}}=0.32\\, \\ce{Hz}$\n\n5. Air resistance is always in the opposite direction to the motion / Air resistance increases with the speed $\\checkmark$\n\n    The resultant force is no longer directly proportional with displacement / opposite direction to the motion $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    Air resistance (drag) consistently opposes the motion's direction, contradicting the principle that net force should be in the opposite direction to displacement. Additionally, air resistance increases with the square of the speed, violating the direct relationship between forces and displacement."},{"ref":"PH0939","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0939\n\nA pendulum undergoes simple harmonic motion. The graph below shows how acceleration $a$ varies with time $t$ for the pendulum.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\nWhich of the following correctly shows how the displacement of the pendulum from its equilibrium position $x$ varies with time $t$?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n|| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">  |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">|\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">|","markScheme":"**答案：A**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n- $a=-\\omega^2x$\n\nGraph of $x$ will have same shape as graph of $a$ but will be negative and scaled parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nThe maximum amplitude and displacement are related in the following way\n\n- $a_{max}=\\omega^2 x_{max}$\n\n- $x_{max}=\\dfrac{a_{max}}{\\omega^2}=\\dfrac{a_{max}}{(\\frac{2\\pi}{T})^2}=\\dfrac{a_{max}T^2 }{4\\pi^2}$"},{"ref":"PH0953","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0953\n\nPendulum A has a length $l$ and a bob of mass $m$. Pendulum B has a length $\\dfrac{l}{2}$ and a bob of mass $\\dfrac{m}{2}$.\n\nWhat is the ratio\n\n- $\\dfrac {\\text{Frequency of oscillations of pendulum A}}{\\text{Frequency of oscillations of pendulum B}}$ ?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em} $ $\\dfrac{1}{2}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em} $ $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em} $ 1\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em} $ $\\sqrt 2$","markScheme":"**答案：B**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n- $T = 2 \\pi \\sqrt {\\dfrac{l}{g}} \\hspace{1em}$ and $\\hspace{1em} f = \\dfrac{1}{T}$ so we may write\n\n- $f = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{l}{g}}} = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g}{l}}$\n\n$g$ is constant therefore\n\n- $f$ $\\alpha$ $\\sqrt \\dfrac{1}{l}$\n\n(Note: frequency is independent of mass)\n\n- $\\dfrac{f}{\\sqrt\\dfrac{1}{l}}$ = constant\n\n-  $f\\sqrt l$ = constant\n\n- $f_{A}\\sqrt l_{A} = f_{B}\\sqrt l_{B}$\n\n- $\\dfrac {f_{A}}{f_{B}} = \\dfrac {\\sqrt l_{B}}{\\sqrt l_{A}} = \\dfrac {\\sqrt \\dfrac {l_A}{2}}{l_A} = \\sqrt \\dfrac {l_A}{2l_A} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}$"},{"ref":"PH0988","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0988\n\nA mass is placed on the end of a spring of natural length 5 cm causing it to oscillate in simple harmonic motion with an angular frequency $\\omega$. The length of the spring when the speed of the mass is maximum is 15 cm\n\nWhat is $\\omega$? Assume $g=10\\,\\ce{N}\\,\\ce{kg}^{-1}$.\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\pi \\, \\text{rad} \\, \\ce{s}^{-1}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $5 \\, \\text{rad} \\, \\ce{s}^{-1}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2\\pi \\, \\text{rad} \\, \\ce{s}^{-1}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $10 \\, \\text{rad} \\, \\ce{s}^{-1}$","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n$\\omega = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{T}$ so we must determine $T$\n\n- $T=2\\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{m}{k}}$\n\nWe have not been given $m$ or $k$ but we know for a mass on a spring.\n\n- $F=mg=kx$\n\nWhere $x$ is the extension of the spring in its equilibrium position when the mass is hanging and at rest.\n\n- $\\dfrac{m}{k}=\\dfrac{x}{g}$\n\n- $T=2\\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{x}{g}}$ (note the similarity between this and the equation for the time period of a pendulum)\n\nThe spring is in its equilibrium position when the speed is maximum so the extension $x=\\text{length in equilibrium}-\\text{natural length}= 15\\, \\ce{cm}-5\\,\\ce{cm}=10\\,\\ce{cm}=0.1\\,\\ce{m}$.\n\n- $T=2\\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{x}{g}}=2\\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.1}{10}}$\n\n- $=2\\pi\\sqrt{0.01}$\n\n- $=2\\pi (0.1)=0.2\\pi$\n\n- $\\omega = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{T}=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{0.2\\pi}=10\\,\\text{rad}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH1102","marks":"12","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1102\n\n**Maximum mark: 12**\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH01102.JPEG\" alt=\"PH01102\">\n\nA wrecking ball is being used to demolish an old building $\\text{\\textendash}$ the ball is a heavy steel mass suspended from a crane.  When the mass is released from a small angle and allowed to swing freely, simple harmonic motion is observed.\n\n1. Define simple harmonic motion. [2 marks]\n\n2. 1. The length of the cable used to suspend the wrecking ball is $12.0\\,\\ce{m}$.  Show that the period of one whole swing (forwards and backwards) would be approximately $7\\,\\ce{s}$. [1 mark]\n\n    2. As shown in the diagram, the wrecking ball was pulled back $2.50\\,\\ce{m}$.  Determine the magnitude of the acceleration of the wrecking ball just after release. [3 marks]\n\n3. 1. Use conservation of energy to determine the maximum possible speed of the wrecking ball. [2 marks]\n\n    2. On the axes provided, sketch a graph of how the gravitational potential energy $E_p$ varies with time $t$ for one period, starting from when the wrecking ball is released. Assume friction is negligible. The maximum energy $E_{p (max)}$ and time period $T$ have been marked on the graph. [2 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/Screenshot%202025-03-04%20at%2011.16.20%E2%80%AFAM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-03-04 at 11.16.20 AM\">\n\n    3. Describe how friction would affect the amplitude and period of the swing of the wrecking ball. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Motion where:\n\n    The restoring force/acceleration is $\\ll$directly$\\gg$ $\\underline{\\textrm{proportional}}$ to the displacement from the equilibrium position;  $\\checkmark$\\\n    The restoring force/acceleration is in the $\\underline{\\textrm{opposite}}$ direction to the displacement towards the equilibrium position;  $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    Simple harmonic motion (SHM) is periodic motion where the restoring force is proportional to the displacement from the equilibrium position.  Since the restoring force is also proportional to the acceleration (Newton’s Second Law), it follows that the acceleration is also proportional to the displacement.  Furthermore, because the force is acting to restore equilibrium, it is in the opposite direction to the displacement from equilibrium.\n\n    In equation form, SHM is represented as:\n\n    - $a = -{\\omega}^2x$\n\n    Where,\n\n    - $a$ = linear acceleration ($\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}$)\n    - $\\omega$ = angular velocity (rad$\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$)\n    - $x$ = linear displacement (m)\n\n    The restoring force/acceleration is zero at the equilibrium, and is maximum when the displacement is at the maximum amplitude of the swing.\n\n    For a pendulum, proportionality only applies for small displacement angles where $\\sin{\\theta}$ is approximately equal to $\\theta$ radian (small angle approximation, up to $15\\degree$).\n\n2. 1. $T=2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{12.0}{9.81}}$;  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $T = 6.95\\,\\ce{s}$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        The wrecking ball is acting as a pendulum.\n\n        The period of oscillation of a pendulum is given by,\n\n        - $T=2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{L}{g}}$\n\n        Substituting in values gives,\n\n        - $T=2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{12.0}{9.81}}$\n\n        - $T=6.95\\, \\ce{s}$\n\n        Note that the period of a pendulum is independent of mass.\n\n        At least 2 significant figures must be shown on the calculated answer.\n\n    2. $\\omega=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{6.95}$\n\n        OR\n\n        $\\omega=0.904\\,\\textrm{rad}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$;  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $a=-{\\omega}^2x$;  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $a=-(0.904)^2(2.50)$\n\n        $a=(-)2.04\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}$;  $\\checkmark$\n\n        Note that the negative sign is not required.\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        Angular velocity ($\\omega$) is given by,\n\n        - $\\omega=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{T}$\n\n        Substituting in values gives,\n\n        - $\\omega=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{6.95}$\n\n        - $\\omega=0.904\\,\\textrm{rad}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$\n\n        Simple harmonic motion is represented as,\n\n        - $a=-{\\omega}^2x$\n\n        Substituting in values gives,\n\n        - $a=(-)(0.904)^2(2.50)$\n\n        - $a=(-)2.04\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-2}$\n\n        The negative sign relates to the direction of the acceleration opposing the direction of the displacement, $x$.\n\n        Although a centripetal acceleration will occur when the ball is in motion, at the point of release the the speed is zero and so there will be no centripetal acceleration at that instant.\n\n3. 1. $\\Delta h=L-L'$\n\n        $\\ll\\Delta h=12.0-\\sqrt{12^2-2.5^2}\\gg$\n\n        $\\Delta h=0.263\\,\\ce{m}$;  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\Delta E_k= -\\Delta E_p$ (Conservation of Energy)\n\n        $\\dfrac{1}{2}mv_{max}^2= mg\\Delta h$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $v_{\\text{max}} = \\sqrt{2g\\Delta h}$\n\n        $\\ll v_{max} = \\sqrt{2\\times9.81\\times0.263}\\gg$\n\n        $v_{max} = 2.27\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$;  $\\checkmark$\n\n        Accept solution using $v_{max} = A\\omega$.\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH1102cims.png\" alt=\"PH1102cims\">\n\n        With reference to the diagram, use Pythagoras to determine $L'$,\n\n        - $L'= \\sqrt{12^2-2.5^2}$\n\n        - $L'=11.7\\, \\ce{m}$\n\n        Then find $\\Delta h$,\n\n        - $\\Delta h=L-L'$\n\n        - $\\Delta h=12.0-11.7$\n\n        - $\\Delta h=0.263\\, \\ce{m}$\n\n        Maximum speed is when wrecking ball at the equilibrium point.\n\n        Conservation of Energy says,\n\n        - $\\Delta E_k= -\\Delta E_p$ (Conservation of Energy)\n\n        OR\n\n        - $\\dfrac{1}{2}mv_{max}^2= mg\\Delta $h\n\n        Rearranging and substituting in values gives,\n\n        - $v_{max}= 2g\\Delta h$\n\n        - $v_{max}= \\sqrt{2\\times9.81\\times0.263}$\n\n        - $v_{max}= 2.27\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$\n\n        This will be the maximum speed since it is assumed that energy losses due to friction is negligible.\n\n        The maximum speed can also be determined with the relationship (HL),\n\n        - $v_{max} = A\\omega$\n\n        Substituting with rounded values,\n\n        - $v_{max} =(2.50)(0.905)$\n\n        - $v_{max} =2.26\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$\n\n    2. Sinusoidal shape on positive vertical axis, with $E_p$ = maximum at $t=0$; $\\checkmark$\n\n        Maxima at $t=0$, $t=\\dfrac{T}{2}$ and $T$;  $\\checkmark$\n\n        Note: Ignore any attempt to show damping.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH1102ciims.png\" alt=\"PH1102ciims\">\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        Kinetic energy is given by,\n\n        - $E_k= \\dfrac{1}{2}mv^2$\n\n        In a system undergoing simple harmonic motion the size of the velocity is sinusoidal with respect to time, with minima at the endpoints and a maximum at equilibrium.  Kinetic energy is therefore sinusoidal.  Because kinetic energy and gravitational potential energy are out of phase during oscillations, this means that gravitational potential energy is also sinusoidal.\n\n        At $t=0$, the wrecking ball is released.  This is the point of maximum displacement where kinetic energy is zero and the gravitational potential energy is at a maximum.  Therefore the shape of the graph for gravitational potential energy is that of cosine.\n\n        Graphically,\n        - the motion is sinusoidal\n        - is a maximum when $t=0$\n        - is always positive (since proportional to the square of the velocity)\n        - there are two energy cycles with each period of oscillation\n\n        Maximum gravitational potential energy occurs at each maximum displacement.  This is also when kinetic energy is zero and note that the total energy remains the same throughout, since the effect of friction is ignored.\n\n    3. Amplitude/Total Energy decreases;  $\\checkmark$\n\n        Period remains constant OR increase in period;  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        When friction acts on a system, energy is lost from the system as heat.  This means that the wrecking ball “system” would lose total energy if friction was experienced.\n\n        The loss of total energy would show a decrease in $E_p$ as a decreased amplitude of the periodic motion.  Also, the maximum velocity through the equilibrium position would decrease since this is related to the decrease in $E_k$.\n\n        In the absence of damping, the period of the motion is given by,\n\n        - $T=2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{L}{g}}$\n\n        and neither $L$ or $g$ change, the period would therefore remain constant. With light damping, the period would increase because the friction slows the motion of the pendulum. This is sometimes considered to be negligible."},{"ref":"PH1106","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1106\n\nA simple pendulum A of length $L$ is set in motion from its lowest point so that it oscillates freely. When Pendulum A reaches maximum height, another simple pendulum B of length $4L$ begins to oscillate from its lowest point, with the same initial direction of motion as Pendulum A.\n\nWhich graph illustrates the angular displacement $x$ of each pendulum at time $t$?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n|| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH1106A.png\" alt=\"PH1106A\">  |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH1106B.png\" alt=\"PH1106B\"> |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH1106C.png\" alt=\"PH1106C\">  |  |  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH1106D.png\" alt=\"PH1106D\"> |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe period of a simple pendulum is given by\n\n- $T=2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{l}{g}}$\n\nHence, the period of Pendulum B can be calculated using the equation\n\n- $T_B=2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{4L}{g}}=2\\Bigl(2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{L}{g}}\\Bigr)=2T_A$\n\nThe frequency of Pendulum B is therefore half that of Pendulum A.\n\nFinally, it is stated that Pendulum B commences its motion when Pendulum A is at maximum displacement, and swings in the same initial direction as Pendulum A. Thus, the $x$ value of Pendulum B increases at the beginning of its motion."},{"ref":"PH1107","marks":"1","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1107\n\nA tennis ball is held at rest above a simple pendulum consisting of a bucket attached at the end of a rope of length $l$. The bucket and ball are released from rest with the string held taut from an elevated position. The ball subsequently lands in the bucket as the pendulum passes through its lowest point for the second time.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/PH1107.png\" alt=\"PH1107\">\n\nWhat is the approximate distance $h$ between the initial position of the tennis ball and the upper end of the rope?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\Bigl(\\dfrac{9}{8}\\pi^2 - 1\\Bigr)l$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\Bigl(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\pi^2 - 1\\Bigr)l$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\Bigl(2\\pi^2 - 1\\Bigr)l$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\Bigl(\\dfrac{9}{4}\\pi^2 - 1\\Bigr)l$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAs the bucket and ball are released at the same instant, and the ball lands in the bucket as it passes through its lowest point for the second time, the transit time of the ball is equal to 0.75 periods of the bucket’s oscillation: the pendulum first passes through its lowest point after 0.25 periods, and it subsequently arrives at the same point once every half-cycle.\n\nHence, the transit time of the ball can be expressed as\n\n- $t_{ball}=\\dfrac{3}{4}T=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\Bigl(2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{l}{g}}\\Bigr)=\\dfrac{3}{2}\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{l}{g}}$\n\nThe distance $x$ travelled by the ball is given by the equations of linear motion:\n\n- $x=\\dfrac{gt_{ball}^2}{2}=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\Bigl(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\pi\\Bigr)^2l=\\dfrac{9}{8}\\pi^2l$\n\nThus, the initial height $h$ of the ball above the top of the rope is given by\n\n- $h=x-l=\\Bigl(\\dfrac{9}{8}\\pi^2 - 1\\Bigr)l$"},{"ref":"PH1860","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1860\n\nA particle undergoes simple harmonic motion.\n\nThe graph below shows the variation of velocity of the particle with time.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/Screensho000t%202025-08-25%20at%206.06.50%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screensho000t 2025-08-25 at 6.06.50 PM\">\n\nWhich graph correctly shows the variation of potential energy $E_p$, of the particle with time?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    | $\\hspace{2cm}$  |B.   ||\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/Screenshott%202025-08-25%20at%206.09.36%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshott 2025-08-25 at 6.09.36 PM\">|  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/Screenshot%202025-08-25%20at%206.09.42%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-08-25 at 6.09.42 PM\">|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/Screenshot%202025-08-25%20at%206.09.47%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-08-25 at 6.09.47 PM\">|  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Simple%20Harmonic%20Motion/images/Screenshot%202025-08-25%20at%206.09.52%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-08-25 at 6.09.52 PM\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe magnitude of the particle's velocity reaches its maximum values at $t=0$ s, 1 s, 2 s, 3 s...\n\nAt these times, the oscillating particle is passing through the equilibrium position, which means that the potential energy must be zero at these times.\n\nGraph B correctly shows the potential energy of the particle which is what we were asked.\n\nGraph A would correspond to the kinetic energy of the particle."}]},{"name":"Standing Waves & Resonance","questions":[{"ref":"PH0046","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0046\n\nThe string $X$ of length $L_X$ has two fixed ends. The string $Y$ of length $L_Y$ has one free end and one fixed end. Waves travel at the same speed in both strings.\n\nThe frequency of the third harmonic of both strings is the same.  What is $\\dfrac{L_X}{L_Y}$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{4}{3}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{3}{4}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{1}{2}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\n\\\n\nIf frequencies and the speed of wave travel are the same, the wavelengths are also the same. \n\nFor two fixed ends, the length of the third harmonic must be equal to $1.5\\lambda$\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0046.png\" alt=\"PH0046\">\n\nWith one free end, third harmonic corresponds to the frequency that is three times the first harmonic. The first harmonic frequency is given by \n\n- $f_1=\\dfrac{v}{4L}$\n\nThe third harmonic frequency is given by \n\n- $f_3=\\dfrac{3v}{4L}$\n\n**note that when there is one free end and one fixed end, the convention is that there is no second harmonic frequency and the harmonic frequencies that are possible are first, third, fifth, etc. The number of the harmonic corresponds to the multiple of the fundamental frequency.\n\nThis leads to the equation \n\n- $\\lambda=\\dfrac{4L}{3}$\n\nthe length of the third harmonic must therefore be $0.75\\lambda$ (as shown)\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0046-1.png\" alt=\"PH0046-1\">\n\n- $\\dfrac{L_X}{L_Y}=\\dfrac{1.5\\lambda}{0.75\\lambda}= 2$"},{"ref":"PH0103","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0103\n\nA taut string on a guitar is fixed at both ends. \n\nWhat is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{wavelength of fourth harmonic}}{\\textrm{wavelength of second harmonic}}$ for the string?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 4\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{1}{2}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{1}{4}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\n\\\n\nSince the $n^{th}$ harmonic has frequency of $n$ times of first harmonic frequency and the ratio of frequencies is $\\dfrac{4}{2}=2$. \n\nUsing $v=f\\lambda$ and knowing that the speed of the wave in the string $v$ is unchanged, the ratio of wavelengths is $\\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n\nAnother way to consider this is to visualise the second harmonic, which would have two antinodes and the length of the string would be equal to one full wavelength. For the fourth harmonic, there will be four full antinodes and so two full wavelengths in the same distance. Therefore the wavelength of the fourth harmonic is half that for the second harmonic."},{"ref":"PH0275","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0275\n\nA student blows across the open end of a narrow cylinder which is closed at the other end. If the length of the cylinder is 15.0 cm and the speed of sound is 340 m s$^{-1}$, what is the frequency of the wave at the third harmonic?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $1.1 \\times 10^3$ Hz\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $1.7 \\times 10^3$ Hz\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2.8 \\times 10^3$ Hz\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $3.4 \\times 10^3$ Hz","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nThe first harmonic in a pipe closed at one end will be set up at $\\dfrac{1}{4}\\lambda$. The next harmonic will be formed at the next odd multiple of $\\dfrac{1}{4}\\lambda$ so as to form an antinode at the opening of the cylinder. The next odd multiple of $\\dfrac{1}{4}\\lambda$ is $\\dfrac{3}{4}\\lambda$. Note that for an open pipe, this is considered to be the third harmonic, and there is no second harmonic for a tube that is closed at one end and open at the other.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0275.png\" alt=\"PH0275\">\n\nSince $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ of the wave is $15\\,cm$ as shown in the diagram above wavelength can be found:\n\n- $\\dfrac{3}{4}\\lambda = 15$       \n\n- $\\lambda = (\\dfrac{4}{3})(15) = 20$ cm $= 0.2 $ m.\n\nRearranging the formula $v=f\\lambda$ we have $f=\\dfrac{v}{\\lambda}$ therefore \n\n- $f=\\dfrac{340}{0.2}=1700\\,$ Hz $ = 1.7\\,\\times\\,10^3\\,$Hz."},{"ref":"PH0296","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0296\n\nA tuning fork is held above a hollow pipe which is in a long cylinder filled with water as shown in the diagram below. \n\nInitially, the pipe is fully immersed in the water. The pipe and the vibrating tuning fork are slowly lifted upward from the surface of the water, creating an air column inside the pipe above the water surface.  \n\nA maximum intensity of the sound is first heard when the air column reaches a length $a$ and again at length $b$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0296.png\" alt=\"PH0296\">\n\nConsider the following statements about the lengths $a$ and $b$, and the wavelength $\\lambda$\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n\n  | | |\n\n  | :-- | :-- |\n\n  | I. | $\\hspace{1em}$ $b=3a$ |\n\n  | II. | $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\lambda=\\dfrac{3b}{4}$ |\n\n  | III. | $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\lambda=4a$ |\n\nWhich of the statements are correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Only I\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ Only III\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nThe first harmonic will be found when length is equal to $\\dfrac{\\lambda}{4}$, which is $a$. \n\nThe next harmonic at $b$, is when the length is equal to $\\dfrac{3\\lambda}{4}$.\n\n \n\nTherefore, statements I and III are correct."},{"ref":"PH0354","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0354\n\nA first harmonic standing wave in a pipe that is opened at one end and closed at the other has a period of 5 ms. For a pipe of the same length that is closed at both ends, what is the frequency of the first harmonic?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 50 Hz\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 75 Hz\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 100 Hz\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 400 Hz","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nSince \n\n- $f=\\dfrac{1}{T}$\n\n- $f_{opened}=\\dfrac{1}{5\\times 10^{-3}} = 200$ Hz\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0354.png\" alt=\"PH0354\">\n\nFor a pipe opened at one end and closed at the other at the first harmonic, \n\n- $L=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{4}$\n\n- $\\lambda =4L$\n\nSince $c=\\lambda f \\hspace{1em}$ therefore $f=\\dfrac{v}{\\lambda}$\n\n- $f_{opened}=\\dfrac{v}{4L}$\n\nFor a pipe closed at both ends at the first harmonic\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0354-1.png\" alt=\"PH0354-1\">\n\n- $L=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{2}$\n\n- $\\lambda_{closed}=2L$\n\nAnd the frequency will be \n\n- $f_{closed}=\\dfrac{v}{2L}$\n\nTherefore $f_{closed}=2f_{opened}=(2)(200)=400$ Hz"},{"ref":"PH0398","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0398\n\nA pipe of length $L_1$ is open at one end and closed at the other. It has a first harmonic frequency equal to that of the third harmonic frequency in a pipe with length $L_2$  that is closed at both ends.\n\nWhat is the ratio $\\dfrac{L_1}{L_2}$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 6\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 3\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{1}{3}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{1}{6}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nFor the pipe that is opened from one end and closed from the other\n\n- $f=\\dfrac{nv}{4L_1}$\n\nat the first harmonic\n\n- $f= \\dfrac{v}{4L_1}$\n\nFor the pipe that is closed from both ends\n\n- $f=\\dfrac{nv}{2L_2}$\n\nat the third harmonic\n\n- $f= \\dfrac{3v}{2L_2}$\n\nSince both have the same frequency\n\n- $\\dfrac{v}{4L_1}=\\dfrac{3v}{2L_2}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{L_1}{L_2} = \\dfrac{1}{6}$"},{"ref":"PH0399","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0399\n\nA string fixed at both ends oscillates in a standing wave as shown with a wavelength of 400 mm.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0399.png\" alt=\"PH0399\">\n\nWhat is the length of the string?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 600 mm\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 800 mm\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1200 mm\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2400 mm","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe distance between two successive nodes in a standing wave is half a wavelength. There are 6 such sections, therefore 3 full wavelengths. \n\n- $(3)(400\\, \\ce{mm}) = 1200$ mm\n\nAlternative:\n\nFrom the diagram, the standing wave is in the sixth harmonic.\n\nFor a string fixed from both ends\n\n- $\\lambda=\\dfrac{2L}{n}$\n\n- $400 \\times 10^{-3}=\\dfrac{2L}{6}$\n\n- $L=\\dfrac{(6)(400\\times 10^{-3})}{2}=1.2\\, \\ce{m}=1200$ mm"},{"ref":"PH0400","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0400\n\nFor an open pipe, the frequency of the first harmonic is $f$ and its wavelength is $\\lambda$. \n\nFor a given harmonic of the same pipe, the ranges of motion of the particles in the pipe are shown below. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0400.png\" alt=\"PH0400\">\n\nThree statements about the standing wave shown in this pipe are\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n\n  | | |\n\n  | :-- | :-- |\n\n  | I. | The frequency is $2f$ |\n\n  | II. | The wavelength is $\\dfrac{\\lambda}{4}$ |\n\n  | III. | The wavelength is less than $\\lambda$ |\n\nWhich statements are true?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nThe displacements in the diagram shows there are three antinodes and two nodes. This can be represented using a transverse standing wave pattern as shown.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0400-1.png\" alt=\"PH0400-1\">\n\nThe standing wave is in the second harmonic\n\nFor an open pipe, the second harmonic frequency is double that of the first harmonic frequency, therefore statement I is correct.\n\nAs the frequency has doubled and the speed of sound has remained the same, through $v = f\\lambda$, the wavelength will be halved, or $\\dfrac{\\lambda}{2}$ which means that statement II in incorrect but statement III is correct."},{"ref":"PH0401","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0401\n\nA 50 cm string that is fixed at both ends produces a standing wave in the third harmonic. The speed of the wave on the string is $200\\ \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$\n\nWhat is the frequency of oscillation?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 6 Hz\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 40 Hz\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 300 Hz\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 600 Hz","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nThe frequency of a string fixed at both ends in the third harmonic: \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0401.png\" alt=\"PH0401\">\n\n$L = \\dfrac{3}{2}(\\lambda)$\n\n$\\lambda = (\\dfrac{2}{3})L$\n\n$v = f\\lambda$\n\n$f = \\dfrac{v}{\\lambda} = \\dfrac{(3v)}{(2L)} = \\dfrac{(3)(200)}{(2)(0.5)} = 600$ Hz"},{"ref":"PH0402","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0402\n\nA speaker produces a sound near a pipe that is closed at one end by a movable piston. The piston is moved in the shown direction until a sound is first heard from the pipe. At this time, the length of the part of the tube near the speaker is $L$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0402.png\" alt=\"PH0402\">\n\n$\\small{\\textrm{[Created with Chemix https://chemix.org/]}}$\n\nWhat is the distance moved by the piston to hear the next resonant sound from the pipe?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{L}{2}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $L$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $2L$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $3L$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \\\n\nFor the first sound to come out of the pipe, a first harmonic is produced.\n\nFor the next sound to be produced, this means a third harmonic is produced. Remember that for a pipe opened from one end and closed from the other, odd harmonics only are formed.\n\nThe wavelength for a standing wave in a pipe closed from one end and opened from the other\n\n- $\\lambda=\\dfrac{4L}{n}$\n\nFor the first harmonic, $n=1$\n\n- $\\lambda=4L$\n\n- $L=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{4}$\n\nFor the third harmonic, $n=3$ \n\n- $\\lambda=\\dfrac{4L_2}{3}$\n\n- $L_2=\\dfrac{3\\lambda}{4}=3L$\n\nWhere $L_2$ is the length of the pipe from the open end.\n\nSo the moved distance by the piston is $3L - L= 2L$"},{"ref":"PH0515","marks":"11","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH0515\n\n**Maximum mark: 11**\n\n1. A student investigates standing waves in strings. A string is connected to an oscillator where a function generator can adjust the frequency. The other end of the string passes through a pulley and supports a mass.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0515aaa.png\" alt=\"PH0515aaa\">\n    $\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with chemix - https ://chemix.org/]}}$\n\n    The student varied the mass $m$ and recorded the values for frequency $f$ for which the first harmonic was visible on the string. The following graph shows the recorded data.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%2011.23.36%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 11.23.36 AM\">\n\n    The uncertainty of the mass is negligible.\n\n    1. Draw the line of best fit for the plotted data on the graph. [1 mark]\n\n    2. By referring to the plotted data, explain why it cannot be stated that frequency is directly proportional to mass.     [1 mark]\n\n    3. Use the graph to determine the value for $f$ with its absolute uncertainty for $m=1.0\\ $kg [2 marks]\n\n2. The theory suggests that\n\n    $f^2=km$\n\n    where *k* is a constant.\n\n    The variation of $f^2$ with *m* is seen on the graph below.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%2011.31.35%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 11.31.35 AM\">\n\n    1.     Show that the absolute uncertainty of $f^2$ is $\\pm0.01\\ $kHz$^2$ for $m=1.0\\ $kg. [3 marks]\n\n    2. The absolute uncertainty of $f^2$ is $0.03\\ $kHz$^2$ for $m=0.25\\ $kg. Construct error bars for $f^2$ when  $m=0.25\\ $kg and $m=1.00\\ $kg. [1 mark]\n\n    3. State and explain whether the theory is supported by data or not. [2 marks]\n\n3. Determine the unit of *k*, in fundamental SI units. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. A curved line that passes through the uncertainty bars $\\checkmark$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%2011.25.54%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 11.25.54 AM\">\n\n    2. A straight line cannot be drawn\n\n        **OR**\n\n        The line does not pass through the origin $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        To represent direct proportionality in graphs, a straight line passes through the origin is drawn. According to the data, it is not possible.\n\n    3. :answer[530] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\pm10 \\ll \\ce{Hz} \\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        According to the graph, each small square in the y-axis increases the value of frequency by 20 Hz. Since the error bars are drawn as the length of one square, the absolute uncertainty is half of 20 Hz.\n\n2.\n    1. Percentage uncertainty of $f = \\dfrac{\\Delta f}{f} \\times 100\\% = \\dfrac{10}{530} \\times 100 \\% = 1.9\\%$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Percentage uncertainty of $f^2 = \\dfrac{\\Delta (f^2)}{f^2} \\times 100 \\% = 2 \\times \\dfrac{\\Delta f}{f} \\times 100 \\% = 2 \\times 1.9\\% = 3.8\\%$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        The absolute uncertainty of$\\ f^2$=$f^2\\times3.8\\%={530}^2\\times3.8\\%=10674\\,\\ce{Hz}^2=0.01\\ \\textrm{kHz}^2$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The percentage uncertainty of *f* for $m=1.0\\ $kg;\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta f}{f} \\times 100\\% = \\dfrac{10}{530} \\times 100\\% = 1.9\\%$\n\n        By using the rules of propagation of uncertainties;\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta (f^2)}{f^2}\\times 100\\% = 2 \\times \\dfrac{\\Delta f}{f} \\times 100\\% = 2 \\times 1.9\\% = 3.8\\%$\n\n        The absolute uncertainty of $f^2$;\n\n        - $\\Delta (f^2) = f^2 \\times 3.8\\% = 530^2 \\times 3.8\\% = 0.01 \\ \\textrm{kHz}^2$\n\n    2. Error bar added to first point as $\\pm3$ square and to the fourth point as $\\pm1$ square $\\checkmark$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%2011.31.51%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 11.31.51 AM\">\n\n    3. The theory suggests a direct proportionality between $f^2$ and *m* $\\checkmark$\n\n        A straight line that passes within the uncertainty bars and through the origin can be drawn. Therefore it matches the theory.\n\n        **OR**\n\n        A straight line that passes within the uncertainty bars and through the origin cannot be drawn. Therefore it does not match the theory. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Theory suggests a direct proportionality between $f^2$ and *m*. According to the data and error bars of two points, a straight line that passes through the origin can be drawn. Therefore, the theory is supported.\n\n3. SI unit of $k=\\ll \\dfrac{{\\textrm{kHz}}^2}{\\ce{kg}}=\\ \\dfrac{{(\\ce{s}^{-1})}^2}{\\ce{kg}}=\\gg \\ce{s}^{-2}\\,{\\ce{kg}}^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    When the equation is rearranged:\n\n    - $k=\\dfrac{f^2}{m}$\n\n    Substitute SI units of the quantities:\n\n    SI unit of $k=\\dfrac{{(\\ce{s}^{-1}})^2}{\\ce{kg}}=\\ce{s}^{-2}\\,{\\ce{kg}}^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH0527","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0527\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nA thin tube with a length of $L$ is immersed in water and fixed to the base of a container of liquid by a string. The length of the tube above the surface of the water is $X$ and inside the water is $Y$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0527.png\" alt=\"PH0527\">\n\nA speaker is emitting a single-frequency sound above the tube. For particular values of $X$, a standing wave is established in the tube.\n\n1. Explain how a standing wave is formed in this tube. [2 marks]\n\n2. The frequency of the sound is 200 Hz. It is found that the smallest value of $X$ for which a standing wave is established in the tube is 42.0 cm. Estimate the speed of sound in the tube. [3 marks]\n\n3. The string is cut and the tube rises in the container, decreasing the length of tube $Y$ inside the water. When $Y$ is 22 cm, a standing wave is next formed in the tube. Calculate the total length of the tube. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. Sound waves move down the tube and are reflected from the closed end of the tube/surface of the water $\\checkmark$\n\n    then incident waves interfere/superpose with the reflected waves $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    Standing waves occur when two identical waves travel in opposite directions in a medium. This situation arises here because of the reflection of the waves from the closed end.\n\n2. $X = (\\dfrac{1}{4})\\lambda$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $v=\\lambda f$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $v = 4Xf = (4)(0.42)(200)$\n\n    $v$ = :answer[$336\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0527-1.png\" alt=\"PH0527-1\">\n\n    For the first resonant length, the distance $X$ will be equal to $\\dfrac{\\lambda}{4}$.\n\n    Thus $\\lambda = 4X = 4(0.42) = 1.68$ m\n\n    And knowing that the frequency $f =$ 200 Hz\n\n    Therefore $v = f\\lambda = (200)(1.68) = 336\\ \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$\n\n3. $X = (\\dfrac{3}{4}) \\lambda$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $X = (0.75)(1.68) = 1.26$ m $\\checkmark$\n\n    $L = 0.22 + 1.26 = 1.48$ m $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0527-2.png\" alt=\"PH0527-2\">\n\n    The next resonant length occurs when $X$ has lengthened to ($\\dfrac{3}{4}$) $\\lambda$\n\n    The wavelength has already been calculated, therefore $X = (\\dfrac{3}{4})\\lambda = (0.75)(1.68)=1.26$ m\n\n    It is given that the section in the water has a length of $22$ cm\n\n    Therefore the total length is $1.26 + 0.22 = 1.48$ m"},{"ref":"PH0528","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0528\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nA speaker emits sound towards a tube that is open at both ends as shown in Figure A. The speed of sound is $340\\ \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$ and a standing wave is formed in the pipe. At a particular instant, the positions of particles $X$ and $Y$ are shown.\n\nFigure B shows the displacement of molecules of air in the pipe at this instant in time. Displacement to the right is positive.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0528-1.png\" alt=\"PH0528-1\">\n\n1.  1.  Explain whether particle $X$ is in the center of a compression or a rarefaction.   [2 marks]\n\n    2.  On Figure A, use $Y'$ to mark the equilibrium position of particle $Y$ [1 mark]\n\n2. When the frequency of the sound is increased by 120 Hz the next harmonic of the standing wave is formed in the pipe. Calculate the length of the pipe.  [3 marks]","markScheme":"1.  1.  $X$ is in a compression. $\\checkmark$\n\n        The particles to the left of $X$ have been displaced to the right and the particles to the right of $X$ have been displaced to the left.  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The displacement-position graph can be used to determine that $X$ is in the middle of a compression. On the graph, $X$ has a displacement of zero from its equilibrium position. Particles to its left, however, have a positive displacement. This means that the particles are displaced to the right, towards particle $X$. The graph also shows that particles to the right of $X$ have been displaced in the negative direction which is to the left of their equilibrium positions which is also towards particle $X$. Thus the particles either side of $X$ have been displaced closer to $X$ and so $X$ is in a compression.\n\n    2. The graph shows that $Y$ has been displaced in the negative direction which is to the left. Thus, the equilibrium position of $Y$ must be to the right of the position shown.\n\n2.  ::indent\n\n    - $f = n (\\dfrac{v}{2L})$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $f + 120 = (n+1) (\\dfrac{v}{2L})$\n\n    - $\\dfrac{v}{2L} = 120$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $L = \\dfrac{340}{240} = 1.416\\, \\ce{m} \\approx 142\\  \\ce{cm}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    The pipe is seen to be vibrating at the first harmonic frequency. This is when the pipe's length is equal to half a wavelength of the sound.\n\n    Subsequent harmonics are found at multiples of the lowest harmonic frequency.\n\n    Because the difference between two harmonic frequencies is 120 Hz, this means that the harmonic frequency is equal to 120 Hz.\n\n    At the first harmonic is also equal to $\\dfrac{v}{2L}$\n\n    - $\\dfrac{v}{2L}= 120$ Hz     \n\n    Which leads to \n\n    - $L = \\dfrac{340}{240} = 1.416 \\, \\ce{m}  \\approx 142$ cm"},{"ref":"PH0529","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0529\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n1. Discuss the differences between traveling waves and standing waves [2 marks]\n\n2. A 45 cm guitar string fixed at both ends is raised and released so that it vibrates at its fundamental mode. The midpoint of the string passes through its equilibrium position 475 times in one second. Estimate the speed of the wave on the string. [2 marks]\n\n3. The guitar string is now gently touched 15 cm from one end such that a node is created at that point, but the entire length of the string still oscillates. Calculate the frequency of the sound generated. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Energy is propagated in traveling waves whereas it is stored in standing waves $\\checkmark$\n\n    In a traveling wave every particle in the medium oscillates with the same amplitude, whereas in a standing wave particles oscillate with different amplitudes. $\\checkmark$\n\n2. $f = \\dfrac{475}{2} = 237.5\\, \\ce{Hz}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $f = n(\\dfrac{v}{2L}) \\hspace{1em} n= 1$ for fundamental  \n\n    \n\n    $v = 2fL = (237.5)(2)(0.45)$ = :answer[$214\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    In an oscillation, the string will pass its equilibrium point twice, therefore the frequency is half of the value given\n\n    $f = (\\dfrac{475}{2}) = 237.5\\,\\ce{Hz}$\n\n    In the fundamental mode for a string fixed at both ends, the length of the string is equal to one half of a wavelength\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0529.png\" alt=\"PH0529\">\n\n    Thus $\\lambda = 2L = 2(0.45) = 0.90\\,\\ce{m}$\n\n    Using the universal wave equation, \n\n    $v = f\\lambda = (237.5)(0.9) = 214\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$\n\n3. Distance between nodes becomes $15\\,cm$, thus wavelength is 30 cm $\\checkmark$\n\n    $f = \\dfrac{v}{\\lambda}$\n\n    $\\hspace{.9em} = \\dfrac{214}{0.30}$\n\n    $\\hspace{.7em}$ = :answer[$712\\,\\ce{Hz}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    ecf from 3(b) for MP2\n\n    This answer can also be obtained by recognizing the frequency is $3f_o$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    Creating a node at the 15 cm mark will force a node at that location and restrict the first and second harmonic oscillation modes. Thus the third harmonic frequency will be heard.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0529-1.png\" alt=\"PH0529-1\">\n\n    Multiples of this frequency are also possible answers here, as a node would be created in the same 15 cm position for $6f_o$ , $9f_o$ etc."},{"ref":"PH0577","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0577\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nA loudspeaker producing sound of a single frequency is placed at a distance 1.5 m in front of a barrier as shown in the diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0577.png\" alt=\"PH0577\">\n\n1. Describe the conditions for which standing waves occur. [1 mark]\n\n2. \n\n    1. A microphone moves in a horizontal direction from the barrier to the speaker. The microphone detects loud sound at some positions while at others it detects low intensity sound. Explain why this happens. [3 marks]\n\n    \n\n    2. The microphone detects three positions of high intensity sound. On the axes given, sketch the relationship between the amplitude of oscillation of the air particles and the distance from the wall. [2 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0577-1.png\" alt=\"PH0577-1\">\n\n    3. The distance between two successive loud sound positions is 0.60 m. Calculate the frequency of the sound produced by the loudspeaker. The speed of sound in air is $340\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$. [2 marks]\n\n3. The microphone is placed at the location where the sound is loudest. The barrier is then moved towards the loudspeaker until the sound at the microphone has become quiet. Calculate the distance the barrier has moved. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Occur when two identical waves traveling in opposite directions superpose/when a wave interferes with its own reflection in a medium **OWTTE**. $\\checkmark$\n\n2. \n\n    1. Sound is reflected at the barrier. $\\checkmark$\n\n        Standing wave is formed. $\\checkmark$\n\n        Loud sound at antinodes/points of constructive interference **and** low intensity sound at nodes/points of destructive interference. $\\checkmark$\n\n    2. Node/zero amplitude at wall $\\checkmark$\n\n        Three antinodes shown. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        At the wall, the sound reflects out of phase which results in destructive interference and therefore a node. Antinodes occur where there are loud sounds, so three must be shown. There is no specific end condition required for the wave at the speaker.\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0577-2.png\" alt=\"PH0577-2\">\n\n        $\\small{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra, - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\n    3. $\\lambda = \\ll(2)(0.60)\\gg$ \n\n        $\\lambda=1.2\\ll \\ce{m}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $f=\\dfrac{340}{1.2}$ = :answer[$283\\ll \\ce{Hz}\\gg$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The wavelength is double the distance between two antinodes.\n\n        $\\lambda=(2)(0.6)=1.2\\,\\ce{m}$\n\n        Then the frequency is\n\n        \n\n        $f=\\dfrac{v}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{340}{1.2}=283.33\\,\\ce{Hz}$\n\n3. Distance moved = $\\ll\\dfrac{\\lambda}{4}\\gg=\\dfrac{1.2}{4}$\n\n    **OR**\n\n    Distance moved = $\\ll\\dfrac{1}{2}$ distance between two antinodes $\\gg =\\dfrac{0.6}{2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    \n\n    Distance moved = :answer[$0.3\\, \\ce{m}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    Moving the barrier will shift the whole wave pattern to the right. Changing from a loud area to a quiet area involves moving from an antinode to a node. A node and an antinode are separated by $\\dfrac{\\lambda}{4}$"},{"ref":"PH0578","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH0578\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nIn an experiment to determine the speed of sound in air, some students used a number of tuning forks of known frequencies and a tube with a moveable piston arranged as shown below.\n\nFor each tuning fork they used the setup shown in the diagram.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/Screenshot%202025-04-16%20at%204.11.10%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-16 at 4.11.10 PM\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with chemix - https:// chemix.org/]}}$\n\nThe students would sound the tuning fork, then move the piston slowly downwards until a loud sound is first heard. The length $L$ was then measured. This was repeated for different tuning forks.\n\nThe graph shows the variation of $L^{-1}$ with frequency $f$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/Screenshot%202026-02-18%20at%209.03.22%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-02-18 at 9.03.22 AM\">\n\n1. Draw the line of best fit. [1 mark]\n\n2. Calculate the fractional uncertainty of $L^{-1}$ when the length is $20\\, $cm. [2 marks]\n\n3.\n    1. Determine the gradient, expressing your answer in fundamental SI units. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Use the gradient calculated in (i) to determine the speed of sound. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. A straight line passing within all error bars $\\checkmark$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/Screenshot%202026-02-18%20at%209.04.13%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-02-18 at 9.04.13 AM\">\n\n2. $\\dfrac{1}{L}=0.05\\ll \\ce{cm}^{-1}\\gg$, uncertainty is $0.001\\ \\ce{cm}^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    Fractional uncertainty = :answer[0.02] $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    From the graph, the uncertainty on the 0.05 is 0.001.\n\n    The fractional uncertainty is therefore $\\dfrac{0.001}{0.05}=0.02\\ or\\ 2\\%$\n\n3.\n    1. Gradient = $\\left(\\dfrac{480-180}{5.8-2.2}\\right)=$ :answer[83.33] $\\checkmark$. (Accept answers from 82.5 to 86.25)\n\n       $\\ce{m s}^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n       $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n       Note that over half of the line should be used to find the gradient.\n\n       The values on the horizontal axis and therefore the denominator are $\\times 10^{-2}\\, \\ce{cm}^{-1}$. Within the gradient calculation, this becomes $\\times 10^2\\,\\ce{cm}$ which is equvalent to m.\n\n       The values on the vertical axis are Hz, which are equivalent to s$^{-1}$.\n\n       Combined, this leaves the units in m s$^{-1}$.\n\n    2. $\\ll\\ v=4(gradient)\\gg$\n\n        $v=\\left(4\\right)\\left(83.33\\right)$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        v= :answer[333] $\\ll\\ \\ce{m s}^{-1}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\\\n        *Accept answers from 330 to 345*\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        This model is similar to a pipe that is open at one end and closed at the other.\n\n        The frequency in such a pipe is given by\n        $f=\\dfrac{nv}{4L}$\n\n        The first loud sound is heard at the first harmonics at $n=1$\n\n        Then the gradient for this graph is\n\n        $\\begin{aligned}\n        gradient &= \\dfrac{v}{4}\\\\\n        v &=4\\left(gradient\\right)\\\\\n        v &=\\left(4\\right)\\left(83.33\\right)\\\\\n        &=333.32\\, \\ce{m s}^{-1}\\\\\n        \\end{aligned}$"},{"ref":"PH0656","marks":"13","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0656\n\n**Maximum mark: 13**\n\n1. An engineer designs a bridge. By using software, they simulate how the amplitude $A$ of the vibration of the bridge varies with the driving frequency $f$ of vibration due to wind.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0656.png\" alt=\"PH0656\">\n\n    To avoid resonance, they decided to increase the amount of damping by changing the design of the bridge.\n\n    1. State the difference between forced and free vibrations. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Explain why resonance is dangerous for bridges. [3 marks]\n\n    3. On the graph below, sketch the variation of the amplitude of the vibration of the bridge with the frequency of the forced vibration after the damping is increased. [2 marks]\n\n        *There is no need to add values to the axes*\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0656a.png\" alt=\"PH0656a\">\n\n        *Dashed lines are showing the graph before the damping is increased*\n\n    4. In some cases, resonance can be useful. Describe a practical application of resonance. [2 marks]\n\n2. A restaurant uses a swinging door to provide a convenient way for people to move in and out.\n\n    Identify the damping type of the door if the door:\n\n    1. swings back very slowly and returns to the closed position without oscillating. [1 mark]\n\n    2. swings back and forth several times before reaching the closed position. [1 mark]\n\n    3. closes smoothly in a short time and does not oscillate back and forth before coming to a stop. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. In free vibrations, the oscillating system oscillates at its own $\\underline{natural frequency}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        In forced vibrations, the oscillating system is driven by external oscillator $\\checkmark$\n\n    2. Resonance happens when the natural frequency of the bridge matches the driving frequency of the wind. $\\checkmark$\n\n        It leads to a significant increase in amplitude (by transferring the energy of the oscillator more efficiently).  $\\checkmark$\n\n        It can be the cause of failure/destruction. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Resonance happens when the natural frequency of the oscillating object matches the driving frequency of the oscillator. It leads to a significant increase in amplitude by transferring the energy of the oscillator more efficiently to the oscillating object, giving a \"push\" to the oscillation at just the right time to increase its amplitude. This can lead even to the point of failure.\n\n    3. Maximum amplitude is smaller $\\checkmark$\n\n        Maximum amplitude is shifted to the left $\\checkmark$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0656_MS.png\" alt=\"PH0656_MS\">\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Damping decreases the maximum amplitude of the vibration and the resonant frequency.\n\n    4. Any device/application that utilizes resonance $\\checkmark$\n\n        Explanation of how resonance is used  in mentioned device/application $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        A microwave oven uses resonance to quickly heat food by generating electromagnetic waves at a frequency that matches the natural frequency of water molecules. Hence, water molecules vibrate in maximum displacement and energy is transferred to them at maximum efficiency.\n\n        The guitar is another illustration that can be used for useful resonance. The vibration of guitar strings produces a sound that is amplified using resonance. The guitar's resonant frequencies are carefully tuned to correspond with each note produced by its strings. Therefore, the produced sound is clear and amplified.\n\n2.\n    1. Over/heavily damped $\\checkmark$\n\n    2. Under/lightly damped $\\checkmark$\n\n    3. Critically damped $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    In overly or heavily damped situations, the damping force is so big that it brings the door to the rest without oscillation. But the time of closure is long. In under or lightly damped situations, the damping force is small, such that it takes several oscillations to stop. In critically damped situations, the damping force is precisely adjusted to close the door in the shortest time without oscillating."},{"ref":"PH0672-2","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0672-2\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n1. A loudspeaker is placed near the open end of a tube which is closed at the other end.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/speaker3.png\" alt=\"speaker3\">\n\n    The length of the tube is $0.45\\ m$ and the speed of the sound wave in the air is $340 \\ ms^{-1}$.\n\n    1.     Identify with ticks [$\\checkmark$] the properties of the standing waves and travelling waves. [2 marks]\n\n        | | Standing Waves | Travelling Waves |\n        | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n        | All particles vibrate with the same amplitude | | |\n        | Particles one wavelength apart vibrate in the same phase | | |\n        | Energy is not transferred along the wave | | |\n\n    2. Waves are also classified as transverse and longitudinal. Explain why longitudinal waves are generated by the speaker. [1 mark]\n\n    3.     Show that the lowest frequency of a sound wave that will form a standing wave in the tube is approximately $190\\ Hz$. [2 marks]\n\n2. In a school concert, two coherent loudspeakers are used in an open area. A student suggests that the quality of the sound that different spectators hear will be affected due to interference. Explain how the interference of sound waves can affect the quality of the sound. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. *One mark for each correct column*\n\n        | | Standing Waves | Travelling Waves |\n        | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n        | All particles vibrate with the same amplitude | | $\\checkmark$ |\n        | Particles one wavelength apart vibrate in the same phase |  $\\checkmark$ |  $\\checkmark$ |\n        | Energy is not transferred along the wave |  $\\checkmark$ | |\n\n    2. The speaker oscillates in the same direction that the wave energy is transferred  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nIn longitudinal waves, the vibration of the particles is along the direction of motion of the wave/energy. The sound wave created by the speaker is travelling horizontally and the air particles are also oscillating horizontally. Therefore, the wave formed in the tube is longitudinal.\n\n    3. $\\lambda=4\\left(0.45\\right)=$ $1.8\\, m$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $f=\\dfrac{v}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{340}{1.8}$ = :answer[$189\\ Hz$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nIn a tube with one closed and one open end, the fundamental harmonic standing wave has a wavelength that is four times the length of the tube:\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\lambda=4\\left(0.45\\right)=1.8\\ m$\n\n        By using the equation of the speed of the waves:\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $v=\\lambda\\ f$\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $f=\\dfrac{v}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{340}{1.8}=189\\ Hz$\n\n2. Sound can be louder or quieter at different locations $\\checkmark$\n\n    where sound waves interfere constructively or destructively $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\nIn regions of constructive interference, where the sound waves reinforce each other, the volume and intensity of the sound will be greater. However, in regions of destructive interference, where the sound waves cancel each other out, the volume will decrease, resulting in quieter areas or even dead spots where the sound may be barely audible or absent."},{"ref":"PH0811","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0811\n\nA pendulum oscillates in simple harmonic motion inside a box filled with a fluid as shown in the diagram. The system is moderately damped.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/Box.png\" alt=\"Box\">\n\nA driving force is applied to the pendulum, the graph below shows how the amplitude of oscillation of the pendulum varies as the frequency of the driving force is increased.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/Damping3.png\" alt=\"Damping3\">\n\nHow would the shape of the graph change if the fluid inside the box were replaced with a fluid of lower density?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Peak lower and to the left\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Peak higher and to the left\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Peak lower and to the right\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Peak higher and to the right","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nA lower density fluid means less damping (the resistive force acting on the pendulum as it moves is lower).\n\nThe amplitude of vibration at the resonant frequency is higher so the peak is higher. (Damping reduces amplitude so less damping means that a greater amplitude can be reached.)\n\nResonant frequency will be higher so peak moves to the right. (Resonant frequency will be slightly lower than natural frequency for non-zero damping. The less damping there is, the closer the resonant frequency is to the natural frequency. (For light damping it is assumed that natural frequency = resonant frequency.)\n\nNote: The peak will also be thinner due to the maximum amplitude being larger.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">"},{"ref":"PH0813","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0813\n\nA tall building is fitted with a stabilizing mechanism to prevent swaying during earthquakes. The stabilizing mechanism is a damped harmonic oscillator that transfers vibration energy into thermal energy.\n\nBelow are three statements about the stabilizing mechanism.\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | The natural frequency of the harmonic oscillator should be much greater than the natural frequency of oscillation of the building during the largest expected earthquake.   |\n  | II. | The thermal energy absorbed by the oscillator will be at a maximum when the driving force provided by the swaying building causes the oscillator to resonate. |\n  | III. | The stabilizing mechanism will be most effective when the oscillator exhibits critical damping. |\n\nWhich of the statements are correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nI is incorrect. The natural frequency of the oscillator should be approximately equal to the natural frequency of oscillation of the building during the largest expected earthquake. This will allow for resonance and therefore maximum absorption of energy (during the most dangerous of situations).\n\nII is correct. Maximum energy absorption occurs at resonance.\n\nIII is correct. Critical damping will mean that amplitude of vibration falls to zero in the shortest possible time."},{"ref":"PH0814","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0814\n\nA U-tube is partially filled with water as shown. The moveable piston is pushed up and down providing a driving force, this causes the water in the tube to oscillate with an amplitude, $A$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/utube.png\" alt=\"utube\">\n\nThe frequency of the driving force is varied. Which graph correctly shows the variation of frequency of the driving force, $f$ with the amplitude of oscillation, $A$?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n|| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">  |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">|\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">  |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">|","markScheme":"**答案：B**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nGraph B shows a single resonance peak. The system will oscillate with maximum amplitude when frequency of the driving force is approximately equal to the natural frequency of the system."},{"ref":"PH0815","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0815\n\nA loudspeaker is placed above a cylindrical tube of diameter $d$ as shown in the diagram. The loudspeaker plays a sound of constant frequency, $f$. The tube is partially filled with water and a resonant sound is heard. Water is then slowly drained from the bottom of the tube and the next resonant sound is heard when a volume of water, $V$ has been removed.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/tube.png\" alt=\"tube\">\n\nWhich of the following is a correct expression for the speed of sound?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{Vf}{\\pi d^2}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{2Vf}{\\pi d^2}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{4Vf}{\\pi d^2}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $\\dfrac{8Vf}{\\pi d^2}$","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAt the open end, vibrations are free, so this must be an antinode.\nAt the surface of the water, vibrations are fixed, so this must be a node.\n\nThe first resonant sound of the two sounds heard in this scenario is the $n^{th}$ harmonic; the second resonant sound must be the $(n+2)^{th}$   harmonic. The difference in height of the water between where $n^{th}$ and $(n+2)^{th}$ harmonics are heard is equal to half of the wavelength of the sound wave.\n\nThere is not enough information to determine $n$. But let us assume $n=3$ for illustrative purposes.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\nVolume of a cylinder $=\\pi r^2 h$\n\nTherefore\n\n-  $V=\\pi \\Bigl(\\dfrac{d}{2}\\Bigr)^2 \\Bigl(\\dfrac{\\lambda}{2}\\Bigr)$\n\nRearranging for $\\lambda$ gives\n\n- $λ=\\dfrac{8V}{\\pi d^2}$\n\nSpeed of sound\n\n- $v= f\\lambda=\\dfrac{8Vf}{\\pi d^2}$"},{"ref":"PH0816","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0816\n\nA loudspeaker is placed next to a tube $AB$ which is open at both ends as shown in the diagram. The loudspeaker plays sound of constant frequency causing a standing wave of the second harmonic to be established inside the tube.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/stadningtube.png\" alt=\"stadningtube\">\n\nBelow are three statements about the standing wave.\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | The standing wave is formed from the superposition of two waves travelling in opposite directions.   |\n  | II. | The sound wave is partially reflected at $B.$ |\n  | III. | The wavelength of the sound wave is equal to the length of the tube. |\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I and II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I and III only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I, II and III","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nI is correct. Standing waves are formed when two identical progressive waves are travelling in opposite directions.\n\nII is correct. The wave moving to the right is produced from the loudspeaker and the wave traveling to the left is due to partial, in-phase, reflection of the traveling wave at the open end of the tube. This wave is of the same frequency as the original wave.\n\nIII is correct. The second harmonic for a tube open at both ends can be represented as follows:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">"},{"ref":"PH0817","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0817\n\nA string of length $L$ is free at both ends. The string vibrates with a frequency $f$ and a standing wave of the third harmonic is formed.\n\nOne end of the string is then fixed, and the string is shortened with the speed of waves in the string remaining unchanged. The string vibrates with the same frequency $f$ and a standing wave is formed.\n\nWhich of the following could not be the new length of the string?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{L}{6}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{L}{3}$\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{L}{2}$\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{5L}{6}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\\\nA string that is free at both ends will have an antinode at each end. The third harmonic can be represented as follows.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/harmonic.png\" alt=\"harmonic\">\n\nBecause the frequency is kept constant, the wavelength will be the same in both situations.\n\nA string that is free at one end and fixed at the other will have a node at one end and an antinode at the other.\n\nWe can rule out $\\dfrac{L}{3}$. Three of the $\\lq$free at one end fixed at one end' standing waves lined up will always have a node at one end and an antinode at the other end. So $\\dfrac{L}{3}$ could not possibly be a standing wave that is free at one end and fixed at the other end given that $l$ is a standing wave for two free ends.\n\nThis is enough to determine the answer is **B**.\n\nPossible standing waves for shorter string may be represented as follows.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0817bbb.png\" alt=\"PH0817bbb\">"},{"ref":"PH0818","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0818\n\nA standing wave of the third harmonic is formed inside a tube which is closed at both ends. The instantaneous displacements of points $P$ and $Q$ from the equilibrium position are represented in the diagram below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\nWhich row correctly identifies the phase difference between the oscillations of $P$ and $Q$ and the nature of the point $P$?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Phase difference / radians$\\hspace{1.5em}$  | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Nature of the point P$\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$ |node |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$ | antinode |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\pi$ | node |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\pi$ | antinode |","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nWithin an antinode section, all points oscillate in phase although with different amplitudes. Adjacent antinode sections oscillate opposite to one another, and so are $\\pi$ radians or 180 degrees out of phase.\n\nBecause points P and Q are on adjacent antinode sections, the phase difference is $\\pi$ radians.\n\n$P$ is a point of maximum displacement so is a (displacement) antinode. In the context of sound waves maximum displacement refers to maximum longitudinal amplitude of vibration of air molecules.\n\nNote: It is true that the points P and Q are separated by $\\dfrac{3}{8}\\lambda$ and in a normal progressive wave would have a phase difference of $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$ in their oscillations. Because they are in adjacent antinodal sections, they are fully out of phase ($\\pi$ radians)."},{"ref":"PH0823","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0823\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\n1. A mass, $m$, at rest is hanging from a spring and the energy stored in the spring is $E_H$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n    Show that the spring constant, $k$, may be expressed as follows:\n\n    $k=\\dfrac{m^2 g^2}{2E_H}$\n\n    Where $g$ is the acceleration of freefall on Earth's surface. [2 marks]\n\n2. It is given that $m=50\\, \\ce{g}$ and $E_H=9.8\\,\\ce{m}\\ce{J}$. Determine the natural frequency of oscillation of the system. [2 marks]\n\n3. The mass-spring system is then suspended from a vibration generator that provides a driving force.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n    The frequency of the driving force, $f$, is varied. For each value of the driving frequency a steady constant amplitude $A$ is reached by the mass-spring system. The oscillations of the mass spring system are lightly damped.\n\n    On the axes below, sketch a graph to show the variation of $A$ with $f$. [2 marks]\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n4. Deduce the frequency at which the peak amplitude of oscillation would be achieved. Explain your answer. [2 marks]\n\n5. The vibration generator is removed and the mass spring system is now submerged in a beaker of oil and as a result the oscillations are critically damped. Describe what is meant by $\\lq$critically damped'. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. ::indent\n    - $E_H=\\dfrac{1}{2}kx^2$ and $F=(-)kx$ seen\n\n    And\n\n    - $F=(-)mg$  or $mg=kx$ seen.  $\\checkmark$\n\n    Substitution of $x=\\dfrac{mg}{k}$ into $E_H=\\dfrac{1}{2} kx^2$\n\n    Or $x=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2E_H}{k}}$ into $mg=kx$   $\\checkmark$\n\n    Resulting in\n\n    - $k=\\dfrac{m^2 g^2}{2E_H}$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\n\n    - $E_H=\\dfrac{1}{2} kx^2$ and $F=-kx$\n\n    The elastic restoring force is equal and opposite to the weight hanging on the end of the spring so $F=-mg$\n\n    Combining the equations $E_H=\\dfrac{1}{2}kx^2$ and $mg=kx$ gives:\n\n    - $E_H=\\dfrac{1}{2}k\\Bigl(\\dfrac{mg}{k}\\Bigr)^2$\n\n    - $E_H=\\dfrac{m^2 g^2}{2k}$\n\n    - $k=\\dfrac{m^2 g^2}{2E_H}$\n\n2. Frequency is given by $f=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{k}{m}}$\n\n    Or\n\n    $k=12.25\\,(\\,\\ce{N}\\,\\ce{m}^{-1})$   $\\checkmark$\n\n    $f=2.5\\,\\ce{Hz}$ (2sf)   $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    Time period of mass spring system is given by:\n\n    - $T=2\\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{m}{k}}$\n\n    And\n\n    - $f=\\dfrac{1}{T}$\n\n    Therefore\n\n    -  $f=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}\\sqrt{\\dfrac{k}{m}}$\n\n    Using the equation from part a:\n\n    - $k=\\dfrac{m^2 g^2}{2E_H}=\\dfrac{(0.05)^2 (9.8)^2}{2(9.8\\times 10^{-3})}=12.25\\,\\ce{N}\\,\\ce{m}^{-1}$\n\n    Now substituting values into frequency equation:\n\n    - $f=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{k}{m}}=\\dfrac{1}{2\\pi}\\sqrt{\\dfrac{12.25}{0.05}}=2.49\\, \\ce{Hz}=2.5\\, \\ce{Hz}$ (2sf)\n\n3. One maximum shown and curve broadly similar to the example above.  $\\checkmark$\n\n    Amplitude falls on each side as shown.  $\\checkmark$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n4. $2.5\\,\\ce{Hz}$  (Allow ecf from part b)  $\\checkmark$\n\n    Resonance occurs when the driving frequency is close to/equal to the natural frequency of the system (for a lightly damped system).  $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    Resonant frequency = natural frequency when there is no damping (not physically possible). As damping increases, resonant frequency will become smaller than the natural frequency but for light damping this difference is negligible.\n\n5. motion rapidly dies away / rapidly stops / stops in the minimum time.  $\\checkmark$\n\n    without completing an oscillation / without overshooting.  $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    A critically damped system will return to its equilibrium in the shortest time possible without\noscillating.\n\n    The displacement time graph for a critically damped system looks as follows:\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n    Curve does not cross the time axis."},{"ref":"PH0825","marks":"10","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0825\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\n1. A uniform string is set up to investigate standing waves as shown in the diagram below. A mass of 500 g is suspended on the end of the string.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0825a.png\" alt=\"PH0825a\">\n\n    The vibration generator is set to a frequency of 350 Hz and a standing wave is formed between points **A** and **B**.\n\n    Explain how the standing wave is formed on the string. [1 mark]\n\n2. The frequency of the vibration generator is gradually increased until the next standing wave is formed at 420 Hz.\n\n    Explain why the lowest frequency of oscillation that will produce a standing wave will be 70 Hz. [2 marks]\n\n3. The speed of a wave in a string, $v$ is given by the following equation:\n\n    $\\hspace{1em}$ $v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{T}{\\mu}}$\n\n    Where $T$ is the tension in the string and $\\mu$ is the mass per unit length of the string.\n\n    It is given that the mass of the part of the string between points **A** and **B** is 0.5 g.\n\n    1. Show that the displacement between A and B, $s=50\\ \\ce{cm}$. [3 marks]\n\n    2. Calculate the velocity of the wave on the string. [1 mark]\n\n4.  The frequency of the vibration generator is changed such that a standing wave of the second harmonic is formed on the string as shown in the diagram below.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0825e.png\" alt=\"PH0825e\">\n\n    1. **P**, **Q** and **R**, are points on the standing wave. State the point that represents a node. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Compare the frequency and amplitude of oscillation of points **P** and **R**. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Wave (created by vibration generator at **A**) is reflected at **B**. Incident and reflected wave superpose/ interfere. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    The outgoing waves from the vibration generator will reflect back at point **B**. The incident and reflected waves have the same amplitude, speed, and frequency so their superposition results in points where interference is always constructive (antinodes) and points where interference is always destructive (nodes).\n\n2. Clear explanation that each harmonic frequency is a multiple of the first harmonic frequency. $\\checkmark$\n\n    Idea that difference between two successive harmonic frequencies is the first harmonic frequency so $f=70\\ \\ce{Hz}$\\\n*OR*\\\n$420-350=70\\ \\ce{Hz}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    Each harmonic frequency is a multiple of the first harmonic frequency e.g.\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$For a string that is fixed at both ends, the $n^{th}$ harmonic frequency $f_n$ is given by $f_n = nf$ where $f$ is the frequency of the first harmonic.''\n\n    Use of the fact that the difference between two successive harmonic frequencies is always equal to first harmonic frequency: $f_{n+1}-f_n=(n+1)f-(nf)=f$\n\n    The question implies that 420 Hz and 350 Hz are successive harmonic frequencies so $f=420-350=70\\ \\ce{Hz}$\n\n3. 1. $T=mg$\\\n        and\\\n        $v=f\\lambda=f(2s)$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $2fs=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{Mgs}{m}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Giving\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $s=\\dfrac{Mg}{4mf^2}=\\dfrac{(0.5)(9.8)}{(4)(5 \\times 10^{-4})(70)^2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $=(0.5\\ \\ce{m} =50\\ \\ce{cm})$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        We can use the equation given in the question to find an expression for $v$ in terms of $s$ and parameters given.\n\n        Tension = weight of hanging mass $= Mg$ where $M$ is the hanging mass and $g$ is acceleration of free fall on Earth.\n\n        Mass per unit length = $\\dfrac{m}{s}$ where $m$ is the mass of the wire between **A** and **B**.\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $v = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{T}{\\mu}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{Mg}{\\left (\\dfrac{m}{s} \\right )}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{Mgs}{m}}$\n\n        We can use our knowledge of stationary waves to find another expression for $v$ in terms of $s$ (and the known parameter $f$).\n\n        For the first harmonic $s=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{2}$ so we can write\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $v=2sf$\n\n        where $f$ is the frequency of the first harmonic.\n\n        Equating our two expressions for $v$ gives:\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{Mgs}{m}} = 2sf$\n\n        $\\hspace{2em}$ $\\dfrac{Mgs}{m} = 4s^2f^2$\n\n        $\\hspace{2.5em}$ $\\dfrac{Mg}{m} = 4sf^2$\n\n        $\\hspace{4em}$ $s = \\dfrac{Mg}{4mf^2}$\n\n        Now substituting values:\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $M = 0.5\\ \\ce{kg}$\\\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $g = 9.8\\ \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$\\\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $m = 5 \\times 10^{-4}\\ \\ce{kg}$\\\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $f = 420-350=70\\ \\ce{Hz}$\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $s = \\dfrac{(0.5)(9.8)}{(4)(5 \\times 10^{-4})(70)^2} = 0.5\\ \\ce{m} = 50\\ \\ce{cm}$\n\n        Note that the same result can be obtained using different harmonic frequency and their corresponding wavelengths.\n\n    2.  $v=f \\lambda$, $\\hspace{3em} \\lambda = 2(0.5)=1\\, \\ce{m}$\n\n        :answer[$v = 70\\ \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        $v = 2sf = 2(0.5)(70) = 70\\ \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$\n\n        Alternatively:\n\n        $\\hspace{1em}$ $v = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{Mgs}{m}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{(0.5)(9.8)(0.5)}{(0.5 \\times 10^{-3})}} = 70\\ \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$\n\n4. 1. **Q** $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        **Q** is a node $\\text{\\textendash}$ the displacement of the string at **Q** is always zero, interference at point **Q** is always destructive.\n\n    2. **P** and **R** oscillate with the same frequency/ Frequency of both **P** and **R** is $140\\ \\ce{Hz}$. $\\checkmark$\n\n        Amplitude of oscillation of **R** is greater than that of **P**. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        Frequency of oscillation is the same at every point on the standing wave (apart from nodes where there is no oscillation).\\\n        Amplitude of oscillation on a standing wave is greatest at antinodes, amplitude decreases when moving from a node to an antinode. The amplitude at a node is zero (no oscillation)."},{"ref":"PH0942","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0942\n\nThe diagram below shows a loudspeaker above a cylindrical tube of length $l$. When the height of the water is $h$ a resonant sound of the first harmonic is heard.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0942ooo.png\" alt=\"PH0942ooo\">\n\nWater is then drained slowly from the bottom of the tube. What is the height of the water when the next resonant sound is heard?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em} $ 2$h - l$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em} $ 3$h - l$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em} $ 2$h - 2l$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em} $ 3$h - 2l$","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe open end will be an antinode and the water surface will be a node. First harmonic may be represented as follows\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0942Ans.png\" alt=\"PH0942Ans\">\n\nFrom this we can see that\n\n- $l - h = \\dfrac {\\lambda}{4}$.\n\n- $\\lambda = 4(l - h)$\n\nThird harmonic will occur when the water level has fallen a distance of $\\dfrac {\\lambda}{2}$. This may be represented as follows\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH0942AnsB.png\" alt=\"PH0942AnsB\">\n\nTherefore the new height is given by\n\n- $h - \\dfrac {\\lambda}{2} = h - \\dfrac {4(l - h)}{2} $\n- $\\hspace{3em} =h - 2(l - h) $\n- $\\hspace{3em}=h - 2l + 2h $\n- $\\hspace{3em}=3h - 2l$"},{"ref":"PH0977","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0977\n\nThe shortest distance between a node and an antinode on a standing wave is 10 cm. The frequency of the wave is 100 Hz. What is the speed of the wave?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $2.5\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $10\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $20\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ $40\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$","markScheme":"**答案：D**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe shortest distance between a node and an antinode is $\\dfrac{\\lambda}{4}$. This is illustrated below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\lambda}{4} = 10\\,\\ce{cm} = 0.1\\, \\ce{m}$\n\n- $\\lambda = (4)(0.1) = 0.4\\, \\ce{m}$\n\n- $v=f\\lambda = (100)(0.4) = 40\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH1043-2","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1043-2\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n1. In an experiment, a loudspeaker is placed near a tube that is open at both ends. A sound of constant frequency is emitted from the loudspeaker and a standing wave of the first harmonic is formed inside the tube. The displacement of the particles from equilibrium at different locations along the tube is represented in the diagram below.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH2043a%20copy.png\" alt=\"PH2043a copy\">\n\n    Outline how the standing wave is formed inside the tube. [2 marks]\n\n2. Particle $P$ has equilibrium position $\\bold{P}$ labeled on the diagram.\n\n    State whether the motion of paricle $P$ is parallel or perpendicular to the length of the tube. [1 mark]\n\n3. The graph below shows the displacement $s$ of $P$ against time $t$ for one full cycle of the standing wave.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/2044bb.png\" alt=\"2044bb\">\n    $\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\n    The speed of sound is $330\\,m\\,s^{-1}$. Calculate the length of the tube. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Incident wave is reflected at the open end/far end of the tube  $\\checkmark$\n\n    Incident and reflected wave interfere/superpose/ combine  $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    The sound wave travels through the tube and is partially reflected at the open end due to a difference in pressure inside and outside of the tube. The incident and reflected waves then superpose to form a standing wave with nodes and antinodes.\n\n2. Parallel  $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Sound waves are longitudinal. Particle motion is parallel to the direction of propagation of the wave. Here we have a stationary wave but the superposed waves are propagating parallel to the direction of the tube.\n\n2. $\\lambda=vT=(330)(0.02)=6.6$ m   $\\checkmark$\n\n    $l=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{2}=\\dfrac{6.6}{2}=3.3\\,\\ce{m}$  $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    One full oscillation of the standing wave occurs in the same time as a full oscillation for particle $P$. So, time period of the standing wave is $0.02$ s (from graph of $P$).\n\n    - $v=f\\lambda$ and $f=\\dfrac{1}{T}$ so $v=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{T}$ (alternatively we can think of this equation as speed = distance/ time for a wave)\n\n    Rearranging $v=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{T}$ for $\\lambda$ gives\n\n    - $\\lambda=vT=(330)(0.02)=6.6$ m\n\n    For the first harmonic of a tube open at both ends $l=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{2}$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH2043c%20copy.png\" alt=\"PH2043c copy\">\n\n    - $l=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{2}=\\dfrac{6.6}{2}=3.3$ m"},{"ref":"PH1817","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1817\n\nA string is fixed at one end and free at the other. Which of the following represents the string oscillating in its third harmonic frequency?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    | $\\hspace{2cm}$  |B.   ||\n| |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH1817q1.JPEG\" alt=\"PH1817q1\">|  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH1817q2.JPEG\" alt=\"PH1817q2\">|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH1817q3.JPEG\" alt=\"PH1817q3\">|  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/PH1817q4.JPEG\" alt=\"PH1817q4\">|","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nA stationary wave on a string that is fixed at one end and free at the other will have a node (point of zero amplitude) at the fixed end and an antinode (point of maximum displacement) at the free end. The following patterns show different strings oscillating with these end conditions:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/Screenshot%202025-05-07%20at%203.17.34%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-07 at 3.17.34 PM\">\n\nThe top waveform shows a string oscillating at its first harmonic frequency.\n\nThe second diagram shows a string oscillating at three times its first harmonic frequency, this is referred to as the third harmonic frequency. This corresponds to option $A$ and is the correct answer.\n\nThe third diagram shows a string oscillating at its fifth harmonic frequency. Note that although it is the third lowest resonant frequency possible, it is still called the fifth harmonic because the frequency is five times the first harmonic frequency.\n\nThis arrangement is possible when the length of the string is $n\\dfrac{\\lambda}{4}$where $n=1$, $3$, $5...$. The third harmonic occurs when $l=3\\dfrac{\\lambda}{4}=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\lambda$.\n\nIn the image below, we see that $l=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\lambda$:\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/Screenshot%202025-05-07%20at%203.19.03%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-05-07 at 3.19.03 PM\">"},{"ref":"PH1818","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1818\n\nA lightly damped mass-spring system undergoes peak resonance when a driving force $F$ with frequency $f$ is applied, resulting in a resonant amplitude $A$. If the system is more heavily damped while $F$ remains unchanged, how do the peak resonant frequency $f$ and amplitude $A$ change?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $f$ | $A$ |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| $\\hspace{1.5em}$Decreases$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$Decreases $\\hspace{1.5em}$|\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Decreases | Increases |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Increases | Decreases |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Increases | Increases |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nWhen the system experiences more damping its oscillations are more affected by energy dissipated due to resistive forces. The natural frequency of oscillation therefore decreases.\n\nAmplitude depends on how much energy is stored in oscillations, increased damping means more energy is lost per cycle, therefore amplitude decreases."},{"ref":"PH1861","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1861\n\nThe graph below corresponds to a standing wave which is formed on a string fixed at both ends while vibrating at a frequency of 3000 Hz.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/ph1861q.png\" alt=\"ph1861q\">\n\nWhich of the following corresponds to a **possible** frequency at which a standing wave could be formed on the same string?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 300 Hz\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 500 Hz\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1200 Hz\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1500 Hz","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe five antinodal sections tells us that the diagram shows the fifth harmonic of the string.\n\nThe given frequency then corresponds to five times the fundamental frequency of the string.\n\n- $f_1=\\dfrac{f_5}{5}=\\dfrac{3000 \\ce{Hz}}{5}=600$ Hz\n\nIt is given that the string is fixed at both ends, therefore a standing wave will be formed at frequencies that are multiples of 600 Hz.\n\nAmong the given answers, 1200 Hz is the only frequency that is a multiple of 600 Hz."},{"ref":"PH1862","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1862\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n1. Two sources, $S$ and $T$ produce sound waves of frequency $f$.\n\n    Source $S$ is located at position $x=0.0$ m and emits a wave that travels in the positive $x$-direction. Source $T$ is located at position $x=1.62$ m and emits a wave that travels in the negative $x$-direction.\n\n    The diagram shows the positions of the points of maximum and minimum intensity between sources $S$ and $T$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/Sscreenshot%202025-09-05%20at%209.39.44%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Sscreenshot 2025-09-05 at 9.39.44 AM\">\n\n    1.    Explain why these points of maximum and minimum intensity occur. [3 marks]\n\n    2. The speed of sound in air is 343 m s $^{-1}$. Show that the frequency of the wave is approximately 500 Hz. [2 marks]\n\n2. Source $T$ begins to move away from $S$ at a speed of 3.5 m s$^{-1}$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/Sscreenshot%202025-09-05%20at%209.38.45%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Sscreenshot 2025-09-05 at 9.38.45 AM\">\n\n    Explain why a standing wave pattern is no longer produced between the sources. You may assume that the intensity of the sound does not decrease with distance for each individual sound wave. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1. 1. Waves superpose/interfere to form a standing wave. $\\checkmark$\n\n        At maxima/antinodes there is constructive interference. $\\checkmark$\n\n        At minima/nodes there is destructive interference. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The points of maximum and minimum intensity occur because the waves from sources $S$ and $T$ travel in opposite directions along the $x$-axis and superpose when they meet.\n\n        At certain positions, the waves arrive in phase, so their displacements add constructively. This results in points of maximum amplitudes known as antinodes. At other positions, the waves meet completely out of phase, so their displacements cancel out and produce points of zero amplitude called nodes.\n\n        The repeating pattern of nodes and antinodes forms a standing wave, as the two waves have the same frequency and wavelength and are travelling in opposite directions.\n\n    2. $\\hspace{1.65em}$ $\\dfrac{\\lambda}{2}=0.34$ m\n\n        OR\n\n        - $\\lambda=0.68$ m $\\checkmark$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{343}{0.68}=$ :answer[$504$ Hz] (3sf) $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The points of maximum intensity are antinodes of the stationary wave. The distance between two antinodes is equal to half the wavelength of the wave, so we have:\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\lambda}{2}=0.64-0.30=0.34$ m\n\n        - $\\lambda=2(0.34)=0.68$ m\n\n        Now using $v=f\\lambda$ we have:\n\n        - $f=\\dfrac{v}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{343}{0.68}=504$ Hz (3sf)\n\n2. The frequency/wavelength of wave $T$ changes $\\ll$ relative to wave $S$/ stationary observer $\\gg$ due to the Doppler effect $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    When source $T$ begins to move away from $S$, the frequency of the wave it produces decreases relative to a stationary observer or relative to source $S$. This happens because of the Doppler effect. As a result, the two waves no longer have the same frequency, so they no longer produce a stable stationary wave pattern. The nodes and antinodes shift their positions over time, and the intensity pattern between the sources changes."},{"ref":"PH1954","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1954\n\nA loudspeaker is placed near the open end of a pipe that is closed at the other end.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Standing%20Waves%20%26%20Resonance/images/Screenshot%202026-02-19%20at%202.53.02%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-02-19 at 2.53.02 PM\">\n\nAs the frequency of the loudspeaker is increased, resonance is heard at two consecutive frequencies of\n425 Hz and 595 Hz. The speed of sound in air is 340 m s$^{-1}$. What is the length of the pipe $L$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.5 m\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1.0 m\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1.5 m\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2.0 m","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe pipe is closed at one end and open at the other. For resonance to occur there must be a node at one end and an antinode at the other.\n\nThe resonant frequencies are odd multiples of the first harmonic frequency,\n\n- $f_1, 3f_1, 5f_1,$ etc\n\nWhich shows the difference between two consecutive harmonic frequencies to be $2f_1$\n\nSo, we have:\n\n- $2f_1=595-425=170$ Hz\n\n- $f_1=85$ Hz\n\nNow the wavelength of the first harmonic is given by:\n\n- $\\lambda_1=\\dfrac{v}{f_1}=\\dfrac{340}{85}=4.0$ m\n\nFor the first harmonic the length of the pipe is one quarter of the wavelength:\n\n- $l=\\dfrac{\\lambda_1}{4}=\\dfrac{4.0}{4}=1.0$ m"}]},{"name":"Travelling Waves","questions":[{"ref":"PH0226","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0226\n\nWater drips onto a pond every 3.0 seconds. The speed of water waves in the pond has been measured to be $1.2 \\, \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$. What is the wavelength of water waves generated because of the drops? \n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 0.4 m\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1.6 m\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 3.6 m\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 4.8 m","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nIf water drips onto the pond every 3 seconds, then we know that the period of water waves is $3 \\, \\ce{s}$.\n\nThe frequency $f$ of the water waves is \n\n$f = \\dfrac{1}{T} = \\dfrac{1}{3} = 0.33\\, \\ce{Hz}$ \n\nThe speed of the waves $v$ is therefore:\n\n$\\begin{aligned}\n\nv &= \\lambda f\\\\\n\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n\\lambda &= \\frac{v}{f}\\\\\n\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\ \n\n&= \\frac{1.2}{0.33}\\\\\n\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\  \n\n&= 3.6\\, \\ce{m}\n\n\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlternatively, \n\n- $T=3.0\\, \\ce{s}$ and $v=1.2\\, \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$\n\n- $v=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{T}$ and $\\lambda=vT$\n\n- $\\lambda = (1.2)(3)=3.6\\, \\ce{m}$"},{"ref":"PH0373","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0373\n\nFor a wave traveling in a medium, the variation of the particles' displacement with distance travelled is shown in the following graphs. The graphs represent a wave, travelling to the right, at different moments in time.\n\nGraph I shows the wave at $t = 0\\,\\ce{s}$, while graph II is at $t = 1\\,\\ce{s}$. \n\nThe time difference between the two graphs is less than the wave's period.\n\nGraph I\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH0373.png\" alt=\"PH0373\">\n\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\nGraph II\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH0373-1.png\" alt=\"PH0373-1\">\n\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\nWhat is the wave speed?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 4 $\\ce{cm}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2 $\\ce{cm}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1 $\\ce{cm}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 16 $\\ce{cm}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nTo calculate the wave speed, we can use the wave equation: \n\n- $v=\\lambda f$\n\nSince \n\n- $f = \\dfrac{1}{T} \\rightarrow$\n\n$v = \\dfrac{\\lambda}{T}$\n\nThe wavelength is determined from either displacement-distance graph:\n\n \n\n- $\\lambda=4\\,\\ce{cm}$\n\nTo calculate the period, we notice that the difference between the first and second graphs means that the wave has travelled one quarter of a wavelength. This means the period is 4 times the time given between the two displacement-distance graphs.\n\n- $T=4(1\\,\\ce{s}) = 4\\,\\ce{s}$\n\nTherefore,\n\n- $v=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{T}=\\dfrac{4}{4}=1\\,\\ce{cm}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$\n\nLooking at the two graphs, we can also see a crest that is moved 1 cm in 1 second, which gives a wave speed of $1\\,\\ce{cm}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$."},{"ref":"PH0374","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0374\n\nA wave travels on a rope as shown, where $P$ is a point on the rope halfway between the equilibrium position and the maximum displacement at the instant shown.  \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH0374.png\" alt=\"PH0374\">\n\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\nWhich is correct about the direction and magnitude of the velocity of point $P$ after half a period compared to the shown position?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$ Direction $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Magnitude $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ A. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Up | Increases |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ B. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Up | Same |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ C. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Down | Decrease |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ D. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Down | Same |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nA wave travelling on a rope is an example of a transverse wave, where particles of the medium move up and down in a direction perpendicular to the direction of propagation. As the wave moves to the right, particle $P$ oscillates only up and down.\n\nAfter half a period, the particle will be in the position shown below. \n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH0374-1.png\" alt=\"PH0374-1\">\n\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\nThe wave is moving to the right. This means that a very short time after the current position, the solid line representing the waveform will be higher at the current position of $P$. As a result, point $P$ is moving upwards, and the velocity vector will be directed upwards.\n\nTo determine the magnitude of the velocity, we can recognize that because particle P has moved through half a period of its cycle, particle $P$ will be displaced from equilibrium the exact same distance as before - half of the amplitude. \n\nThis means that the particle will be moving at the exact same speed as before."},{"ref":"PH0375","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0375\n\nA student ties two identical ropes to a fixed wall for a fitness exercise. They then shake the ropes to form waves.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH0375.png\" alt=\"PH0375\">\n\nA point on the right rope oscillates with a period of $0.4 \\,\\ce{s}$. A point on the left rope oscillates with a period of $0.2\\,\\ce{s}$. \n\nWhat is the ratio $\\dfrac{\\textrm{wavelength of right rope}}{\\textrm{wavelength of left rope}}$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 2\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\dfrac{1}{2}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 1\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 4","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nSince \n\n$v=f\\lambda\\,\\,$and$\\,\\,f = \\dfrac{1}{T}$\n\n$v = \\dfrac{\\lambda}{T}$ giving\n\n$T = \\dfrac{\\lambda}{v}$\n\nWhich means wavelength and period are directly proportional.\n\nSince both ropes are made of the same material, they have the same wave speed.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{period of right rope}}{\\textrm{period of left rope}}=\\frac{0.4\\,\\ce{s}}{0.2\\,\\ce{s}}=2$\n\nmeaning that:\n\n \n\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{wavelength of right rope}}{\\textrm{wavelength of left rope}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{period of right rope}}{\\textrm{period of left rope}}=2$"},{"ref":"PH0376","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0376\n\nA radio wave of frequency 90 MHz travels a distance $d$, which is equivalent to 6000 wavelengths of the wave. What is d?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ $1.8\\,\\ce{km}$\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ $3.3\\,\\ce{km}$\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ $20\\,\\ce{km}$\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ $200\\,\\ce{km}$","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nFirst, we determine the wavelength using the wave equation:\n\n- $c=f\\lambda$\n\nand radio waves travel at the speed of light, $3 \\times 10^8\\, \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$\n\n- $c=f\\lambda$ \n\n- $\\lambda = \\dfrac{c}{f}$\n\n- = $\\dfrac{3\\,\\times \\,10^8}{90\\,\\times \\,10^6}=\\dfrac{10}{3}\\,\\ce{m}$\n\nThe number of waves $N$ can be found by dividing the total distance d by the wavelength:\n\n- $N = \\dfrac{d}{\\lambda}$\n\ngiving\n\n- $\\begin{aligned}\n\nd &= N \\lambda\\\\\n\n&=6000( \\frac{10}{3})\\,\\ce{m}\\\\\n\n&=20000\\,\\ce{m}\\\\\n\n&=20\\,\\ce{km}\n\n\\end{aligned}$"},{"ref":"PH0377","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0377\n\nA speaker produces a sound wave in a gas. The graph shows the variation of the gas particles' displacement from equilibrium with distance.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH0377q.png\" alt=\"PH0377q\">\n\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source for all graphs: GeoGebra - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\nWhich of the following could show this variation when the sound wave enters a solid?\n\n| | | | | |\n\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---| \n\n| A.  |    |   |  $\\hspace{1em}$  |B.|\n\n| |  <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH0377a1.png\" alt=\"PH0377a1\"> |  |  |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH0377a2.png\" alt=\"PH0377a2\">|\n\n| $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n|C. |   |   |  |D. |\n\n| | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH0377a3.png\" alt=\"PH0377a3\">  |  | |<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH0377a4.png\" alt=\"PH0377a4\"> |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nWe know that particles in solids are more tightly bound to one another than particles in the gas. As a result, each particle will move faster back to equilibrium upon being disturbed, and so the speed of sound in the solid will be greater than the speed of sound in the gas.\n\nThe frequency, or rate of oscillations stays constant when the wave travels from the solid to the gas. \n\nThe wave equation: $v = \\lambda f \\rightarrow \\lambda = \\dfrac{v}{f}$\n\nTherefore, if the wave speed increases in the solid relative to the gas, the wavelength increases as well.\n\nThe wavelength of the original wave is approximately 6, which eliminates choices A and C.\n\nThe particles in solids are far closer to each other than that in the gas. So, the particles displacement will be less in magnitude than that in the gas, and the graph will have decreased amplitude. This means that the only reasonable choice is Option D."},{"ref":"PH0449","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0449\n\nA traveling wave is generated on a string tied to a speaker as shown.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH0449.png\" alt=\"PH0449\">\n\nWhich of the following statements are correct about the travelling wave?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n\n  | | |\n\n  | :-- | :-- |\n\n  | I. | If the frequency    of the sound decreases, the wavelength of the travelling wave increases. |\n\n  | II. | If the speaker emits a louder sound the amplitude of the travelling wave increases. |\n\n  | III. | If the frequency of the sound increases, the travelling wave moves along the string faster. |\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ Only I\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II, and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe speed of a wave depends on the medium, and the frequency of the wave depends on the source.\n\nHere, the source of the travelling wave is the vibration of the speaker up and down, and the medium of that wave is the string.\n\nStatement I is correct. A decrease in the frequency of the sound from the speaker results in a decrease in the frequency of the travelling wave in the string. The wave speed is constant, as the medium of the travelling wave remains the string. The wavelength is inversely proportional to the frequency according to the wave equation:\n\n- $v = \\lambda f \\rightarrow \\lambda = \\dfrac{v}{f}$\n\nAs a result, the wavelength must increase.\n\nStatement II is also correct. A louder sound means that the amplitude of the longitudinal sound wave in the air is larger. This is caused by greater amplitude vibrations from the speaker, which also produces larger amplitude vibrations in the string.\n\nStatement III is incorrect. As previously mentioned, the wave speed is constant as the medium remains the same. Even though the frequency of the wave has increased, the wavelength will decrease in proportion to keep the wave speed equal to the wavelength times the frequency.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B, I and II only."},{"ref":"PH0450","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0450\n\nA vibration generator with a frequency of 5 Hz produces a travelling wave in a string. The graph shows how displacement of particles in the string varies with the distance from the source.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH0450.png\" alt=\"PH0450\">\n\n$\\small{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra, https://www.geogebra.org/m/jrWMapS7 by Tom Walsh]}}$\n\nWhat is the wavelength and the speed of the wave?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | $\\hspace{1em}$ Wavelength $\\hspace{1em}$ | $\\hspace{1em}$ Speed $\\hspace{1em}$ |\n\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ A. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 5 cm | $25 \\ce{\\ cm\\,s^{-1}}$ |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ B. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 5 cm | $1 \\ce{\\ cm\\,s^{-1}}$ |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ C. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 5 mm | $25 \\ce{\\ mm\\,s^{-1}}$ |\n\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$ D. $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | 5 mm | $1 \\ce{\\ mm\\,s^{-1}}$ |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$: \n\nThe frequency of the vibration generator will be the same as the frequency of the wave in the string, 5 Hz = $5\\, \\ce{s}^{-1}$.\n\nFrom the graph provided, the distance peak-to-peak is 5 cm, and so the wavelength of the wave is 5 cm.\n\nAs a result, the wave speed is:\n\n- $\\begin{aligned}\n\nv &= \\lambda f\\\\\n\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n&= (5)(5)\\\\\n\n\\hspace{1em}\\\\\n\n&= 25\\,\\ce{cm\\,s^{-1}}\n\n\\end{aligned}$"},{"ref":"PH0509","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH0509\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nA student uses the shown setup to measure the speed of sound.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/Screenshot%202025-04-16%20at%204.01.15%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-16 at 4.01.15 PM\">\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Image created using Images by OpenClipart-Vectors and Josy Dom Alexis from Pixabay - https ://pixabay.com]}}$\n\nThe sound source has a frequency of 590 Hz. The student changes the distance between the two microphones *d* and records the time difference $\\Delta$*t* between the received sounds from the microphones.\n\nThe graph below shows the data from this experiment.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/Screenshot%202025-06-04%20at%204.52.12%E2%80%AFPM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-06-04 at 4.52.12 PM\">\n\n1. Draw the line of best fit. [1 mark]\n\n2. Determine the fractional uncertainty of *d* when the time difference *$\\Delta$t* is 2.2 ms. [2 marks]\n\n3. Showing your working on the graph, calculate the speed of the sound wave.[3 marks]\n\n4. Determine the wavelength of the sound. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. $\\hspace{1em}$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/Screenshot%202025-04-15%20at%2010.51.21%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-04-15 at 10.51.21 AM\">\n\n    Any straight line that passes through all uncertainty bars $\\checkmark$\n\n2. correct value and uncertainty for d: $d=0.75\\pm0.04 $ m  $\\checkmark$\n\n    $fractional\\ unceratinty \\ = \\ \\ll \\dfrac{\\Delta d}{d} \\gg \\ = \\ \\ll \\dfrac{0.04}{0.75} \\gg \\ = \\dfrac{4}{75}$  or 5\\% $\\checkmark$\n\n3. Recognises that gradient of the line is $\\dfrac{s}{t} $\n\n    Use of gradient, with selected points indicated on graph. Points must be separated by at least 2 ms to receive mark $\\checkmark$\n\n    Shown calculation of gradient.\n\n    - $v=gradient=\\dfrac{0.9-0.18}{(2.6-0.5)\\times{10}^{-3}}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    *If points used do not fall on line of best fit, do not award this marking point*\n\n    - $v=$ :answer[$343\\ll\\ \\ce{m s}^{-1}\\gg$] $\\checkmark$ \\\n    (Values between 320 to 350 m s$^{-1}$ accepted)\n\n4.  $v=f\\lambda$\n\n    $\\lambda=\\dfrac{v}{f}=\\dfrac{343}{590}$ / divide the speed by the given frequency $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\lambda=$ :answer[$0.58\\ll\\ \\ce{m}\\gg$] $\\checkmark$ \\\n(allow ECF for use of value obtained from part (c))"},{"ref":"PH0522","marks":"14","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0522\n\n**Maximum mark: 14**\n\nA longitudinal wave travels through a medium from left to right. Graph 1 below shows how the displacement $x$ of a particle $P$ in the medium varies with time. Positive displacement is to the right.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH0522a.png\" alt=\"PH0522a\">\n\n$\\small{\\textrm{Graph 1}}$\n\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\n1. Describe what is meant by a longitudinal wave. [2 marks]\n\n2. Use the graph to determine:\n\n    1. the amplitude of the wave [1 mark]\n\n    2. the frequency of the wave [2 marks]\n\n    3. the direction of motion of the particle at $t$ = 10 ms [1 mark]\n\n3. Graph 2 below shows variations of displacement of particles in the medium with distance from the source of wave at an instant in time.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH0522-1a.jpg\" alt=\"PH0522-1a\">\n\n    $\\small{\\textrm{Graph 2}}$\n\n    $\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\n    1. Use the graph to calculate the speed of the wave. State an appropriate unit for your answer. [3 marks]\n\n    2. Label with the letter C a position on the graph at the center of a compression, and a letter R at the center of a rarefaction. [2 marks]\n\nGraph 1 showing the displacement of particle $P$ with time is reproduced below.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH0522-2a.png\" alt=\"PH0522-2a\">\n\n$\\small{\\textrm{Graph 1}}$\n\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\nA second source that oscillates in phase with the original source is added to the medium that generates identical waves 14 cm closer to $P$ than the original source. \n\n4. Draw a second dotted line to show how the displacement, $x$ of a particle in the medium would vary with time due solely to the second source. [3 marks]","markScheme":"1. A wave where the directions of the displacement of particles and the direction of energy propagation / direction of the wave travel; $\\checkmark$\n\n    are parallel. $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    The particles in a longitudinal wave oscillate parallel to the direction of energy transfer of the wave; particles in a transverse wave oscillate perpendicular to the direction of energy transfer.\n\n    Note that although this graph appears to be the shape of a transverse wave, it is a representation of a longitudinal wave, with positive values indicating displacements to the right, and negative values indicating displacements to the left.\n\n2. \n\n    1. :answer[$30\\,\\ce{mm}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The distance between the equilibrium position and the maximum displacement is the amplitude.\n\n    2. $T = 28\\,\\ce{ms}$ (from the graph) $\\checkmark$ \n\n        $f = \\dfrac{1}{T} =$ :answer[$36\\,\\ce{Hz}$]  $\\checkmark$ \n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        On a displacement-time graph, the period of oscillation of the particle in the medium can be read off from the time from peak to peak on the graph $-$ 28 ms. \n\n        The period of oscillation of an individual particle is the same as the period of the wave as it travels through the medium.\n\n        The frequency can then be calculated using the relationship $f = \\dfrac{1}{T}=\\dfrac{1}{0.028}$, giving a value of 36 Hz.\n\n    3. :answer[Left] $\\checkmark$ \n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        We are told that positive displacement on the graph means displacement to the right of the equilibrium for a particle, and negative displacement means displacement to the left of equilibrium. \n\n        We know that the particle is displaced to the right at $t = 10$ ms because the graph is positive. A short time later, at approximately $t = 14$ ms, the particle will be at equilibrium. This means that the particle must be moving to the left during this time.\n\n3. \n\n    1. The wavelength of the wave from the graph is 56 cm $\\checkmark$\n\n        $v=\\lambda f$ and $f=\\dfrac{1}{T}$ \n\n        $v=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{T}$\n\n        \n\n        $v=\\dfrac{56\\,\\times\\, 10^{-2}}{28\\,\\times\\, 10^{-3}}=$ :answer[20] $\\checkmark$ :answer[$\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The wavelength of the wave can be read from the graph as the distance peak-to-peak $-$ $56$ cm.\n\n    2. $\\hspace{1em}$\n\n        <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH0522-3.jpg\" alt=\"PH0522-3\">\n\n        $\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\n        C at distance 14 cm **OR** 70 cm $\\checkmark$\n\n        R at distance $42$ cm $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        Particles to the immediate left of the points labelled C are displaced to the right as they are positive, and particles to the immediate right of C are displaced to the left as they are negative. As a result, C is at the center of a compression.\n\n        Similarly, particles to the immediate left of the point labelled R are displaced to the left as they are negative, and particles to the immediate right of R are displaced to the right. As a result, R is at the center of a rarefaction.\n\n4. $\\hspace{1em}$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH0522-4.png\" alt=\"PH0522-4\">\n\n    $\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra - https://www.geogebra.org/graphing?lang=en]}}$\n\n    Displacement is 30 mm at $t = 0$ ms $\\checkmark$\n\n    Graph of displacement-time is shifted to the left by 7 ms $\\checkmark$\n\n    With the same amplitude **AND** period $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n    Because the second source is located in front of the first source relative to the wave direction, the particles in the medium will displace just like in the case of the first source, but a short time earlier.\n\n    The wavelength of the wave is $56\\,\\ce{cm}$. Because the second source is $14\\,\\ce{cm}$ ahead of the first, the expected phase shift is:\n\n    $\\phi = \\bigg(\\dfrac{14\\,\\ce{cm}}{56\\,\\ce{cm}}\\bigg)(2\\pi) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n    Therefore, the graph of the displacement of the particle solely due to the second source will show an identical oscillation of the particle with the same amplitude and period, except occurring one quarter of a period earlier, or 7 ms earlier."},{"ref":"PH0531","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH0531\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nWaves travel through a wave tank. A small bob on the surface of the water is attached to an elastic rope which is fixed to the base of the tank.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH0531.png\" alt=\"PH0531\">\n\nThe graph below shows the variation of vertical displacement of the bob with time.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH0531-1.png\" alt=\"PH0531-1\">\n\n$\\footnotesize{\\textrm{[Source: Created with GeoGebra by Tom Walsh- https://www.geogebra.org/m/jrWMapS7]}}$\n\n1. Use the figure and the graph above to determine:\n\n    1. the frequency of the water wave. [2 marks]\n\n    \n\n    2. the speed of the water wave. [2 marks]\n\n2. State two times when the bob is at a crest of the water wave and two times when the bob is at the trough of the water wave. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. \n\n    1. T = 50 ms. $\\checkmark$\n\n        $f = \\dfrac{1}{T} = \\dfrac{1}{(50\\,\\times\\,10^{-3})}$\n\n        $f$ = :answer[$20\\,\\ce{Hz}\\,(\\ce{s}^{-1})$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        The frequency of the oscillation of the bob is equal to the frequency of water wave  .\n\n        \n\n        The period can be determined by the time from peak to peak on the graph. The period is therefore T = 50 ms. Taking the reciprocal of the period, and account for the units, we find the final frequency:\n\n        $f = \\dfrac{1}{T} =\\dfrac{1}{(50\\,\\times\\,10^{-3})}$\n\n        $f = 20\\, \\ce{Hz}\\,(\\ce{s}^{-1})$ \n\n    2. $\\lambda = 2.5 \\ce{m}$ $\\checkmark$ \n\n        $v=\\lambda f$\n\n        $v = 2.5(20) =$ :answer[$50\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$] $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n\n        From the figure, we know that the wavelength of the wave is 2.5 m. We can then use the wave equation $v=\\lambda f$ to determine the velocity by multiplying by the frequency in the previous answer, giving a speed of $50\\, \\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$.\n\n2. The crest is where the bob is at the maximum positive displacement.\\\n\n    At $t = 12.5\\,\\ce{ms}$, and $62.5\\,\\ce{ms}$. $\\checkmark$\n\n    The trough is where the bob is at the maximum negative displacement.\\\n\n    At $t = 37.5\\,\\ce{ms}$ and $87.5\\,\\ce{ms}$. $\\checkmark$"},{"ref":"PH0940","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0940\n\nThe diagram below shows a wave of frequency 300 Hz travelling along a string $AB$. The speed of the wave is $90\\,$m s$^{-1}$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\nTwo points $P$ and $Q$ lie on the wave such that the phase difference between them is $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$. Which of the following could be the displacement between $P$ and $Q$?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ 10 cm\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ 15 cm\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ 25 cm\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ 36 cm","markScheme":"**答案：C**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe wavelength of the wave is given by\n\n- $\\dfrac{v}{f} = \\dfrac{90}{300} = 0.3\\,$m = 30 cm\n\nPoints that are $\\dfrac{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}{2\\pi}(30)=\\dfrac{1}{6}(30)=\\ce{5\\,cm}$ away from each other will have a phase difference of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$.\n\nSo a point that is 5 cm ahead of point P or 5 cm behind point P will have this phase difference.\n\nBecause 5 cm is not an answer choice, we will need to look at other possibilities.\n\nBecause the point 5 cm ahead of our point is not an option answer, we realise that a point 25 cm behind our point will be in phase with a point 5 cm ahead of our point, and so 25 cm is the correct answer here.\n\nIn more general terms:\n\nTwo points that are separated by an integer number of wavelengths, $n\\lambda$, will have a phase difference of 0.\n\nAll points that are a distance $n\\lambda \\pm \\left( \\dfrac{\\lambda}{6} \\right) $ from each other will have a phase difference of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$\n\nSo, all points that are a distance\n\n- $n(30) \\pm 5 = 30n \\pm 5$\naway from $P$ where $n$ is an integer will be $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ out of phase with $P$.\n\nAnswer is $C$ ($30-5=25\\,$cm)"},{"ref":"PH0941","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH0941\n\nBelow are three statements about sound waves.\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | Sound waves can be diffracted.  |\n  | II. | Sound waves can be refracted. |\n  | III. | Oscillations of particles are perpendicular to the direction of energy transfer.|\n\nWhich of the statements are correct?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and II only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$ I and III only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$ II and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$ I, II and III","markScheme":"**答案：A**\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nI is correct – Sound can be diffracted. Sound waves change direction as they pass around the edge of an object.\n\nII is correct – Sound can be refracted. The speed of a sound wave depends on the medium – for example sound travels much faster in water than it does in air.\n\nIII is incorrect – this is the definition of a transverse wave e.g., light. Sound waves are longitudinal. Oscillations of particles are parallel to direction of energy transfer."},{"ref":"PH1008-1","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1008-1\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nA longitudinal wave is travelling in a medium from left to right. The diagram below is a snapshot of plane wave fronts of showing position of particles $X$ (center of compression) and $Y$ (center of rarefaction) in the medium at $t =0 \\ s$.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/Screen%20Shot%202023-04-07%20at%207.12.02%20PM.png\" alt=\"Screen Shot 2023-04-07 at 7.12.02 PM\">\n\n1. Distinguish between a transverse wave and a longitudinal wave. [2 marks]\n\n2. In the graph below, the solid line shows the variation of displacement $s$ of particle $X$ with time, and the dotted line shows the variation of displacement of particle $Y$ with time.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/image.png\" alt=\"image\">\n\n    1.     State the phase difference between particles $X$ and $Y$. [1 mark]\n\n    2. Calculate the speed of the wave in the medium. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. For transverse wave; the displacement of particles/ movement of particles / oscillations of particles is **perpendicular** to the direction of energy transfer/ wave movement / wave travel. $\\checkmark$\n\n    For longitudinal wave; the displacement of particles/ movement of particles / oscillations of particles is **parallel** to the direction of energy transfer/ wave movement / wave travel. $\\checkmark$\n\n2.\n    1. half cycle/ $\\pi / \\dfrac{1}{2}$  $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        **Alternative 1**\\\n        At any instant of time magnitudes of displacement of $X$ and $Y$ are same, but in opposite direction. That is phase difference is  half cycle/ $\\pi / \\dfrac{1}{2}$\n\n        **Alternative 2**\\\n        $\\phi=\\dfrac{\\Delta t}{T}\\times2\\pi=\\ <\\dfrac{0.8}{1.6}\\times2\\pi>\\ =\\ \\pi$\n\n    2. $\\lambda = 8 \\ cm$  and  $T =1.6 \\ s$. $\\checkmark$\n\n        So; $v=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{T}\\ =\\dfrac{8}{1.6}\\ =5.0\\ cm\\ s^{-1}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        From the figure, the wavelength is two times distance between the center of a rarefaction and the center of the next compression, $\\lambda =8 \\ cm$\n\n        Period of the wave from the graph, $T= 1.6 \\ s$\n\n        $v=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{T}\\ =\\dfrac{8}{1.6}\\ =5.0\\ cm\\ s^{-1}$"},{"ref":"PH1109","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1109\n\nWaves of light and sound are transmitted through a glass box filled with air. Two experiments are carried out to investigate the effects of box contents on the wave speed $v$ in each case. In Experiment 1, air is removed from the box to create a vacuum. In Experiment 2, the box is instead filled with water.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH1109.png\" alt=\"PH1109\">\n\nWhich line of the table correctly describes the outcomes of the two experiments?\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| | Light | Light | Sound | Sound |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| | $\\hspace{1.5em}$ Experiment 1 $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$ Experiment 2 $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$ Experiment 1 $\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $\\hspace{1.5em}$ Experiment 2 $\\hspace{1.5em}$ |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $v$ unchanged | $v$ unchanged | $v$ unchanged | $v$ unchanged |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $v$ unchanged | $v$ unchanged | $v$ is zero | $v$ unchanged |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $v$ increases | $v$ decreases | $v$ unchanged | $v$ increases |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | $v$ increases | $v$ decreases | $v$ is zero | $v$ increases |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nIn a vacuum, there are no particles to transfer the mechanical vibrations needed for sound to travel. Hence, the speed of sound in Experiment 1 is zero. Conversely, light consists of electromagnetic waves that can travel through a vacuum, and the absence of particles enables them to travel more rapidly as the waves undergo no interactions with matter during travel (the refractive index of air is greater than one).\n\nDue to its greater particle density, sound travels faster in water than in air as collisions between the particles occur more rapidly. By contrast, the speed of light decreases when the air is replaced with water, as the light interacts with a greater number of particles during travel (the refractive index of water is greater than that of air)."},{"ref":"PH1110","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1110\n\nAn electromagnetic wave travels in the $x$-direction with its electric component oscillating in the $y$-direction. Which line of the table is correct?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH1110.png\" alt=\"PH1110\">\n\n$\\\\$\n\n| |  Direction of magnetic field oscillation  |  Direction of energy transfer  |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$A.$\\hspace{1.5em}$| Parallel to $y$ axis | Parallel to $x$ axis |\n|$\\hspace{1.5em}$B.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Parallel to $y$ axis | Parallel to $y$ axis |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$C.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Parallel to $z$ axis | Parallel to $x$ axis |\n| $\\hspace{1.5em}$D.$\\hspace{1.5em}$ | Parallel to $z$ axis | Parallel to $y$ axis |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe magnetic component of an electromagnetic wave is always perpendicular to the electric component and the direction of travel, meaning that it is orientated in the $z$-direction in this case.\n\nThe wave transfers energy in the direction of travel, which is in the $x$-direction."},{"ref":"PH1111","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1111\n\nThree antennae are designed to transmit electromagnetic waves to a receiver located east of the transmission location. The designs are viewed from above as shown. Design A consists of an oscillating electric current $I$ directed horizontally in the east-west direction. In Design B, electric current instead oscillates horizontally in the north-south direction. Finally, Design C consists of a continuous electric current travelling in a horizontal circle.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH1111.png\" alt=\"PH1111\">\n\nWhich design should be selected?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Design A\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Design B\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Design C\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ None of the designs are appropriate","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nElectromagnetic waves are instigated by the oscillation of an electrical charge, and they propagate perpendicular to the direction of oscillation. Therefore, transmission from west to east requires the electric current to oscillate in the north-south direction."},{"ref":"PH1150","marks":"10","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 2","type":"SAQ","body":"# PH1150\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nDuring an ultrasound imaging procedure, a sound wave with a frequency of 8.5 MHz  is employed to generate an image of the baby in the womb.\\\nThe diagram shows a transmitter/detector alongside the layers through which ultrasound waves must travel.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH1150.png\" alt=\"PH1150\">\n\n1. 1. State what is meant by a frequency of 8.5 MHz. [1 mark]\n\n    2. For a transmitted ultrasound wave, reflected waves are detected at $2.70\\times10^{-6}\\,\\ce{s}$, $30.3\\times10^{-6}\\,\\ce{s}$ and $55.7\\times10^{-6}\\,\\ce{s}$. The average speed of the ultrasound wave in fat is $1.5\\times10^3\\,\\ce{m}\\,\\ce{s}^{-1}$. Determine the thickness of the layer of fat. [3 marks]\n\nThe diagram represents equally spaced particles in the layer of fat when no ultrasound wave is passing through.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH1150b1.png\" alt=\"PH1150b1\">\n\nThe graph shows how the displacement $s$ of the particles in the layer of fat varies with the position $x$ of the particles at a specific instant in time when an ultrasound wave is passing through.\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH1150b2.png\" alt=\"PH1150b2\">\n\n2. Mark on the diagram the new positions of the particles at that instant. [2 marks]\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH1150b3.png\" alt=\"PH1150b3\">\n\n3. To improve the resolution of monitoring the baby, the frequency of the generated wave is increased. Deduce how each of the following characteristics of the wave change in the layer of fat.\n\n    1. Speed. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Wavelength. [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. 1. 8.5 million waves / oscillations / vibrations per second $\\checkmark$\n\n    2. time spent in the layer of fat = $(30.3\\times10^{-6}) - (2.70\\times10^{-6}) = 27.6\\times10^{-6}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        distance travelled in fat = $(1.5\\times10^3)(27.6\\times10^{-6})=4.1\\times10^{-2}\\,\\ce{m}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        thickness = $\\dfrac{4.1\\times10^{-2}}{2}=2.1\\times10^{-2}\\,\\ce{m}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        Accept the solutions using half of the time spent in the layer of fat leading to the same answer.\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        The wave first reflects off the boundary between the skin and the fat layer. Then it reflects off the boundary between the fat layer and the muscle. Therefore, the difference in detection times between the first and second waves corresponds to the time the $t$ wave spends travelling through the fat layer.\n\n        - $t=(30.3\\times10^{-6}) - (2.70\\times10^{-6})=27.6\\times10^{-6}$\n\n        By rearranging the equation of speed, the distance d travelled in fat:\n\n        - $d=vt$\n\n        - $d=(1.5\\times10^3)(27.6\\times10^{-6})=4.1\\times10^{-2}\\,\\ce{m}$\n\n        Since the thickness of the layer should be half of the distance covered by wave inside the layer:\n\n        - thickness = $\\dfrac{4.1\\times10^{-2}}{2}=2.1\\times10^{-2}\\, \\ce{\\mu} \\ce{m}$\n\n2. Correct positions for the particles $x=0$, 2 and 4 $\\checkmark$\n\n    Correct positions for the particles $x=1$ and 3 $\\checkmark$\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH1150b4.png\" alt=\"PH1150b4\">\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    According to the graph, the particles at the position of $x = 0$, 2 and 4 are not displaced from equilibrium. On the other hand, the particle at $x = 1$ has a negative $0.5\\,\\ce{\\mu}\\ce{m}$ displacement and the particle at $x = 3$ has a positive $0.5\\,\\ce{\\mu}\\ce{m}$ displacement.\n\n3. 1. Speed remains same $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\lambda=\\dfrac{v}{f}$ where $f$ increases but $v$ is constant, therefore wavelength decreases $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n    The speed of a wave depends solely on the medium through which it travels. Since the medium is still fat, the speed of the wave remains constant.\n\n    2. Wavelength decreases $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\lambda=\\dfrac{v}{f}$ where $f$ increases but $v$ is constant $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n        According to the wave equation:\n\n        - $v=\\lambda f$\n\n        When the frequency $f$ is increased, the wavelength  must decrease if the wave speed remains constant. Therefore, increasing the frequency results in a shorter wavelength, enhancing the resolution of the monitoring device."},{"ref":"PH1158","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1158\n\nWhich of the following can NOT transmit both longitudinal and transverse waves?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Water\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Vacuum\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Air\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Glass","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe only type of waves that can travel in vacuum is electromagnetic waves and they are transverse.\n\nThe other three media can all transmit longitudinal waves in the form of sound, and transverse waves in the form of electromagnetic radiation."},{"ref":"PH1159","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1159\n\nA water wave propagates through a region where the depth of the water varies. Which graph correctly represents the relationship between the wavelength $\\lambda$ and speed $V$ of the water wave?\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | ---| ---| ---|\n| A.  |    |  | B.  ||\n|| <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH1159A.png\" alt=\"PH1159A\"> |$\\hspace{1cm}$  |  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH1159B.png\" alt=\"PH1159B\"> |\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|\n|C. |   |   | D.  ||\n|  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH1159C.png\" alt=\"PH1159C\"> |  |  | <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/PH1159D.png\" alt=\"PH1159D\"> |","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nWater waves propagate at varying speeds in different depths, but the frequency of the wave remains constant regardless of the depth of the water. Because frequency is not dependent on the medium. It follows that wavelength and speed are directly proportional.\n\n- $v=\\lambda f$\n\n- $v \\propto \\lambda$\n\nAmong the provided graphs, only graph C aligns with this deduction."},{"ref":"PH1160","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1160\n\nWhich of the following statements is correct for the sound waves?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | The vibration of the particles is parallel to the direction of the motion of the sound waves |\n  | II. | They travel faster in liquids than in gases |\n  | III. | They cannot travel in a vacuum |\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I and III only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nSound waves are classified as longitudinal waves, whereby the vibration of particles occurs parallel to the direction of propagation of the wave. Moreover, they typically travel faster in solids than in liquids, and faster in liquids than in gases. In addition, sound waves cannot propagate in a vacuum due to the absence of a medium necessary for their transmission. Therefore all three statements are correct."},{"ref":"PH1161","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1161\n\nWhich of the following statements is/are correct for electromagnetic waves?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | The frequency of ultraviolet radiation is greater than the frequency of green light.  |\n  | II. | The wavelength of infrared radiation is greater than X-rays.  |\n  | III. | Gamma rays travel faster than radio waves in a vacuum. |\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I and II only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nAmong the given statements, the first two are accurate. Ultraviolet light indeed has a higher frequency than green light, making statement I true. Additionally, infrared waves possess longer wavelengths compared to X-rays, as stated in statement II. However, the third statement is incorrect. In a vacuum, all electromagnetic waves, including gamma rays and radio waves, propagate at the speed of light, which remains constant for all of them. Thus, statement III is inaccurate."},{"ref":"PH1162","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1162\n\nWhich of the following is NOT true for both mechanical and electromagnetic waves?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ They can be transverse\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ They transfer energy\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ The wavelength is inversely proportional to the frequency of the wave in the same medium\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ They can be longitudinal","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\n Electromagnetic waves are always composed of transverse oscillations of electric and magnetic fields, with the planes of oscillation of the two fields perpendicular to one another.\n\nAll other statements are true for both types of wave."},{"ref":"PH1163","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1163\n\nThe diagram shows the side view of a wave generated on a rope at a specific time. Which of the marked points on the rope have maximum speed at this instant?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/Screenshot%202024-10-26%20at%2012.00.12%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2024-10-26 at 12.00.12 PM\">\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ W only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ W and Y\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ X and Z only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ W, X, Y and Z","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nThe wave on the rope is a transverse wave, where particles oscillate perpendicular to the direction of the wave. The motion of particles can be viewed as simple harmonic motion, with particles reaching maximum speed at equilibrium points, such as points X and Z."},{"ref":"PH1164","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1164\n\nWhich of the following statements are not always true?\n\n- :::tableoptions{col1=\"hide\" col2=\"hide\"}\n  | | |\n  | :-- | :-- |\n  | I. | Along one wavelength of a travelling wave, no two points have the same displacement from the equilibrium position |\n  | II. | The wave generated on a spring is longitudinal |\n  | III. | The energy of an electromagnetic wave is proportional to the wavelength of the wave |\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I only\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ II only\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I and II only\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ I, II and III","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nWithin a wavelength, two points will have the same displacement from equilibrium, although their velocities will be opposite in direction. The first statement is incorrect.\n\nWaves generated on a spring can also be transverse waves. The second statement is not always true.\n\nThe energy of an electromagnetic wave is proportional to frequency and inversely proportional to the wavelength. This statement is incorrect.\n\nTherefore, all of the statements are not necessarily true."},{"ref":"PH1165","marks":"1","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1165\n\nWhich of the following combinations correctly matches the type of wave with its typical usage application?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Radio waves $\\text{\\textemdash}$ Wi-Fi\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Gamma rays $\\text{\\textemdash}$ Remote controller\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Microwaves $\\text{\\textemdash}$ Cancer treatment\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}  $ Infrared $\\text{\\textemdash}$ TV broadcasting","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanation}}$:\n\nRadio waves are commonly used for Wi-Fi. (This is the correct answer)\n\nRemote controllers typically use infrared radiation.\n\nGamma rays are used for cancer treatment\n\nRadio waves are used for TV broadcasting."},{"ref":"PH1166","marks":"1","difficulty":"medium","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"MCQ","body":"# PH1166\n\nThe graph shows the displacement $s$ of particles in a longitudinal wave as a function of distance $d$. Positive displacements are in a direction to the right of a particle's equilibrium position. Which letter, as labeled on the graph, shows the location of a particle at the centre of a compression?\n\n<img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Travelling%20Waves/images/Screenshot%202025-03-25%20at%201.52.52%E2%80%AFPM.JPEG\" alt=\"Screenshot 2025-03-25 at 1.52.52 PM\">","markScheme":"$\\underline{\\textrm{Explanations}}$:\n\nPoints A and C represent positions where particles are displaced towards point B. Therefore, point B is the position where the particle is at the centre of the compression."},{"ref":"PH1832","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1A","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH1832\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nThe speed of sound $v$ in a gas is hypothesized to depend on the pressure $P$ and the density of the gas $\\rho$ according to\n\n- $v=kP^a\\rho^b$\n\nwhere $k$ is a dimensionless constant.\n\n1. Determine the values of $a$ and $b$. [3 marks]\n\nA student wishes to test this hypothesis by measuring the speed\n$v$ of sound in a gas at different pressures and maintaining the density $\\rho$ of the gas constant.\n\n2. Identify the independent and dependent variables [2 marks]","markScheme":"1. Expressing speed, pressure and density as:\n\n    - $[v]=$ L T$^{-1}$ (or m s$^{-1}$)\n\n    - $[P]=$ F A$^{-1}$ (or kg m$^{-1}$ s$^{-2}$)\n\n    - $[\\rho]=$ M L$^{-3}$ (or kg m$^{-3}$)\n\n    Any 2 of these three magnitudes correctly expressed in terms of the correct dimensions (or fundamental units) $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $a=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    - $b=-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ $\\checkmark$\n\n    $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n    According to $v=kP^a\\rho^b$, we start by expressing the dimensional forms of $v$, $P$, and $\\rho$ in terms of dimensions mass $M$ (mass), $L$ (length), and $T$ (time):\n\n    - $[v]=$ L T$^{-1}$ (speed is distance/time)\n\n    - $[P]=$ F A$^{-1}=$ (M L T$^{-2}$)(L$^{-2}$) = M L$^{-1}$ T$^{-2}$ (pressure is force/area))\n\n    - $[\\rho]=$ M L$^{-3}$ (density is mass/volume)\n\n    We can now substitute the dimensions of each magnitude into the equation:\n\n    - L T$^{-1}$ = (M L$^{-1}$ T$^{-2})^a$(M L$^{-3})^b$\n\n    By expanding the exponents we get:\n\n    - L T$^{-1}$ = M$^a$ L$^{-a}$ T$^{-2a}$ M$^b$ L$^{-3b}$\n\n    - L T$^{-1}$ = M$^{a+b}$ L$^{-a-3b}$ T$^{-2a}$\n\n    Since both sides must have the same dimensions for mass $M$ (mass), $L$ (length), and $T$ (time) we can setup equation by comparing exponents:\n\n    - For mass: $a+b=0$\n    - For length: $-a-3b=1$\n    - For time: $-2a=-1$\n\n    From the third equation $a=\\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n\n    Then by substituting this value in the first equation we get $b=-\\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n\n    Thus, $v=kP^{1/2}\\rho^{-1/2}$, which can be written as:\n\n    - $v=k\\sqrt{\\dfrac{P}{\\rho}}$\n\n    This is consistent with the Newton-Laplace equation for the speed of sound in an ideal gas.\n\n2. 1. IV: Pressure / $P$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        DV: Speed of sound in the gas / $v$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        Pressure is the variable that would be adjusted independently and its effect of the speed would be studied.\n\n        The speed of sound $v$ is the dependent variable given that its value is assumed to depend on the pressure.\n\n        In this case the density of the gas is a controlled variable and must be kept at a constant value."}]},{"name":"Unassigned","questions":[{"ref":"PH0526-1","marks":"10","difficulty":"easy","calculator":true,"paper":"Paper 1B","type":"DBQ","body":"# PH0526-1\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\n1.\n    Young\\'s double slit experiment is used to determine the wavelength of a monochromatic light source.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Unassigned/images/Screenshot%202026-08-23%20at%207.45.59%E2%80%AFAM.png\" alt=\"Screenshot 2026-08-23 at 7.45.59 AM\">\n\n    The separation between the two slits $d$ = 0.15 $\\pm$ 0.01 mm.\\\n        The distance between the double slit and the screen $D$ = 1.30 $\\pm$ 0.05 m.\\\n        The distance between the bright fringes $s$ = 3.2 $\\pm$ 0.1 mm\n\n    1. Explain why a double slit is used instead of two light sources. [2 marks]\n\n    2. Determine the wavelength of the light, including its uncertainty. [3 marks]\n\n    3. The experiment is repeated with a larger distance $D$ but the same measurement tools were used.\n\n        State and explain the impact of this change on the uncertainty on the calculated value for wavelength. [3 marks]\n\n2. The experiment was repeated by changing the separation between the slits and the screen.\n\n    The graph shows the variation of the distance between the bright fringes $s$ and the distance between the double slit and the screen $D$.\n\n    <img src=\"questions/IB%20Physics%20SL/Wave%20Behaviour/Unassigned/images/PH0526%20Graph.png\" alt=\"PH0526 Graph\">\n\n    Describe how the wavelength can be determined using the graph. You do not need to do the calculations.          [2 marks]","markScheme":"1.\n    1. coherent waves $\\checkmark$\n\n        to have interference pattern/phase difference affects the path difference $\\checkmark$\n\n    2. $\\lambda=\\dfrac{(3.2\\times{10}^{-3})(0.15\\times{10}^{-3})}{1.3}$\n\n        $\\lambda=369.2\\times{10}^{-9}\\ll\\ \\ce{mm}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\Delta \\lambda = 50.3 \\times 10^{-9}\\ll\\ \\ce{m}\\gg$ $\\checkmark$\n\n        $\\lambda=$ :answer[$370\\times{10}^{-9}\\ \\pm\\ 50\\ \\times{10}^{-9}$ nm]  $\\checkmark$ ($369\\times{10}^{-9}\\ \\pm\\ 50\\ \\times{10}^{-9}$ also accepted if 2 significant figures are kept on the uncertainty)\n\n        $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$: \\\n        The wavelength can be calculated from the formula\n\n         - $s=\\dfrac{\\lambda D}{d}$\n\n        - $\\lambda=\\dfrac{sd}{D}$\n\n        - $\\lambda=\\dfrac{(3.2\\times{10}^{-3})(0.15\\times{10}^{-3})}{1.3}=369$.$23\\times{10}^{-9}\\ce{m}=369.23 \\ \\ce{nm}$\n\n        To calculate the fractional uncertainty in the wavelength, we add the fractional uncertainty of $s$, $d$, and $D$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta \\lambda}{\\lambda} = \\dfrac{\\Delta s}{s} + \\dfrac{\\Delta d}{d} + \\dfrac{\\Delta D}{D}$\n\n        - $\\dfrac{\\Delta \\lambda}{\\lambda} = \\dfrac{0.1}{3.2} + \\dfrac{0.01}{0.15} + \\dfrac{0.05}{1.3} = 0.13$\n\n        The uncertainty in wavelength $\\Delta \\lambda$ can be found from\n$\\Delta \\lambda = (0.13) \\lambda =(0.13)(369.23 \\times 10^{-9})=50.3 \\times 10^{-9} \\ \\ce{m} = 50 \\ \\ce{nm}$\n\n        Therefore\n\n         - $\\lambda=370\\pm50 \\ $nm\n\n         where 369 has been rounded to match the 1 significant figure precision of the uncertainty. As 2 significant figures are also permissible on uncertainty,\n\n         - $\\lambda=369\\pm50 \\ $nm\n\n         is also acceptable here.\n\n   3. Decreases $\\checkmark$\n\n        The measurement/absolute uncertainty remains the same $\\checkmark$\n\n        But with larger values of $D$ and of fringe spacing, the relative uncertainty decreases $\\checkmark$\n\n2. Calculate the gradient $\\checkmark$\n\n      $\\lambda=gradient\\times\\ \\dfrac{d}{gradient} \\times 0.15 \\ $mm $\\checkmark$\n\n      $\\underline{\\textrm{Further Explanation}}$:\\\n      The distance between bright fringes $s$ is given by\n      $s=\\dfrac{\\lambda D}{d}$\\\n      Then the gradient of this graph is\n      $gradient=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{d}$\\\n      The wavelength can be calculated from\n      $\\lambda=gradient\\times\\ d$"}]}]}]};
